window.LESSONS = [null,{"n":1,"kind":"lesson","title":"Comment allez-vous ?","titleEn":"How are you?","intro":"Before starting out, please be sure to read the introduction and pronunciation guide – even if you already studied French before. This will help you understand how the Assimil method works.","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour Jeanne, comment allez-vous ?","en":"Hello Jeanne, how are you?","lit":"how go-you"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien, et vous ?","en":"Well, and you?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça va très bien, merci.","en":"Very well, thanks.","lit":"it goes"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vous présente ma fille, Chloé.","en":"Let me introduce my daughter, Chloé.","lit":"I present-you"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour, Chloé. Comment ça va ?","en":"Hello, Chloé. How are you?","lit":"it goes"}],"notes":[{"ref":"","text":"In the English translation of the dialogues, we use square brackets [ ] to indicate words that are necessary in English but omitted in French, while italicised words in parentheses ( ) give a literal word-for-word translation to help you understand the mechanics of the language."},{"ref":"pron","text":"In most French words, the final consonant is silent. For example, vous sounds like [voo]. And comment is generally pronounced [kohmœhⁿ] – as in line 5. But if the following word starts with a vowel, then the normally silent final consonant of the first word is pronounced and “linked” to the second word: comment allez: [kohmœhⁿ-talay]. This link is called a liaison. Listen to the difference in the pronunciation of comment in sentences 1 and 5. We indicate the liaison with an undertie between the two words."},{"ref":"1","text":"bonjour literally means good (bon) day (jour). It can be used as the equivalent of good morning but also as a formal way of saying hello throughout the day until around 6 p.m."},{"ref":"2","text":"allez comes from the irregular verb aller, to go, the verb used to enquire about someone’s health or well-being (instead of to be in English: How are you?). Allez is the form used with the formal address for you (vous) when speaking to people other than close friends or family."},{"ref":"3","text":"ça va is the third-person singular (he/she/it) of aller."},{"ref":"4","text":"présenter means to present but, in this context, to introduce. Je vous présente is the equivalent of May I introduce / I would like to introduce, etc."},{"ref":"5","text":"Comment ça va ? How are you doing? is equivalent to Comment allez-vous ?, but slightly more familiar. Here, Jeanne (the female equivalent of the masculine forename Jean) is talking to her friend’s daughter, so she uses a less formal phrase."},{"ref":"","text":"French has two forms of address: formal and familiar. We’ll start by learning the honorific, or formal, mode, marked by the pronoun vous, because this is the one you are most likely to encounter when visiting France and interacting with people you don’t know. (In other French-speaking countries, such as Canada, the informal address, marked by tu, is used more frequently.) Which form to use in which circumstances is a much debated issue, but we’ve adopted the rule of thumb that tu is strictly for family and friends."},{"ref":"","text":"Congratulations! In a short space of time, and without too much effort, you’ve learned some important – and useful – words and phrases. Remember that the best way to make progress is to learn a little every day. That’s the secret of the Assimil method!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Bonjour, comment allez-vous ?","a":"Hello, how are you?"},{"q":"Ça va très bien.","a":"I’m very well."},{"q":"Comment ça va, Jean ?","a":"How are you, Jean?"},{"q":"Je vous présente ma fille.","a":"Let me introduce my daughter."},{"q":"Chloé va très bien.","a":"Chloé is very well."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"How are you?","t":"{{Comment}} allez-vous ?"},{"cue":"Very well, thank you.","t":"Ça {{va}} très bien, {{merci}}."},{"cue":"Let me introduce Jeanne and Chloé.","t":"Je vous {{présente}} Jeanne {{et}} Chloé."},{"cue":"Well, and you?","t":"{{Bien}}, et vous ?"},{"cue":"My first lesson.","t":"Ma {{première}} leçon."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"bonjour","en":"hello, good day","zh":"你好;日安","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"bon + jour = good day","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Bonjour Jeanne, comment allez-vous ?","exEn":"Hello Jeanne, how are you?","src":1},{"fr":"merci","en":"thank you","zh":"谢谢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ mercy (a thank-you for a kindness)","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Ça va très bien, merci.","exEn":"Very well, thanks.","src":3},{"fr":"comment allez-vous ?","en":"how are you? (formal)","zh":"您好吗?(正式)","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonjour Jeanne, comment allez-vous ?","exEn":"Hello Jeanne, how are you?","src":1},{"fr":"ça va très bien, merci","en":"I’m very well, thanks","zh":"我很好,谢谢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça va très bien, merci.","exEn":"Very well, thanks.","src":3},{"fr":"et vous ?","en":"and you?","zh":"那您呢?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien, et vous ?","exEn":"Well, and you?","src":2},{"fr":"je vous présente ma fille","en":"let me introduce my daughter","zh":"我来介绍一下我的女儿","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"présenter ≈ to present, here: to introduce","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vous présente ma fille, Chloé.","exEn":"Let me introduce my daughter, Chloé.","src":4},{"fr":"ma fille","en":"my daughter","zh":"我的女儿","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"family","kw":"sounds like “fee-y” – picture a little girl holding out a coin to pay her ‘fee’","ex":"Je vous présente ma fille.","exEn":"Let me introduce my daughter.","src":0},{"fr":"une leçon","en":"a lesson","zh":"一节课","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ lesson (ç = s sound)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ma première leçon.","exEn":"My first lesson.","src":0},{"fr":"première","en":"first (f)","zh":"第一(阴性)","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"≈ premier","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ma première leçon.","exEn":"My first lesson.","src":0},{"fr":"très","en":"very","zh":"很;非常","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça va très bien, merci.","exEn":"Very well, thanks.","src":3},{"fr":"bien","en":"well, fine","zh":"好;很好","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien, et vous ?","exEn":"Well, and you?","src":2},{"fr":"vous","en":"you (formal / plural)","zh":"您;你们","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien, et vous ?","exEn":"Well, and you?","src":2}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":17,"gaps":[[1.56,4.02],[4.86,7.64],[14.92,16.62],[20.72,21.9],[22.68,25.58],[26.44,28.62],[29.44,32.04],[34.84,39.1],[40.46,43.6],[45.3,49.08],[51.2,55.28],[56.48,57.66],[58.44,64.68],[67.42,69.58],[71.48,74.64],[75.62,79.24],[80.5,83.6],[85.26,88.36],[89.44,94.3]],"dur":94.3},{"n":2,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le café","titleEn":"The café","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Monsieur, madame, vous désirez ?","en":"Sir, Madam, what would you like?","lit":"you desire"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Deux cafés et deux croissants, s’il vous plaît.","en":"Two coffees and two croissants, please.","lit":"if it pleases you"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je préfère une tartine beurrée pour le petit-déjeuner.","en":"No, I prefer a slice of buttered bread for breakfast.","lit":"for the breakfast"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Donc, deux expressos, un croissant et une tartine ?","en":"So, two espressos, one croissant and one slice of buttered bread?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, c’est ça. Le croissant est pour moi et la tartine pour elle.","en":"Yes, that’s right. The croissant is for me and the bread for her.","lit":"this is it"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"This is the easiest way to ask a question in French: use the affirmative (vous désirez, you want) but with a rising intonation at the end of the sentence."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(2) Remember that the final s of a noun is generally not pronounced, so the plural form often sounds exactly the same as the singular. Likewise, the final x of deux and the final z of désirez are silent: [duh] and [dayzeeray].\n(4) Note the liaison: deux expressos becomes a single sound. The x is pronounced as a [z]: [duhz-expresso].\n(5) Notice that est, is, and et, and, are pronounced in exactly the same way."},{"ref":"2","text":"un café is both a beverage (a coffee) and the place where you drink it (a café). If you order un café in France, you will get a shot of espresso without milk, also called un expresso or un express."},{"ref":"3","text":"Although s’il vous plaît (“if it pleases you”) may seem very formal, it isn’t. It just means please."},{"ref":"4","text":"une tartine beurrée (or simply une tartine) is a slice of buttered bread, a popular breakfast alternative to a croissant. Beurré is the adjective formed from le beurre, butter. In this sentence, it ‘agrees’ with the noun une tartine, which is feminine, so we add another -e: beurrée. More on the question of agreement later."},{"ref":"5","text":"le déjeuner means lunch, so le petit-déjeuner (literally “small lunch”) is breakfast."},{"ref":"6","text":"All French nouns have a gender: they are either masculine or feminine. A masculine noun is indicated by the articles un/le, a/the, and a feminine noun by une/la. Whenever you learn a new noun, make sure you learn its gender. Note that French does not have a separate word for one, so un café can mean a coffee or one coffee, depending on the context."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je préfère un croissant pour le petit-déjeuner.","a":"I prefer a croissant for breakfast."},{"q":"Un café, s’il vous plaît.","a":"One/A coffee, please."},{"q":"Oui, c’est ça.","a":"Yes, that’s right."},{"q":"La tartine est pour elle.","a":"The slice of bread is for her."},{"q":"Et l’expresso est pour moi.","a":"And the espresso is for me."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"One/A coffee and a/one slice of buttered bread.","t":"{{Un}} café et {{une}} tartine {{beurrée}}."},{"cue":"The croissant and the slice of bread.","t":"{{Le}} croissant et {{la}} tartine."},{"cue":"Two croissants, please.","t":"{{Deux}} croissants, {{s’il vous plaît}}."},{"cue":"No, in fact, I prefer an espresso.","t":"{{Non}}, {{en fait}}, je {{préfère}} un expresso."},{"cue":"Good morning, madam and sir.","t":"Bonjour, {{madame et monsieur}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un café","en":"a coffee; a café","zh":"一杯咖啡;咖啡馆","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ café / coffee","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"Un café, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"One/A coffee, please.","src":0},{"fr":"un croissant","en":"a croissant","zh":"一个羊角面包","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"same as English","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"Le croissant est pour moi et la tartine pour elle.","exEn":"The croissant is for me and the bread for her.","src":5},{"fr":"une tartine beurrée","en":"a slice of buttered bread","zh":"一片涂黄油的面包","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"le beurre = butter","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"sounds like “tart-een” – picture a jam tart spread out flat on a slice of buttered toast","ex":"Non, je préfère une tartine beurrée pour le petit-déjeuner.","exEn":"No, I prefer a slice of buttered bread for breakfast.","src":3},{"fr":"le petit-déjeuner","en":"breakfast","zh":"早餐","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je préfère une tartine beurrée pour le petit-déjeuner.","exEn":"No, I prefer a slice of buttered bread for breakfast.","src":3},{"fr":"s’il vous plaît","en":"please","zh":"请","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Deux cafés et deux croissants, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Two coffees and two croissants, please.","src":2},{"fr":"vous désirez ?","en":"what would you like?","zh":"您想要什么?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"désirer ≈ to desire","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Monsieur, madame, vous désirez ?","exEn":"Sir, Madam, what would you like?","src":1},{"fr":"je préfère un croissant","en":"I prefer a croissant","zh":"我更喜欢羊角面包","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"préférer ≈ to prefer","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je préfère un croissant pour le petit-déjeuner.","exEn":"I prefer a croissant for breakfast.","src":0},{"fr":"oui, c’est ça","en":"yes, that’s right","zh":"对,没错","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, c’est ça.","exEn":"Yes, that’s right.","src":5},{"fr":"pour moi","en":"for me","zh":"给我","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le croissant est pour moi et la tartine pour elle.","exEn":"The croissant is for me and the bread for her.","src":5},{"fr":"donc","en":"so, therefore","zh":"所以;那么","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Donc, deux expressos, un croissant et une tartine ?","exEn":"So, two espressos, one croissant and one slice of buttered bread?","src":4},{"fr":"deux cafés","en":"two coffees","zh":"两杯咖啡","pos":"num","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Deux cafés et deux croissants, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Two coffees and two croissants, please.","src":2},{"fr":"non, en fait","en":"no, in fact","zh":"不,其实","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"en fait ≈ in fact","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, en fait, je préfère un expresso.","exEn":"No, in fact, I prefer an espresso.","src":0},{"fr":"et","en":"and","zh":"和;而且","pos":"conj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et l’expresso est pour moi.","exEn":"And the espresso is for me.","src":0}],"uncertain":["note 5: the printed note is cut off after 'Logically' in the OCR; the truncated word was left out"],"pdfPage":19,"gaps":[[2.02,4.1],[4.84,7.7],[27.42,31.1],[32.44,34.52],[35.26,37.34],[40.08,43.86],[46.4,50.86],[54.38,59.96],[64.04,70.34],[74.3,82.42],[84.94,86.66],[89.44,94.82],[96.18,99.68],[100.7,103.54],[105.26,108.26],[109.68,114.58]],"dur":114.6},{"n":3,"kind":"lesson","title":"Présentations","titleEn":"Introductions","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je me présente : je m’appelle Sophie Mercier.","en":"Let me introduce myself: I am called Sophie Mercier.","lit":"I introduce me: I call-myself"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Et voici mon ami Loïc Le Gall. Il est breton.","en":"And here is my friend Loïc Le Gall. He is Breton.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Et vous, êtes-vous bretonne aussi ?","en":"And you, are you Breton, too?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas du tout, je suis de Nîmes, dans le Midi de la France.","en":"Not at all, I am from Nîmes, in the south of France.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais maintenant nous sommes parisiens.","en":"But now we are Parisians.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(1) Most final consonants are not pronounced. That is why the second r in Mercier is silent: [mairsyay].\n(2) Note the liaison: mon ami is pronounced [mohⁿ-amee]."},{"ref":"1","text":"appeler, to call. Je m’appelle literally means “I call myself,” but is equivalent to My name’s … French can sometimes seem very formal (s’il vous plaît for please, see Note 3 Lesson 2), but you’ll soon get used to it."},{"ref":"2","text":"We saw in the last lesson that you can ask a question simply by raising the intonation of an affirmative phrase. Here is a more formal – but very simple – way of forming a question with the verb être (to be): you simply switch the verb and pronoun: Vous êtes breton ? ➔ Êtes-vous breton ?"},{"ref":"3","text":"Remember that every noun is either masculine or feminine. But some nouns, particularly those describing men and women, have two forms: bretonne is the feminine form of breton."},{"ref":"4","text":"The first person pronoun je, I, does not take an initial capital unless it is the first word of the sentence."},{"ref":"5","text":"Now you know the main forms of être: je suis, il est, vous êtes, nous sommes, I am, he is, you are, we are."},{"ref":"","text":"France is a huge country – the largest in western Europe – with an incredibly varied landscape ranging from the rugged granite cliffs of Bretagne, Brittany, to the lush olive groves of southern France. We will learn more about the French regions as we progress: today we’ve met a Breton and a woman from the area known as le Midi, another word for the south of France. (If you arrive by train in the Belgian capital, Brussels, one of the two main stations is Bruxelles Midi, so-called because it is in the south of the city.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je m’appelle Loïc.","a":"My name is Loïc."},{"q":"Et voici mes deux amis.","a":"And these are my two friends."},{"q":"Êtes-vous parisien ?","a":"Are you Parisian?"},{"q":"Pas du tout ! Je suis breton.","a":"Not at all! I’m Breton."},{"q":"Il est de Nîmes aussi.","a":"He’s from Nîmes, too."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I’m Breton and she’s Breton, too.","t":"Je suis {{breton}} et elle est {{bretonne aussi}}."},{"cue":"We are Parisians.","t":"{{Nous sommes}} parisiens."},{"cue":"Are you from Nîmes?","t":"{{Êtes-vous}} de Nîmes ?"},{"cue":"I’m called Sophie. And you?","t":"{{Je m’appelle}} Sophie. {{Et vous}} ?"},{"cue":"I’m now at the third lesson.","t":"{{Je suis}} maintenant à la {{troisième}} leçon."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je m’appelle Sophie","en":"my name is Sophie","zh":"我叫苏菲","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"appeler ≈ to appeal/call","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me présente : je m’appelle Sophie Mercier.","exEn":"Let me introduce myself: I am called Sophie Mercier.","src":1},{"fr":"je me présente","en":"let me introduce myself","zh":"我来自我介绍一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"présenter ≈ to present","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me présente : je m’appelle Sophie Mercier.","exEn":"Let me introduce myself: I am called Sophie Mercier.","src":1},{"fr":"voici mon ami","en":"here is my friend","zh":"这是我的朋友","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et voici mon ami Loïc Le Gall.","exEn":"And here is my friend Loïc Le Gall.","src":2},{"fr":"un ami","en":"a friend (m)","zh":"一位(男)朋友","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ amiable, amicable","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et voici mon ami Loïc Le Gall.","exEn":"And here is my friend Loïc Le Gall.","src":2},{"fr":"il est breton","en":"he is Breton","zh":"他是布列塔尼人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"breton / bretonne ≈ Breton","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et voici mon ami Loïc Le Gall. Il est breton.","exEn":"And here is my friend Loïc Le Gall. He is Breton.","src":2},{"fr":"êtes-vous bretonne aussi ?","en":"are you Breton, too?","zh":"您也是布列塔尼人吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et vous, êtes-vous bretonne aussi ?","exEn":"And you, are you Breton, too?","src":3},{"fr":"aussi","en":"too, also","zh":"也","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est de Nîmes aussi.","exEn":"He’s from Nîmes, too.","src":0},{"fr":"pas du tout","en":"not at all","zh":"一点也不","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas du tout, je suis de Nîmes, dans le Midi de la France.","exEn":"Not at all, I am from Nîmes, in the south of France.","src":4},{"fr":"je suis de Nîmes","en":"I am from Nîmes","zh":"我来自尼姆","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas du tout, je suis de Nîmes, dans le Midi de la France.","exEn":"Not at all, I am from Nîmes, in the south of France.","src":4},{"fr":"dans le Midi de la France","en":"in the south of France","zh":"在法国南部","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas du tout, je suis de Nîmes, dans le Midi de la France.","exEn":"Not at all, I am from Nîmes, in the south of France.","src":4},{"fr":"maintenant","en":"now","zh":"现在","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “mint-nonh” – picture a mint candy that must be eaten NOW","ex":"Mais maintenant nous sommes parisiens.","exEn":"But now we are Parisians.","src":5},{"fr":"nous sommes parisiens","en":"we are Parisians","zh":"我们是巴黎人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"parisien ≈ Parisian","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais maintenant nous sommes parisiens.","exEn":"But now we are Parisians.","src":5},{"fr":"mais","en":"but","zh":"但是","pos":"conj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais maintenant nous sommes parisiens.","exEn":"But now we are Parisians.","src":5}],"uncertain":["dialogue 2 and 5: the book gives no dash for these sentences; 'Le Gall' read as the surname from OCR 'Le Cjall'"],"pdfPage":23,"gaps":[[1.46,3.72],[4.5,7.48],[11.56,12.9],[17.22,18.72],[26.74,28.06],[30.58,33.46],[34.5,36.76],[37.54,40.2],[44.28,50.5],[54.82,61.26],[63.7,68.38],[73.04,79.84],[82.34,88.72],[90.96,93.42],[94.78,97.78],[99.48,103.24],[104.2,107.56],[109.7,112.86],[114.16,118.34]],"dur":118.4},{"n":4,"kind":"lesson","title":"L’heure","titleEn":"The time","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Pardon, est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","en":"Excuse me, do you have the time?","lit":"pardon; hour"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’ai pas de montre mais je pense qu’il est midi dix.","en":"I don’t have a watch, but I think that it is ten past noon.","lit":"noon ten"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais comment le savez-vous ?","en":"But how do you know that?","lit":"how it you-know"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma femme à midi…","en":"Because I’m meeting my wife at noon…","lit":"I have an appointment with"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"… et elle a toujours dix minutes de retard !","en":"…and she is always ten minutes late!","lit":"has; of"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(1), (3), (4) The final -z of avez and savez is silent. The same rule applies to rendez in the next sentence.\n(2), (5) In line 2, dix is pronounced [dees] because it comes at the end of the sentence. But in 5, it is followed by another word, so the final x is silent: [dee meenewt].\n(5) Remember that the final -s is (almost) never pronounced."},{"ref":"1","text":"We know that le and la are, respectively, the masculine and feminine definite articles (the). But if the noun begins with a vowel or an h, we use l’. You see how important it is to learn your genders! L’heure (it’s feminine) literally means the hour, but we also use the word when asking the time. Avez-vous l’heure ?, Do you have (i.e. can you tell me) the time?"},{"ref":"2","text":"Another very common way of asking a question is to put est-ce que (literally, “is it that”) before the verb. So Avez-vous l’heure ? and Est-ce que vous avez l’heure ? have the same meaning."},{"ref":"3","text":"To form the negative, you need two words: ne (or n’ before a vowel or an h) directly before the verb, and pas directly after it: Je n’ai pas l’heure, I don’t have the time."},{"ref":"4","text":"French does not have a neutral pronoun such as it. You have to use either il or elle, depending on the gender of the noun. In neutral contexts, such as when you are telling the time, always use il."},{"ref":"5","text":"In the last lesson, we saw that le Midi is the south of France. The word also means noon (“midday”)."},{"ref":"6","text":"Here, the article le means it or that. Notice that it is placed before the verb, not afterwards, as in English."},{"ref":"","text":"One of the basic principles of our method is that we do not try to explain everything at once. We believe that when you start learning a language, it’s important to get used to the new sounds and constructions without trying to find an explanation for everything straight away. Trust us: we’ve been teaching people successfully for more than 90 years!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","a":"Do you have the time?"},{"q":"J’ai rendez-vous avec ma fille.","a":"I’m meeting my daughter."},{"q":"Ma femme n’a pas de montre.","a":"My wife does not have a watch."},{"q":"Il a toujours dix minutes de retard.","a":"He’s always ten minutes late."},{"q":"Je pense qu’il est midi.","a":"I think it’s noon."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Do you have a wife?","t":"{{Est-ce que}} vous {{avez une}} femme ?"},{"cue":"How do you know (it)?","t":"Comment le {{savez-vous}} ?"},{"cue":"I don’t have a watch.","t":"{{Je n’ai pas}} de montre."},{"cue":"Because I’m ten minutes late.","t":"{{Parce que j’ai}} dix minutes de {{retard}}."},{"cue":"Excuse me, is it midday?","t":"{{Pardon}}, {{est-il}} midi ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"pardon","en":"excuse me, pardon","zh":"对不起;劳驾","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"same as English pardon","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Pardon, est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","exEn":"Excuse me, do you have the time?","src":1},{"fr":"l’heure (f)","en":"the hour; the time","zh":"小时;时间(几点)","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"sounds like “ur” – picture an ‘err’ clock that gets the hour wrong","ex":"Pardon, est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","exEn":"Excuse me, do you have the time?","src":1},{"fr":"est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","en":"do you have the time?","zh":"您知道现在几点吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","exEn":"Do you have the time?","src":0},{"fr":"je n’ai pas de montre","en":"I don’t have a watch","zh":"我没有手表","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “mohn-truh” – picture a ‘monster’ wearing a huge watch","ex":"Je n’ai pas de montre mais je pense qu’il est midi dix.","exEn":"I don’t have a watch, but I think that it is ten past noon.","src":2},{"fr":"je pense qu’il est midi","en":"I think it is noon","zh":"我想现在是中午十二点","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"penser ≈ pensive","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je pense qu’il est midi.","exEn":"I think it’s noon.","src":0},{"fr":"midi","en":"noon, midday","zh":"中午十二点","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma femme à midi…","exEn":"Because I’m meeting my wife at noon…","src":4},{"fr":"comment le savez-vous ?","en":"how do you know (that)?","zh":"您怎么知道的?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais comment le savez-vous ?","exEn":"But how do you know that?","src":3},{"fr":"parce que","en":"because","zh":"因为","pos":"conj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma femme à midi…","exEn":"Because I’m meeting my wife at noon…","src":4},{"fr":"j’ai rendez-vous avec ma femme","en":"I’m meeting my wife","zh":"我和妻子有约","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma femme à midi…","exEn":"Because I’m meeting my wife at noon…","src":4},{"fr":"ma femme","en":"my wife","zh":"我的妻子","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"femme = wife or woman, not ‘feminine’","cluster":"family","kw":"","ex":"Ma femme n’a pas de montre.","exEn":"My wife does not have a watch.","src":0},{"fr":"elle a toujours dix minutes de retard","en":"she is always ten minutes late","zh":"她总是迟到十分钟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"minute ≈ minute; retard ≈ tardy","ff":"un retard = a delay; avoir dix minutes de retard = to be ten minutes late","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"… et elle a toujours dix minutes de retard !","exEn":"…and she is always ten minutes late!","src":5},{"fr":"toujours","en":"always","zh":"总是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il a toujours dix minutes de retard.","exEn":"He’s always ten minutes late.","src":0},{"fr":"est-il midi ?","en":"is it noon?","zh":"现在是中午吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pardon, est-il midi ?","exEn":"Excuse me, is it midday?","src":0}],"uncertain":["culture box 'One of the basic principles of our method…' sits at the top of the lesson 5 English page; attributed here to lesson 4 by the book's layout (could belong to lesson 5)"],"pdfPage":25,"gaps":[[1.58,3.6],[4.28,7.38],[9.5,10.52],[23.08,26.16],[27.2,29.44],[30.14,32.34],[34.48,38.74],[42.36,48.04],[49.3,52.76],[55.56,60],[62.48,67.56],[69.64,71.3],[72.58,76.02],[78.18,81.72],[83.3,86.5],[88.96,92.12],[93.52,98.28]],"dur":98.3},{"n":5,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je cherche le métro","titleEn":"I’m looking for the metro [station]","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Excusez-moi, je cherche le métro Saint-Michel.","en":"Excuse me, I am looking for the Saint-Michel metro station.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Désolée, madame, je ne suis pas d’ici.","en":"Sorry, madam, I’m not from here.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais attendez une minute, j’ai un plan de la ville.","en":"But wait a minute, I have a map of the city.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Voilà : traversez le boulevard et tournez à gauche à la fontaine.","en":"Here we are: cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","lit":"to left"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci beaucoup pour votre aide !","en":"Thank you very much for your help!","lit":"a lot"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(5) Notice how a word that ends in a vowel (votre) runs into the next word that begins with one: votre aide = [vohtred]."},{"ref":"1","text":"French does not have a separate verb form to express a present continuous action (e.g. I am looking). Instead, you use the simple form (I look), which makes life much easier!"},{"ref":"2","text":"le Métropolitain, known universally as le métro, is the underground railway system (subway) of major cities such as Paris, Marseille or Lyon. The word is also used to refer to a specific station: le métro Châtelet, the Châtelet metro station, as well as to the train itself. (You can also ask for une station de métro, a metro station.)"},{"ref":"3","text":"As we will see throughout this course, a substantial portion of English vocabulary is derived from French, and, in many cases, the imported words or expressions are quite formal. For instance, while désolé is the root of desolate and desolation, it is an ordinary, everyday word equivalent to the Germanic-origin sorry: Je suis désolé, I’m sorry."},{"ref":"4","text":"You will notice that French people use madame and monsieur a lot when addressing strangers. This is not as formal as it might sound in English, which lacks a convenient, polite way of catching someone’s attention."},{"ref":"5","text":"une ville, a city or a town. French makes no difference between the two."},{"ref":"6","text":"voilà is the companion word to voici (Lesson 3 line 2). In principle, we use voici to designate something or someone located close to us (like here is/are) and voilà for something farther away (there is/are). But in practice, we use voilà in both instances. For instance, Voilà le métro, Here’s the metro station / train. We can also use it at the beginning of a sentence to present something or provide information. We’ll see voilà and voici again in different contexts in the coming weeks."},{"ref":"","text":"The Saint-Michel district of Paris is synonymous with the Quartier latin, or Latin Quarter (so-called because it is home to the Sorbonne University, where classes were originally taught in Latin). The Place Saint-Michel is a central meeting point, dominated by a 19th-century monumental fountain. Leading off it is the Boulevard Saint-Michel, famed for its bookstores and cafés."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Excusez-moi, je cherche le métro.","a":"Excuse me, I’m looking for the metro [station]."},{"q":"Désolé, je ne suis pas d’ici.","a":"I’m sorry, I’m not from here."},{"q":"Merci beaucoup de votre aide.","a":"Thanks very much for your help."},{"q":"Je n’ai pas de plan.","a":"I don’t have a map."},{"q":"Attendez une minute, s’il vous plaît.","a":"Wait a minute, please."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","t":"{{Traversez le}} boulevard et {{tournez}} à gauche à {{la}} fontaine."},{"cue":"Wait two minutes, please.","t":"{{Attendez}} deux minutes, {{s’il vous plaît}}."},{"cue":"Are you looking for the metro [station]?","t":"Vous {{cherchez le}} métro ?"},{"cue":"Here’s a city map.","t":"{{Voilà un}} plan {{de la}} ville."},{"cue":"Sorry, I’m an hour late. (if the person speaking is a man)","t":"{{Désolé}}, j’ai {{une}} heure de {{retard}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"excusez-moi","en":"excuse me","zh":"打扰一下;请问","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ excuse","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Excusez-moi, je cherche le métro Saint-Michel.","exEn":"Excuse me, I am looking for the Saint-Michel metro station.","src":1},{"fr":"je cherche le métro","en":"I’m looking for the metro","zh":"我在找地铁站","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"métro ≈ metro","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “shairsh” – picture a ‘church’ you search for in the city","ex":"Excusez-moi, je cherche le métro Saint-Michel.","exEn":"Excuse me, I am looking for the Saint-Michel metro station.","src":1},{"fr":"je suis désolé","en":"I’m sorry","zh":"我很抱歉","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ desolate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Désolée, madame, je ne suis pas d’ici.","exEn":"Sorry, madam, I’m not from here.","src":2},{"fr":"je ne suis pas d’ici","en":"I’m not from here","zh":"我不是本地人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Désolée, madame, je ne suis pas d’ici.","exEn":"Sorry, madam, I’m not from here.","src":2},{"fr":"attendez une minute","en":"wait a minute","zh":"请等一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"minute ≈ minute","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais attendez une minute, j’ai un plan de la ville.","exEn":"But wait a minute, I have a map of the city.","src":3},{"fr":"un plan","en":"a map (of a city)","zh":"(城市)地图","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ plan","ff":"un plan = a street map; also ‘a plan’","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais attendez une minute, j’ai un plan de la ville.","exEn":"But wait a minute, I have a map of the city.","src":3},{"fr":"une ville","en":"a city, a town","zh":"城市;城镇","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ village, villa","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Mais attendez une minute, j’ai un plan de la ville.","exEn":"But wait a minute, I have a map of the city.","src":3},{"fr":"voilà","en":"here is / there is; here you are","zh":"给你;就在那里","pos":"other","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voilà : traversez le boulevard et tournez à gauche à la fontaine.","exEn":"Here we are: cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","src":4},{"fr":"traversez le boulevard","en":"cross the boulevard","zh":"穿过大街","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"traverser ≈ to traverse","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voilà : traversez le boulevard et tournez à gauche à la fontaine.","exEn":"Here we are: cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","src":4},{"fr":"tournez à gauche","en":"turn left","zh":"向左转","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"tourner ≈ to turn","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “goash” – picture a gosh-awful gauche (clumsy) driver who always veers left","ex":"Voilà : traversez le boulevard et tournez à gauche à la fontaine.","exEn":"Here we are: cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","src":4},{"fr":"une fontaine","en":"a fountain","zh":"喷泉","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ fountain (-ain = -ain/-ount)","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Voilà : traversez le boulevard et tournez à gauche à la fontaine.","exEn":"Here we are: cross the boulevard and turn left at the fountain.","src":4},{"fr":"merci beaucoup","en":"thank you very much","zh":"非常感谢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci beaucoup pour votre aide !","exEn":"Thank you very much for your help!","src":5},{"fr":"l’aide (f)","en":"the help","zh":"帮助","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ aid","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci beaucoup pour votre aide !","exEn":"Thank you very much for your help!","src":5}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":29,"gaps":[[1.6,3.9],[5.26,8.56],[12.94,14.32],[16.92,18.18],[21.34,22.64],[27.76,28.9],[30.6,33.68],[34.78,37.08],[38.44,41.74],[46.12,53.26],[55.84,60.56],[63.72,69.24],[74.36,81.84],[83.52,88.1],[90.28,92.66],[95.1,98.82],[100.88,103.98],[105.84,109.46],[110.3,113.44],[115.78,120.56]],"dur":120.6},{"n":6,"kind":"lesson","title":"À l’hôtel","titleEn":"At the hotel","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonsoir monsieur, bonsoir madame. Bienvenue à l’Hôtel Molière.","en":"Good evening, sir; good evening, madam. Welcome to the Molière Hotel.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonsoir, nous avons une chambre réservée pour deux nuits.","en":"Good evening, we have a room reserved for two nights.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Quel est votre nom, s’il vous plaît ?","en":"What is your name, please?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Je m’appelle Bailly : B-A-I deux L-Y. Alain.","en":"My name is Bailly: B-A-I-double-L-Y. Alain.","lit":"I call-myself"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Absolument : une grande chambre avec un grand lit et une salle de bains.","en":"Absolutely: a large room with a double bed and a bathroom.","lit":"big bed; room of bath"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est au quatrième étage. Voici la clé.","en":"It’s on the fourth floor. Here’s the key.","lit":"at"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Où sont vos bagages ?","en":"Where are your bags?","lit":"your luggage"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"bonsoir, good evening, is the formal greeting used after 6 p.m. (See Lesson 1 note 1.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"Remember that the definite article l’ replaces le or la in front of a word beginning with a vowel or an h. Un hôtel is masculine."},{"ref":"3","text":"le nom, the name. The word generally refers to a person’s surname, the proper term of which is le nom de famille, family name. Le prénom (literally “pre-name”) is the term for the first name."},{"ref":"4","text":"grand is an adjective meaning big or large. Adjectives ‘agree’ with the gender of the noun they qualify, so we say un grand lit, a large bed, but une grande chambre. You see now why learning noun genders is so important."},{"ref":"5","text":"Here’s another type of agreement: pronouns also need to change depending on whether the noun is plural or singular. Vos is the plural of votre: votre nom, your name, but vos bagages, your bags. We’ll learn more about agreements later on."},{"ref":"","text":"When you start learning a language, you often get the feeling that people are speaking at breakneck speed! In many cases, this is because several words are run together to form a single sound. In French, this is often due to the liaison, which can make four words such as nous avons une chambre sound like two – or even one: [nooz-ahvohⁿzewn shohⁿbruh]. Remember that we do exactly the same thing in English (I want to be a star ➔ [eyewonabeeya-star]). You’ll soon get used to listening out for phrases rather than individual words."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Bienvenue à l’hôtel.","a":"Welcome to the hotel."},{"q":"Votre chambre est au sixième étage. Voici la clé.","a":"Your room is on the sixth floor. Here’s the key."},{"q":"Votre nom est Bailly ? – Absolument. Mon prénom est Alain.","a":"Is your surname Bailly? – Absolutely. My first name is Alain."},{"q":"J’ai une chambre réservée pour trois nuits.","a":"I have a room reserved for three nights."},{"q":"Où est la salle de bains, s’il vous plaît ?","a":"Where’s the bathroom, please?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I have a large room with a big bed.","t":"J’ai {{une grande}} chambre {{avec un grand}} lit."},{"cue":"Where is the room and where are the bags?","t":"{{Où est la}} chambre et {{où sont les}} bagages ?"},{"cue":"The bathroom is big.","t":"{{La salle de bains}} est {{grande}}."},{"cue":"What is your name?","t":"{{Quel}} est {{votre}} nom ?"},{"cue":"Good evening. My name is Alain Bailly.","t":"{{Bonsoir. Je m’appelle}} Alain Bailly."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"bonsoir","en":"good evening","zh":"晚上好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"bon + soir = good evening","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Bonsoir monsieur, bonsoir madame. Bienvenue à l’Hôtel Molière.","exEn":"Good evening, sir; good evening, madam. Welcome to the Molière Hotel.","src":1},{"fr":"bienvenue à l’hôtel","en":"welcome to the hotel","zh":"欢迎光临酒店","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"bien + venue = well-come","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bienvenue à l’hôtel.","exEn":"Welcome to the hotel.","src":0},{"fr":"l’hôtel (m)","en":"the hotel","zh":"酒店","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ hotel (ô = os)","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Bienvenue à l’hôtel.","exEn":"Welcome to the hotel.","src":0},{"fr":"nous avons une chambre réservée","en":"we have a room reserved","zh":"我们预订了一个房间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"réservée ≈ reserved","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonsoir, nous avons une chambre réservée pour deux nuits.","exEn":"Good evening, we have a room reserved for two nights.","src":2},{"fr":"une nuit","en":"a night","zh":"一夜;一晚","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"sounds like “nwee” – picture a ‘newie’ sleeping all night in a new bed","ex":"Bonsoir, nous avons une chambre réservée pour deux nuits.","exEn":"Good evening, we have a room reserved for two nights.","src":2},{"fr":"quel est votre nom ?","en":"what is your name?","zh":"您叫什么名字?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"nom ≈ name","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel est votre nom, s’il vous plaît ?","exEn":"What is your name, please?","src":3},{"fr":"absolument","en":"absolutely","zh":"当然;绝对","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ absolutely","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Absolument : une grande chambre avec un grand lit et une salle de bains.","exEn":"Absolutely: a large room with a double bed and a bathroom.","src":5},{"fr":"une grande chambre","en":"a large room","zh":"一个大房间","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"chambre ≈ chamber","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Absolument : une grande chambre avec un grand lit et une salle de bains.","exEn":"Absolutely: a large room with a double bed and a bathroom.","src":5},{"fr":"un grand lit","en":"a big bed","zh":"一张大床","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Absolument : une grande chambre avec un grand lit et une salle de bains.","exEn":"Absolutely: a large room with a double bed and a bathroom.","src":5},{"fr":"une salle de bains","en":"a bathroom","zh":"浴室","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"bains ≈ bath","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Absolument : une grande chambre avec un grand lit et une salle de bains.","exEn":"Absolutely: a large room with a double bed and a bathroom.","src":5},{"fr":"au quatrième étage","en":"on the fourth floor","zh":"在四楼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"étage = floor/storey of a building","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est au quatrième étage. Voici la clé.","exEn":"It’s on the fourth floor. Here’s the key.","src":6},{"fr":"la clé","en":"the key","zh":"钥匙","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “clay” – picture a key moulded from clay","ex":"C’est au quatrième étage. Voici la clé.","exEn":"It’s on the fourth floor. Here’s the key.","src":6},{"fr":"où sont vos bagages ?","en":"where are your bags?","zh":"您的行李在哪里?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"bagages ≈ baggage","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Où sont vos bagages ?","exEn":"Where are your bags?","src":7},{"fr":"mon prénom","en":"my first name","zh":"我的名(不含姓)","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"pré + nom = pre-name","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon prénom est Alain.","exEn":"My first name is Alain.","src":0}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":33,"gaps":[[1.58,3.74],[4.7,7.48],[36.16,39.26],[40.32,42.48],[43.44,46.22],[50.2,56.32],[60.68,67.2],[68.62,72.22],[78.64,87.36],[91.32,97.52],[101.68,109.52],[111.6,113.74],[115.38,118.64],[122.08,126.02],[131.92,135.6],[138,141.58],[143.22,147.76]],"dur":147.8},{"n":7,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"Here is a short dialogue that reviews some of the main things you’ve seen in the previous six lessons. If you have the recordings, listen to each line and repeat it. (Reading aloud is vital for practising the pronunciation.) Then, translate it. You’ll be surprised at your progress and your ability to remember!","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour Chloé, comment allez-vous ?","en":"Hello Chloé, how are you?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça va très bien merci.","en":"Very well, thanks.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Et vous ?","en":"And you?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Très bien. Est-ce que vous avez l’heure ?","en":"Very well. Do you have the time?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Désolée, je n’ai pas de montre.","en":"Sorry, I don’t have a watch.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je pense qu’il est midi.","en":"But I think it’s noon.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment le savez-vous ?","en":"How do you know?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma fille","en":"Because I have a meeting with my daughter","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"et elle a toujours cinq minutes de retard.","en":"and she is always five minutes late.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci beaucoup pour votre aide.","en":"Thanks very much for your help.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"Introduction","text":"Every seventh lesson of this course is devoted to revising and explaining in greater detail what you have learned over the previous days."},{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Gender","text":"This is possibly the biggest difficulty facing English speakers when they start learning French. Every noun is either masculine or feminine. Masculine nouns take the articles le, the, and un, a, an; feminine nouns take la and une. There is no neuter pronoun, like it in English.\nThere are some basic rules for determining the right gender: the words for woman, wife, grandmother, etc. are feminine (we already know une femme and une fille), while those for man, boy, etc. are masculine. So far, so logical. But why is coffee (le café) masculine and a slice of bread (une tartine) feminine? The simple answer is: because! In fact there are some patterns, which you will learn to recognise. But basically you will have to rely on your memory. We suggest you start by writing down every noun you learn, together with both articles (definite and indefinite), under one of two columns. Here’s a short list to help you get started:\nMasculine nouns: un/le boulevard, un/le café, un/le croissant, un/l’hôtel, un/le lit, un/le métro, un/le plan\nFeminine nouns: une/la chambre, une/la tartine, une/la fille, une/la fontaine, une/l’heure, une/la minute, une/la montre, une/la nuit, une/la salle de bains\nOne of the reasons why it is so important to remember genders is that adjectives ‘agree’ with the noun they describe, and therefore have both a masculine and a feminine form (we saw this briefly in Lesson 6: un grand lit / une grande chambre). We’ll learn more about agreements very soon."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Verbs","text":"French verbs are divided into three groups, depending on the ending of the infinitive. (Unlike English infinitives, which are formed with to, French infinitives consist of a single word.) By far the largest group comprises verbs ending in -er. We have already seen quite a few of them, including appeler, to call, chercher, to look for, désirer, to want, penser, to think, présenter, to introduce, tourner, to turn, and traverser, to cross.\nAs in English, French verb endings change depending on the ‘person’ doing the action (I think, but she thinks). Let’s see what we have learned already:\nje pense, I think, vous pensez, you [formal] think, and nous pensons, we think.\nAs you can see, there are more forms than in English, which generally has just two in the present tense, but you will soon get the hang of them. Remember, too, that there are no continuous verb forms in French, so je cherche means both I look for and I am looking for.\nWe have also seen some parts of a key verb, être, to be, which is irregular. Remember: je suis, I am, il/elle est, he/she/it is, and vous êtes, you are. This verb is very important because it is used as an auxiliary, as we will see later on.\nAnother major irregular verb is aller, to go: je vais, I go, il/elle va, he/she goes, nous allons, we go, vous allez, you go, ils/elles vont, they go.\nBoth être and aller are used as auxiliaries.\nLastly, remember that you need two words, ne (n’ before a vowel) and pas, on either side of a verb to form a negative: Je n’ai pas l’heure, I don’t have the time; Il n’est pas breton, He’s not Breton.\nThat’s enough for the time being. But just think what you’re already capable of saying in French after just six lessons!"},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 The alphabet","text":"As in English, each letter of the French alphabet has a name, which we use when spelling words aloud. Here is a table to help you pronounce the letter names.\na [ah]\nb [bay]\nc [say]\nd [day]\ne [uh]\nf [ef]\ng [zhay]\nh [ash]\ni [ee]\nj [zhee]\nk [kah]\nl [el]\nm [em]\nn [en]\no [oh]\np [pay]\nq between [koo] and [kew]\nr [er]\ns [ess]\nt [tay]\nu [ew]\nv [vay]\nw [doobluh-vay]\nx [eeks]\ny [ee grek]\nz [zed]\nA couple of the letters are unusual. For instance, y is i grec or Greek i (which is what the Romans used to call it) and w is double v instead of ‘double u’ (w is rarely used in French). When spelling a word or name with a double letter, for example, Bailly, you can either repeat the letter (l, l) or say ‘double l’ (as in English). Lastly, remember that the pronoun je, I, does not take a capital letter unless it is at the beginning of a sentence."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Vocabulary","text":"French is a Romance language, and most of its vocabulary comes from Latin, which can make it sound rather formal or ‘starchy’ to an English speaker. But this is not the case. It’s simply that there are very few Anglo-Saxon words in French. Whereas English often has pairs of words, such as kingly and regal, that have similar meanings but slight nuances, French has only one. You’ll soon get past the impression of formality.\nFrench and English share some of the same roots, so many words are identical. But beware of what we call ‘false friends’ – words that look the same in both languages but have different meanings. For example, we saw the word retard, which means late. (Although our word retard has the same etymology, the meaning is certainly not the same!) However, you may recognise retard as the root of our word tardy, so there are definite links.\nLastly, in these early stages, be very careful of French words that look the same as their English counterparts but that are pronounced differently. A classic example is Jean, which is pronounced [zhœhⁿ] and is a man’s name. It may be useful to underline these tricky words or note them down, just to remind yourself."},{"ref":"","text":"Don’t worry: after only a couple of weeks of regular practice all these new things – pronunciations, word orders, genders and so on – will be almost second nature!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai rendez-vous avec ma fille","en":"I have a meeting with my daughter","zh":"我和女儿有约","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"rendez-vous = appointment/meeting, not only a romantic date","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Parce que j’ai rendez-vous avec ma fille","exEn":"Because I have a meeting with my daughter","src":8},{"fr":"elle a toujours cinq minutes de retard","en":"she is always five minutes late","zh":"她总是迟到五分钟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et elle a toujours cinq minutes de retard.","exEn":"and she is always five minutes late.","src":9},{"fr":"merci beaucoup pour votre aide","en":"thank you very much for your help","zh":"非常感谢您的帮助","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci beaucoup pour votre aide.","exEn":"Thanks very much for your help.","src":10},{"fr":"il n’est pas breton","en":"he’s not Breton","zh":"他不是布列塔尼人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il n’est pas breton.","exEn":"He’s not Breton.","src":0}],"uncertain":["dialogue 9: the book's English translation says only 'and she's always late'; 'five minutes' added to match the French","revision: the noun table's column layout was reconstructed from OCR into two lists (masculine / feminine); the alphabet table was flattened into one letter per line"],"pdfPage":35,"gaps":[[1.56,3.78],[5.52,8.98],[13.72,14.78],[24.66,25.68],[33.56,37.12]],"dur":37.1},{"n":8,"kind":"lesson","title":"Une visite","titleEn":"A visit","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour mademoiselle, est-ce que votre père est à la maison ?","en":"Good morning, miss. Is your father at home?","lit":"good-day; is it that your father is at the house"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, monsieur ; il est au bureau aujourd’hui.","en":"No, sir; he is at the office today.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"À quelle heure est-ce qu’il rentre après le travail ?","en":"At what time does he come back after work?","lit":"at what hour is it that he returns after the work"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Oh, pas avant huit heures normalement.","en":"Oh, not before 8 o’clock normally.","lit":"hours"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous voulez l’adresse de son bureau ?","en":"Do you want the address of his office?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, s’il vous plaît.","en":"Yes, please.","lit":"if it pleases you"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Attendez un moment, je cherche mon carnet d’adresses.","en":"Wait a moment, I’m looking for my address book.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Voilà. Sept rue Marbeuf, dans le huitième.","en":"There it is. 7 rue Marbeuf, in the 8th district.","lit":"street"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci beaucoup, mademoiselle. Au revoir.","en":"Thank you very much, miss. Goodbye.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"De rien, monsieur. Au revoir.","en":"You’re welcome, sir. Goodbye.","lit":"of nothing"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"You’re already beginning to familiarise yourself with the basic pronunciation rules. So, to avoid unnecessary repetition, we will confine ourselves from now on to presenting only new words (or word groups) or unconventional pronunciations."},{"ref":"1","text":"We have seen that there are fewer prepositions in French than in English. Thus à la can mean to the, at the or in the, depending on the preceding verb. We say à la if the noun is feminine: à la maison, at home, and au if the noun is masculine: au bureau, at the office. Remember, all nouns are either masculine or feminine."},{"ref":"2","text":"We know that jour means day (as in bonjour, good day, or, more generally, hello). The adverb aujourd’hui means today."},{"ref":"3","text":"Remember that the present tense, temps présent in French, translates both the simple and continuous forms of our present tense: je cherche, I look for and I’m looking for."},{"ref":"4","text":"un carnet is a common word that crops up in many guises. The basic meaning is a notebook but, if you take the Paris metro, you can buy un carnet de tickets, a book of tickets, which is cheaper than buying tickets individually. Un carnet d’adresses, an address book. (Notice that une adresse has only one “d”.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"beaucoup is an adverb of quantity that translates much, many (and a lot of), making no distinction between countable and uncountable nouns: beaucoup de temps, much time; beaucoup de croissants, many croissants. Merci beaucoup, Thanks very much; Thanks a lot."},{"ref":"","text":"The three major cities in France (Paris, Lyon, Marseille) are divided into districts called arrondissements (from the adjective rond, round). Paris has 20 arrondissements numbered from the first, le premier, to the twentieth, le vingtième. In everyday conversation, it is usual to omit the word arrondissement after the figure. Elle habite dans le septième, She lives in the seventh [district]. In Belgium, un arrondissement is a subdivision of one of the country’s ten provinces. And in Switzerland, the word refers to an administrative area in the canton of Bern. Remember to read aloud the numbers at the beginning of each lesson and at the top of each page."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Est-ce que votre père est à la maison aujourd’hui ?","a":"Is your father at home today?"},{"q":"Il est au bureau, monsieur.","a":"He’s at the office, sir."},{"q":"Voici l’adresse : il habite dans le sixième.","a":"Here is the address: he lives in the sixth [district]."},{"q":"Merci beaucoup, monsieur. – De rien, mademoiselle.","a":"Thank you very much, sir. – Don’t mention it, miss."},{"q":"À quelle heure est-ce qu’il rentre ? – Pas avant sept heures.","a":"What time does he come home? – Not before 7.00."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"My father is at home (at the house) and my daughter is at the office.","t":"Mon {{père}} est {{à la maison}} et ma fille est {{au bureau}}."},{"cue":"Do you want the address? Wait, I’m looking for my book.","t":"Vous {{voulez}} l’{{adresse}} ? Attendez, je {{cherche mon carnet}}."},{"cue":"(At) What time does he come back?","t":"{{À quelle heure}} est-ce qu’il {{rentre}} ?"},{"cue":"I’m going to the office and after I’m going back.","t":"Je vais {{au bureau}} et après je {{rentre}}."},{"cue":"Do you want my father’s address? – Yes please.","t":"{{Vous}} voulez l’adresse {{de mon père}} ? – Oui, s’il vous plaît."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"à la maison","en":"at home","zh":"在家","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “may-zohn” – picture a ‘mason’ building a house at home","ex":"Bonjour mademoiselle, est-ce que votre père est à la maison ?","exEn":"Good morning, miss. Is your father at home?","src":1},{"fr":"il est au bureau","en":"he is at the office","zh":"他在办公室","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"bureau = office (or desk), not a government ‘bureau’ only","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, monsieur ; il est au bureau aujourd’hui.","exEn":"No, sir; he is at the office today.","src":2},{"fr":"aujourd’hui","en":"today","zh":"今天","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Non, monsieur ; il est au bureau aujourd’hui.","exEn":"No, sir; he is at the office today.","src":2},{"fr":"à quelle heure est-ce qu’il rentre ?","en":"what time does he come back?","zh":"他几点回来?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À quelle heure est-ce qu’il rentre après le travail ?","exEn":"At what time does he come back after work?","src":3},{"fr":"le travail","en":"work","zh":"工作","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ travail, travel (same root)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À quelle heure est-ce qu’il rentre après le travail ?","exEn":"At what time does he come back after work?","src":3},{"fr":"pas avant huit heures","en":"not before eight o’clock","zh":"八点以前不会","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Oh, pas avant huit heures normalement.","exEn":"Oh, not before 8 o’clock normally.","src":4},{"fr":"normalement","en":"normally","zh":"通常","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ normally (-ment = -ly)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oh, pas avant huit heures normalement.","exEn":"Oh, not before 8 o’clock normally.","src":4},{"fr":"vous voulez l’adresse ?","en":"do you want the address?","zh":"您要地址吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"adresse ≈ address","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous voulez l’adresse de son bureau ?","exEn":"Do you want the address of his office?","src":5},{"fr":"un carnet d’adresses","en":"an address book","zh":"通讯录","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Attendez un moment, je cherche mon carnet d’adresses.","exEn":"Wait a moment, I’m looking for my address book.","src":7},{"fr":"attendez un moment","en":"wait a moment","zh":"请稍等","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"moment ≈ moment","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Attendez un moment, je cherche mon carnet d’adresses.","exEn":"Wait a moment, I’m looking for my address book.","src":7},{"fr":"une rue","en":"a street","zh":"街道","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"sounds like “rew” – picture a street where everyone ‘rues’ (regrets) the traffic","ex":"Voilà. Sept rue Marbeuf, dans le huitième.","exEn":"There it is. 7 rue Marbeuf, in the 8th district.","src":8},{"fr":"mademoiselle","en":"miss","zh":"小姐","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ ma + demoiselle (damsel)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci beaucoup, mademoiselle. Au revoir.","exEn":"Thank you very much, miss. Goodbye.","src":9},{"fr":"au revoir","en":"goodbye","zh":"再见","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"De rien, monsieur. Au revoir.","exEn":"You’re welcome, sir. Goodbye.","src":10},{"fr":"de rien","en":"you’re welcome","zh":"不客气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"De rien, monsieur. Au revoir.","exEn":"You’re welcome, sir. Goodbye.","src":10}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":41,"gaps":[[1.64,3.26],[4.44,6.04],[26.4,27.58],[37.52,40.78],[41.9,43.52],[44.7,46.3],[49.34,54.94],[57.5,62.18],[64.14,68.3],[70.58,75.12],[76.76,80.36],[81.28,84.76],[87.94,93.38],[97.12,103.34],[105.96,111.18],[113.32,119.22],[122.5,124.76],[127.58,130.48],[132.18,135.7],[138.32,141.7],[144.9,148.06],[151,156.4]],"dur":156.4},{"n":9,"kind":"lesson","title":"À la mairie","titleEn":"At the town hall","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce monsieur s’appelle Jérôme Lafarge et cette dame est sa femme.","en":"This gentleman is called Jérôme Lafarge and this lady is his wife.","lit":"calls-himself"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","en":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","lit":"for"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Quel âge a cet enfant ?","en":"How old is this child?","lit":"what age has"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Il a huit ans, monsieur.","en":"He is eight years old, sir.","lit":"has"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Et il s’appelle Lafarge ? Est-ce que c’est votre enfant ?","en":"And he is called Lafarge? Is he your child?","lit":"calls-himself"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, monsieur.","en":"Yes, sir.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien. Et il habite chez vous ?","en":"Fine. And does he live with you?","lit":"at"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais évidemment, avec moi et sa mère !","en":"Obviously, with me and his mother!","lit":"but"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"this or that is ce for a masculine noun and cette for a feminine noun. Ce garçon, this boy; cette femme, this woman. But if a masculine noun begins with a vowel or a silent h, we write cet: cet ami, this friend; cet homme, this man. (cette and cet are pronounced the same.) You see why it’s so important to learn the gender of each and every noun!"},{"ref":"2","text":"un monsieur is used as a noun in formal French to mean a gentleman. (We saw this word used as a greeting in Lesson 2 line 1.)"},{"ref":"3","text":"Don’t confuse the preposition à (at, to, etc.) with a, the third person singular of the verb avoir (to have). The pronunciation is the same but the grave accent (`) shows the grammatical difference between the two words."},{"ref":"4","text":"In French, you have your age, whereas in English you are your age: elle a dix ans, she is 10 years old; quel âge a cet enfant ?, how old is this child? Unlike in English, we always add ans, years, after the age: vingt ans, 20 years old."},{"ref":"5","text":"chez [shay] means basically “home of”: chez moi, my place; my home (it comes from the Latin word for house, casa). But chez can also mean the shop of: chez le boulanger, at the baker’s. We’ll see some “abstract” uses of chez later on in the course."},{"ref":"","text":"La mairie (from the personal noun un maire, a mayor) is the equivalent of a town hall. (In major cities, each arrondissement has its own mairie, and the principal administrative centre is called l’hôtel de ville.) La mairie plays a vital part in community life: citizens register births, marriages and deaths there, and they also renew la carte nationale d’identité, or national identity card (see next lesson), which has to be produced for official purposes (voting, identity checks, etc.) and, sometimes, when picking up a registered letter or parcel, etc."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Quel âge a cet enfant ? – Il a neuf ans.","a":"How old is this child? – He is nine years [old]."},{"q":"Ce monsieur s’appelle Jérôme et cette dame s’appelle Marie.","a":"This gentleman is called Jérôme and this lady is called Marie."},{"q":"J’habite chez un ami à Lyon.","a":"I am living at a friend’s in Lyon."},{"q":"Est-ce que c’est votre carte d’identité ?","a":"Is this your identity card?"},{"q":"Oui, évidemment !","a":"Yes, obviously!"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"This gentleman, this lady and this child are Breton.","t":"{{Ce}} monsieur, {{cette}} dame et {{cet}} enfant sont bretons."},{"cue":"Here is Henri and his wife. How are you?","t":"{{Voici}} Henri et {{sa}} femme. Comment {{ça va}} ?"},{"cue":"How old is this woman? – She is twenty years old.","t":"{{Quel âge a cette}} femme ? – Elle {{a}} vingt {{ans}}."},{"cue":"I’m doing my job, that’s all.","t":"Je {{fais mon travail}}, c’est tout."},{"cue":"He lives with us.","t":"Il {{habite chez}} nous."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"la mairie","en":"the town hall","zh":"市政厅;镇公所","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"maire ≈ mayor","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","exEn":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","src":2},{"fr":"ce monsieur","en":"this gentleman","zh":"这位先生","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce monsieur s’appelle Jérôme Lafarge et cette dame est sa femme.","exEn":"This gentleman is called Jérôme Lafarge and this lady is his wife.","src":1},{"fr":"cette dame","en":"this lady","zh":"这位女士","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"dame ≈ dame","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce monsieur s’appelle Jérôme Lafarge et cette dame est sa femme.","exEn":"This gentleman is called Jérôme Lafarge and this lady is his wife.","src":1},{"fr":"cet enfant","en":"this child","zh":"这个孩子","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"enfant ≈ infant","ff":"","cluster":"family","kw":"","ex":"Quel âge a cet enfant ?","exEn":"How old is this child?","src":3},{"fr":"un fils","en":"a son","zh":"儿子","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"family","kw":"sounds like “feess” – picture your son handing you a ‘fee’ with a hiss, asking for pocket money","ex":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","exEn":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","src":2},{"fr":"quel âge a cet enfant ?","en":"how old is this child?","zh":"这个孩子几岁?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"âge ≈ age","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel âge a cet enfant ?","exEn":"How old is this child?","src":3},{"fr":"il a huit ans","en":"he is eight years old","zh":"他八岁","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"French says ‘has’ for age: il a huit ans, not ‘il est huit ans’","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Il a huit ans, monsieur.","exEn":"He is eight years old, sir.","src":4},{"fr":"il s’appelle Lafarge","en":"he is called Lafarge","zh":"他姓拉法热","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et il s’appelle Lafarge ?","exEn":"And he is called Lafarge?","src":5},{"fr":"il habite chez vous","en":"he lives with you / at your place","zh":"他住在您家","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"habiter ≈ to inhabit","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et il habite chez vous ?","exEn":"And does he live with you?","src":7},{"fr":"une carte d’identité","en":"an identity card","zh":"身份证","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"identité ≈ identity (-té = -ty)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","exEn":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","src":2},{"fr":"une nouvelle carte","en":"a new card","zh":"一张新卡","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"nouveau ≈ novel, new","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","exEn":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","src":2},{"fr":"pour demander une carte","en":"to ask for a card","zh":"为了申请一张卡","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"demander ≈ to demand","ff":"demander = to ask (for), not ‘to demand’","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont à la mairie pour demander une nouvelle carte d’identité pour leur fils.","exEn":"They are at the town hall to ask for a new identity card for their son.","src":2},{"fr":"évidemment","en":"obviously, of course","zh":"显然;当然","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ evidently","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais évidemment, avec moi et sa mère !","exEn":"Obviously, with me and his mother!","src":8},{"fr":"avec moi","en":"with me","zh":"和我一起","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais évidemment, avec moi et sa mère !","exEn":"Obviously, with me and his mother!","src":8}],"uncertain":["dialogue 1: the English page prints 'Jérôme Laforge' but the French (and exercises) have 'Lafarge'; 'Lafarge' used throughout","note 3: accent symbol shown as a grave accent (`) in the OCR"],"pdfPage":45,"gaps":[[1.44,3.68],[4.38,7.28],[20.64,21.64],[35.34,38.44],[39.42,41.68],[42.38,45.26],[51.8,60.52],[66.46,74.78],[75.72,78.86],[80.18,83.64],[86.44,91.66],[92.3,95.12],[96.96,100.74],[103.82,110.32],[112.4,114.12],[116.68,119.52],[123.44,128.52],[130.82,135.26],[136.92,140.62],[141.74,146.44]],"dur":146.5},{"n":10,"kind":"lesson","title":"C’est très simple !","titleEn":"It’s very simple!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"D’accord. Je fais mon travail, c’est tout.","en":"OK. I’m doing my job, that’s all.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Est-ce que vous avez le formulaire B-52 ?","en":"Do you have the form B-52?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui monsieur, nous l’avons.","en":"Yes, sir, we have it.","lit":"we it have"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Et l’imprimé A-65 ?","en":"And the printed form A-65?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça aussi, nous l’avons.","en":"That also, we have it.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah bon ? Mais est-ce que vous avez son passeport ?","en":"Oh really? But do you have his passport?","lit":"good"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr. Nous avons même sa photo.","en":"Of course. We even have his photo.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Très bien. Alors je vous fais la carte. Voilà.","en":"Very well. So I’ll do you the card. Here it is.","lit":"am-doing"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Réglez à la caisse, s’il vous plaît.","en":"Pay at the cash desk, please.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Zut ! Je ne trouve pas mon portefeuille !","en":"Damn! I can’t find my wallet!","lit":"not find not"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"d’accord is used to express agreement or consent (it’s also the origin of the English word accord). In everyday speech, however, many French speakers simply use the universally understood OK (or even OK, d’accord!)"},{"ref":"2","text":"Unlike English, the possessive adjective in French agrees with the gender of the object possessed, not the owner. In the previous lesson, we learned son bureau. Why son? Because le bureau is masculine. Here, we have son passeport and sa photo (une photo), both of which “belong” to the young boy. But what matters, as far as the possessive adjective is concerned, is the gender of the noun. We’ll go into greater detail in Lesson 14."},{"ref":"3","text":"même has several meanings: here it means even. Followed by que it means the same as. For the time being, simply remember this usage. Other forms will crop up later."},{"ref":"4","text":"Notice how the present simple tense in French can express an immediate future (Je vous fais, I’m doing = I will do) as well as the auxiliary can (Je ne trouve pas, I don’t find = I can’t find). Don’t worry about the rule for the time being; just remember these two examples."},{"ref":"5","text":"régler is a formal way of saying to pay (similar to our settle up). Une caisse is a cash desk, checkout, etc."},{"ref":"6","text":"Zut ! is a mild, inoffensive expletive to express annoyance, similar to our damn, heck, etc."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Est-ce que je règle à la caisse ?","a":"Do I pay at the cash desk?"},{"q":"Il ne trouve pas son portefeuille.","a":"He can’t find his wallet."},{"q":"Est-ce que vous avez une photo ?","a":"Do you have a photo?"},{"q":"Oui. J’ai même mon passeport.","a":"Yes. I even have my passport."},{"q":"Ah bon ? Très bien.","a":"Oh really? Very good."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"She’s doing her job, that’s all.","t":"{{Elle fait son}} travail, {{c’est tout}}."},{"cue":"I will do you his/her card and his/her passport if you like.","t":"Je vous {{fais sa carte}} et {{son passeport}} si vous {{voulez}}."},{"cue":"He’s doing his job, that’s all.","t":"Il {{fait son travail}}, c’est tout."},{"cue":"Pay at the cash desk please.","t":"{{Réglez à la}} caisse, s’il vous plaît."},{"cue":"No sir, we don’t have his/her passport.","t":"Non, monsieur, {{nous n’avons pas son passeport}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"d’accord","en":"OK, agreed","zh":"好的;同意","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ accord","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’accord. Je fais mon travail, c’est tout.","exEn":"OK. I’m doing my job, that’s all.","src":1},{"fr":"je fais mon travail","en":"I’m doing my job","zh":"我在做我的工作","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’accord. Je fais mon travail, c’est tout.","exEn":"OK. I’m doing my job, that’s all.","src":1},{"fr":"c’est tout","en":"that’s all","zh":"就这些","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’accord. Je fais mon travail, c’est tout.","exEn":"OK. I’m doing my job, that’s all.","src":1},{"fr":"nous l’avons","en":"we have it","zh":"我们有","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui monsieur, nous l’avons.","exEn":"Yes, sir, we have it.","src":3},{"fr":"ah bon ?","en":"oh really?","zh":"是吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah bon ? Mais est-ce que vous avez son passeport ?","exEn":"Oh really? But do you have his passport?","src":6},{"fr":"un passeport","en":"a passport","zh":"护照","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"same as English (passport)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah bon ? Mais est-ce que vous avez son passeport ?","exEn":"Oh really? But do you have his passport?","src":6},{"fr":"bien sûr","en":"of course","zh":"当然","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr. Nous avons même sa photo.","exEn":"Of course. We even have his photo.","src":7},{"fr":"même","en":"even; same","zh":"甚至;相同的","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr. Nous avons même sa photo.","exEn":"Of course. We even have his photo.","src":7},{"fr":"une photo","en":"a photo","zh":"照片","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"same as English (photo)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr. Nous avons même sa photo.","exEn":"Of course. We even have his photo.","src":7},{"fr":"très bien","en":"very well, very good","zh":"很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Très bien. Alors je vous fais la carte. Voilà.","exEn":"Very well. So I’ll do you the card. Here it is.","src":8},{"fr":"alors","en":"so, then","zh":"那么;于是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Très bien. Alors je vous fais la carte. Voilà.","exEn":"Very well. So I’ll do you the card. Here it is.","src":8},{"fr":"réglez à la caisse","en":"pay at the cash desk","zh":"请到收银台付款","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Réglez à la caisse, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Pay at the cash desk, please.","src":9},{"fr":"une caisse","en":"a cash desk, checkout","zh":"收银台","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Réglez à la caisse, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Pay at the cash desk, please.","src":9},{"fr":"zut !","en":"damn!, heck!","zh":"糟糕!","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Zut ! Je ne trouve pas mon portefeuille !","exEn":"Damn! I can’t find my wallet!","src":10},{"fr":"je ne trouve pas mon portefeuille","en":"I can’t find my wallet","zh":"我找不到我的钱包","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Zut ! Je ne trouve pas mon portefeuille !","exEn":"Damn! I can’t find my wallet!","src":10}],"uncertain":["dialogue 1: the French first sentence is split between a captioned box and the numbered line in the OCR; read as 'D’accord. Je fais mon travail, c’est tout.'"],"pdfPage":47,"gaps":[[1.66,3.72],[4.82,7.44],[30.06,31.18],[35.9,38.94],[39.94,42],[43.1,45.72],[48.06,52.36],[54.4,58.34],[60.02,63.84],[65.14,68.6],[69.98,73.4],[75.78,80.28],[82.96,86.94],[90.22,95.34],[96.7,100.26],[103,108.98],[111.84,113.72],[115.18,118.88],[120.54,124],[125.82,129.62],[132.04,135.6],[136.14,137.14],[137.7,142.24]],"dur":142.2},{"n":11,"kind":"lesson","title":"Au marché (Première partie)","titleEn":"At the market (First part)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi allons-nous au marché ? Il est trop cher.","en":"Why are we going to the market? It’s too expensive.","lit":"why go we to-the market"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Parce que tous les placards sont vides !","en":"Because all the cupboards are empty!","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons besoin de tout : de fruits et de légumes,","en":"We need everything: fruit and vegetables,","lit":"we have need of everything: of fruit and of vegetables"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"mais aussi de pain, de fromage, de beurre, de crème, de jambon, et ...","en":"but also bread, cheese, butter, cream, ham, and ...","lit":"but also of bread, of cheese, of butter, of cream, of ham"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon, je comprends. Il n’y a rien à manger. Allons-y.","en":"OK, I understand. There’s nothing to eat. Let’s go.","lit":"good, I understand. It there not has nothing to eat. Let’s-go-there"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour madame. Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous ?","en":"Good morning madam. What can I do for you?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vais acheter une livre de cerises, un kilo de pommes,","en":"I am going to buy a pound of cherries, a kilo of apples,","lit":"I go buy a pound of cherries, a kilo of apples"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"trois cents grammes de champignons, s’ils ne sont pas trop chers,","en":"300 grams of mushrooms, if they’re not too expensive,","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"quelques bananes, deux kilos de pommes de terre et un peu de lait frais.","en":"a few bananas, two kilos of potatoes and a little fresh milk.","lit":"two kilos of apples of earth"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"pourquoi (literally: “for-what”): why. Parce que, because, is always spelled with two words."},{"ref":"2","text":"When used as an adjective, tout, all / every, has to “agree” in number and gender with its noun: tout le fromage, all the cheese (masculine singular); toute la journée, all day (feminine singular); tous les fruits, all the fruit (masculine plural); toutes les pommes, all the apples (feminine plural). It is also a pronoun (line 3): J’ai besoin de tout, I need everything."},{"ref":"3","text":"avoir besoin de, to need. Note that in very formal English, we use exactly the same construction: to have need of."},{"ref":"4","text":"une livre, a half-kilo (500 g, almost equivalent to one pound). To order by weight, you can also ask for un kilo or the number of grams: huit cents grammes, 800 grams. (Une livre is also used for a pound sterling.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"quelque, some / a few. It takes an “s” in the plural. Quelques champignons, a few mushrooms. Quelqu’un, somebody; quelque chose, something. Quelque part, somewhere."},{"ref":"","text":"There may seem to be lots of detail, but don’t try to remember everything. We always repeat important points several times in different situations so that you become familiar with them automatically. Just relax and enjoy yourself! And remember, we don’t try to explain everything at once."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Nous allons au marché parce que nous avons besoin de fruits et de légumes.","a":"We are going to the market because we need fruit and vegetables."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous aujourd’hui ?","a":"What can I do for you today?"},{"q":"Elle va prendre trois kilos de pommes de terre.","a":"She’s going to take three kilos of potatoes."},{"q":"Il n’y a rien à manger à la maison.","a":"There’s nothing to eat at home."},{"q":"J’ai besoin de quelques pommes et d’une livre de cerises.","a":"I need a few apples and a pound of cherries."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I need a little milk and a few apples, please.","t":"J’{{ai besoin}} d’{{un peu de}} lait et {{quelques}} pommes, s’il vous plaît."},{"cue":"He wants all the cheese, all the mushrooms and all the apples.","t":"Il veut {{tout}} le fromage, {{tous}} les champignons et {{toutes}} les pommes."},{"cue":"All the cupboards are empty.","t":"{{Tous}} les placards sont {{vides}}."},{"cue":"We’re going to take a few kilos of cherries.","t":"{{Nous allons}} prendre {{quelques}} kilos de cerises."},{"cue":"You’re not going to the market? Why?","t":"{{Vous n’allez pas au}} marché ? {{Pourquoi}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"pourquoi allons-nous au marché ?","en":"why are we going to the market?","zh":"我们为什么去市场?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “poor-kwa”: a poor quack asks WHY it has to go to market","ex":"Pourquoi allons-nous au marché ?","exEn":"Why are we going to the market?","src":1},{"fr":"un placard","en":"a cupboard","zh":"橱柜","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"placard in English = a sign; un placard = a cupboard","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Parce que tous les placards sont vides !","exEn":"Because all the cupboards are empty!","src":2},{"fr":"nous avons besoin de tout","en":"we need everything","zh":"我们什么都需要","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons besoin de tout : de fruits et de légumes,","exEn":"We need everything: fruit and vegetables,","src":3},{"fr":"mais aussi de pain","en":"but also bread","zh":"但是还有面包","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais aussi de pain, de fromage, de beurre, de crème, de jambon, et ...","exEn":"but also bread, cheese, butter, cream, ham, and ...","src":4},{"fr":"il n’y a rien à manger","en":"there is nothing to eat","zh":"没有什么可吃的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il n’y a rien à manger.","exEn":"There’s nothing to eat.","src":5},{"fr":"allons-y","en":"let’s go","zh":"我们走吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Allons-y.","exEn":"Let’s go.","src":5},{"fr":"qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous ?","en":"what can I do for you?","zh":"我能为您做什么?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous ?","exEn":"What can I do for you?","src":6},{"fr":"je vais acheter une livre de cerises","en":"I’m going to buy a pound of cherries","zh":"我要买一磅樱桃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"une livre = about 500 g (a half-kilo)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vais acheter une livre de cerises, un kilo de pommes,","exEn":"I am going to buy a pound of cherries, a kilo of apples,","src":7},{"fr":"quelques bananes","en":"a few bananas","zh":"几根香蕉","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ banana","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"quelques bananes, deux kilos de pommes de terre et un peu de lait frais.","exEn":"a few bananas, two kilos of potatoes and a little fresh milk.","src":9},{"fr":"deux kilos de pommes de terre","en":"two kilos of potatoes","zh":"两公斤土豆","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"quelques bananes, deux kilos de pommes de terre et un peu de lait frais.","exEn":"a few bananas, two kilos of potatoes and a little fresh milk.","src":9},{"fr":"un peu de lait frais","en":"a little fresh milk","zh":"一点鲜牛奶","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"quelques bananes, deux kilos de pommes de terre et un peu de lait frais.","exEn":"a few bananas, two kilos of potatoes and a little fresh milk.","src":9},{"fr":"il est trop cher","en":"it is too expensive","zh":"太贵了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Pourquoi allons-nous au marché ? Il est trop cher.","exEn":"Why are we going to the market? It’s too expensive.","src":1},{"fr":"je comprends","en":"I understand","zh":"我明白","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ comprehend","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon, je comprends.","exEn":"OK, I understand.","src":5}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 5: English cue in the scan reads “You’re going to the market?”, but the French line and answer key (Vous n’allez pas au … Pourquoi) mean “You’re not going to the market? Why?”; cue corrected to match the French."],"pdfPage":51,"gaps":[[1.5,3.66],[5.72,8.6],[18.98,20],[31.02,32.14],[35.36,36.76],[46.16,47.32],[52.82,55.68],[56.66,58.8],[60.86,63.76],[66.46,71.38],[73.32,77.74],[81.82,88.48],[95.44,104.34],[107.74,113.3],[116.52,121.86],[126.66,133.28],[137.06,143],[148.48,157.74],[160.24,162.46],[166.48,171.34],[173.02,176.44],[179.18,183.84],[186.14,190.64],[193.62,198.32]],"dur":198.3},{"n":12,"kind":"lesson","title":"Au marché (Deuxième partie)","titleEn":"At the market (Second part)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Et avec ceci ? Vous voulez des fraises, peut-être ?","en":"And with that? Do you want some strawberries, perhaps?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que je peux les goûter ?","en":"Can I taste them?","lit":"is it that I can them taste"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Allez-y ! N’hésitez pas. Toutes mes fraises sont bio !","en":"Go ahead. Don’t hesitate. All my strawberries are organic.","lit":"go-there"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Mm, c’est vrai, elles sont vraiment délicieuses. Elles sont à combien ?","en":"Mm, it’s true, they’re really delicious. How much are they?","lit":"they are at how-much"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Le prix ? Elles ne sont pas chères. Seulement quinze euros le kilo.","en":"The price? They’re not expensive. Only 15 euros a kilo.","lit":"only 15 euros the kilo"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, c’est trop cher. J’ai tout ce que je veux pour l’instant.","en":"No, it’s too expensive. I have all that I want for the time being.","lit":"I have everything it that I want for the instant"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors, ça vous fait vingt-six euros soixante s’il vous plaît.","en":"Right, that makes 26 euros 60 please.","lit":"then, that you makes"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Voilà trente.","en":"Here is 30.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Et voici votre monnaie. Bonne journée.","en":"And here’s your change. Have a good day.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"des in this context is an indefinite article. It is often translated by some in English: Je veux des fraises, I want some strawberries, but, depending on the context, it can also be omitted (I want strawberries). We now know all three indefinite articles: un (masculine), une (feminine) and des (plural for both genders)."},{"ref":"2","text":"Allez-y is a useful expression whenever you want someone to go ahead and do something, try something out, walk in front of you, etc. It is translated by Go ahead, etc. It can also be used in the first person plural: Allons-y, Let’s go; and with the familiar form of you, which we will learn in due course."},{"ref":"3","text":"bio is a shortened form of biologique (literally “biological”). It means organic. Because the adjective is idiomatic, it does not agree with the noun."},{"ref":"4","text":"combien (de), how much / how many?; Combien de kilos ?, How many kilos?; Combien de beurre ?, How much butter? The phrase Ils / Elles sont à combien ? is a common, idiomatic way of asking the price of something."},{"ref":"5","text":"As we have seen, cher – like all adjectives – agrees in number and gender with its noun. Masculine adjectives ending in “er” are changed to feminine by putting a grave accent over the “è” and adding a final, silent “e”: le beurre n’est pas cher, la livre de fraises est chère; the butter isn’t expensive; the pound of strawberries is expensive. When used as an adverb, cher always takes the masculine form: C’est très cher, It’s very expensive. As we progress, we’ll learn more about how the masculine gender takes precedence over the feminine."},{"ref":"6","text":"tout is used as an indefinite pronoun, meaning everything. See lesson 11 note 3."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ça vous fait dix euros vingt, madame.","a":"That makes ten euros twenty, madam."},{"q":"Est-ce que tous vos fruits sont bio ?","a":"Is all your fruit organic?"},{"q":"Les cerises sont vraiment délicieuses. Elles sont à combien ?","a":"The cherries are really delicious. How much are they?"},{"q":"N’hésitez pas. Allez-y !","a":"Don’t hesitate. Go ahead!"},{"q":"Voilà trente euros. – Et voici votre monnaie.","a":"Here’s thirty euros. – And here’s your change."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The market is very expensive but the strawberries aren’t expensive.","t":"{{Le marché}} est très {{cher}} mais {{les fraises ne sont pas chères}}."},{"cue":"I have all that I want for the time being.","t":"J’ai {{tout ce que je veux}} pour l’instant."},{"cue":"I want some apples, some strawberries and some mushrooms, please.","t":"Je {{veux des}} pommes, {{des}} fraises et {{des}} champignons, {{s’il}} vous plaît."},{"cue":"I think that it’s noon. – Let’s go! We’re late.","t":"Je {{pense}} qu’il est midi. – {{Allons-y}} ! Nous {{sommes}} en retard."},{"cue":"How many kilos of potatoes do you want? – How much are they?","t":"{{Combien de}} kilos de pommes de terre voulez-vous ? – Elles {{sont à combien}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"et avec ceci ?","en":"anything else? (lit. and with this?)","zh":"还要别的吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et avec ceci ? Vous voulez des fraises, peut-être ?","exEn":"And with that? Do you want some strawberries, perhaps?","src":1},{"fr":"des fraises","en":"(some) strawberries","zh":"草莓","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"sounds like “frez”: picture a FRESH strawberry freezing in a fridge","ex":"Vous voulez des fraises, peut-être ?","exEn":"Do you want some strawberries, perhaps?","src":1},{"fr":"est-ce que je peux les goûter ?","en":"can I taste them?","zh":"我可以尝一尝吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que je peux les goûter ?","exEn":"Can I taste them?","src":2},{"fr":"allez-y !","en":"go ahead!","zh":"请吧!/ 去吧!","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allez-y ! N’hésitez pas.","exEn":"Go ahead. Don’t hesitate.","src":3},{"fr":"n’hésitez pas","en":"don’t hesitate","zh":"不要犹豫","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ hesitate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allez-y ! N’hésitez pas.","exEn":"Go ahead. Don’t hesitate.","src":3},{"fr":"bio","en":"organic","zh":"有机的","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"≈ bio- (short for biologique); invariable","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Toutes mes fraises sont bio !","exEn":"All my strawberries are organic.","src":3},{"fr":"c’est vrai","en":"it’s true","zh":"是真的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mm, c’est vrai, elles sont vraiment délicieuses.","exEn":"Mm, it’s true, they’re really delicious.","src":4},{"fr":"elles sont vraiment délicieuses","en":"they are really delicious","zh":"它们真的很好吃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"délicieux ≈ delicious","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mm, c’est vrai, elles sont vraiment délicieuses.","exEn":"Mm, it’s true, they’re really delicious.","src":4},{"fr":"elles sont à combien ?","en":"how much are they?","zh":"它们多少钱?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Elles sont à combien ?","exEn":"How much are they?","src":4},{"fr":"seulement quinze euros le kilo","en":"only 15 euros a kilo","zh":"每公斤只要十五欧元","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Seulement quinze euros le kilo.","exEn":"Only 15 euros a kilo.","src":5},{"fr":"c’est trop cher","en":"it’s too expensive","zh":"太贵了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, c’est trop cher.","exEn":"No, it’s too expensive.","src":6},{"fr":"j’ai tout ce que je veux","en":"I have everything I want","zh":"我想要的都有了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai tout ce que je veux pour l’instant.","exEn":"I have all that I want for the time being.","src":6},{"fr":"pour l’instant","en":"for the time being","zh":"暂时,目前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ instant","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai tout ce que je veux pour l’instant.","exEn":"I have all that I want for the time being.","src":6},{"fr":"ça vous fait vingt-six euros soixante","en":"that comes to 26 euros 60","zh":"一共二十六欧元六十分","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Alors, ça vous fait vingt-six euros soixante s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Right, that makes 26 euros 60 please.","src":7},{"fr":"la monnaie","en":"the change (money back)","zh":"找零","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ monetary","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et voici votre monnaie.","exEn":"And here’s your change.","src":9},{"fr":"bonne journée","en":"have a good day","zh":"祝您今天愉快","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Et voici votre monnaie. Bonne journée.","exEn":"And here’s your change. Have a good day.","src":9}],"uncertain":["dialogue 3: final punctuation of “Allez-y !” and “bio !” read as bullets in the scan; exclamation marks assumed (consistent with exercise 1 item 4)."],"pdfPage":53,"gaps":[[1.4,3.6],[5.6,8.34],[27.52,28.52],[47.08,50.42],[51.32,53.54],[55.54,58.28],[61.54,67.06],[68.6,72.08],[76.3,83],[89.64,98.32],[103.32,110.26],[114.7,121.22],[124.86,130.16],[131.24,133.92],[136.66,141.4],[143.32,145.46],[147.54,150.52],[152.42,155.7],[160.16,165.92],[167.42,170.86],[173.96,180.9]],"dur":180.9},{"n":13,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un cadeau","titleEn":"A gift","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Louise, Lucas ! Venez voir mon cadeau d’anniversaire !","en":"Louise, Lucas! Come and see my birthday present!","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que c’est ?","en":"What is it?","lit":"what is that it is"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous ne savez pas ?","en":"Don’t you know?","lit":"you not know"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un nouveau téléphone.","en":"It’s a new phone.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Il fait absolument tout.","en":"It does absolutely everything.","lit":"it makes absolutely all"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous pouvez envoyer des messages, commander un repas,","en":"You can send messages, order a meal,","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"payer vos courses, écouter la musique, regarder des vidéos ...","en":"pay for your shopping, listen to music, watch videos ...","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"et vous pouvez même parler à quelqu’un.","en":"and you can even speak to someone.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que je peux l’essayer ?","en":"Can I try it?","lit":"is it that I can it-try"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, malheureusement. Ce n’est pas possible.","en":"No, unfortunately. It’s not possible.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Il ne marche pas ?","en":"Isn’t it working?","lit":"it not walks not"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne connais pas le code secret.","en":"I don’t know the secret passcode.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Qu’est-ce means What is this/that. But in everyday French, we usually add ... que c’est (literally ... that it is), so we say qu’est-ce que c’est ... ? There is no difference in meaning, and the answer starts with C’est ..."},{"ref":"2","text":"We know that adjectives usually come after the nouns they qualify: un téléphone portable, a mobile phone. But some are placed before the noun. That is the case with nouveau (feminine: nouvelle), new. We will learn more about these exceptions later on. (Incidentally, the French word for a phone with computer functions is ... un smartphone!)"},{"ref":"3","text":"pouvez and peux are respectively the second person plural and the first person singular (also the second person singular) of the verb pouvoir, can / to be able to. The first person plural is pouvons. Notice that the verb which follows pouvoir is in the infinitive: Vous pouvez essayer mon téléphone, you can try my phone."},{"ref":"4","text":"Some English verbs that are followed by a preposition (pay for, listen to, look at, etc.) are expressed with a single verb in French: écouter la musique, listen to music; payer un repas, to pay for a meal. There are no hard-and-fast rules on adding these prepositions, so the best way to remember any differences between French and English is to write them down."},{"ref":"5","text":"marcher means to walk (it’s the origin of our word march), but the verb is used colloquially in the sense of to function, to work. Cette montre ne marche pas, This watch isn’t working. The expression Ça marche ! is used idiomatically to indicate that the person being addressed understands or agrees. – Deux cafés s’il vous plaît. – Ça marche ! – Two coffees please. – Coming up!"},{"ref":"6","text":"There are two ways of saying I know in French. We say je sais when referring to a fact or some other abstract thing, and je connais for a person or a place. Je connais sa mère, I know his/her mother. Nous ne connaissons pas cette ville, We don’t know this town. Il sait beaucoup de choses, He knows many things. Both verbs are irregular, and the infinitives are savoir and connaître. We’ll see them again later on."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Qu’est-ce que c’est ? – C’est mon nouveau téléphone.","a":"What is it? – It’s my new phone."},{"q":"Est-ce que je peux parler à Louise, s’il vous plaît ?","a":"Can I speak to Louise, please?"},{"q":"Malheureusement, ma montre ne marche pas.","a":"Unfortunately, my watch isn’t working."},{"q":"Je ne connais pas l’adresse de son bureau.","a":"I don’t know the address of his office."},{"q":"Vous pouvez l’essayer si vous voulez.","a":"You can try it if you like."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We can listen to music, order a meal and send a message.","t":"Nous pouvons {{écouter de la musique}}, commander {{un repas}} et {{envoyer un}} message."},{"cue":"Can I try it please? – No, it doesn’t work.","t":"{{Est-ce que je peux}} l’essayer, {{s’il}} vous {{plaît}} ? – Non, il {{ne marche pas}}."},{"cue":"What’s that? – Don’t you know?","t":"Qu’{{est-ce que c’est}} ? – Vous {{ne savez pas}} ?"},{"cue":"My new smartphone does absolutely everything.","t":"{{Mon nouveau}} smartphone {{fait}} absolument {{tout}}."},{"cue":"Can I send a message to Louise? – I don’t know her address.","t":"Est-ce que {{je peux envoyer}} un message {{à}} Louise ? – Je {{ne connais pas son}} adresse."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un cadeau d’anniversaire","en":"a birthday present","zh":"生日礼物","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ka-DOH”: a birthday present that goes “ka-doh!” when you open it","ex":"Venez voir mon cadeau d’anniversaire !","exEn":"Come and see my birthday present!","src":1},{"fr":"venez voir mon cadeau","en":"come and see my present","zh":"来看看我的礼物","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Venez voir mon cadeau d’anniversaire !","exEn":"Come and see my birthday present!","src":1},{"fr":"qu’est-ce que c’est ?","en":"what is it?","zh":"这是什么?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’est-ce que c’est ?","exEn":"What is it?","src":2},{"fr":"vous ne savez pas ?","en":"don’t you know?","zh":"您不知道吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous ne savez pas ?","exEn":"Don’t you know?","src":3},{"fr":"un nouveau téléphone","en":"a new phone","zh":"一部新手机","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ telephone","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un nouveau téléphone.","exEn":"It’s a new phone.","src":4},{"fr":"il fait absolument tout","en":"it does absolutely everything","zh":"它什么都能做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"absolument ≈ absolutely","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il fait absolument tout.","exEn":"It does absolutely everything.","src":5},{"fr":"vous pouvez envoyer des messages","en":"you can send messages","zh":"您可以发信息","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"message ≈ message","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez envoyer des messages, commander un repas,","exEn":"You can send messages, order a meal,","src":6},{"fr":"commander un repas","en":"to order a meal","zh":"点餐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ command (= order)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez envoyer des messages, commander un repas,","exEn":"You can send messages, order a meal,","src":6},{"fr":"payer vos courses","en":"to pay for your shopping","zh":"付购物的钱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"payer vos courses, écouter la musique, regarder des vidéos ...","exEn":"pay for your shopping, listen to music, watch videos ...","src":7},{"fr":"écouter la musique","en":"to listen to music","zh":"听音乐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"musique ≈ music","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"payer vos courses, écouter la musique, regarder des vidéos ...","exEn":"pay for your shopping, listen to music, watch videos ...","src":7},{"fr":"regarder des vidéos","en":"to watch videos","zh":"看视频","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"vidéo ≈ video","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"payer vos courses, écouter la musique, regarder des vidéos ...","exEn":"pay for your shopping, listen to music, watch videos ...","src":7},{"fr":"vous pouvez même parler à quelqu’un","en":"you can even speak to someone","zh":"您甚至可以和人说话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et vous pouvez même parler à quelqu’un.","exEn":"and you can even speak to someone.","src":8},{"fr":"est-ce que je peux l’essayer ?","en":"can I try it?","zh":"我可以试一试吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que je peux l’essayer ?","exEn":"Can I try it?","src":9},{"fr":"malheureusement","en":"unfortunately","zh":"不幸地,很遗憾","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ unhappily (-ment = -ly)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, malheureusement. Ce n’est pas possible.","exEn":"No, unfortunately. It’s not possible.","src":10},{"fr":"ce n’est pas possible","en":"it’s not possible","zh":"这不可能","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"possible ≈ possible","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, malheureusement. Ce n’est pas possible.","exEn":"No, unfortunately. It’s not possible.","src":10},{"fr":"il ne marche pas","en":"it isn’t working","zh":"它坏了/不能用","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"marcher = to work (a device), not only “to march/walk”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ne marche pas ?","exEn":"Isn’t it working?","src":11}],"uncertain":["note 3: the scan says “peux” is the second person singular; it is the first person singular (je peux), also tu peux; wording adjusted."],"pdfPage":57,"gaps":[[1.9,3.62],[4.38,7.14],[36.1,39.32],[40.76,42.46],[43.24,45.98],[49.1,54.08],[54.74,57.96],[58.74,61.18],[62.44,65.4],[66.64,69.84],[72.34,77.72],[81.8,88.44],[89.96,93.44],[94.62,97.9],[100.04,104.38],[105.24,108],[109.42,114.78],[116.68,118.8],[121.9,125.7],[127.98,131.46],[133.44,136.58],[139.2,142.44],[144.12,149.06]],"dur":149.1},{"n":14,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"Dialogue de révision","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour. Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous ?","en":"Good morning, what can I do for you?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que votre père est à la maison aujourd’hui ?","en":"Is your father at home today?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, il est au bureau toute la journée.","en":"No, he’s at the office all day.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Il rentre avant sept heures normalement.","en":"He comes home before seven o’clock normally.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Est-ce que vous avez besoin de son adresse ?","en":"Do you need his address?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Non merci, je la connais.","en":"No thanks, I know it.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Voici son cadeau d’anniversaire.","en":"Here’s his birthday present.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que c’est ?","en":"What is it?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est un téléphone qui fait absolument tout.","en":"It’s a phone that does absolutely everything.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Très bien. Merci beaucoup !","en":"Very good. Thanks very much!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Possessive adjectives","text":"Look at these examples:\nmy father, mon père    my mother, ma mère\nmy brother, mon frère    my sister, ma sœur\nThe possession word (adjective) changes if the singular noun is masculine or feminine. For the plural, the word is mes [may] irrespective of the gender: mes parents, mes enfants.\nFor your, our and their, we have one singular form and one plural form, irrespective of gender:\nvotre carte, your card; votre père; vos parents;\nnotre bureau; nos amis;\nleur adresse; leur maison; leurs photos. (The plural s is silent unless the noun begins with a vowel: leurs amis.)\nBe careful: the rule for his and her (its) is slightly different from English, where we say his if the possessor is masculine and her if feminine. In French however, the words for his/her/its are adjectives and must therefore agree with the noun they qualify.\nson bureau can mean: his or her office; we use son because bureau is masculine. In the same way sa mère can be his or her mother. The plural is ses: ses enfants, his or her children.\nThere is one exception to these rules of agreement: if the qualified noun begins with a vowel, we use the masculine adjective to make pronunciation easier. For example, *sa amie would be difficult to pronounce (rather like “a apple”); so we say son amie and mon amie, allowing the context to show whether the friend is male or female."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Verbs ending in -er","text":"In the last set of lessons we have seen many different verbs with infinitives ending in -er. This is the largest category of French verbs, with literally thousands of “members.” Let’s look at an example:\naimer; to like / to love: j’aime, il/elle aime, nous aimons, vous aimez, ils/elles aiment (the final -ent is silent).\nAs we mentioned in the first lesson, there is also an informal mode of address, tu, which we’ll see later on.\nThis is the pattern for all -er verbs. Do you remember these?\nacheter, to buy\ncommander, to order\ndemander, to ask\nécouter, to listen to\nenvoyer, to send\nessayer, to try\nhabiter, to live\nmarcher, to walk, to function (idiomatic)\nparler, to speak\npayer, to pay (for)\nregarder, to look at\nrentrer, to return\ntéléphoner, to phone\nNote that the past participle, seen briefly in lesson 9, is formed simply by dropping the final r of the infinitive and putting an accent on the final e: trouver → trouvé; aimer → aimé; payer → payé, etc. The pronunciation does not change."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 faire and pouvoir","text":"Now that you know aller and être, here are two more important irregular verbs:\n– Faire translates two English verbs: to do and to make:\nje fais, il/elle fait, nous faisons, vous faites, ils/elles font\nQu’est-ce que nous pouvons faire pour vous ?, What can we do for you?\nJe fais quelque chose à manger, I’m making something to eat.\n– Pouvoir also has more than one meaning, though all of them are connected to the idea of ability or possibility: can, to be able, may, etc.\nje peux, il/elle peut, nous pouvons, vous pouvez, ils/elles peuvent:\nEst-ce que vous pouvez prendre des cerises au marché s’il vous plaît ?, Can you get some cherries at the market please?\nVous ne pouvez pas faire ça, You’re not allowed to do that.\n(The noun le pouvoir means power.)"},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 C’est: It is","text":"We have seen two ways of saying it is: c’est and il est (or elle est with a feminine noun). There is a difference between the two.\nWe use c’est to explain what a thing (or who a person) is:\nQu’est-ce que c’est ? – C’est une photo de famille.\nWhat is it? – It’s a family photo.\nRegardez, c’est mon cadeau, Look, that’s my present.\nor to say:\nIt’s me – C’est moi\nIt’s you – C’est vous\nor if the noun is qualified by an adjective:\nC’est un bon ami, He’s a good friend.\nThe plural is ce sont.\nCe sont mes bons amis, They are my good friends.\nIl/elle est is used to refer to a noun just mentioned:\nOù est le beurre ? – Il est sur la table.\nWhere is the butter? – It’s on the table.\nOù est votre femme ? – Elle est au marché.\nWhere is your wife? – She’s at the market.\nWe also use il est if we are talking about the weather:\nIl pleut, It’s raining. Il fait beau, It’s fine.\nor telling the time:\nIl est huit heures et demie, It’s half past eight."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5.1 Adjectives: masculine and feminine forms","text":"As a rule, we form the feminine singular of an adjective by adding an e to the masculine:\npetit (small) → petite\nblond (blond / fair-haired) → blonde\nfort (strong) → forte\nIt’s important to remember that some masculine adjectives end in -e and do not therefore change when used with a feminine noun: un placard vide, an empty cupboard; un téléphone (portable), a mobile phone.\nBut if the masculine ending is -é, we do add an e:\noccupé (busy) → occupée\nfatigué (tired) → fatiguée\nMasculine adjectives ending in -er form their feminine by replacing the ending with -ère:\ndernier (last) → dernière\npremier (first) → première\nAnother group of masculine adjectives ending in -eux replace the x by -se in the feminine:\nheureux (happy) → heureuse\ndélicieux (delicious) → délicieuse\nThere are a couple more rules and a few irregular forms, which we will learn later on."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5.2 Adjectives: before or after?","text":"We know that adjectives are generally placed after the noun they qualify but that some are placed before it. These are the words that describe beauty, age, goodness and size. Think BAGS:\nBeauty: beau (beautiful), joli (pretty)\nAge: vieux (old), jeune (young), nouveau (new)\nGoodness: bon (good), mauvais (bad), gentil (kind / nice), vrai (true)\nSize: grand (big), petit (small), haut (high / tall), gros (fat)\nSome of these adjectives are new to you but don’t worry: we’ll see them all again in the near future. There are a few more that do not fall into these categories; we will point them out as we go along."},{"ref":"","text":"So much for the rules! In time you will “feel” the correct usage, so don’t try to do too much at once. But from now on, when you memorize the gender of a noun, try adding a simple adjective (grand or petit, for instance) and making it agree: this will help you to acquire the right reflexes very quickly."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"votre père","en":"your father","zh":"您的父亲","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"family","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que votre père est à la maison aujourd’hui ?","exEn":"Is your father at home today?","src":2},{"fr":"il est au bureau toute la journée","en":"he is at the office all day","zh":"他整天都在办公室","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, il est au bureau toute la journée.","exEn":"No, he’s at the office all day.","src":3},{"fr":"il rentre avant sept heures","en":"he gets home before seven o’clock","zh":"他七点以前回家","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il rentre avant sept heures normalement.","exEn":"He comes home before seven o’clock normally.","src":4},{"fr":"avoir besoin de son adresse","en":"to need his/her address","zh":"需要他/她的地址","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"adresse ≈ address","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que vous avez besoin de son adresse ?","exEn":"Do you need his address?","src":5},{"fr":"je la connais","en":"I know it/her","zh":"我认识她/知道它","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non merci, je la connais.","exEn":"No thanks, I know it.","src":6},{"fr":"un téléphone qui fait absolument tout","en":"a phone that does absolutely everything","zh":"一部无所不能的手机","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un téléphone qui fait absolument tout.","exEn":"It’s a phone that does absolutely everything.","src":9},{"fr":"c’est un bon ami","en":"he’s a good friend","zh":"他是个好朋友","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un bon ami.","exEn":"He’s a good friend.","src":0},{"fr":"il fait beau","en":"the weather is nice","zh":"天气很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Il fait beau.","exEn":"It’s fine.","src":0},{"fr":"je fais quelque chose à manger","en":"I’m making something to eat","zh":"我在做点吃的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je fais quelque chose à manger.","exEn":"I’m making something to eat.","src":0},{"fr":"vous ne pouvez pas faire ça","en":"you’re not allowed to do that","zh":"您不能这样做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous ne pouvez pas faire ça.","exEn":"You’re not allowed to do that.","src":0}],"uncertain":["notes 5.1: scan reads “dernier (fast)”; corrected to “last” (dernier = last).","dialogue 8: scan reads “Qu’est que c’est” (word dropped); restored as “Qu’est-ce que c’est ?”."],"pdfPage":61,"gaps":[[2.1,4.22],[5.62,8.4],[17.28,18.34],[20,21.04],[34.66,38.88]],"dur":38.9},{"n":15,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un tour dans Paris (Première partie)","titleEn":"A tour of Paris (First part)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour mesdames, bonjour mesdemoiselles, bonjour messieurs.","en":"Good morning ladies, good morning young ladies, good morning gentlemen.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon nom est Michèle Laroche et je suis votre guide ce matin.","en":"My name is Michèle Laroche and I am your guide this morning.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous traversons actuellement la place de la Concorde.","en":"We are crossing Concorde Square at present.","lit":"we cross currently the place of the Concorde"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Devant vous, vous pouvez voir le Louvre et, à droite, la tour Eiffel.","en":"In front of you, you can see the Louvre and, on the right, the Eiffel Tower.","lit":"to right"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"S’il vous plaît madame, où sont ... ?","en":"Please, madam, where are ...","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Un instant. Laissez-moi terminer, s’il vous plaît.","en":"Just a minute. Let me finish please.","lit":"one instant"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais c’est très important : je ne peux pas attendre cet après-midi.","en":"But it’s very important: I can’t wait until this afternoon.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Eh bien, qu’est-ce que vous voulez savoir ?","en":"Well, what do you want to know?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Où sont les toilettes ?","en":"Where is the toilet?","lit":"where are the toilets"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know that possessive adjectives have singular, plural, masculine and feminine forms (lesson 7). So, when used in a compound word – monsieur (mon sieur), madame (ma dame), mademoiselle (ma demoiselle), respectively “my sire,” “my lady,” “my damsel” – they follow the usual agreement rule: monsieur → messieurs, etc. A casual way of greeting a mixed-sex group of people – often heard in cafés and bistros – is Messieurs-dames, bonjour, Good morning ladies and gentlemen. The phrase should be recognised but not used."},{"ref":"2","text":"A number of French words are identical or very similar to English words but have different meanings. Actuel means current, and the adverbial form actuellement means currently, NOT actually."},{"ref":"3","text":"Remember that any verb which follows pouvoir, to be able to, is always in the infinitive."},{"ref":"4","text":"Learning the gender of French nouns is vital, especially since some have two genders, each with a different meaning. Here’s an example: le tour (masculine) is a tour whereas la tour means a tower (monument, tower block, etc.). Another example is la livre (see lesson 11 note 4), a pound (sterling, weight), and un livre, a book. Only a handful of these dual-gender nouns are used in everyday conversation, but they have to be learned! We’ll look at them in greater detail at the end of the course."},{"ref":"5","text":"We have already seen two ways to attract attention when asking a question: Pardon (lesson 4) and Excusez-moi (lesson 5). You can also use s’il vous plaît, please, in exactly the same way: S’il vous plaît, où est le métro ? Please, can you tell me where the metro is?"},{"ref":"6","text":"Some nouns are singular in English but plural in French, or vice versa. A good example is les toilettes, the toilet. We’ll learn more of these words as we progress."},{"ref":"","text":"La place (or Place) de la Concorde is a large public square at the bottom of les Champs-Élysées (literally “Elysian Fields”), one of the main thoroughfares in Paris. It is close to le musée du Louvre (Louvre museum), one of the world’s greatest art galleries that houses treasures such as La Joconde (the Mona Lisa), la Vénus de Milo (Venus of Milo) and La liberté guidant le peuple (“Liberty Guiding the People”, the painting by Eugène Delacroix that symbolises the French republic)."},{"ref":"","text":"Don’t forget to read the numbers at the beginning of each lesson and at the top of each page."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Messieurs, mesdames, bonjour.","a":"Good morning ladies and gentlemen."},{"q":"Son nom est Michel Laroche et il est votre guide.","a":"His name is Michel Laroche and he is your guide."},{"q":"Je ne peux pas attendre ce soir.","a":"I can’t wait until this evening."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que vous voulez savoir ?","a":"What do you want to know?"},{"q":"S’il vous plaît, où sont les toilettes ?","a":"Excuse me, where is the toilet?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You can see the Louvre in front of you.","t":"Vous pouvez {{voir}} le Louvre {{devant vous}}."},{"cue":"We are currently crossing Concorde Square.","t":"{{Nous traversons actuellement}} la place de la Concorde."},{"cue":"You can’t wait until this afternoon.","t":"Vous {{ne pouvez pas attendre}} cet après-midi."},{"cue":"Let me finish please.","t":"{{Laissez-moi}} terminer, {{s’il vous plaît}}."},{"cue":"It’s not very important.","t":"Ce {{n’est pas}} très {{important}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je suis votre guide ce matin","en":"I am your guide this morning","zh":"今天上午我是您的导游","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"guide ≈ guide","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon nom est Michèle Laroche et je suis votre guide ce matin.","exEn":"My name is Michèle Laroche and I am your guide this morning.","src":2},{"fr":"nous traversons actuellement la place","en":"we are currently crossing the square","zh":"我们目前正在穿过广场","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"actuellement = currently, NOT actually","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous traversons actuellement la place de la Concorde.","exEn":"We are crossing Concorde Square at present.","src":3},{"fr":"devant vous","en":"in front of you","zh":"在您面前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Devant vous, vous pouvez voir le Louvre et, à droite, la tour Eiffel.","exEn":"In front of you, you can see the Louvre and, on the right, the Eiffel Tower.","src":4},{"fr":"vous pouvez voir","en":"you can see","zh":"您可以看到","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Devant vous, vous pouvez voir le Louvre et, à droite, la tour Eiffel.","exEn":"In front of you, you can see the Louvre and, on the right, the Eiffel Tower.","src":4},{"fr":"à droite","en":"on the right","zh":"在右边","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Devant vous, vous pouvez voir le Louvre et, à droite, la tour Eiffel.","exEn":"In front of you, you can see the Louvre and, on the right, the Eiffel Tower.","src":4},{"fr":"la tour (f)","en":"the tower","zh":"塔","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ tower","ff":"le tour (m) = the tour/trip; la tour (f) = the tower","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Devant vous, vous pouvez voir le Louvre et, à droite, la tour Eiffel.","exEn":"In front of you, you can see the Louvre and, on the right, the Eiffel Tower.","src":4},{"fr":"s’il vous plaît madame, où sont ... ?","en":"excuse me madam, where are ...?","zh":"女士,请问……在哪里?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"S’il vous plaît madame, où sont ... ?","exEn":"Please, madam, where are ...","src":5},{"fr":"un instant","en":"just a minute","zh":"稍等一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ instant","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un instant. Laissez-moi terminer, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Just a minute. Let me finish please.","src":6},{"fr":"laissez-moi terminer","en":"let me finish","zh":"让我说完","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"terminer ≈ terminate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un instant. Laissez-moi terminer, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"Just a minute. Let me finish please.","src":6},{"fr":"c’est très important","en":"it’s very important","zh":"这非常重要","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"important ≈ important","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais c’est très important : je ne peux pas attendre cet après-midi.","exEn":"But it’s very important: I can’t wait until this afternoon.","src":7},{"fr":"je ne peux pas attendre","en":"I can’t wait","zh":"我不能等","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais c’est très important : je ne peux pas attendre cet après-midi.","exEn":"But it’s very important: I can’t wait until this afternoon.","src":7},{"fr":"cet après-midi","en":"this afternoon","zh":"今天下午","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Mais c’est très important : je ne peux pas attendre cet après-midi.","exEn":"But it’s very important: I can’t wait until this afternoon.","src":7},{"fr":"eh bien, qu’est-ce que vous voulez savoir ?","en":"well, what do you want to know?","zh":"好吧,您想知道什么?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Eh bien, qu’est-ce que vous voulez savoir ?","exEn":"Well, what do you want to know?","src":8},{"fr":"où sont les toilettes ?","en":"where is the toilet?","zh":"洗手间在哪里?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"les toilettes is plural in French","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Où sont les toilettes ?","exEn":"Where is the toilet?","src":9}],"uncertain":["exercise 2 item 3: the English cue in the scan says “this evening” but the French line (cet après-midi) and dialogue 7 say afternoon; cue changed to afternoon to match the French.","dialogue 5: “où sont ... ?” is deliberately interrupted in the book."],"pdfPage":67,"gaps":[[1.4,3.72],[6.88,9.84],[44.16,47.48],[49.86,51.76],[54.72,57.62],[60.42,63.4],[65.46,68.38],[69.88,73.2],[75.42,78.96]],"dur":79},{"n":16,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un tour dans Paris (Deuxième partie)","titleEn":"A tour of Paris (Second part)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Comparé à d’autres capitales, comme Moscou ou Pékin, Paris n’est pas très grand.","en":"Compared to some other capitals, like Moscow or Beijing, Paris is not very big.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais la région parisienne, c’est-à-dire la ville et sa banlieue, est grande comme Londres.","en":"But the Parisian region, that is to say the city and its suburbs, is as big as London.","lit":"that it is to say"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"On l’appelle la Ville lumière, avec ses grands monuments et ses longues avenues,","en":"It is called The City of Light, with its great monuments and long avenues,","lit":"one it calls"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"ses grandes églises et ses vieilles rues.","en":"its grand churches and its old streets.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Regardez là-bas : ce bâtiment superbe est l’Opéra,","en":"Look over there: this superb building is the Opera,","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"construit par le grand architecte Charles Garnier.","en":"built by the great architect Charles Garnier.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Derrière, vous avez les grands magasins, et, en face, on voit le Café de la Paix.","en":"Behind, you have the major department stores, and, opposite, you can see the Café de la Paix.","lit":"big shops; one sees; Peace Café"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un bon endroit pour une photo, et la lumière est bonne aujourd’hui.","en":"It’s a good spot for a photo, and the light is good today.","lit":"place"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous pouvez faire beaucoup de belles images.","en":"You can take lots of beautiful pictures.","lit":"make"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Si vous voulez, vous pouvez descendre du bus ici, à côté de l’arrêt.","en":"If you want, you can get off the bus here, next to the stop.","lit":"descend from the bus"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(6) When the masculine adjective grand is followed by a word beginning with a vowel, for example architecte, the final -d is pronounced as a t when making the liaison: grand architecte sounds like “grahn-tar-shee-tekt”."},{"ref":"1","text":"on is the third person singular impersonal pronoun. It has the same meaning as one in English but is much less formal. In practical use, it can replace either nous (we) – On est en retard, We’re late – or the passive voice: On appelle Paris “la Ville lumière”, Paris is called the City of Light. We’ll see this quirky pronoun in greater detail later on."},{"ref":"2","text":"The adjective grand, big, can be used to mean great, eminent, etc. Like all adjectives, it agrees in gender and number with its noun: un grand architecte (a great architect), une grande ville (a big OR great city), ses grands magasins (its big stores), ses grandes avenues (its major avenues). See Culture Note below. Also, the adjective vieille is the irregular feminine form of vieux (old)."},{"ref":"3","text":"The second person (the vous form, for one person or several) is used without a pronoun as an imperative: Regardez ! Look! The negative form is regular: Ne traversez pas ! Don’t cross!"},{"ref":"4","text":"Just because an adjective ends in e does not necessarily mean that it’s in the feminine form. Adjectives such as superbe, superb, facile, easy, malade, ill, and moderne, modern, are both masculine and feminine. See lesson 21."},{"ref":"5","text":"Likewise, not all nouns ending in -e are feminine. This is particularly true with words relating to professions, such as un architecte, an architect. However, the feminine form can be formed simply by changing the article: une architecte. This is a recent development in the French language, which we’ll discuss later in the course."},{"ref":"6","text":"Some masculine adjectives ending in a consonant are changed to the feminine by doubling the last letter and adding an e: bon → bonne (good), ancien → ancienne (ancient, old). To make the plural, simply add an -s to either form. See lesson 14."},{"ref":"","text":"Literally, un grand magasin means a big shop, but the term usually refers to a department store. In Paris, several such stores can be found along les Grands Boulevards near the Opera. Another famous landmark in that neighbourhood is le Café de la Paix, which opened in the mid-19th century and was frequented by famous composers, writers and intellectuals during La Belle Époque, the period of peace and prosperity that preceded the first world war.\nLutèce (Lutetia in Latin) is the name of the Gallo-Roman city that eventually became Paris. Today the toponym can be found in the names of cafés, restaurants, hotels and other businesses all over France."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"L’église est derrière ce grand bâtiment.","a":"The church is behind this big building."},{"q":"Lutèce est l’ancien nom de Paris.","a":"Lutetia is the old name of Paris."},{"q":"Le café est en face de l’Opéra.","a":"The café is opposite the Opera."},{"q":"Vous pouvez descendre ici si vous voulez.","a":"You can get off here if you want."},{"q":"Je pense que l’arrêt de bus est là-bas.","a":"I think that the bus stop is over there."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Wait! Don’t cross the road now!","t":"{{Attendez}} ! {{Ne traversez pas}} la rue maintenant !"},{"cue":"There are big stores, great churches and a big monument next to the Louvre Square.","t":"Il y a de {{grands magasins}}, de {{grandes églises}} et {{un grand}} monument {{à côté de}} la place du Louvre."},{"cue":"It’s not a good spot for a photo.","t":"Ce {{n’est pas}} un {{bon endroit}} pour une photo."},{"cue":"Because the light is not very good.","t":"Parce que la lumière {{n’est pas très bonne}}."},{"cue":"Don’t get off here. It’s not the right station.","t":"{{Ne descendez pas}} ici. Ce {{n’est pas la bonne}} station."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"comparé à d’autres capitales","en":"compared to other capitals","zh":"与其他首都相比","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"comparé ≈ compared; capitale ≈ capital","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comparé à d’autres capitales, comme Moscou ou Pékin, Paris n’est pas très grand.","exEn":"Compared to some other capitals, like Moscow or Beijing, Paris is not very big.","src":1},{"fr":"c’est-à-dire","en":"that is to say","zh":"也就是说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais la région parisienne, c’est-à-dire la ville et sa banlieue, est grande comme Londres.","exEn":"But the Parisian region, that is to say the city and its suburbs, is as big as London.","src":2},{"fr":"la banlieue","en":"the suburbs","zh":"郊区","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"sounds like “bon-lyuh”: a ‘bone’ lying in the suburbs outside the city","ex":"Mais la région parisienne, c’est-à-dire la ville et sa banlieue, est grande comme Londres.","exEn":"But the Parisian region, that is to say the city and its suburbs, is as big as London.","src":2},{"fr":"on l’appelle la Ville lumière","en":"it is called the City of Light","zh":"人们称它为光之城","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On l’appelle la Ville lumière, avec ses grands monuments et ses longues avenues,","exEn":"It is called The City of Light, with its great monuments and long avenues,","src":3},{"fr":"de grandes églises","en":"grand churches","zh":"宏伟的教堂","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"église ≈ ecclesiastical","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"ses grandes églises et ses vieilles rues.","exEn":"its grand churches and its old streets.","src":4},{"fr":"ses vieilles rues","en":"its old streets","zh":"它古老的街道","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"ses grandes églises et ses vieilles rues.","exEn":"its grand churches and its old streets.","src":4},{"fr":"regardez là-bas","en":"look over there","zh":"请看那边","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Regardez là-bas : ce bâtiment superbe est l’Opéra,","exEn":"Look over there: this superb building is the Opera,","src":5},{"fr":"un bâtiment superbe","en":"a superb building","zh":"一栋壮观的建筑","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"superbe ≈ superb","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Regardez là-bas : ce bâtiment superbe est l’Opéra,","exEn":"Look over there: this superb building is the Opera,","src":5},{"fr":"en face","en":"opposite","zh":"在对面","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Derrière, vous avez les grands magasins, et, en face, on voit le Café de la Paix.","exEn":"Behind, you have the major department stores, and, opposite, you can see the Café de la Paix.","src":7},{"fr":"un bon endroit pour une photo","en":"a good spot for a photo","zh":"拍照的好地方","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"photo ≈ photo","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un bon endroit pour une photo, et la lumière est bonne aujourd’hui.","exEn":"It’s a good spot for a photo, and the light is good today.","src":8},{"fr":"la lumière est bonne","en":"the light is good","zh":"光线很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un bon endroit pour une photo, et la lumière est bonne aujourd’hui.","exEn":"It’s a good spot for a photo, and the light is good today.","src":8},{"fr":"beaucoup de belles images","en":"lots of beautiful pictures","zh":"许多漂亮的照片","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez faire beaucoup de belles images.","exEn":"You can take lots of beautiful pictures.","src":9},{"fr":"vous pouvez descendre du bus ici","en":"you can get off the bus here","zh":"您可以在这里下车","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Si vous voulez, vous pouvez descendre du bus ici, à côté de l’arrêt.","exEn":"If you want, you can get off the bus here, next to the stop.","src":10},{"fr":"à côté de l’arrêt","en":"next to the stop","zh":"在车站旁边","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Si vous voulez, vous pouvez descendre du bus ici, à côté de l’arrêt.","exEn":"If you want, you can get off the bus here, next to the stop.","src":10}],"uncertain":["exercise 2 item 3: scan answer key reads “n’est pas – un bon endroit” while the printed line seems to show “un” before the second gap; kept “un” printed and gap = “bon endroit”.","note 3: scan says “second person (singular and plural)”; reworded as the vous form."],"pdfPage":71,"gaps":[[1.8,3.78],[6.64,9.46],[15.62,16.88],[23.68,24.9],[42.14,43.18],[53.98,55.02],[62.78,65.94],[67.8,69.72],[72.4,75.52],[78.46,81.48],[83.34,86.34],[88.7,91.78],[94.88,98.8]],"dur":98.8},{"n":17,"kind":"lesson","title":"Quels sont vos projets ?","titleEn":"What are your plans?","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Dites-moi, quels sont vos projets pour demain ?","en":"Tell me, what are your plans for tomorrow?","lit":"tell-me, which are your projects for tomorrow"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y a une nouvelle exposition à La Piscine que j’ai très envie de voir.","en":"There is a new exhibition at La Piscine that I really want to see.","lit":"(the Swimming Pool) that I have very envy of to see"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous voulez venir avec moi ?","en":"Do you want to come with me?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne suis pas sûr : je dois aller chez le médecin cet après-midi.","en":"I’m not sure: I have to go to the doctor this afternoon.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"À quelle heure est votre rendez-vous ?","en":"What time is your appointment?","lit":"at what hour"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Assez tard : vers seize ou dix-sept heures. Ça ne laisse pas beaucoup de temps.","en":"Quite late: around four or five o’clock. That does not leave a lot of time.","lit":"towards 16 or 17 hours"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais on peut y aller aujourd’hui si vous voulez.","en":"But we can go today if you want.","lit":"one can there go"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi pas ? Quelle heure est-il maintenant ?","en":"Why not? What time is it now?","lit":"what hour is-it"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est presque treize heures : très précisément, midi quarante-cinq.","en":"It’s almost 1 o’clock: very precisely, 12.45.","lit":"nearly 13 hours"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Donc nous avons le temps : l’expo ferme à dix-huit heures.","en":"So we have time: the exhibition closes at 6 o’clock.","lit":"the time; 18 hours"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Quelles sont les choses à voir ? Et quel est le titre de cette exposition ?","en":"What are the things to see? And what is the title of this exhibition?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"« Le Sujet, l’objet et l’eau ». Très profond, mais pas très clair !","en":"“The Subject, the Object and Water.” Very deep, but not very clear!","lit":"profound"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"In hyphenated compounds, a liaison is pronounced if the second word begins with a vowel or a silent h. Thus est-il (8) is [ay-teel]. (The liaison is also pronounced in treize heures because both the final e of the first word and the initial h of the second are silent: [trayz-uhr].)"},{"ref":"1","text":"The interrogative adjective quel is the equivalent of what or which. Quel est votre nom ? What is your name? Like all adjectives, it agrees in number and gender with the associated noun (quelle heure, quelles choses). See lesson 21."},{"ref":"2","text":"Several English words ending in -ect come from French (via Latin). To find a French equivalent, simply remove the c. Thus a project → un projet, a subject → un sujet, an object → un objet."},{"ref":"3","text":"il y a (literally “it there has”) is an invariable construction that basically means there is or there are: Il y a une exposition, There is an exhibition; Il y a deux choses, There are two things. (Be careful: when used with an expression of time, il y a means ago: Il y a trois jours, Three days ago. We’ll see this again when we learn the past tense.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"avoir envie de (the root of our word envy) means to want to, to feel like: J’ai envie de voir mes cadeaux, I want to see my presents. Envie is invariable but the verb avoir has to be conjugated (see lesson 27). The construction is similar in form to avoir besoin de (see lesson 11 note 3)."},{"ref":"5","text":"devoir means both must and to have to (French makes no distinction between the two notions). The verb is irregular: je dois, tu dois, il/elle doit (all three pronounced [dwa]), nous devons ([duh-vo]), vous devez ([duh-vay]), ils doivent ([dwav]). Any verb immediately following devoir is in the infinitive: il doit aller ..., he must/has to go ..."},{"ref":"6","text":"The 24-hour clock is used not only in official or formal settings; it is frequently found in ordinary conversation, too. See lesson 21."},{"ref":"7","text":"Many words, especially long ones, are abbreviated in everyday usage (the process is known as apocope). Rather than une exposition or le petit-déjeuner, for example, it’s common to hear une expo and le petit-déj. Although you should avoid using such familiar terms, it’s important to recognise and understand them."},{"ref":"","text":"Contrary to what many visitors to France may think (along with many Parisians), Paris is not the only city with cultural treasures aplenty. Of course, the likes of Marseille, Lyon and Toulouse have many spectacular museums and monuments, but so do smaller towns like Lens, which houses an outpost of the Louvre, and Metz, home to part of the Paris-based Pompidou Arts Centre. One of the more original attractions can be found in the former textile capital of Roubaix: La Piscine is an art museum housed in what was once an Art Deco swimming bath, hence the name. Anyone spending time in France should wander off the beaten track to discover its rich and diverse cultural heritage, or le patrimoine culturel."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Le rendez-vous est à dix-sept heures.","a":"The appointment is at five o’clock."},{"q":"Ça laisse beaucoup de temps.","a":"That leaves a lot of time."},{"q":"On peut aller à l’expo demain.","a":"We can go to the exhibition tomorrow."},{"q":"Vous voulez venir avec moi ? – Pourquoi pas ?","a":"Do you want to come with me? – Why not?"},{"q":"Désolée, mais nous n’avons pas le temps.","a":"Sorry, but we don’t have the time."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"What is your plan for tomorrow?","t":"{{Quel est}} votre projet pour {{demain}} ?"},{"cue":"I really want to see that new exhibition on water.","t":"{{J’ai très envie de}} voir {{cette}} nouvelle expo sur {{l’eau}}."},{"cue":"Monique has to go to the doctor’s this afternoon.","t":"Monique {{doit aller chez}} le médecin {{cet après-midi}}."},{"cue":"We can go now if you want.","t":"{{On peut aller}} maintenant {{si vous voulez}}."},{"cue":"What time is it? It’s midday.","t":"{{Quelle heure est-il}} ? Il est {{midi}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"dites-moi","en":"tell me","zh":"请告诉我","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dites-moi, quels sont vos projets pour demain ?","exEn":"Tell me, what are your plans for tomorrow?","src":1},{"fr":"quels sont vos projets ?","en":"what are your plans?","zh":"您有什么计划?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"projet ≈ project (remove the c: project → projet)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dites-moi, quels sont vos projets pour demain ?","exEn":"Tell me, what are your plans for tomorrow?","src":1},{"fr":"j’ai très envie de voir","en":"I really want to see","zh":"我很想看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"envie ≈ envy","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il y a une nouvelle exposition à La Piscine que j’ai très envie de voir.","exEn":"There is a new exhibition at La Piscine that I really want to see.","src":2},{"fr":"vous voulez venir avec moi ?","en":"do you want to come with me?","zh":"您想和我一起来吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous voulez venir avec moi ?","exEn":"Do you want to come with me?","src":3},{"fr":"je dois aller chez le médecin","en":"I have to go to the doctor’s","zh":"我得去看医生","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne suis pas sûr : je dois aller chez le médecin cet après-midi.","exEn":"I’m not sure: I have to go to the doctor this afternoon.","src":4},{"fr":"un rendez-vous","en":"an appointment","zh":"约会,预约","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À quelle heure est votre rendez-vous ?","exEn":"What time is your appointment?","src":5},{"fr":"à quelle heure est votre rendez-vous ?","en":"what time is your appointment?","zh":"您的约会是几点?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"À quelle heure est votre rendez-vous ?","exEn":"What time is your appointment?","src":5},{"fr":"assez tard","en":"quite late","zh":"相当晚","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Assez tard : vers seize ou dix-sept heures.","exEn":"Quite late: around four or five o’clock.","src":6},{"fr":"ça ne laisse pas beaucoup de temps","en":"that doesn’t leave much time","zh":"这样时间就不多了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça ne laisse pas beaucoup de temps.","exEn":"That does not leave a lot of time.","src":6},{"fr":"pourquoi pas ?","en":"why not?","zh":"为什么不呢?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pourquoi pas ? Quelle heure est-il maintenant ?","exEn":"Why not? What time is it now?","src":8},{"fr":"quelle heure est-il ?","en":"what time is it?","zh":"现在几点?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Quelle heure est-il maintenant ?","exEn":"What time is it now?","src":8},{"fr":"il est presque treize heures","en":"it’s almost one o’clock (13:00)","zh":"差不多十三点了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est presque treize heures : très précisément, midi quarante-cinq.","exEn":"It’s almost 1 o’clock: very precisely, 12.45.","src":9},{"fr":"nous avons le temps","en":"we have time","zh":"我们有时间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Donc nous avons le temps : l’expo ferme à dix-huit heures.","exEn":"So we have time: the exhibition closes at 6 o’clock.","src":10},{"fr":"très profond, mais pas très clair","en":"very deep, but not very clear","zh":"很深奥,但不太清楚","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"profond ≈ profound; clair ≈ clear","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Très profond, mais pas très clair !","exEn":"Very deep, but not very clear!","src":12}],"uncertain":["dialogue 12: the guillemets around the exhibition title are my rendering of the book’s quotation marks."],"pdfPage":75,"gaps":[[1.94,4.02],[4.94,7.88],[10.6,11.7],[32.08,33.18],[35.72,36.76],[56.34,59.84],[62.24,64],[65.86,68.94],[70.26,73.06],[75.04,78.14],[80.78,84.24],[86.56,91.24]],"dur":91.3},{"n":18,"kind":"lesson","title":"Prenons rendez-vous avec le banquier","titleEn":"Let’s make an appointment with the banker","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour Monsieur Castille. Nous voulons prendre un rendez-vous avec vous.","en":"Good morning Mr Castille. We want to make an appointment with you.","lit":"take an appointment"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que vous pouvez venir le mardi vingt-deux mars ?","en":"Can you come on Tuesday the twenty-second of March?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis libre entre neuf heures et onze heures, ou plus tard dans la journée.","en":"I’m free between nine o’clock and eleven o’clock, or later in the day.","lit":"more late"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, nous sommes pris toute la semaine, du lundi vingt et un au vendredi vingt-cinq.","en":"No, we are taken the whole week, from Monday the twenty-first to Friday the twenty-fifth.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Avez-vous une autre date ? En mai, par exemple ?","en":"Do you have another date? In May, for example?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je regrette : mai n’est pas possible, à cause des ponts.","en":"I’m sorry: May is not possible, because of the long weekends.","lit":"I regret; bridges"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne peux pas vous voir avant le mercredi trente juin. Mon emploi du temps est très chargé.","en":"I can’t see you before Wednesday the thirtieth of June. My schedule is very full.","lit":"my job of time is very loaded"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais nous, nous sommes en voyage en juin et juillet.","en":"But we are travelling in June and July.","lit":"we, we are on journey"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, je suis en vacances au mois d’août. Je prends l’avion le jeudi douze pour aller aux États-Unis.","en":"I’m on holiday in the month of August. I am flying on Thursday the twelfth to the United States.","lit":"I take the plane"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Septembre est hors de question à cause de la rentrée scolaire.","en":"September is out of the question because of the new academic year.","lit":"the return to school"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Et en octobre j’ai déjà trois conférences et un stage de formation. Novembre ?","en":"And in October I already have three lectures and a training course. November?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce n’est pas un bon mois : mes parents passent trois semaines chez nous.","en":"It’s not a good month: my parents are spending three weeks with us.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Attendez : je peux vous proposer le samedi vingt-cinq décembre ou le dimanche vingt-six.","en":"Wait: I can offer you Saturday the twenty-fifth of December or Sunday the twenty-sixth.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon agenda est enfin vide !","en":"My diary is empty at last!","lit":"finally"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"The -ille termination is pronounced [-eeyy], more or less as in the English word tea. For example, the old Parisian fortress La Bastille is pronounced [la basteeyy]."},{"ref":"1","text":"la journée, the day, refers to the period between dawn and dusk: toute la journée means the whole day (or daytime) rather than a specific day or moment in that day, for which we use le jour: le jour de mon mariage, the day of my wedding. A common, and useful, leave-taking expression is Bonne journée, Have a good day."},{"ref":"2","text":"By now you are familiar with the partitive articles du, de la and des, reviewed in lesson 21. But de also means from (for example Il est de Nîmes, lesson 3). When talking about a period of time (from ... to/until) we say de ... à (de neuf heures à dix heures, from 9 until 10 o’clock) but if the following noun is preceded by le, la or les, the partitive becomes du, de la or des, respectively. For example, le lundi et le jeudi → du lundi au jeudi."},{"ref":"3","text":"regretter, to regret, can be used in conversation when refusing something politely: Je regrette mais je ne peux pas venir demain, I’m sorry but I can’t come tomorrow."},{"ref":"4","text":"chargé, the adjective and past participle of charger, to load, is one of les faux-amis, or false friends, discussed in lesson 7. Used in connection with a timetable or programme, it means to have a heavy schedule or to be very busy (i.e. “laden” with work)."},{"ref":"5","text":"un voyage has a broader scope than its English cognate, referring to travel in general (rather than just a sea voyage). It basically means a journey or a trip – Le voyage en bus est très long, The journey by bus is very long – but can also refer to travel in general: une agence de voyages, a travel agency. Be careful not to confuse une journée (see note 1) with a journey."},{"ref":"6","text":"Other examples of faux-amis (see note 4) include un stage, a course or an internship, and la formation, training or continuing education; and un agenda (line 14), a schedule. It’s important to remember these false cognates because they can have very different meanings from one language to the other."},{"ref":"","text":"One of the pleasures of learning a language is discovering how common words or terms are used in culture-specific ways. Take, for example, le pont, meaning a bridge (le pont de la Concorde, for example). The word also refers to the French custom of taking an extra day off work if a public holiday falls, say, on a Tuesday or Thursday, in order to make a long weekend. The expression in this case is faire le pont (“to make the bridge”). Another culture-specific term is la rentrée, which comes from the verb rentrer, to go back in, and generally refers to the start of the new academic year (called officially la rentrée scolaire), when students go back to school. But the term is used more broadly to mean any post-holiday “return,” such as la rentrée politique, when parliamentarians return after the summer recess, or la rentrée littéraire, the period in September/October when new books are published ahead of the literary award season."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Nous sommes libres toute la journée, de huit heures à dix-huit heures.","a":"We are free all day, from eight o’clock until six o’clock."},{"q":"Est-ce que nous pouvons prendre rendez-vous avec vous aujourd’hui ?","a":"Can we make an appointment with you today?"},{"q":"Je suis en voyage au mois d’août. Je vais aux États-Unis.","a":"I’m travelling in August. I’m going to the United States."},{"q":"Avez-vous une autre date à proposer ? – Je regrette, mais non.","a":"Do you have another date to offer? – I’m sorry but I don’t."},{"q":"Au revoir et bonne journée. – Bonne journée à vous aussi.","a":"Goodbye and have a good day. – You have a good day too."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I am already taken this Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, and my wife is taken on Thursday and Friday.","t":"Je suis {{déjà}} pris ce {{lundi}}, {{mardi}} et {{mercredi}}, et ma femme est prise {{jeudi}} et {{vendredi}}."},{"cue":"We are not free from Saturday the third of July until Sunday the eighth of August.","t":"Nous ne sommes pas libres {{du samedi}} trois {{juillet au dimanche}} huit {{août}}."},{"cue":"It’s not possible because of the training course in May.","t":"Ce n’est pas possible {{à cause du stage}} de formation en {{mai}}."},{"cue":"Wait: he can offer us the twenty-fifth of December. – I’m not free.","t":"{{Attendez}} : il peut nous proposer {{le vingt-cinq décembre}}. – Je {{ne suis pas libre}}."},{"cue":"September is out of the question: it’s the start of the academic year.","t":"Septembre {{est hors de question}} : c’est {{la rentrée scolaire}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"prendre rendez-vous avec vous","en":"to make an appointment with you","zh":"和您预约","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous voulons prendre un rendez-vous avec vous.","exEn":"We want to make an appointment with you.","src":1},{"fr":"je suis libre","en":"I’m free (available)","zh":"我有空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"libre ≈ liberty","ff":"libre = free (available); gratuit = free (no charge)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis libre entre neuf heures et onze heures, ou plus tard dans la journée.","exEn":"I’m free between nine o’clock and eleven o’clock, or later in the day.","src":3},{"fr":"nous sommes pris toute la semaine","en":"we are booked all week","zh":"我们整个星期都没空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, nous sommes pris toute la semaine, du lundi vingt et un au vendredi vingt-cinq.","exEn":"No, we are taken the whole week, from Monday the twenty-first to Friday the twenty-fifth.","src":4},{"fr":"avez-vous une autre date ?","en":"do you have another date?","zh":"您还有别的日期吗?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"date ≈ date","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avez-vous une autre date ? En mai, par exemple ?","exEn":"Do you have another date? In May, for example?","src":5},{"fr":"par exemple","en":"for example","zh":"例如","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ for example","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avez-vous une autre date ? En mai, par exemple ?","exEn":"Do you have another date? In May, for example?","src":5},{"fr":"je regrette","en":"I’m sorry (I regret)","zh":"很抱歉","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ regret","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je regrette : mai n’est pas possible, à cause des ponts.","exEn":"I’m sorry: May is not possible, because of the long weekends.","src":6},{"fr":"à cause de","en":"because of","zh":"由于","pos":"prep","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je regrette : mai n’est pas possible, à cause des ponts.","exEn":"I’m sorry: May is not possible, because of the long weekends.","src":6},{"fr":"mon emploi du temps est très chargé","en":"my schedule is very full","zh":"我的日程排得很满","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"chargé here = busy/full, not “loaded”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon emploi du temps est très chargé.","exEn":"My schedule is very full.","src":7},{"fr":"nous sommes en voyage","en":"we are travelling (on a trip)","zh":"我们在出差/旅行","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"voyage ≈ voyage","ff":"un voyage = a trip/journey in general; une journée = a day","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais nous, nous sommes en voyage en juin et juillet.","exEn":"But we are travelling in June and July.","src":8},{"fr":"je suis en vacances au mois d’août","en":"I’m on holiday in August","zh":"八月我在休假","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"months","kw":"","ex":"Moi, je suis en vacances au mois d’août.","exEn":"I’m on holiday in the month of August.","src":9},{"fr":"c’est hors de question","en":"it’s out of the question","zh":"绝对不可能","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Septembre est hors de question à cause de la rentrée scolaire.","exEn":"September is out of the question because of the new academic year.","src":10},{"fr":"la rentrée scolaire","en":"the start of the school year","zh":"新学年开学","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Septembre est hors de question à cause de la rentrée scolaire.","exEn":"September is out of the question because of the new academic year.","src":10},{"fr":"j’ai déjà trois conférences","en":"I already have three lectures","zh":"我已经有三场讲座了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"conférence = lecture/conference, not “conference” only","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et en octobre j’ai déjà trois conférences et un stage de formation.","exEn":"And in October I already have three lectures and a training course.","src":11},{"fr":"je peux vous proposer","en":"I can offer you","zh":"我可以向您提议","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"proposer ≈ propose","ff":"proposer = to offer/suggest","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Attendez : je peux vous proposer le samedi vingt-cinq décembre ou le dimanche vingt-six.","exEn":"Wait: I can offer you Saturday the twenty-fifth of December or Sunday the twenty-sixth.","src":13},{"fr":"mon agenda est enfin vide","en":"my diary is empty at last","zh":"我的日程终于空了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un agenda = a diary/schedule, not an “agenda” of a meeting","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon agenda est enfin vide !","exEn":"My diary is empty at last!","src":14}],"uncertain":["note 6: the scan gives “line 13” for agenda; the word is in sentence 14, so corrected."],"pdfPage":79,"gaps":[[1.88,3.9],[5.84,8.84],[16.94,17.98],[22.1,23.18],[30.06,31.22],[76.66,80.08],[82.42,84.64],[89.04,91.94],[94.48,97.68],[101.54,104.52],[108.52,111.42],[115.8,120.14]],"dur":120.2},{"n":19,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un bel endroit pour une fête","titleEn":"A great place for a party","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Hôtel Sacré Cœur. Comment est-ce que je peux vous aider ?","en":"Hôtel Sacré Cœur. How can I help you?","lit":"sacred heart"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"J’organise une fête pour mon anniversaire et je veux louer une salle.","en":"I am organising a party for my birthday and I want to rent a room.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Pour combien de personnes ?","en":"For how many people?","lit":"for how-many of people"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Une trentaine : il y a la famille, la belle-famille, des copains.","en":"About thirty: there is the family, the family-in-law, some mates.","lit":"the beautiful-family"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Je veux quelque chose de beau et pas trop cher.","en":"I want something lovely and not too expensive.","lit":"handsome"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est pour quand, cette fête ? Pas à la fin de l’année, j’espère ?","en":"When is this party? Not at the end of the year, I hope?","lit":"it’s for when"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous fermons entre Noël et le Nouvel An.","en":"We close between Christmas and the New Year.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas de problème : c’est pour la soirée du trente juin, jusqu’à minuit.","en":"No problem: it’s for the evening of the thirtieth of June, until midnight.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Voyons : j’ai une belle salle pour une cinquantaine de personnes.","en":"Let’s see: I have a lovely room for about fifty people.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un bel endroit pour une fête.","en":"It’s a great place for a party.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça coûte combien ? Je n’ai pas un gros budget.","en":"How much does it cost? I don’t have a big budget.","lit":"that costs how-much"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors j’ai quelque chose de plus petit et moins cher :","en":"Then I have something smaller and less expensive:","lit":"of more small and less dear"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"le placard à balais : c’est gratuit !","en":"the broom cupboard: it’s free!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"The ligature œ (so-called because o and e are bound together) is pronounced in three different ways, depending on the following letter. In the case of cœur and œuf it is pronounced [uh]: [kuhr], [uhf]. We will see the other pronunciations later."},{"ref":"1","text":"The adjective beau has several meanings: handsome, beautiful, lovely, great, etc. It is also irregular: the feminine form is belle and the masculine plural is beaux. But because French does not permit a hiatus (a break between the terminal vowel sound of one word and the initial vowel of the following word), for example un *beau endroit, there is another masculine form: bel: un bel endroit. The same rule applies to nouveau/nouvelle (new): nouvel an (new year). Three other adjectives have this additional masculine form. (We have seen a similar construction with ce/cette: ce matin, cette femme, but cet après-midi.)\nNote that une belle-famille (with a hyphen) has nothing to do with beauty: it means a family-in-law, usually consisting of une belle-mère (mother-in-law) and un beau-père (father-in-law)."},{"ref":"2","text":"combien is a useful word (lesson 12 note 4). Remember that it translates both how much and how many. One useful phrase is Ça coûte combien ? or Combien est-ce que ça coûte ? How much does it cost? (from the verb coûter, to cost)."},{"ref":"3","text":"The termination -aine can be added to a cardinal number, generally a multiple of ten or five (dix, quinze, vingt, etc.), to indicate an approximation (une dizaine, about 10, une quinzaine, about 15, une vingtaine, about 20). Note that une douzaine can mean about twelve OR, when buying eggs, a dozen (une douzaine d’œufs)."},{"ref":"4","text":"The difference between the two words for the evening, le soir and la soirée, is the same as for le matin and la matinée. But une soirée can also mean an event held in the evening, such as une soirée dansante, a dinner dance."},{"ref":"5","text":"The adjective free has two translations: the first, seen in the previous lesson, is libre (i.e. no commitments or engagements: J’ai une soirée libre, I have a free evening); the second is gratuit (absence of cost: Le bus est gratuit ce soir, The bus is free this evening). Moreover, gratuit is the root of the English word a gratuity, something given freely as a gift."},{"ref":"","text":"La Basilique du Sacré Cœur (Basilica of the Sacred Heart) is a famous late nineteenth-century church located atop la Butte Montmartre, the hill overlooking Paris. Built of white stone with a flamboyant Byzantine-style dome, it is sometimes called la grosse meringue (“the great meringue”) by its detractors but it is nevertheless one of the most recognisable buildings in the capital."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Bruno organise une grande fête pour son anniversaire en mai.","a":"Bruno is organising a big party for his birthday in May."},{"q":"C’est pour quand ? – Pour le Nouvel An.","a":"When’s it for? – For the New Year."},{"q":"Nous voulons quelque chose de beau et cher.","a":"We want something lovely and expensive."},{"q":"Voyons voir si j’ai quelque chose de plus grand.","a":"Let’s see if I have something bigger."},{"q":"Combien est-ce que ça coûte ? – C’est gratuit.","a":"How much does it cost? – It’s free."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"It’s a lovely place. The buildings are handsome and the rooms are pretty.","t":"C’est {{un bel}} endroit. Les bâtiments {{sont beaux}} et les salles {{sont belles}}."},{"cue":"There are about thirty cafés in that town and about fifty restaurants.","t":"Il y a {{une trentaine de}} cafés dans cette ville et {{une cinquantaine de}} restaurants."},{"cue":"The room is free [i.e. unoccupied] until midnight but it is not free [of charge].","t":"La salle est {{libre jusqu’à}} minuit mais {{elle n’est pas gratuite}}."},{"cue":"Do you have something smaller and less expensive?","t":"Avez-vous {{quelque chose}} de {{plus petit}} et {{moins cher}} ?"},{"cue":"I’m taken this morning. – No problem. I can come this afternoon.","t":"Je suis pris {{ce matin}}. – {{Pas de problème}}. Je peux venir {{cet après-midi}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"comment est-ce que je peux vous aider ?","en":"how can I help you?","zh":"我能怎么帮您?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Hôtel Sacré Cœur. Comment est-ce que je peux vous aider ?","exEn":"Hôtel Sacré Cœur. How can I help you?","src":1},{"fr":"j’organise une fête","en":"I’m organising a party","zh":"我在筹办一场聚会","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"organiser ≈ organise","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’organise une fête pour mon anniversaire et je veux louer une salle.","exEn":"I am organising a party for my birthday and I want to rent a room.","src":2},{"fr":"je veux louer une salle","en":"I want to rent a room (hall)","zh":"我想租一个大厅/房间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"louer = to rent (not “to praise”)","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “loo-ay”: a ‘loo’ you rent for the day","ex":"J’organise une fête pour mon anniversaire et je veux louer une salle.","exEn":"I am organising a party for my birthday and I want to rent a room.","src":2},{"fr":"pour combien de personnes ?","en":"for how many people?","zh":"多少人?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour combien de personnes ?","exEn":"For how many people?","src":3},{"fr":"une trentaine de personnes","en":"about thirty people","zh":"大约三十人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Une trentaine : il y a la famille, la belle-famille, des copains.","exEn":"About thirty: there is the family, the family-in-law, some mates.","src":4},{"fr":"un copain","en":"a mate, a friend","zh":"朋友,伙伴(口语)","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Une trentaine : il y a la famille, la belle-famille, des copains.","exEn":"About thirty: there is the family, the family-in-law, some mates.","src":4},{"fr":"quelque chose de beau et pas trop cher","en":"something lovely and not too expensive","zh":"漂亮又不太贵的东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je veux quelque chose de beau et pas trop cher.","exEn":"I want something lovely and not too expensive.","src":5},{"fr":"c’est pour quand ?","en":"when is it for?","zh":"是什么时候的事?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est pour quand, cette fête ?","exEn":"When is this party?","src":6},{"fr":"nous fermons entre Noël et le Nouvel An","en":"we close between Christmas and New Year","zh":"我们在圣诞节和新年之间休息","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous fermons entre Noël et le Nouvel An.","exEn":"We close between Christmas and the New Year.","src":7},{"fr":"pas de problème","en":"no problem","zh":"没问题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ no problem","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas de problème : c’est pour la soirée du trente juin, jusqu’à minuit.","exEn":"No problem: it’s for the evening of the thirtieth of June, until midnight.","src":8},{"fr":"jusqu’à minuit","en":"until midnight","zh":"直到午夜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Pas de problème : c’est pour la soirée du trente juin, jusqu’à minuit.","exEn":"No problem: it’s for the evening of the thirtieth of June, until midnight.","src":8},{"fr":"un bel endroit pour une fête","en":"a great place for a party","zh":"办派对的好地方","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un bel endroit pour une fête.","exEn":"It’s a great place for a party.","src":10},{"fr":"ça coûte combien ?","en":"how much does it cost?","zh":"这要多少钱?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ça coûte combien ? Je n’ai pas un gros budget.","exEn":"How much does it cost? I don’t have a big budget.","src":11},{"fr":"je n’ai pas un gros budget","en":"I don’t have a big budget","zh":"我预算不多","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"budget ≈ budget","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ça coûte combien ? Je n’ai pas un gros budget.","exEn":"How much does it cost? I don’t have a big budget.","src":11},{"fr":"plus petit et moins cher","en":"smaller and less expensive","zh":"更小也更便宜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Alors j’ai quelque chose de plus petit et moins cher :","exEn":"Then I have something smaller and less expensive:","src":12},{"fr":"c’est gratuit","en":"it’s free (no charge)","zh":"免费","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"gratuit ≈ gratuity","ff":"gratuit = free of charge; libre = free (available)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"le placard à balais : c’est gratuit !","exEn":"the broom cupboard: it’s free!","src":13}],"uncertain":["exercise 2 item 1: the scan cue says “the gardens are pretty”, but the French line (and answer key) is about “les salles”; cue changed to “rooms” to match the French."],"pdfPage":85,"gaps":[[1.78,3.68],[5.7,8.5],[51.58,52.62],[56.46,57.58],[60.24,63.72],[66.28,68.46],[71.68,74.74],[76.82,79.84],[82.7,85.94],[88.04,90.88],[93.5,97.04]],"dur":97.1},{"n":20,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un monde idéal","titleEn":"An ideal world","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Le sujet de l’émission Carte blanche cette semaine est « Un monde idéal ».","en":"The subject of the programme Carte Blanche this week is “An ideal world.”","lit":"card white"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons posé la question suivante aux ambassadeurs de quatre pays :","en":"We put the following question to the ambassadors of four countries:","lit":"we have put"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"le Japon, l’Inde, les États-Unis et la France.","en":"Japan, India, the United States and France.","lit":"the Japan, the India, the United States and the France"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"« Que voulez-vous pour Noël, Monsieur l’Ambassadeur ? »","en":"“What do you want for Christmas, Mr Ambassador?”","lit":"Mr the Ambassador"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Voici leurs réponses, dans l’ordre.","en":"Here are their answers, in order.","lit":"in the order"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"L’ambassadeur japonais veut « du bonheur, de la paix et des droits humains ».","en":"The Japanese ambassador wants “happiness, peace and human rights.”","lit":"of the happiness, of the peace and of the rights human"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Son collègue indien veut « de la santé et de l’éducation pour tout le monde ».","en":"His Indian colleague wants “health and education for everyone.”","lit":"all the world"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"L’Américain veut « des emplois, de l’argent et du bien-être » pour son pays.","en":"The American wants “jobs, money and well-being” for his country.","lit":"well-to-be"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais la réponse de l’ambassadeur français est la plus intéressante.","en":"But the answer of the French ambassador is the most interesting.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah bon ? Qu’est-ce qu’il veut ?","en":"Really? What does he want?","lit":"ah good"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"« Des chocolats, du vin blanc et de la crème fraîche. »","en":"“Chocolates, white wine and fresh cream.”","lit":"of the chocolates, of the white wine and of the cream fresh"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce n’est pas vrai ! Il est honnête, au moins !","en":"I don’t believe it! He’s honest, at least!","lit":"it is not true"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know that au (to the) is the equivalent of à le for masculine nouns (lesson 8 note 1). Aux is the plural form for both genders: Cet été, nous allons aux États-Unis (masc.) / Je vais aux toilettes (fem.). Like all plural endings, the -x is silent [oh] unless it precedes a vowel."},{"ref":"2","text":"Country names, like all nouns, are either masculine or feminine. Most of those ending in -e are feminine (la France, la Belgique (Belgium), l’Italie (Italy), etc.); the others are masculine. Some toponyms are plural, notably les États-Unis, the United States. There are a few exceptions, which we will see later."},{"ref":"3","text":"Adjectives of nationality do not take an initial capital (l’ambassadeur américain, the American ambassador), but nouns of nationality do: un(e) Américain(e), an American man/woman."},{"ref":"4","text":"The partitive articles du, de la and des give information about the quantity of the noun they precede. They are often translated by some/any but, in some cases, can be omitted in English. Du and des are the combined forms of de le and de les, respectively (see lesson 12 note 1). Il veut du pain, de la crème et des champignons, He wants (some) bread, (some) cream and (some) mushrooms. If a singular noun following a partitive begins with a vowel (or an h), the article becomes de l’, whatever the noun’s gender: (de l’argent, masc.; de l’éducation, fem.). Yet another good reason for learning genders!"},{"ref":"5","text":"Despite the final -s, pays can be singular as well as plural (mon pays, my country, nos pays, our countries). The same applies to nouns ending in -x (le prix/les prix, price/prices) and -z, as we will see later on."},{"ref":"6","text":"vrai(e), true, can also mean real (la vraie réponse, the real answer). The exclamation Ce n’est pas vrai !, It isn’t true! can also be used (as in sentence 12) to mean I don’t believe it!, You’re kidding! In everyday usage, careless speakers tend to drop the negative particle n’, but don’t be tempted to imitate them!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ses réponses sont les plus intéressantes.","a":"His/Her answers are the most interesting [ones]."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que vous voulez pour Noël ?","a":"What do you want for Christmas?"},{"q":"Ils sont honnêtes, au moins !","a":"They’re honest, at least!"},{"q":"Ne posez pas cette question à votre guide.","a":"Don’t ask your guide that question."},{"q":"Elle est en retard. – Ce n’est pas vrai !","a":"She’s late. – I don’t believe it!"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We want happiness, peace and jobs.","t":"Nous voulons {{du bonheur}}, {{de la paix}} et {{des emplois}}."},{"cue":"What do you want to eat? – I want some bread, some strawberries and some fresh cream.","t":"{{Que voulez-vous}} manger ? – Je veux {{du pain}}, {{des}} fraises et {{de la}} crème {{fraîche}}."},{"cue":"This is Steve, an American, and his French friend, Alain.","t":"Voici Steve, {{un Américain}}, et {{son ami français}}, Alain."},{"cue":"And here is his wife, Marianne, and her Japanese [female] friend.","t":"Et voici {{sa femme}}, Marianne, et {{son amie japonaise}}."},{"cue":"It’s important to have money, health, and good wine. – Really?","t":"Il est important d’avoir {{de l’argent}}, {{de la santé}}, et {{du bon vin}}. – Ah {{bon}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"le sujet de l’émission","en":"the subject of the programme","zh":"节目的主题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"sujet ≈ subject","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le sujet de l’émission Carte blanche cette semaine est « Un monde idéal ».","exEn":"The subject of the programme Carte Blanche this week is “An ideal world.”","src":1},{"fr":"un monde idéal","en":"an ideal world","zh":"理想的世界","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"idéal ≈ ideal","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le sujet de l’émission Carte blanche cette semaine est « Un monde idéal ».","exEn":"The subject of the programme Carte Blanche this week is “An ideal world.”","src":1},{"fr":"nous avons posé la question","en":"we put/asked the question","zh":"我们提出了问题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons posé la question suivante aux ambassadeurs de quatre pays :","exEn":"We put the following question to the ambassadors of four countries:","src":2},{"fr":"un pays","en":"a country","zh":"国家","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “pay-ee”: you ‘pay’ tax to your country","ex":"L’Américain veut « des emplois, de l’argent et du bien-être » pour son pays.","exEn":"The American wants “jobs, money and well-being” for his country.","src":8},{"fr":"que voulez-vous pour Noël ?","en":"what do you want for Christmas?","zh":"您圣诞节想要什么?","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"Noël ≈ Noel","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Que voulez-vous pour Noël, Monsieur l’Ambassadeur ? »","exEn":"“What do you want for Christmas, Mr Ambassador?”","src":4},{"fr":"voici leurs réponses","en":"here are their answers","zh":"这是他们的回答","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici leurs réponses, dans l’ordre.","exEn":"Here are their answers, in order.","src":5},{"fr":"la paix","en":"peace","zh":"和平","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’ambassadeur japonais veut « du bonheur, de la paix et des droits humains ».","exEn":"The Japanese ambassador wants “happiness, peace and human rights.”","src":6},{"fr":"de la santé","en":"health","zh":"健康","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ sanitary","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Son collègue indien veut « de la santé et de l’éducation pour tout le monde ».","exEn":"His Indian colleague wants “health and education for everyone.”","src":7},{"fr":"pour tout le monde","en":"for everyone","zh":"为了每个人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Son collègue indien veut « de la santé et de l’éducation pour tout le monde ».","exEn":"His Indian colleague wants “health and education for everyone.”","src":7},{"fr":"de l’argent","en":"(some) money","zh":"钱","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"argent also means silver","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"L’Américain veut « des emplois, de l’argent et du bien-être » pour son pays.","exEn":"The American wants “jobs, money and well-being” for his country.","src":8},{"fr":"des emplois","en":"jobs","zh":"工作,职位","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"L’Américain veut « des emplois, de l’argent et du bien-être » pour son pays.","exEn":"The American wants “jobs, money and well-being” for his country.","src":8},{"fr":"la réponse la plus intéressante","en":"the most interesting answer","zh":"最有意思的回答","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"intéressant ≈ interesting","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais la réponse de l’ambassadeur français est la plus intéressante.","exEn":"But the answer of the French ambassador is the most interesting.","src":9},{"fr":"du vin blanc","en":"(some) white wine","zh":"白葡萄酒","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"« Des chocolats, du vin blanc et de la crème fraîche. »","exEn":"“Chocolates, white wine and fresh cream.”","src":11},{"fr":"de la crème fraîche","en":"(some) fresh cream","zh":"鲜奶油","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"« Des chocolats, du vin blanc et de la crème fraîche. »","exEn":"“Chocolates, white wine and fresh cream.”","src":11},{"fr":"ce n’est pas vrai !","en":"I don’t believe it! (it isn’t true)","zh":"不会吧!(难以置信)","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce n’est pas vrai ! Il est honnête, au moins !","exEn":"I don’t believe it! He’s honest, at least!","src":12}],"uncertain":["dialogue/ex2: the guillemets « » around quoted speech are my rendering of the book’s quotation marks.","exercise 2 item 2: the answer key gives “Que voulez-vous” for the first gap; the second sentence word order follows the dots."],"pdfPage":89,"gaps":[[1.4,3.36],[4.68,7.68],[13.92,14.98],[24.8,25.94],[32.36,33.42],[38.98,40.18],[60.76,61.82],[68.44,72.38],[74.28,76.44],[78.86,82.08],[83.68,86.68],[88.34,91.36],[93.28,96.26],[98.56,103.2]],"dur":103.2},{"n":21,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je veux descendre du bus à la place de la Concorde.","en":"I want to get off the bus at Concorde Square.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un bel endroit pour faire une belle photo de ce beau bâtiment et, en face, de cette grande église.","en":"It’s a fine place to take a nice photo of that handsome building and, opposite, that grand/big church.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais nous n’avons pas beaucoup de temps.","en":"But we don’t have much time.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi ? Quelle heure est-il maintenant ?","en":"Why? What time is it now?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est déjà cinq heures et quart, presque cinq heures et demie.","en":"It’s already quarter past five, nearly half past five.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Dix-sept heures quinze ! Ce n’est pas vrai !","en":"Five fifteen! You’re kidding!","lit":"it is not true"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous devons attendre demain matin, je pense.","en":"We have to wait until tomorrow morning, I think.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons la soirée libre.","en":"We have the whole evening free.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que vous voulez faire maintenant ?","en":"What do you want to do now?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"On peut aller regarder la Tour Eiffel : c’est gratuit !","en":"We can go and look at the Eiffel Tower: it’s free!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 être, avoir and vouloir","text":"These are three of the most common verbs in French. The first two – être and avoir – are main verbs as well as auxiliaries, just like their English counterparts to be and to have. All three are irregular (or “strong”) verbs.\n\nêtre, to be\nje suis – I am\ntu es* – you are\nil/elle est – he/she/it is\nnous sommes – we are\nvous* êtes – you are\nils/elles sont – they are\n\navoir, to have\nj’ai – I have\ntu as* – you have\nil/elle a – he/she/it has\nnous avons – we have\nvous* avez – you have\nils/elles ont – they have\n\nThe third verb, vouloir, is used to say what you want, and also when offering something or inviting someone:\n\nvouloir, to want\nje veux – I want\ntu veux* – you want\nil/elle veut – he/she/it wants\nnous voulons – we want\nvous* voulez – you want\nils/elles veulent – they want\n\n* French, like many languages, has both a formal and an informal way of addressing people. The corresponding pronouns are vous (formal) and tu (informal). For the time being, we will concentrate on using vous (the verb is vouvoyer), which is always the case when talking to a stranger or a person to whom we want to show respect. Vous is also the second person plural pronoun, used when addressing more than one person.\n\nLike all verbs, the negative of vouloir is formed with two words: ne (or n’ before a vowel or mute h) and pas (see lesson 7).\nJe ne suis pas libre demain, I’m not free tomorrow.\nElle n’a pas de projets, She doesn’t have any plans.\nNous ne voulons pas venir, We don’t want to come.\n\nThe interrogative can be formed in three ways, depending on the context:\n– simply by raising the voice at the end of an affirmative sentence: Vous voulez venir ? You want to come? This is very informal (but also very common);\n– by adding est-ce que (literally “is it that”) before the pronoun: Est-ce que vous voulez venir ? Do you want to come? (see lesson 4 note 2). This is the usual construction in everyday spoken French;\n– by inverting the pronoun and the verb: Voulez-vous venir ? Do you want to come? This is the most formal of the three constructions.\n\nThese negative and interrogative forms are used for all French verbs. Choosing the right one will depend on the person speaking and the context. For the time being, go back over the previous lessons and take note of the constructions we have used."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Agreement of nouns and adjectives","text":"We know that adjectives agree in number and gender with the nouns they qualify. Here are some basic rules for identifying noun genders (though there are quite a few exceptions).\n\n2.1 Nouns\nIn many cases, the gender can be identified by its ending.\n– Most nouns ending in -age and -eau are masculine. For example, l’âge (age), un stage (a training course), un bureau (an office/a desk), un cadeau (a gift). Two notable exceptions are une image (a picture) and l’eau (water).\n– Another typical masculine ending is -er: le déjeuner (lunch) or -ier, le quartier (the neighbourhood). Both these terminals are often found in the nouns for jobs or professions: un boulanger (a baker), un banquier (a banker), and so on.\n– Finally, nouns that end in -in are generally masculine: un jardin (a garden), le matin (the morning). Possibly the most common exception is la fin (the end).\n– The majority of nouns ending in -ion and -té are feminine: une région (a region), l’éducation (education), la santé (health), la liberté (freedom, liberty). Two exceptions are le côté (the side) and l’avion (the plane).\n– Likewise, the -ée ending is usually feminine, for instance la soirée (the evening), une idée (an idea). One of the very few exceptions is un musée (a museum).\nThere are several other “typical” endings, which we will see in lesson 28.\n\n2.2 Adjectives\nWe have learned the basic rules for the agreement of adjectives, but some of them have an irregular feminine form. Here are three common ones:\nle lait frais (masc. fresh milk) → une fraise fraîche (fem. a fresh strawberry) – note the circumflex on the i\nle vin blanc (masc. white wine) → une carte blanche (fem. a white card)\nun long moment (masc. a long time/moment) → une longue avenue (fem. a long avenue)\nTo form the plural, simply add the final s.\n\nWe saw in lesson 19 that a handful of the adjectives coming before a noun have two masculine forms, depending on the first letter of the following word: a vowel (or h) or a consonant. In addition to beau and nouveau, the third commonly used adjective is vieux: un vieux monument (an old monument) but un bel endroit (a pretty place or spot). Note that bel is pronounced in exactly the same way as the feminine form belle.\n\nFinally, twenty or so adjectives ending in -e are both masculine and feminine. In addition to superbe, facile, malade and moderne (lesson 16), vide (lesson 18) and honnête (lesson 20), three others worth learning are faible, weak, pauvre, poor, and triste, sad. You will also recognise confortable, comfortable (note the n), drôle, funny (the origin of droll) and sincère, sincere, genuine."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Days, months, and dates","text":"The days of the week are:\nlundi – Monday\nmardi – Tuesday\nmercredi – Wednesday\njeudi – Thursday\nvendredi – Friday\nsamedi – Saturday\ndimanche – Sunday\n\nThe final syllable, -di, is from the Latin dies, a day, found in the English equivalents (Monday, etc.). As for the weekend, most people in France (and Belgium) use the same word, le week-end (note the hyphen), rather than the preferred term la fin de semaine (litt. “the end of week”), which is used in Canada.\n\nThe months of the year are:\njanvier – January\nfévrier – February\nmars – March\navril – April\nmai – May\njuin – June\njuillet – July\naoût – August\nseptembre – September\noctobre – October\nnovembre – November\ndécembre – December\n\nNone of the day or month nouns take an initial capital (unless they start a sentence) and are all masculine.\n\nWhen giving the date, French uses a cardinal number, not an ordinal, and puts the masculine definite article before the day, not the month, as in English: le lundi dix mai, Monday the tenth of May, le jeudi vingt et un août, Thursday the twenty-first of August.\n\nIt takes practice to master the day and date constructions. We recommend flipping through a desk or phone calendar, slowly at first then faster and faster, saying the dates out loud as you go. You’ll be surprised how quickly it becomes second nature."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Telling the time","text":"French has two main words for time: le temps (a measured or measurable period) and l’heure, a feminine noun that literally means the hour but is used to give the precise time. To ask the time, always use the inverted question form (never est-ce que): Quelle heure est-il ? What time is it? (Here il is the impersonal pronoun, performing the same function as it in English.)\n\n– There is no equivalent of o’clock. Simply put heures (or heure for 1 am or 1 pm) after the hour: Il est une heure, It’s one o’clock, Il est quatre heures, It’s four o’clock.\n– For minutes past the hour, use the cardinal and invert the word order: Il est trois heures dix, It’s ten past three; Il est six heures vingt-cinq, It’s twenty-five past six.\n– For minutes before the next hour, use moins (less, minus) after the cardinal: Il est une heure moins dix, It’s ten to one, Il est onze heures moins vingt, It’s twenty to eleven.\n– For the quarter-hours, we use et quart and moins le quart, placed after the word heure(s): Il est deux heures et quart, It’s a quarter past two, Il est huit heures moins le quart, It’s a quarter to eight. Note the difference with the English word order.\n– The term for half past is et demie, placed after the hour. Demi means half, and, since une heure is feminine, we add a final e (but no s): Il est neuf heures et demie, It’s half past nine.\n– The words for midday (noon) and midnight are midi [meedee] and minuit [meenwee].\n– French uses the twenty-four hour clock, not only in official contexts such as appointments and travel or broadcasting schedules, but in everyday conversation, too:\ntreize heures – 1 pm\nquatorze heures – 2 pm\nquinze heures – 3 pm\nseize heures – 4 pm\ndix-sept heures – 5 pm\ndix-huit heures – 6 pm\ndix-neuf heures – 7 pm\nvingt heures – 8 pm\nvingt et une heures – 9 pm\nvingt-deux heures – 10 pm\nvingt-trois heures – 11 pm\nvingt-quatre heures – 12 midnight\n\nThe hour is followed simply by the number of minutes (et quart and moins le quart are not used): Il est seize heures trente, It’s half past four (in the afternoon); Il est dix-neuf heures quarante, It’s twenty to eight in the evening. This system can take some getting used to initially (it’s easy to confuse, say, quinze heures with five o’clock!) but it’s actually very logical."},{"ref":"","text":"You may feel that there is a lot of detail in today’s revision lesson. But in fact, we are going back over things you have already learned and helping you to organise them. With the exception of noun genders, don’t try to learn anything by heart for the time being. Just enjoy yourself: there are no exams to pass!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"il est cinq heures et quart","en":"it’s a quarter past five","zh":"现在五点一刻","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Il est déjà cinq heures et quart, presque cinq heures et demie.","exEn":"It’s already quarter past five, nearly half past five.","src":5},{"fr":"je veux descendre du bus","en":"I want to get off the bus","zh":"我想下公共汽车","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je veux descendre du bus à la place de la Concorde.","exEn":"I want to get off the bus at Concorde Square.","src":1},{"fr":"un bel endroit","en":"a fine place","zh":"一个漂亮的地方","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un bel endroit pour faire une belle photo de ce beau bâtiment et, en face, de cette grande église.","exEn":"It’s a fine place to take a nice photo of that handsome building and, opposite, that grand/big church.","src":2},{"fr":"nous n’avons pas beaucoup de temps","en":"we don’t have much time","zh":"我们没有多少时间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais nous n’avons pas beaucoup de temps.","exEn":"But we don’t have much time.","src":3},{"fr":"nous avons la soirée libre","en":"we have the evening free","zh":"我们晚上有空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons la soirée libre.","exEn":"We have the whole evening free.","src":8},{"fr":"Voulez-vous venir ?","en":"Do you want to come?","zh":"您想来吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voulez-vous venir ?","exEn":"Do you want to come?","src":0},{"fr":"je ne suis pas libre demain","en":"I’m not free tomorrow","zh":"我明天没空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne suis pas libre demain.","exEn":"I’m not free tomorrow.","src":0}],"uncertain":["note 2.2: the OCR gives 'un vieux monument ... but un bel endroit (a pretty place)' as the example for vieux; kept as printed but the original may read 'un vieil endroit' (not recoverable).","note 2.1: 'which we will see in lesson 28' kept as printed."],"pdfPage":95,"gaps":[[1.76,3.68],[5.18,8.2],[24.54,25.6],[44.7,48.96]],"dur":49},{"n":22,"kind":"lesson","title":"Réfléchissez, choisissez, jouez","titleEn":"Think, choose, play","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’aime pas les jeux de hasard, parce qu’ils sont tous risqués. Et vous ?","en":"I don’t like games of chance, because they’re all risky. And you?","lit":"the games of chance"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis comme vous. Mais il y a un jeu que j’aime bien.","en":"I’m like you. But there is one game that I really like.","lit":"like well"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Vraiment ? Lequel ? Les machines à sous ?","en":"Really? Which one? Slot machines?","lit":"machines at pennies"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais non ! Ce sont les courses de chevaux.","en":"Not at all! It’s horse races.","lit":"but no; it are"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Je regarde tous les journaux spécialisés, surtout un journal intitulé Mille gagnants,","en":"I read all the specialised newspapers, especially a paper called “A Thousand Winners,”","lit":"entitled"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’établis une petite liste.","en":"and I make a little list.","lit":"establish"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je réfléchis pendant quelques secondes, et quand je suis prêt,","en":"I think for a few seconds, and when I’m ready,","lit":"reflect"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"je choisis le cheval qui a une bonne chance de finir en premier.","en":"I choose the horse that has a good chance of finishing first.","lit":"to finish in"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Puis je remplis la grille de jeu… et je croise les doigts.","en":"Then I fill in the race card and I cross my fingers.","lit":"game grid; the fingers"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Au fait, comment est-ce que vous choisissez vos chevaux ?","en":"By the way, how do you choose your horses?","lit":"to the fact"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que vous espérez ?","en":"And what do you hope for?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis comme tous les joueurs ; nous avons tous la même idée :","en":"I’m like all gamblers: we all have the same idea:","lit":"players"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"gagner beaucoup d’argent et devenir très riche.","en":"to earn a lot of money and to become very rich.","lit":"win"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Au fait, est-ce que vous pouvez me prêter cinquante euros ?","en":"By the way, can you lend me fifty euros?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’ai pas un sou.","en":"I’m broke.","lit":"I don’t have a penny"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Most nouns ending in -eu form the plural with -x: un jeu, des jeux (game(s)), un feu, des feux (fire(s)). Another set of nouns with an irregular ending are those ending in -al. The l is deleted and replaced by -ux: un cheval, des chevaux (horse(s)), un journal, des journaux (newspaper(s)). There are a couple more of these irregular forms, which we will learn later on."},{"ref":"2","text":"tous is the masculine plural of the adjective tout, all. Placed before a noun, it is pronounced [too], just like the singular (tous les jeux, all the games → [too lay zheu]). But if tous comes after a verb, it means all of you/them/everyone, and is pronounced [toos]: Je les aime tous, I like all of them → [zhuh layzem toos]. Always check the preceding word before trying to pronounce tous!"},{"ref":"3","text":"Mais non !, literally “But no!”, is an emphatic contradiction, equivalent to Of course not! or Not at all! Vous êtes malade ? – Mais non, je vais très bien !, Are you ill? – Not at all, I’m very well! Another common exclamatory expression, in line 10 and line 14, is Au fait, literally “to the fact.” Placed at the beginning of a sentence, it means By the way. Au fait, quel est votre nom ? By the way, what’s your name?"},{"ref":"4","text":"établis is the first person singular of établir, to establish, to draw up. It belongs to the second major group of verbs, which end in -ir and are conjugated like this: j’établis, tu établis, il/elle établit, nous établissons, vous établissez, ils/elles établissent. Other common verbs in this group are choisir, to choose, finir, to finish, remplir, to fill, and réfléchir, to think, reflect. (More details in the next lesson and an overview in lesson 28.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"choisissez is the second person plural and honorific singular form of choisir (see above). Note the pronunciation: [shwahzeessay]."},{"ref":"6","text":"gagner means to win: Elle gagne toujours quand elle joue, She always wins when she plays. But it also means to earn (money): Il gagne cinq mille euros par mois, He earns five thousand euros per month. Note the context whenever you come across this very common verb. Un(e) gagnant(e), a winner (male/female)."},{"ref":"7","text":"un sou is the name of an old French coin. It no longer exists, but the word still crops up in popular expressions – Je n’ai pas un sou, I don’t have a penny/a bean; Il est sans le sou, He doesn’t have a bean – as well as in the familiar term une machine à sous, a slot machine. (Pay attention to the context: sous is also a preposition meaning under.)"},{"ref":"pron","text":"(8) The double-l sound is pronounced in several ways. One of the trickiest is -ill- preceded by a consonant, which is usually pronounced [eey]. We already know fille ([feeyy]) and Bastille ([basteeyy]). The same applies to grille ([greeyy]). But there are exceptions: mille in line 5 is [meel]. We recommend keeping a list of the various pronunciations so as not to get confused."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Vous devez réfléchir avant de choisir.","a":"You must think before choosing."},{"q":"Je sais que tous les jeux de hasard sont risqués.","a":"I know that all games of chance are risky."},{"q":"Ils ont tous la même idée : devenir riche.","a":"They all have the same idea: to get rich."},{"q":"Désolé, je ne peux pas vous prêter trente euros.","a":"Sorry, I can’t lend you thirty euros."},{"q":"Je suis comme vous, je n’ai pas un sou.","a":"I’m like you, I don’t have a penny."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I think for a few seconds then, when I’m ready, I fill in the form.","t":"Je {{réfléchis}} pendant quelques {{secondes}} et quand je suis {{prêt}}, je {{remplis}} le formulaire."},{"cue":"All the players have the same idea: to earn/win money.","t":"{{Tous}} les joueurs {{ont}} la même idée : {{gagner}} de {{l’argent}}."},{"cue":"Can you lend me twenty-two euros?","t":"{{Est-ce que}} vous {{pouvez me prêter}} vingt-deux euros ?"},{"cue":"We buy three newspapers every day to play the horse races.","t":"Nous achetons trois {{journaux}} {{tous}} les jours pour jouer {{aux courses de chevaux}}."},{"cue":"She doesn’t like games of chance, especially a game called “Become Rich.”","t":"Elle {{n’aime pas}} les {{jeux}} de hasard, surtout {{un jeu}} intitulé « {{Devenir riche}} »."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je n’aime pas les jeux de hasard","en":"I don’t like games of chance","zh":"我不喜欢碰运气的游戏","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’aime pas les jeux de hasard, parce qu’ils sont tous risqués. Et vous ?","exEn":"I don’t like games of chance, because they’re all risky. And you?","src":1},{"fr":"mais non !","en":"not at all! / of course not!","zh":"当然不是！哪里的话！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais non ! Ce sont les courses de chevaux.","exEn":"Not at all! It’s horse races.","src":4},{"fr":"au fait","en":"by the way","zh":"顺便问一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au fait, comment est-ce que vous choisissez vos chevaux ?","exEn":"By the way, how do you choose your horses?","src":10},{"fr":"un journal","en":"a newspaper","zh":"报纸","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ journal","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je regarde tous les journaux spécialisés, surtout un journal intitulé Mille gagnants,","exEn":"I read all the specialised newspapers, especially a paper called “A Thousand Winners,”","src":5},{"fr":"un cheval","en":"a horse","zh":"马","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ cavalry / chivalry (cheval = horse)","ff":"","cluster":"animals","kw":"sounds like “shove-al”: picture a stubborn horse you have to shove along with a shovel.","ex":"je choisis le cheval qui a une bonne chance de finir en premier.","exEn":"I choose the horse that has a good chance of finishing first.","src":8},{"fr":"une machine à sous","en":"a slot machine","zh":"老虎机","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ machine","ff":"sous = old coin (penny), not “under”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vraiment ? Lequel ? Les machines à sous ?","exEn":"Really? Which one? Slot machines?","src":3},{"fr":"je réfléchis pendant quelques secondes","en":"I think for a few seconds","zh":"我思考几秒钟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ reflect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je réfléchis pendant quelques secondes, et quand je suis prêt,","exEn":"I think for a few seconds, and when I’m ready,","src":7},{"fr":"je choisis le cheval","en":"I choose the horse","zh":"我选这匹马","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"je choisis le cheval qui a une bonne chance de finir en premier.","exEn":"I choose the horse that has a good chance of finishing first.","src":8},{"fr":"je remplis la grille de jeu","en":"I fill in the game grid","zh":"我填写游戏表格","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Puis je remplis la grille de jeu… et je croise les doigts.","exEn":"Then I fill in the race card and I cross my fingers.","src":9},{"fr":"je croise les doigts","en":"I cross my fingers","zh":"我祈祷好运（交叉手指）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Puis je remplis la grille de jeu… et je croise les doigts.","exEn":"Then I fill in the race card and I cross my fingers.","src":9},{"fr":"nous avons tous la même idée","en":"we all have the same idea","zh":"我们都有同样的想法","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis comme tous les joueurs ; nous avons tous la même idée :","exEn":"I’m like all gamblers: we all have the same idea:","src":12},{"fr":"gagner beaucoup d’argent","en":"to earn / win a lot of money","zh":"赚/赢很多钱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"gagner beaucoup d’argent et devenir très riche.","exEn":"to earn a lot of money and to become very rich.","src":13},{"fr":"devenir très riche","en":"to become very rich","zh":"变得非常富有","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"gagner beaucoup d’argent et devenir très riche.","exEn":"to earn a lot of money and to become very rich.","src":13},{"fr":"vous pouvez me prêter cinquante euros ?","en":"can you lend me fifty euros?","zh":"您能借我五十欧元吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au fait, est-ce que vous pouvez me prêter cinquante euros ?","exEn":"By the way, can you lend me fifty euros?","src":14},{"fr":"je n’ai pas un sou","en":"I’m broke (I don’t have a penny)","zh":"我身无分文","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai pas un sou.","exEn":"I’m broke.","src":15}],"uncertain":["note 3: OCR says 'in line 9 and line 13' for Au fait; corrected to lines 10 and 14 (where it appears).","dialogue 11: OCR joined line 11 to 10; English 'And what do you hope for?' kept as the book’s separate sentence."],"pdfPage":101,"gaps":[[2.16,4.32],[7.66,10.44],[32.4,33.44],[66.84,70.04],[71.84,73.8],[76.3,79.36],[81.76,84.66],[87.86,91.08],[93.5,96.78],[99.5,103.4]],"dur":103.4},{"n":23,"kind":"lesson","title":"Comment réussir au loto","titleEn":"How to succeed at the lotto","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour, qu’est-ce que je vous sers ?","en":"Good morning, what can I get you?","lit":"what-is-it that I you serve"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je veux un café crème, un croque-monsieur et deux billets de loto, s’il vous plaît.","en":"I want a white coffee, a toasted ham-and-cheese sandwich and two lotto tickets, please.","lit":"coffee cream; bite-sir"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous pouvez jouer au loto en ligne, vous savez. C’est très simple.","en":"You can play the lotto online, you know. It’s very simple.","lit":"at the lotto"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Et il n’y a pas de queues au comptoir, comme ce matin.","en":"And there are no queues at the counter, like this morning.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Je sais, mais je préfère venir ici : l’ambiance est sympa.","en":"I know, but I prefer to come here: the atmosphere is nice.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je saisis l’occasion pour quitter mon appartement et voir d’autres gens.","en":"I take the opportunity to get out of my flat and see other people.","lit":"I seize; to leave; to see"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai des clients qui viennent jouer tous les jours.","en":"I have customers who come to play every day.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Dites-moi : avez-vous un secret pour le loto ?","en":"Tell me, do you have a secret for the lotto?","lit":"say-me"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout à fait. Je joue toujours les mêmes numéros :","en":"Absolutely. I always play the same numbers:","lit":"all to fact; play always"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"tous ceux finissant par quatre, plus le soixante-deux, le quarante-trois, le dix-sept, et le cinquante-cinq,","en":"all those finishing in 4, plus the 62, the 43, the 17 and the 55,","lit":"by"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"ma date de naissance : le vingt-trois, douze, soixante-neuf,","en":"my birth date: the 23-12-69","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"et, évidemment, le treize, mon chiffre préféré.","en":"and, of course, the 13, my favourite number.","lit":"evidently"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Avec ça, vous réussissez à gagner ? Je vous applaudis !","en":"With that, you manage to win? I applaud you!","lit":"succeed"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, pas vraiment, mais j’investis pour l’avenir. Je vais gagner un de ces jours !","en":"No, not really, but I’m investing for the future. I’m going to win one day!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"","text":"Cafés are an essential feature of everyday life in France. The generic name, un café, is subdivided into several categories, notably un tabac (literally tobacco) or un bar-tabac, a café that also sells tobacco products, newspapers, phone cards, postage and tax stamps and lottery tickets. That is where this 23rd lesson takes place. As the name un café implies, the basic drink is coffee, served either as an espresso (see lesson 2) or with milk, un café au lait, also known as un café crème (“coffee cream”). Most cafés also serve basic foods, notably un sandwich (plural: sandwichs), une omelette or un croque-monsieur, a ham sandwich topped with melted cheese. (The verb croquer means to crunch, or to bite into.)"},{"ref":"1","text":"Remember to always learn the preposition or adverb that usually follows a verb, particularly when the equivalent English verb does not have one. Here, jouer, to play, is followed by à if the object noun is feminine (jouer à la loterie, to play the lottery), au for a masculine singular noun (jouer au loto, to play the lotto) and aux for a masculine or feminine plural: jouer aux échecs, to play chess."},{"ref":"2","text":"The negative particle pas is followed by de (or d’ before a vowel) regardless of whether the following noun is singular or plural: Il n’a pas de billets, He doesn’t have any tickets; Elle n’a pas d’argent, She doesn’t have any money."},{"ref":"3","text":"The idiomatic adjective sympa is an abbreviated form of sympathique, equivalent to nice, pleasant, etc. Michel est une personne très sympa, Michel’s a really nice person. Because the word is an abbreviation, it is invariable in the singular, but it takes a final -s in the plural: Ce sont des personnes très sympas, They’re really nice people. Note that sympa is as imprecise as nice – and just as common!"},{"ref":"4","text":"autre is an indefinite adjective, like tout, that is used to give a general description: Choisissez un autre chiffre, Choose another figure. When followed by a plural noun, it is preceded by the partitive d’: Choisissez d’autres chiffres, Choose other figures. Now look back at lesson 16 sentence 1, and lesson 18 sentence 5."},{"ref":"5","text":"dire (pronounced [deer]) is an important irregular verb that translates both to say and to tell. The present tense is je dis, tu dis, il/elle dit, nous disons, vous dites, ils/elles disent. Sami dit qu’il est désolé, Sami says he’s very sorry. Dites-moi votre nom, Tell me your name. We’ll learn more about the subtle differences in construction later on."},{"ref":"6","text":"tout à fait (“all to fact”) is a common expression of agreement or confirmation, equivalent to Exactly or Absolutely: Êtes-vous prêt ? – Tout à fait, Are you ready? – Absolutely. It can also be used for emphasis: Je suis tout à fait d’accord, I totally agree. Remember that French does not use an auxiliary to add emphasis (I do like that game), so expressions such as tout à fait are very common."},{"ref":"7","text":"toujours comes after the verb, in contrast to always: Nous jouons toujours aux mêmes jeux, We always play the same games. And whereas English makes a difference between always and still, toujours covers both notions: Est-ce que tu habites toujours à Toulouse ? Do you still live in Toulouse?"},{"ref":"8","text":"The terms for numbers, figures, digits and numerals are awkward to translate because the terms are not always used precisely. Thus un chiffre, which gives us the English word cipher, refers to a written figure or a digit: le chiffre sept translates as the figure 7, and un numéro refers to a specific unit such as a phone number or a house number (numéro de téléphone / de maison). To complicate matters slightly, there is also the word un nombre, which we will see later on."},{"ref":"9","text":"Second-group verbs ending in -ir are characterised by their present participle, which ends in -issant (finir → finissant, applaudir → applaudissant, réussir → réussissant, etc.): Il choisit tous les numéros finissant par huit, He chooses all the numbers finishing in eight."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(4), (8) Pay attention when you come across a French word with the same spelling as an English word: the pronunciation is generally different. For example, la queue and le secret are pronounced [lah kuh] and [luh suhkray], respectively."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je suis tout à fait d’accord : l’ambiance dans ce bar n’est pas très sympa.","a":"I totally agree: the atmosphere in this bar isn’t very nice."},{"q":"J’ai un client qui vient tous les jours à la même heure. Et il est toujours là quand je ferme !","a":"I have a customer who comes every day at the same time. And he’s still there when I close!"},{"q":"C’est très simple de jouer en ligne, vous savez.","a":"It’s very easy to play online, you know."},{"q":"Nous jouons toujours les mêmes numéros : le vingt-trois, le soixante-neuf et le treize.","a":"We always play the same numbers: 23, 69 and 13."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que je vous sers, madame ? Un café crème ?","a":"What can I get you, madam? A white coffee?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I’m investing for the future. – I applaud you!","t":"{{J’investis}} pour l’avenir. – Je vous {{applaudis}} !"},{"cue":"He says that he wants to see other people but he doesn’t always succeed.","t":"Il {{dit qu’}}il veut voir {{d’autres}} gens, mais il {{ne réussit pas}} toujours."},{"cue":"Don’t you have any plans for the holidays? – We don’t have any money.","t":"Vous {{n’avez pas de}} projets pour les vacances ? – Nous {{n’avons pas d’}}argent."},{"cue":"My favourite numbers are 57, 29, 48 and 62.","t":"Mes {{numéros préférés}} sont le {{cinquante-sept}}, le {{vingt-neuf}}, le {{quarante-huit}} et le {{soixante-deux}}."},{"cue":"Choose another name and other dates. – Really?","t":"{{Choisissez un autre}} nom et {{d’autres}} dates. – {{Vraiment}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"qu’est-ce que je vous sers ?","en":"what can I get you?","zh":"您要点什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonjour, qu’est-ce que je vous sers ?","exEn":"Good morning, what can I get you?","src":1},{"fr":"un café crème","en":"a white coffee (coffee with milk)","zh":"奶咖啡","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"Je veux un café crème, un croque-monsieur et deux billets de loto, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"I want a white coffee, a toasted ham-and-cheese sandwich and two lotto tickets, please.","src":2},{"fr":"deux billets de loto","en":"two lotto tickets","zh":"两张乐透彩票","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je veux un café crème, un croque-monsieur et deux billets de loto, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"I want a white coffee, a toasted ham-and-cheese sandwich and two lotto tickets, please.","src":2},{"fr":"vous pouvez jouer au loto en ligne","en":"you can play the lotto online","zh":"您可以在网上玩乐透","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez jouer au loto en ligne, vous savez. C’est très simple.","exEn":"You can play the lotto online, you know. It’s very simple.","src":3},{"fr":"il n’y a pas de queues au comptoir","en":"there are no queues at the counter","zh":"柜台前没有排队","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"queue = queue/tail, pronounced [kuh]","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et il n’y a pas de queues au comptoir, comme ce matin.","exEn":"And there are no queues at the counter, like this morning.","src":4},{"fr":"l’ambiance est sympa","en":"the atmosphere is nice","zh":"气氛很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ ambience; sympa = sympathique","ff":"sympa = nice, not “sympathetic”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je sais, mais je préfère venir ici : l’ambiance est sympa.","exEn":"I know, but I prefer to come here: the atmosphere is nice.","src":5},{"fr":"je préfère venir ici","en":"I prefer to come here","zh":"我更喜欢来这里","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ prefer","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je sais, mais je préfère venir ici : l’ambiance est sympa.","exEn":"I know, but I prefer to come here: the atmosphere is nice.","src":5},{"fr":"un client","en":"a customer","zh":"顾客","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ client","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai un client qui vient tous les jours à la même heure. Et il est toujours là quand je ferme !","exEn":"I have a customer who comes every day at the same time. And he’s still there when I close!","src":0},{"fr":"tout à fait","en":"absolutely, exactly","zh":"完全正确","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout à fait. Je joue toujours les mêmes numéros :","exEn":"Absolutely. I always play the same numbers:","src":9},{"fr":"je joue toujours les mêmes numéros","en":"I always play the same numbers","zh":"我总是买同样的号码","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"toujours also means “still”","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Tout à fait. Je joue toujours les mêmes numéros :","exEn":"Absolutely. I always play the same numbers:","src":9},{"fr":"ma date de naissance","en":"my date of birth","zh":"我的出生日期","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"ma date de naissance : le vingt-trois, douze, soixante-neuf,","exEn":"my birth date: the 23-12-69","src":11},{"fr":"vous réussissez à gagner ?","en":"do you manage to win?","zh":"您能成功赢钱吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avec ça, vous réussissez à gagner ? Je vous applaudis !","exEn":"With that, you manage to win? I applaud you!","src":13},{"fr":"j’investis pour l’avenir","en":"I’m investing for the future","zh":"我是在为未来投资","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ invest","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, pas vraiment, mais j’investis pour l’avenir. Je vais gagner un de ces jours !","exEn":"No, not really, but I’m investing for the future. I’m going to win one day!","src":14},{"fr":"je vais gagner un de ces jours","en":"I’m going to win one of these days","zh":"总有一天我会赢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, pas vraiment, mais j’investis pour l’avenir. Je vais gagner un de ces jours !","exEn":"No, not really, but I’m investing for the future. I’m going to win one day!","src":14}],"uncertain":["dialogue 7: the printed English (OCR) reads 'who come every day'; I translated the French fully as 'who come to play every day'.","culture box (ref '') appears at the end of lesson 23 in the scan (pages 111-112) and is placed here."],"pdfPage":105,"gaps":[[2.18,4.22],[6.28,9.18],[16.9,17.98],[69.3,72.66],[74.8,76.9],[81.8,84.98],[89.36,92.7],[95.32,98.52],[104.26,107.5],[110.24,114.5]],"dur":114.5},{"n":24,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je ne vais pas bien du tout","titleEn":"I’m not well at all","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Asseyez-vous, mademoiselle. Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ?","en":"Sit down, miss. What’s the matter?","lit":"what is-it-that not go not"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne vais pas bien du tout, docteur.","en":"I’m not well at all, doctor.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai mal à la tête, mal aux oreilles et mal au genou droit,","en":"I have a headache, an earache and a pain in my right knee,","lit":"bad at the head; bad at the ears; bad at"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’ai aussi de la fièvre. Est-ce que c’est grave ?","en":"and I also have a temperature. Is it serious?","lit":"the fever"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"En effet, vous avez l’air fatiguée. Vous n’êtes pas en forme.","en":"Indeed, you look tired. You’re not on form.","lit":"in effect; have the air"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais prendre votre température. Mm, vous avez trente-huit.","en":"I’m going to take your temperature. Mm, it’s 38 100.4°F.","lit":"you have"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un peu élevé. Maintenant, ouvrez la bouche. Fermez.","en":"That’s a little high. Now, open your mouth. Close.","lit":"the mouth"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais vérifier votre tension et écouter votre cœur. Respirez.","en":"I’m going to check your blood pressure and listen to your heart. Breathe.","lit":"tension"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous avez une bonne vieille grippe, c’est tout.","en":"You have a good old-fashioned flu, that’s all.","lit":"a good old"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vous donne trois médicaments, que vous devez prendre pendant huit jours.","en":"I’m giving you three drugs, which you must take for eight days.","lit":"medicines"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"D’abord, un sirop. Vous le prenez trois fois par jour : le matin, le midi et le soir.","en":"Firstly, a syrup. You take it three times a day: morning, noon and evening.","lit":"it take; by day"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite, des comprimés. Il faut les avaler avec un grand verre d’eau.","en":"Next, pills. You have to swallow them with a large glass of water.","lit":"some pills; them swallow"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Et enfin, il y a une gélule par jour. Vous la prenez avant le dîner.","en":"And lastly, there is one capsule a day. You take it after dinner.","lit":"it take"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Voici votre ordonnance et une feuille de maladie.","en":"Here is your prescription and a refund form.","lit":"sheet of illness"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"qu’est-ce qui is an interrogative expression referring to the subject of the sentence: Qu’est-ce qui est important pour vous ? What’s important for you? Compare it with qu’est-ce que, which refers to a direct object: Qu’est-ce que vous voulez ? What do you want?"},{"ref":"2","text":"We know that aller, to go, is used to talk about someone’s health (see lesson 1). Je vais bien, I’m well. The negative form, Je ne vais pas bien, means I’m not well. To ask what is wrong, a doctor will say Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ? What’s not right?, or, more colloquially, What’s the matter? / What’s wrong?"},{"ref":"3","text":"avoir mal (“to have bad”) is an intransitive expression used to talk about pain or discomfort. It is generally followed by à la (or au/aux) and the painful body part: J’ai mal au ventre (“I have bad to the stomach”), I have a stomach ache. The word order is often different to the English form: Elle a mal à la tête, She has a headache; Il a mal aux pieds, His feet hurt. (Note that there is no distinction between ache and hurt in French.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"une fièvre, literally a fever, is used in everyday speech, generally with de, to mean a high temperature: Mon fils a de la fièvre, My son has a (high) temperature. In a technical context, however, fièvre is translated by the cognate: la fièvre jaune, yellow fever. Note that the Fahrenheit scale is not used in most French-speaking countries (including Canada): all temperatures are in Celsius. So when the doctor says, as in line 6, Vous avez trente-huit (de fièvre), You have a temperature of 38°C, the equivalent in Fahrenheit would be 100.4°F."},{"ref":"5","text":"The masculine noun l’air means the air. But the meaning of the expression avoir l’air (de) is to look like or look as if: Marc a l’air fatigué, Marc looks tired. Elle a l’air d’une femme très sympa, She looks like a very nice woman."},{"ref":"6","text":"The direct object pronouns are me, me, te/vous, you, le/la, him/her/it, nous, us, and les, them. In contrast to English, they come before the verb: Je les aime, I love them. Nous la voyons, We see her. Remember, though, that there are no neutral pronouns in French, so the last sentence can also mean We see it, if the pronoun is replacing a feminine noun."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(1), (3), (9), (14) More about the double-l sound (see lesson 22). If -ll- is preceded and followed by a single vowel, it is pronounced simply as an [l] (mademoiselle, [mahduhmwazel]). When preceded by two vowels, it reverts to a palatised sound (like the y sound in boy in English), which we have represented here by [yy]. The pronunciation of the previous syllable depends on the vowel combination. Thus oreille and vieille are pronounced [ohrayy] and [vyayy], while feuille is pronounced [feuyy]. Listen to the difference between the vowel sounds in the last syllables of fatigué and oreille. Now play the entire audio track again, repeating aloud feuille, oreille and vieille until you feel comfortable. Then add them to your -ll- list."},{"ref":"","text":"Under France’s healthcare system (le système de santé), the cost of medical care is covered by the national social security scheme (la Sécurité sociale, often shortened to la Sécu). In most cases, people are free to choose un médecin traitant, or a general practitioner. Once a medical consultation is complete, the doctor fills in une feuille de soins (literally “a care sheet”), which is forwarded – usually electronically – to the local health insurance branch. Note that un médecin means a doctor (see lesson 17), whereas the word docteur is used only as a title (le Docteur Bellier, for example) or when addressing the practitioner directly: Bonjour docteur."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ? Vous n’allez pas bien ?","a":"What’s wrong? Aren’t you well?"},{"q":"Le docteur Lefebvre a l’air très fatigué.","a":"Dr Lefebvre looks very tired."},{"q":"Votre température est un peu élevée, c’est tout.","a":"Your temperature is a bit high, that’s all."},{"q":"Asseyez-vous, madame. Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous ?","a":"Sit down, madam. What can I do for you?"},{"q":"Comment va votre fils ? – Il est en forme.","a":"How is your son? – He’s on form."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Here’s the syrup. You take it twice a day.","t":"Voici le sirop. Vous {{le prenez}} deux {{fois par jour}}."},{"cue":"If you have pills, you have to swallow them with a glass of water.","t":"Si vous avez {{des comprimés}}, il faut {{les avaler}} avec un verre {{d’eau}}."},{"cue":"Marie is arriving in Paris this evening and we are seeing her tomorrow morning.","t":"Marie {{arrive à Paris}} ce soir et nous {{la voyons}} demain matin."},{"cue":"She has a headache, an earache and a pain in her right knee.","t":"Elle a {{mal à la tête}}, {{mal aux oreilles}} et {{mal au genou droit}}."},{"cue":"My son has a temperature this morning, but it’s not serious.","t":"Mon fils {{a de la fièvre}} ce matin, mais ce {{n’est pas grave}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"asseyez-vous","en":"sit down","zh":"请坐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Asseyez-vous, mademoiselle. Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ?","exEn":"Sit down, miss. What’s the matter?","src":1},{"fr":"qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ?","en":"what’s the matter?","zh":"哪里不舒服？出什么问题了？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Asseyez-vous, mademoiselle. Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ?","exEn":"Sit down, miss. What’s the matter?","src":1},{"fr":"je ne vais pas bien du tout","en":"I’m not well at all","zh":"我一点也不舒服","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne vais pas bien du tout, docteur.","exEn":"I’m not well at all, doctor.","src":2},{"fr":"j’ai mal à la tête","en":"I have a headache","zh":"我头疼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"J’ai mal à la tête, mal aux oreilles et mal au genou droit,","exEn":"I have a headache, an earache and a pain in my right knee,","src":3},{"fr":"mal au genou droit","en":"a pain in the right knee","zh":"右膝盖疼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"J’ai mal à la tête, mal aux oreilles et mal au genou droit,","exEn":"I have a headache, an earache and a pain in my right knee,","src":3},{"fr":"j’ai de la fièvre","en":"I have a temperature / fever","zh":"我发烧了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"fièvre ≈ fever","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et j’ai aussi de la fièvre. Est-ce que c’est grave ?","exEn":"and I also have a temperature. Is it serious?","src":4},{"fr":"Est-ce que c’est grave ?","en":"is it serious?","zh":"严重吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"grave ≈ grave (serious)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et j’ai aussi de la fièvre. Est-ce que c’est grave ?","exEn":"and I also have a temperature. Is it serious?","src":4},{"fr":"vous avez l’air fatiguée","en":"you look tired","zh":"您看起来很累","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"fatigué ≈ fatigued","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En effet, vous avez l’air fatiguée. Vous n’êtes pas en forme.","exEn":"Indeed, you look tired. You’re not on form.","src":5},{"fr":"vous n’êtes pas en forme","en":"you’re not on form / not feeling fit","zh":"您状态不好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En effet, vous avez l’air fatiguée. Vous n’êtes pas en forme.","exEn":"Indeed, you look tired. You’re not on form.","src":5},{"fr":"votre température","en":"your temperature","zh":"您的体温","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ temperature","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vais prendre votre température. Mm, vous avez trente-huit.","exEn":"I’m going to take your temperature. Mm, it’s 38 100.4°F.","src":6},{"fr":"une bonne vieille grippe","en":"a good old-fashioned flu","zh":"一场普通的流感","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous avez une bonne vieille grippe, c’est tout.","exEn":"You have a good old-fashioned flu, that’s all.","src":9},{"fr":"un médicament","en":"a drug, a medicine","zh":"药物","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ medicament","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vous donne trois médicaments, que vous devez prendre pendant huit jours.","exEn":"I’m giving you three drugs, which you must take for eight days.","src":10},{"fr":"un sirop","en":"a syrup","zh":"糖浆","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ syrup","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord, un sirop. Vous le prenez trois fois par jour : le matin, le midi et le soir.","exEn":"Firstly, a syrup. You take it three times a day: morning, noon and evening.","src":11},{"fr":"vous le prenez trois fois par jour","en":"you take it three times a day","zh":"您每天服用三次","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord, un sirop. Vous le prenez trois fois par jour : le matin, le midi et le soir.","exEn":"Firstly, a syrup. You take it three times a day: morning, noon and evening.","src":11},{"fr":"il faut les avaler avec un grand verre d’eau","en":"you have to swallow them with a large glass of water","zh":"必须用一大杯水送服","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ensuite, des comprimés. Il faut les avaler avec un grand verre d’eau.","exEn":"Next, pills. You have to swallow them with a large glass of water.","src":12},{"fr":"une ordonnance","en":"a prescription","zh":"处方","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"≈ ordinance, but means prescription","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “or-donuts”: the doctor hands you a prescription for a box of donuts.","ex":"Voici votre ordonnance et une feuille de maladie.","exEn":"Here is your prescription and a refund form.","src":14}],"uncertain":["ex1 item 1: OCR 'ne vas pas' corrected to 'ne va pas'.","culture box (ref '') belongs to the end of lesson 24 (page 116) and is placed here."],"pdfPage":111,"gaps":[[1.82,3.82],[5.26,8.38],[11.38,12.44],[23.46,24.5],[53.14,54.24],[63.38,64.4],[66.76,68.22],[72.8,74.02],[77.4,80.64],[82.64,84.88],[87.5,90.52],[93.58,96.68],[99.28,102.38],[105.64,108.66],[111.68,115.68]],"dur":115.7},{"n":25,"kind":"lesson","title":"Déjeunons ensemble","titleEn":"Let’s have lunch together","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est déjà midi ! J’ai faim, et vous ?","en":"It’s already noon. I am hungry. And you?","lit":"have hunger"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Un petit peu, mais je vous accompagne avec plaisir.","en":"A little bit. But I’ll come with you with pleasure.","lit":"accompany"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Je connais plusieurs restaurants dans le quartier :","en":"I know several restaurants in the neighbourhood:","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"il y a une pizzéria près d’ici, une crêperie dans la rue à gauche, ou une brasserie à droite de l’église.","en":"there’s a pizzeria near here, a pancake house in the street on the left or a brasserie to the right of the church.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Laquelle est-ce que vous préférez ?","en":"Which one do you prefer?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça m’est égal. Et vous ?","en":"It’s all the same to me. And you?","lit":"equal"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"La crêperie : elle est excellente et pas chère. Ça vous dit ?","en":"The pancake house. It’s excellent and not expensive. Does that tempt you?","lit":"that you speaks"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Où est-ce que c’est ? J’espère que ce n’est pas trop loin.","en":"Where is it? I hope that it’s not too far.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis assez pressé parce que j’ai un rendez-vous à quatorze heures.","en":"I’m in quite a hurry because I have an appointment at 2 pm.","lit":"14 hours"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, elle est à trois cents mètres d’ici. À seulement cinq minutes à pied.","en":"No, it’s 300 metres from here. Only 5 minutes on foot.","lit":"at; to foot"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"On va tout droit puis à droite et ensuite c’est la troisième rue à gauche.","en":"We go straight ahead then right and afterwards it’s the third street on the left.","lit":"one goes"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je viens de penser à quelque chose : elle est fermée le lundi.","en":"But I have just thought of something: it’s closed on Monday.","lit":"I came to think"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Allons à la brasserie, alors. Mais faisons vite.","en":"Let’s go to the brasserie, then. But let’s make it quick.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know that the second person singular and plural are used in the imperative (lesson 16 note 3). The same is true of the first person plural: Déjeunons, Let’s have lunch. Faisons vite, Let’s make it fast. (Remember that the final -s is silent.) See lesson 18."},{"ref":"2","text":"Most French words come from either Latin or Greek, whereas at least a third of English words are of Anglo-Saxon origin. Which means that one French word can often be translated in two ways. For example, accompagner is either to accompany or to go/come with. For many English speakers, used to short phrasal verbs, French can sound very formal. It takes time to get used to this “double” vocabulary, so let’s start now!"},{"ref":"3","text":"Don’t confuse un quartier, a district or neighbourhood of a city (lesson 21) with un quart, a quarter. The noun can also be used attributively: un restaurant de quartier, a neighbourhood restaurant. (It is also used to refer to a segment of fruit or a cut of meat.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"lequel (masc.), laquelle (fem.) and lesquels/lesquelles (plur.) are pronouns formed by combining the definite articles le/la/les and the interrogative adjectives quel/quelle/quels/quelles, meaning which/what. In this lesson and lesson 22, they are used as interrogatives: J’aime ces trois restaurants. – Lequel est-ce que vous préférez ? I like these three restaurants. – Which one do you prefer? Nous cherchons la crêperie. – Laquelle ? We’re looking for the pancake house. – Which one? We will see later how to use these compounds as pronouns."},{"ref":"5","text":"An idiom is an expression whose meaning can’t be guessed from its various parts. For example, Ça m’est égal (“It to me is equal”) is equivalent to It’s all the same to me or I don’t mind. Similarly, the interrogative Ça vous dit ? (literally: “that you speaks”) means something like Do you fancy that? Remember to make a special note of idioms and expressions whenever you come across them, because the meaning is not always obvious."},{"ref":"6","text":"The adjective droit has two meanings: right (the opposite of gauche, left) and straight. Le genou droit, the right knee; un chemin droit, a straight path. Of course, both adjectives agree with their nouns: l’épaule droite, the right shoulder; deux lignes droites, two straight lines. The expression tout droit, straight ahead, is an adverb and therefore invariable."},{"ref":"7","text":"venir de, to come from, followed by an infinitive, is an idiomatic way to express the immediate past. It is the translation of to have just + past participle in English: Je viens de voir Elsa, I’ve just seen Elsa. The expression is not used in the negative and is rarely found in the interrogative."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(3) Remember that French is syllable-timed, unlike English, where stress plays a much greater role. For example: restaurant has three evenly-pronounced syllables in French: [res-toh-ron] but only two in English: [res-tront]. Try not to put too much emphasis on any one syllable in French words, however long they may be."},{"ref":"","text":"Une brasserie is a full-service restaurant offering traditional French cuisine throughout the day, and often late into the night. The word actually means a brewery (from brasser, to brew), because brasseries once brewed their own beer.\nNote how the -ie suffix, which generally denotes a status or condition, is used to create nouns – most of them feminine – indicating an activity or profession: brasser → brasserie; une crêpe (a pancake) → une crêperie (a pancake house), un sandwich → une sandwicherie (a sandwich shop), etc. Similarly, many words for different kinds of businesses end in -erie: une boulangerie (a bakery), une fromagerie (a cheese shop), une crèmerie (a dairy), etc. These are just a few examples of how to expand your vocabulary from a corpus of basic words."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je ne suis pas pressée ; je n’ai pas de rendez-vous cet après-midi.","a":"I’m not in a hurry; I don’t have any appointments this afternoon."},{"q":"Ça vous dit, des fraises et de la crème fraîche ? – Ça m’est égal.","a":"Do you fancy some strawberries and fresh cream? – It’s all the same to me."},{"q":"Je ne vous accompagne pas à la crêperie, je n’ai pas faim.","a":"I’m not coming with you to the pancake house, I’m not hungry."},{"q":"Faisons vite. Nous sommes déjà en retard.","a":"Let’s make it quick. We’re already late."},{"q":"Ce n’est pas loin ; c’est seulement à deux minutes à pied.","a":"It is not far; it’s just two minutes on foot."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Go straight ahead, turn right and then left.","t":"Allez {{tout droit}}, tournez {{à droite}} et puis {{à gauche}}."},{"cue":"Are you hungry? – No, we’ve just eaten.","t":"Est-ce que {{vous avez faim}} ? – Non, nous {{venons de manger}}."},{"cue":"Michel knows several pizzerias. – Which one does he prefer?","t":"Michel {{connaît}} plusieurs pizzérias. – {{Laquelle est-ce qu’}}il préfère."},{"cue":"Is there a brasserie in the neighbourhood?","t":"{{Est-ce qu’il y a}} une brasserie dans {{le quartier}} ?"},{"cue":"We have sandwiches and croissants. Which ones do you prefer?","t":"Nous avons {{des}} sandwichs et {{des}} croissants. {{Lesquels est-ce}} que vous {{préférez}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai faim","en":"I’m hungry","zh":"我饿了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est déjà midi ! J’ai faim, et vous ?","exEn":"It’s already noon. I am hungry. And you?","src":1},{"fr":"je vous accompagne avec plaisir","en":"I’ll come with you with pleasure","zh":"我很乐意陪您去","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"accompagner ≈ accompany","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un petit peu, mais je vous accompagne avec plaisir.","exEn":"A little bit. But I’ll come with you with pleasure.","src":2},{"fr":"je connais plusieurs restaurants","en":"I know several restaurants","zh":"我知道好几家餐厅","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je connais plusieurs restaurants dans le quartier :","exEn":"I know several restaurants in the neighbourhood:","src":3},{"fr":"près d’ici","en":"near here","zh":"离这里很近","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il y a une pizzéria près d’ici, une crêperie dans la rue à gauche, ou une brasserie à droite de l’église.","exEn":"there’s a pizzeria near here, a pancake house in the street on the left or a brasserie to the right of the church.","src":4},{"fr":"à gauche","en":"on / to the left","zh":"在左边","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il y a une pizzéria près d’ici, une crêperie dans la rue à gauche, ou une brasserie à droite de l’église.","exEn":"there’s a pizzeria near here, a pancake house in the street on the left or a brasserie to the right of the church.","src":4},{"fr":"tout droit","en":"straight ahead","zh":"一直往前","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On va tout droit puis à droite et ensuite c’est la troisième rue à gauche.","exEn":"We go straight ahead then right and afterwards it’s the third street on the left.","src":11},{"fr":"Laquelle est-ce que vous préférez ?","en":"which one do you prefer?","zh":"您更喜欢哪一个？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Laquelle est-ce que vous préférez ?","exEn":"Which one do you prefer?","src":5},{"fr":"ça m’est égal","en":"I don’t mind / it’s all the same to me","zh":"我无所谓","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"égal ≈ equal","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça m’est égal. Et vous ?","exEn":"It’s all the same to me. And you?","src":6},{"fr":"Ça vous dit ?","en":"do you fancy that?","zh":"您觉得怎么样？有兴趣吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La crêperie : elle est excellente et pas chère. Ça vous dit ?","exEn":"The pancake house. It’s excellent and not expensive. Does that tempt you?","src":7},{"fr":"elle est excellente et pas chère","en":"it’s excellent and not expensive","zh":"非常好而且不贵","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"excellente ≈ excellent","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La crêperie : elle est excellente et pas chère. Ça vous dit ?","exEn":"The pancake house. It’s excellent and not expensive. Does that tempt you?","src":7},{"fr":"j’espère que ce n’est pas trop loin","en":"I hope it’s not too far","zh":"我希望不太远","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Où est-ce que c’est ? J’espère que ce n’est pas trop loin.","exEn":"Where is it? I hope that it’s not too far.","src":8},{"fr":"je suis assez pressé","en":"I’m in quite a hurry","zh":"我挺赶时间的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis assez pressé parce que j’ai un rendez-vous à quatorze heures.","exEn":"I’m in quite a hurry because I have an appointment at 2 pm.","src":9},{"fr":"à cinq minutes à pied","en":"five minutes on foot","zh":"步行五分钟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, elle est à trois cents mètres d’ici. À seulement cinq minutes à pied.","exEn":"No, it’s 300 metres from here. Only 5 minutes on foot.","src":10},{"fr":"je viens de penser à quelque chose","en":"I’ve just thought of something","zh":"我刚想到一件事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais je viens de penser à quelque chose : elle est fermée le lundi.","exEn":"But I have just thought of something: it’s closed on Monday.","src":12},{"fr":"faisons vite","en":"let’s make it quick","zh":"我们快点吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allons à la brasserie, alors. Mais faisons vite.","exEn":"Let’s go to the brasserie, then. But let’s make it quick.","src":13}],"uncertain":["culture box (ref '') appears after lesson 25's answers (page 121) and is placed here.","pron note: respelling of restaurant given as [res-toh-ron] (OCR used a nasal symbol)."],"pdfPage":115,"gaps":[[2.2,4.18],[5.78,8.82],[26.6,27.9],[29.28,30.38],[50.84,51.88],[66.32,69.98],[72.36,74.34],[78.82,81.76],[85.72,88.82],[91.98,94.88],[98.18,101],[103.96,108.3]],"dur":108.3},{"n":26,"kind":"lesson","title":"Déjeunons ensemble (suite)","titleEn":"Let’s have lunch together (continued)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Avez-vous une table pour deux s’il vous plaît ?","en":"Do you have a table for two, please?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Si vous voulez bien me suivre ! Est-ce que cette table vous convient ?","en":"Follow me, please. Does this table suit you?","lit":"if you want well me to follow"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Non. Je veux être près de la fenêtre parce que j’ai chaud.","en":"No it doesn’t. I want to be close to the window because I am hot.","lit":"have hot"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Asseyez-vous ici. Je vous apporte le menu et la carte des vins.","en":"Sit here. I’ll bring you the menu and the wine list.","lit":"card"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’arrive tout de suite… Alors, vous êtes prêts ?","en":"I’ll be right there… So, are you ready?","lit":"I arrive all of following"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Quel menu prenez-vous : celui à vingt euros ou celui à quarante ?","en":"Which menu are you taking: the one at 20 euros or the one at 40?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Le menu à vingt. Quel est le plat du jour ? Sinon, quelles sont les spécialités ?","en":"The menu at 20. What is the dish of the day? If not, what are your specials?","lit":"specialities"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Il n’y en a pas. Je vous conseille de prendre l’entrecôte ou le saumon froid.","en":"We’re out. I advise you to take the rib steak or the cold salmon.","lit":"there aren’t any; between-rib"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Dans ce cas, deux entrecôtes s’il vous plaît.","en":"In that case, two rib steaks, please.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Quelle cuisson pour la viande, et avec quels légumes ?","en":"How do you want the meat cooked? And with which vegetables?","lit":"what cooking for the meat"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Saignante pour tous les deux, avec des frites.","en":"Rare for the two of us, with chips.","lit":"bleeding"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Voulez-vous boire quelque chose ? Du vin ou de la bière ?","en":"Do you want something to drink? Wine or beer?","lit":"to drink something; some wine"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Juste une bouteille d’eau minérale. Nous avons soif. […]","en":"Just a bottle of mineral water. We are thirsty. …","lit":"have thirst"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"L’addition s’il vous plaît. Est-ce que le service est compris ?","en":"The bill, please. Is the service included?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, mais pas le pourboire.","en":"Yes, but not the tip.","lit":"for-drink"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The verb suivre, to follow, is the root of la suite, the continuation (i.e. that which follows). The expression Si vous voulez bien me suivre is a formal way of asking someone to follow you. In a less formal setting, the server might say Suivez-moi (Follow me) as they lead you to your table. It’s important to recognise the register, or level of formality, that is being used in order to respond accordingly."},{"ref":"2","text":"convenir is another rather formal verb, meaning to suit, to be convenient/suitable for, etc. For the time being, just remember the interrogative expression Est-ce que ça vous convient ?, Does that suit you? or, more idiomatically, Is that okay with you? And the response Ça me convient / Ça ne me convient pas, That’s fine / That doesn’t suit me. Remember that idiomatic phrases can be translated in different ways, depending on the context, but the basic meaning is the same."},{"ref":"3","text":"As we know, the verb avoir is used with several expressions whose English equivalents are formed with to be (J’ai trente ans, I am thirty). Similar constructions include avoir faim, to be hungry (lesson 25), avoir soif, to be thirsty, avoir chaud, to be hot, and avoir froid, to be cold."},{"ref":"4","text":"Remember that the present simple tense in French can express an immediate future (see lesson 10 note 4). Je vous apporte la carte, I’ll bring you the menu."},{"ref":"5","text":"We saw in note 1 that la suite, from suivre, to follow, means a continuation. But tout de suite is an idiomatic expression meaning immediately, at once. Faites-le tout de suite, Do it straight away. Note the pronunciation [toot-sweet]."},{"ref":"6","text":"In the previous lesson we learned a group of compound pronouns (lequel, etc.). The root word is the interrogative adjective quel, what or, when used discriminatively, which, and its feminine and plural forms: quel plat … ?, which dish, quelle carte … ?, which card/menu, quels légumes … ?, which vegetables, quelles tables … ?, which tables. All four adjectives are pronounced identically [kel], unless the following noun starts with a vowel, in which case the two are liaised: quelles églises [kelz-ayg-leez]."},{"ref":"7","text":"le sang (pron. [sahn]), blood, is the root of the verb saigner, to bleed, and the adjective saignant (fem. saignante) which literally means bleeding but, when applied to a steak, signifies rare."},{"ref":"8","text":"une addition, literally “an addition,” also means a bill in the context of food service. L’addition, s’il vous plaît. The bill, please. A common synonym is la note (pron. [not]). The word for an invoice is une facture."},{"ref":"","text":"Most restaurants in France offer both une carte, a full-service menu (hence the expression manger à la carte, to eat à la carte), and un menu (literally “thin” or “slim”), a fixed-price or “slimmed-down” menu. Most menus feature un plat du jour, a dish of the day. And since the wine list describes the full selection of bottles on offer, rather than a short selection, the term is la carte (not le menu) des vins. Lastly, French restaurants include a service charge (le service) in their menu prices (service compris, service “understood,” i.e. included), but it is customary to leave a tip (un pourboire, literally “for-drink”) if the dining experience is satisfactory."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Cette table ne me convient pas. Elle est trop petite.","a":"This table doesn’t suit me. It’s too small."},{"q":"Et je ne veux pas être près de la fenêtre. J’ai froid.","a":"And I don’t want to be close to the window. I’m cold."},{"q":"Puis-je avoir la carte des vins ? – J’arrive tout de suite.","a":"May I have the wine list? – I’ll be right there."},{"q":"Une entrecôte s’il vous plaît. – Il n’y en a pas.","a":"A rib steak please. – We’re out."},{"q":"Il me conseille de ne pas prendre le saumon.","a":"He’s advising me not to take the salmon."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"With what vegetables can I accompany the meat?","t":"Avec {{quels légumes est-ce que}} je {{peux}} accompagner la viande ?"},{"cue":"We are hungry and we are thirsty. – Follow me.","t":"Nous {{avons faim}} et nous {{avons soif}}. – {{Suivez-moi}}."},{"cue":"Are you coming with me? – I’m not ready.","t":"{{Est-ce que}} vous {{venez}} avec moi ? – Je {{ne suis pas prêt}}."},{"cue":"What is the speciality of your restaurant? And what are the prices?","t":"{{Quelle est}} la spécialité de votre restaurant ? Et {{quels sont}} les prix ?"},{"cue":"Do you want something to eat? What’s your favourite dish?","t":"{{Voulez-vous}} manger {{quelque chose}} ? {{Quel est}} votre plat préféré ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"une table pour deux","en":"a table for two","zh":"一张两人桌","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"table ≈ table","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avez-vous une table pour deux s’il vous plaît ?","exEn":"Do you have a table for two, please?","src":1},{"fr":"si vous voulez bien me suivre","en":"please follow me","zh":"请跟我来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si vous voulez bien me suivre ! Est-ce que cette table vous convient ?","exEn":"Follow me, please. Does this table suit you?","src":2},{"fr":"cette table vous convient ?","en":"does this table suit you?","zh":"这张桌子合适吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si vous voulez bien me suivre ! Est-ce que cette table vous convient ?","exEn":"Follow me, please. Does this table suit you?","src":2},{"fr":"j’ai chaud","en":"I’m hot","zh":"我觉得热","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"j’ai chaud = I am hot (avoir, not être)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non. Je veux être près de la fenêtre parce que j’ai chaud.","exEn":"No it doesn’t. I want to be close to the window because I am hot.","src":3},{"fr":"je vous apporte le menu","en":"I’ll bring you the menu","zh":"我给您拿菜单来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Asseyez-vous ici. Je vous apporte le menu et la carte des vins.","exEn":"Sit here. I’ll bring you the menu and the wine list.","src":4},{"fr":"la carte des vins","en":"the wine list","zh":"酒单","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"carte = list/menu; le menu = fixed-price set menu","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"Asseyez-vous ici. Je vous apporte le menu et la carte des vins.","exEn":"Sit here. I’ll bring you the menu and the wine list.","src":4},{"fr":"j’arrive tout de suite","en":"I’ll be right there","zh":"我马上就来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’arrive tout de suite… Alors, vous êtes prêts ?","exEn":"I’ll be right there… So, are you ready?","src":5},{"fr":"Quel est le plat du jour ?","en":"what is the dish of the day?","zh":"今日特色菜是什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le menu à vingt. Quel est le plat du jour ? Sinon, quelles sont les spécialités ?","exEn":"The menu at 20. What is the dish of the day? If not, what are your specials?","src":7},{"fr":"je vous conseille de prendre l’entrecôte","en":"I advise you to take the rib steak","zh":"我建议您点肋眼牛排","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"conseiller ≈ counsel","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"Il n’y en a pas. Je vous conseille de prendre l’entrecôte ou le saumon froid.","exEn":"We’re out. I advise you to take the rib steak or the cold salmon.","src":8},{"fr":"deux entrecôtes s’il vous plaît","en":"two rib steaks, please","zh":"请来两份肋眼牛排","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dans ce cas, deux entrecôtes s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"In that case, two rib steaks, please.","src":9},{"fr":"Quelle cuisson pour la viande ?","en":"how do you want the meat cooked?","zh":"肉要几分熟？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"Quelle cuisson pour la viande, et avec quels légumes ?","exEn":"How do you want the meat cooked? And with which vegetables?","src":10},{"fr":"voulez-vous boire quelque chose ?","en":"do you want something to drink?","zh":"您想喝点什么吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voulez-vous boire quelque chose ? Du vin ou de la bière ?","exEn":"Do you want something to drink? Wine or beer?","src":12},{"fr":"une bouteille d’eau minérale","en":"a bottle of mineral water","zh":"一瓶矿泉水","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"bouteille ≈ bottle; minérale ≈ mineral","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"Juste une bouteille d’eau minérale. Nous avons soif.","exEn":"Just a bottle of mineral water. We are thirsty.","src":13},{"fr":"nous avons soif","en":"we are thirsty","zh":"我们渴了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Juste une bouteille d’eau minérale. Nous avons soif.","exEn":"Just a bottle of mineral water. We are thirsty.","src":13},{"fr":"L’addition s’il vous plaît","en":"the bill, please","zh":"请结账","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"addition = the bill, not just “addition”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’addition s’il vous plaît. Est-ce que le service est compris ?","exEn":"The bill, please. Is the service included?","src":14},{"fr":"le pourboire","en":"the tip","zh":"小费","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"sounds like “poor bwar”: a poor man bwar-ing (drinking) away the tip you give him “pour boire”.","ex":"Oui, mais pas le pourboire.","exEn":"Yes, but not the tip.","src":15}],"uncertain":["dialogue 8: English 'We’re out' is the book’s translation; the literal gloss in brackets is 'there aren’t any'.","culture box (ref '') appears after lesson 26's answers (page 126) and is placed here."],"pdfPage":121,"gaps":[[2.2,4.22],[6.52,8.72],[25.66,26.68],[27.74,28.88],[30.38,31.8],[38.38,39.38],[40.36,41.38],[67.78,68.92],[70.04,71.04],[74.86,77.9],[79.7,81.46],[85.12,88.38],[91.4,94.4],[97.32,100.5],[103.72,106.74],[108.66,112.7]],"dur":112.7},{"n":27,"kind":"lesson","title":"« Hôtel complet »","titleEn":"“No vacancies”","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Quels hôtels sont situés à proximité de l’aéroport ?","en":"What hotels are located close to the airport?","lit":"situated; at proximity"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon vol part très tôt le samedi matin et je n’ai pas envie de le rater.","en":"My flight leaves very early on Saturday morning and I don’t want to miss it.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi ne pas faire une recherche sur Internet ?","en":"Why not search online?","lit":"make a research on internet"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonne idée ! Je me connecte.","en":"Good idea! I’ll log on.","lit":"me connect"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y a un hôtel trois étoiles juste à côté du terminal et un autre à deux kilomètres.","en":"There’s a three-star hotel just next to the terminal and another two kilometres away.","lit":"at"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Regardons celui qui est le plus près. Il s’appelle l’Hôtel des voyageurs.","en":"Let’s look at the one which is the nearest. It’s called the Travellers’ Hotel.","lit":"most near"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Voici une chambre supérieure : qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","en":"Here’s a superior room: what do you think of it?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Je préfère celles au premier étage : elles ont l’air plus confortables.","en":"I prefer those on the first floor: they look more comfortable.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Celle qui est à côté de l’ascenseur est moins chère que les autres","en":"The one which is next to the lift is less expensive than the others","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"mais je parie qu’elle est plus bruyante.","en":"but I bet that it’s noisier.","lit":"more noisy"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Celles au rez-de-chaussée sont petites, à mon avis","en":"Those on the ground floor are small, in my opinion","lit":"at my advice"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"mais les équipements sont identiques à ceux des chambres supérieures","en":"but the facilities are identical to those of the superior rooms","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"et les tarifs ne sont pas les mêmes. C’est beaucoup moins cher.","en":"and the rates are not the same. It’s much less expensive.","lit":"tariffs"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce qu’il y a des chambres disponibles pour le vendredi soir ?","en":"Are there any rooms available for the Friday night?","lit":"evening"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Mince alors ! Tout est complet. Je vais regarder le deuxième hôtel.","en":"Drat! Everything is full. I’ll look at the second hotel.","lit":"thin then"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Be careful of the verb voler, which means to fly but also to steal. Thus un vol means either a flight or a theft, but hopefully the context will make the meaning clear!"},{"ref":"2","text":"In the transitive form, rater means to miss (a flight, an opportunity, etc.). Ne ratez pas votre avion ! Don’t miss your plane! It does not, however, have the same emotional connotation of loss or absence as the English verb. For this we use another verb, manquer, which we will see later on."},{"ref":"3","text":"The pronouns celui (masc. sing.), celle (fem. sing.), ceux (masc. plur., see lesson 23) and celles (fem. plur.) are similar to the one/the ones (or those) in English, and perform the same function of avoiding the repetition of a noun. Il y a deux hôtels : celui près de l’aéroport est cher. There are two hotels: the one near the airport is expensive."},{"ref":"4","text":"un avis (note the pronunciation: [un-ah-vee]) shares the same root as the English word advice (a credit or debit advice from a bank is un avis de débit/crédit). But the most common meaning is an opinion: Donnez-nous votre avis, Give us your opinion. The word is frequently used in expressions with the possessive adjectives: à mon avis, in my opinion, à son avis, in his or her opinion, etc. The plural is the same as the singular: Les avis sont partagés, Opinions are divided."},{"ref":"5","text":"As we saw in lesson 15 note 6, some nouns are singular in English but plural in French, and vice versa. The collective masculine noun l’équipement means equipment, but, when used in the plural, les équipements has a much broader meaning, similar to facilities or amenities."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(1), (5) Although the initial h of hôtel is silent, it is a “voiceless vowel,” so the rules governing liaisons apply. Thus the final s of quels runs into the o of hôtel: [kelzohtel]. The same applies to the singular: quel hôtel: [kelohtel], un hôtel: [unohtel]."},{"ref":"","text":"Mince alors ! Like every language, French has many euphemisms that stand in for vulgarisms and swear words. This is especially true of exclamations and expletives. We already know Zut (see lesson 10). Now meet mince, an adjective meaning thin or slender. Used as a polite expletive, however, it replaces a much stronger word, merde, equivalent in meaning to (but slightly less offensive than) shit. The reason for learning this kind of vocabulary is that many native speakers use such words on a daily basis, so it is important to be aware of them. However, we strongly recommend that you avoid using them, at least until you feel totally comfortable in French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Pourquoi ne pas prendre un hôtel à proximité de l’aéroport ?","a":"Why not take a hotel close to the airport?"},{"q":"Bonne idée ! Je vais faire une recherche sur Internet.","a":"Good idea! I’ll do a search online."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que vous pensez de mon idée ?","a":"What do you think of my idea?"},{"q":"Mon bureau est au rez-de-chaussée, près de l’ascenseur.","a":"My office is on the ground floor, near the lift."},{"q":"Est-ce que vous avez deux chambres disponibles pour le samedi vingt-six ?","a":"Do you have two rooms available for Saturday the twenty-sixth?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The rooms on the ground floor are identical to those on the first floor.","t":"Les chambres {{au rez-de-chaussée}} sont {{identiques}} à {{celles}} au premier étage."},{"cue":"In my opinion, the facilities are the same as those in the hotel next to the airport.","t":"{{À mon avis}}, les {{équipements}} sont les mêmes que {{ceux}} de l’hôtel {{à côté de}} l’aéroport."},{"cue":"We prefer the office on the second floor. It looks more comfortable.","t":"{{Nous préférons}} le bureau {{au deuxième étage}}. Il {{a l’air}} plus {{confortable}}."},{"cue":"For the menu, do you want the thirty-euro one or the forty-euro one?","t":"Pour le menu, vous voulez {{celui}} à {{trente}} euros ou {{celui}} à {{quarante}} euros ?"},{"cue":"Which of these two bottles do you prefer: the one on the left or the one on the right?","t":"{{Laquelle}} de ces deux bouteilles préférez-vous : {{celle}} de {{gauche}} ou {{celle}} de {{droite}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"à proximité de l’aéroport","en":"close to the airport","zh":"在机场附近","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"proximité ≈ proximity; aéroport ≈ airport","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Quels hôtels sont situés à proximité de l’aéroport ?","exEn":"What hotels are located close to the airport?","src":1},{"fr":"mon vol part très tôt","en":"my flight leaves very early","zh":"我的航班起飞很早","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un vol = a flight OR a theft","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon vol part très tôt le samedi matin et je n’ai pas envie de le rater.","exEn":"My flight leaves very early on Saturday morning and I don’t want to miss it.","src":2},{"fr":"je n’ai pas envie de le rater","en":"I don’t want to miss it","zh":"我不想错过它","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon vol part très tôt le samedi matin et je n’ai pas envie de le rater.","exEn":"My flight leaves very early on Saturday morning and I don’t want to miss it.","src":2},{"fr":"faire une recherche sur Internet","en":"to search online","zh":"上网搜索","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"recherche ≈ research","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pourquoi ne pas faire une recherche sur Internet ?","exEn":"Why not search online?","src":3},{"fr":"je me connecte","en":"I log on / I connect","zh":"我登录／上网","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ connect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonne idée ! Je me connecte.","exEn":"Good idea! I’ll log on.","src":4},{"fr":"un hôtel trois étoiles","en":"a three-star hotel","zh":"三星级酒店","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"hôtel ≈ hotel; étoile ≈ stellar","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il y a un hôtel trois étoiles juste à côté du terminal et un autre à deux kilomètres.","exEn":"There’s a three-star hotel just next to the terminal and another two kilometres away.","src":5},{"fr":"une chambre supérieure","en":"a superior room","zh":"高级客房","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ superior","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici une chambre supérieure : qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","exEn":"Here’s a superior room: what do you think of it?","src":7},{"fr":"qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","en":"what do you think of it?","zh":"您觉得怎么样？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici une chambre supérieure : qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","exEn":"Here’s a superior room: what do you think of it?","src":7},{"fr":"l’ascenseur (m)","en":"the lift / elevator","zh":"电梯","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ass-on-sir”: picture a sir riding an ass up in the lift.","ex":"Celle qui est à côté de l’ascenseur est moins chère que les autres","exEn":"The one which is next to the lift is less expensive than the others","src":9},{"fr":"je parie qu’elle est plus bruyante","en":"I bet it’s noisier","zh":"我敢打赌它更吵","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais je parie qu’elle est plus bruyante.","exEn":"but I bet that it’s noisier.","src":10},{"fr":"le rez-de-chaussée","en":"the ground floor","zh":"底层","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"le premier étage = first floor above ground","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Celles au rez-de-chaussée sont petites, à mon avis","exEn":"Those on the ground floor are small, in my opinion","src":11},{"fr":"à mon avis","en":"in my opinion","zh":"我认为","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"avis = opinion, not “advice”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Celles au rez-de-chaussée sont petites, à mon avis","exEn":"Those on the ground floor are small, in my opinion","src":11},{"fr":"les équipements","en":"the facilities, amenities","zh":"设施","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ equipment","ff":"plural = facilities, not just “equipment”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais les équipements sont identiques à ceux des chambres supérieures","exEn":"but the facilities are identical to those of the superior rooms","src":12},{"fr":"des chambres disponibles","en":"available rooms","zh":"有空的房间","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"disponible ≈ disposable/available","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce qu’il y a des chambres disponibles pour le vendredi soir ?","exEn":"Are there any rooms available for the Friday night?","src":14},{"fr":"Mince alors !","en":"drat! (mild exclamation)","zh":"哎呀！糟糕！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mince alors ! Tout est complet. Je vais regarder le deuxième hôtel.","exEn":"Drat! Everything is full. I’ll look at the second hotel.","src":15},{"fr":"tout est complet","en":"everything is full (no vacancies)","zh":"全都客满了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mince alors ! Tout est complet. Je vais regarder le deuxième hôtel.","exEn":"Drat! Everything is full. I’ll look at the second hotel.","src":15}],"uncertain":["pron note: 'voiceless vowel' kept as printed (OCR); respelling of 'un avis' / 'un hôtel' approximated as [un-ah-vee] / [unohtel].","line 11 English: OCR had 'really small'; French only says 'petites', so I wrote 'small'.","culture box 'Mince alors !' (page 131) belongs to the end of lesson 27 and is placed here."],"pdfPage":125,"gaps":[[1.9,3.94],[4.94,6.08],[9.52,10.68],[34.3,35.48],[71.58,75.38],[77.76,79.8],[82.54,85.72],[89.7,92.76],[94.38,97.6],[100.76,103.72],[107.72,111.5]],"dur":111.5},{"n":28,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"André joue à la loterie chaque semaine pour devenir très riche.","en":"André plays the lottery every week to become very rich.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Il me dit qu’il lit le journal et réfléchit pendant quelques minutes.","en":"He tells me that he reads the paper and thinks for a few minutes.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite il choisit ses numéros – mais ce sont toujours les mêmes !","en":"Then he chooses his numbers – but they are always the same.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Et vous, comment est-ce que vous les choisissez ?","en":"And you, how do you choose them?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Je joue les dates des anniversaires : celui de ma femme – je ne l’oublie pas – et ceux de mes deux fils.","en":"I play birthdays: that of my wife – I never forget it – and those of my two sons.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je choisis aussi des dates de mariage : celui de ma fille et celui de ma mère.","en":"I also play wedding dates: that of my daughter and my mother.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Attendez : je finis de remplir la grille de jeu. Voilà.","en":"Wait: I’ll finish filling in my game grid. There it is.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant j’ai faim et j’ai soif : allons déjeuner ensemble.","en":"Now I’m hungry and thirsty: let’s go and have lunch together.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je connais une bonne pizzéria et aussi un restaurant breton dans le quartier : lequel préférez-vous ?","en":"I know a good pizzeria and also a Breton restaurant in the neighbourhood: which do you prefer?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça m’est égal. Disons celui qui ne me donne pas mal au ventre.","en":"I don’t mind. Let’s say the one that doesn’t give me a stomach ache.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis tout à fait d’accord avec vous.","en":"I absolutely agree with you.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 The second group of verbs: -ir","text":"Most verbs terminating in -ir form the second largest group of regular verbs, after the -er group seen in lesson 7. The conjugation endings are added to the stem, obtained by dropping the infinitive ending. Here are two very common -ir verbs:\n\nchoisir, to choose\nje choisis – I choose\ntu* choisis – you choose\nil/elle** choisit – he/she/it chooses\nnous choisissons – we choose\nvous choisissez – you choose\nils/elles choisissent – they choose\n\n* We will start using the familiar form, le tutoiement, in the dialogues from lesson 29 onwards.\n** The third person singular is also used with the third person singular impersonal pronoun on. This is true for all verbs.\n\nfinir, to finish\nje finis – I finish\ntu finis – you finish\nil/elle finit – he/she/it finishes\nnous finissons – we finish\nvous finissez – you finish\nils/elles finissent – they finish\n\nAs always, the final consonant (or double consonant) is silent. Note also that the nous and vous forms have an extra syllable (je choisis, nous choisissons; tu finis, vous finissez).\n\nThe negative is formed in the usual way, with ne … pas: je ne finis pas, vous ne finissez pas, etc.\n\nThe interrogatives follow the same pattern (rising intonation, est-ce que, inversion) as for -er verbs:\nVous finissez votre café ?\nEst-ce que vous finissez votre café ? Are you finishing your coffee?\nFinissez-vous votre café ?\n(Remember that the inverted interrogative is very formal and rarely used in everyday speech.)\n\nThe distinguishing feature of regular second group verbs is a present participle ending in -issant (finissant, finishing, etc.). This is important to remember because some -ir verbs actually belong to the third group. We will discuss these later."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Direct object pronouns","text":"Here is the full table of direct object pronouns:\nme* – me\nte* – you\nle / la* – him/her/it\nnous – us\nvous – you\nles – them\n\n* The final vowel is elided if the next word in the sentence starts with a vowel.\n\nThe main difference with English is that these pronouns come before the verb, not after it.\nJe le veux, I want it.\nNous les achetons à l’aéroport, We buy them at the airport.\nJe l’écoute tous les jours, I listen to him/her/it every day.\n\nBecause of the elision, it is important to know the context of the sentence (in our example, je l’écoute, the direct object pronoun could refer to a proper or personal noun that is feminine (la radio, the radio, la chanteuse, the singer) or masculine (le bruit, the noise, le guide, the guide).\n\nFinally, in a construction with an auxiliary like pouvoir or vouloir followed by an infinitive, the pronoun is placed between the two:\nJe peux le voir, I can see him/it.\nNous voulons vous aider, We want to help you."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Interrogative adjectives and pronouns","text":"3.1 Quel … ?\nQuel can be used with a noun, in which case it serves as an adjective, or as a pronoun to replace a noun:\nquel (masc. sing.), quelle (fem. sing.) – what/which\nquels (masc. plu.), quelles (fem. plu.) – what/which\n\nQuel âge a votre enfant ? How old is your child?\nQuelle heure est-il ? What time is it?\nQuels sont vos projets ? What are your plans?\nQuelles expositions sont intéressantes ? Which exhibitions are interesting?\n\nOf course, the final plural -s is silent unless quels/quelles comes before a word starting with a vowel: quels enfants [kelz-ahn-fahn], quelles expositions [kelz-ek-spo-zee-syon] or with an h: quels hôtels [kelzohtel].\n\n3.2 Lequel … ?\nQuel is used to ask questions about a large group of things or people, but if the group is smaller or the choice relates to a predefined group, the adjective is combined with the definite article:\nlequel (masc. sing.), laquelle (fem. sing.) – which one(s)\nlesquels (masc. plu.), lesquelles (fem. plu.) – which one(s)\n\nLequel de ces hôtels préférez-vous ? Which of these hotels do you prefer?\nLaquelle des deux montres voulez-vous ? Which of these two watches do you want?\nPrendre des comprimés, d’accord, mais lesquels ? Take pills, okay, but which ones?\nJ’ai des grandes et des petites bananes. – Lesquelles sont les moins chères ? I have large and small bananas. – Which are the least expensive?"},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Demonstrative pronouns: celui","text":"This family of pronouns is used to refer to something that has been mentioned previously, thus avoiding repetition:\ncelui (masc. sing.), celle (fem. sing.) – the one(s), those\nceux (masc. plu.), celles (fem. plu.) – the one(s), those\n\nQuel chemin prendre : celui de droite ou celui de gauche ? Which path to take, the one on the right or the one on the left?\nLes deux chambres sont chères, mais celle du premier étage n’est pas très confortable. Both rooms are expensive but the one on the first floor isn’t very comfortable.\nEt celles du rez-de-chaussée sont petites. And the ones on the ground floor are small.\nLes menus des crêperies sont moins chers que ceux des brasseries. The menus at pancake houses are cheaper than those at brasseries."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Avoir instead of “to be”","text":"Many expressions describing an emotional or physical state are formed with an auxiliary and a descriptive word. Whereas the majority of such expressions in English use to be and an adjective (I’m hungry), French uses avoir and a noun (J’ai faim). Here are some more examples:\n\navoir faim: to be hungry\n– Avez-vous faim ? – Are you hungry?\navoir soif: to be thirsty\n– J’ai très soif. – I’m really thirsty.\navoir chaud: to be hot\n– Avez-vous chaud ? – Are you hot?\navoir froid: to be cold\n– Il n’a pas froid. – He’s not cold.\navoir raison: to be right\n– Vous avez raison ! – You’re right!\navoir tort: to be wrong\n– Ils n’ont pas tort. – They’re not wrong.\navoir peur: to be afraid\n– Je n’ai pas peur. – I’m not afraid.\navoir sommeil: to be sleepy\n– Elle a sommeil. – She’s sleepy.\navoir besoin (de): to need\n– Nous n’avons pas besoin de vous. – We don’t need you.\navoir de la chance: to be lucky\n– Ils ont vraiment de la chance ! – They’re really lucky.\n\nSince the descriptive word in all these expressions is a noun, not an adjective, there is no need for agreement in gender or number: J’ai raison, Vous avez raison."},{"ref":"rev","title":"6 Numbers and figures: cardinals","text":"Let’s revise the cardinal numbers, which you have assimilated naturally by reading them at the foot of each page. (If you’ve forgotten to do so, read on and then go back to the beginning of the book!)\n\nun 1\ndeux 2\ntrois 3\nquatre 4\ncinq 5\nsix 6\nsept 7\nhuit 8\nneuf 9\ndix 10\nonze 11\ndouze 12\ntreize 13\nquatorze 14\nquinze 15\nseize 16\ndix-sept 17\ndix-huit 18\ndix-neuf 19\nvingt 20\ntrente 30\nquarante 40\ncinquante 50\nsoixante 60\nsoixante-dix 70\nquatre-vingts 80\nquatre-vingt-dix 90\ncent 100\n\nTo form numbers above 20, use the multiple of ten followed by the second digit, e.g. 66 = soixante-six. However, this rule does not apply to 70, 80 and 90, which are expressed in base-20 rather than base-10:\n– 70 is 60+10 (soixante-dix), so soixante et onze (71), soixante-douze (72), soixante-treize (73), soixante-quatorze (74), soixante-quinze (75), soixante-seize (76), soixante-dix-sept (77), soixante-dix-huit (78), soixante-dix-neuf (79).\n– 80 is 4×20: quatre-vingts, then quatre-vingt-un (81), quatre-vingt-deux (82), quatre-vingt-trois (83), etc.\n– 90 is 80+10: quatre-vingt-dix, then quatre-vingt-onze (91), quatre-vingt-douze (92), quatre-vingt-treize (93), etc.\n\nThis is the system used in France. It takes some practice at first, but soon becomes second nature. By contrast, Belgium and Switzerland have slightly different – and arguably more logical – systems. Both use septante for 70 (thus septante et un for 71, etc.) and nonante for 90 (nonante et un, etc.). In addition, Switzerland often uses huitante for 80.\n\nThe rules governing the terminal -s on vingt, in particular, the use of hyphens are quite complex, so for the time being concentrate on learning the numbers and acquiring the automatic reflex of seeing them, then saying them aloud.\n\nLastly, there is only one word for 0 in French: zéro, which translates both zero and nought, as well as the oh used in phone numbers."},{"ref":"","text":"You’ll notice that, for the time being, the English translations are fairly literal. This is deliberate because we want you to concentrate on the French syntax, rather than systematically comparing the two languages. As we progress, however, the translations will become more idiomatic.\n\nAnd now that you are familiar with the basic rules on liaisons, we will no longer mark them with the undertie (vous_avez) which has been used solely to get you used to this particular feature of spoken French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"il choisit ses numéros","en":"he chooses his numbers","zh":"他选自己的号码","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Ensuite il choisit ses numéros – mais ce sont toujours les mêmes !","exEn":"Then he chooses his numbers – but they are always the same.","src":3},{"fr":"je finis de remplir la grille de jeu","en":"I finish filling in the game grid","zh":"我把表格填完","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Attendez : je finis de remplir la grille de jeu. Voilà.","exEn":"Wait: I’ll finish filling in my game grid. There it is.","src":7},{"fr":"j’ai faim et j’ai soif","en":"I’m hungry and thirsty","zh":"我又饿又渴","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant j’ai faim et j’ai soif : allons déjeuner ensemble.","exEn":"Now I’m hungry and thirsty: let’s go and have lunch together.","src":8},{"fr":"allons déjeuner ensemble","en":"let’s go and have lunch together","zh":"我们一起去吃午饭吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant j’ai faim et j’ai soif : allons déjeuner ensemble.","exEn":"Now I’m hungry and thirsty: let’s go and have lunch together.","src":8},{"fr":"je suis tout à fait d’accord avec vous","en":"I absolutely agree with you","zh":"我完全同意您的看法","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis tout à fait d’accord avec vous.","exEn":"I absolutely agree with you.","src":11},{"fr":"vous avez raison","en":"you’re right","zh":"您说得对","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"avoir raison = to be right (avoir, not être)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous avez raison !","exEn":"You’re right!","src":0},{"fr":"nous n’avons pas besoin de vous","en":"we don’t need you","zh":"我们不需要您","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous n’avons pas besoin de vous.","exEn":"We don’t need you.","src":0},{"fr":"ils ont vraiment de la chance","en":"they’re really lucky","zh":"他们真幸运","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils ont vraiment de la chance !","exEn":"They’re really lucky.","src":0},{"fr":"Quel âge a votre enfant ?","en":"how old is your child?","zh":"您的孩子几岁了？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"âge ≈ age","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel âge a votre enfant ?","exEn":"How old is your child?","src":0}],"uncertain":["note 1: the printed 'finir' table lost its last two rows in the OCR (vous finissez / ils/elles finissent); I completed them from the pattern of choisir.","note 2: the table row 'te* – you' was garbled in the OCR; reconstructed.","note 6: OCR printed '80 is 60+20'; corrected to 4×20 (quatre-vingts).","note 3.2: the bananas example is 'grandes et petites' (large and small); the OCR English read 'small and large', I wrote 'large and small'.","dialogue 7-8: the OCR split 'Attendez … Voilà.' (7) and 'Maintenant … ensemble.' (8) oddly; assigned following the numbered English translation."],"pdfPage":131,"gaps":[[1.72,3.46],[5.04,8.32],[12.16,13.3],[17.04,18.38],[22.78,23.88],[33.22,34.26],[43.96,45.32],[49.3,50.44],[56.78,57.78],[63.68,67.88]],"dur":67.9},{"n":29,"kind":"lesson","title":"Comment vas-tu ?","titleEn":"How are you?","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Salut, Louis ! Comment vas-tu ?","en":"Hi, Louis! How are you?","lit":"how go-you"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vais très bien. Et toi, Manon ? La forme ?","en":"I’m very well. And you, Manon? On good form?","lit":"the form"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça va, merci. Qu’est-ce que tu fais ici ?","en":"Fine, thanks. What are you doing here?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Mes courses. Je cherche une pâtisserie pour acheter une tarte ou un gâteau.","en":"My shopping. I’m looking for a cake shop to buy a tart or a cake.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Les Mercier viennent dîner ce soir et je sais que Clément adore les pâtisseries.","en":"The Merciers are coming to dinner this evening and I know that Clément loves pastries.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je dois aussi trouver un cadeau à leur offrir","en":"I also have to find a present to give them","lit":"to offer them"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"parce que c’est leur anniversaire de mariage samedi prochain.","en":"because it’s their wedding anniversary next Saturday.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je n’ai pas d’idées précises. Je ne connais pas leurs goûts.","en":"But I don’t have any precise ideas. I don’t know their tastes.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Est-ce que tu sais où je peux acheter quelque chose d’original ?","en":"Do you know where I can buy something original?","lit":"of original"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Il y a un centre commercial là-bas, près du bureau de poste. Tu le vois ?","en":"There’s a shopping centre over there, near the post office. Can you see it?","lit":"commercial centre"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Dedans, tu as une superbe pâtisserie qui vend des tartes délicieuses pour le dessert,","en":"Inside, there’s a superb cake shop that sells delicious tarts for dessert","lit":"you have"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"et des gâteaux délicieux avec des noix, des morceaux de chocolat et de la chantilly.","en":"and delicious cakes with walnuts, bits of chocolate and whipped cream.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Excellente idée ! Je sais que nos amis sont très gourmands.","en":"Excellent idea! I know that our friends are very fond of food.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Et pour le cadeau, qu’est-ce que tu penses d’un tableau ?","en":"And for the present, what do you think of a painting?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Plutôt des tisanes, pour faciliter la digestion !","en":"Herb teas instead, to help with digestion!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Salut ! (the origin of salute) is a familiar greeting, equivalent to Hi! As a singular noun, un salut is either a wave (of the hand) or a nod (of the head)."},{"ref":"2","text":"tu is the familiar pronoun for the singular you. It is used when talking to friends, family members and children. Toi, in this context, is the object pronoun."},{"ref":"3","text":"la forme, meaning the form or shape, is also used to refer to fitness or health, as we saw in lesson 24. Louis est en forme aujourd’hui, Louis is on top form today. The polite expression J’espère que tu es en forme ? I hope you’re doing well? is often shortened to just La forme ?, with a rising intonation, in familiar contexts."},{"ref":"4","text":"You should be familiar with the two meanings of les courses: errands, shopping (lesson 13) and horse races (lesson 22). Similarly, un anniversaire can mean both a birthday and an anniversary. Remember to take special note of these homonyms, which can often be disconcerting."},{"ref":"5","text":"Unlike English, French surnames do not take an -s in the plural: Monsieur et Madame Mercier → Les Mercier. Of course, this does not affect the pronunciation."},{"ref":"6","text":"une pâtisserie refers to both a pastry (the circumflex replacing the letter s) and the shop in which it is bought."},{"ref":"7","text":"offrir, to offer, can also mean to give when used in the context of a gift: J’offre souvent des cadeaux à mes enfants, I often give my children presents. See also note 9."},{"ref":"8","text":"See lesson 13 note 6 and lesson 35 for more about the difference between savoir and connaître."},{"ref":"9","text":"Singular nouns ending in -eau form their plural with a (silent) final x. They include un cadeau (a gift), un bureau (an office), un morceau (a piece, a bit, a slice), un gâteau (a cake) and un tableau (a painting)."},{"ref":"10","text":"The adverb plutôt, rather, instead, is a useful way of qualifying a statement or expressing a preference: Je n’aime pas les tartes, je préfère plutôt les gâteaux, I don’t like tarts, I prefer cakes instead."},{"ref":"pron","text":"As always, the final s marking the familiar form of the verb (vas, fais, sais, vois, penses, etc.) is silent: [vah], [fay], [say], [vwah], [pahns], etc.\n(11) Don’t use the (irregular) English pronunciation for ‘dessert’: the double s is sibilant: [dayssayr].\n(12) Note the pronunciation of the double l in Chantilly: [shahn-tee-yee]."},{"ref":"","text":"Chantilly is a town to the north of Paris, renowned for its chateau and gardens, an extensive library of illuminated manuscripts, and world-famous racehorse training stables. But it is arguably better known for the delicacy that bears its name: la crème de Chantilly – or, more commonly la crème chantilly (whipped cream with vanilla and sugar) – as well as for la dentelle de Chantilly (handmade bobbin lace)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Clément n’est pas en forme. Il a la grippe.","a":"Clément isn’t on form. He has the flu."},{"q":"J’ai une excellente idée : je vais acheter une tarte aux noix pour le dessert.","a":"I have an excellent idea: I’m going to buy a walnut tart for dessert."},{"q":"Je ne connais pas ses goûts et je ne sais pas ce qu’il aime.","a":"I don’t know his tastes and I don’t know what he likes."},{"q":"Nous devons acheter un cadeau pour Marc parce que c’est son anniversaire mardi prochain.","a":"We have to buy a gift for Marc because it’s his birthday next Tuesday."},{"q":"Tu veux un café ? – Je préfère plutôt une tisane, s’il te plaît.","a":"Do you want a coffee? – I’d rather have a herb tea, please."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We have three cakes for dessert. – So give me three slices.","t":"Nous avons trois {{gâteaux}} pour {{le dessert}}. – {{Donnez-moi}} trois {{morceaux}} alors."},{"cue":"Do you know where you are going? – Yes, I’m going to the post office.","t":"{{Est-ce que tu sais}} où {{tu vas}} ? – Oui, je vais {{au bureau de poste}}."},{"cue":"Hi! How are you? – I’m very well. And you?","t":"{{Salut ! Comment vas-tu}} ? – Je {{vais}} très bien. {{Et toi}} ?"},{"cue":"Dinner is excellent. These pastries are delicious. And the salmon is delicious, too.","t":"Le {{dîner}} est excellent. Ces {{pâtisseries}} sont {{délicieuses}}. Et le {{saumon}} est {{délicieux}} aussi."},{"cue":"We have to buy some presents for their daughter because it’s her birthday tomorrow.","t":"Nous {{devons acheter}} des {{cadeaux}} pour leur fille parce que c’est {{son anniversaire}} demain."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Salut !","en":"hi! (familiar greeting)","zh":"嗨！（熟人间打招呼）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ salute","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Salut, Louis ! Comment vas-tu ?","exEn":"Hi, Louis! How are you?","src":1},{"fr":"Comment vas-tu ?","en":"how are you? (familiar)","zh":"你好吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Salut, Louis ! Comment vas-tu ?","exEn":"Hi, Louis! How are you?","src":1},{"fr":"Qu’est-ce que tu fais ici ?","en":"what are you doing here?","zh":"你在这里做什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça va, merci. Qu’est-ce que tu fais ici ?","exEn":"Fine, thanks. What are you doing here?","src":3},{"fr":"mes courses","en":"my shopping, my errands","zh":"我的采购；跑腿的事","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"les courses also = horse races","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mes courses. Je cherche une pâtisserie pour acheter une tarte ou un gâteau.","exEn":"My shopping. I’m looking for a cake shop to buy a tart or a cake.","src":4},{"fr":"une pâtisserie","en":"a cake shop; a pastry","zh":"糕点店；糕点","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ pastry (the ^ replaces s)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mes courses. Je cherche une pâtisserie pour acheter une tarte ou un gâteau.","exEn":"My shopping. I’m looking for a cake shop to buy a tart or a cake.","src":4},{"fr":"un gâteau","en":"a cake","zh":"蛋糕","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ gateau","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"Mes courses. Je cherche une pâtisserie pour acheter une tarte ou un gâteau.","exEn":"My shopping. I’m looking for a cake shop to buy a tart or a cake.","src":4},{"fr":"les Mercier viennent dîner ce soir","en":"the Merciers are coming to dinner this evening","zh":"梅西耶一家今晚来吃晚饭","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les Mercier viennent dîner ce soir et je sais que Clément adore les pâtisseries.","exEn":"The Merciers are coming to dinner this evening and I know that Clément loves pastries.","src":5},{"fr":"un cadeau","en":"a present, a gift","zh":"礼物","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “cat-dough”: picture a cat wrapped up as a present, rolling in dough.","ex":"Je dois aussi trouver un cadeau à leur offrir","exEn":"I also have to find a present to give them","src":6},{"fr":"c’est leur anniversaire de mariage","en":"it’s their wedding anniversary","zh":"这是他们的结婚纪念日","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"anniversaire ≈ anniversary","ff":"un anniversaire = a birthday OR an anniversary","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"parce que c’est leur anniversaire de mariage samedi prochain.","exEn":"because it’s their wedding anniversary next Saturday.","src":7},{"fr":"je ne connais pas leurs goûts","en":"I don’t know their tastes","zh":"我不了解他们的口味","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais je n’ai pas d’idées précises. Je ne connais pas leurs goûts.","exEn":"But I don’t have any precise ideas. I don’t know their tastes.","src":8},{"fr":"où je peux acheter quelque chose d’original","en":"where I can buy something original","zh":"我在哪里能买到别致的东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"original ≈ original","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que tu sais où je peux acheter quelque chose d’original ?","exEn":"Do you know where I can buy something original?","src":9},{"fr":"un centre commercial","en":"a shopping centre","zh":"购物中心","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ commercial centre","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Il y a un centre commercial là-bas, près du bureau de poste. Tu le vois ?","exEn":"There’s a shopping centre over there, near the post office. Can you see it?","src":10},{"fr":"le bureau de poste","en":"the post office","zh":"邮局","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"Il y a un centre commercial là-bas, près du bureau de poste. Tu le vois ?","exEn":"There’s a shopping centre over there, near the post office. Can you see it?","src":10},{"fr":"des morceaux de chocolat","en":"bits / pieces of chocolate","zh":"巧克力块","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"chocolat ≈ chocolate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et des gâteaux délicieux avec des noix, des morceaux de chocolat et de la chantilly.","exEn":"and delicious cakes with walnuts, bits of chocolate and whipped cream.","src":12},{"fr":"plutôt des tisanes","en":"herb teas instead","zh":"还是喝草本茶吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Plutôt des tisanes, pour faciliter la digestion !","exEn":"Herb teas instead, to help with digestion!","src":15}],"uncertain":["pron note: respelling of Chantilly/penses adapted from nasal symbols in the OCR."],"pdfPage":139,"gaps":[[2.1,4.14],[4.82,7.74],[45.44,46.44],[69.34,72.62],[75.06,77.18],[80.16,83.44],[88.5,91.48],[94.46,97.5],[102.84,105.64],[109.42,113.4]],"dur":113.4},{"n":30,"kind":"lesson","title":"J’ai un truc à te demander…","titleEn":"I’ve got something to ask you…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Excuse-moi un instant, Juliette. Je dois répondre au téléphone.","en":"Excuse me a second, Juliette. I have to answer the phone.","lit":"instant"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Allô ? Ah, c’est toi, Antoine. Je t’entends très mal.","en":"Hello? Ah, it’s you, Antoine. I can’t hear you very well.","lit":"I hear you very badly"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je perds le signal. Attends, ne quitte pas.","en":"I’m losing the signal. Wait, don’t hang up.","lit":"leave"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Reste où tu es. Je vais descendre au premier étage.","en":"Stay where you are. I’m going to go down to the first floor.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu sais que nous sommes au milieu de la campagne, sans antenne de téléphone !","en":"You know that we’re in the middle of the countryside, without a telephone aerial!","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Voilà : est-ce que tu entends ma voix ? Je mets le haut-parleur.","en":"There: can you hear my voice? I’m putting on the loudspeaker.","lit":"is-it that; high-speaker"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, c’est bien. J’ai un truc à te demander, Stéphane.","en":"Yes, that’s fine. I’ve got something to ask you, Stéphane.","lit":"a thing"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Vas-y. Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour toi ?","en":"Go ahead. What can I do for you?","lit":"go there"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Peux-tu me prêter ton aspirateur ? J’en ai besoin pour nettoyer ma piscine.","en":"Can you lend me your vacuum cleaner? I need it to clean my swimming pool.","lit":"of it"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Le magasin dans notre village n’en vend pas.","en":"The shop in our village doesn’t sell them.","lit":"of them"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Je promets de ne pas le perdre ! Tu peux me faire confiance.","en":"I promise not to lose it! You can trust me.","lit":"make me confidence"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Écoute, je veux bien mais ça ne dépend pas entièrement de moi.","en":"Listen, I’m fine with that but it doesn’t depend entirely on me.","lit":"I want well"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais demander à Juliette si elle en a besoin. […]","en":"I’ll go and ask Juliette if she needs it. …","lit":"of it"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Bon, c’est d’accord si tu nous le rends avant la fin de la semaine.","en":"Well, she agrees if you return it to us before the end of the week.","lit":"it’s okay"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci. Je te le rends après-demain. À tout à l’heure.","en":"Thanks. I’ll give it back to you the day after tomorrow. See you later.","lit":"to all to the hour"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"un truc is a familiar but very common placeholder word meaning a thing, a whatsit, etc."},{"ref":"2","text":"Allô comes from the English hello and, in France, is used only when answering the phone (in Quebec, it is also a greeting, like Hi!). The word is often found in the business names of emergency call-out services, such as Allô Serrurier for a 24/7 locksmith."},{"ref":"3","text":"entendre, to hear, along with vendre (lesson 29) and several other verbs in this lesson, provide an introduction to Group Three, which comprises three types of endings divided into sub-categories. Verbs in the first sub-category end in -re. The inflexions are j’entends, tu entends, il/elle entend, nous entendons, vous entendez, ils entendent. Note that the -s is usually dropped from the second person singular in the imperative form (but see note 6)."},{"ref":"4","text":"quitter, to leave, is the origin of the verb to quit. Quittez l’autoroute à la sortie trente, Leave the motorway at exit 30. On the telephone, Ne quittez pas, or Ne quitte pas in the familiar form, means Please hold (“Don’t leave”)."},{"ref":"5","text":"mettre, to put, is an important verb (je mets, tu mets, il/elle met, nous mettons, vous mettez, ils mettent). Unlike English, it does not usually take a preposition (mettre un manteau, to put on a coat)."},{"ref":"6","text":"Vas-y is the familiar form of Allez-y, Go ahead (lesson 12 note 2). The terminal -s is retained in the imperative before the adverbial pronouns y and en to make pronunciation easier ([vahzee])."},{"ref":"7","text":"vouloir bien (literally “to wish well”) is used in polite expressions like Je veux bien or Si vous voulez / tu veux bien to express acceptance (similar to the formal If you will in English)."},{"ref":"8","text":"Used with the present or future tenses, the adverbial phrase à tout à l’heure (“to all to the hour”) is equivalent to later on or, as a valediction, See you later: Je reviens dans quelques minutes. – À tout à l’heure. I’ll be back in a few minutes. – See you later. It can also be used with a past tense, meaning earlier. (The phrase is probably the origin of the English slang term toodle-oo.)"},{"ref":"pron","text":"(4) The terminal -ier is usually pronounced as [-yay]: premier, [pruhmyay]. But when the following word begins with a vowel, that final syllable becomes [yer]: premier étage: [pruhmyer-aytahzh]."},{"ref":"","text":"For English-speakers, le tutoiement can be difficult to master at first (When to use it? With whom?). But in most cases, the context will help you: if someone addresses you in the tu form, you can respond likewise. If you’re not sure which form of address (tu or vous) is appropriate, always opt for the formal one. For more information, see lesson 35."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je vous entends très mal. – En effet, nous perdons le signal.","a":"I can’t hear you very well. – Yes indeed, we’re losing the signal."},{"q":"J’ai un truc à vous demander. – Qu’est-ce que c’est ?","a":"I’ve got something to ask you. – What is it?"},{"q":"Pouvez-vous me prêter votre voiture ?","a":"Can you lend me your car?"},{"q":"Allô ? Ne quittez pas : je vais voir si elle est là.","a":"Hello? Hold on, I’ll go and see if she’s there."},{"q":"Non, elle n’est pas au bureau actuellement mais elle revient tout à l’heure.","a":"No, she’s not in the office at the moment but she’s coming back later."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Excuse me (formal) a second. There: can you hear (formal) me now?","t":"{{Excusez-moi}} un instant. Voilà : est-ce que {{vous m’entendez}} maintenant ?"},{"cue":"I’ll lend you (formal) the vacuum cleaner if you promise (formal) to return it tomorrow.","t":"Je {{vous prête l’aspirateur}} si {{vous promettez}} de me le {{rendre}} demain."},{"cue":"What can we do for you? Cakes? Sorry, we don’t sell any.","t":"Qu’est-ce que {{nous pouvons faire}} pour {{vous}} ? Des gâteaux ? Désolé, {{nous n’}}en {{vendons pas}}."},{"cue":"You know that we are in the middle of the countryside, and without a car.","t":"{{Vous savez}} que {{nous sommes au milieu de}} la campagne, et {{sans}} voiture !"},{"cue":"Stay where you are. We’re coming down to the third floor.","t":"{{Restez}} où {{vous êtes. Nous descendons au}} troisième étage."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"excuse-moi un instant","en":"excuse me a second (familiar)","zh":"（对熟人）请稍等一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"instant ≈ instant","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Excuse-moi un instant, Juliette. Je dois répondre au téléphone.","exEn":"Excuse me a second, Juliette. I have to answer the phone.","src":1},{"fr":"je dois répondre au téléphone","en":"I have to answer the phone","zh":"我得接电话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"téléphone ≈ telephone","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Excuse-moi un instant, Juliette. Je dois répondre au téléphone.","exEn":"Excuse me a second, Juliette. I have to answer the phone.","src":1},{"fr":"je t’entends très mal","en":"I can’t hear you well","zh":"我听不清你说话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allô ? Ah, c’est toi, Antoine. Je t’entends très mal.","exEn":"Hello? Ah, it’s you, Antoine. I can’t hear you very well.","src":2},{"fr":"ne quitte pas","en":"hold on (on the phone)","zh":"别挂电话（请稍等）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"quitter ≈ quit","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je perds le signal. Attends, ne quitte pas.","exEn":"I’m losing the signal. Wait, don’t hang up.","src":3},{"fr":"reste où tu es","en":"stay where you are","zh":"待在原地","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Reste où tu es. Je vais descendre au premier étage.","exEn":"Stay where you are. I’m going to go down to the first floor.","src":4},{"fr":"je vais descendre au premier étage","en":"I’m going to go down to the first floor","zh":"我要下到二楼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Reste où tu es. Je vais descendre au premier étage.","exEn":"Stay where you are. I’m going to go down to the first floor.","src":4},{"fr":"nous sommes au milieu de la campagne","en":"we’re in the middle of the countryside","zh":"我们在乡下的正中间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu sais que nous sommes au milieu de la campagne, sans antenne de téléphone !","exEn":"You know that we’re in the middle of the countryside, without a telephone aerial!","src":5},{"fr":"est-ce que tu entends ma voix ?","en":"can you hear my voice?","zh":"你能听到我的声音吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voilà : est-ce que tu entends ma voix ? Je mets le haut-parleur.","exEn":"There: can you hear my voice? I’m putting on the loudspeaker.","src":6},{"fr":"j’ai un truc à te demander","en":"I’ve got something to ask you","zh":"我有件事想问你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, c’est bien. J’ai un truc à te demander, Stéphane.","exEn":"Yes, that’s fine. I’ve got something to ask you, Stéphane.","src":7},{"fr":"vas-y","en":"go ahead (familiar)","zh":"说吧；去吧（对熟人）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vas-y. Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour toi ?","exEn":"Go ahead. What can I do for you?","src":8},{"fr":"peux-tu me prêter ton aspirateur ?","en":"can you lend me your vacuum cleaner?","zh":"你能把吸尘器借给我吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “aspire-a-tur”: a machine that aspires (sucks up) dust in a tower of dirt.","ex":"Peux-tu me prêter ton aspirateur ? J’en ai besoin pour nettoyer ma piscine.","exEn":"Can you lend me your vacuum cleaner? I need it to clean my swimming pool.","src":9},{"fr":"j’en ai besoin pour nettoyer ma piscine","en":"I need it to clean my swimming pool","zh":"我需要它来清洗我的游泳池","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"piscine ≈ piscine (swimming pool)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peux-tu me prêter ton aspirateur ? J’en ai besoin pour nettoyer ma piscine.","exEn":"Can you lend me your vacuum cleaner? I need it to clean my swimming pool.","src":9},{"fr":"tu peux me faire confiance","en":"you can trust me","zh":"你可以信任我","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"confiance ≈ confidence","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je promets de ne pas le perdre ! Tu peux me faire confiance.","exEn":"I promise not to lose it! You can trust me.","src":11},{"fr":"je veux bien","en":"I’m fine with that / gladly","zh":"好的，我愿意","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Écoute, je veux bien mais ça ne dépend pas entièrement de moi.","exEn":"Listen, I’m fine with that but it doesn’t depend entirely on me.","src":12},{"fr":"je te le rends après-demain","en":"I’ll give it back to you the day after tomorrow","zh":"我后天还给你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Merci. Je te le rends après-demain. À tout à l’heure.","exEn":"Thanks. I’ll give it back to you the day after tomorrow. See you later.","src":15},{"fr":"À tout à l’heure","en":"see you later","zh":"一会儿见","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Merci. Je te le rends après-demain. À tout à l’heure.","exEn":"Thanks. I’ll give it back to you the day after tomorrow. See you later.","src":15}],"uncertain":["dialogue 8/9: OCR displaced 'Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour toi ?' (moved to sentence 8 following the English translation).","dialogue 9: English printed in the book merges 'lend me your vacuum cleaner to clean my swimming pool'; I translated both French sentences.","note 3: OCR said '(but see note 5)'; changed to note 6 (Vas-y, where the -s is retained).","note 1: OCR 'a whatname' read as 'a whatsit'.","ex2 item 5: gap boundaries ('vous êtes. Nous descendons au') follow the answer key."],"pdfPage":143,"gaps":[[1.4,3.54],[5.3,8.6],[19.32,20.58],[46.72,47.72],[55.42,56.48],[67.48,71.16],[73.2,75.18],[79,82.06],[84.92,88.06],[89.8,93.04],[95.78,98.84],[103.62,107.6]],"dur":107.6},{"n":31,"kind":"lesson","title":"J’en ai besoin rapidement","titleEn":"I need one quickly","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Que puis-je faire pour vous, madame ?","en":"What might I do for you, madam?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que vous avez des bouilloires électriques ?","en":"Do you have electric kettles?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"J’en ai deux : celle-ci, à moins de cent euros,","en":"I have two (of them): this one here, at less than a hundred euros,","lit":"I of-them have two: this-one-here, at less than a hundred euros,"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"et celle-là, qui est à quatre cents euros.","en":"and that one there, which is for four hundred euros.","lit":"and that-one-there, which is at four hundred euros."},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais c’est hors de prix ! Elle est en argent ?","en":"But that’s outrageous! Is it made of silver?","lit":"but it is out of price! it is in silver?"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr que non. C’est un appareil très sophistiqué : il est même connecté à Internet !","en":"Of course not. It’s a very sophisticated appliance: it’s even connected to the Internet!","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout le monde en parle, et j’en vends beaucoup.","en":"Everyone is talking about them, and I sell a lot of them.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais celle à cent euros marche très bien aussi, je vous assure.","en":"But the one for a hundred euros works very well, too, I assure you.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que vous en êtes sûr ?","en":"Are you sure of that?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, j’en suis certain. Tous mes clients en sont contents, enfin, presque tous…","en":"Yes, I’m certain of it. All my customers are happy, well, almost all…","lit":"yes, I of-it am certain. all my customers of-it are content, at-last, almost all…"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon, d’accord. Donnez-moi celle à quatre cents.","en":"Okay, alright. Give me the one for four hundred.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Excellent choix. Je regarde sur mon ordinateur…","en":"Excellent choice. I’m checking my computer…","lit":"excellent choice. I look on my computer…"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Ah, excusez-moi : nous n’en avons plus.","en":"Ah, excuse me: we don’t have any more.","lit":"ah, excuse-me: we not-of-it have more."},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous en avez besoin aujourd’hui ? Sinon, je peux en commander une pour demain.","en":"Do you need one today? If not, I can order one for tomorrow.","lit":"you of-it have need today? if-not, I can of-it order one for tomorrow."},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, j’en ai vraiment besoin rapidement. Tant pis. Merci pour votre aide.","en":"No, I really need one quickly. Never mind. Thanks for your help.","lit":"no, I of-it have really need quickly."},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vous en prie. Merci de votre visite.","en":"You’re welcome. Thanks for visiting.","lit":"I you of-it pray. thanks of your visit."}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(2) Here’s another -ill- word. As usual after a double vowel, the phoneme is pronounced [yy]: [booyy-wahr]. It’s worth repeating the word several times aloud in order to get to grips with the pronunciation."},{"ref":"1","text":"We came across puis-je in the exercises in lesson 26. The term, derived from the old interrogative form of je peux, I can, is now used only in polite, formal questions, like may/might in English: Puis-je avoir votre nom ? Might I have your name?; Que puis-je faire pour vous ? What may I do for you? (Puis-je cannot be used in questions formed with est-ce que … ?, which, by definition, are less formal than the inverted interrogative.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"The adverbial pronoun en was introduced in the previous lesson. It has several functions, notably to avoid repetition of a noun. En is frequently used with the partitives du/de la/des: J’ai du café. Vous en voulez ? I have coffee. Do you want some? See also note 5."},{"ref":"3","text":"The demonstrative pronouns celui / celle / ceux / celles (lesson 28) can’t be used on their own (Les tartes ? J’aime celles aux fraises). But they can take the suffixes -ci (here, an abbreviation of ici, which has no cedilla under the c) and -là when indicating (or “demonstrating”) a specific thing that is either close to or distant from the speaker: Tu veux celui-ci ?, Do you want this one [here]? Nous préférons celles-là, We prefer those ones [over there]."},{"ref":"4","text":"hors (pronounced [or]) means out of, away from. Followed by de, it is a prepositional phrase: Il est hors du bureau pour le moment, He’s out of the office for the moment. It can also be used in set expressions such as hors de question (see lesson 18), hors de prix (“out of price”), overpriced or outrageously expensive, as well as the culinary term hors d’œuvre (“out of work”), a starter."},{"ref":"5","text":"en is also a preposition meaning in. It can be used in phrases indicating either a state – en or, in/of gold – or a situation: en vacances, on holiday. For the time being, simply memorise the phrases you come across. We’ll learn more as we go along."},{"ref":"6","text":"We know that l’argent means money, but the other meaning is silver. In this case, it is preceded by de (la médaille d’argent, the silver medal) or en: Ma nouvelle montre est en argent, My new watch is in silver."},{"ref":"7","text":"Don’t confuse the preposition sur, on, with the adjective sûr, sure, safe. Both words (and the feminine adjective sûre) are pronounced identically, but the circumflex on the second word makes it easier to tell the difference between them."},{"ref":"8","text":"tant pis is a set expression meaning Too bad or Never mind. (Pis is a literary form of pire, worse/worst, which we will see shortly.)"},{"ref":"9","text":"Je vous en prie is the standard response to Merci, Thank you. The familiar (tu) form is Je t’en prie. Although the literal translation, “I pray you,” is very formal (and rarely used) in English, the French expression – both the vous and the tu forms – is standard."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Les clés de la voiture sont sur la table, j’en suis sûre.","a":"The car keys are on the table, I am sure of it."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que je peux faire pour vous, monsieur ?","a":"What can I do for you, sir?"},{"q":"Est-ce que cette montre est en or ? – Bien sûr que non !","a":"Is this watch made of gold? – Of course not!"},{"q":"Avez-vous du lait pour le café ? – Non, nous n’en avons plus.","a":"Do you have any milk for the coffee? – No, we have none left."},{"q":"Cet hôtel est hors de prix. Toutes les chambres sont à plus de cinq cents euros !","a":"That/This hotel is outrageously expensive. All the rooms are more than five hundred euros."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We have two tarts: this one, which is with chocolate, and that one, with strawberries.","t":"Nous avons deux tartes : {{celle-ci}}, qui est {{au}} chocolat, et {{celle-là}}, {{aux}} fraises."},{"cue":"I’m sorry, but we don’t have any left. – But I need some before tomorrow.","t":"Je suis désolé, {{nous n’en avons plus}}. – Mais {{j’en ai besoin}} avant demain."},{"cue":"There are several wines: these are red and those are white. But me, I prefer this one.","t":"Il y a {{plusieurs}} vins : {{ceux-ci}} sont {{rouges}} et {{ceux-là}} sont {{blancs}}. Mais moi, je préfère {{celui-ci}}."},{"cue":"You’re sure, Michel? – I’m absolutely certain! Everybody is talking about it.","t":"Vous êtes {{sûr}}, Michel ? – {{J’en suis}} absolument {{certain}} ! {{Tout le monde en}} parle."},{"cue":"All my customers are happy. – All, or almost all?","t":"{{Tous}} mes clients {{sont contents}}. – {{Tous}}, ou presque {{tous}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Que puis-je faire pour vous ?","en":"What may I do for you?","zh":"我能为您做什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Que puis-je faire pour vous, madame ?","exEn":"What might I do for you, madam?","src":1},{"fr":"une bouilloire électrique","en":"an electric kettle","zh":"电热水壶","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ boil (bouillir) + electric","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “boo-ywar”: a boy shouts “Boo! Why? War!” at a kettle that is boiling over","ex":"Est-ce que vous avez des bouilloires électriques ?","exEn":"Do you have electric kettles?","src":2},{"fr":"j’en ai deux","en":"I have two (of them)","zh":"我有两个（这样的东西）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en ai deux : celle-ci, à moins de cent euros,","exEn":"I have two (of them): this one here, at less than a hundred euros,","src":3},{"fr":"celle-ci / celle-là","en":"this one / that one (f)","zh":"这一个／那一个（阴性）","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en ai deux : celle-ci, à moins de cent euros,","exEn":"I have two (of them): this one here, at less than a hundred euros,","src":3},{"fr":"hors de prix","en":"outrageously expensive","zh":"贵得离谱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"sounds like “or duh pree”: a golden (or = gold) kettle whose price tag has flown out of the price list","ex":"Mais c’est hors de prix ! Elle est en argent ?","exEn":"But that’s outrageous! Is it made of silver?","src":5},{"fr":"bien sûr que non","en":"of course not","zh":"当然不是","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr que non. C’est un appareil très sophistiqué : il est même connecté à Internet !","exEn":"Of course not. It’s a very sophisticated appliance: it’s even connected to the Internet!","src":6},{"fr":"un appareil","en":"an appliance, a device","zh":"器具，设备","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ apparatus","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr que non. C’est un appareil très sophistiqué : il est même connecté à Internet !","exEn":"Of course not. It’s a very sophisticated appliance: it’s even connected to the Internet!","src":6},{"fr":"tout le monde en parle","en":"everyone is talking about it","zh":"大家都在谈论它","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout le monde en parle, et j’en vends beaucoup.","exEn":"Everyone is talking about them, and I sell a lot of them.","src":7},{"fr":"je vous assure","en":"I assure you","zh":"我向您保证","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ assure","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais celle à cent euros marche très bien aussi, je vous assure.","exEn":"But the one for a hundred euros works very well, too, I assure you.","src":8},{"fr":"j’en suis certain","en":"I’m certain of it","zh":"我对此很确定","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ certain","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, j’en suis certain. Tous mes clients en sont contents, enfin, presque tous…","exEn":"Yes, I’m certain of it. All my customers are happy, well, almost all…","src":10},{"fr":"nous n’en avons plus","en":"we don’t have any more","zh":"我们没有了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah, excusez-moi : nous n’en avons plus.","exEn":"Ah, excuse me: we don’t have any more.","src":13},{"fr":"sinon","en":"if not, otherwise","zh":"否则，不然","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous en avez besoin aujourd’hui ? Sinon, je peux en commander une pour demain.","exEn":"Do you need one today? If not, I can order one for tomorrow.","src":14},{"fr":"j’en ai besoin rapidement","en":"I need one quickly","zh":"我急需一个","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, j’en ai vraiment besoin rapidement. Tant pis. Merci pour votre aide.","exEn":"No, I really need one quickly. Never mind. Thanks for your help.","src":15},{"fr":"tant pis","en":"too bad, never mind","zh":"太糟了，算了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, j’en ai vraiment besoin rapidement. Tant pis. Merci pour votre aide.","exEn":"No, I really need one quickly. Never mind. Thanks for your help.","src":15},{"fr":"je vous en prie","en":"you’re welcome","zh":"不客气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Je vous en prie. Merci de votre visite.","exEn":"You’re welcome. Thanks for visiting.","src":16}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":149,"gaps":[[1.78,3.8],[5.36,8.48],[31.1,32.22],[65.58,68.88],[71.48,73.58],[76.56,79.58],[81.36,84.58],[87.4,90.42],[93.88,96.98],[101.98,105.96]],"dur":106},{"n":32,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je ne peux plus continuer comme ça","titleEn":"I can no longer continue like this","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi est-ce que vous êtes si fatigué quand vous rentrez chez vous le soir ?","en":"Why are you so tired when you go home in the evening?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Mon travail est vraiment épuisant, vous savez.","en":"My job is really exhausting, you know.","lit":"my work is really exhausting, you know."},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis responsable d’une équipe d’une dizaine de personnes","en":"I’m responsible for a team of around ten people","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"dans le service commercial d’une petite entreprise, Gagner Plus.","en":"in the marketing department of a small company, Gagner Plus (Earn More).","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Je connais cette société. Qu’est-ce que vous y faites ?","en":"I know that company. What do you do there?","lit":"what is-it that you there do?"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous répondons aux mails des clients : nous en recevons des centaines chaque jour.","en":"We answer emails from customers: we receive hundreds each day.","lit":"we answer to-the emails of-the customers: we of-them receive hundreds each day."},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"En principe, nous devons les lire systématiquement, mais ce n’est pas toujours possible.","en":"In principle, we must read them systematically, but it’s not always possible.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Bien sûr, nous ne pouvons pas répondre à chacun d’entre eux, même si nous faisons de notre mieux.","en":"Of course, we can’t answer each one, even if we do our best.","lit":"of course, we cannot answer to each among them, even if we do of our best."},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous savons que nous faisons quelque chose de très important.","en":"We know that we are doing something very important.","lit":"we know that we do something of very important."},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne refuse jamais d’aider mes collègues : je jette un coup d’œil à leur travail","en":"I never refuse to help my colleagues: I glance at their work","lit":"I throw a blow of eye at their work"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"et je leur donne un coup de main quand je peux.","en":"and I give them a hand when I can.","lit":"and I to-them give a blow of hand when I can."},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"À la fin de la journée, j’ai mal aux yeux et souvent des maux de tête.","en":"At the end of the day, I have sore eyes and often headaches.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne peux plus regarder la télé, même quand il y a quelque chose qui me plaît.","en":"I can no longer watch TV, even when there is something that I like.","lit":"even when there is something that me pleases."},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Demain je vais voir le directeur et lui dire que je ne peux plus continuer comme ça.","en":"Tomorrow I’m going to see the director and tell him that I can no longer continue like this.","lit":"tomorrow I go see the director and to-him say that I not can more continue like that."},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah, vous n’êtes pas au courant ? Vous ne pouvez plus lui parler :","en":"Ah, aren’t you aware? You can no longer talk to him:","lit":"ah, you are not at current?"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"il vient de démissionner – à cause de la fatigue.","en":"he’s just resigned – because of tiredness.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(10) The ligature œ has no equivalent sound in English. To pronounce un œil, an eye, use the “open-u” sound found in the verb “to purr” and add a long “y” sound (represented by [yy]) at the end: [uh-yy]. Listen carefully to the recording and try to imitate the speaker."},{"ref":"1","text":"As we know, si means if, but it can also be an intensifier, like so. Ma collègue est si fatiguée qu’elle ne peut plus continuer, My colleague is so tired that she can’t continue."},{"ref":"2","text":"This lesson looks at the second sub-category of third-group verbs, those ending in -oir: devoir, to have to / I must; pouvoir, to be able to / I can; recevoir, to receive; savoir, to know; and voir, to see. You have already come across most of them, but now you can see that they are conjugated in the same way."},{"ref":"3","text":"Just like une dizaine, approximately ten (lesson 19 note 3), une centaine means about a hundred. The indefinite plural can be used in the same way as hundreds in English: Je reçois des centaines de mails d’une dizaine de collègues, I receive hundreds of emails from ten or so colleagues."},{"ref":"4","text":"The indefinite pronouns chacun (masculine) and chacune (feminine) are compounds of chaque plus un/une: each one. Je regarde chacun des mails en détail, I look at each email in detail (or, alternatively, each one of these emails). For emphasis, the pronoun can be placed at the end of the phrase, as in English: Ces bouilloires coûtent cent euros chacune, These kettles cost 100 euros each. Don’t confuse chacun(e) with chaque, which is followed by a noun: Chaque appareil coûte cent euros, Each device costs 100 euros. Obviously, neither chacun(e) nor chaque has a plural form."},{"ref":"5","text":"The negative verb form is usually constructed with ne … pas. In some cases, however, pas can be replaced with an adverb, notably jamais and plus. Il ne répond jamais au téléphone, He never answers the phone; Elle ne répond plus à mes messages, She is no longer answering my messages."},{"ref":"6","text":"Another very common irregular plural is the masculine noun for the eye, l’œil, which becomes les yeux (pronounced [yeu]). Note also the idiom un coup d’œil, a glance, used with the verb jeter, to throw. (The expression donner un coup de main, used in line 11, is equivalent to the English idiom to give someone a hand.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"The indirect object pronouns are me, te, lui, nous, vous and leur. Max nous donne un coup de main, Max is giving us a hand. Peux-tu me prêter dix euros ? Can you lend me ten euros? They are identical to the direct object pronouns, except for the third person singular and plural: lui and leur. Note that the singular lui can mean him or her. Je lui donne un cadeau : I’m giving him OR her a present. Last, don’t confuse the pronoun leur with the possessive adjective, spelled identically: leur travail, their work."},{"ref":"8","text":"The plural of un mal, a pain, an ache, is des maux. See lesson 24 note 3."},{"ref":"","title":"Franglais","text":"The use of “franglais” – English words (or words derived from English) – is a perennial hot-button issue. Here’s a first example. The word email was imported directly into French decades ago as a noun (but not a verb). Efforts have since been made to find a home-grown equivalent – most notably un courriel, a portmanteau of le courrier électronique (electronic mail), which is making headway (and is commonly used in Canada). But in everyday usage, the collective noun le mail (email in general) and the singular un mail (pronounced [mayl], an email) have become more or less accepted. (Beware: the noun émail, with an acute accent, means enamel.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Mon collègue Simon est très fatigué parce qu’il travaille trop en ce moment.","a":"My colleague Simon is very tired because he’s working too much at the moment."},{"q":"Je suis au courant que tu fais un travail très important.","a":"I’m aware that you are doing a very important job."},{"q":"Je reçois des centaines de mails par jour et je réponds à chacun.","a":"I get hundreds of emails a day and I answer each one."},{"q":"Jetez un coup d’œil à cette photo. C’est qui ?","a":"Take a glance at this photo. Who is it?"},{"q":"Donnez-moi un coup de main, s’il vous plaît. J’ai mal à la tête.","a":"Give me a hand please. I have a headache."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Every Christmas I give them a present but they never say “Thank you” to me.","t":"{{Chaque}} Noël {{je leur donne}} un cadeau mais ils {{ne me disent jamais}} « Merci »."},{"cue":"Can you lend us some money. – Perhaps. I don’t know.","t":"{{Pouvez-vous nous}} prêter {{de l’argent}} ? – Peut-être. Je {{ne sais pas}}."},{"cue":"These devices are very expensive. Each one costs around a hundred euros.","t":"{{Ces}} appareils sont {{très chers}}. {{Chacun}} coûte une {{centaine}} d’euros."},{"cue":"Max never answers my letters. This can no longer continue like that.","t":"Max {{ne répond jamais}} à mes lettres. Ça {{ne peut plus continuer}} comme ça."},{"cue":"They don’t want to help me. – I’ll talk to them, but it’s their company.","t":"Ils {{ne veulent pas}} m’aider. – Je vais {{leur}} parler, mais c’est {{leur société}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"mon travail est vraiment épuisant","en":"my job is really exhausting","zh":"我的工作真的很累人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ exhaust (épuiser)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mon travail est vraiment épuisant, vous savez.","exEn":"My job is really exhausting, you know.","src":2},{"fr":"une équipe","en":"a team","zh":"团队","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ay-keep”: a team that must all “keep” a rowing rhythm, “ay, keep going!”","ex":"Je suis responsable d’une équipe d’une dizaine de personnes","exEn":"I’m responsible for a team of around ten people","src":3},{"fr":"une dizaine de personnes","en":"about ten people","zh":"大约十个人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ dozen (-aine = about)","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Je suis responsable d’une équipe d’une dizaine de personnes","exEn":"I’m responsible for a team of around ten people","src":3},{"fr":"une entreprise","en":"a company, a firm","zh":"企业，公司","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ enterprise","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"dans le service commercial d’une petite entreprise, Gagner Plus.","exEn":"in the marketing department of a small company, Gagner Plus (Earn More).","src":4},{"fr":"nous répondons aux mails des clients","en":"we answer customers’ emails","zh":"我们回复客户的邮件","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous répondons aux mails des clients : nous en recevons des centaines chaque jour.","exEn":"We answer emails from customers: we receive hundreds each day.","src":6},{"fr":"en principe","en":"in principle, normally","zh":"原则上","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"same as English (in principle)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En principe, nous devons les lire systématiquement, mais ce n’est pas toujours possible.","exEn":"In principle, we must read them systematically, but it’s not always possible.","src":7},{"fr":"chacun","en":"each one","zh":"每一个（人）","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr, nous ne pouvons pas répondre à chacun d’entre eux, même si nous faisons de notre mieux.","exEn":"Of course, we can’t answer each one, even if we do our best.","src":8},{"fr":"je fais de mon mieux","en":"I do my best","zh":"我尽我所能","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr, nous ne pouvons pas répondre à chacun d’entre eux, même si nous faisons de notre mieux.","exEn":"Of course, we can’t answer each one, even if we do our best.","src":8},{"fr":"je ne refuse jamais d’aider mes collègues","en":"I never refuse to help my colleagues","zh":"我从不拒绝帮助同事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne refuse jamais d’aider mes collègues : je jette un coup d’œil à leur travail","exEn":"I never refuse to help my colleagues: I glance at their work","src":10},{"fr":"un coup d’œil","en":"a glance","zh":"瞥一眼","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne refuse jamais d’aider mes collègues : je jette un coup d’œil à leur travail","exEn":"I never refuse to help my colleagues: I glance at their work","src":10},{"fr":"un coup de main","en":"a helping hand","zh":"帮一把","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et je leur donne un coup de main quand je peux.","exEn":"and I give them a hand when I can.","src":11},{"fr":"j’ai mal aux yeux","en":"my eyes hurt","zh":"我眼睛疼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"À la fin de la journée, j’ai mal aux yeux et souvent des maux de tête.","exEn":"At the end of the day, I have sore eyes and often headaches.","src":12},{"fr":"je ne peux plus continuer comme ça","en":"I can no longer go on like this","zh":"我不能再这样下去了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Demain je vais voir le directeur et lui dire que je ne peux plus continuer comme ça.","exEn":"Tomorrow I’m going to see the director and tell him that I can no longer continue like this.","src":14},{"fr":"vous n’êtes pas au courant ?","en":"aren’t you aware?","zh":"您还不知道吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah, vous n’êtes pas au courant ? Vous ne pouvez plus lui parler :","exEn":"Ah, aren’t you aware? You can no longer talk to him:","src":15},{"fr":"il vient de démissionner","en":"he has just resigned","zh":"他刚刚辞职了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ démission = resignation (≈ demission)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il vient de démissionner – à cause de la fatigue.","exEn":"he’s just resigned – because of tiredness.","src":16}],"uncertain":["Note 8 reference “lesson 24 note 3”: OCR shows “note J” so the note number is a best guess.","The franglais box (after the answers of lesson 32) is placed here as an unnumbered note."],"pdfPage":153,"gaps":[[1.96,4.02],[5.8,8.68],[73.46,76.96],[79.26,81.14],[85.92,88.96],[91.88,94.88],[98.26,101.48],[104.42,107.6],[110.98,114.96]],"dur":115},{"n":33,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je n’ai rien dans ma garde-robe","titleEn":"I have nothing in my wardrobe","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah, c’est toi, Margot. Entre et assieds-toi.","en":"Ah, it’s you, Margot. Come in and sit down.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne vous dérange pas ? Je peux revenir si vous voulez.","en":"I’m not disturbing you, am I? I can come back if you want.","lit":"I not you disturb not?"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu ne nous déranges pas du tout. Qu’est-ce que tu veux ?","en":"You’re not disturbing us at all. What do you want?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai besoin d’une nouvelle robe d’été : aidez-moi à la choisir.","en":"I need a new summer dress: help me to choose it.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu en as besoin, ou tu en as envie ? Ce n’est pas la même chose !","en":"Do you need one or do you want one? It’s not the same thing!","lit":"you of-it have need, or you of-it have desire?"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je sais bien, mais quelle importance ? Je veux en acheter une.","en":"I know very well, but what does it matter? I want to buy one.","lit":"I know well, but what importance?"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je n’ai rien dans ma garde-robe. Tous mes vêtements sont vieux.","en":"I have nothing in my wardrobe. All my clothes are old.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien, regardons en ligne. Tu connais ce site, mode.fr ?","en":"Well, let’s look online. Do you know this site, mode.fr (“fashion dot fr”)?","lit":"fashion point fr"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"« Choisissez parmi des milliers de modèles pour trouver le vêtement de vos rêves. »","en":"“Choose among thousands of models to find the item of clothing of your dreams.”","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est génial. Tu trouves tout : des robes, des jupes, des pulls, des manteaux.","en":"It’s fantastic. You can find everything: dresses, skirts, jumpers, coats.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce n’est pas vraiment bon marché mais les prix sont raisonnables.","en":"It’s not really cheap but the prices are reasonable.","lit":"it is not really good market but the prices are reasonable."},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Regarde : ce chemisier est joli, n’est-ce pas ? Il existe en rouge, en bleu et en orange.","en":"Look: this blouse is pretty, isn’t it? It comes in red, blue, and orange.","lit":"it exists in red, in blue and in orange."},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Je cherche une robe verte, noire ou bleue, pas un chemisier orange ou jaune !","en":"I’m looking for a green, black or blue dress, not an orange or yellow blouse!","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Et celle-là ? Qu’est-ce que tu en penses ? C’est très chic.","en":"And this one? What do you think of it? It’s very stylish.","lit":"what is-it that you of-it think?"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je ne vois rien qui m’intéresse. Mais merci quand même ! À demain.","en":"No, I can’t see anything that interests me. But thank you all the same! See you tomorrow.","lit":"but thank-you even the same! to tomorrow."}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(8) For internet addresses, French uses the nouns corresponding to the grammatical function of punctuation signs. Hence a dot is called un point [pwahn], which means a full stop (or period)."},{"ref":"1","text":"Here is another example of the lexical links between French and English. Over the centuries, une garde-robe (plural garde-robes), literally “keep dress”, evolved into a wardrobe."},{"ref":"2","text":"Remember that the second person singular imperative of -er verbs has no final -s (tu entres ➔ Entre ! You come in ➔ Come in!). However, for all regular and most irregular -ir and -re verbs, the imperative is identical to the indicative: tu attends ➔ Attends !"},{"ref":"3","text":"When used with the imperative, the direct object pronouns me and te become -moi and -toi: vous me donnez la clé ➔ Donnez-moi la clé ! ; tu t’assieds ➔ Assieds-toi !"},{"ref":"4","text":"We saw pas du tout, not at all, used on its own (lesson 3) as an emphatic negative. The phrase can also be used after a verb: Je ne les connais pas du tout, I don’t know them at all."},{"ref":"5","text":"Here is another negative construction formed with ne and an adverb (see lesson 32 note 5), in this case rien: Je n’ai rien à faire à la maison, I have nothing to do at home; Il ne voit rien la nuit, He can’t see anything at night. Remember that pas is not used in this type of construction, otherwise it would create a double negative."},{"ref":"6","text":"This is another example of a word that can be singular in French but not in English (lesson 27 note 5): les vêtements means clothes (Ses vêtements sont chics, His/Her clothes are smart) whereas un vêtement is an item of clothing. Ce vêtement est trop petit, This piece of clothing is too small. In practice, we would generally use another, more precise word in English (this coat, etc.) but un vêtement is standard French."},{"ref":"7","text":"mille is a thousand so un millier is approximately a thousand, just like dix / une dizaine (lesson 19 note 3) and cent / une centaine (lesson 32 note 3). It, too, has a plural form: Des milliers de gens, Thousands of people. And it’s masculine, not feminine."},{"ref":"8","text":"The compound adjective bon marché (“good market”) means inexpensive, cheap (with no negative connotation). It is invariable: Voici une liste des hôtels bon marché à Bordeaux, Here’s a list of inexpensive hotels in Bordeaux. A synonym is pas cher, not expensive, but in this case, the adjective agrees: des hôtels pas chers."},{"ref":"9","text":"When used as adjectives, colours generally agree with the noun in number and gender: un manteau bleu, a blue coat, des jupes bleues, blue skirts. Of course, if the masculine adjective ends in -e, it does not change: un pull jaune, une robe jaune."},{"ref":"","title":"Le Bon Marché","text":"Le Bon Marché is the name of the world’s first-ever grand magasin (department store), which opened in Paris in 1852. Although French uses the verbal expression faire ses courses to talk about shopping for food and other staples, the term faire du shopping is preferred when talking about browsing for and buying other goods, especially clothes. Naturally, purists dislike this import – they insist on faire des achats (to make purchases), while French-speaking Canadians say faire du magasinage – but it is largely (and unfortunately) unavoidable in everyday usage."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Entrez et asseyez-vous. Vous ne nous dérangez pas.","a":"Come in and sit down. You’re not disturbing us."},{"q":"Et celui-ci, qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","a":"And what do you think of this one?"},{"q":"Est-ce que nous pouvons vous aider ? – Non, mais merci quand même.","a":"Can we help you? – No, but thanks anyway."},{"q":"Nous pouvons revenir demain ou après-demain si tu veux.","a":"We can come back tomorrow or the day after tomorrow if you want."},{"q":"Ce vêtement est un peu trop petit, mais il est joli et bon marché.","a":"This piece of clothing is a bit small, but it’s pretty and inexpensive."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Come in. – I can’t, I don’t have the key. Give it to me.","t":"{{Entre}}. – Je {{ne peux pas}}, je n’ai pas la clé. {{Donne}}-la-{{moi}}."},{"cue":"There are thousands of websites. Help me to choose please.","t":"Il y a {{des milliers de}} sites Internet. {{Aide-moi à}} choisir {{s’il te plaît}}."},{"cue":"I want to buy some clothes: a skirt, a jumper and a coat. – That’s all?!","t":"Je {{veux}} acheter {{des vêtements}} : {{une jupe}}, {{un pull}} et {{un manteau}}. – C’est {{tout}} ?!"},{"cue":"We have nothing to do tomorrow. – I know, but does it matter?","t":"Nous {{n’avons rien à faire}} demain. – Je sais, {{mais quelle importance}} ?"},{"cue":"Is his/her coat green or blue? – I think that it’s black.","t":"Est-ce que {{son manteau}} est {{vert}} ou {{bleu}} ? – Je pense {{qu’il est noir}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"entre et assieds-toi","en":"come in and sit down","zh":"进来坐下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah, c’est toi, Margot. Entre et assieds-toi.","exEn":"Ah, it’s you, Margot. Come in and sit down.","src":1},{"fr":"je ne vous dérange pas ?","en":"I’m not disturbing you, am I?","zh":"我没有打扰您吧？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne vous dérange pas ? Je peux revenir si vous voulez.","exEn":"I’m not disturbing you, am I? I can come back if you want.","src":2},{"fr":"j’ai besoin d’une nouvelle robe","en":"I need a new dress","zh":"我需要一条新裙子","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"J’ai besoin d’une nouvelle robe d’été : aidez-moi à la choisir.","exEn":"I need a new summer dress: help me to choose it.","src":4},{"fr":"aidez-moi à la choisir","en":"help me to choose it","zh":"帮我挑选一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai besoin d’une nouvelle robe d’été : aidez-moi à la choisir.","exEn":"I need a new summer dress: help me to choose it.","src":4},{"fr":"tu en as envie ?","en":"do you want one? (feel like it)","zh":"你想要吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu en as besoin, ou tu en as envie ? Ce n’est pas la même chose !","exEn":"Do you need one or do you want one? It’s not the same thing!","src":5},{"fr":"quelle importance ?","en":"what does it matter?","zh":"有什么要紧？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ importance","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je sais bien, mais quelle importance ? Je veux en acheter une.","exEn":"I know very well, but what does it matter? I want to buy one.","src":6},{"fr":"je n’ai rien dans ma garde-robe","en":"I have nothing in my wardrobe","zh":"我的衣柜里什么都没有","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"garde-robe ≈ “guard robe” → wardrobe","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai rien dans ma garde-robe. Tous mes vêtements sont vieux.","exEn":"I have nothing in my wardrobe. All my clothes are old.","src":7},{"fr":"un vêtement","en":"an item of clothing","zh":"一件衣服","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"« Choisissez parmi des milliers de modèles pour trouver le vêtement de vos rêves. »","exEn":"“Choose among thousands of models to find the item of clothing of your dreams.”","src":9},{"fr":"des milliers de","en":"thousands of","zh":"成千上万的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"mille → millier ≈ millennium","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"« Choisissez parmi des milliers de modèles pour trouver le vêtement de vos rêves. »","exEn":"“Choose among thousands of models to find the item of clothing of your dreams.”","src":9},{"fr":"regardons en ligne","en":"let’s look online","zh":"我们上网看看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien, regardons en ligne. Tu connais ce site, mode.fr ?","exEn":"Well, let’s look online. Do you know this site, mode.fr (“fashion dot fr”)?","src":8},{"fr":"bon marché","en":"cheap, inexpensive","zh":"便宜","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"marché = market here, but bon marché means cheap (invariable)","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ce n’est pas vraiment bon marché mais les prix sont raisonnables.","exEn":"It’s not really cheap but the prices are reasonable.","src":11},{"fr":"les prix sont raisonnables","en":"the prices are reasonable","zh":"价格合理","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ reasonable","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ce n’est pas vraiment bon marché mais les prix sont raisonnables.","exEn":"It’s not really cheap but the prices are reasonable.","src":11},{"fr":"qu’est-ce que tu en penses ?","en":"what do you think of it?","zh":"你觉得怎么样？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et celle-là ? Qu’est-ce que tu en penses ? C’est très chic.","exEn":"And this one? What do you think of it? It’s very stylish.","src":14},{"fr":"je ne vois rien qui m’intéresse","en":"I can’t see anything that interests me","zh":"我没看到感兴趣的东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je ne vois rien qui m’intéresse. Mais merci quand même ! À demain.","exEn":"No, I can’t see anything that interests me. But thank you all the same! See you tomorrow.","src":15},{"fr":"merci quand même","en":"thanks all the same","zh":"还是谢谢你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Non, je ne vois rien qui m’intéresse. Mais merci quand même ! À demain.","exEn":"No, I can’t see anything that interests me. But thank you all the same! See you tomorrow.","src":15}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 1: the word order “Donne-la-moi” is reconstructed from the dots (…-la-…) and the answer “donne – moi”.","ex2 item 2: dots after “choisir” are lost in the OCR; “s’il te plaît” taken from the answer list."],"pdfPage":159,"gaps":[[1.66,3.72],[5.5,8.22],[32.72,33.88],[43.16,44.44],[56.26,57.5],[80.9,81.96],[87.16,90.6],[92.6,94.54],[97.58,100.62],[103,106.26],[110.42,113.62],[116.16,119.12],[123.3,127.24]],"dur":127.2},{"n":34,"kind":"lesson","title":"Les randonneurs","titleEn":"The hikers","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne cours plus parce que j’ai trop mal aux genoux – je souffre énormément,","en":"I don’t run anymore because my knees hurt too much – I’m in great pain,","lit":"I not run more because I have too bad to-the knees – I suffer enormously,"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"mais je ne peux pas rester sans rien faire.","en":"but I can’t stay here without doing anything.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Cette année, je fais une marche de soixante-dix kilomètres","en":"This year, I’m doing a 70 km walk","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"entre les villes de Colmar et Strasbourg, en Alsace.","en":"between the cities of Colmar and Strasbourg, in Alsace.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pars bien avant le soleil de midi, et j’arrive le lendemain vers vingt heures.","en":"I leave well before the noon sun, and I arrive the following day around 8 pm.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je m’arrête à mi-chemin, près de Sélestat, pour manger et dormir.","en":"I stop halfway, near Sélestat, to eat and sleep.","lit":"I myself stop at half-path"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"J’y passe la nuit et repars après le petit-déjeuner.","en":"I spend the night there and leave again after breakfast.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous faites cette randonnée tout seul, ou avec d’autres gens ?","en":"Are you doing this hike alone, or with other people?","lit":"you do this hike all alone"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Avec des copains. Ils l’organisent quatre fois par an, y compris en automne et en hiver.","en":"With mates. They organise it four times a year, including in autumn and winter.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Le temps est souvent pluvieux et il peut faire très froid, mais ce n’est pas grave.","en":"The weather is often rainy and it can be very cold, but it doesn’t matter.","lit":"it can make very cold, but it is not serious."},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu veux dire qu’il pleut ou il neige tout le temps ? Moi, je ne mets pas le nez dehors.","en":"You mean that it rains or it snows all the time! Me, I never go outside.","lit":"you want to say… me, I not put the nose outside."},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, mais la pluie ne me gêne pas, et je ne sens pas le froid.","en":"Yes, but the rain doesn’t bother me, and I don’t feel the cold.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, mon moment préféré, c’est l’arrivée. On va tout de suite au café.","en":"For me, my favourite moment is the arrival. We go straight away to the café.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Le patron ouvre la porte et nous dit :","en":"The boss opens the door and says to us:","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"« C’est moi qui offre la tournée : qu’est-ce que je vous sers ? »","en":"“It’s my round: what can I get you?”","lit":"it’s me who offers the round: what is-it that I you serve?"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(9) The terminal -er of the masculine noun l’hiver is pronounced [air], as in cher (rather than [ay], as in aller). Don’t forget that the silent h is not pronounced (just like hour in English) so, with en hiver, the final n of en runs into the i of hiver: [œhneevair]."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(11) nez is pronounced [nay], like the final syllable of the second person plural of an -er verb: venez: [vuhnay]."},{"ref":"1","text":"This is the final sub-category of third-group verbs which end in -ir but are irregular (unlike Group 2 verbs). They include courir, to run; dormir, to sleep; sentir, to feel; servir, to serve; ouvrir, to open; souffrir, to suffer; and offrir, to offer (see lesson 29 note 7). The endings are, for example, je cours, tu cours, il/elle court, nous courons, vous courez, ils/elles courent."},{"ref":"2","text":"Normally, the plural of nouns ending in -ou is formed by adding an s (un trou, a hole, des trous). But a handful, including un genou, a knee, un bijou, a jewel, and un caillou [kie-you], a pebble, take an -x. There are seven of these nouns in all, but these three are the most common."},{"ref":"3","text":"rien can mean both anything and nothing (lesson 33 note 5). Note the place of this pronoun, which, unlike English, comes before the verb: Il ne peut rien faire pour toi, He can’t do anything for you."},{"ref":"4","text":"mi- is a shortened form of demi, half. It is used in a number of expressions, notably à mi-chemin (“half-path”), halfway: Sélestat est à mi-chemin entre Colmar et Strasbourg, Sélestat is halfway between Colmar and Strasbourg."},{"ref":"5","text":"We know that the adverb y means there, as in il y a. But it can also be used to avoid repeating a noun, just like en (lesson 31 note 2). Tu connais Colmar ? – J’y habite, Do you know Colmar? – I live there (instead of J’habite à Colmar). Like rien (note 3), y comes before the verb. It can also be used in the expression y compris, including. Toute la famille est invitée, y compris ton frère, The whole family is invited, including your brother. In this case, y has no intrinsic meaning."},{"ref":"6","text":"Here’s another very common vernacular word: un copain (feminine: une copine) means a pal, a mate. Depending on the context, the term can be used for a boyfriend/girlfriend. Le copain de Noémie est un artiste qui s’appelle Florian, Noémie’s boyfriend is an artist called Florian."},{"ref":"7","text":"Like une course (lesson 29 note 4), le temps has two meanings: the time and the weather (despite the final s, the word is singular). The context should make it easy to tell the difference, but remember this mnemonic just in case: Je n’ai pas le temps pour ce mauvais temps, I don’t have time for this bad weather. (Un temps can also mean a (verb) tense: Le futur simple est un temps de l’indicatif, The future simple is an indicative tense – see lesson 8 note 3.)"},{"ref":"","title":"L’Alsace","text":"Located in eastern France, Alsace is a region with a distinct personality. Bordered by the Vosges mountains (les Vosges, pron. [vohzh]) to the west and the river Rhine (le Rhin, pron. [rahn]) to the east, it is part of the administrative area known as le Grand Est (“the great east”). Alsace has strong cultural ties with its immediate neighbour, Germany. It also has its own Germanic language, l’alsacien or Elsässisch, used alongside French on highway signage and street name signs, and still spoken by some Alsaciens (Alsatians). The region’s capital, Strasbourg, is home to the European Parliament."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je connais très bien Strasbourg. J’y habite.","a":"I know Strasbourg very well. I live there."},{"q":"Je me sens un peu fatiguée. Je dors tout le temps.","a":"I feel a bit tired. I sleep all the time."},{"q":"Nous ne pouvons rien faire pour vous. Il est trop tard.","a":"We can’t do anything for you. It’s too late."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que je te sers : un café ou une bière ?","a":"What can I get you: a coffee or a beer?"},{"q":"Ne mettez pas le nez dehors. Il fait trop froid.","a":"Don’t go outside today. It’s too cold."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We leave well before noon and we sleep in Strasbourg.","t":"Nous {{partons bien}} avant midi et nous {{dormons à}} Strasbourg."},{"cue":"We spend the night there and we set off again the next day, after breakfast.","t":"Nous {{y passons}} la nuit et nous {{repartons le lendemain}}, après {{le petit-déjeuner}}."},{"cue":"We prefer the office on the second floor. It looks more comfortable.","t":"{{Nous préférons}} le bureau du {{deuxième étage}} : il {{a l’air}} plus {{confortable}}."},{"cue":"“There are jewels in the holes under your knees” – “Not at all! They are pebbles.”","t":"« Il y a des {{bijoux dans}} les {{trous sous}} tes {{genoux}} » – « Mais non, ce sont {{des cailloux}} »."},{"cue":"It can get very cold there because it rains and snows all the time. – It doesn’t matter.","t":"Il peut {{y faire}} très {{froid}} parce qu’il {{pleut}} et il {{neige}} tout le temps. – Ce {{n’est pas grave}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai mal aux genoux","en":"my knees hurt","zh":"我膝盖疼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"Je ne cours plus parce que j’ai trop mal aux genoux – je souffre énormément,","exEn":"I don’t run anymore because my knees hurt too much – I’m in great pain,","src":1},{"fr":"je souffre énormément","en":"I’m suffering greatly","zh":"我非常痛苦","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ suffer; énormément ≈ enormously","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne cours plus parce que j’ai trop mal aux genoux – je souffre énormément,","exEn":"I don’t run anymore because my knees hurt too much – I’m in great pain,","src":1},{"fr":"je ne peux pas rester sans rien faire","en":"I can’t stay without doing anything","zh":"我不能什么都不做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais je ne peux pas rester sans rien faire.","exEn":"but I can’t stay here without doing anything.","src":2},{"fr":"une randonnée","en":"a hike","zh":"徒步旅行","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “rahn-doh-nay”: a ranger donates a sandy trail for hikers","ex":"Vous faites cette randonnée tout seul, ou avec d’autres gens ?","exEn":"Are you doing this hike alone, or with other people?","src":8},{"fr":"le lendemain","en":"the next day","zh":"第二天","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Je pars bien avant le soleil de midi, et j’arrive le lendemain vers vingt heures.","exEn":"I leave well before the noon sun, and I arrive the following day around 8 pm.","src":5},{"fr":"à mi-chemin","en":"halfway","zh":"在半路上","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’arrête à mi-chemin, près de Sélestat, pour manger et dormir.","exEn":"I stop halfway, near Sélestat, to eat and sleep.","src":6},{"fr":"j’y passe la nuit","en":"I spend the night there","zh":"我在那里过夜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’y passe la nuit et repars après le petit-déjeuner.","exEn":"I spend the night there and leave again after breakfast.","src":7},{"fr":"y compris","en":"including","zh":"包括","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"compris ≈ comprise","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avec des copains. Ils l’organisent quatre fois par an, y compris en automne et en hiver.","exEn":"With mates. They organise it four times a year, including in autumn and winter.","src":9},{"fr":"ce n’est pas grave","en":"it doesn’t matter, it’s not serious","zh":"没关系，不严重","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"grave ≈ grave (serious)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le temps est souvent pluvieux et il peut faire très froid, mais ce n’est pas grave.","exEn":"The weather is often rainy and it can be very cold, but it doesn’t matter.","src":10},{"fr":"il pleut","en":"it’s raining","zh":"下雨了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Tu veux dire qu’il pleut ou il neige tout le temps ? Moi, je ne mets pas le nez dehors.","exEn":"You mean that it rains or it snows all the time! Me, I never go outside.","src":11},{"fr":"je ne mets pas le nez dehors","en":"I don’t put my nose outside","zh":"我不出门","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu veux dire qu’il pleut ou il neige tout le temps ? Moi, je ne mets pas le nez dehors.","exEn":"You mean that it rains or it snows all the time! Me, I never go outside.","src":11},{"fr":"tout de suite","en":"straight away","zh":"立刻，马上","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Moi, mon moment préféré, c’est l’arrivée. On va tout de suite au café.","exEn":"For me, my favourite moment is the arrival. We go straight away to the café.","src":13},{"fr":"le patron","en":"the boss, the owner","zh":"老板","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ patron","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Le patron ouvre la porte et nous dit :","exEn":"The boss opens the door and says to us:","src":14},{"fr":"c’est moi qui offre la tournée","en":"it’s my round (I’m buying the drinks)","zh":"这一轮我请客","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« C’est moi qui offre la tournée : qu’est-ce que je vous sers ? »","exEn":"“It’s my round: what can I get you?”","src":15}],"uncertain":["Note 1: OCR lists servir twice; the duplicate was dropped.","ex1 item 5: the book answer says “today” but the French sentence as printed has no “aujourd’hui”.","Dialogue 15: dash left false (no dash visible in the French line)."],"pdfPage":165,"gaps":[[1.92,4.1],[5.18,8.1],[69.74,73.32],[75.5,77.58],[80.86,83.82],[87.08,89.88],[92.98,95.92],[98.46,101.66],[104.4,108.28]],"dur":108.3},{"n":35,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Demain est l’anniversaire de ma sœur et elle organise une fête.","en":"Tomorrow is my sister’s birthday and she’s organising a party.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais acheter des cadeaux. Et je vais aussi prendre quelques gâteaux.","en":"I’m going to buy some presents. And I’m also going to get a few cakes.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce qu’on peut venir avec toi, s’il te plaît ?","en":"Can we come with you please?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr, mais mettez vos manteaux : il fait froid dehors.","en":"Of course, but put your coats on: it’s cold outside.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu vas à quelle pâtisserie ?","en":"Which cake shop are you going to?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Celle à côté de la poste. C’est celle que je préfère.","en":"The one next to the post office. That is the one I prefer.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais leurs gâteaux sont hors de prix ! Chacun coûte au moins trente euros.","en":"But their cakes are ruinous. Each one costs at least 30 euros.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Tant pis : c’est son anniversaire et je vais lui acheter un truc sympa.","en":"Never mind: it’s her birthday and I’m going to buy her something nice.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Voici les gâteaux : je prends celui-ci, aux fraises, celui-là, au chocolat, et aussi quelques sandwichs pour notre déjeuner.","en":"Here are the cakes: I’m taking this one, with strawberries, this one, with chocolate, and also a few sandwiches for our lunch.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Les sandwichs sont là-bas, monsieur. Ceux-ci sont très bons, et ceux-là aussi. En fait, tout est bon chez nous.","en":"The sandwiches are over there, sir. These ones are very good, and those ones too. In fact, everything is good at our place.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Où sont les sandwichs au jambon ? Je ne les vois pas.","en":"Where are the ham sandwiches? I don’t see them.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Il n’y en a plus. Mais je peux en commander si vous voulez.","en":"There are none left. But I can order some if you want.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 The third group of verbs: -re, -oir and -ir","text":"The third group is the most complex of the three, largely because the component verbs are all irregular. It can be divided into three sub-categories according to the endings: -re, -oir and -ir.\n\n1.1 Verbs ending in -re\nprendre, to take\nje prends – I take | nous prenons – we take\ntu prends – you take | vous prenez – you take\nil/elle prend – he/she/it takes | ils/elles prennent – they take\nThis sub-category includes common verbs such as attendre, to wait; mettre, to put; and boire, to drink.\n\n1.2 Verbs ending in -oir\nsavoir, to know\nje sais – I know | nous savons – we know\ntu sais – you know | vous savez – you know\nil/elle sait – he/she/it knows | ils/elles savent – they know\nSeveral key verbs come under this sub-category, particularly vouloir, to want; and pouvoir, to be able to.\n\n1.3 Verbs ending in -ir\ndormir, to sleep\nje dors – I sleep | nous dormons – we sleep\ntu dors – you sleep | vous dormez – you sleep\nil/elle dort – he/she/it sleeps | ils/elles dorment – they sleep\nThis sub-category, comprising irregular verbs such as sortir, to go out; souffrir, to suffer; partir, to leave (and repartir, to restart); and ouvrir, to open, is slightly disorienting because it can be confused with the second group of regular verbs. The difference can be seen in the present participle (suffering, leaving, etc.), which ends in -issant for Group 2 but -ant for Group 3. Thus, finir, to finish, and choisir, to choose, are regular Group 2 verbs with the present participle finissant and choisissant, whereas the present participle of the irregular dormir is dormant.\n\nThe third group also contains one very irregular -er verb: aller, to go.\n\nSince Group 3 verbs are, by definition, irregular, they need to be learned by heart. There are, however, some recognisable patterns, which we shall see later on."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Plural nouns: an overview","text":"By now, you should know how to form the plural of nouns and adjectives. Here is an overview.\n\nThe basic rule is simply to add a terminal (and silent) -s to the singular: un croissant, deux croissants. There are, of course, several exceptions depending on the word ending:\n-ail: travail ➔ travaux, work(s) (construction, etc.)\n-al: journal ➔ journaux, newspaper(s)\n-eu: jeu ➔ jeux, game(s)\n-eau: manteau ➔ manteaux, coat(s)\n\nSingular nouns ending in -s, -x or -z do not change in the plural:\nun Français ➔ des Français, one Frenchman, Frenchmen\nune voix ➔ des voix, a voice, voices\nun nez ➔ des nez, a nose, noses\n\nThe final letter is usually silent, but there are a couple of exceptions, notably un fils / des fils, a son / sons (listen to the dialogue in lesson 28)."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 y and en","text":"The adverbial pronouns y and en have no direct equivalents in English. The meaning varies according to their function and where they are placed in the sentence. Here are some basic rules:\n\n3.1 y is used to avoid repetition\nLe musée ferme dans trois heures. On peut y aller maintenant.\nThe museum closes in three hours. We can go there now.\nThe noun does not have to refer to a place:\nJ’aime mon travail et j’y pense tout le temps.\nI like my job and I think about it all the time.\nTo form the negative, place the particle before the pronoun: je n’y pense pas.\nIt’s important to note that y cannot relate to a person: J’aime Annette : je pense à elle tout le temps. (The verbs in this type of sentence are generally followed by the preposition à: aller à, penser à, etc.)\nAnd of course, y is used in the phrase il y a (“it there has”) to express the existence of something:\nIl y a beaucoup de neige cet hiver, There is a lot of snow this winter.\n\n3.2 en replaces a word, often a verb, followed by de\nEn can be the equivalent of some / any:\nAvez-vous du lait ? Oui, j’en ai, Do you have any milk? – Yes, I have some. It always comes before the verb.\nWhile some is often dropped in this context in English (Yes, I have), en can never be omitted.\nIt can also replace the partitive article (du / de la / des), meaning of or about it/them:\nEst-ce qu’elle a des enfants ? – Oui, elle en a trois.\nDoes she have any children? – Yes, she has three [of them].\nAlain connaît bien l’Alsace : il en parle beaucoup.\nAlain knows Alsace very well: he talks about it a lot.\nAs with y, the negative particle is placed before the pronoun: il n’en parle pas beaucoup.\nIdiomatically, en is used in the expressions Je vous en prie / Je t’en prie, Don’t mention it.\nFinally, en is also a preposition, usually translated as in:\nIls habitent en Bretagne, They live in Brittany.\nNous sommes en vacances la semaine prochaine, We’re on holiday next week."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 savoir and connaître","text":"Both verbs mean to know, but they are used differently. In essence, savoir means to know a fact or how to do something. It is usually used with a clause, introduced by que, qui, où, quand, etc. For example, Je sais où habite Emmanuel, I know where Emmanuel lives (note the word order, with the verb before the subject). Used with an infinitive, savoir means the same as can:\nIl sait parler l’anglais et le français, He can speak English and French.\n\nBy contrast, connaître means to know or be familiar with a person or a place. In a sentence, the verb is followed by a direct object noun: Je connais Emmanuel, Nous connaissons Paris.\n\nIn sum, we would say Je connais Emmanuel mais je ne sais pas où il habite, I know (“am acquainted with”) Emmanuel but I don’t know (“am not aware”) where he lives.\n\nThere are a few cases where either verb can be used in a specific context, but these are few and far between."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Indirect object pronouns","text":"Indirect object pronouns are used to avoid repeating a noun. They represent the person on whom an action is being performed. They are:\nme – to me | nous – to us\nte – to you | vous – to you\nlui – to him / her | leur – to them\n\nme and te become m’ and t’ when followed by a vowel.\n\nIn contrast to English, indirect object pronouns in French always come before the verb. In many cases, they are used with verbs followed by à (écrire à, to write to; donner à, to give to; parler à, to speak to), replacing both the noun and the preposition: Je vais parler au directeur = Je vais lui parler.\n\nOne slight complication is when the equivalent verb in English takes a direct object: to phone someone / téléphoner à quelqu’un. As a result, I’m phoning my sister is Je téléphone à ma sœur, which becomes Je lui téléphone. That’s why it is so important to learn verbs together with their prepositions. Here are some examples:\nDites à vos amis de venir ➔ Dites-leur de venir.\nTell your friends / Tell them to come.\nIl donne de l’argent à ma femme et à moi ➔ Il nous donne de l’argent.\nHe gives money to me and my wife ➔ He gives us money.\n\nThe third person pronouns lui and leur replace both masculine and feminine nouns:\nIl faut demander au patron / à ma femme ➔ Il faut lui demander.\nJ’écris à mes copains / à mes amies ➔ Je leur écris.\n\nThe pronoun leur is invariable. Do not confuse it with the possessive adjective leur / leurs, which can agree with a plural noun (leurs amis). See lessons 14 and 29 note 7."},{"ref":"rev","title":"6 Le tutoiement","text":"The “familiar” form of address no longer exists in English (thee and thou disappeared centuries ago), so mastering the equivalent in French, le tutoiement (from the pronoun tu), can be tricky at first. Here are some basic rules:\n- tu is used when talking with family, close friends, lovers, children and people in the same social group. Young people use tu (the verb is tutoyer) among themselves more so than older people; and you always tutoie pets, animals, God and inanimate objects that might irritate you (computer, car, etc.);\n- vous (le vouvoiement, vouvoyer) is the formal version, used in everyday interaction with people you don’t know. It is also the plural pronoun, used collectively, even with family and friends;\n- using tu instead of vous outside these contexts can be seen as disrespectful or even rude. Tutoiement can even be a form of insult, for instance when used by two drivers involved in a fender-bender.\n\nIn practice, however, the situations in which to use the familiar form vary according to a number of factors. For instance, staff in a big company will use vous with superiors and tu with colleagues, whereas employees in a tech firm will all use le tutoiement, regardless of rank or seniority. Similarly, an adult will address a child other than a family member as tu but the child will respond with the vous form. And members of an online forum will tutoie each other even though they have never met. So, when in doubt, stick to the simple guideline that vous should always be used with strangers and in formal situations. You may then be invited to switch to the familiar form (On peut se tutoyer). Always take the lead from the person you are interacting with.\n\nHere are the vous and tu forms of each verb group:\naimer – vous aimez | tu aimes\nfinir – vous finissez | tu finis\nmettre – vous mettez | tu mets\nboire – vous buvez | tu bois\ndire – vous dites | tu dis\n\nTwo very common familiar phrases are Comment vas-tu ? How are you? and Vas-y ! Go ahead!, Go for it! And since tu is the familiar form, it is often used in an informal register. In spoken French, tu as (you have) is frequently shortened to t’as (and tu n’as pas sometimes becomes t’as pas). Needless to say, you should not imitate these colloquialisms but it is important to recognise them because they are commonly used by native speakers."},{"ref":"","title":"Idiomatic French","text":"You have certainly noticed that the language content of our lessons is becoming a little more idiomatic. This is deliberate because as soon as you start speaking in real-life settings, the people you interact with will rarely make an effort to confine themselves to “formal,” academic French. So, without venturing into hard-core slang, we will present you with language that is as realistic as possible while still allowing you to learn at a relaxed pace. Speaking of which, don’t forget to pick up the book or listen to the recordings every day, even if you only have ten minutes. C’est en forgeant qu’on devient forgeron, “It’s by forging that one becomes a blacksmith,” equivalent to the English proverb Practice makes perfect."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"un anniversaire","en":"a birthday","zh":"生日","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ anniversary","ff":"anniversaire = birthday (or anniversary)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Demain est l’anniversaire de ma sœur et elle organise une fête.","exEn":"Tomorrow is my sister’s birthday and she’s organising a party.","src":1},{"fr":"elle organise une fête","en":"she is organising a party","zh":"她要办一个派对","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ organise","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Demain est l’anniversaire de ma sœur et elle organise une fête.","exEn":"Tomorrow is my sister’s birthday and she’s organising a party.","src":1},{"fr":"mettez vos manteaux","en":"put your coats on","zh":"穿上你们的外套","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr, mais mettez vos manteaux : il fait froid dehors.","exEn":"Of course, but put your coats on: it’s cold outside.","src":4},{"fr":"c’est celle que je préfère","en":"that’s the one I prefer","zh":"那是我最喜欢的那个","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Celle à côté de la poste. C’est celle que je préfère.","exEn":"The one next to the post office. That is the one I prefer.","src":6},{"fr":"chacun coûte au moins trente euros","en":"each one costs at least thirty euros","zh":"每一个至少三十欧元","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Mais leurs gâteaux sont hors de prix ! Chacun coûte au moins trente euros.","exEn":"But their cakes are ruinous. Each one costs at least 30 euros.","src":7},{"fr":"un truc sympa","en":"something nice","zh":"一件不错的东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tant pis : c’est son anniversaire et je vais lui acheter un truc sympa.","exEn":"Never mind: it’s her birthday and I’m going to buy her something nice.","src":8},{"fr":"je prends celui-ci","en":"I’ll take this one","zh":"我要这个","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici les gâteaux : je prends celui-ci, aux fraises, celui-là, au chocolat, et aussi quelques sandwichs pour notre déjeuner.","exEn":"Here are the cakes: I’m taking this one, with strawberries, this one, with chocolate, and also a few sandwiches for our lunch.","src":9},{"fr":"en fait","en":"in fact","zh":"事实上","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ in fact","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les sandwichs sont là-bas, monsieur. Ceux-ci sont très bons, et ceux-là aussi. En fait, tout est bon chez nous.","exEn":"The sandwiches are over there, sir. These ones are very good, and those ones too. In fact, everything is good at our place.","src":10},{"fr":"il n’y en a plus","en":"there are none left","zh":"没有了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il n’y en a plus. Mais je peux en commander si vous voulez.","exEn":"There are none left. But I can order some if you want.","src":12}],"uncertain":["Note 4: the book says the verb comes before the “direct object”; changed to “subject” (Emmanuel is the subject).","Note 5: the printed example “Il donne de l’argent à moi et ma femme” is not good French; corrected to “à ma femme et à moi”.","Dialogue 9 dash: speaker unclear, dash kept as printed."],"pdfPage":169,"gaps":[[1.6,3.7],[5.18,8.18],[48.74,49.74],[58.36,59.36],[62.74,65.96]],"dur":66},{"n":36,"kind":"lesson","title":"J’espère que je n’ai rien oublié…","titleEn":"I hope that I haven’t forgotten anything…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça y est. J’ai commandé le taxi. On peut partir à l’aéroport dans cinq minutes.","en":"Here we go. I’ve ordered the taxi. We can leave for the airport in five minutes.","lit":"that there is."},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que tu as fermé toutes les fenêtres et tous les volets ?","en":"Have you closed all the windows and all the shutters?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, j’ai tout vérifié, même le grenier. Tout est verrouillé.","en":"Yes, I’ve checked everything, even the attic. Everything is bolted.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai vidé le réfrigérateur et le congélateur tout à l’heure","en":"I’ve emptied the refrigerator and the freezer just now","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’ai fermé la porte de la cave.","en":"and I’ve closed the door of the cellar.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que tu as vidé les poubelles de la cuisine et la salle de bains ?","en":"Have you emptied the rubbish bins in the kitchen and the bathroom?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais oui ! Et la corbeille à papier. Ne t’inquiète pas.","en":"But yes I have! And the waste paper basket. Don’t worry!","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que tu as caché mes bagues et mes colliers ?","en":"Have you hidden my rings and my necklaces?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai fait tout ce que tu m’as demandé.","en":"I have done everything that you asked me.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Hier, j’ai fait aussi des copies de nos passeports et déposé tout sur notre coffre en ligne.","en":"Yesterday I also made copies of our passports and deposited everything on our online safe.","lit":"… on our trunk online."},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai demandé à notre voisine de vérifier notre boîte à lettres et de la vider de temps en temps.","en":"I’ve asked our neighbour to check our letter box and to empty it from time to time.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, j’ai changé les draps, nettoyé la cuisine et lavé la vaisselle.","en":"As for me, I’ve changed the sheets, cleaned the kitchen and washed the dishes.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai arrosé les plantes et débranché le four et tous les autres appareils électriques.","en":"I’ve watered the plants and unplugged the oven and all the other electrical appliances.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Heureusement, j’ai pensé à tout et je n’ai rien oublié. […]","en":"Fortunately, I’ve thought of everything and I’ve forgotten nothing. …","lit":"happily, …"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour, vos passeports et billets d’avion, s’il vous plaît.","en":"Good morning, your passports and plane tickets, please.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Oh non ! J’ai laissé mon sac à main sur la table du salon !","en":"Oh no! I’ve left my handbag on the table in the living room!","lit":"bag at hand on the table of the living room"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(3) verrouillé looks difficult to pronounce, but take the word apart: verr- is pronounced as in very, -ou- is [oo], the i feeds into the double-l, pronounced [yy], and the final acute é is [ay]: [verooyyay]. Because French is syllable-timed, it’s easier to hear the different sounds that make up a word."},{"ref":"1","text":"The expression Ça y est (“that there is”) is useful for acknowledging that something is about to happen – Ça y est : la pluie arrive, Here we go, the rain is on its way – or has happened: Ça y est : le week-end est terminé, That’s it. The weekend is over. As usual, the translation will depend on the context, but the basic idea is that of recognition. As for the pronunciation, the three words are run together in a single sound: [sayay]."},{"ref":"2","text":"This is the perfect tense – le passé composé – of verbs ending in -er. Generally formed with avoir, appropriately conjugated, and the past participle of the verb, it is used to describe an action that was completed (“perfected”) in the past. Although similar in structure to the present perfect tense in English (I have ordered), it is not a one-to-one equivalent. (Some verbs form their passé composé with être, as we will see later on.)"},{"ref":"3","text":"The interrogative form of the perfect tense can be constructed, like the present, in three ways: by raising the voice, inverting the auxiliary and pronoun or, as here, with est-ce que : Est-ce que vous avez fermé la fenêtre ? Did you / Have you closed the window?"},{"ref":"4","text":"When used with the perfect tense, tout is usually placed as an adverb between the auxiliary and the participle (Il a tout fermé, He’s closed everything). Remember that the adverb tout is invariable (Il a tout fermé, ils ont tout fermé)."},{"ref":"5","text":"fermer means to close (Le magasin est fermé, The shop is closed) but also to lock. To avoid ambiguity, to lock can also be expressed as fermer à clé (“close to key”). The noun a lock is une serrure. Doors and windows can also be secured with un verrou, a bolt, whence the verb verrouiller ([verooyay]), to bolt."},{"ref":"6","text":"We saw in lesson 30 note 8 that tout à l’heure, later on, could be used in reference to the past. In this case, it refers to an immediate or very recent event. J’ai vérifié la cuisine tout à l’heure, I checked the kitchen just now / a few minutes ago / earlier, etc. Remember that tout is used here as part of the expression, not as an adverb, as in sentence 3."},{"ref":"7","text":"The relative pronoun ce que, first seen in lesson 12, is equivalent to what / which. It functions as the direct object of a subordinate clause, often with a subject pronoun: Je mange ce que je veux, I eat what I want. Prenez ce que vous voulez, Take what you want."},{"ref":"8","text":"nettoyer is a useful verb that means to clean or clear up, depending on the context. Like fermer, it is often followed by a noun to make the action more specific, whereas English tends to use an entirely different verb. For example, J’ai nettoyé la piscine à l’éponge, I’ve sponged down the swimming pool. We’ll see more of these equivalent constructions (verb + noun in French vs. single verb in English) as we advance. The noun nettoyage à sec means dry cleaning."},{"ref":"","title":"Eponyms","text":"Words can teach us a lot about a country’s culture and history. This is especially true of eponyms, or words derived from the proper names of real (or imaginary) people. The word for a dustbin, une poubelle, is named for Eugène Poubelle, a 19th-century city administrator who ordered refuse containers to be installed in all Parisian buildings for public health reasons. French is well-endowed with eponyms, notably in science and mathematics, yet many ordinary words originated from people who are often forgotten. For example, un martinet is a type of whip, named for the stern disciplinarian Général Jean Martinet; la nicotine is named after the philologist Jean Nicot; while la guillotine was invented by a doctor, Joseph Guillotin, as a “humanitarian” form of execution! Tracking down the etymology of words, particularly eponyms, is an excellent way to build up a vocabulary."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ça y est : le taxi est arrivé. – Déjà ?","a":"Here we go. The taxi has arrived. – Already?"},{"q":"Ne vous inquiétez pas. Tout va bien, je vous assure.","a":"Don’t worry. Everything is fine, I assure you."},{"q":"Mes frères ont pensé à tout. Tout est prêt et nous pouvons partir.","a":"My brothers have thought of everything. Everything is ready and we can leave."},{"q":"Vous avez fermé la porte à clé ? Car j’ai oublié.","a":"Have you locked the door? Because I forgot."},{"q":"Est-ce que vous pouvez vérifier la boîte à lettres et la vider tous les jours ?","a":"Can you check the mailbox and empty it every day?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We closed all the windows and checked all the shutters.","t":"Nous {{avons fermé toutes}} les fenêtres et {{vérifié tous}} les volets."},{"cue":"She didn’t empty the waste bin but she cleaned the oven.","t":"{{Elle n’a pas vidé}} la {{poubelle}} mais {{elle a nettoyé}} le four."},{"cue":"Have you changed the sheets? – Yes, and I’ve watered the plants.","t":"{{Est-ce que vous avez changé}} les draps ? – Oui, et {{j’ai arrosé}} les plantes."},{"cue":"My wife has left her handbag on the kitchen table. – I know, I hid it!","t":"Ma femme {{a laissé son}} sac sur la table de {{la cuisine}}. – Je sais, {{je l’ai caché}} !"},{"cue":"We have done everything that you asked. – Have you bolted the cellar?","t":"Nous {{avons fait}} tout {{ce que}} vous {{avez demandé}}. – Est-ce que {{vous avez verrouillé}} la cave ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"ça y est","en":"that’s it, here we go","zh":"好了，成了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça y est. J’ai commandé le taxi. On peut partir à l’aéroport dans cinq minutes.","exEn":"Here we go. I’ve ordered the taxi. We can leave for the airport in five minutes.","src":1},{"fr":"j’ai commandé le taxi","en":"I’ve ordered the taxi","zh":"我叫了出租车","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"commander ≈ command / commandeer","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça y est. J’ai commandé le taxi. On peut partir à l’aéroport dans cinq minutes.","exEn":"Here we go. I’ve ordered the taxi. We can leave for the airport in five minutes.","src":1},{"fr":"j’ai tout vérifié","en":"I’ve checked everything","zh":"我全都检查过了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"vérifier ≈ verify","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, j’ai tout vérifié, même le grenier. Tout est verrouillé.","exEn":"Yes, I’ve checked everything, even the attic. Everything is bolted.","src":3},{"fr":"une poubelle","en":"a rubbish bin, a dustbin","zh":"垃圾桶","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “poo-bell”: a bin so smelly that its bell rings “poo!”","ex":"Est-ce que tu as vidé les poubelles de la cuisine et la salle de bains ?","exEn":"Have you emptied the rubbish bins in the kitchen and the bathroom?","src":6},{"fr":"ne t’inquiète pas","en":"don’t worry","zh":"别担心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais oui ! Et la corbeille à papier. Ne t’inquiète pas.","exEn":"But yes I have! And the waste paper basket. Don’t worry!","src":7},{"fr":"j’ai caché mes bagues","en":"I’ve hidden my rings","zh":"我把我的戒指藏起来了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que tu as caché mes bagues et mes colliers ?","exEn":"Have you hidden my rings and my necklaces?","src":8},{"fr":"j’ai fait tout ce que tu m’as demandé","en":"I’ve done everything you asked me","zh":"你要求的我都做了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai fait tout ce que tu m’as demandé.","exEn":"I have done everything that you asked me.","src":9},{"fr":"une boîte à lettres","en":"a letter box, a mailbox","zh":"信箱","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai demandé à notre voisine de vérifier notre boîte à lettres et de la vider de temps en temps.","exEn":"I’ve asked our neighbour to check our letter box and to empty it from time to time.","src":11},{"fr":"de temps en temps","en":"from time to time","zh":"时不时","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ from time to time","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"J’ai demandé à notre voisine de vérifier notre boîte à lettres et de la vider de temps en temps.","exEn":"I’ve asked our neighbour to check our letter box and to empty it from time to time.","src":11},{"fr":"j’ai nettoyé la cuisine","en":"I’ve cleaned the kitchen","zh":"我打扫了厨房","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Moi, j’ai changé les draps, nettoyé la cuisine et lavé la vaisselle.","exEn":"As for me, I’ve changed the sheets, cleaned the kitchen and washed the dishes.","src":12},{"fr":"j’ai arrosé les plantes","en":"I’ve watered the plants","zh":"我给植物浇了水","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai arrosé les plantes et débranché le four et tous les autres appareils électriques.","exEn":"I’ve watered the plants and unplugged the oven and all the other electrical appliances.","src":13},{"fr":"heureusement","en":"fortunately","zh":"幸运的是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"heureux (happy) + -ment = -ly","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Heureusement, j’ai pensé à tout et je n’ai rien oublié. […]","exEn":"Fortunately, I’ve thought of everything and I’ve forgotten nothing. …","src":14},{"fr":"je n’ai rien oublié","en":"I’ve forgotten nothing","zh":"我什么都没忘","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Heureusement, j’ai pensé à tout et je n’ai rien oublié. […]","exEn":"Fortunately, I’ve thought of everything and I’ve forgotten nothing. …","src":14},{"fr":"j’ai laissé mon sac à main sur la table","en":"I’ve left my handbag on the table","zh":"我把手提包落在桌上了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oh non ! J’ai laissé mon sac à main sur la table du salon !","exEn":"Oh no! I’ve left my handbag on the table in the living room!","src":16},{"fr":"tout à l’heure","en":"just now, a little while ago (or later)","zh":"刚才；稍后","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"J’ai vidé le réfrigérateur et le congélateur tout à l’heure","exEn":"I’ve emptied the refrigerator and the freezer just now","src":4}],"uncertain":["Dialogue 10: the book gloss for “en ligne” is partly unreadable; English follows the book translation.","Exercise 2 item 1: gaps follow the printed dot groups and the answer list (“avons fermé toutes – vérifié tous”)."],"pdfPage":177,"gaps":[[1.7,3.68],[5.58,8.46],[21.32,22.4],[49,50.3],[80.4,84.22],[86,87.9],[91.18,94.44],[97.16,100.28],[104.22,107.38],[110.58,114.1],[117.64,121.06]],"dur":121.1},{"n":37,"kind":"lesson","title":"J’ai réfléchi à la question du loyer…","titleEn":"I’ve thought about the question of rent…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça fait deux mois que je cherche un appartement à louer.","en":"I’ve been looking for a flat to rent for two months.","lit":"that makes two months that I look-for a flat to rent."},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"As-tu trouvé quelque chose ?","en":"Have you found something?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas encore. C’est plus compliqué que ça, et ce n’est pas fini !","en":"Not yet. It’s more complicated than that, and it’s not over!","lit":"it is not finished!"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai cherché d’abord dans l’arrondissement où j’ai grandi, mais sans succès.","en":"I looked first in the neighbourhood where I grew up, but without success.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite, j’ai élargi mes recherches, et j’ai aussi contacté un agent immobilier.","en":"Then I broadened my search, and I also contacted an estate agent.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je n’ai pas réussi à le joindre par téléphone, mais j’ai rempli le formulaire sur son site","en":"I didn’t manage to get him on the phone, but I filled in the form on his site","lit":"I have not succeeded to him join by telephone"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’ai fourni tous les renseignements nécessaires.","en":"and I supplied all the necessary information.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Il m’a rappelée le lendemain et nous avons choisi plusieurs propriétés à visiter.","en":"He called me back the next day and we chose several properties to visit.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Et alors ? Tu as trouvé ton bonheur ?","en":"Well? Did you find what you were looking for?","lit":"and then? you have found your happiness?"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y a deux apparts dans mes moyens : l’un est plus petit que l’autre","en":"There are two flats that I can afford: one is smaller than the other","lit":"there are two flats in my means"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"mais il est aussi plus cher parce qu’il donne sur une cour intérieure très calme.","en":"but it’s also more expensive because it gives onto a very calm interior courtyard.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"L’autre, au premier étage, est moins intéressant : il est plus grand mais aussi plus bruyant.","en":"The other, on the first floor, is less interesting: it is bigger but also noisier.","lit":"more big … more noisy"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y en a un troisième, le plus spacieux, au dernier étage. Il est plus grand que tous les autres","en":"There is a third one, the roomiest, on the top floor. It’s bigger than all the others","lit":"the most roomy"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"et beaucoup plus joli, avec une énorme terrasse tout autour et rien au-dessus.","en":"and much prettier, with a huge terrace all around and nothing above.","lit":"much more pretty"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais, malheureusement, le loyer est prohibitif. Il est plus élevé que mon salaire mensuel.","en":"But, unfortunately, the rent is prohibitive. It’s higher than my monthly salary.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Eh oui, mon amie ! C’est difficile mais il faut vivre au-dessous de ses moyens.","en":"Oh yes, my friend! It’s hard but you have to live below your means.","lit":"it is necessary to live below of one’s means."},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je peux t’aider, peut-être.","en":"But perhaps I can help you.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"En effet, j’ai réfléchi sérieusement à la question : est-ce que tu peux garantir mon loyer… ?","en":"Indeed, I’ve thought seriously about the question: can you guarantee my rent…?","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(9) Remember not to pronounce the h in bonheur: [boh-nuhr] (the same applies to the antonym malheur, misfortune: [mah-luhr])."},{"ref":"1","text":"un arrondissement, a neighbourhood (see lesson 8), comes from the Group 2 verb arrondir, to round off, and rond, round. The -ment suffix, which generally denotes an adverb, is also found in certain nouns derived from “action” verbs from all three groups. For example, juger (to judge) ➔ un jugement (a judgement – notice the difference in spelling) and avertir (to warn) ➔ un avertissement (a warning). The difference between -ment adverbs and nouns is easy to spot because the latter are usually preceded by a definite or indefinite article."},{"ref":"2","text":"The perfect tense of Group 2 verbs (regular, ending in -ir) is usually constructed with avoir and the past participle, formed by dropping the final -r from the infinitive. For example, grandir (to grow up) ➔ grandi: j’ai grandi, tu as grandi, il/elle a grandi, nous avons grandi, vous avez grandi, ils/elles ont grandi. Simple!"},{"ref":"3","text":"Continuing the series of nouns that can be plural in French but not in English, la recherche has two meanings: research, in which case the noun is usually singular and has a definite article – La recherche scientifique est très importante, Scientific research is very important – and, more generally, a search or an investigation, in which case it takes an indefinite article but can also be plural: Nous allons continuer nos recherches, We are going to keep searching. For web browsing (see lesson 27), we use un moteur de recherche, a search engine."},{"ref":"4","text":"le bonheur means happiness or good fortune. Après trois mariages, elle a enfin trouvé le bonheur : être seule, After three marriages, she finally found happiness: being alone. The word can also be used in idiomatic expressions such as Est-ce que tu as trouvé ton bonheur ?, Have you found what you wanted or were looking for? The opposite of le bonheur is le malheur, unhappiness, misfortune. Remember this proverb: Un malheur n’arrive jamais seul, Misfortunes never come on their own, or, more colloquially, It never rains but it pours. (The h is silent in both words.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"un appartement, a flat or an apartment (notice the difference in spelling), is one of the multisyllabic words that are frequently abbreviated in spoken French (lesson 17 note 7). It becomes un appart, with the final t pronounced."},{"ref":"6","text":"le moyen, in the singular, is the way or the means: Je connais un bon moyen d’apprendre une langue, I know a good way to learn a language. In the plural, it is equivalent to the singular English noun means, i.e. money. Nous n’avons pas les moyens d’aller en vacances cette année, We don’t have the money to go on holiday this year. In this case, avoir les moyens is a good paraphrase for to afford, which has no direct equivalent in French."},{"ref":"7","text":"By now, you should be familiar with the comparatives of superiority and inferiority, where more is translated by plus and less by moins. In both cases the conjunction than is que. Lyon est plus grand que Toulouse mais plus petit que Paris, Lyon is bigger than Toulouse but smaller than Paris. To form the superlative, simply put the definite article le or la for the singular (depending on the gender of the noun) and les for the plural before plus or moins."},{"ref":"8","text":"dessus and dessous mean above and below / beneath, respectively. They are used in different forms, but here we see them, with au-, as adverbs. J’habite au troisième étage et ma copine habite à l’étage au-dessus, I live on the third floor and my girlfriend lives on the floor above. La température est deux degrés au-dessous de zéro, The temperature is two degrees below zero. (You will have recognised the preposition sous, under.) In some cases, au-dessous is replaced with en dessous, but the meaning is exactly the same."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Mon père a garanti mon loyer. J’ai vraiment de la chance !","a":"My father has guaranteed my rent. I am really lucky!"},{"q":"J’ai oublié mon manteau, et maintenant il pleut !","a":"I’ve forgotten my coat, and now it’s raining!"},{"q":"Je sais seulement qu’il a grandi dans le deuxième arrondissement de Marseille.","a":"I know only that he grew up in the second district of Marseille."},{"q":"Tu as les moyens de prendre des vacances cette année ?","a":"Can you afford to go on holiday this year?"},{"q":"Est-ce que tu as réussi à joindre l’agent immobilier par téléphone ?","a":"Did you manage to get the estate agent on the phone?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Did you manage to get in touch with the estate agent? – Not yet.","t":"{{Est-ce que}} vous {{avez réussi à joindre}} l’agent immobilier ? – {{Pas encore}}."},{"cue":"You haven’t provided all the necessary information. – I haven’t managed to find it.","t":"{{Vous n’avez pas fourni tous}} les renseignements nécessaires. – Je {{n’ai pas réussi}} à {{les}} trouver."},{"cue":"That flat is less expensive than the other one and it’s also smaller.","t":"{{Cet}} appartement est {{moins cher que}} l’autre et il est aussi {{plus petit}}."},{"cue":"It’s the longest and hardest exercise I have done this week.","t":"C’est l’exercice {{le plus long}} et {{le plus difficile}} que {{j’ai fait}} cette semaine."},{"cue":"Unfortunately, the rent is higher than your salary. – But I live beneath my means!","t":"{{Malheureusement}}, le loyer est {{plus élevé que}} ton salaire. – Mais je vis {{en dessous de}} mes moyens !"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"ça fait deux mois que je cherche","en":"I’ve been looking for two months","zh":"我已经找了两个月了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Ça fait deux mois que je cherche un appartement à louer.","exEn":"I’ve been looking for a flat to rent for two months.","src":1},{"fr":"un appartement à louer","en":"a flat to rent","zh":"出租的公寓","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"appartement ≈ apartment","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça fait deux mois que je cherche un appartement à louer.","exEn":"I’ve been looking for a flat to rent for two months.","src":1},{"fr":"pas encore","en":"not yet","zh":"还没有","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas encore. C’est plus compliqué que ça, et ce n’est pas fini !","exEn":"Not yet. It’s more complicated than that, and it’s not over!","src":3},{"fr":"sans succès","en":"without success","zh":"没有成功","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ without success","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai cherché d’abord dans l’arrondissement où j’ai grandi, mais sans succès.","exEn":"I looked first in the neighbourhood where I grew up, but without success.","src":4},{"fr":"j’ai grandi","en":"I grew up","zh":"我在那里长大","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai cherché d’abord dans l’arrondissement où j’ai grandi, mais sans succès.","exEn":"I looked first in the neighbourhood where I grew up, but without success.","src":4},{"fr":"un agent immobilier","en":"an estate agent","zh":"房产中介","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ immobile → real estate (immobilier)","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Ensuite, j’ai élargi mes recherches, et j’ai aussi contacté un agent immobilier.","exEn":"Then I broadened my search, and I also contacted an estate agent.","src":5},{"fr":"je n’ai pas réussi à le joindre","en":"I didn’t manage to reach him","zh":"我没能联系上他","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"réussir ≈ succeed","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai pas réussi à le joindre par téléphone, mais j’ai rempli le formulaire sur son site","exEn":"I didn’t manage to get him on the phone, but I filled in the form on his site","src":6},{"fr":"les renseignements","en":"information","zh":"信息，资料","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ron-sane-yuh-mon”: a ranger who is sane gives you information","ex":"et j’ai fourni tous les renseignements nécessaires.","exEn":"and I supplied all the necessary information.","src":7},{"fr":"tu as trouvé ton bonheur ?","en":"did you find what you were looking for?","zh":"你找到你想要的了吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et alors ? Tu as trouvé ton bonheur ?","exEn":"Well? Did you find what you were looking for?","src":9},{"fr":"dans mes moyens","en":"within my means","zh":"在我的经济能力之内","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Il y a deux apparts dans mes moyens : l’un est plus petit que l’autre","exEn":"There are two flats that I can afford: one is smaller than the other","src":10},{"fr":"plus petit que l’autre","en":"smaller than the other one","zh":"比另一个小","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il y a deux apparts dans mes moyens : l’un est plus petit que l’autre","exEn":"There are two flats that I can afford: one is smaller than the other","src":10},{"fr":"le loyer est prohibitif","en":"the rent is prohibitive","zh":"房租高得负担不起","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ prohibitive","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Mais, malheureusement, le loyer est prohibitif. Il est plus élevé que mon salaire mensuel.","exEn":"But, unfortunately, the rent is prohibitive. It’s higher than my monthly salary.","src":15},{"fr":"il faut vivre au-dessous de ses moyens","en":"you have to live below your means","zh":"要量入为出","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Eh oui, mon amie ! C’est difficile mais il faut vivre au-dessous de ses moyens.","exEn":"Oh yes, my friend! It’s hard but you have to live below your means.","src":16},{"fr":"en effet","en":"indeed","zh":"的确，没错","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ in effect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En effet, j’ai réfléchi sérieusement à la question : est-ce que tu peux garantir mon loyer… ?","exEn":"Indeed, I’ve thought seriously about the question: can you guarantee my rent…?","src":18},{"fr":"j’ai réfléchi sérieusement à la question","en":"I’ve thought seriously about the question","zh":"我认真考虑过这个问题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"réfléchir ≈ reflect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En effet, j’ai réfléchi sérieusement à la question : est-ce que tu peux garantir mon loyer… ?","exEn":"Indeed, I’ve thought seriously about the question: can you guarantee my rent…?","src":18}],"uncertain":["Dialogue 6: OCR “J’ai n’a pas réussi” read as “Je n’ai pas réussi”.","Note 8: the book says “adjective sous”; changed to “preposition”."],"pdfPage":183,"gaps":[[1.86,3.94],[5.76,8.88],[18.74,19.88],[23.66,24.68],[29.6,30.78],[38.72,40.18],[74.72,76],[87.16,88.26],[95.88,99.04],[101.14,103.14],[106.8,109.62],[111.94,115.1],[118.8,122.06],[124.88,128],[131.12,135]],"dur":135},{"n":38,"kind":"lesson","title":"C’est de la part de qui ?","titleEn":"Who is calling?","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour, j’essaie de joindre Alain Bolland. Est-il au bureau aujourd’hui ?","en":"Good morning. I’m trying to reach Alain Bolland. Is he at the office today?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne sais pas, je vais vérifier. C’est de la part de qui ?","en":"I don’t know. I’ll check. Who is calling?","lit":"it’s of the part of who?"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Dites-lui que Madame Lacroix veut lui parler.","en":"Tell him that Mrs Lacroix wants to speak with him.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Je l’ai rencontré au Salon de l’automobile et j’ai pris sa carte de visite.","en":"I met him at the Motor Show and I took his business card.","lit":"card of visit"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai appris qu’il est le nouveau directeur commercial pour l’Île-de-France.","en":"I learned that he is the new sales manager for the Île-de-France.","lit":"island of France"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Il m’a dit de l’appeler en début de semaine pour organiser une visite de vos locaux.","en":"He told me to call him at the beginning of the week to organise a visit of your offices.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vous mets en attente. Ne quittez pas, je vous prie.","en":"I’m putting you on hold. Please hold the line.","lit":"I you put in wait. not leave not, I you pray."},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je regrette, mais son poste ne répond pas. Il doit être à l’extérieur.","en":"I’m sorry, but his extension is not answering. He must be out of the office.","lit":"he must be at the exterior."},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne suis pas surpris parce que nous avons plein de travail en ce moment et l’agenda de Monsieur Bolland est plein.","en":"I’m not surprised because we have loads of work at the moment and Mr Bolland’s diary is full.","lit":"full of work"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Voulez-vous laisser un message ?","en":"Do you want to leave a message?","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Pouvez-vous lui demander de me recontacter plus tard dans la journée ?","en":"Can you ask him to contact me again later in the day?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Entendu. Il a vos coordonnées, je présume ?","en":"Of course. He has your contact details, I presume?","lit":"heard."},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Normalement oui, mais je vais vous donner un numéro où il peut me trouver à tout moment.","en":"He should do, but I’m going to give you a number where he can get me at any time.","lit":"normally yes"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est le 07 28 91 12. Attendez, excusez-moi, c’est le 02 à la fin.","en":"It’s 07 28 91 12. Wait, excuse me, it’s 02 at the end.","lit":"it’s the 07 …"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Si je ne réponds pas c’est parce que je suis en réunion.","en":"If I don’t answer it’s because I’m in a meeting.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai compris. Je lui laisse votre message. Au revoir et merci de votre appel.","en":"I’ve understood. I’ll leave him your message. Goodbye and thank you for calling.","lit":"thanks of your call"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Un grand merci pour votre coopération.","en":"Thank you very much for your cooperation.","lit":"a big thank-you"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(12), (17) Unlike in English, oo is not pronounced as a long vowel but as two separate ones: [oh-oh]. This is particularly the case with the prefix co-. Thus the first syllables of coordonnées and coopération are pronounced [koh-or] and [koh-op], respectively."},{"ref":"1","text":"une part is a piece or a slice: Voulez-vous une part de gâteau au chocolat ? Do you want a piece of chocolate cake? In a telephone conversation, however, the expression de la part de identifies the person on behalf of whom the call is being placed. The standard question is C’est de la part de qui ?, equivalent in register to Who shall I say is calling? Giving one’s name is a sufficient response. The caller can also say, for example, Je l’appelle de la part de Michelle, in which case the caller may be Michelle herself or someone calling on her behalf."},{"ref":"2","text":"Here is the perfect tense of certain Group 3 verbs ending in -re, such as prendre, to take, and its derivatives: j’ai pris, tu as pris, il/elle a pris, nous avons pris, vous avez pris, ils/elles ont pris."},{"ref":"3","text":"Another set of -re verbs, including dire, to say, lire, to read, and écrire, to write, form their past participle with a final (and silent) -t: j’ai dit, tu as dit, il/elle a dit, nous avons dit, vous avez dit, ils/elles ont dit. Naturally, any verbs derived from these three, such as contredire, to contradict, are conjugated in the same way."},{"ref":"4","text":"The adjective local has the same meaning as in English (un guide local, a local guide). As a noun, however, it means premises. In contrast to English, it can be either singular or plural: un local commercial, des locaux commerciaux, commercial premises. The difference is merely a question of size, with the plural being used for a larger facility. (Remember that nouns and adjectives ending in -al form the plural with -aux.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"We are familiar with je vous en prie (see lessons 31 and 35). In very formal contexts, however, the en can be dropped, particularly if the expression appears at the end of a sentence. Entrez, je vous prie, Please come in. You are unlikely to use je vous prie (and the tu form is very rarely used); however, you will probably hear it in certain circumstances, notably on the phone."},{"ref":"6","text":"A few nouns have two genders, each with a different meaning. One of the most common is poste. Une poste is a post office (lessons 29 and 35) but un poste means a position or job: Il quitte le poste de directeur en mai, He is leaving the position of director in May. The masculine word also means a switchboard extension in a telephone system: Le poste ne répond pas, The extension is not answering. You can see why we insist on memorising not only the meaning of nouns but also their gender!"},{"ref":"7","text":"The adjective plein (feminine pleine, pronounced [plen]) means full or busy (think: plenty): Les hôtels sont toujours pleins en été, The hotels are always full in summer. The invariable expression plein de means full of and, more broadly, lots of: Elle a plein de choses à faire, She has lots to do."},{"ref":"8","text":"entendu is the past participle of entendre, to hear: J’ai entendu un bruit, I heard a noise. But the word can be used alone in response to a statement that you acknowledge or agree with. Venez tôt demain. – Entendu. Come early tomorrow. – Will do. Of course, the actual translation will depend on the context but, in every case, avoid the (bad) French habit of saying OK instead of Entendu !"},{"ref":"9","text":"les coordonnées, coordinates, is the geographical term for distances or numbers identifying a position. By extension, it is commonly used to refer to a person’s contact details in the general sense. Thus Donnez-moi vos coordonnées can be translated as Give me your address / your contact details / your name and number, etc. If further details are needed, an adjective can be added – for instance, Donnez-moi vos coordonnées téléphoniques or bancaires if you want the person’s phone number or bank details."},{"ref":"","title":"L’Île-de-France","text":"L’Île-de-France – literally “The Island of France” – is the official designation of the greater Paris area, the most populous of the country’s 18 regions, but is often referred to simply as la région parisienne. The toponym is used to form an adjective, francilien(ne) (referring to the Paris region) and a proper noun, un Francilien / une Francilienne, a local inhabitant. L’Île-de-France comprises eight administrative areas, or départements, that are subdivided into two “rings,” an inner one (la Petite Couronne, or “small crown”) and an outer one, la Grande Couronne, “big crown”). The region is circled by a network of motorways and expressways called… La Francilienne."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Tu ne reçois plus de mails parce que ta boîte est pleine.","a":"You are no longer receiving emails because your box is full."},{"q":"Je sais que Madame Doillon est en réunion mais puis-je laisser un message ?","a":"I know that Mrs Doillon is in a meeting, but may I leave a message?"},{"q":"Vous pouvez la joindre à tout moment au 06 82 92 73.","a":"You can contact her at any time on 06 82 92 73."},{"q":"C’est de la part de qui ? Ah, c’est toi. Ne quitte pas.","a":"Who is calling? Ah, it’s you. Hold on."},{"q":"J’ai plein de travail en ce moment à cause du Salon de l’automobile.","a":"I have loads of work at the moment because of the Motor Show."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I’ve found out that Mr Aubry is stepping down as manager of the post office this summer.","t":"{{J’ai appris}} que Monsieur Aubry {{quitte le poste}} de directeur {{de la poste cet}} été."},{"cue":"They told us to call at the beginning of the week to visit your premises.","t":"{{Ils}} nous {{ont dit d’appeler}} en début de semaine {{pour}} visiter {{vos locaux}}."},{"cue":"What is his/her name? – I don’t know. I didn’t take his/her business card after the meeting.","t":"{{Quel est}} son nom ? – Je ne sais pas : {{je n’ai pas pris}} sa {{carte de visite}} après {{la réunion}}."},{"cue":"Sorry, I didn’t understand. What did you say?","t":"Désolé, {{je n’ai pas compris}}. Qu’est-ce que {{vous avez dit}} ?"},{"cue":"His diary is full because he has loads of work. That surprised me.","t":"Son {{agenda est plein}} parce qu’il {{a plein de}} travail. Ça {{m’a surpris}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"c’est de la part de qui ?","en":"who is calling? (who shall I say is calling?)","zh":"请问您是哪位？（电话用语）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne sais pas, je vais vérifier. C’est de la part de qui ?","exEn":"I don’t know. I’ll check. Who is calling?","src":2},{"fr":"j’essaie de joindre Alain Bolland","en":"I’m trying to reach Alain Bolland","zh":"我想联系阿兰·博朗","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"essayer ≈ essay / try","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonjour, j’essaie de joindre Alain Bolland. Est-il au bureau aujourd’hui ?","exEn":"Good morning. I’m trying to reach Alain Bolland. Is he at the office today?","src":1},{"fr":"dites-lui que…","en":"tell him that…","zh":"请告诉他……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dites-lui que Madame Lacroix veut lui parler.","exEn":"Tell him that Mrs Lacroix wants to speak with him.","src":3},{"fr":"une carte de visite","en":"a business card","zh":"名片","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"carte de visite = business card, not “visiting card” only","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je l’ai rencontré au Salon de l’automobile et j’ai pris sa carte de visite.","exEn":"I met him at the Motor Show and I took his business card.","src":4},{"fr":"j’ai appris qu’il est le nouveau directeur","en":"I learned that he is the new manager","zh":"我得知他是新任经理","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai appris qu’il est le nouveau directeur commercial pour l’Île-de-France.","exEn":"I learned that he is the new sales manager for the Île-de-France.","src":5},{"fr":"en début de semaine","en":"at the beginning of the week","zh":"在这周初","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Il m’a dit de l’appeler en début de semaine pour organiser une visite de vos locaux.","exEn":"He told me to call him at the beginning of the week to organise a visit of your offices.","src":6},{"fr":"ne quittez pas","en":"hold the line","zh":"请别挂电话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vous mets en attente. Ne quittez pas, je vous prie.","exEn":"I’m putting you on hold. Please hold the line.","src":7},{"fr":"son poste ne répond pas","en":"his extension is not answering","zh":"他的分机没人接","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un poste (m) = extension, job; une poste (f) = post office","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je regrette, mais son poste ne répond pas. Il doit être à l’extérieur.","exEn":"I’m sorry, but his extension is not answering. He must be out of the office.","src":8},{"fr":"plein de travail","en":"loads of work","zh":"一大堆工作","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"plein ≈ plenty","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne suis pas surpris parce que nous avons plein de travail en ce moment et l’agenda de Monsieur Bolland est plein.","exEn":"I’m not surprised because we have loads of work at the moment and Mr Bolland’s diary is full.","src":9},{"fr":"un message","en":"a message","zh":"留言","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voulez-vous laisser un message ?","exEn":"Do you want to leave a message?","src":10},{"fr":"pouvez-vous lui demander de me recontacter ?","en":"can you ask him to contact me again?","zh":"您能让他再联系我吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"recontacter ≈ re-contact","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pouvez-vous lui demander de me recontacter plus tard dans la journée ?","exEn":"Can you ask him to contact me again later in the day?","src":11},{"fr":"entendu","en":"of course, will do","zh":"好的，明白","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Entendu. Il a vos coordonnées, je présume ?","exEn":"Of course. He has your contact details, I presume?","src":12},{"fr":"les coordonnées","en":"contact details","zh":"联系方式","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ coordinates","ff":"coordonnées = contact details, not just coordinates","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Entendu. Il a vos coordonnées, je présume ?","exEn":"Of course. He has your contact details, I presume?","src":12},{"fr":"je suis en réunion","en":"I’m in a meeting","zh":"我在开会","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"réunion ≈ reunion → meeting","ff":"réunion = meeting, not “reunion”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si je ne réponds pas c’est parce que je suis en réunion.","exEn":"If I don’t answer it’s because I’m in a meeting.","src":15},{"fr":"merci de votre appel","en":"thank you for calling","zh":"感谢您的来电","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"J’ai compris. Je lui laisse votre message. Au revoir et merci de votre appel.","exEn":"I’ve understood. I’ll leave him your message. Goodbye and thank you for calling.","src":16}],"uncertain":["Name written “Île-de-France” with hyphens (standard spelling); the book prints “Île de France”.","ex1 item 3 and dialogue 14: phone numbers read as printed in OCR."],"pdfPage":189,"gaps":[[1.64,4.08],[5.2,8.42],[25.56,26.56],[71.5,72.52],[88.42,92.02],[94.18,96.14],[98.98,102.32],[105.92,109.16],[115.74,118.8],[119.94,121.02],[123.22,126.32],[129.62,133.36]],"dur":133.4},{"n":39,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le septième art","titleEn":"The cinema (the seventh art)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"La France possède une longue tradition cinématographique, et le « septième art » est bien vivant aujourd’hui.","en":"France has a long cinematographic tradition, and the “seventh art” is truly alive today.","lit":"France possesses …"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Malgré toutes les plateformes de diffusion et tous les autres supports disponibles,","en":"Despite all the streaming platforms and all the other media available,","lit":"platforms of broadcasting … supports"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"beaucoup de gens vont deux ou trois fois par semaine au cinéma.","en":"many people go twice or three times a week to the cinema.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Et les metteurs en scène et comédiens français sont très appréciés par le public.","en":"And French directors and actors are much appreciated by audiences.","lit":"putters in stage"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Le choix de films est énorme – comédies, aventures, policiers, dessins animés, et cætera.","en":"The choice of movies is huge: comedies, adventures, detective films, cartoons, etc.","lit":"animated drawings"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Naturellement, il y a aussi beaucoup de films étrangers dans les « salles obscures »,","en":"Naturally, there are also a lot of foreign films in the “dark rooms,”","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"qu’on peut voir en version originale ou en version française (c’est-à-dire doublés).","en":"which one can see in the original version or in the French version (that is to say, dubbed).","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Deux rendez-vous annuels sont indispensables pour les cinéphiles :","en":"Two annual get-togethers are indispensable for movie lovers:","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"le festival de Cannes avec sa Palme d’Or, et la soirée des Césars ;","en":"the Cannes festival with its Golden Palm, and the Césars evening;","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"ces prix récompensent le meilleur du cinéma de l’année, notamment le meilleur film et le meilleur scénario.","en":"these prizes reward the best of the year’s cinema, notably best film and best screenplay.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"La semaine dernière, des millions d’amateurs en ont suivi la cérémonie de clôture.","en":"Last week millions of fans followed the closing ceremony.","lit":"of it have followed"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils ont vu la maîtresse et le maître de cérémonie présenter les prix aux gagnants.","en":"They saw the hostess and the host present the awards to the winners.","lit":"the mistress and the master of ceremony"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Et maintenant, le moment que nous attendons tous : le César du meilleur metteur en scène.","en":"And now the moment that we’ve all been waiting for: the César for the best director.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Cette année il est attribué à… Michel Bonnaud pour « Commencez sans moi ».","en":"This year it is awarded to… Michel Bonnaud for his movie Start Without Me.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Essayant de rester décontracté, le gagnant répond :","en":"Trying to remain relaxed, the winner answered:","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci, je n’ai jamais reçu un prix de la mise en scène.","en":"Thank you. I’ve never received a director’s award.","lit":"I have never received a prize of the putting in stage."},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je suis étonné car je n’ai pas tourné un seul film depuis vingt ans.","en":"But I’m astonished because I haven’t made a single film for twenty years.","lit":"I have not turned a single film"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est justement pour ça que le jury vous donne cette récompense, mon cher Michel !","en":"That’s exactly why the jury is giving you this reward, my dear Michel!","lit":"it is justly for that"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(3), (6) In a formal register, careful speakers such as actors or newscasters tend to use linkage (see Introduction) more than would otherwise be the case. In this lesson, contrast the liaisons made in deux_ou trois and films_étrangers with je suis étonné in line 17 and sentence 5 in the first exercise, both of which use the non-formal pronunciation found in everyday speech."},{"ref":"1","text":"Most of this text is in a formal register, as used in the press, for example. In these contexts, French often shuns “ordinary” verbs such as être and avoir, replacing them with fancier synonyms. Here, for example, La France a une longue tradition is rephrased using the regular verb posséder, to possess. Likewise, the cinema is known as le septième art, the seventh art. This tendency to “upgrade” simple concepts can be disconcerting at first, but it soon becomes a familiar trope."},{"ref":"2","text":"la diffusion comes from the verb diffuser, to diffuse but, more commonly, to spread or broadcast. The noun is used in terms such as la diffusion vidéo, streaming, and la baladodiffusion, podcasting. These are relatively new coinages that have certainly not replaced the imported terms le streaming and le podcasting in everyday speech."},{"ref":"3","text":"Here is a good example of how nouns are derived from verbs. From the verb mettre, to put, we get un metteur en scène (“a putter in stage”), a film director or a stage director; and la mise en scène, direction."},{"ref":"4","text":"Another literary device used in formal writing is metonymy, the use of a related term to stand in for an object or concept: les salles obscures (“the dark rooms”) is used – always in the plural – to mean cinemas. However, the regular adjectives for dark are sombre (the absence of light, pronounced [sohn-bruh]) or foncé (any colour approaching black): Mon nouveau pull est bleu foncé, My new sweater is dark blue."},{"ref":"5","text":"We know that un rendez-vous means a meeting or an appointment (lesson 4, for example). But it can also be used figuratively when referring to an event attracting large numbers of people. Le Salon de l’automobile est le rendez-vous annuel de tous ceux qui aiment les voitures, The Motor Show is the annual get-together for everyone who loves cars. Because the final letters on both components of this compound noun are -s and -z, respectively, it does not change in the plural (des rendez-vous)."},{"ref":"6","text":"The Latin and Greek origins of French are evident in many everyday words. Here, for example, we have the suffix -phile (from the Greek philos, friend). With its Anglo-Saxon roots, English would translate this as lover: for example un bibliophile, a book-lover; un cinéphile, a film lover or movie buff. The opposite suffix is -phobe (from phobos, fear): un aquaphobe : someone who is scared of water. These types of words are not considered formal."},{"ref":"7","text":"suivi is the past participle of the Group 3 verb suivre, to follow. Two other common verbs with the same -i participle ending are finir ➔ fini (to finish / finished) and dormir ➔ dormi (to sleep / slept)."},{"ref":"8","text":"Here’s another past participle of a Group 3 verb: voir ➔ vu, to see ➔ seen. Note also venir ➔ venu, to come ➔ come, and avoir ➔ eu, to have ➔ had."},{"ref":"9","text":"Introduced in lesson 23 note 9, the present participle of all three verb groups ends in -ant: essayer ➔ essayant (try ➔ trying), finir ➔ finissant (finish ➔ finishing), rendre ➔ rendant (give back ➔ giving back). Note how the participle is used as an adjective: les arts vivants, the performing (“living”) arts."},{"ref":"","title":"Le cinéma en France","text":"In many French-speaking countries, the cinema is known as le grand écran (“the big screen”) and the TV as le petit écran (“small screen”). One of France’s top film awards is la Palme d’Or, Golden Palm, presented to the best movie at le Festival de Cannes. And instead of the Oscars, the French movie industry organises its own annual awards, les Césars (named for the sculptor who designed the trophy). Foreign films are popular in France and are generally screened in two versions: en VO (version originale, with subtitles) or en VF (version française, dubbed into French)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Cet artiste, qui est toujours vivant, vit près de Nice.","a":"This artist, who is still alive, lives close to Nice."},{"q":"Le public français apprécie vraiment cette jeune comédienne américaine Michelle May.","a":"The French audience really appreciates this young American actress, Michelle May."},{"q":"Ce metteur en scène n’a pas tourné une comédie depuis dix ans.","a":"That director hasn’t filmed a comedy for ten years."},{"q":"Et maintenant, le prix qui récompense le meilleur film étranger de l’année !","a":"And now, the prize that rewards the best foreign movie of the year!"},{"q":"Il y a deux ou trois dessins animés que j’ai vraiment aimés cette année.","a":"There are two or three cartoons that I really liked this year."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I haven’t seen Michel for ten years. – Have you seen his wife?","t":"{{Je n’ai pas vu}} Michel {{depuis}} dix ans. – Est-ce que {{tu as vu}} sa femme ?"},{"cue":"We had a message from Philippe. He told us that his son didn’t sleep all night.","t":"{{Nous avons eu}} un message de Philippe. Il {{nous a dit}} que son fils {{n’a pas dormi}} de la nuit."},{"cue":"Did you follow the Césars this year? Three actors all won an award.","t":"Est-ce que {{tu as suivi}} les Césars cette année ? Trois comédiens {{ont tous gagné}} un prix."},{"cue":"I haven’t yet finished the book that you lent me, but I love it. – Yes, it won several prizes.","t":"{{Je n’ai pas}} encore {{fini}} le livre que {{tu m’as prêté}}, mais je l’adore. – Oui, il {{a gagné}} plusieurs {{prix}}."},{"cue":"Trying to stay relaxed, the actress said: “Thank you, but I’m very astonished.”","t":"{{Essayant}} de rester {{décontractée, la comédienne}} a dit : « Merci, mais je suis {{très étonnée}} »."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"malgré","en":"despite, in spite of","zh":"尽管，不顾","pos":"prep","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “mal-gray”: a bad (mal) grey sky, yet people go to the cinema in spite of it","ex":"Malgré toutes les plateformes de diffusion et tous les autres supports disponibles,","exEn":"Despite all the streaming platforms and all the other media available,","src":2},{"fr":"beaucoup de gens","en":"many people","zh":"很多人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"beaucoup de gens vont deux ou trois fois par semaine au cinéma.","exEn":"many people go twice or three times a week to the cinema.","src":3},{"fr":"deux ou trois fois par semaine","en":"two or three times a week","zh":"每周两三次","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"beaucoup de gens vont deux ou trois fois par semaine au cinéma.","exEn":"many people go twice or three times a week to the cinema.","src":3},{"fr":"un metteur en scène","en":"a director (film, stage)","zh":"导演","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Et les metteurs en scène et comédiens français sont très appréciés par le public.","exEn":"And French directors and actors are much appreciated by audiences.","src":4},{"fr":"le public","en":"the public, the audience","zh":"公众，观众","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ public","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et les metteurs en scène et comédiens français sont très appréciés par le public.","exEn":"And French directors and actors are much appreciated by audiences.","src":4},{"fr":"le choix de films est énorme","en":"the choice of films is huge","zh":"电影的选择非常多","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"énorme ≈ enormous","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le choix de films est énorme – comédies, aventures, policiers, dessins animés, et cætera.","exEn":"The choice of movies is huge: comedies, adventures, detective films, cartoons, etc.","src":5},{"fr":"un dessin animé","en":"a cartoon","zh":"动画片","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"dessin ≈ design; animé ≈ animated","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le choix de films est énorme – comédies, aventures, policiers, dessins animés, et cætera.","exEn":"The choice of movies is huge: comedies, adventures, detective films, cartoons, etc.","src":5},{"fr":"un film étranger","en":"a foreign film","zh":"外国电影","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Naturellement, il y a aussi beaucoup de films étrangers dans les « salles obscures »,","exEn":"Naturally, there are also a lot of foreign films in the “dark rooms,”","src":6},{"fr":"en version originale","en":"in the original version","zh":"原声版","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ original version","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"qu’on peut voir en version originale ou en version française (c’est-à-dire doublés).","exEn":"which one can see in the original version or in the French version (that is to say, dubbed).","src":7},{"fr":"le meilleur film","en":"the best film","zh":"最佳影片","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"ces prix récompensent le meilleur du cinéma de l’année, notamment le meilleur film et le meilleur scénario.","exEn":"these prizes reward the best of the year’s cinema, notably best film and best screenplay.","src":10},{"fr":"un prix","en":"a prize, an award (also: a price)","zh":"奖项；价格","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"prix = price AND prize/award","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci, je n’ai jamais reçu un prix de la mise en scène.","exEn":"Thank you. I’ve never received a director’s award.","src":16},{"fr":"je suis étonné","en":"I’m astonished","zh":"我很惊讶","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"étonné ≈ astonished","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais je suis étonné car je n’ai pas tourné un seul film depuis vingt ans.","exEn":"But I’m astonished because I haven’t made a single film for twenty years.","src":17},{"fr":"depuis vingt ans","en":"for twenty years","zh":"二十年来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Mais je suis étonné car je n’ai pas tourné un seul film depuis vingt ans.","exEn":"But I’m astonished because I haven’t made a single film for twenty years.","src":17}],"uncertain":["Pronunciation note: the book refers to “line 16” for je suis étonné; it is sentence 17 here.","Note 7: the book calls finir a Group 3 verb; the wording was adjusted (finir is Group 2).","Note 8: the book says “to come ➔ came”; given as “come” (past participle)."],"pdfPage":195,"gaps":[[1.58,3.56],[4.94,7.84],[23.8,24.92],[59.4,60.5],[67.72,68.82],[80.6,81.62],[91.84,92.86],[109.54,113.4],[115.42,117.42],[120.9,123.96],[128.42,131.34],[134.8,137.98],[142.98,146.06],[149.06,152.82]],"dur":152.8},{"n":40,"kind":"lesson","title":"Tu es si impatient !","titleEn":"You’re so impatient!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu veux venir avec moi au ciné ce soir ? Il y a plein de nouveautés à voir cette semaine.","en":"Do you want to come with me to the flicks this evening? There are loads of new things to see this week.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Où est-ce que j’ai mis le guide des spectacles ? Je l’ai perdu ? Non, le voici.","en":"Where did I put the entertainment guide? Did I lose it? No, here it is.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Regarde : « L’Étranger du nord » : c’est le dernier film avec l’acteur russe que tu aimes bien.","en":"Look: “The Stranger from the North”: it’s the latest film with the Russian actor you like a lot.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Bof, j’ai vu la bande-annonce. Je déteste les films d’action et, en réalité, je n’aime pas trop cet acteur.","en":"Meh. I’ve seen the trailer. I hate action movies and, in reality, I’m not too keen on that actor.","lit":"band-announcement"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Fais un effort ! Bois ton café et aide-moi à choisir quelque chose.","en":"Make an effort! Drink your coffee and help me to choose something.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je l’ai bu. En fait, j’en ai déjà pris trois ce matin. As-tu une autre proposition de film ?","en":"I’ve drunk it. In fact, I’ve already had three this morning. Have you got another film to suggest?","lit":"I of-them have already taken three"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"« Le Vieux Port ». L’action a lieu dans le sud de la France. J’ai entendu de bonnes critiques.","en":"“The Old Port.” The action takes place in the south of France. I’ve heard good reviews.","lit":"the action has place"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah non, il est nul, ce film ! Autre chose ?","en":"Oh no, it’s a dreadful film! Anything else?","lit":"it is null, this film! other thing?"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Une comédie musicale ? Non, ce n’est pas vraiment ton truc.","en":"A musical (comedy)? No, it’s not really your thing.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Si, si. J’en ai vu une – « Entre l’est et l’ouest » – et elle m’a plu.","en":"Oh yes it is. I saw one – “Between East and West” – and I liked it.","lit":"it me has pleased"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"À quelle heure sont les séances au multiplexe du centre commercial ? […]","en":"What time are the showings at the multiplex in the shopping centre? …","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Dans quelle salle passe le film ? Il y en a une douzaine.","en":"Which screen is the movie showing in? There are around twelve of them.","lit":"in which room passes the film?"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Il faut d’abord faire la queue ici pour avoir le tarif étudiant.","en":"You first have to queue here to get the student rate.","lit":"it is necessary firstly to make the queue"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment, tu n’as pas pris les billets à la billetterie en ligne ?","en":"What, you didn’t get the tickets from the online box office?","lit":"how, you have not taken the tickets"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je suis bête. Je n’y ai pas pensé.","en":"No, I’m silly. I didn’t think of it.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai horreur d’attendre. Allons prendre un verre quelque part.","en":"I hate waiting. Let’s go and have a drink somewhere.","lit":"I have horror of waiting. let’s-go take a glass somewhere."}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"In this lesson we come across two sets of Group 3 verbs with their past participles: mettre ➔ mis and prendre ➔ pris; along with perdre ➔ perdu, boire ➔ bu, entendre ➔ entendu, and plaire ➔ plu. More details in lesson 42."},{"ref":"2","text":"We saw the adjective étranger, foreign, in lesson 39. The noun is identical: un étranger / une étrangère. It has two meanings: a foreigner and a stranger (the latter derived directly from French). The context should make the meaning clear – for example, la Légion étrangère, the Foreign Legion. Don’t confuse the adjectives étranger and étrange, strange: Quelle question étrange ! What a strange question!"},{"ref":"3","text":"dernier/dernière means both last (lesson 14) and latest. Once again, context is key, because le dernier film de l’acteur can mean the actor’s last film (if he made no more movies or is deceased) and the actor’s latest film (their most recent performance). If in doubt, ask a movie buff or google the name!"},{"ref":"4","text":"The familiar register in this lesson is in sharp contrast to the formality of the previous one. For example: the one-word exclamation Bof is a concise idiomatic way of expressing indifference. It can be translated in several ways: Qu’est-ce que tu penses de ce film ? – Bof. What do you think of this movie? – Not much! / Meh / Whatever, etc."},{"ref":"5","text":"Another familiar usage is nul (line 8). After a noun, the word literally means nil or null and void. But in idiomatic language, it indicates that the thing or person being described is useless, worthless, terrible, etc. Cet acteur est nul, That actor is terrible. Being an adjective, nul agrees with its noun: Cette actrice est nulle."},{"ref":"6","text":"un truc, a thing (lesson 30 note 1), is used in the same way as in English to express something one likes or is keen on: Ce n’est pas mon/son/leur truc, It’s not my/his/her/their thing. Le sport, c’est son truc, He/She is into sports."},{"ref":"7","text":"We know that si means both if and so (lesson 32 note 1). It can also be used like oui when contradicting a negative statement, thus performing the same function as the repetition of the auxiliary in English: Il n’aime pas le chocolat. – Si. He doesn’t like chocolate. – Yes he does. To add extra insistence, the word is repeated: Si si, il l’adore, Oh yes he does, he loves it."},{"ref":"8","text":"The suffix -erie, seen in lesson 25, can designate any place serving a specific function: un billet ➔ une billetterie, a ticket / ticket office; une huile ➔ une huilerie, oil (vegetable, etc.) / an oil mill or factory. Look for the root word and work from there."},{"ref":"9","text":"une bête (the origin of beast) means an animal or a creature. As an adjective, it is used colloquially to mean silly or idiotic. Ton idée est bête, Your idea is silly. A useful expression, in response to an acceptable suggestion, is Ce n’est pas bête : Nous allons vendre notre voiture. – Ce n’est pas bête. We are going to sell our car. – That’s not a bad idea."},{"ref":"10","text":"un verre, a glass (lesson 24 line 12), is used socially in the expression prendre un verre, to have / get a drink: Tu veux voir un film ou aller prendre un verre ? Do you want to see a movie or go for a drink?"},{"ref":"","title":"Registers","text":"Identifying different registers in another language is difficult, particularly in the early stages of learning, and takes quite a bit of practice. This lesson and the previous one are designed to underline some of the differences between formal and informal interactions in everyday French. For the time being, simply listen to the dialogues and do the exercises. You’ll soon be able to differentiate between registers and take part actively in conversations using the appropriate language. Continuez le bon travail ! (Keep up the good work!)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Il y a de la pluie sur l’est et le sud de la France aujourd’hui et du soleil sur l’ouest et le nord.","a":"There is rain over the east and the south of France today and sun over the west and north."},{"q":"Pour avoir le tarif étudiant, il faut attendre, mais j’ai horreur de faire la queue.","a":"To get the student rate, you have to wait, but I hate queuing."},{"q":"Ma femme joue au tennis ; moi, le sport, ce n’est pas mon truc.","a":"My wife plays tennis; as for me, sport isn’t my thing."},{"q":"Faites un effort ! Buvez votre café et aidez-moi à choisir un film.","a":"Make an effort! Drink your coffee and help me choose a film."},{"q":"Votre femme m’a dit que vous n’aimez pas le chocolat. – Si, si, je l’adore.","a":"Your wife told me that you don’t like chocolate. – Yes I do. I love it."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Where did you put the Paris guide? – I’ve lost it.","t":"{{Où}} est-ce que {{tu as mis}} le guide de Paris ? – Je {{l’ai perdu}}."},{"cue":"I read a good review of this film. – I saw it, and it’s terrible.","t":"{{J’ai lu}} une {{bonne critique}} de ce film. – Je {{l’ai vu}} et il est {{nul}}."},{"cue":"What screen is the movie showing in, and what time are the showings?","t":"Dans {{quelle salle passe}} le film et {{à quelle heure sont}} les séances ?"},{"cue":"Have you drunk an espresso? – What a strange question. I’ve had four.","t":"Est-ce que {{tu as bu}} un expresso ? – {{Quelle}} question {{étrange}} : {{j’en ai pris}} quatre."},{"cue":"You haven’t got the tickets? – No, I hate musicals.","t":"Vous {{n’avez pas pris}} les billets ? – Non, {{j’ai horreur des}} comédies musicales."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"il y a plein de nouveautés","en":"there are loads of new things","zh":"有很多新东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"nouveauté ≈ novelty","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu veux venir avec moi au ciné ce soir ? Il y a plein de nouveautés à voir cette semaine.","exEn":"Do you want to come with me to the flicks this evening? There are loads of new things to see this week.","src":1},{"fr":"je l’ai perdu","en":"I’ve lost it","zh":"我把它弄丢了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Où est-ce que j’ai mis le guide des spectacles ? Je l’ai perdu ? Non, le voici.","exEn":"Where did I put the entertainment guide? Did I lose it? No, here it is.","src":2},{"fr":"en réalité","en":"in reality, actually","zh":"实际上","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ in reality","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bof, j’ai vu la bande-annonce. Je déteste les films d’action et, en réalité, je n’aime pas trop cet acteur.","exEn":"Meh. I’ve seen the trailer. I hate action movies and, in reality, I’m not too keen on that actor.","src":4},{"fr":"fais un effort !","en":"make an effort!","zh":"努力一下！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ effort","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Fais un effort ! Bois ton café et aide-moi à choisir quelque chose.","exEn":"Make an effort! Drink your coffee and help me to choose something.","src":5},{"fr":"une critique","en":"a review; a criticism","zh":"评论，影评","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ critique","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Le Vieux Port ». L’action a lieu dans le sud de la France. J’ai entendu de bonnes critiques.","exEn":"“The Old Port.” The action takes place in the south of France. I’ve heard good reviews.","src":7},{"fr":"il est nul, ce film !","en":"it’s a dreadful film!","zh":"这部电影糟透了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"nul = null; informally: terrible, useless","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah non, il est nul, ce film ! Autre chose ?","exEn":"Oh no, it’s a dreadful film! Anything else?","src":8},{"fr":"ce n’est pas vraiment ton truc","en":"it’s not really your thing","zh":"这不太是你的菜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Une comédie musicale ? Non, ce n’est pas vraiment ton truc.","exEn":"A musical (comedy)? No, it’s not really your thing.","src":9},{"fr":"si, si","en":"oh yes it is! (contradicting a negative)","zh":"不，是的！（反驳否定句）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si, si. J’en ai vu une – « Entre l’est et l’ouest » – et elle m’a plu.","exEn":"Oh yes it is. I saw one – “Between East and West” – and I liked it.","src":10},{"fr":"elle m’a plu","en":"I liked it (it pleased me)","zh":"我很喜欢它","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si, si. J’en ai vu une – « Entre l’est et l’ouest » – et elle m’a plu.","exEn":"Oh yes it is. I saw one – “Between East and West” – and I liked it.","src":10},{"fr":"faire la queue","en":"to queue, to wait in line","zh":"排队","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “kuh”: a cat stands in line holding its tail (la queue) in front of the cinema","ex":"Il faut d’abord faire la queue ici pour avoir le tarif étudiant.","exEn":"You first have to queue here to get the student rate.","src":13},{"fr":"un billet","en":"a ticket","zh":"票","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"billet = ticket (or banknote), not “billet”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment, tu n’as pas pris les billets à la billetterie en ligne ?","exEn":"What, you didn’t get the tickets from the online box office?","src":14},{"fr":"je suis bête","en":"I’m silly","zh":"我真傻","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"bête ≈ beast","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je suis bête. Je n’y ai pas pensé.","exEn":"No, I’m silly. I didn’t think of it.","src":15},{"fr":"j’ai horreur d’attendre","en":"I hate waiting","zh":"我讨厌等待","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"horreur ≈ horror","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai horreur d’attendre. Allons prendre un verre quelque part.","exEn":"I hate waiting. Let’s go and have a drink somewhere.","src":16},{"fr":"prendre un verre","en":"to have a drink","zh":"喝一杯","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"J’ai horreur d’attendre. Allons prendre un verre quelque part.","exEn":"I hate waiting. Let’s go and have a drink somewhere.","src":16}],"uncertain":["Dialogue 4: the book translates Bof as “You reckon?”; rendered as “Meh.” in line with note 4.","Exercise 2 item 4: the printed English ended with “four?”; read as “four.”"],"pdfPage":203,"gaps":[[1.66,3.54],[4.94,7.84],[68.3,70.32],[88.54,92.04],[94.66,96.78],[102.78,105.68],[110.48,113.6],[117.18,120.42],[125.82,128.82],[133.44,137.34]],"dur":137.4},{"n":41,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un accueil désagréable","titleEn":"An unpleasant reception","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Allô, bonjour. Je suis bien au restaurant “La Fleur d’Argent” ?","en":"Hello, good morning. Have I reached the restaurant “The Silver Flower”?","lit":"I am well at the restaurant"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui monsieur. Vous y êtes.","en":"Yes sir, you are there.","lit":"you there are"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"J’essaie de vous joindre depuis deux jours. Ça sonne toujours occupé.","en":"I’ve been trying to reach you for two days. The line always rings busy.","lit":"it rings"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"J’en suis conscient. Mais depuis que nous sommes dans le guide touristique,","en":"I am aware of that. But since we have been in the tourist guidebook,","lit":"touristic"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"le téléphone n’arrête pas de sonner et nous sommes complets presque tous les soirs.","en":"the phone hasn’t stopped ringing and we are full almost every night.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous pouvez toujours essayer de réserver avec l’application MaTable.fr. C’est plus sûr.","en":"You can always try to reserve with the MaTable.fr application. It’s safer.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, je connais cette appli, mais je préfère avoir une voix humaine au bout du fil.","en":"Yes, I know that app but I prefer to have a human voice at the other end of the line.","lit":"at the end of the wire"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Comme vous voulez. Combien êtes-vous et à quelle heure souhaitez-vous dîner ?","en":"As you wish. How many are you and at what time do you wish to have dinner?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Vers vingt heures trente, pour quatre couverts. À la terrasse si possible.","en":"Around 8.30, for four people. On the terrace if possible.","lit":"for four covers"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Huit heures et demie, dites-vous ? Oui, il me reste une petite table pour quatre.","en":"8.30, you say? Yes, I still have a small table for four.","lit":"there me remains"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais à l’intérieur, car toutes les tables dehors sont prises. Cela vous convient-il ?","en":"But inside, because all the tables outside are taken. Does that suit you?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Si vous n’avez rien d’autre.","en":"If you have nothing else.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors je note. C’est à quel nom ?","en":"Alright, I’ll note that down. What’s the name?","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Ferré, comme le chanteur. Mais rassurez-vous : je ne chanterai pas.","en":"Ferré, like the singer. But rest assured: I won’t sing.","lit":"reassure-you"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Très drôle… J’ai besoin d’un numéro de portable","en":"Very funny… I need a mobile number","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"ainsi que les détails de votre carte bancaire, pour éviter une annulation de dernière minute.","en":"as well as the details of your bank card, to avoid a last-minute cancellation.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous voulez aussi mon chéquier, ou mes empreintes digitales peut-être ?","en":"Maybe you also want my cheque book or my fingerprints?","lit":"digital imprints"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est bien ça. Vous pouvez me les envoyer par texto.","en":"That’s right. You can text them to me.","lit":"send them to me by text"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(Title) The terminal -euil has exactly the same sound as -œil (lesson 32), so accueil is pronounced [ak-uh-yy]. In the verb accueillir, it precedes the y sound of the double l, hence [ak-uh-yeer]. It’s important to practise this sound, which does not exist in English."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(8) souhaitez: [soo-ay-tay]. (9) couverts: [koo-vair]. (18) texto: [teks-toh]."},{"ref":"1","text":"accueillir has many different translations. Basically, it means to welcome, but it can be used in the broader sense of to meet, to pick up, etc. Mon amie Nadège m’a accueilli à l’aéroport, My friend Nadège met me at the airport. The noun un accueil means a welcome, a reception, but the translation will depend on the context: Merci de votre accueil can mean Thanks for making me welcome or Thanks for having me. In a public building, look for the sign Accueil, the reception desk."},{"ref":"2","text":"The preposition depuis (lesson 39 line 17) translates both for and since. It is used with the present tense, not the past: Je connais cet homme depuis vingt ans, I have known that man for twenty years. Elle est malade depuis Noël, She has been sick since Christmas."},{"ref":"3","text":"Here is another common example of apocope (lesson 17 note 7), which mirrors English usage. The noun for a software routine, une application (line 6), is generally shortened to une appli, an app. This process of truncating long words is usual in technical contexts such as computing."},{"ref":"4","text":"un fil, a wire, is often used in reference to telephones, even though landlines have almost vanished. Two common expressions are passer un coup de fil, to make a phone call, give someone a ring, and au bout du fil, on the line. Do not confuse the plural les fils with the word for son: un fils / des fils. In the latter case, the singular and the plural are both pronounced [feess], compared with [feel] for fil / fils."},{"ref":"5","text":"souhaiter is a formal verb meaning to wish (for) or to hope that. It is common in polite contexts: À quelle heure souhaitez-vous venir ? What time do you wish/want to come? The noun, un souhait ([soo-ay]) means a desire, a wish. The verbal response to someone sneezing is À tes/vos souhaits ! (“to your desires”), an exclamation inducted into English as Atishoo!"},{"ref":"6","text":"un couvert, from the verb couvrir, to cover, means a place-setting in culinary contexts, hence the expression mettre le couvert, to lay the table. In a restaurant context, the noun refers to a customer or diner (known in the trade as “a cover”). Don’t confuse un couvert with un couvercle, meaning a lid or top of a container."},{"ref":"7","text":"Remember that past participles used as adjectives must agree with their noun, so Gabriel est pris ce soir / Gabriel et Marc sont pris ce soir / Lucie est prise ce soir / Toutes les tables sont prises ce soir."},{"ref":"8","text":"See lesson 26 note 2. Using an inverted pronoun, rather than est-ce que, to ask a question is formal but quite common in formal social interaction. So instead of Est-ce que cela vous convient ? the restaurant owner enquires Cela vous convient-il ? (Listen to the liaison between the final t and the initial vowel.)"},{"ref":"9","text":"This is our first encounter with the future tense, which, in contrast to English, is formed not with an auxiliary but by changing the verb ending: chanter, to sing → je chanterai."},{"ref":"10","text":"Despite the prevalence of English, French has succeeded in coining “native” terms in the field of technology. One example is un texto, an SMS. Donne-moi ton numéro et je t’envoie un texto tout à l’heure, Give me your number and I’ll send you an SMS later. Even so, many young people in France (though not in Canada) opt for the English term un SMS. Which is unnecessary because the verb texter means… to text!"},{"ref":"","text":"Léo Ferré (1916–1993) was a French singer, poet and composer who marked several generations of fans with his music, writing and politics. Ferré, along with his contemporaries Georges Brassens and Jacques Brel, was un auteur-compositeur-interprète (“author, composer, interpreter”), the nearest equivalent to the singer-songwriter typical of Anglo-American music. That tradition, known as la chanson (the song), attaches equal value to the music and the lyrics. More recently, the importance of the written word in French music has spawned several generations of rap and hip-hop artists who have become household names. Song lyrics can be hard to follow for language learners, but the pleasure of choosing a single text and learning it by heart is definitely worth the effort."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ce soir, à vingt heures trente, nous accueillons la grande chanteuse et comédienne, Elsa Violet.","a":"This evening at eight thirty we are welcoming the great singer and actress Elsa Violet."},{"q":"Michel, j’ai ton fils au bout du fil. Je lui dis d’attendre deux minutes ?","a":"Michel, I have your son on the line. Do I tell him to wait two minutes?"},{"q":"Vous souhaitez venir à quelle heure ?","a":"What time do you want to come?"},{"q":"Ça ne me convient pas, mais si vous n’avez rien d’autre…","a":"That doesn’t suit me, but if you don’t have anything else…"},{"q":"J’essaie de passer un coup de fil, mais la ligne sonne toujours occupé.","a":"I’m trying to give him a ring, but the line is still engaged/busy."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We have been trying to contact the reception for an hour but with no luck (success).","t":"Nous {{essayons}} de contacter {{l’accueil depuis}} une heure, mais {{sans succès}}."},{"cue":"How many are you and what time do you want to come?","t":"{{Combien êtes-vous}} et {{à quelle heure souhaitez-vous}} venir ?"},{"cue":"Chantal has been ill since Christmas. – Oh, really? What’s wrong?","t":"Chantal {{est malade depuis}} Noël. – {{Ah bon}} ? Qu’est-ce {{qui ne va pas}} ?"},{"cue":"The big table outside is taken, but you can have dinner inside if you wish.","t":"La grande table {{dehors est prise}}, mais {{vous pouvez dîner à l’intérieur}} si vous voulez."},{"cue":"Do you need a mobile number? – Yes, to avoid a last-minute cancellation.","t":"{{Avez-vous besoin d’}}un numéro de portable ? – Oui, {{pour éviter}} une annulation {{de dernière minute}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"l’accueil (m)","en":"reception desk; welcome","zh":"前台；接待；欢迎","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous essayons de contacter l’accueil depuis une heure, mais sans succès.","exEn":"We have been trying to contact the reception for an hour but with no luck.","src":0},{"fr":"Je suis bien au restaurant… ?","en":"Have I reached the restaurant…? (phone)","zh":"请问是……餐厅吗？（打电话）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allô, bonjour. Je suis bien au restaurant “La Fleur d’Argent” ?","exEn":"Hello, good morning. Have I reached the restaurant “The Silver Flower”?","src":1},{"fr":"j’essaie de vous joindre","en":"I’m trying to reach you","zh":"我在设法联系您","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’essaie de vous joindre depuis deux jours.","exEn":"I’ve been trying to reach you for two days.","src":3},{"fr":"ça sonne toujours occupé","en":"it always rings busy","zh":"总是占线","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça sonne toujours occupé.","exEn":"The line always rings busy.","src":3},{"fr":"il est malade depuis Noël","en":"he has been sick since Christmas","zh":"他从圣诞节起一直生病","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"depuis + present tense = “for/since”, not a past tense","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Chantal est malade depuis Noël.","exEn":"Chantal has been ill since Christmas.","src":0},{"fr":"nous sommes complets","en":"we are full (booked up)","zh":"我们客满了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ complete","ff":"complet = full / fully booked, not “complete”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le téléphone n’arrête pas de sonner et nous sommes complets presque tous les soirs.","exEn":"The phone hasn’t stopped ringing and we are full almost every night.","src":5},{"fr":"au bout du fil","en":"on the line (phone)","zh":"在电话那头","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “feel”: you feel a human voice at the end of the wire","ex":"Je préfère avoir une voix humaine au bout du fil.","exEn":"I prefer to have a human voice at the other end of the line.","src":7},{"fr":"À quelle heure souhaitez-vous dîner ?","en":"What time do you wish to have dinner?","zh":"您想几点用晚餐？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Combien êtes-vous et à quelle heure souhaitez-vous dîner ?","exEn":"How many are you and at what time do you wish to have dinner?","src":8},{"fr":"Il me reste une petite table.","en":"I still have a small table.","zh":"我还剩一张小桌子。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, il me reste une petite table pour quatre.","exEn":"Yes, I still have a small table for four.","src":10},{"fr":"Cela vous convient-il ?","en":"Does that suit you?","zh":"这样您可以吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Cela vous convient-il ?","exEn":"Does that suit you?","src":11},{"fr":"si vous n’avez rien d’autre","en":"if you have nothing else","zh":"如果您没有别的了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si vous n’avez rien d’autre.","exEn":"If you have nothing else.","src":12},{"fr":"C’est à quel nom ?","en":"What’s the name (for the booking)?","zh":"用谁的名字订？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Alors je note. C’est à quel nom ?","exEn":"Alright, I’ll note that down. What’s the name?","src":13},{"fr":"j’ai besoin d’un numéro de portable","en":"I need a mobile number","zh":"我需要一个手机号码","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai besoin d’un numéro de portable","exEn":"I need a mobile number","src":15},{"fr":"une annulation de dernière minute","en":"a last-minute cancellation","zh":"最后一刻的取消","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"≈ annulation = annulment/cancellation","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…pour éviter une annulation de dernière minute.","exEn":"…to avoid a last-minute cancellation.","src":16},{"fr":"un texto","en":"a text message (SMS)","zh":"短信","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ text","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez me les envoyer par texto.","exEn":"You can text them to me.","src":18}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":209,"gaps":[[1.92,3.92],[5.68,8.94],[83.18,87.16],[89.74,91.76],[97.42,100.36],[104.26,107.76],[109.66,112.66],[115.72,118.98],[122.38,126.32]],"dur":126.3},{"n":42,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que vous avez vidé la corbeille à papier ce matin ?","en":"Did you empty the wastepaper basket this morning?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui. Pourquoi ? Avez-vous perdu quelque chose ?","en":"Yes, I did. Why? Have you lost something?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne trouve pas mon chéquier, mais je l’ai peut-être oublié à la maison ce matin.","en":"I can’t find my cheque book, but perhaps I left it at home this morning.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis sûr que je l’ai vu quelque part. Sur votre bureau, peut-être ?","en":"I’m sure that I’ve seen it somewhere. On your desk, perhaps?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, j’ai regardé mais il n’y est pas. Mais où est-ce que je l’ai mis ?","en":"No, I’ve looked but it’s not there. But where did I put it?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais attendez, je suis bête ! Je l’ai laissé à l’accueil tout à l’heure.","en":"But wait, I’m stupid! I left it at the reception desk earlier.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Heureusement que vous l’avez trouvé !","en":"Luckily you found it!","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai oublié de vous dire que Cécile Durieux, la comédienne, a appelé tout à l’heure.","en":"I forgot to tell you that Cécile Durieux, the actress, called earlier.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah bon ? Je suis étonné. Qu’est-ce qu’elle veut ?","en":"Really? I’m surprised. What does she want?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Elle dit qu’elle essaie de vous joindre depuis quelques jours.","en":"She said that she has been trying to get in touch with you for several days.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Depuis mardi dernier, précisément. Elle a laissé plusieurs messages.","en":"Since last Tuesday, precisely. She has left several messages.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Pouvez-vous la rappeler ? Son numéro est le 07 70 80 90.","en":"Can you call her back? Her number is 07 70 80 90.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Participles – 1.1 Le participe présent (Present participle)","text":"Present participles, seen in lesson 22 note 9 and lesson 23 note 6, are used less than in English because there is no progressive verb form (to be + -ing) in French. For most regular and irregular verbs, the participle is formed by dropping the ending from the first person plural of the present tense and adding -ant to the root:\npenser → pensons → pensant;\ndormir → dormons → dormant;\nperdre → perdons → perdant.\n(Remember that verbs ending -cer take a cedilla in the first person plural, and thus in the participle (commencer → commençons → commençant), while -ger verbs take an e: manger → mangeons → mangeant.)\nPresent participles are used in two ways. First, as in English, to describe an ongoing action:\nLes personnes habitant près d’un aéroport souffrent souvent de maux de tête.\nPeople living near an airport often get headaches.\nIn this case, the participle does not agree with the noun. A similar usage is in a clause starting with the preposition en:\nEn vérifiant la cave, j’ai trouvé la porte ouverte.\nWhile a literal (and literary) translation of that first clause would be While (or upon) checking the cellar…, it is easier to recast the sentence: When I checked the cellar, I found the door open. The structure can be very different:\nIl arrive. En attendant, commençons à nettoyer la cuisine.\nHe’s on his way. In the meantime, let’s start cleaning the kitchen.\nThe other main use of the present participle is as an adjective, in which case it has to agree with the noun: C’est une histoire intéressante."},{"ref":"rev","title":"1.2 Le participe passé (Past participle)","text":"In addition to functioning as adjectives, past participles are used to form le passé composé (see below). They are therefore important.\n• For Group 1 verbs (-er), simply drop the final -r and replace the -e- with -é. Hence commander → commandé, fermer → fermé, etc. There are no exceptions.\n• The rule for Group 2 (regular verbs ending in -ir with the present participle in -issant) is even simpler: simply drop the last letter: finir → fini, réussir → réussi.\n• For Group 3 verbs (-re, -oir, -ir with a present participle in -ant), the rules are:\n-re is replaced by -u: vendre → vendu; attendre → attendu\n-oir verbs also end in -u: recevoir → reçu; vouloir → voulu\n-ir behave mostly as regular Group 2 verbs: partir → parti; sentir → senti\nThere are, however, many exceptions, notably frequently used verbs such as prendre → pris; faire → fait; dire → dit; courir → couru; mettre → mis; and, more importantly, the auxiliaries être → été, and avoir → eu. Two other common verbs with irregular past participles are pouvoir → pu, and savoir → su.\nWhen you learn a new verb, especially one from Group 3, always check the past participle.\nFinally, when a past participle is used alone as an adjective, it has to agree in number and gender with its noun:\nToutes les tables sont prises, All the tables are taken;\nCette robe est vendue en plusieurs couleurs, This dress is sold in several colours."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Le passé composé – the perfect tense","text":"The perfect tense is known as le passé composé because it is “composed” of two parts: an auxiliary and a past participle. It is used in the spoken and written language to translate two English tenses: the past simple and the present perfect. There is an equivalent tense, le passé simple (“simple” because it consists of a single word), which we cover only briefly at the end of this book because it is used chiefly in literature and, occasionally, very formal speech.\nThe usual auxiliary is avoir, although some verbs take être, as we shall see in the next set of lessons. The past participle sometimes has to agree with the direct object of the sentence, but we will look at this rule in more detail later on, particularly because it rarely affects the pronunciation.\n• Group 1: manger, to eat\nj’ai mangé, I ate; nous avons mangé, we ate\ntu as mangé, you ate; vous avez mangé, you ate\nil/elle a mangé, he/she/it ate; ils/elles ont mangé, they ate\n• Group 2: finir, to finish\nj’ai fini, I finished; nous avons fini, we finished\ntu as fini, you finished; vous avez fini, you finished\nil/elle a fini, he/she/it finished; ils/elles ont fini, they finished\n• Group 3: voir, to see\nj’ai vu, I saw; nous avons vu, we saw\ntu as vu, you saw; vous avez vu, you saw\nil/elle a vu, he/she/it saw; ils/elles ont vu, they saw\nThe negative is formed by placing ne … pas around the auxiliary: je n’ai pas mangé, nous n’avons pas fini, ils n’ont pas vu.\nThe three forms of the interrogative are (i) a rising tone Tu as vu ? (ii) with est-ce que: Est-ce que tu as vu ? or (iii) inversion: As-tu vu ?\nThe perfect tense relates to actions that took place in the past and are now complete. In this context, it is equivalent to the past simple tense in English: J’ai vu le notaire hier, I saw the notary yesterday.\nBut if we remove the time marker hier, the French verb remains the same (J’ai vu le notaire), whereas the English equivalent is either I saw or the present perfect, I have seen the notary.\nWhen adverbs such as jamais and toujours are used with le passé composé, the verb is translated with the present perfect:\nJ’ai toujours voulu / Je n’ai jamais voulu apprendre le français.\nI’ve always/never wanted to learn French.\nIn some cases, however, particularly with the preposition depuis, which translates both for and since, French uses the present rather than a perfect tense:\nElle travaille ici depuis deux mois / depuis l’année dernière.\nShe has been working here for two months / since last year.\nLast, while English automatically uses the past tense to describe an action or sequence of actions that have taken place and are now complete, French tends to use the present form of the verb. This narrative device, known as le présent historique, or présent de narration, historic or narrative present, brings the events to life and makes them more compelling:\nHier, je vais au marché pour faire les courses mais j’oublie mon portefeuille.\nYesterday, I went to the market to do the shopping but I forgot my wallet.\nOf course, le passé composé can also be used in these cases, with absolutely no difference in meaning.\nTo sum up, le passé composé does the work of both the simple past and the present perfect tenses in English and, for that reason, is easier to use. For instance:\nJ’ai vu Max à Noël mais je ne l’ai pas vu depuis.\nI saw Max at Christmas but I haven’t seen him since."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Comparatives and superlatives","text":"• The comparative of superiority is always formed with plus before the adjective and que after it:\nLondres est plus grand que Paris., London is bigger than Paris.\nUnlike English, the length of the adjective does not matter:\nParis est plus cher que Bruxelles.\nParis is more expensive than Brussels.\n• The same rule applies to comparisons of inferiority, formed with moins … que:\nLe film est moins intéressant que le livre.\nThe movie is less interesting than the book.\n(In some cases, particularly moins cher, English can choose between the superior or the inferior comparative: it’s cheaper / less expensive.)\n• To form the superlative, simply put the definite article le or la for the singular (masculine/feminine) and les for the plural before plus or moins:\nLe bus est le moyen le plus rapide d’aller à Lille.\nThe bus is the quickest way to get to Lille;\nC’est l’arrondissement le moins bruyant de la ville.\nIt’s the least noisy district in the city.\nA handful of adjectives are irregular, notably bon, bien and mauvais:\nAdjective – Comparative – Superlative\nbon, good – meilleur (que), better (than) – le meilleur / la meilleure, the best\nbien, good/well – mieux (que), better (than) – le mieux, the best\nmauvais, bad – pire (que), worse (than) – le/la pire, the worst\nBien can be confusing at first because it is an adjective (good) but also an adverb (well). As we will see later, it is invariable when used adjectivally: Elles sont très bien, tes nouvelles chaussures, Your new shoes are very nice.\nFor adverbs, the superlative is formed with le (never la) before the comparative plus: le plus rapidement possible, as fast as possible (of course, there is no agreement)."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 On the phone","text":"Using the telephone can be difficult, even in one’s native language, for many reasons. In a foreign language, those difficulties are compounded by rapid speech, an unfamiliar vocabulary and, usually, being unable to see the person you are talking to. And although texting, videoconferencing and other forms of digital communication are omnipresent, being able to communicate by phone – téléphone (portable) (mobile) or fixe (landline) – is still an important skill to learn. (Interestingly, although most communications today are wireless, the familiar expression for a phone call is still un coup de fil!)\nHere are some of the set phrases used in most conversations.\n• Allô is used when answering the phone or checking that the person you are calling can hear you. (One variant is Allô oui ?, although some people reply simply Oui ?, which is just as abrupt as Yes? in English.) To enquire about the identity of the caller, use the expression Qui est à l’appareil ? Who is calling? (see lesson 31).\n• To confirm that you have reached the right number, say Je suis bien chez… ? (for a person) or Je suis bien au/à la… (for a business, an administration, etc.)\n• If you ask to speak to someone other than the person answering, you will usually be asked: C’est de la part de qui ? (or, more succinctly and less politely, De la part de qui ?) Who shall I say is calling?\n• Before transferring the call, the person will say Ne quittez/quitte pas (Hold the line) or Je vous le/la passe, I’ll put you through to him/her, or both phrases together.\n• If the line is engaged, the message will be La ligne est occupée or Ça sonne occupé. If the person you wish to speak to is busy, you might want to leave them a message: Est-ce que je peux laisser un message ? or have them call you back: Est-ce qu’il/qu’elle peut me rappeler s’il vous plaît ? Or you can always say that you will call back later: Je rappellerai plus tard.\n• If you cannot hear the other person, tell them Je ne vous entends pas, parlez plus fort s’il vous plaît. And if you have difficulty hearing or understanding, use one of these phrases: Je ne vous entends pas bien, I can’t hear you very well, or Pouvez-vous répéter lentement s’il vous plaît ? Can you repeat slowly please? (Remember that simply asking the person to repeat does not guarantee that they will slow down or enunciate more clearly!)\nOne slight difference between French and Anglo-American usage is that phone numbers are written and pronounced in two-digit groups. For example 06 32 72 91 would be given as zéro six / trente-deux / soixante-douze / quatre-vingt-onze. A word of advice: when taking down a number, pause if you hear soixante… because the next digit could be, for example, deux (62) or douze (72). The same applies to quatre-vingt…, which might be continued as quatre-vingt-quatre (84) or quatre-vingt-quatorze (94). Remember: practice makes perfect!"},{"ref":"rev","title":"Closing note","text":"In the past six lessons, you have expanded your knowledge of the different language registers that you’re likely to encounter on a regular basis, either directly through conversation or indirectly through movies, books or videos. When you come back to this lesson series in the second part, or productive phase, of the learning process, you will realise just how much you have learned in a relatively short space of time."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"plus grand que","en":"bigger than","zh":"比……大","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Londres est plus grand que Paris.","exEn":"London is bigger than Paris.","src":0},{"fr":"moins intéressant que","en":"less interesting than","zh":"不如……有趣","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ interesting","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le film est moins intéressant que le livre.","exEn":"The movie is less interesting than the book.","src":0},{"fr":"le moyen le plus rapide","en":"the quickest way","zh":"最快的方式","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ rapid","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le bus est le moyen le plus rapide d’aller à Lille.","exEn":"The bus is the quickest way to get to Lille.","src":0},{"fr":"Qui est à l’appareil ?","en":"Who is calling?","zh":"请问是哪位？（电话）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qui est à l’appareil ?","exEn":"Who is calling?","src":0},{"fr":"Ne quittez pas.","en":"Hold the line.","zh":"请别挂电话。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ne quittez pas.","exEn":"Hold the line.","src":0},{"fr":"Est-ce que je peux laisser un message ?","en":"Can I leave a message?","zh":"我可以留言吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ message","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce que je peux laisser un message ?","exEn":"Can I leave a message?","src":0},{"fr":"Je ne vous entends pas bien.","en":"I can’t hear you very well.","zh":"我听不太清楚您说的。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne vous entends pas bien.","exEn":"I can’t hear you very well.","src":0},{"fr":"Je ne trouve pas mon chéquier.","en":"I can’t find my cheque book.","zh":"我找不到我的支票本。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne trouve pas mon chéquier, mais je l’ai peut-être oublié à la maison ce matin.","exEn":"I can’t find my cheque book, but perhaps I left it at home this morning.","src":3},{"fr":"j’ai oublié de vous dire que…","en":"I forgot to tell you that…","zh":"我忘了告诉您……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai oublié de vous dire que Cécile Durieux, la comédienne, a appelé tout à l’heure.","exEn":"I forgot to tell you that Cécile Durieux, the actress, called earlier.","src":8}],"uncertain":["Table in section 3: OCR shows “le mieux / la mieux” for bien; corrected to “le mieux” (adverb superlative is invariable).","Section 4 text: “Pouvez-vous répétez len tement” corrected to “répéter lentement”."],"pdfPage":215,"gaps":[[1.72,3.6],[5.1,8.26],[29.44,30.66],[61.74,65.62]],"dur":65.6},{"n":43,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le foot féminin","titleEn":"Women’s football","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai trouvé cet article, sur le-foot.fr, qui peut t’intéresser.","en":"I found this article on le-foot.fr, which may interest you.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"“Manuel Alonso est nommé entraîneur de l’équipe nationale de football féminine,","en":"“Manuel Alonso has been named trainer of the women’s national football team,","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"succédant à Madame Manon Bardouin, qui est partie à la retraite après une carrière bien remplie.","en":"taking over from Mrs Manon Bardouin, who has retired after a fulfilled career.","lit":"left to the retirement; well filled"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est né en Espagne en mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf. Ses parents sont morts dans un accident de la route.","en":"He was born in Spain in 1999. His parents died in a road accident.","lit":"is born; are dead"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Peu de temps après, Manuel est venu vivre avec son oncle et sa tante dans le sud de la France.","en":"Shortly after, Manuel came to live with his uncle and his aunt in the south of France.","lit":"little time; is come"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est allé au lycée à Nice et, ensuite, est monté à Paris pour faire des études à l’université.","en":"He went to high school in Nice and then went up to Paris to study at university.","lit":"is gone; is mounted"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est entré à la faculté de droit, où il est devenu un fan de sports d’équipe.","en":"He went to law school, where he became a fan of team sports.","lit":"is entered; faculty of law; is become"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Un soir, il est allé voir un match international féminin entre les Pays-Bas et l’Allemagne.","en":"One evening, he went to a women’s international match between the Netherlands and Germany.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Le jeu lui a vraiment plu et il a pris la décision de travailler comme entraîneur dans ce sport en Europe.”","en":"He really liked the game and he decided to work as a coach in that sport in Europe.”","lit":"the game to-him was really pleasing; took the decision"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce qu’il est rentré en Espagne ?","en":"Did he go back to Spain?","lit":"is re-entered"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, il est retourné à la fac pour étudier la gestion sportive. L’article continue :","en":"No, he returned to the university to study sports management. The article continues:","lit":"is returned; sporty management"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"“Manuel est sorti trois ans plus tard, avec son diplôme en poche et plusieurs offres d’emploi.","en":"“Manuel came out three years later, with a diploma in his pocket and several job offers.","lit":"is exited"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est allé travailler avec l’équipe de Rennes, où il est resté longtemps.","en":"He went to work with the Rennes team, where he stayed a long time.","lit":"is remained"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Après cette expérience, il est passé d’un club à un autre pendant quelque temps","en":"After that experience, he went from one club to another for some time","lit":"is passed"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"avant d’être nommé au poste d’entraîneur officiel de l’équipe féminine de Bordeaux.","en":"before being appointed to the post of official trainer of the Bordeaux women’s team.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est arrivé au bon moment. L’équipe est montée très vite en haut du classement,","en":"He arrived at the right time. The team quickly rose to the top of the ranking,","lit":"good moment; is mounted"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"et le directeur lui a demandé de rester.”","en":"and the director asked him to stay.”","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais alors, pour quelle raison ? Pourquoi choisir un entraîneur et pas une entraîneuse ?","en":"But then, for what reason? Why choose a male trainer and not a female trainer?","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonne question ! Je ne peux pas l’expliquer facilement.","en":"Good question! I can’t explain it easily.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Personne ne le sait vraiment.","en":"No one really knows.","lit":"no-one not it knows"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The names of many sports have been imported into French as masculine nouns. They include le tennis, le golf, le squash and le volley-ball. In a couple of cases, the names have been shortened. This is notably the case of le football → le foot and le basket-ball → le basket (the final t is pronounced). However, not all French sports have English names (for example, la natation, swimming) so always check in a dictionary."},{"ref":"2","text":"When making the agreement between a compound noun and an adjective, always look for the root word, in this case équipe, which is feminine (une équipe féminine, see line 15). With the noun l’équipe de football, the adjective will agree with the head word, l’équipe, not the second noun, football. By contrast, with a single noun, the agreement is simple: le football féminin."},{"ref":"3","text":"Certain verbs are conjugated in the perfect tense with être instead of avoir when used intransitively. This lesson contains the main ones you will need to know, such as partir, to leave: je suis parti, tu es parti, il est parti, nous sommes partis, vous êtes partis, ils sont partis. Another major difference with avoir-conjugated verbs is that the past participle agrees with the subject (see lesson 42), thus Monsieur Grégoire est parti / Madame Grégoire est partie."},{"ref":"4","text":"naître is a very irregular verb, meaning to be born. It is conjugated with être in the perfect tense, so il est né / elle est née means he/she was born (not is). (The noun from naître is la naissance, birth, see lesson 23.) The same conjugation rule applies to mourir, to die: je suis mort/morte, tu es mort/-e, il/elle est mort/-e, etc."},{"ref":"5","text":"plaire (past participle: plu) means to please (see lesson 32 line 13) but is used with the preposition à and an indirect object as a synonym for to like. In this case, the word order is the reverse of English: La nouvelle maison plaît à la famille, The family likes the new house (literally: “The new house is pleasing to the family”). If a pronoun replaces the noun, it comes before the verb: Le film lui a plu, He liked the film. The same rule applies, for example, to demander à: Je vais demander à l’entraîneur → Je vais lui demander, I’m going to ask the coach / him or her. When learning a new verb, make sure to check the preposition, if any, that goes with it."},{"ref":"6","text":"Masculine adjectives ending in -if form the feminine with -ive: sportif → sportive (sporty), créatif → créative (creative), agressif → agressive (aggressive). Note how the feminine form is often used in English as an adjective."},{"ref":"7","text":"quelque temps is an indefinite (and thus invariable) adjective describing a short time period: a while, or the literal translation some time: Je ne les ai pas vus depuis quelque temps, I haven’t seen them for a while. Contrast this with longtemps (line 13), a long time, written as a single word. (Don’t confuse quelque temps with the single word quelquefois, sometimes.)"},{"ref":"8","text":"Adding the definite article to bon, good, changes the meaning slightly, to correct, proper, or right: Tu es arrivé au bon moment, You came at the right time. Ce n’est pas la bonne question, That’s not the right question. One of the all-time classic guides to French grammar (written by a Belgian) is Le Bon Usage, which translates as proper usage."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here, personne is not a noun (lesson 19 line 3) but an indefinite pronoun, meaning no one, nobody, anyone. It can be either the subject or the object of a verb, and the accompanying verb is always in the negative: Personne ne le sait, No one knows; Je ne connais personne ici, I don’t know anyone here."},{"ref":"","text":"The French school system is divided into four levels: l’école maternelle (“maternal” or nursery school), l’école primaire (primary school), le collège (roughly equivalent to the middle school in the UK system) and le lycée (secondary school). Students who graduate with the school-leaving certificate, le baccalauréat (known colloquially as le bac) can go on to l’université (university), which comprises several facultés (faculties). However, that word – often abbreviated to la fac – is also used in a familiar register as a synonym for university."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Deux de mes sports préférés sont le basket et le foot. – Moi, je préfère la natation.","a":"Two of my favourite sports are basketball and football. – I prefer swimming."},{"q":"J’ai parlé à Simon Perret il y a quelque temps. Tu le connais ?","a":"I talked to Simon Perret a little while ago. Do you know him?"},{"q":"Tu arrives au bon moment. J’ai besoin de ton aide.","a":"You’ve arrived at just the right time. I need your help."},{"q":"Je ne sais pas pourquoi, mais mon idée ne leur a pas plu.","a":"I don’t know why, but they didn’t like my idea."},{"q":"La date de naissance de votre femme est le trois janvier deux mille, n’est-ce pas ?","a":"Your wife’s date of birth is the third of January 2000, isn’t it?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Luc hasn’t said anything, so nobody knows whether he liked the present. – I’m going to ask his wife.","t":"Luc {{n’a rien}} dit, donc {{personne ne sait}} si le cadeau {{lui a plu}}. – Je vais {{demander à sa}} femme."},{"cue":"Élodie left early this morning. I think she went to the university.","t":"Élodie {{est partie}} tôt ce matin. Je pense {{qu’elle est allée}} à {{la faculté}}."},{"cue":"His father was born in Spain but came to France at the age of 20. He died in Lyon in 1999.","t":"Son père {{est né}} en Espagne mais {{est venu en}} France à l’âge de vingt ans. Il {{est mort à}} Lyon {{en mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf}}."},{"cue":"André retired last year, but he is still active, sporty and creative.","t":"André {{est parti}} à la {{retraite}} l’année dernière, mais il est toujours {{actif}}, {{sportif}} et {{créatif}}."},{"cue":"I asked him if he went to work on Monday. He told me no.","t":"Je {{lui ai demandé}} s’il {{est allé}} travailler {{lundi}}. Il {{m’a dit}} “non”."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un entraîneur","en":"a coach, trainer","zh":"教练","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"sounds like “on-tray-nur”: a coach carrying a tray of water for the players","ex":"Manuel Alonso est nommé entraîneur de l’équipe nationale de football féminine,","exEn":"Manuel Alonso has been named trainer of the women’s national football team,","src":2},{"fr":"une équipe (f)","en":"a team","zh":"队伍","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est entré à la faculté de droit, où il est devenu un fan de sports d’équipe.","exEn":"He went to law school, where he became a fan of team sports.","src":7},{"fr":"elle est partie à la retraite","en":"she retired","zh":"她退休了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…qui est partie à la retraite après une carrière bien remplie.","exEn":"…who has retired after a fulfilled career.","src":3},{"fr":"il est né en Espagne","en":"he was born in Spain","zh":"他出生在西班牙","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"être, not avoir, with naître","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est né en Espagne en mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix-neuf.","exEn":"He was born in Spain in 1999.","src":4},{"fr":"un accident de la route","en":"a road accident","zh":"交通事故","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ accident","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ses parents sont morts dans un accident de la route.","exEn":"His parents died in a road accident.","src":4},{"fr":"il est allé au lycée","en":"he went to high school","zh":"他上了高中","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est allé au lycée à Nice et, ensuite, est monté à Paris pour faire des études à l’université.","exEn":"He went to high school in Nice and then went up to Paris to study at university.","src":6},{"fr":"le jeu lui a plu","en":"he liked the game","zh":"他喜欢这场比赛","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"plaire à = to like (word order reversed)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le jeu lui a vraiment plu et il a pris la décision de travailler comme entraîneur…","exEn":"He really liked the game and he decided to work as a coach…","src":9},{"fr":"il a pris la décision de travailler","en":"he decided to work","zh":"他决定去工作","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ decision (-sion = -sion)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…il a pris la décision de travailler comme entraîneur dans ce sport en Europe.","exEn":"…he decided to work as a coach in that sport in Europe.","src":9},{"fr":"il est resté longtemps","en":"he stayed a long time","zh":"他待了很久","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Il est allé travailler avec l’équipe de Rennes, où il est resté longtemps.","exEn":"He went to work with the Rennes team, where he stayed a long time.","src":13},{"fr":"il y a quelque temps","en":"a while ago","zh":"前一阵子","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"J’ai parlé à Simon Perret il y a quelque temps.","exEn":"I talked to Simon Perret a little while ago.","src":0},{"fr":"plusieurs offres d’emploi","en":"several job offers","zh":"几个工作机会","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Manuel est sorti trois ans plus tard, avec son diplôme en poche et plusieurs offres d’emploi.","exEn":"Manuel came out three years later, with a diploma in his pocket and several job offers.","src":12},{"fr":"un diplôme","en":"a diploma, degree","zh":"文凭","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ diploma","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…avec son diplôme en poche…","exEn":"…with his diploma in his pocket…","src":12},{"fr":"au bon moment","en":"at the right time","zh":"在恰当的时候","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Tu arrives au bon moment.","exEn":"You’ve arrived at just the right time.","src":0},{"fr":"Personne ne le sait.","en":"No one knows.","zh":"没人知道。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"personne here = “no one”, not “person”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Personne ne le sait vraiment.","exEn":"No one really knows.","src":20}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 5: the answer line reads “l’a dit”; the French “Il m’a dit « non »” fits the English (“He told me no”) and the dots, so I used “m’a dit”.","ex2 item 1: the answer line gives “demander à”, but the dots before “femme” cover “demander à sa”, so “sa” is inside the gap."],"pdfPage":223,"gaps":[[2.06,4.14],[5.64,8.7],[43.74,44.86],[89.88,90.96],[108.18,111.4],[113.84,115.9],[122.24,125.38],[128.88,132.06],[134.86,138.18],[141.1,144.2],[147.74,151.86]],"dur":151.9},{"n":44,"kind":"lesson","title":"Perturbations dans les transports…","titleEn":"Transport disruptions…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Si vous n’avez pas un titre de transport électronique, madame,","en":"If you don’t have an e-ticket, madam,","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"vous pouvez acheter votre billet dans le distributeur automatique au bout du quai.","en":"you can buy your ticket in the automatic machine at the end of the platform.","lit":"dispenser"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci, mais il m’en faut deux : un aller simple pour moi et un aller-retour pour mon cousin.","en":"Thanks, but I need two: a single for me and a return for my cousin.","lit":"a to-go simple; a to-go-return"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Dans ce cas, la billetterie est là-bas, vers les consignes et les objets trouvés.","en":"In that case, the ticket office is over there, towards the left-luggage lockers and the lost property office.","lit":"found objects"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous voyez les deux files d’attente devant les guichets ?","en":"You see those two queues in front of the ticket offices?","lit":"lines of wait"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Prenez celle de gauche : elle avance plus vite que l’autre… enfin, moins lentement. […]","en":"Take the one on the left: it’s moving faster than the other one… well, less slowly. …","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est bon, Jules, j’ai les billets. Je vais les composter.","en":"It’s okay, Jules, I have the tickets. I’m going to time-stamp them.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Fais attention à ne pas les mettre dans le mauvais sens dans la machine !","en":"Be careful not to put them the wrong way around in the machine!","lit":"do attention; bad direction"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est énervant ! On affiche partout “Le compostage est obligatoire”,","en":"That’s annoying! There are signs everywhere saying: “Time-stamping is obligatory,”","lit":"one posts everywhere"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"mais toutes les machines sont hors service et je n’ai pas pu valider les billets.","en":"but all the machines are out of order and I couldn’t validate the tickets.","lit":"out of service"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"À ce moment-là, il faut le signaler au contrôleur à bord du train.","en":"In that case, you have to report it to the inspector on board the train.","lit":"at that moment"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Veux-tu de l’aide pour porter ton sac ? Il a l’air très lourd.","en":"Do you want help carrying your bag? It looks very heavy.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Non merci, il est grand mais en réalité il est assez léger.","en":"No thanks, it’s big but it’s actually quite light.","lit":"in reality"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Attends une minute, je regarde le tableau d’affichage des trains de grandes lignes :","en":"Wait a minute, I’m looking at the display board for the main line trains:","lit":"trains of big lines"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"“Circulation perturbée : Nancy : Train en panne ; Colmar : Retard ; Mulhouse : Annulé”.","en":"“Traffic disrupted: Nancy: Train broken down; Colmar: Late; Mulhouse: Cancelled.”","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Il n’y a même pas de trains omnibus ! C’est toujours la même chose et j’en ai marre !","en":"There aren’t even any stopping trains! It’s always the same thing and I’m fed up!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"À chaque fois il y a des soucis avec ces lignes, vrai ou faux ?","en":"Every time there are issues with these lines, true or false?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis d’accord, mais il vaut mieux prendre notre mal en patience.","en":"I agree, but it’s better to grin and bear it.","lit":"am of accord; take our pain in patience"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Il n’en est pas question. Je vais plutôt prendre le car !","en":"Out of the question. I’m going to take the coach instead!","lit":"it not of-it is question"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(8) sens: the final s is pronounced: [sonss], as is the case for fils (son/sons, lesson 41 note 4) and for plus (more) at the end of a sentence."},{"ref":"1","text":"We know that un titre means a title (book, movie, job, etc., lesson 17 line 11). By extension it also means a deed or a right, as found in expressions like un titre de transport, a ticket, travel card or other “document of carriage”. As usual, context is all-important: when buying a train ticket, you would ask for un billet but an inspector would likely ask you to show your titre de transport. (Remember that un ticket is used for tickets on le métro.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"un quai is the origin of the English cognate quay (it also means wharf). More broadly, it designates an embankment or riverside road. In a train station, un quai is a platform, although the synonym une voie (“a way”) is also used, particularly in passenger announcements: Le train partira de la voie numéro trois, The train will depart from platform number three."},{"ref":"3","text":"In a travel context, un billet aller simple (or just un aller simple) means a one-way or single ticket. A return (or round-trip) ticket is un (billet) aller-retour. The term for a season ticket is une carte d’abonnement, literally “a subscription card”, or simply un abonnement (from the verb s’abonner, to take out a subscription)."},{"ref":"4","text":"In some cases, mauvais (fem. mauvaise), bad, can mean wrong: Vous avez fait un mauvais numéro, You’ve got the wrong number. It is usually used as an attributive adjective and comes before the noun, without a connecting verb. However, there are several other ways of saying wrong, including faux (line 17), as we shall see later on."},{"ref":"5","text":"un sens generally means a sense, in terms of either rationality, L’écologie est une question de bon sens, Ecology is a question of common sense, or the ability to perceive something: Il a un grand sens de l’humour, He has a great sense of humour. But sens can also relate to direction: Vous n’allez pas dans le bon sens, You’re not going the right way. When buying a train ticket, you can select a seat that faces forward: dans le sens de la marche, literally “the direction of walking”, i.e. movement."},{"ref":"6","text":"The suffix -age can be added to the root of some verbs to make a substantive noun. Thus composter means to time-stamp or punch a ticket, hence the noun le compostage: time-stamping/punching. (Context is all-important here, because the verb also means to compost!) Likewise, afficher, to display, gives us l’affichage, display or billposting."},{"ref":"7","text":"marre, which is loosely related to la mer, the sea and hence the sense of discomfort or irritation brought on by sea-sickness, is found in the idiomatic expression en avoir marre, to be fed up. This can be used simply as an exclamation J’en ai marre ! I’m fed up!, I’ve had enough!, or with a direct object: Il en a marre de son travail, He’s fed up with his job. (Being idiomatic, the expression uses both the indirect object – son travail – and the pronoun en, which would usually replace it.)"},{"ref":"8","text":"The adjective faux (fem. fausse) has several meanings, all related to things that are incorrect, fake or false. For example, un faux billet is a forged ticket or forged bank note while, in music, une fausse note is a wrong note. The expression C’est faux means It’s wrong or a falsehood. Remember, however, that to be wrong is avoir tort (see lesson 28)."},{"ref":"9","text":"The opposite of être d’accord avec, to agree with, is ne pas être d’accord avec, to disagree with: Je ne suis pas d’accord avec vous, I disagree with you. It’s simple!"},{"ref":"10","text":"vaut is the third person singular of the irregular valoir, to be worth: je vaux, tu vaux, il/elle vaut, nous valons, vous valez, ils/elles valent. Combien vaut ce tableau ? How much is this painting worth? We will see this important verb in greater detail in another lesson."},{"ref":"11","text":"mal can be an adverb meaning badly (lesson 30 line 2) as well as a noun, meaning pain or sickness, used in the invariable expression avoir mal à (lesson 24 line 3). It can also be used figuratively, with du: J’ai du mal à le comprendre, I have difficulty understanding him. (Remember that the plural is maux, lesson 32 note 8.) The expression prendre son mal en patience is equivalent to grin and bear it (note that the possessive adjective agrees with the “suffering” person: je prends mon mal / tu prends ton mal en patience, etc.)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Vous arrivez toujours au mauvais moment : c’est l’heure du déjeuner.","a":"You always come at a bad time: it’s lunchtime."},{"q":"On dit que cet auteur canadien n’a pas le sens de l’humour, mais c’est faux.","a":"They say that this Canadian author doesn’t have a sense of humour, but that’s wrong."},{"q":"La machine est hors service et ils n’ont pas pu composter les billets. C’est énervant !","a":"The machine is out of order and they weren’t able to time-stamp the tickets. How annoying!"},{"q":"Les étudiants de cette fac n’ont jamais de mal à trouver du travail, vrai ou faux ?","a":"The students in this university never find it difficult to get work, true or false?"},{"q":"Je suis désolé mais je pense que j’ai fait un mauvais numéro.","a":"I’m sorry but I think I’ve dialled the wrong number."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I know these notes are forged but how much are they really worth? – You’re wrong, they’re genuine.","t":"Je sais que {{ces billets sont faux}}, mais {{combien valent}}-ils vraiment ? – {{Tu as tort}}, ils sont vrais."},{"cue":"Your bags look very heavy. Do you want any help carrying them?","t":"Vos sacs {{ont l’air très lourds}}. Est-ce que {{vous voulez de l’aide}} pour {{les porter}} ?"},{"cue":"Three trains are cancelled. I’ve had enough! – You have to grin and bear it.","t":"Trois trains sont {{annulés}} ! {{J’en ai marre}} ! – Il faut {{prendre son mal en patience}}."},{"cue":"Are you going to take the coach? – Out of the question!","t":"Vous allez {{prendre le car}} ? – {{Il n’en est pas question}} !"},{"cue":"I have the right phone number but the wrong address. It’s better to call.","t":"J’ai le bon numéro de téléphone mais {{la mauvaise adresse}}. Il {{vaut mieux}} passer un coup de fil."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un aller simple","en":"a single (one-way) ticket","zh":"单程票","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Merci, mais il m’en faut deux : un aller simple pour moi et un aller-retour pour mon cousin.","exEn":"Thanks, but I need two: a single for me and a return for my cousin.","src":3},{"fr":"un quai","en":"a platform; a quay","zh":"站台；码头","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ quay (pronounced [kay])","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Vous pouvez acheter votre billet dans le distributeur automatique au bout du quai.","exEn":"You can buy your ticket in the automatic machine at the end of the platform.","src":2},{"fr":"une file d’attente","en":"a queue","zh":"队伍；排队","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous voyez les deux files d’attente devant les guichets ?","exEn":"You see those two queues in front of the ticket offices?","src":5},{"fr":"Elle avance plus vite que l’autre.","en":"It moves faster than the other one.","zh":"它比另一个走得快。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Prenez celle de gauche : elle avance plus vite que l’autre…","exEn":"Take the one on the left: it’s moving faster than the other one…","src":6},{"fr":"je vais composter les billets","en":"I’m going to time-stamp the tickets","zh":"我要给车票打孔/验票","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"composter = to punch/stamp a ticket (also “to compost”)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est bon, Jules, j’ai les billets. Je vais les composter.","exEn":"It’s okay, Jules, I have the tickets. I’m going to time-stamp them.","src":7},{"fr":"la machine est hors service","en":"the machine is out of order","zh":"机器坏了（停用）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…toutes les machines sont hors service et je n’ai pas pu valider les billets.","exEn":"…all the machines are out of order and I couldn’t validate the tickets.","src":10},{"fr":"il faut le signaler au contrôleur","en":"you have to report it to the inspector","zh":"您得向检票员报告","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ signal","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À ce moment-là, il faut le signaler au contrôleur à bord du train.","exEn":"In that case, you have to report it to the inspector on board the train.","src":11},{"fr":"il a l’air très lourd","en":"it looks very heavy","zh":"它看起来很重","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “loor”: a heavy bag that “lures” you to drop it","ex":"Veux-tu de l’aide pour porter ton sac ? Il a l’air très lourd.","exEn":"Do you want help carrying your bag? It looks very heavy.","src":12},{"fr":"un train en panne","en":"a broken-down train","zh":"故障的火车","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Circulation perturbée : Nancy : Train en panne ;","exEn":"Traffic disrupted: Nancy: Train broken down;","src":15},{"fr":"j’en ai marre","en":"I’m fed up","zh":"我受够了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est toujours la même chose et j’en ai marre !","exEn":"It’s always the same thing and I’m fed up!","src":16},{"fr":"Je suis d’accord.","en":"I agree.","zh":"我同意。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis d’accord, mais il vaut mieux prendre notre mal en patience.","exEn":"I agree, but it’s better to grin and bear it.","src":18},{"fr":"il vaut mieux prendre son mal en patience","en":"it’s better to grin and bear it","zh":"最好还是耐心忍耐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis d’accord, mais il vaut mieux prendre notre mal en patience.","exEn":"I agree, but it’s better to grin and bear it.","src":18},{"fr":"Il n’en est pas question.","en":"Out of the question.","zh":"绝对不可能。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il n’en est pas question. Je vais plutôt prendre le car !","exEn":"Out of the question. I’m going to take the coach instead!","src":19},{"fr":"j’ai fait un mauvais numéro","en":"I dialled the wrong number","zh":"我打错电话了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis désolé mais je pense que j’ai fait un mauvais numéro.","exEn":"I’m sorry but I think I’ve dialled the wrong number.","src":0}],"uncertain":["dialogue 18 English: OCR reads “It’s true but it’s better to grin and bear it” after “I agree”; rendered as “I agree, but it’s better…” to match the French.","dialogue 4/6: the book shows “[ … ]” (a break in the scene) after line 6 – kept in the French and English."],"pdfPage":229,"gaps":[[1.86,4],[5.82,8.8],[38.5,39.64],[97.54,101.08],[103.32,105.42],[108.86,111.72],[115.2,118.24],[123.72,126.66],[132.2,135.36],[138.1,141.74]],"dur":141.7},{"n":45,"kind":"lesson","title":"Il n’y a pas de métiers inutiles","titleEn":"There are no useless jobs","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"On m’a invitée aujourd’hui pour vous parler des réseaux sociaux professionnels.","en":"I have been invited today to talk to you about professional social networks.","lit":"one me has invited"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Bien entendu, le monde du travail a changé considérablement depuis vingt ans.","en":"Of course, the world of work has changed considerably in twenty years.","lit":"well heard; since"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon père, que j’admire, a travaillé toute sa vie dans la même usine,","en":"My father, whom I admire, worked all his life in the same factory,","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"alors que mon frère, qui a vingt-cinq ans, a déjà changé plusieurs fois d’entreprise.","en":"whereas my brother, who is twenty-five, has already changed companies several times.","lit":"then that"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"De nos jours, on peut devenir avocat, ingénieur, journaliste, comptable ou programmeur,","en":"Nowadays, you can become a lawyer, an engineer, a journalist, an accountant or a programmer,","lit":"of our days"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"mais aussi boucher, coiffeur, cuisinier ou plombier : il n’y a pas de métiers inutiles.","en":"but also a butcher, a hairdresser, a cook or a plumber: there are no useless jobs.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je veux vous parler de ce que je sais faire, plutôt que de ce que je voudrais faire.","en":"I want to talk to you about what I know how to do, rather than what I would like to do.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis conceptrice multimédia et consultante,","en":"I am a multimedia designer and consultant,","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"un métier qui demande une excellente formation, et pas mal de connaissances techniques.","en":"a job that requires excellent training, and quite a lot of technical knowledge.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce que j’apprécie le plus dans mon travail c’est qu’il m’offre la possibilité de rencontrer des gens aussi passionnés que moi.","en":"What I appreciate the most about my work is that it gives me the possibility to meet people who are as passionate as I am.","lit":"offers me; as passionate as me"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce qui est difficile est qu’on est toujours en train de voyager : en avion, en train, parfois en bateau ;","en":"What is difficult is that we’re always in the process of travelling: by plane, train and sometimes boat;","lit":"in plane"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"on n’arrête pas de bouger ! Nous ne pouvons pas prendre de congés,","en":"we never stop moving! We can’t take any holidays,","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"et nous ne passons jamais assez de temps dans les villes que nous visitons, ce qui est dommage.","en":"and we never spend enough time in the cities that we visit, which is a pity.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais, bon, c’est comme ça. Et les choses changent tellement vite !","en":"But, well, that’s the way it is. And things change so quickly!","lit":"it’s like that"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Que faites-vous maintenant, madame ? Toujours le même métier ?","en":"What do you do now, madam? Still the same job?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je suis entre deux emplois. Ce qui veut dire que je suis au chômage.","en":"No, I’m between two jobs. Which means that I’m unemployed.","lit":"wants to say"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Most adjectives ending in -al, including social and royal, form their masculine plural with -aux: sociaux, royaux, etc. The feminine forms, sociale, royale, simply take an -s (sociales, royales). (Un réseau, a network, forms its plural with an -x, as explained in lesson 29 note 9.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"entendu, the past participle of entendre, to hear (lesson 40 note 1), can be used as a response, meaning Of course (lesson 38 line 12). The phrase Bien entendu has the same meaning but is more flexible and can be placed at the beginning, middle or end of a complete sentence: Bien entendu, nous comprenons votre décision, Of course, we understand your decision."},{"ref":"3","text":"qui and que are relative pronouns that can refer both to people and to things, qui as the subject and que as the direct object. It is important not to translate qui systematically as who because the subject can be inanimate: Prenez le bus qui part à dix heures, Take the bus that leaves at ten o’clock."},{"ref":"4","text":"We know the noun un emploi, a job (lesson 20 line 8). Two related words are un métier, which, in addition to an occupation, can also mean a trade or craft, and une profession, a profession. (French also uses un job, meaning a low-skilled and/or low-paid… job! Please avoid.) The opposite, unemployment, is le chômage (line 16). Être au chômage, to be unemployed."},{"ref":"5","text":"ce qui and ce que are indefinite pronouns meaning which or what. They introduce a subordinate clause, in the same way as qui and que, but are used in sentences where the noun or phrase referred to by a pronoun is not expressed."},{"ref":"6","text":"voudrais is the first person singular of the conditional form of vouloir: je veux → je voudrais, I want → I would like."},{"ref":"7","text":"Masculine job titles ending in -eur that are derived directly from a verb have a feminine form ending in -euse – for example, un programmeur (from the verb programmer) becomes une programmeuse – whereas -eur nouns that do not come from a verb stem have the feminine ending -trice: un concept → un concepteur / une conceptrice."},{"ref":"8","text":"The invariable phrase pas mal de is useful for describing a fairly large quantity: Il y a pas mal de neige ici en janvier, There’s quite a lot of snow here in January. Despite the adjective mal, bad, the term is not pejorative."},{"ref":"9","text":"aussi… que is the comparative of equality, equivalent to as… as: Il est aussi grand que son père, He’s as tall as his father. The second noun can be replaced by a stressed pronoun (but never a subject pronoun, as in English): Elle est aussi surprise que moi, She’s as surprised as I [am]."},{"ref":"10","text":"We know that French verbs do not have a progressive form. Where necessary, though, the expression en train de, followed by an infinitive, allows us to insist on the ongoing nature of an action: Je ne peux pas venir, je suis en train de regarder le match, I can’t come, I’m watching the game. The important element is duration: Voici une bonne adresse pour votre prochain voyage ou votre voyage en cours si vous êtes en train de voyager, Here’s a good address for your next trip or your current trip if you are in the process of travelling. Don’t confuse en train de with en train, by train!"},{"ref":"11","text":"The compound verb vouloir dire (“to want to say”) translates as to mean, i.e. to imply or indicate. Only vouloir is conjugated: Qu’est-ce que tu veux / vous voulez dire ? What do you mean? However, in the sense to denote, we use the verb signifier (the origin of to signify): Que signifie ce mot ? What does this word mean?"},{"ref":"pron","text":"(16) chômage: the circumflex over the o of chômage lengthens the vowel: [show-mahzh]; compare this sound with the o in dommage: [do-mahzh]."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Alain a lu leur lettre et il est aussi surpris que moi.","a":"Alain read their letter and he is as surprised as I am."},{"q":"J’ai pas mal de travail à finir avant ce soir. Heureusement, je travaille vite !","a":"I have quite a lot of work to finish before this evening. Luckily, I work quickly!"},{"q":"Prends le train de huit heures, car je voudrais partir plus tôt, si possible.","a":"Take the 8 o’clock train for I would like to leave earlier if possible."},{"q":"Je comprends ta décision, bien entendu, mais je ne suis pas du tout d’accord.","a":"I understand your decision, of course, but I totally disagree."},{"q":"Ce que j’apprécie le moins dans cette profession c’est que les choses changent trop vite.","a":"What I appreciate the least in this profession is that things change too quickly."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You can become a lawyer or an accountant but also a hairdresser or a cook. There are no useless jobs.","t":"{{Tu peux}} devenir {{avocat}} ou {{comptable}} mais aussi {{coiffeur ou cuisinier}}. {{Il n’y a pas de}} métiers inutiles."},{"cue":"On social networks, there is much talk about the royal gardens in Brussels at the moment.","t":"Sur les {{réseaux sociaux}}, on parle beaucoup {{des jardins royaux}} de Bruxelles {{en ce moment}}."},{"cue":"We are going to talk to you about what we can do for you.","t":"Nous {{allons vous}} parler {{de ce que}} nous {{pouvons faire}} pour vous."},{"cue":"What does the word “homage” mean? – I think that you mean “chômage”. It means that a person has no work.","t":"{{Que signifie}} le mot “hommage” ? – Je pense que tu {{veux dire}} “chômage”. {{Ça signifie}} qu’on n’a pas de {{travail}}."},{"cue":"Hélène is a multimedia designer, and she meets people who are as passionate as she is.","t":"Hélène est {{conceptrice}} multimédia, et elle {{rencontre}} des gens qui sont {{aussi passionnés qu’elle}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"le monde du travail","en":"the world of work","zh":"职场；工作世界","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien entendu, le monde du travail a changé considérablement depuis vingt ans.","exEn":"Of course, the world of work has changed considerably in twenty years.","src":2},{"fr":"bien entendu","en":"of course","zh":"当然","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien entendu, le monde du travail a changé considérablement depuis vingt ans.","exEn":"Of course, the world of work has changed considerably in twenty years.","src":2},{"fr":"de nos jours","en":"nowadays","zh":"如今","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"De nos jours, on peut devenir avocat, ingénieur, journaliste, comptable ou programmeur,","exEn":"Nowadays, you can become a lawyer, an engineer, a journalist, an accountant or a programmer,","src":5},{"fr":"un métier","en":"a job, trade, occupation","zh":"职业；行业","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Il n’y a pas de métiers inutiles.","exEn":"There are no useless jobs.","src":6},{"fr":"il peut devenir avocat","en":"he can become a lawyer","zh":"他可以成为律师","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"no article before the job: devenir avocat","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"De nos jours, on peut devenir avocat, ingénieur, journaliste, comptable ou programmeur,","exEn":"Nowadays, you can become a lawyer, an engineer, a journalist, an accountant or a programmer,","src":5},{"fr":"ce que je sais faire","en":"what I know how to do","zh":"我会做的事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je veux vous parler de ce que je sais faire, plutôt que de ce que je voudrais faire.","exEn":"I want to talk to you about what I know how to do, rather than what I would like to do.","src":7},{"fr":"pas mal de connaissances techniques","en":"quite a lot of technical knowledge","zh":"相当多的技术知识","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ technical","ff":"pas mal de = quite a lot, not “not bad”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…une excellente formation, et pas mal de connaissances techniques.","exEn":"…excellent training, and quite a lot of technical knowledge.","src":9},{"fr":"ce que j’apprécie le plus","en":"what I appreciate the most","zh":"我最欣赏的是","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ appreciate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce que j’apprécie le plus dans mon travail c’est qu’il m’offre la possibilité de rencontrer des gens…","exEn":"What I appreciate the most about my work is that it gives me the possibility to meet people…","src":10},{"fr":"aussi passionnés que moi","en":"as passionate as I am","zh":"和我一样热情","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ passionate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…rencontrer des gens aussi passionnés que moi.","exEn":"…meet people who are as passionate as I am.","src":10},{"fr":"on est toujours en train de voyager","en":"we’re always in the process of travelling","zh":"我们总是在旅途中","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce qui est difficile est qu’on est toujours en train de voyager…","exEn":"What is difficult is that we’re always in the process of travelling…","src":11},{"fr":"on n’arrête pas de bouger","en":"we never stop moving","zh":"我们总是在动个不停","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On n’arrête pas de bouger !","exEn":"We never stop moving!","src":12},{"fr":"c’est comme ça","en":"that’s the way it is","zh":"就是这样","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais, bon, c’est comme ça.","exEn":"But, well, that’s the way it is.","src":14},{"fr":"je suis entre deux emplois","en":"I’m between two jobs","zh":"我正处于两份工作之间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je suis entre deux emplois.","exEn":"No, I’m between two jobs.","src":16},{"fr":"je suis au chômage","en":"I’m unemployed","zh":"我失业了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “show-mazh”: a man on the dole “shows” his empty pockets","ex":"Ce qui veut dire que je suis au chômage.","exEn":"Which means that I’m unemployed.","src":16}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 4: the book prints “homage”; the French word is “hommage”, which I used.","dialogue 2 translation: “changé considérablement… in twenty years” – lit gloss “since” kept."],"pdfPage":235,"gaps":[[1.58,3.7],[5.68,8.7],[13.26,14.26],[31.2,32.42],[46.52,47.94],[52.82,53.94],[63.66,65.06],[72.56,73.76],[91.84,92.84],[101.08,102.42],[107.5,110.98],[113.16,115.08],[117.88,120.84],[124.94,127.96],[132.74,135.92],[140.36,143.34],[148,151.58]],"dur":151.6},{"n":46,"kind":"lesson","title":"Depuis, je n’ai aucune nouvelle","titleEn":"I’ve had no news since then","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai perdu mon mari, monsieur l’agent. Il a disparu.","en":"I’ve lost my husband, officer. He has disappeared.","lit":"mister the agent"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Quand l’avez-vous vu pour la dernière fois ?","en":"When did you see him for the last time?","lit":"when have you seen him"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Hier matin. Nous sommes partis ensemble pour faire les courses.","en":"Yesterday morning. We left together to go shopping.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Moi, je suis allée à l’hypermarché près du Boulevard périphérique et lui est allé à la boulangerie.","en":"I went to the hypermarket near the Boulevard Périphérique and he went to the bakery.","lit":"(me); peripheral; (him)"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Et depuis, je n’ai aucune nouvelle, même pas un petit coup de téléphone.","en":"And since then I’ve had no news, not even a little phone call.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Pouvez-vous décrire votre époux, s’il vous plaît ?","en":"Can you describe your husband, please?","lit":"spouse"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Il a une quarantaine d’années, mince et assez grand ; environ un mètre quatre-vingt-cinq.","en":"He’s about 40, thin and quite tall – about 1 metre 85.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Un visage carré avec des cheveux bruns, longs et raides, et des lunettes noires.","en":"A square face with brown hair, long and straight, and dark glasses.","lit":"black glasses"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment est-il habillé ? En costume ? En jean ?","en":"How is he dressed? In a suit? In jeans?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Il porte un pantalon gris et une chemise à manches courtes, une écharpe et une veste en cuir marron.","en":"He’s wearing a pair of grey trousers and a short-sleeved shirt, a scarf and a brown leather jacket.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Oh, et des chaussures noires et des chaussettes assorties.","en":"Oh, and black shoes with matching socks.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Voilà une description vraiment détaillée, meilleure qu’une photographie !","en":"That’s a really detailed description, better than a photograph!","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Je peux faire mieux : j’ai un petit film sur mon téléphone. Vous voulez le voir ?","en":"I can do better: I have a little film on my phone. Do you want to see it?","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, ce n’est pas la peine. Vous le décrivez très bien. Mieux que moi !","en":"No, don’t bother. You describe him very well. Better than me!","lit":"this is not the difficulty"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai l’habitude de le perdre : il déteste faire du shopping et il disparaît une fois sur deux.","en":"I’m used to losing him: he hates shopping and disappears half of the time.","lit":"I have the habit; one time out of two"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais cette fois-ci je suis vraiment inquiète car il avait une grande valise dans une main et un billet d’avion dans l’autre.","en":"But this time, I’m really worried because he had a big suitcase in one hand and a plane ticket in the other.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Aucun souci : nous allons faire de notre mieux pour le retrouver.","en":"No worries: we’re going to do our best to find him.","lit":"of our best"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"In formal language, monsieur and madame (but not mademoiselle) are used, along with an honorific, when addressing someone in an official position: monsieur le député (“mister member of parliament”), madame la directrice (“madam director”). (Un agent de police, a police officer; though masculine, l’agent can apply to officers of both sexes.) Likewise, un époux / une épouse, a spouse, is used in official contexts, instead of un mari / une femme, to mean a husband / a wife."},{"ref":"2","text":"Remember that the past participle of verbs forming their perfect tense with être (lesson 43 note 3) agrees in number and gender with the subject, not the object, of the sentence. If a plural subject comprises both a male and a female agent, as here, then the masculine takes precedence – an issue we will discuss in greater detail later on."},{"ref":"3","text":"The stressed pronouns moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux and elles are used for emphasis, especially when a contrast is involved: Moi, j’aime le café au petit-déjeuner, mais lui préfère le thé. In this context, the pronouns do not necessarily need to be translated into English: I like coffee for breakfast but he prefers tea. Note how the stressed pronoun is followed immediately by a subject pronoun (the second clause in our example could be phrased: … mais lui, il préfère le thé, in order to add even greater emphasis)."},{"ref":"4","text":"aucun is a useful adjective and pronoun, meaning not any, no, or none. As a determiner, it is not used in the plural (it comes from Old French, meaning “not one”) and, in a negative construction, is not followed by pas: Ils n’ont aucun problème de santé, They have no health problems. It must, however, agree with the singular noun it determines: Je n’ai aucune idée, I have no idea. In informal language, a sentence with the negative is often shortened: Aucune idée."},{"ref":"5","text":"A number of nouns are plural in French but singular in English. We already know les toilettes, the toilet (lesson 15 note 6), and en vacances, on holiday (lesson 18 line 9); another one is les cheveux, hair: Tes cheveux sont trop longs, Your hair is too long. The singular un cheveu is used in certain expressions, such as avoir un cheveu sur la langue (“to have a hair on the tongue”), to have a lisp, but rarely in the physical meaning of a hair."},{"ref":"6","text":"In contrast to note 5, some nouns are plural in English but singular in French. This rule applies in particular to “two-legged” garments, which can be singularised in French: un jean, a pair of jeans, un pantalon, a pair of trousers. Logically, the plural two pairs of trousers is translated as deux pantalons."},{"ref":"7","text":"meilleur, the comparative form of bon (lesson 42 section 3), is an adjective and therefore agrees with its noun: J’ai une meilleure idée, I have a better idea. Ce café sert les meilleures crêpes et les meilleurs gâteaux de Bretagne, This café serves the best pancakes and the best cakes in Brittany. Don’t confuse meilleur with mieux, the comparative adverb of bien, well (see lesson 42 section 3): Comment va ton frère ? – Il va mieux."},{"ref":"8","text":"The abstract noun la peine has several meanings, ranging from sadness to difficulty (though not pain, despite the etymological link). Ce n’est pas la peine is an idiomatic expression meaning something like Don’t bother or Don’t worry about it. Vous voulez de l’aide ? – Ce n’est pas la peine, merci. Do you want help? – Don’t bother, but thanks. In a full sentence, the expression is followed by the preposition de: Ce n’est pas la peine de chercher du sucre, il n’y en a plus. There’s no point looking for sugar, there’s none left."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here’s another idiomatic expression. Un souci is a concern, a worry. The common interjection Aucun souci is the equivalent of No problem or Don’t worry about it. An even commoner form (and arguably the back translation of the Anglo-Australian No worries) is Pas de souci(s), in which case the noun can be in the plural."},{"ref":"","text":"With a surface area of just 84.5 square kilometres, the city of Paris is small compared with the likes of London or Los Angeles. One of the reasons is that it is surrounded by a 35 km ring road called le Boulevard périphérique (in compound proper nouns, only the first word usually takes an initial capital). By contrast, the entire conurbation, known as le Grand Paris (greater Paris), is ten times larger. The city within le Périph’ – the ring road’s nickname – is often referred to as Paris intra-muros, from the Latin phrase meaning “within walls”."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Elle porte un pantalon bleu, une chemise grise à manches longues, une veste en cuir et un sac à main.","a":"She’s wearing a pair of blue trousers, a grey long-sleeved shirt, a leather jacket and a handbag."},{"q":"J’ai l’habitude de me perdre en ville. C’est pour ça que j’ai toujours mon téléphone avec moi.","a":"I usually get lost in town. That’s why I always have my phone with me."},{"q":"Vous chantez très bien. Enfin, mieux que lui.","a":"You sing very well. At least, better than he does."},{"q":"Ce n’est pas la peine d’appeler Marianne, elle est déjà partie.","a":"No need to call Marianne, she has already left."},{"q":"Nous n’avons aucune nouvelle d’eux depuis une semaine, et nous sommes très inquiets.","a":"We’ve had no news from them for a week and we’re very worried."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"When did you last see your friend? – I saw him yesterday.","t":"Quand avez-vous {{vu}} votre {{ami}} pour la {{dernière fois}} ? – Je {{l’ai vu}} hier."},{"cue":"“Chez Yannick” serves the best pancakes in town. – No, the best pancake house is “Chez Rozenn”.","t":"“Chez Yannick” sert les {{meilleures}} crêpes de la ville. – Non, la {{meilleure}} crêperie, c’est “Chez Rozenn”."},{"cue":"Marianne speaks Japanese better than you and me. She’s the best student in the university, and we have to do better.","t":"Marianne parle le japonais {{mieux que vous et moi}}. Elle est {{la meilleure}} étudiante de la fac, et nous {{devons faire mieux}}."},{"cue":"My daughter has no health problems. At least, she has no serious illnesses.","t":"{{Ma}} fille {{n’a aucun}} problème de {{santé}}. Enfin, elle {{n’a aucune maladie}} grave."},{"cue":"You’re very badly dressed, my son. Your jeans are too short and your hair is too long.","t":"Tu es très {{mal habillé}}, mon fils. {{Ton jean est}} trop {{court}} et {{tes cheveux sont}} trop {{longs}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai perdu mon mari","en":"I’ve lost my husband","zh":"我丢了我丈夫","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"family","kw":"","ex":"J’ai perdu mon mari, monsieur l’agent.","exEn":"I’ve lost my husband, officer.","src":1},{"fr":"il a disparu","en":"he has disappeared","zh":"他不见了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ disappear","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il a disparu.","exEn":"He has disappeared.","src":1},{"fr":"je n’ai aucune nouvelle","en":"I’ve had no news","zh":"我没有任何消息","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"nouvelle(s) = news (a single piece of news is une nouvelle)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et depuis, je n’ai aucune nouvelle, même pas un petit coup de téléphone.","exEn":"And since then I’ve had no news, not even a little phone call.","src":5},{"fr":"Pouvez-vous décrire votre époux ?","en":"Can you describe your husband?","zh":"您能描述一下您的丈夫吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ describe","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pouvez-vous décrire votre époux, s’il vous plaît ?","exEn":"Can you describe your husband, please?","src":6},{"fr":"des cheveux bruns, longs et raides","en":"brown hair, long and straight","zh":"棕色的长直发","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"les cheveux is plural in French (hair)","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"Un visage carré avec des cheveux bruns, longs et raides, et des lunettes noires.","exEn":"A square face with brown hair, long and straight, and dark glasses.","src":8},{"fr":"il porte un pantalon gris","en":"he’s wearing grey trousers","zh":"他穿着一条灰色裤子","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un pantalon = one pair of trousers (singular in French)","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"Il porte un pantalon gris et une chemise à manches courtes…","exEn":"He’s wearing a pair of grey trousers and a short-sleeved shirt…","src":10},{"fr":"une veste en cuir marron","en":"a brown leather jacket","zh":"一件棕色皮夹克","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"…une écharpe et une veste en cuir marron.","exEn":"…a scarf and a brown leather jacket.","src":10},{"fr":"mieux que moi","en":"better than me","zh":"比我好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"mieux (adverb) ≠ meilleur (adjective)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous le décrivez très bien. Mieux que moi !","exEn":"You describe him very well. Better than me!","src":14},{"fr":"une meilleure idée","en":"a better idea","zh":"更好的主意","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai une meilleure idée.","exEn":"I have a better idea.","src":0},{"fr":"Ce n’est pas la peine.","en":"Don’t bother. / There’s no need.","zh":"不必了。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"la peine ≠ pain; here = trouble","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, ce n’est pas la peine.","exEn":"No, don’t bother.","src":14},{"fr":"j’ai l’habitude de le perdre","en":"I’m used to losing him","zh":"我习惯了弄丢他","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ habit","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai l’habitude de le perdre.","exEn":"I’m used to losing him.","src":15},{"fr":"je suis vraiment inquiète","en":"I’m really worried","zh":"我真的很担心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"inquiet / inquiète = worried, not “inquiring”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais cette fois-ci je suis vraiment inquiète…","exEn":"But this time, I’m really worried…","src":16},{"fr":"Aucun souci !","en":"No worries!","zh":"没问题！别担心！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aucun souci : nous allons faire de notre mieux pour le retrouver.","exEn":"No worries: we’re going to do our best to find him.","src":17},{"fr":"nous allons faire de notre mieux","en":"we’re going to do our best","zh":"我们会尽力","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aucun souci : nous allons faire de notre mieux pour le retrouver.","exEn":"No worries: we’re going to do our best to find him.","src":17}],"uncertain":["Dialogue line 15 speaker/dash assumed from alternation (wife answers): dash true."],"pdfPage":241,"gaps":[[1.86,4.04],[6.06,9.26],[90.3,93.6],[95.48,97.42],[104,107.16],[111.94,115.04],[117.94,120.96],[124.44,127.22],[131.06,134.74]],"dur":134.7},{"n":47,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un déménagement","titleEn":"A removal","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai un service à te demander : peux-tu nous aider à déménager samedi et dimanche prochains ?","en":"I have a favour to ask you: can you help us to move house next Saturday and Sunday?","lit":"service"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais aussi demander à Stéphane et à Olivier : plus on est nombreux, plus on s’amuse.","en":"I’m also going to ask Stéphane and Olivier: the more, the merrier.","lit":"more we are numerous, more we amuse ourselves"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu vas leur demander de travailler un samedi ? Bonne chance ! Ce sont de gros paresseux.","en":"You’re going to ask them to work on a Saturday? Good luck, they’re big slackers.","lit":"big"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu pourrais éventuellement parler à Didier, mon beau-frère : il a une entreprise de déménagement.","en":"You could maybe talk to Didier, my brother-in-law: he has a removal firm.","lit":"possibly; handsome-brother"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que tu peux lui parler, toi ? Je ne le connais pas. Tu penses qu’il peut le faire gratuitement ?","en":"Can you talk to him? I don’t know him. Do you think he can do it free of charge?","lit":"you; freely"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"J’en doute, car ses affaires vont mal, mais je peux toujours essayer. Je te tiens au courant. […]","en":"I doubt it, because his business is going badly, but I can always ask him. I’ll keep you posted. …","lit":"his affairs go badly; I hold you in the current"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon beau-frère est d’accord pour t’aider. Je lui ai dit que tu es un de mes meilleurs amis.","en":"My brother-in-law agrees to help you. I told him that you’re one of my best friends.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"En plus, il est aimable : il te facture seulement la location du camion et une demi-journée de travail.","en":"What’s more, he’s nice: he’s charging you only for the rental of the lorry and a half-day’s work.","lit":"he invoices you"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"En gros, cela te fait quelques centaines d’euros, au lieu de mille ou plus, son tarif normal.","en":"All in all, that makes a few hundred euros for you, instead of a thousand or more, his normal rate.","lit":"in fat"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est très bien, ce Didier ! C’est très gentil de sa part. Tu peux le remercier ?","en":"He’s great, that Didier! It’s very kind of him. Can you thank him?","lit":"very good; from his part"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu peux lui dire toi-même : il arrive dans une heure et demie avec un collègue. On va les attendre. […]","en":"You can say it yourself: he’s arriving in an hour and a half with a colleague. We’re going to wait for them. …","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Maintenant, au boulot ! Tout ce qui est gros et lourd va dans le camion de Didier :","en":"Now, let’s get cracking! Everything that’s big and heavy goes in Didier’s lorry:","lit":"to work"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"le canapé, les fauteuils, les lits, les armoires, la machine à laver, le lave-vaisselle et la cuisinière.","en":"the sofa, the armchairs, the beds, the wardrobes, the washing machine, the dishwasher and the stove.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Le reste – la vaisselle, les tableaux, la télé, le linge, les rideaux,","en":"The rest – the dishes, the paintings, the TV, the linen, the curtains,","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"les cartons dans l’escalier et les piles de bouquins contre le mur –","en":"the boxes on the stairs and the piles of books against the wall –","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"on le prend avec nous dans la camionnette. Je vais la chercher tout de suite.","en":"we’re taking it with us in the van. I’m going to fetch it right away.","lit":"look for"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon courage à tout le monde : on a une longue journée devant nous.","en":"Best of luck everyone: we have a long day ahead of us.","lit":"good courage"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Plus vite on finit, plus vite on peut se coucher.","en":"The quicker we finish, the quicker we can go to bed.","lit":"more fast one finishes, more fast one can lie down"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The English comparative phrase the more… the more has a direct equivalent in French: plus… plus. Typically, this type of construction uses the impersonal pronoun on: Plus on gagne de l’argent, plus on dépense, The more money you earn, the more you spend. (Another well-known saying, Plus on est de fous, plus on rit – literally “The more madmen there are, the more we laugh” – is the equivalent of The more, the merrier.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"ce is both a singular demonstrative adjective (lesson 9 note 1) and a demonstrative pronoun. It can replace il/elle est (→ c’est) but also ils/elles sont (→ ce sont), in which case it is translated by they. Ce sont deux de mes films préférés, They are two of my favourite movies. The differences between il/elle est (ils/elles sont) and c’est (ce sont) are quite complex but, in this case, c’est / ce sont describes a situation (“they’re lazy”) as opposed to the specific person or object."},{"ref":"3","text":"As we know, gros / grosse means fat (lesson 14 section 5.2). But it has a much broader meaning, including big (lesson 19 line 11), thick (un gros manteau, a thick coat), and serious (une grosse erreur, a serious mistake). To translate the adjective properly or find an idiomatic equivalent, always check the noun it is qualifying: Tu es un gros paresseux ! You’re a slacker / a lazybones! The expression en gros, usually placed at the beginning of a sentence or clause, means broadly, basically, etc."},{"ref":"4","text":"éventuellement is a faux-ami. It expresses the idea of possibility: Je ne connais pas la réponse, mais vous pouvez éventuellement la trouver en ligne, I don’t know the answer but you may be able to find it online. Nicole vient à la fête demain ? – Éventuellement, Is Nicole coming to the party tomorrow? – Maybe. (The adjective is éventuel, possible.) The adverb eventually is rendered finalement or enfin: Il a finalement acheté une nouvelle collection de timbres, He eventually bought a new stamp collection."},{"ref":"5","text":"courant is both a noun and an adjective: le courant means the current (water, electricity, etc.), while the adjective means ordinary, standard, common, etc. (but not current – another faux-ami!). C’est un problème courant, It’s a common problem. The expression être au courant, first seen in lesson 32, means to be aware of something: Ma belle-sœur arrive demain. – Oui, je suis au courant. My sister-in-law is arriving tomorrow. – Yes, I’m aware of that. Tenir au courant, to keep someone informed/posted."},{"ref":"6","text":"Some verbs have no direct equivalent in other languages. That is the case for to charge (money). The most convenient equivalent is probably facturer, to invoice: Le plombier m’a facturé six cents euros de l’heure, The plumber charged me 600 euros an hour. Another possibility is prendre, to take, usually with a monetary amount or an adjective: Combien prend-il pour une leçon privée ? How much does he charge for a private lesson?"},{"ref":"7","text":"When demi, half, comes before a noun, it is invariable: une demi-heure, un demi-kilo. After the noun, however, it can be feminine or masculine depending on the gender – une heure et demie, un kilo et demi – but never plural. In every case, the pronunciation is identical [duhmee]."},{"ref":"8","text":"We have seen bien used as an adverb meaning well, but it can also be an adjective, usually as part of an expression before a noun: Elle est très bien, cette chanteuse, She’s very good, that singer. In such cases, however, it does not agree with the subject: Les fauteuils sont très bien, The armchairs are very nice."},{"ref":"9","text":"We know that indirect object pronouns come before the verb in French (lesson 35 section 5). One slight complication is that some verbs taking an indirect object in English require a direct object in French. Among the most common of these are chercher, to look for, to fetch, and attendre, to wait for. In this case, the noun is replaced by a direct object pronoun: J’attends Didier et son collègue → Je les attends (not Je leur attends)."},{"ref":"10","text":"le boulot is a familiar but very common word for work, for which there is no one-on-one equivalent. Unlike its English counterpart, the noun is both countable and uncountable. J’ai beaucoup de boulot en ce moment, I’ve got a lot of work at the moment; Jeanne commence son nouveau boulot ce lundi, Jeanne is starting her new job this Monday. The imperative expression Allez, au boulot ! means something like: Let’s get cracking! Another slang word that has no equivalent is un bouquin, a book, line 15, used in a familiar register in the same way as un livre."},{"ref":"","text":"L’argot (slang) was once the language of thieves, footpads and other ne’er-do-wells. In its current acceptance, it means a set of non-technical words and expressions used by a particular social group, whereas familiar language has a much broader reach. In this book, we have largely avoided slang, while introducing familiar words and expressions, such as un bouquin and le boulot, which are widely used across all social classes and groups. Significantly, some of these colloquialisms have no direct equivalents in English, as these two examples show."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"On est déjà en retard. Je vais chercher le camion en attendant ta belle-sœur.","a":"We’re already late. I’ll go and fetch the lorry while waiting for your sister-in-law."},{"q":"Il ne fera pas le déménagement aujourd’hui, mais éventuellement demain s’il a le temps.","a":"He won’t do the move today, but possibly tomorrow if he has time."},{"q":"Mon comptable m’a facturé huit cents euros, mais son tarif normal est plus cher.","a":"My accountant charged me 800 euros, but his normal rate is much more expensive."},{"q":"Bon courage ! Vous avez une longue journée devant vous.","a":"Best of luck! You have a long day ahead of you."},{"q":"Vous savez tous ce que vous devez faire. Maintenant, au boulot !","a":"You all know what you have to do. Now, let’s get down to work."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Have you asked your brother-in-law? – I’m going to ask him right away.","t":"{{Est-ce que}} vous {{avez demandé à}} votre beau-frère ? – Je vais {{lui demander}} tout de suite."},{"cue":"The bus is arriving in half an hour. – But I’ve been waiting for it for three and a half hours!","t":"Le bus arrive {{dans une demi-heure}}. – Mais je {{l’}}attends depuis {{trois heures et demie}} !"},{"cue":"But these armchairs are very nice. Why don’t you like them?","t":"Mais {{ils sont très bien}}, ces fauteuils. Pourquoi tu {{ne les aimes pas}} ?"},{"cue":"My parents agree to help us. I told them that you were my best friends.","t":"Mes parents {{sont d’accord}} pour {{nous}} aider. Je {{leur ai dit}} que vous étiez mes {{meilleurs amis}}."},{"cue":"There are about a hundred people to help us. – I’m going to thank them: the more, the merrier!","t":"Il y a {{une centaine}} de personnes pour {{nous aider}}. – Je vais {{les remercier}} : {{plus on est}} de fous, {{plus on}} rit !"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai un service à te demander","en":"I have a favour to ask you","zh":"我有件事想请你帮忙","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un service = a favour (also “service”)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai un service à te demander : peux-tu nous aider à déménager samedi et dimanche prochains ?","exEn":"I have a favour to ask you: can you help us to move house next Saturday and Sunday?","src":1},{"fr":"nous allons déménager","en":"we’re going to move house","zh":"我们要搬家了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peux-tu nous aider à déménager samedi et dimanche prochains ?","exEn":"Can you help us to move house next Saturday and Sunday?","src":1},{"fr":"Plus on est nombreux, plus on s’amuse.","en":"The more, the merrier.","zh":"人越多越热闹。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Plus on est nombreux, plus on s’amuse.","exEn":"The more, the merrier.","src":2},{"fr":"éventuellement","en":"possibly, perhaps","zh":"也许；有可能","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"éventuellement = possibly, NOT “eventually” (= finalement)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu pourrais éventuellement parler à Didier, mon beau-frère.","exEn":"You could maybe talk to Didier, my brother-in-law.","src":4},{"fr":"il peut le faire gratuitement","en":"he can do it for free","zh":"他可以免费做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ gratuitous / gratis","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Tu penses qu’il peut le faire gratuitement ?","exEn":"Do you think he can do it free of charge?","src":5},{"fr":"J’en doute.","en":"I doubt it.","zh":"我对此表示怀疑。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ doubt","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en doute, car ses affaires vont mal, mais je peux toujours essayer.","exEn":"I doubt it, because his business is going badly, but I can always ask him.","src":6},{"fr":"je te tiens au courant","en":"I’ll keep you posted","zh":"我会随时告诉你进展","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"courant ≠ “current” in this sense","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je te tiens au courant.","exEn":"I’ll keep you posted.","src":6},{"fr":"il te facture la location du camion","en":"he charges you for the rental of the lorry","zh":"他向你收卡车的租金","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ invoice (facture)","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Il te facture seulement la location du camion et une demi-journée de travail.","exEn":"He’s charging you only for the rental of the lorry and a half-day’s work.","src":8},{"fr":"en gros","en":"all in all, basically","zh":"大体上；总之","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En gros, cela te fait quelques centaines d’euros.","exEn":"All in all, that makes a few hundred euros for you.","src":9},{"fr":"au boulot !","en":"let’s get to work!","zh":"开工吧！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, au boulot !","exEn":"Now, let’s get cracking!","src":12},{"fr":"le canapé","en":"the sofa","zh":"沙发","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ canapé / couch","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…le canapé, les fauteuils, les lits, les armoires…","exEn":"…the sofa, the armchairs, the beds, the wardrobes…","src":13},{"fr":"une armoire","en":"a wardrobe, cupboard","zh":"衣柜；柜子","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"armoire ≠ armoury","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “arm-wah”: you hang your arm-ful of clothes in the wardrobe","ex":"…les lits, les armoires, la machine à laver…","exEn":"…the beds, the wardrobes, the washing machine…","src":13},{"fr":"Bon courage !","en":"Best of luck! / Hang in there!","zh":"加油！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ courage","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon courage à tout le monde : on a une longue journée devant nous.","exEn":"Best of luck everyone: we have a long day ahead of us.","src":17},{"fr":"Plus vite on finit, plus vite on peut se coucher.","en":"The quicker we finish, the quicker we can go to bed.","zh":"越早做完，就越早能睡觉。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Plus vite on finit, plus vite on peut se coucher.","exEn":"The quicker we finish, the quicker we can go to bed.","src":18}],"uncertain":["dialogue 7-9 speaker dashes: no dash printed on 7, 8, 9 (same speaker as 6).","Dialogue line 11: “[ … ]” scene break printed after “On va les attendre.”; kept."],"pdfPage":247,"gaps":[[1.84,3.88],[4.78,7.64],[19.76,20.78],[25.3,26.34],[42.82,44.34],[99.08,100.18],[103.4,104.54],[107.08,110.42],[112.76,115.62],[120.7,123.82],[127.92,130.94],[135.5,138.66],[142.24,145.18],[148.64,152.6]],"dur":152.6},{"n":48,"kind":"lesson","title":"Ça ne me dit rien","titleEn":"I don’t feel like it","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Comme il fait chaud aujourd’hui, nous allons pique-niquer dans la forêt de Fontainebleau.","en":"As it’s warm today, we’ll go for a picnic in the Fontainebleau forest.","lit":"hot"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Honnêtement, un pique-nique ne me dit rien. C’est tellement inconfortable.","en":"Honestly, I don’t feel like a picnic. It’s so uncomfortable.","lit":"a picnic not to-me says nothing"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"On est mal assis par terre et on mange mal : les chips, les salades de riz dégoûtantes,","en":"You’re seated uncomfortably on the ground and you eat badly: crisps, disgusting rice salads,","lit":"one is badly seated; one eats badly"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"des sandwichs de poulet sans goût, les bananes trop mûres – et des fourmis et des guêpes partout.","en":"chicken sandwiches with no taste, over-ripe bananas – and ants and wasps everywhere.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Et puis on a du mal à manger proprement, avec les assiettes en papier, les gobelets en carton,","en":"And then it’s hard to eat cleanly, with paper plates, cardboard beakers,","lit":"one has difficulty"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"les fourchettes en plastique et les couteaux qui ne coupent rien.","en":"plastic forks, and knives that don’t cut anything.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Bref, je ne supporte pas les pique-niques.","en":"In short, I can’t stand picnics.","lit":"brief; I don’t support"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais ce n’est pas vraiment une mauvaise idée, tu sais. Au contraire.","en":"But it’s not really a bad idea, you know. On the contrary.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"D’abord, c’est nous qui allons préparer la nourriture, avec de bons produits sains.","en":"First, it’s us who are going to prepare the food, with good, healthy produce.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite, pas de problème de confort. Regarde ceci : c’est notre nouveau panier à pique-nique.","en":"Next, no comfort problems. Look at this: it’s our new picnic hamper.","lit":"basket"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais ce n’est pas un panier, c’est un sac à dos !","en":"But it’s not a basket, it’s a backpack!","lit":"bag to back"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, mais c’est un sac à dos de luxe ! Il y a tout ce qu’il faut pour faire de vrais repas.","en":"Yes, but it’s a luxury backpack! There’s everything you need for a good meal.","lit":"to make real meals"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Des couverts en métal, des assiettes en porcelaine, et des verres à vin et à eau.","en":"Metal cutlery, china plates, and wine and water glasses.","lit":"porcelain"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Et regarde cela ! Qu’est-ce que tu en penses ?","en":"And look at that! What do you think of it?","lit":"of it"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que c’est ?","en":"What is it?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est un sac spécifique pour les bouteilles de vin blanc. Sophistiqué, non ?","en":"It’s a special bag for bottles of white wine. Sophisticated, isn’t it?","lit":"no"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"On a aussi quatre chaises pliantes, une table basse et une vraie couverture.","en":"We also have four folding chairs, a low table and a real blanket.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais attends, qu’est-ce qui se passe ? La pluie commence à tomber.","en":"But hang on, what’s happening? The rain is starting to fall.","lit":"wait"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Oh, il pleut. Quel dommage ! Pas de pique-nique aujourd’hui…","en":"Oh, it’s raining. What a pity! No picnic today…","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(3) chips, meaning crisps, is a faux-ami but, above all, a foreign import. Which is why the final s is pronounced. (Remember, too, that ch- is pronounced [sh].) The word is therefore pronounced [sheeps]."},{"ref":"1","text":"We have already seen dire used idiomatically to mean to fancy, to like, etc. (lesson 25 note 5). A related expression, Ça ne me dit rien, expresses a lack of interest or enthusiasm: Tu veux venir au cinéma avec nous ? – Ça ne me dit rien, Do you want to come to the movies with us? – I don’t feel like it. As in our example (line 2), ça can be replaced by a noun. The same structure can also express a lack of recognition or familiarity: Ce nom ne me dit rien, I’m not familiar with that name."},{"ref":"2","text":"When describing temperature conditions, French does not use the same verbs as English. We already know avoir chaud and avoir froid (lesson 26 note 3). Likewise, faire chaud / froid describes the ambient temperature: Il y a une grande différence de température dans cette région de France, il fait très chaud en été et il fait très froid en hiver, There is a big difference in temperature in this region of France, it is very hot in summer and very cold in winter. When talking about the weather, chaud by itself can mean both hot and warm."},{"ref":"3","text":"Like bien (lesson 47 note 8), mal is usually an adverb – Je dors mal quand il fait chaud comme ça, I sleep badly when it’s hot like that – but it can sometimes be used as an adjective. In this case, it is usually invariable and can only be used with state-of-being verbs like être: Je n’ai rien fait de mal, I haven’t done anything wrong. Don’t confuse mal with the adjective mauvais(e): C’est une mauvaise idée, It’s a bad idea. And don’t confuse avoir mal, to feel pain, (lesson 32 line 12) with the idiomatic expression avoir du mal (lesson 44 note 11)."},{"ref":"4","text":"Remember that the adjective describes the head noun, in this case une salade, and not the complement, de riz. It therefore has to agree in the feminine plural. For example, une salade de fruits délicieuse, a delicious fruit salad. This rule applies to all such compound nouns. (Note that English, which has no grammatical genders, is less precise than French in this respect: does delicious apply to fruit, salad or both? If we want to insist on the first noun, we would have to say a salad of delicious fruit, whereas French makes the distinction through adjective agreement: une salade de fruits délicieux.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"The preposition en is used between two nouns to describe what the first object is made of: une assiette en carton, a cardboard plate, deux tasses en porcelaine, two china cups, etc."},{"ref":"6","text":"supporter, to support, also means to tolerate. The verb is often used idiomatically in the negative: Je ne supporte pas cette émission, I can’t stand that show."},{"ref":"7","text":"When a BAGS adjective (lesson 14 section 5.2) comes before a plural noun, the partitive article des becomes de (or d’ before a vowel). For example, Nous achetons des produits sains, We buy healthy produce, but Nous achetons de bons produits sains, We buy good healthy produce."},{"ref":"8","text":"Ceci and cela (line 14) are demonstrative pronouns meaning this, that, and, in impersonal sentences, it. Ceci is not widely used in everyday language, except when referring to something that is about to be mentioned or presented: Écoutez ceci et dites-moi ce que vous en pensez, Listen to this and tell me what you think. Cela (lesson 47 line 9) is usually abbreviated to ça in informal language: Ça te fait quelques centaines d’euros…"},{"ref":"9","text":"à is used in compound nouns to indicate the purpose of a designated object, whereas English strings two nouns together: un panier à pique-nique, a picnic basket, un sac à dos, a backpack (“a bag to the back”). Likewise, un verre à eau, a water glass, etc. Be careful not to confuse à with de (of) in this type of noun: un verre à vin, a wine glass, un verre de vin, a glass of wine. The difference can be disappointing…"},{"ref":"10","text":"passer means to pass, which we have seen in lesson 40 line 12, as well as in the idiom passer un coup de fil (lesson 41 note 4). But se passer (literally: “to pass oneself”) is a reflexive verb, which we will study in the next set of lessons. In this context, the idiom se passer means to happen: Qu’est-ce qui se passe ? What’s happening? / What’s going on?"},{"ref":"","text":"Fifty kilometres southeast of Paris, the city of Fontainebleau is famed for its huge forest, now classified as a national park, and its royal palace, le Château de Fontainebleau, dating from the 12th century."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Est-ce qu’il fait chaud dehors ? Moi, j’ai froid : regarde mes mains !","a":"Is it hot outside? Personally, I’m cold: look at my hands!"},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que c’est que ce bruit ? Qu’est-ce qui se passe ?","a":"What’s that noise? What’s going on?"},{"q":"Nous avons tout ce qu’il faut pour faire un vrai café mais ça ne me dit rien.","a":"We’ve got everything needed to make a real coffee but I don’t feel like one."},{"q":"Elle n’aime pas manger dans les assiettes en papier. C’est pour ça qu’elle ne supporte pas les pique-niques.","a":"She doesn’t like eating off paper plates. That’s why she can’t stand picnics."},{"q":"Leur idée n’est pas bonne ; au contraire, elle est très mauvaise.","a":"Their idea isn’t good. On the contrary, it’s very bad."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"These folding chairs are so uncomfortable! We’re really badly seated.","t":"Ces {{chaises pliantes}} sont tellement {{inconfortables}} ! On {{est}} très {{mal assis}}."},{"cue":"We ate a salad of fresh fruit served on real china plates.","t":"Nous avons mangé une {{salade de fruits frais}}, servie sur {{de vraies assiettes en porcelaine}}."},{"cue":"Come here and listen to this. It’s an Indian singer. Do you know him? – No, I’m not familiar with his name.","t":"Viens {{ici}} et {{écoute ceci}}. {{C’est}} un chanteur indien. Tu le {{connais}} ? – Non, son nom {{ne me dit rien}}."},{"cue":"It’s too cold for a picnic and it’s raining. – What a pity!","t":"Il {{fait trop froid}} pour un pique-nique, et il {{pleut}}. {{Quel dommage}} !"},{"cue":"I find it hard to see the difference between the right and the wrong answers.","t":"J’ai {{du mal à voir}} la différence entre {{les bonnes}} et {{les mauvaises}} réponses."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Ça ne me dit rien.","en":"I don’t feel like it. / It doesn’t ring a bell.","zh":"我没兴趣。／我没印象。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Honnêtement, un pique-nique ne me dit rien.","exEn":"Honestly, I don’t feel like a picnic.","src":2},{"fr":"il fait chaud aujourd’hui","en":"it’s hot/warm today","zh":"今天很热","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"il fait chaud (weather) ≠ il a chaud (person feels hot)","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Comme il fait chaud aujourd’hui, nous allons pique-niquer dans la forêt de Fontainebleau.","exEn":"As it’s warm today, we’ll go for a picnic in the Fontainebleau forest.","src":1},{"fr":"nous allons pique-niquer","en":"we’re going to have a picnic","zh":"我们要去野餐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ picnic","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous allons pique-niquer dans la forêt de Fontainebleau.","exEn":"We’ll go for a picnic in the Fontainebleau forest.","src":1},{"fr":"c’est tellement inconfortable","en":"it’s so uncomfortable","zh":"太不舒服了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ uncomfortable (in- = un-)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est tellement inconfortable.","exEn":"It’s so uncomfortable.","src":2},{"fr":"on est mal assis par terre","en":"you’re uncomfortably seated on the ground","zh":"坐在地上很不舒服","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On est mal assis par terre et on mange mal…","exEn":"You’re seated uncomfortably on the ground and you eat badly…","src":3},{"fr":"les chips","en":"crisps (potato chips)","zh":"薯片","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"les chips = crisps; the final s is pronounced","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"…les chips, les salades de riz dégoûtantes,","exEn":"…crisps, disgusting rice salads,","src":3},{"fr":"j’ai du mal à manger proprement","en":"I find it hard to eat cleanly","zh":"我很难吃得干净","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et puis on a du mal à manger proprement, avec les assiettes en papier, les gobelets en carton,","exEn":"And then it’s hard to eat cleanly, with paper plates, cardboard beakers,","src":5},{"fr":"une assiette en papier","en":"a paper plate","zh":"纸盘","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Elle n’aime pas manger dans les assiettes en papier.","exEn":"She doesn’t like eating off paper plates.","src":5},{"fr":"je ne supporte pas les pique-niques","en":"I can’t stand picnics","zh":"我受不了野餐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"supporter = to put up with, NOT “to support”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bref, je ne supporte pas les pique-niques.","exEn":"In short, I can’t stand picnics.","src":7},{"fr":"au contraire","en":"on the contrary","zh":"相反","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ contrary","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais ce n’est pas vraiment une mauvaise idée, tu sais. Au contraire.","exEn":"But it’s not really a bad idea, you know. On the contrary.","src":8},{"fr":"un sac à dos","en":"a backpack","zh":"背包","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais ce n’est pas un panier, c’est un sac à dos !","exEn":"But it’s not a basket, it’s a backpack!","src":11},{"fr":"il y a tout ce qu’il faut","en":"there’s everything you need","zh":"需要的东西应有尽有","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il y a tout ce qu’il faut pour faire de vrais repas.","exEn":"There’s everything you need for a good meal.","src":12},{"fr":"Quel dommage !","en":"What a pity!","zh":"真可惜！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oh, il pleut. Quel dommage ! Pas de pique-nique aujourd’hui…","exEn":"Oh, it’s raining. What a pity! No picnic today…","src":19},{"fr":"Qu’est-ce qui se passe ?","en":"What’s going on?","zh":"发生什么事了？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais attends, qu’est-ce qui se passe ?","exEn":"But hang on, what’s happening?","src":18}],"uncertain":["Dialogue 10 English: “(basket)” gloss merged as lit; English given as “picnic hamper”.","Dialogue 17: the OCR English reads “a folding chairs”; kept “four folding chairs” as printed in French (quatre)."],"pdfPage":255,"gaps":[[1.58,3.32],[4.46,7.26],[88.66,89.98],[99.36,103.58],[105.52,107.6],[111.36,114.56],[117.04,120.14],[124.02,127.08],[132.58,135.62],[139.22,143.58]],"dur":143.6},{"n":49,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Quel est le monument le plus visité de France ?","en":"What is the most visited monument in France?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons cherché sur Internet, mais il y a pas mal de réponses différentes,","en":"We’ve searched the internet, but there are quite a lot of different answers,","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"ce qui nous fait penser que notre question est mal posée.","en":"which makes us think that our question is badly phrased.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"En effet, ce n’est pas la bonne question. Vous n’avez pas assez réfléchi.","en":"Indeed, it’s not the right question. You haven’t thought enough about it.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce qu’il faut chercher est “quel est le monument le plus visité cette année ?”.","en":"What you have to search for is “which is the most visited monument this year?”","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Regardez l’écran : “Le Louvre reçoit plus de visiteurs que la Tour Eiffel”.","en":"Look at the screen: “The Louvre receives more visitors than the Eiffel Tower.”","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Et juste en dessous “Le monument préféré des Français est le Château de Fontainebleau”.","en":"And just below: “The monument preferred by the French is the Fontainebleau château.”","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis aussi surprise que vous par Fontainebleau. À mon avis, c’est certainement le Louvre.","en":"I’m as surprised as you are by Fontainebleau. In my opinion, it’s certainly the Louvre.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, le musée que j’aime le plus est Orsay. Il a la meilleure collection de tableaux impressionnistes du monde.","en":"The museum that I like best is Orsay. It has the best collection of Impressionist paintings in the world.","lit":"the most"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-être, mais le Louvre est plus grand et plus intéressant. Plus je le visite, plus je le trouve superbe.","en":"Perhaps, but the Louvre is bigger and more interesting. The more I visit it, the more I find it superb.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Et la Tour Eiffel ? À mon avis, c’est elle qui reçoit le plus grand nombre de visiteurs. Même moi, j’y suis allé !","en":"And the Eiffel Tower? In my opinion, it’s the one that gets the biggest number of visitors. Even I have been!","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Eh non, désolée. J’ai trouvé les dernières informations qui circulent. Le gagnant est… Le Louvre !","en":"No, sorry. I’ve found the latest information. The winner is… the Louvre!","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr. Nous, les Français, nous apprécions les artistes plutôt que les ingénieurs.","en":"Of course. We French, we appreciate artists rather than engineers.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Pensez ce que vous voulez, Michel, mais la raison est plus simple : le billet d’entrée du musée est moins cher que celui du monument.","en":"Think what you like, Michel, but the reason is simpler: the ticket for the museum is cheaper than the one for the monument.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Verbs conjugated with être instead of avoir in the perfect tense – 1.1 The verbs","text":"A group of verbs expressing either movement or a change of place or condition form their perfect tense with être instead of avoir.\nThere are sixteen main verbs, which can be remembered with a simple mnemonic: meet Dr and Mrs Vandertramp:\nDevenir (to become); Revenir (to come back); Mourir (to die); Retourner (to return); Sortir (to go out); Venir (to come); Arriver (to arrive); Naître (to be born); Descendre (to come down, descend); Entrer (to enter); Rentrer (to come back); Tomber (to fall); Rester (to stay); Aller (to go); Monter (to go up); Partir (to leave).\nFive of these have irregular past participles: venir → venu, and its derivatives devenir → devenu and revenir → revenu, as well as naître → né and mourir → mort (see lesson 43 note 4).\nThere are other members of this family, notably the verb passer, to pass, which can be both transitive and intransitive. We’ll see these later on."},{"ref":"rev","title":"1.2 Conjugation","text":"All these verbs are conjugated with être instead of avoir in the perfect tense. This edifying little story might serve as another mnemonic (or a cautionary tale).\nCe lundi, Paul est arrivé très en retard pour son rendez-vous. Il est entré dans l’immeuble en courant et puis est monté au deuxième étage. Personne. Il est descendu au premier étage. Toujours personne. Il est retourné à l’accueil, où on lui a dit : “Votre client ? Mais il est parti il y a une heure. Il est resté longtemps dans votre bureau mais vous n’êtes pas venu. Il est devenu très impatient et il est sorti en colère. Je pense qu’il est rentré chez lui. Ou il est peut-être allé au café en face. Une chose est sûre : il n’est pas revenu ici”. Paul est sorti tellement vite du bâtiment qu’il est tombé sous un camion. Pauvre Paul : il est né pour travailler mais il est mort au travail.\nThis Monday, Paul arrived very late for his appointment. He ran into the building and then went upstairs to the second floor. No one was there. He went down to the first floor. Still no one. He went back to the reception desk, where he was told: “Your client? He left an hour ago. He was in your office for a long time, but you didn’t show up. He became very impatient and came out in an angry mood. I think he went home. Or maybe he went to the café opposite. One thing is sure: he didn’t come back here.” Paul ran out of the building so fast that he fell under a lorry. Poor Paul: he was born to work but he died at work.\nThe past participle of these être-conjugated verbs agrees in number and gender with the subject of the sentence. Returning to the above example:\nMarie est arrivée très en retard pour son rendez-vous, Mary arrived very late for her appointment.\nLes deux hommes sont montés au deuxième étage, The two men went up to the second floor.\nLes deux femmes sont retournées à l’accueil, The two women went back to the reception desk.\nIf the plural subjects of an être verb include a masculine and a feminine noun, then the participle agrees in the masculine:\nPaul et sa femme sont partis après le dîner, Paul and his wife left after dinner.\nLes hommes et les femmes sont arrivés au même moment, The men and the women arrived at the same time.\nHowever, this centuries-old rule on masculine precedence – Le masculin l’emporte sur le féminin, The masculine takes precedence over the feminine – is increasingly called into question in today’s more egalitarian world, and la règle de proximité, proximity rule, whereby adjectives (including participles) agree with the gender of the nearest noun, is making significant headway. Moreover, there is an ongoing debate about abolishing the rule on past-participle agreement altogether. In the meantime, however, we will stick to the established rule, albeit unenthusiastically."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Stressed, or emphatic, pronouns","text":"These pronouns also exist in English but are not always used in the same way.\nmoi, I, me; nous, we, us\ntoi, you; vous, you\nlui, he, him; eux, they, them (masc.)\nelle, she, her; elles, they, them (fem.)\nThey are used if the personal pronoun is emphasized (hence emphatic) or contrasted with another pronoun (or noun), which they generally precede.\nToi, tu es un vrai ami, You’re a real friend.\nAlain adore les pique-niques mais moi, je ne les supporte pas, Alain loves picnics but I can’t stand them.\n(A similar type of emphatic construction does exist in idiomatic English – You’re a real friend, you are. – but there is no one-on-one equivalent.)\nStressed pronouns are also used in some imperative – or emphatic – constructions:\nFais-le, toi ! You do it!\nTrouvez-la-moi, Find her/it for me.\nThe pronouns can also be used with a multiple subject:\nMa femme et moi, nous aimons travailler ensemble, My wife and I like working together.\nIn these examples, we can see that the pronoun seems disconnected from the sentence and could, theoretically, be omitted (which is why they are also called disjunctive pronouns). In French they are used almost systematically, which is why it’s important to learn them in context.\nFinally, there is an impersonal third-person emphatic pronoun, soi, which we will see later on."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Relative pronouns – 3.1 qui and que","text":"Qui and que are used to avoid repeating a noun or pronoun. Learners often confuse the two because they can refer both to people and to things. First and foremost, it is important to avoid the common mistake of systematically translating qui as who and que as that. Think grammatically: qui is the subject and que the direct object. In a subordinate clause, the pronouns are equivalent to who (or whom), which and that.\n• Qui\nThe subject of a sentence is often a person, but not always:\nL’homme qui nous aide pour le déménagement est mon beau-frère.\nThe man who is helping us with the removal is my brother-in-law.\nJ’ai horreur des couteaux qui ne coupent pas.\nI hate knives that don’t cut.\nSince qui refers to the subject, it is always followed by a verb. It can also be used with a preposition to replace an indirect object:\nNous sommes une société à qui vous pouvez faire confiance.\nWe’re a company that you can trust.\nLa société pour qui j’ai travaillé n’existe plus.\nThe company that I worked for no longer exists.\n(In informal English, the pronoun whom has all but disappeared, yet it is the equivalent of qui in this type of construction. Moreover, English prepositions are often placed alongside the verb – “the company that I work for” rather than “the company for which I work”. In French, however, the preposition and the relative always stay together. Which is why, to an English-speaking ear, French often sounds formal.)\n• Que\nThis pronoun replaces the direct object (thing or person) in a relative clause and is followed by a noun or pronoun:\nLa Tour Eiffel, que tout le monde connaît, est située près de la Seine.\nThe Eiffel Tower, which everyone knows, is located near the Seine.\nLes personnes que nous allons rencontrer aujourd’hui vont nous aider.\nThe people who[m] we are going to meet today are going to help us.\nBefore a vowel or the letter h, que becomes qu’:\nLa banane est le fruit qu’elle aime le moins.\nThe banana is the fruit that she likes least;\nLa chose qu’Henri déteste le plus est la pluie.\nThe thing (that) Henri hates the most is the rain.\nIn every case, the relative pronouns and their subsequent clauses must be placed immediately after the antecedent, i.e. the word or words that they replace (la Tour, les personnes, le morceau, etc.)."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3.2 ce qui and ce que","text":"Like qui and que, the indefinite relative pronouns ce qui and ce que introduce a subordinate clause. They mean which/what and are used in sentences where the noun or phrase referred to by a pronoun is not expressed.\nCe qui relates to the phrase that is the subject of the verb:\nMon mari a disparu, ce qui m’inquiète, My husband has disappeared, which worries me.\nIn this sentence, ce qui replaces the noun phrase his disappearance worries me.\nCe que relates to the object of the verb:\nJe ne comprends pas ce que vous voulez dire. I don’t understand what you mean.\nThe pronoun is often used for emphasis at the beginning of a sentence, just like What:\nCe qu’il veut dire est que nous sommes en retard. What he means is that we’re late.\nNote that only ce que is abbreviated to qu’ before a vowel."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Singular versus plural","text":"Some nouns that are always singular in French are plural in English, and vice versa (lesson 46). In some cases, a French noun exists in both the singular and the plural (i.e. it is countable), for instance une information, a piece of information, les informations, information in general (or the news, see below). One of the most obvious examples concerns garments “with two legs”. In addition to un pantalon, this rule also applies to un collant, tights; un jean, jeans; un maillot de bain, swimming trunks, un pyjama, pyjamas; un short, shorts; and un slip, underpants. To form the plural – for example, three pairs of shorts – simply add an s to the noun: trois shorts.\nOther common examples of this singular-to-plural shift include l’actualité, current affairs, l’escalier, the stairs, la politique, politics, and la vaisselle, dishes; as well as certain sciences, such as la physique, physics and la linguistique, linguistics.\nConversely, nouns that are generally plural in French but singular in English include les affaires, business; les conseils, advice; les toilettes, the toilet, les bagages, luggage, and les connaissances, knowledge.\nIt is important to remember these nouns because, in some cases, the singular and plural forms can have slightly different meanings. For example, les affaires, business, une affaire, a firm; la recherche, research, les recherches, searches; or l’actualité, current affairs, les actualités, the news (bulletin) – a synonym of les informations. We’ll see some more examples in upcoming lessons."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Prepositions","text":"Prepositions are vitally important because they connect different parts of a sentence and alter the meaning of verbs. French has no equivalent of the phrasal verbs that are so hard to learn in English (go up, down, in, out, through, etc.) but even so it is important to learn the preposition that normally accompanies a verb, especially where there is a difference between English and French: écouter, to listen to, for example. Here is a table showing the main prepositions (some of which, marked with an asterisk, are also adverbs). You have already seen all of them.\nà – to – Passe le carton à Jérôme. Pass the box to Jérôme.\naprès* – after – Après vous, madame. After you, madam.\navant* – before – Venez avant neuf heures. Come before 9 o’clock.\navec* – with – Elle sort avec mon meilleur ami. She’s going out with my best friend.\nchez – to/at (the home of) – Je reste chez moi ce soir. I’m staying at home this evening.\ncontre* – against – Le tableau est contre le mur. The painting is against the wall.\ndans – in – Ils connaissent un bon restaurant. They know a good restaurant.\nde – of, from – Sorya vient de Marseille. Sorya is from Marseille.\ndepuis* – since, from – Je suis là depuis ce matin. I have been here since this morning.\nderrière* – behind – Le magasin est derrière l’église. The shop is behind the church.\ndevant* – in front of – Le Louvre est devant vous. The Louvre is in front of you.\nen – in – Êtes-vous libre en avril ou en mai ? Are you free in April or May?\nentre – between – Il y a un match entre la France et l’Espagne. There’s a match between France and Spain.\npar – by – J’ai réussi à la joindre par téléphone. I managed to reach her by phone.\npendant – during, for – Elle a réfléchi pendant quelques secondes. She thought for a few seconds.\npour – for – Avez-vous un secret pour le loto ? Do you have a secret for the lotto?\nprès de – near – Ils sont assis près de la fenêtre. They’re sitting near/by the window.\nsous – under – Tes clés sont sous la table. Your keys are under the table.\nsur – on – On a fait des recherches sur Internet. We searched on the Internet.\nvers – towards – Il va vers la gare. He’s going towards the station.\nUse this table to revise the prepositions from time to time, until you can recognise them instantly."},{"ref":"rev","title":"Closing note","text":"You have now completed the receptive phase of the method. Lesson 50 marks the beginning of the productive phase, when you start to generate your own phrases and sentences. When you reach the end of a new lesson from 50 to 100, go back to the beginning of the book and study a lesson you have already seen, as instructed, and translate the main dialogue and the first exercise into French. For more information, see the Introduction.\nMaintenant, continuons (Now, let’s continue!)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"le monument le plus visité","en":"the most visited monument","zh":"游客最多的名胜","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"≈ monument","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel est le monument le plus visité de France ?","exEn":"What is the most visited monument in France?","src":1},{"fr":"il y a pas mal de réponses","en":"there are quite a lot of answers","zh":"有不少答案","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons cherché sur Internet, mais il y a pas mal de réponses différentes,","exEn":"We’ve searched the internet, but there are quite a lot of different answers,","src":2},{"fr":"ce que j’aime le plus","en":"what I like the most","zh":"我最喜欢的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Moi, le musée que j’aime le plus est Orsay.","exEn":"The museum that I like best is Orsay.","src":9},{"fr":"Plus je le visite, plus je le trouve superbe.","en":"The more I visit it, the more I find it superb.","zh":"我越参观，越觉得它很棒。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Plus je le visite, plus je le trouve superbe.","exEn":"The more I visit it, the more I find it superb.","src":10},{"fr":"Bien sûr.","en":"Of course.","zh":"当然。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr. Nous, les Français, nous apprécions les artistes plutôt que les ingénieurs.","exEn":"Of course. We French, we appreciate artists rather than engineers.","src":13},{"fr":"moins cher que","en":"cheaper than","zh":"比……便宜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le billet d’entrée du musée est moins cher que celui du monument.","exEn":"The ticket for the museum is cheaper than the one for the monument.","src":14},{"fr":"Pensez ce que vous voulez.","en":"Think what you like.","zh":"您爱怎么想就怎么想。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pensez ce que vous voulez, Michel, mais la raison est plus simple.","exEn":"Think what you like, Michel, but the reason is simpler.","src":14}],"uncertain":["Note 1.1: OCR reads “Rester (to rest)”; corrected to “to stay” (standard meaning).","Closing note: OCR reads “from 50 to 700”; the book has 100 lessons here, so written as “from 50 to 100”.","Dialogue 9 lit: gloss only inferred from the book’s “(the most)”."],"pdfPage":261,"gaps":[[1.62,3.68],[5.12,8.02],[84.68,88.56]],"dur":88.6},{"n":50,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je m’ennuie…","titleEn":"I get bored…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Que faites-vous dans la vie ?","en":"What do you do for a living?","lit":"in the life"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je m’ennuie. Tous les jours, sans exception, je fais exactement la même chose.","en":"I get bored. Every day, without exception, I do exactly the same thing.","lit":"I bore myself"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je me réveille à la même heure et je me connecte à Internet pour lire les messages dans ma boîte de réception.","en":"I wake up at the same time and I log on to the internet to read the messages in my inbox.","lit":"I wake myself; I connect myself; box of receipt"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Si j’ai vraiment envie, j’écoute les informations à la radio – je me méfie des journaux télévisés","en":"If I really want to, I listen to the news on the radio – I’m wary of TV news broadcasts","lit":"I have envy; I distrust myself"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"car je trouve qu’ils se trompent constamment et, de toute façon, les infos me dépriment.","en":"because I find that they constantly get things wrong and, in any case, the news depresses me.","lit":"mistake themselves"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Moins on les écoute, plus on est tranquille.","en":"The less you listen to it, the calmer you feel.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"De temps à autre, je lis un magazine sur ma tablette, mais je le finis en cinq minutes.","en":"From time to time, I read a magazine on my tablet, but I finish it in five minutes.","lit":"time to other"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Au bout d’un quart d’heure, je me lève très lentement – je ne me dépêche pas –","en":"After a quarter of an hour, I get up very slowly – I don’t hurry –","lit":"at the end of; I raise myself; I don’t hurry myself"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"puis je vais dans la salle de bains, où je me rase, me lave et me brosse les dents.","en":"then I go into the bathroom, where I shave, wash, and brush my teeth.","lit":"I shave myself, wash myself, brush myself the teeth"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite je m’habille et me prépare à partir au travail, en centre-ville.","en":"Then I get dressed and prepare to leave for work, in the town centre.","lit":"I dress myself"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"J’y vais en voiture, jamais en bus ou en métro, et j’y suis en vingt minutes.","en":"I go by car, never by bus or by metro, and I’m there in twenty minutes.","lit":"in car; in bus; in metro"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"En arrivant, je m’installe dans mon bureau et ferme la porte à clé.","en":"When I arrive, I settle in my office and lock the door.","lit":"on arriving; I install myself"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je fais semblant de travailler, mais j’essaie surtout de ne pas m’endormir.","en":"I pretend to work, but I try above all not to fall asleep.","lit":"I make semblance"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Le travail, c’est dur, vous savez. Je me demande si je suis fait pour ça.","en":"Work is very hard, you know. I wonder whether I’m made for it.","lit":"I ask myself"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est très stressant de ne rien faire. Je dois me détendre autant que possible.","en":"It’s very stressful doing nothing. I must relax as much as possible.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Mon médecin m’a conseillé de ne pas m’énerver et de me reposer quand je peux.","en":"My doctor advised me not to get annoyed and to rest when I can.","lit":"annoy myself; repose myself"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais vous vous amusez quand même un peu ? En fait, quel métier faites-vous ?","en":"But you enjoy yourself a little anyway? In fact, what job do you do?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis Président-directeur général d’une des entreprises de mon père.","en":"I’m Chairman and Managing Director of one of my father’s companies.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça ne m’étonne pas !","en":"That doesn’t surprise me!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"From now on, transcriptions are given only for words that are particularly difficult to pronounce or that resemble English words and could therefore be mispronounced."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(6) tranquille: despite the seemingly feminine ending (-ille), the adjective tranquille is masculine here, so the last syllable is pronounced [-eel]."},{"ref":"1","text":"This lesson looks at pronominal verbs and, more specifically, reflexive verbs, where the subject is the person or thing performing the action. Indicated by se (or s’ in the infinitive: s’ennuyer, for example), these verbs always take reflexive pronouns in French, though not necessarily in English. Thus the transitive verb ennuyer quelqu’un, to bore someone, requires a pronoun in the reflexive form: je m’ennuie, which translates as I am bored (“I bore myself”) or, in some contexts, I get bored. Likewise, Je connecte mon ordinateur à mon téléphone, I’m connecting my computer to my phone, requires a pronoun if the subject and object are the same person: Je me connecte à Internet, I’m connecting to the internet."},{"ref":"2","text":"à with the definite article (au with a masculine noun) translates on in the case of communications media such as the TV, radio or phone: à la radio, à la télévision, au téléphone. Be careful not to use sur."},{"ref":"3","text":"se méfier (de) means to mistrust or be suspicious of. (It is the negative form of se fier à, to trust.) Elle se méfie des informations qu’elle lit en ligne, She mistrusts the news that she reads online. (Remember: de becomes des because informations is plural.) The imperative Méfiez-vous ! is commonly used as a warning – Méfiez-vous du chien !, Beware of the dog – and in certain proverbs: Il faut se méfier de l’eau qui dort (“You need to be wary of water that sleeps”), i.e. Still waters run deep."},{"ref":"4","text":"Here is another apocope (lesson 17 note 7): les informations, the news, is shortened in everyday speech to les infos."},{"ref":"5","text":"me is one of the reflexive pronouns, along with te, se (the same as the infinitive), nous and vous. But it is also a direct object pronoun (lesson 24 note 6). For example, Je me réveille à midi, I wake up at noon, but Le bruit me réveille toutes les nuits, The noise wakes me every night."},{"ref":"6","text":"en, as we know, is both an adverbial pronoun (lesson 31 note 2) and a preposition meaning on, in, to, etc. Note how it is used in this lesson, and how it contrasts with dans (lines 3 and 12). In particular, en is used with modes of transport – en bus, en voiture, etc. – but not for walking: aller à pied (lesson 25 line 10). We’ll look at en vs. dans in greater detail in the next revision lesson."},{"ref":"7","text":"Many verbs that are reflexive in French but not in English concern everyday actions such as getting up (se lever), shaving (se raser), dressing (s’habiller), brushing one’s teeth (se brosser les dents), but also taking a shower (se doucher), applying make-up (se maquiller) and brushing one’s hair (se brosser les cheveux)."},{"ref":"8","text":"See lesson 42 section 1.1."},{"ref":"9","text":"The noun semblant, the origin of semblance, is commonly used in the expression faire semblant (de), to pretend or act as if. Mon enfant fait semblant de ne pas m’entendre quand je dis non, My child pretends not to hear me when I say no."},{"ref":"10","text":"demander (à), to ask, is used in the pronominal form, se demander, to mean to wonder (“to ask oneself”): Je me demande ce qu’il faut faire, I wonder what to do. The verb is rarely used in the negative."},{"ref":"11","text":"Note that the English idiom I don’t wonder means I’m not surprised and is translated with the verb s’étonner (the root of to astonish): Ça ne m’étonne pas, That doesn’t surprise me."},{"ref":"","text":"Job titles are often hard to translate because corporate structures differ from one country to another. Also, since English is widely used in the business world, many French companies anglicise to some extent their in-house vocabulary. Thus a firm may have both un directeur and un manager, who have different positions even though the second title is the translation of the first! But in most public companies, the top job is the same: le Directeur général, or managing director (also known as the chief executive). In some companies, le DG [day-zhay], as he (or she) is often called, can also be the chair of the company, in which case the title is le Président-directeur général (“chairman and managing director”) or le PDG [pay-day-zhay]. Note that most of the nouns used in French corporate job titles are still masculine, even if the person holding the office is female."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Quand je me lève, je me douche, ensuite je me maquille, et enfin je me brosse les cheveux.","a":"When I get up, I shower, then I put on my makeup and, lastly, I brush my hair."},{"q":"Plus on écoute les infos, moins on est tranquille. – Et ça t’étonne ?","a":"The more you listen to the news, the less easy you feel. – And that surprises you?"},{"q":"Je sais que le travail est un peu stressant, mais détendez-vous !","a":"I know the work is a bit stressful, but relax!"},{"q":"Ils s’ennuient, car ils font la même chose tous les jours et toutes les semaines.","a":"They’re bored because they do the same thing, every day and every week."},{"q":"Cette journaliste se trompe de temps à autre, mais ses articles sont toujours intéressants.","a":"That journalist gets things wrong from time to time, but her articles are always interesting."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"He wakes up very early and logs on immediately to his inbox.","t":"Il {{se réveille}} très tôt et {{se connecte}} tout de suite {{à sa boîte}} de réception."},{"cue":"Are you going by metro or by car? – I’m going [there] on foot.","t":"{{Est-ce que}} tu {{y}} vas {{en métro ou en voiture}} ? – {{J’y}} vais {{à pied}}."},{"cue":"When he arrives, he goes into his office, closes the door and pretends to work.","t":"{{En arrivant}}, il va {{dans}} son bureau, ferme la porte et {{fait semblant de travailler}}."},{"cue":"Guy doesn’t get worked up. He rests when he can, and he falls asleep easily in the evening.","t":"Guy {{ne s’énerve pas}}. Il {{se repose}} quand il peut, et il {{s’endort}} facilement le soir."},{"cue":"They’re pretending to be bored. Still waters run deep!","t":"Ils {{font semblant}} de {{s’ennuyer}}. Il faut {{se méfier}} de {{l’eau qui dort}} !"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je m’ennuie","en":"I’m bored","zh":"我很无聊","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’ennuie. Tous les jours, sans exception, je fais exactement la même chose.","exEn":"I get bored. Every day, without exception, I do exactly the same thing.","src":2},{"fr":"je me réveille à la même heure","en":"I wake up at the same time","zh":"我每天同一时间醒来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me réveille à la même heure et je me connecte à Internet…","exEn":"I wake up at the same time and I log on to the internet…","src":3},{"fr":"je me connecte à Internet","en":"I log on to the internet","zh":"我连上网络","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ connect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me connecte à Internet pour lire les messages dans ma boîte de réception.","exEn":"I log on to the internet to read the messages in my inbox.","src":3},{"fr":"j’écoute les informations à la radio","en":"I listen to the news on the radio","zh":"我听广播新闻","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"à la radio, not “sur”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si j’ai vraiment envie, j’écoute les informations à la radio.","exEn":"If I really want to, I listen to the news on the radio.","src":4},{"fr":"je me méfie des journaux télévisés","en":"I distrust the TV news","zh":"我不信任电视新闻","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “may-fee”: a “may-be fee” trap – I stay wary of it","ex":"Je me méfie des journaux télévisés car je trouve qu’ils se trompent constamment.","exEn":"I’m wary of TV news broadcasts because I find that they constantly get things wrong.","src":4},{"fr":"les infos me dépriment","en":"the news depresses me","zh":"新闻让我沮丧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ depress","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…de toute façon, les infos me dépriment.","exEn":"…in any case, the news depresses me.","src":5},{"fr":"Moins on les écoute, plus on est tranquille.","en":"The less you listen to it, the calmer you feel.","zh":"听得越少，心里越平静。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Moins on les écoute, plus on est tranquille.","exEn":"The less you listen to it, the calmer you feel.","src":6},{"fr":"de temps à autre","en":"from time to time","zh":"偶尔","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"De temps à autre, je lis un magazine sur ma tablette.","exEn":"From time to time, I read a magazine on my tablet.","src":7},{"fr":"je ne me dépêche pas","en":"I don’t hurry","zh":"我不着急","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me lève très lentement – je ne me dépêche pas –","exEn":"I get up very slowly – I don’t hurry –","src":8},{"fr":"je me brosse les dents","en":"I brush my teeth","zh":"我刷牙","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"reflexive in French: se brosser les dents","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"…où je me rase, me lave et me brosse les dents.","exEn":"…where I shave, wash, and brush my teeth.","src":9},{"fr":"je fais semblant de travailler","en":"I pretend to work","zh":"我假装在工作","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ semblance","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je fais semblant de travailler, mais j’essaie surtout de ne pas m’endormir.","exEn":"I pretend to work, but I try above all not to fall asleep.","src":13},{"fr":"je me demande si je suis fait pour ça","en":"I wonder whether I’m made for it","zh":"我在想自己是否适合这个","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"se demander = to wonder","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me demande si je suis fait pour ça.","exEn":"I wonder whether I’m made for it.","src":14},{"fr":"je dois me détendre","en":"I must relax","zh":"我必须放松","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ détente","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je dois me détendre autant que possible.","exEn":"I must relax as much as possible.","src":15},{"fr":"je me repose quand je peux","en":"I rest when I can","zh":"我有机会就休息","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"…de ne pas m’énerver et de me reposer quand je peux.","exEn":"…not to get annoyed and to rest when I can.","src":16},{"fr":"Ça ne m’étonne pas !","en":"That doesn’t surprise me!","zh":"我一点也不奇怪！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ astonish","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça ne m’étonne pas !","exEn":"That doesn’t surprise me!","src":19}],"uncertain":["Dialogue 8 English: the book's English says “half an hour” but the French is “un quart d’heure”; written as “a quarter of an hour”.","Note 10: the text breaks off after “rarely used in the negative” at the page end; kept as printed.","Note pron 6: wording of the tranquille explanation follows the OCR text."],"pdfPage":271,"gaps":[[1.72,3.66],[4.6,7.62],[14.3,15.32],[20.54,21.54],[34.36,35.52],[39.68,40.78],[54.32,55.32],[59.78,60.82],[72.88,73.92],[93.18,97],[99.16,101.44],[107.44,110.5],[114.96,118.3],[121.62,124.64],[129.22,132.46],[136.72,140.6]],"dur":140.6},{"n":51,"kind":"lesson","title":"Hâtez-vous lentement !","titleEn":"More haste, less speed","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout le monde est là ? Non, il manque Brice. Où est-il ?","en":"Is everyone there? No, Brice is missing. Where is he?","lit":"it misses Brice"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Toujours au lit, mais ne l’attendons pas. Tu sais très bien qu’il ne se lève jamais avant midi.","en":"Still in bed, but let’s not wait for him. You know very well that he never gets up before noon.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Dépêchons-nous ! Nous perdons du temps.","en":"Let’s hurry up! We’re wasting time.","lit":"we lose time"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"La pièce commence dans vingt minutes et nous allons manquer le début.","en":"The play starts in twenty minutes and we’re going to miss the beginning.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"On n’est pas encore prêts et le théâtre n’est pas tout près. Comment faire ?","en":"We’re not ready yet and the theatre isn’t very close by. What shall we do?","lit":"how to do"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est trop tard pour y aller en transports en commun, donc on va prendre la voiture.","en":"It’s too late to go there by public transport, so we’re going to take the car.","lit":"in common"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous y serons en dix minutes si nous partons dans deux minutes.","en":"We’ll be there in ten minutes if we leave in two minutes.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu plaisantes ! On n’a pas le droit de se garer dans les rues autour du centre.","en":"You’re joking! You’re not allowed to park in the streets around the centre.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Ne vous inquiétez pas : c’est facile comme tout.","en":"Don’t worry: it’s as easy as anything.","lit":"easy like all"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Je m’arrête dix secondes dans une place de livraison mais je n’arrête pas le moteur.","en":"I stop for ten seconds in a delivery space but I don’t turn off the engine.","lit":"I don’t stop the engine"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous descendez vite et je vais garer la voiture dans le parking public qui se trouve du côté droit du tribunal.","en":"You get out quickly and I’ll go and park the car in the public car park that is on the right-hand side of the courthouse.","lit":"that finds itself"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Pendant ce temps, vous achetez les billets et on se retrouve dans le foyer.","en":"Meanwhile, you buy the tickets and we meet up in the foyer.","lit":"during this time"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Bon, on y va. Bougez-vous !","en":"Right, let’s go. Get moving!","lit":"one there goes"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Euh, attendez un instant. Il y a un petit problème.","en":"Um, wait a moment. There’s a slight problem.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce qui se passe ? Nous allons être en retard !","en":"What’s happening? We’re going to be late!","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne trouve pas mes clés de voiture.","en":"I can’t find my car keys.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne me souviens pas où je les ai mises. Elles ne sont pas dans ma poche,","en":"I don’t remember where I put them. They’re not in my pocket,","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"ni dans le tiroir du meuble en bas de l’escalier, où je les mets d’habitude.","en":"nor in the drawer of the piece of furniture at the bottom of the stairs, where I usually put them.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Pourquoi souris-tu ?","en":"Why are you smiling?","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Parce qu’elles sont dans ta main, idiot ! Vite, dépêche-toi. En route !","en":"Because they’re in your hand, dummy! Quick, hurry up. Let’s get going!","lit":"on road"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(Sentence 5) Despite the different spellings, the adjective prêt (ready) and the adverb près (near) are pronounced identically [pray].\n(Sentence 10) The c of the adjective and noun second(e) is pronounced as a hard “g” [suhgoh̃ / suhgoh̃d]."},{"ref":"1","text":"We saw manquer, to miss, in lesson 27 note 2 (and below, in line 4). Here, however, the verb is used in the impersonal form, so il can be translated in different ways: Il manque un mot dans cette phrase, There’s a word missing in this sentence. The pronoun is invariable, even if the subject is plural: Il manque trois personnes, Three people are missing. Compare this with Où est-il ?, where il, of course, means he."},{"ref":"2","text":"As we saw in the previous lesson, the negative of a reflexive verb is formed by placing the particle ne before the pronoun and pas (or an adverb, lesson 32 note 5) after the verb: Je ne me souviens pas où tu habites, I don’t remember where you live. Ils ne s’arrêtent jamais de travailler ! They never stop working!"},{"ref":"3","text":"The imperative form of reflexive verbs depends on whether the sentence is negative or affirmative. In the negative, the pronoun comes before the verb, as usual: Ne nous inquiétons pas, Let’s not worry. In the affirmative, however, the pronoun comes afterwards: Dépêchez-vous !, Hurry up! Remember also that te becomes toi in an affirmative command: Dépêche-toi !; but it does not change if the command is negative: Ne te dépêche pas."},{"ref":"4","text":"Comment faire ? (literally “How to do?”) is a useful, short way of asking what to do or how to do something. It can be used alone – J’ai perdu mon mot de passe. Comment faire ? I’ve lost my password. What should I do? – or with a complement: Comment faire pour fermer une boîte de dialogue ? What do you do to close a dialogue box?"},{"ref":"5","text":"You know some of the differences between en and dans, summarised in lesson 50. With an expression of time, the difference is quite subtle but very important. While en expresses the time taken to do something – Je peux le faire en cinq minutes, I can do it in five minutes – dans indicates when the action takes, or will take, place: Elle peut le faire dans cinq minutes, She can do it in five minutes’ time. Look again at sentence 7: en describes the duration of the trip whereas dans specifies when that trip will begin."},{"ref":"6","text":"le droit means the law (lesson 43 line 7) but also the right (lesson 20 line 6). Thus the expression avoir le droit (de) means to have the right (to) but also, in everyday speech, to be allowed to: Vous n’avez pas le droit de vous garer ici, You’re not allowed to park here. (Remember that droit is also an adjective, meaning right, as opposed to gauche, left.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"Here is an example of a verb used in the transitive and reflexive forms: Où est-ce que tu gares ta voiture ? Where do you park your car? If we drop the direct object of the sentence (i.e. if the subject and object are the same), we use a reflexive: Où est-ce que tu te gares ?, Where do you park / Where are you parking?"},{"ref":"8","text":"Some verbs have a slightly different meaning when used in the reflexive form. In this case, Je m’arrête means I pull up (in a car, for example) whereas J’arrête le moteur means I turn off the engine. Similarly, se trouver (“to find itself / oneself”), the reflexive form of trouver, means more or less the same as to be: Trouville se trouve en face de Deauville, Trouville is opposite Deauville."},{"ref":"9","text":"retrouver (literally “to find again”) means to find something that one has lost. But it is often translated simply by to find: J’ai retrouvé mes clés, I’ve found my keys. The reflexive form generally means to meet up with: On se retrouve au restaurant à vingt heures, We’re meeting up at the restaurant at 8 pm."},{"ref":"10","text":"We know that the pronominal verb se passer means to happen (lesson 48 note 10). The actual translation will depend on the context, though, and is often very simple: La fête se passe chez nous demain soir, The party is at our place tomorrow evening."},{"ref":"","text":"Simple verbs like être and avoir are often shunned – especially in written French – in favour of more complex synonyms such as se trouver and posséder, which have the same meaning but sound more “elegant” than simply be or have. Thus Ils possèdent une grande maison is preferred to Ils ont une grande maison, even though the two sentences are synonymous. This stylistic habit, which underlines the impression of formality discussed in Lesson 7, can be disconcerting at first, but it soon becomes second nature."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Tu n’as pas le droit de te garer du côté gauche de la rue. Gare-toi en face du tribunal.","a":"You’re not allowed to park on the left-hand side of the street. Park opposite the courthouse."},{"q":"Je ne retrouve pas mon grand panier bleu. Je ne sais pas où je l’ai mis.","a":"I can’t find my big blue basket. I don’t know where I put it."},{"q":"Tout le monde est prêt à partir ? – Non, il manque Jade et Camille.","a":"Is everyone ready to leave? – No, Jade and Camille are missing."},{"q":"Je ne me souviens pas comment faire pour ne pas perdre mes messages.","a":"I don’t remember what to do in order not to lose my messages."},{"q":"Où se passe la fête demain soir ? – Chez Simone.","a":"Where is the party tomorrow night? – At Simone’s."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"He is stopping for a couple of seconds in front of the supermarket but he isn’t turning off the engine.","t":"{{Il s’arrête}} deux secondes {{devant}} le supermarché mais il {{n’arrête pas}} le moteur."},{"cue":"What’s going on? – I forgot to buy the tickets. – You’re kidding?","t":"{{Qu’est-ce qui se passe}} ? – {{J’ai oublié d’acheter}} les billets. – {{Vous plaisantez}} ?"},{"cue":"He has lost his car keys. – I think that they are on the piece of furniture where he usually puts them.","t":"Il a perdu ses {{clés de voiture}}. – Je pense {{qu’elles sont}} sur {{le meuble où}} il {{les met d’habitude}}."},{"cue":"Where do you park your car? – I park in the street, beneath my place.","t":"Où {{est-ce que vous garez}} votre voiture ? – Je {{me gare}} dans la rue, {{en bas de}} chez moi."},{"cue":"We can walk there in an hour and a half. – In that case, let’s leave in ten minutes.","t":"On peut {{y}} aller {{à pied en}} une heure et demie. – Dans ce cas, {{partons dans}} dix minutes."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"il manque Brice","en":"Brice is missing","zh":"布里斯没来（缺了布里斯）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “monk” – a monk is missing from the choir, il manque","ex":"Tout le monde est là ? Non, il manque Brice.","exEn":"Is everyone there? No, Brice is missing.","src":1},{"fr":"Dépêchons-nous !","en":"Let’s hurry up!","zh":"我们快点！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “day-pesh-on” – “day, a peach!” – hurry or the peach will rot","ex":"Dépêchons-nous ! Nous perdons du temps.","exEn":"Let’s hurry up! We’re wasting time.","src":3},{"fr":"il est trop tard","en":"it’s too late","zh":"太晚了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est trop tard pour y aller en transports en commun, donc on va prendre la voiture.","exEn":"It’s too late to go there by public transport, so we’re going to take the car.","src":6},{"fr":"Comment faire ?","en":"What shall we do? / How do we do it?","zh":"怎么办？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On n’est pas encore prêts et le théâtre n’est pas tout près. Comment faire ?","exEn":"We’re not ready yet and the theatre isn’t very close by. What shall we do?","src":5},{"fr":"on n’a pas le droit de se garer","en":"you’re not allowed to park","zh":"不允许停车","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"droit ≈ “right” (to have the right to)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On n’a pas le droit de se garer dans les rues autour du centre.","exEn":"You’re not allowed to park in the streets around the centre.","src":8},{"fr":"je vais garer la voiture","en":"I’m going to park the car","zh":"我去把车停好","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"garer ≈ garage","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous descendez vite et je vais garer la voiture dans le parking public qui se trouve du côté droit du tribunal.","exEn":"You get out quickly and I’ll go and park the car in the public car park that is on the right-hand side of the courthouse.","src":11},{"fr":"je m’arrête dix secondes","en":"I stop for ten seconds","zh":"我停十秒钟","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “arrest” – the police arrest you and you stop, je m’arrête","ex":"Je m’arrête dix secondes dans une place de livraison mais je n’arrête pas le moteur.","exEn":"I stop for ten seconds in a delivery space but I don’t turn off the engine.","src":10},{"fr":"le moteur","en":"the engine","zh":"发动机","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ motor","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’arrête dix secondes dans une place de livraison mais je n’arrête pas le moteur.","exEn":"I stop for ten seconds in a delivery space but I don’t turn off the engine.","src":10},{"fr":"nous allons être en retard","en":"we’re going to be late","zh":"我们要迟到了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’est-ce qui se passe ? Nous allons être en retard !","exEn":"What’s happening? We’re going to be late!","src":15},{"fr":"je ne trouve pas mes clés de voiture","en":"I can’t find my car keys","zh":"我找不到我的车钥匙","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne trouve pas mes clés de voiture.","exEn":"I can’t find my car keys.","src":16},{"fr":"je ne me souviens pas où je les ai mises","en":"I don’t remember where I put them","zh":"我不记得把它们放哪儿了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne me souviens pas où je les ai mises. Elles ne sont pas dans ma poche,","exEn":"I don’t remember where I put them. They’re not in my pocket,","src":17},{"fr":"c’est facile comme tout","en":"it’s as easy as anything","zh":"容易极了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ne vous inquiétez pas : c’est facile comme tout.","exEn":"Don’t worry: it’s as easy as anything.","src":9},{"fr":"attendez un instant","en":"wait a moment","zh":"请稍等","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"instant = same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Euh, attendez un instant. Il y a un petit problème.","exEn":"Um, wait a moment. There’s a slight problem.","src":14},{"fr":"on n’est pas encore prêts","en":"we’re not ready yet","zh":"我们还没准备好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On n’est pas encore prêts et le théâtre n’est pas tout près.","exEn":"We’re not ready yet and the theatre isn’t very close by.","src":5}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":277,"gaps":[[1.94,3.8],[5.34,8.16],[88,91.8],[93.62,95.78],[100.68,103.74],[107.96,110.98],[115.06,118.26],[121,124.04],[127,130.88]],"dur":130.9},{"n":52,"kind":"lesson","title":"Nous nous sommes bien amusés !","titleEn":"We really enjoyed ourselves!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai accepté de répondre à un sondage sur « les attentes des Français dans le domaine de l’audiovisuel ».","en":"I’ve agreed to answer a survey on “the expectations of the French in the field of broadcasting.”","lit":"I have accepted to answer a sounding"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Voulez-vous participer ou, au moins, donner votre point de vue sur cette première question :","en":"Do you want to take part or, at least, give your point of view on this first question:","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"« Quelle est votre série préférée, celle qui vous a fait rire ou pleurer le plus ? »","en":"“What is your favourite series, the one that made you laugh or cry the most?”","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"La mienne est l’émission d’humour « Puissance faible ».","en":"Mine is the comedy show “Low Power.”","lit":"the mine"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Et la tienne, Mathilde ? La vôtre, Léon et Adèle ?","en":"And yours, Mathilde? Yours, Léon and Adèle?","lit":"the yours"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, je ne suis pas fan de séries. Je préfère les magazines et les documentaires.","en":"Me, I’m not a fan of series. I prefer magazine programmes and documentaries.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Et vous deux, vous préférez les divertissements ou les débats politiques ?","en":"And you two, do you prefer entertainment shows or political debates?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Ni l’un ni l’autre. Nous regardons la télévision pour nous distraire, pas pour nous instruire !","en":"Neither one nor the other. We watch television to entertain ourselves, not to educate ourselves!","lit":"to distract ourselves, not to instruct ourselves"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est vrai mais j’ai quand même un faible pour les concours de cuisine.","en":"It’s true, but even so I have a soft spot for cooking contests.","lit":"a weak"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour moi, la meilleure émission de tous les temps est « Bon appétit ! ».","en":"For me, the best show ever is “Enjoy Your Meal!”","lit":"of all the times"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous vous en souvenez ? Avec Maïka, la présentatrice célèbre, celle avec l’accent basque.","en":"Do you remember it? With Maïka, the famous presenter, the one with the Basque accent.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"La femme maladroite qui s’est coupé la main plusieurs fois en faisant la cuisine ?","en":"The clumsy woman who cut her hand several times while cooking?","lit":"making the cooking"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est bien elle. En fait, elle s’est coupée une fois seulement, mais tout le monde s’en souvient.","en":"That’s the one. In fact, she cut herself only once, but everyone remembers it.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"L’émission s’est arrêtée il y a dix ans après huit saisons. Je l’ai regardée du début à la fin,","en":"The show ended ten years ago after eight seasons. I watched it from beginning to end,","lit":"stopped itself"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"et je ne me suis jamais ennuyée un seul instant. Chère Maïka, vous me manquez !","en":"and I never got bored for a single moment. Dear Maïka, I miss you!","lit":"you me are missing"},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, nous nous sommes bien amusés, à l’époque. Maintenant, tout est en ligne.","en":"Yes, we really enjoyed ourselves at the time. Now, everything is online.","lit":"well amused"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais écrire ça sur la feuille de réponse.","en":"I’m going to write that on the answer sheet.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Zut, mon stylo ne marche plus. Prête-moi le tien, s’il te plaît Mathilde.","en":"Damn, my pen doesn’t work anymore. Lend me yours, please Mathilde.","lit":"the yours"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu peux prendre le nôtre si tu veux. C’est amusant, ce petit jeu ! Continuons.","en":"You can take ours if you want. This little game is fun! Let’s carry on.","lit":"the ours"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(Sentence 17) The phoneme euille is pronounced like the noun œil: [uhyy] (see lesson 32)."},{"ref":"1","text":"une attente, from attendre, to wait, has a literal meaning, found in terms such as une salle d’attente, a waiting room, and also in sentences such as: L’attente à la caisse est trop longue, The wait at the checkout is too long. But it can also mean an expectation: Nous essayons toujours de répondre aux attentes de nos clients, We always try to meet our customers’ expectations."},{"ref":"2","text":"When used as adverbs, the superlatives le plus, the most, and le moins, the least, can be placed before the verb as well as after it: Quelle est l’émission qui vous a le plus fait rire ? Pre-placement is more literary but nevertheless quite common."},{"ref":"3","text":"Possessive pronouns (yours, mine, etc.) are formed with a definite article (le, la, les) and a pronominal word (mien, tien, etc.). Like possessive adjectives, they agree in number and gender with the noun(s) they replace, not with the possessor. The pronoun vôtre takes a circumflex over the o to distinguish it from the possessive adjective votre: Ceci est votre clé ➔ C’est la vôtre."},{"ref":"4","text":"The adjective fan, an abbreviation of fanatique, fanatical, is used idiomatically to mean wildly enthusiastic. Je suis fan de foot, I’m a football fanatic. The word can also be used as a noun: C’est un fan de l’équipe de Bordeaux, He’s a fan of the Bordeaux team. Both adjective and noun are pronounced [fan] (with a short [a]) because they come from the English word."},{"ref":"5","text":"We have seen ni used in a negative construction to mean nor (lesson 51 line 18). If the negative verb applies to two things (like neither … nor), simply repeat the conjunction instead of using pas: Mes enfants n’aiment ni les fruits ni les légumes, My children like neither fruit nor vegetables. Be careful: unlike in English, the verb must always be negative. Ni l’un ni l’autre can often be translated simply as neither: Ni l’un ni l’autre n’est venu, Neither came."},{"ref":"6","text":"The adjective faible means weak (see lesson 27) but can be used in a broader sense of lacking: Elle a une faible chance de réussir, She has a slim chance of succeeding; Ces légumes sont faibles en calories, These vegetables are low in calories. The corresponding noun is la / une faiblesse, (a) weakness: Ma faiblesse en français est un problème dans mon travail, My weakness in French is a problem in my job. By contrast, the expression avoir un faible pour means to have a weakness for or, idiomatically, a soft spot for: J’ai un faible pour les émissions d’humour, I have a soft spot for comedy shows."},{"ref":"7","text":"While many verbs can take a reflexive pronoun if the subject and object are identical, others are always (or “essentially”) pronominal. For example, se souvenir (de) means to remember and, in everyday usage, is always pronominal: Est-ce que tu te souviens de cette émission ?, Do you remember that show? A near-synonym of se souvenir de is se rappeler (with no preposition): Je me rappelle cette émission, I remember that show. (In the non-reflexive form, rappeler means to remind: Rappelez-moi votre nom, Remind me of your name.)"},{"ref":"8","text":"The agreement of past participles in pronominal verbs is complex. If the reflexive pronoun (me, se, etc.) is the indirect object of the sentence, the participle does not agree with the reflexive pronoun: Ma femme s’est coupé la main, My wife has cut her hand. But if the pronoun is the direct object, then the participle agrees in gender and number: Ma femme s’est coupée, My wife has cut herself. In some cases, notably first-group verbs, there is no difference in the pronunciation of the past participle (coupé and coupée are both pronounced [koopay]). However, this is not the case for the other verb groups, as we shall see."},{"ref":"9","text":"The concrete noun une / la cuisine, the kitchen (lesson 36 line 6), has an abstract use that means cooking / cookery – or even cuisine! J’adore la cuisine basque, I love Basque cooking / cuisine. Faire la cuisine means to cook, or, more precisely, to do the cooking. Je fais toujours la cuisine pour mes amis, I always cook / I do the cooking for my friends."},{"ref":"10","text":"Used with the past tense and followed by a time period, il y a is the equivalent of ago: J’ai vu ce présentateur il y a vingt ans, I saw that presenter 20 years ago. The action described is always complete."},{"ref":"11","text":"An idiosyncratic use of manquer (see lesson 51) is when the verb to miss expresses a feeling of regret or sadness. The French sentence structure is the reverse of English: I miss you is Tu me manques (or Vous me manquez), literally “you me are missing.” Thus the person (or thing) missed is the subject of the sentence in French but the object in English. Thus if Maïka misses me, I would say Je manque à Maïka."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Viktor est faible en français, mais c’est pour ça que j’ai un faible pour lui !","a":"Viktor is weak in French, but that’s why I have a soft spot for him!"},{"q":"C’est l’émission d’humour qui m’a le plus fait rire. – Oui, j’en suis fan aussi.","a":"It’s the comedy programme that made me laugh the most. – Yes, I’m a fan, too."},{"q":"Est-ce que vous vous souvenez de son nom ? – Oui, il s’appelle Marc.","a":"Do you remember his name? – Yes, he’s called Marc."},{"q":"Le café me manque beaucoup mais je n’ai plus le droit d’en boire.","a":"I miss coffee a lot but I’m not allowed to drink it."},{"q":"Je regarde la télé pour me distraire, pas pour m’instruire. Et toi ?","a":"I watch TV to entertain myself, not to educate myself. What about you?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Did your daughters wash this morning? – Not really. They washed their faces. – That’s a start.","t":"Est-ce que tes filles {{se sont lavées}} ce matin ? – {{Pas vraiment}}. Elles {{se sont lavé}} le visage. – C’est un début !"},{"cue":"The noise stopped only two minutes ago. I miss it already.","t":"Le bruit {{s’est arrêté il y a}} deux minutes seulement. {{Il me manque}} déjà !"},{"cue":"I’ve lost my phone. Lend me yours, please. Ours doesn’t work anymore.","t":"J’ai perdu mon téléphone. {{Prêtez}}-moi {{le vôtre}} s’il vous plaît. {{Le nôtre}} ne marche {{plus}}."},{"cue":"He cut his finger four times while making dinner. – He cut himself? What a dummy!","t":"Il {{s’est coupé le doigt}} quatre fois {{en préparant}} le dîner. – Il {{s’est coupé}} ? {{Quel}} idiot !"},{"cue":"What are your expectations in the field of broadcasting? Documentaries? Magazine programmes? – We like neither.","t":"{{Quelles sont}} vos {{attentes}} dans le {{domaine}} de l’audiovisuel ? Des {{documentaires}} ? Des {{magazines}} ? – Nous {{n’aimons ni l’un ni l’autre}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un sondage","en":"a survey, a poll","zh":"民意调查，问卷调查","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “son-dahj” – imagine a man called Son-Dahj being polled in the street","ex":"J’ai accepté de répondre à un sondage sur « les attentes des Français dans le domaine de l’audiovisuel ».","exEn":"I’ve agreed to answer a survey on “the expectations of the French in the field of broadcasting.”","src":1},{"fr":"les attentes","en":"expectations","zh":"期望，期待","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"from attendre = to wait (une attente)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai accepté de répondre à un sondage sur « les attentes des Français dans le domaine de l’audiovisuel ».","exEn":"I’ve agreed to answer a survey on “the expectations of the French in the field of broadcasting.”","src":1},{"fr":"donner votre point de vue","en":"to give your point of view","zh":"发表你的观点","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ point of view","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voulez-vous participer ou, au moins, donner votre point de vue sur cette première question :","exEn":"Do you want to take part or, at least, give your point of view on this first question:","src":2},{"fr":"celle qui vous a fait rire","en":"the one that made you laugh","zh":"让你发笑的那个","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Quelle est votre série préférée, celle qui vous a fait rire ou pleurer le plus ? »","exEn":"“What is your favourite series, the one that made you laugh or cry the most?”","src":3},{"fr":"la mienne","en":"mine (feminine)","zh":"我的（阴性名词）","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La mienne est l’émission d’humour « Puissance faible ».","exEn":"Mine is the comedy show “Low Power.”","src":4},{"fr":"le nôtre","en":"ours (masculine)","zh":"我们的（阳性名词）","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu peux prendre le nôtre si tu veux.","exEn":"You can take ours if you want.","src":19},{"fr":"je ne suis pas fan de séries","en":"I’m not a fan of series","zh":"我不是电视剧迷","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"fan = same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Moi, je ne suis pas fan de séries.","exEn":"Me, I’m not a fan of series.","src":6},{"fr":"ni l’un ni l’autre","en":"neither one nor the other","zh":"两者都不","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ni l’un ni l’autre.","exEn":"Neither one nor the other.","src":8},{"fr":"pour nous distraire","en":"to entertain ourselves","zh":"为了消遣娱乐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ distract","ff":"se distraire = to have fun, not only “to be distracted”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous regardons la télévision pour nous distraire, pas pour nous instruire !","exEn":"We watch television to entertain ourselves, not to educate ourselves!","src":8},{"fr":"un faible pour","en":"a soft spot for","zh":"偏爱，特别喜欢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"faible ≈ feeble (weak)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est vrai mais j’ai quand même un faible pour les concours de cuisine.","exEn":"It’s true, but even so I have a soft spot for cooking contests.","src":9},{"fr":"de tous les temps","en":"of all time, ever","zh":"有史以来的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour moi, la meilleure émission de tous les temps est « Bon appétit ! ».","exEn":"For me, the best show ever is “Enjoy Your Meal!”","src":10},{"fr":"Vous vous en souvenez ?","en":"Do you remember it?","zh":"您还记得吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"souvenir = same word in English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous vous en souvenez ?","exEn":"Do you remember it?","src":11},{"fr":"en faisant la cuisine","en":"while cooking","zh":"做饭的时候","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La femme maladroite qui s’est coupé la main plusieurs fois en faisant la cuisine ?","exEn":"The clumsy woman who cut her hand several times while cooking?","src":12},{"fr":"il y a dix ans","en":"ten years ago","zh":"十年前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"L’émission s’est arrêtée il y a dix ans après huit saisons.","exEn":"The show ended ten years ago after eight seasons.","src":14},{"fr":"vous me manquez","en":"I miss you","zh":"我想念你们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"literally “you are missing to me” – the missed person is the subject","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Chère Maïka, vous me manquez !","exEn":"Dear Maïka, I miss you!","src":15},{"fr":"mon stylo ne marche plus","en":"my pen doesn’t work anymore","zh":"我的笔不能用了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “marsh” – a pen stuck in a marsh no longer works","ex":"Zut, mon stylo ne marche plus.","exEn":"Damn, my pen doesn’t work anymore.","src":18}],"uncertain":["sentence 12: the printed English says she cut her finger; the French text reads la main (kept la main, translated as hand)","note 11: cross-reference given as lesson 57 in the scan, assumed lesson 51"],"pdfPage":283,"gaps":[[1.82,3.9],[5.3,8.26],[19.8,20.8],[47.2,48.24],[104.78,108.58],[110.88,112.66],[117.14,119.88],[124.76,128.1],[131.88,134.76],[138.3,141.22],[145.16,148.98]],"dur":149},{"n":53,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je ne ferai plus jamais ça !","titleEn":"I will never do that again!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ferai mes courses comme d’habitude au marché ce vendredi.","en":"I will do my shopping as usual at the market this Friday.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"J’achèterai des produits frais, du poisson et de la charcuterie – du pâté et du saucisson.","en":"I will buy fresh produce, fish and deli meats – pâté and sausage.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je prendrai aussi des fruits : des pêches, des abricots, et des raisins s’il y en a.","en":"I’ll also take some fruit: peaches, apricots and grapes if there are any.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais pour les produits du quotidien, j’essaierai de passer une commande en ligne.","en":"But for everyday products, I’ll try to place an order online.","lit":"to pass an order"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Dans le rayon épicerie, il me faut des conserves, du sel, du poivre et de la moutarde.","en":"In the grocery section, I need canned goods, salt, pepper and mustard.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai besoin de jus de fruits, de farine, de pâtes, mais aussi de papier toilette et de dentifrice.","en":"I need fruit juice, flour, pasta, but also toilet paper and toothpaste.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Et aussi l’huile d’olive. J’en ajouterai à mon panier si elle est en promotion cette semaine.","en":"And olive oil, too. I’ll add some to my basket if it’s on special offer this week.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Voilà, je valide la commande et je règle mes achats.","en":"There, I confirm the order and I pay for my purchases.","lit":"I validate"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Finalement, c’est simple comme bonjour ! […]","en":"In the end, it’s as easy as pie! …","lit":"simple as good-day"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour madame, on vient vous livrer les courses que vous avez commandées ce matin.","en":"Good morning madam, we’ve come to deliver the shopping that you ordered this morning.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Où est-ce qu’on les met ? Il y a quatre cartons en tout.","en":"Where shall we put them? There are four boxes in all.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Posez-les sur la table contre le frigo dans la cuisine si vous voulez bien.","en":"Put them on the table against the fridge in the kitchen if you please.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est à gauche tout au fond du couloir.","en":"It’s on the left, right at the end of the corridor.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Voulez-vous de l’aide pour vider les cartons ?","en":"Do you want help to empty the boxes?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"D’abord je vais vérifier que tout est là.","en":"First I’m going to check that everything is there.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais ce ne sont pas les articles que j’ai sélectionnés sur le site !","en":"But these aren’t the items I selected on the site!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"En plus, vous m’avez livré un paquet de lessive et un bocal de cornichons !","en":"What’s more, you’ve delivered a packet of washing powder and a jar of gherkins!","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Je n’ai commandé aucun des deux. Vous allez tout rapporter.","en":"I ordered neither of them. You’re going to take everything back.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne ferai plus jamais mes courses à distance : j’irai au magasin et je parlerai avec un être humain.","en":"I’ll never again do my shopping remotely: I’ll go to the shop and I’ll speak with a human being.","lit":"at distance"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce ne sera pas évident car il y aura beaucoup de monde aujourd’hui.","en":"That won’t be easy because there will be a lot of people today.","lit":"obvious; many of world"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"This is the first person singular of the future tense of the irregular verb faire, to do / make: je ferai, tu feras, il/elle fera, nous ferons, vous ferez, ils feront. In most cases, this tense corresponds to the English future with will. Note the difference between this tense and the “immediate” future formed with aller before the infinitive (in line 18, for example)."},{"ref":"2","text":"The future tense of verbs in the first two groups (ending in -er and -ir) is formed by adding the endings -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the stem. (See lesson 41 note 9). In some cases, the stem changes slightly. For example, with acheter, to buy, the first e takes a grave accent (j’achèterai, etc.) while the y in essayer changes to an i (j’essaierai, etc.). Neither of these changes makes a noticeable difference to the pronunciation."},{"ref":"3","text":"Verbs ending in -re form their future from the infinitive, but drop the final -e. Thus the future tense of prendre, to take, is je prendrai, tu prendras, il/elle prendra, nous prendrons, vous prendrez, ils/elles prendront."},{"ref":"4","text":"un rayon means a ray or a beam (Enfin un rayon de soleil ! At last a ray of sunshine!). In a shopping context, however, it means a department, section or counter in a store or supermarket. In this case, there is no need for a preposition between rayon and the qualifying noun: le rayon frais, the fresh [food] section."},{"ref":"5","text":"une promotion has many meanings, including the cognate a promotion (i.e. professional advancement). In stores, it means a special offer. Les meubles de jardin sont en promotion cette semaine, Garden furniture is on special offer this week. The word is often abbreviated to en promo."},{"ref":"6","text":"Bonjour, Good morning, is used in several idioms, notably C’est simple comme bonjour, It’s as easy as pie. It can also be found in the idiomatic exclamation Bonjour les dégâts ! (“Hello the damage”), equivalent to What a mess!"},{"ref":"7","text":"tout, all, everything (see line 18) is also used as an adverb of degree, as seen in lesson 51 line 5. Nous habitons tout près d’ici, We live very near here. As such, it refers not only to physical distance but also to position or intensity: Ton nom est tout en haut de la liste, Your name is right at the top of the list. In this function, tout is invariable."},{"ref":"8","text":"aucun(e), none, not any (lesson 46 note 4) can also mean neither when followed by de / des and a determiner. The accompanying verb is always negative: Elle n’aime aucune de ces robes, She likes neither of these dresses. (Compare with ni l’un … ni l’autre, lesson 52)."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here is the future tense of the irregular verb aller, to go: j’irai, tu iras, il/elle ira, nous irons, vous irez, ils/elles iront. Note that only the stem is irregular, while the endings are exactly the same as for regular verbs (see note 1)."},{"ref":"10","text":"Another irregular future: être, to be, becomes je serai, tu seras, il/elle sera, nous serons, vous serez, ils/elles seront. The negative is formed, as usual, with ne … pas: Les commandes ne seront pas livrées demain matin, The orders will not be delivered tomorrow morning."},{"ref":"","text":"La charcuterie (from the term la chair cuite, or cooked flesh) is a blanket term for cooked products made from pork (le porc) including ham (le jambon), pâté (le pâté), and sausage, for which French has two words: une saucisse, the generic term for any type of sausage eaten hot, and un saucisson, a hard, cooked sausage usually consumed when cold. The word charcuterie can refer to both the products (deli meats or cold cuts) and the shop where they are bought. The term for a grocery, une épicerie, comes from the noun une épice, a spice, which is what these shops originally sold. (The initial syllable ép- is often – but not always – the equivalent of sp- in English: une éponge, a sponge, les épinards, spinach, épier, to spy, etc.)"},{"ref":"","text":"You will have noticed how your vocabulary is expanding daily. To consolidate what you have learned, we are starting to re-use words from earlier lessons so that you can move effortlessly from the receptive phase to the productive phase. Likewise, the English translation of the lesson texts is becoming less literal and more idiomatic. You’re now well on your way to understanding and speaking everyday French. Félicitations ! (Congratulations!)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Demain, j’irai seule au supermarché pour acheter de la lessive.","a":"Tomorrow I’ll go on my own to the supermarket to buy washing powder."},{"q":"Je ne commanderai plus jamais mes billets de train en ligne. C’est trop compliqué.","a":"I’ll never order my train tickets online again. It’s too complicated."},{"q":"Le pâté et le saucisson sont en promo au rayon charcuterie.","a":"Pâté and sausage are on special offer at the deli counter."},{"q":"On vous livrera la semaine prochaine. – Ça sera trop tard.","a":"We’ll deliver to you next week. – That will be too late."},{"q":"Ce n’est pas évident de trouver une solution. – Au contraire, c’est simple comme bonjour.","a":"It isn’t easy to find a solution. – On the contrary, it’s as easy as pie."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We won’t buy any canned foods but we’ll take some fruit, especially peaches and apricots.","t":"{{Nous n’achèterons pas}} de conserves mais {{nous prendrons}} des fruits, surtout des {{pêches}} et des {{abricots}}."},{"cue":"They live really near here, I think, right at the end of that street on the right.","t":"Ils habitent {{tout près d’ici}}, je pense, {{tout au fond de}} cette rue {{à droite}}."},{"cue":"If you want help with your removal, I’ll try to come. I’ll do my best, I promise.","t":"Si tu veux de {{l’aide}} avec {{ton déménagement}}, {{j’essaierai}} de venir. Je {{ferai}} de {{mon mieux}}, je te promets."},{"cue":"He likes neither of these fruit juices. – Neither one nor the other?","t":"{{Il n’aime aucun}} de ces deux {{jus de fruits}}. – {{Ni l’un}}, {{ni l’autre}} ?"},{"cue":"We’ll add your surname and first name to the list, but we won’t add your phone details.","t":"{{Nous ajouterons}} votre {{nom}} et {{prénom}} sur la liste mais {{nous n’ajouterons pas}} vos coordonnées téléphoniques."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je ferai mes courses","en":"I’ll do my shopping","zh":"我会去买东西（购物）","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ferai mes courses comme d’habitude au marché ce vendredi.","exEn":"I will do my shopping as usual at the market this Friday.","src":1},{"fr":"des produits frais","en":"fresh produce","zh":"新鲜食品","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"produit ≈ product","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"J’achèterai des produits frais, du poisson et de la charcuterie – du pâté et du saucisson.","exEn":"I will buy fresh produce, fish and deli meats – pâté and sausage.","src":2},{"fr":"j’essaierai de passer une commande en ligne","en":"I’ll try to place an order online","zh":"我会试着在网上下订单","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"commande ≈ command/order; en ligne = online","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais pour les produits du quotidien, j’essaierai de passer une commande en ligne.","exEn":"But for everyday products, I’ll try to place an order online.","src":4},{"fr":"le rayon","en":"the department, section (of a shop)","zh":"（商店的）专柜，货架区","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ ray (but in a shop = section)","ff":"un rayon = also a ray of light","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dans le rayon épicerie, il me faut des conserves, du sel, du poivre et de la moutarde.","exEn":"In the grocery section, I need canned goods, salt, pepper and mustard.","src":5},{"fr":"j’ai besoin de jus de fruits","en":"I need fruit juice","zh":"我需要果汁","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"J’ai besoin de jus de fruits, de farine, de pâtes, mais aussi de papier toilette et de dentifrice.","exEn":"I need fruit juice, flour, pasta, but also toilet paper and toothpaste.","src":6},{"fr":"en promotion","en":"on special offer","zh":"促销中，特价","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"promotion","ff":"promotion = special offer in shops, not only a career promotion","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en ajouterai à mon panier si elle est en promotion cette semaine.","exEn":"I’ll add some to my basket if it’s on special offer this week.","src":7},{"fr":"je règle mes achats","en":"I pay for my purchases","zh":"我付款（结账）","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"sounds like “reggle” – you pay with a ruler (une règle) laid on the counter","ex":"Voilà, je valide la commande et je règle mes achats.","exEn":"There, I confirm the order and I pay for my purchases.","src":8},{"fr":"c’est simple comme bonjour","en":"it’s as easy as pie","zh":"简单极了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Finalement, c’est simple comme bonjour !","exEn":"In the end, it’s as easy as pie!","src":9},{"fr":"on vient vous livrer les courses","en":"we’ve come to deliver your shopping","zh":"我们来给您送购物订单","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"livrer ≈ deliver","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “leave-ray” – “leave it, ray!” – the courier leaves the boxes at your door","ex":"Bonjour madame, on vient vous livrer les courses que vous avez commandées ce matin.","exEn":"Good morning madam, we’ve come to deliver the shopping that you ordered this morning.","src":10},{"fr":"un carton","en":"a cardboard box","zh":"纸箱","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ carton","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Où est-ce qu’on les met ? Il y a quatre cartons en tout.","exEn":"Where shall we put them? There are four boxes in all.","src":11},{"fr":"tout au fond du couloir","en":"right at the end of the corridor","zh":"在走廊的最里头","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est à gauche tout au fond du couloir.","exEn":"It’s on the left, right at the end of the corridor.","src":13},{"fr":"un paquet de lessive","en":"a packet of washing powder","zh":"一包洗衣粉","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"paquet ≈ packet","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En plus, vous m’avez livré un paquet de lessive et un bocal de cornichons !","exEn":"What’s more, you’ve delivered a packet of washing powder and a jar of gherkins!","src":17},{"fr":"je n’ai commandé aucun des deux","en":"I ordered neither of them","zh":"这两样我都没订","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “oh-kuh” – “oh, no kuh-ind of either one!”","ex":"Je n’ai commandé aucun des deux.","exEn":"I ordered neither of them.","src":18},{"fr":"je ne ferai plus jamais mes courses à distance","en":"I’ll never do my shopping remotely again","zh":"我再也不远程购物了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"distance","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne ferai plus jamais mes courses à distance : j’irai au magasin et je parlerai avec un être humain.","exEn":"I’ll never again do my shopping remotely: I’ll go to the shop and I’ll speak with a human being.","src":19},{"fr":"il y aura beaucoup de monde","en":"there will be a lot of people","zh":"会有很多人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"monde = people here, not only “world”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce ne sera pas évident car il y aura beaucoup de monde aujourd’hui.","exEn":"That won’t be easy because there will be a lot of people today.","src":20}],"uncertain":["note 1: the scan says “in line 4”; changed to line 18 where Vous allez tout rapporter uses aller + infinitive"],"pdfPage":291,"gaps":[[1.74,3.42],[4.72,7.54],[30.12,31.3],[82.86,84.12],[107.4,110.72],[112.54,114.58],[118.1,121.38],[125.52,128.44],[132,135],[138.28,141.38],[146.64,150.52]],"dur":150.5},{"n":54,"kind":"lesson","title":"Votez pour moi : vous ne le regretterez pas…","titleEn":"Vote for me. You won’t regret it…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Les élections législatives auront lieu dans quelques mois","en":"The general election will take place in a few months’ time","lit":"legislative"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"et nous aurons sans doute un nouveau gouvernement.","en":"and we shall probably have a new government.","lit":"without doubt"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Les deux partis de centre-gauche feront cause commune contre la droite","en":"The two centre-left parties will make common cause against the right","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"et présenteront un seul candidat, qui ne sera pas un politique professionnel.","en":"and field a single candidate, who will not be a professional politician.","lit":"present"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils tiendront un grand meeting à Grenoble ce week-end-ci. Est-ce que vous viendrez ?","en":"They will hold a big meeting in Grenoble this weekend. Will you be coming?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, nous ne viendrons pas. Je ne serai pas en France et Romain ne pourra pas se libérer.","en":"No, we won’t. I won’t be in France and Romain won’t be able to get away.","lit":"free himself"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"En tout cas, le fameux candidat ne dira rien d’original. C’est toujours la même chanson.","en":"In any case, the much-vaunted candidate won’t say anything original. It’s always the same old story.","lit":"the same song"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"En es-tu sûre ? Qui sait ? Il nous surprendra peut-être quand il parlera ce soir.","en":"Are you sure of that? Who knows? Perhaps he will surprise us when he speaks tonight.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"J’en suis sûre et certaine, et c’est ça qui me rend triste !","en":"I’m absolutely sure, and that’s what makes me sad!","lit":"sure and certain"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors, contre qui vas-tu t’abstenir cette fois-ci ? […]","en":"So, who are you going to abstain against this time? …","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"« Français, Françaises, mes chers compatriotes, rassurez-vous : je ne serai pas long.","en":"“Frenchmen, Frenchwomen, my dear countrymen, rest assured: I won’t be long-winded.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne parlerai pas pendant des heures pour ne rien dire.","en":"I won’t talk for hours to say nothing.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne promettrai pas une solution miracle pour résoudre les problèmes du pays.","en":"I won’t promise a miracle solution to solve the problems of the country.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais c’est mon devoir de dire la vérité : mon adversaire ne sera ni de droite, ni de gauche :","en":"But it’s my duty to tell the truth: my opponent won’t be from the right or from the left:","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"mon adversaire sera l’argent et la corruption qui l’accompagne.","en":"my opponent will be money and the corruption that goes with it.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous n’aurez jamais à douter de mon intégrité, je vous le jure.","en":"You will never have to doubt my integrity, I swear to you.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, vous savez que vous devrez faire le bon choix, pour vous et votre portefeuille, euh, votre pays.","en":"Now, you know you will have to make the right choice, for you and your wallet, er, your country.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"La situation est urgente et vous ne pourrez plus dire : « Ça ne me concerne pas ».","en":"The situation is urgent and you will no longer be able to say: “It doesn’t concern me.”","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous n’aurez qu’un choix à faire : voter pour moi.","en":"You will have only one choice to make: vote for me.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Le capitalisme, c’est l’exploitation de l’Homme par l’Homme, mais ma philosophie, c’est le contraire ! »","en":"Capitalism is the exploitation of man by man, but my philosophy is the opposite!”","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The abstract noun le doute means doubt. Je n’ai pas de doute sur leur réponse, I have no doubt about their answer. The root verb is douter (de). Il doute de tout, He doubts everything. The phrase sans doute, without a doubt, can be used to mean probably: Elle a raison sans doute, mais nous devons être certains, She’s probably right but we have to be certain."},{"ref":"2","text":"The singular noun la politique, seen in lesson 49, means politics: La politique est l’art du possible, Politics is the art of the possible. In this context, the noun is never plural. But it can also mean a policy: une politique économique, an economic policy, in which case it takes a regular plural -s: les politiques économiques. Un(e) politique can also mean a (male or female) politician, although the loan word un(e) politicien(ne) is frequently used nowadays. (Remember that politique is also an adjective, see lesson 52 line 7)."},{"ref":"3","text":"The noun un meeting is used to describe a political gathering or assembly. It is also used in athletics (un meeting d’athlétisme). Like most loan words, meeting is masculine. (Don’t confuse it with une réunion, a business meeting, lesson 38 line 15)."},{"ref":"4","text":"The demonstrative adverb -ci (lesson 31 note 3) can be used with time periods to emphasise proximity: Ce mois-ci, This coming month. It can also be used in the plural: ces jours-ci, [in] the coming days."},{"ref":"5","text":"The adjective célèbre (the origin of celebrity and celebrated, lesson 52 line 11) means famous or well-known. But fameux has a much broader meaning, often relating to quality: Son pâté est fameux !, His (or Her) pâté is fantastic! The word can also be used to describe something that has been widely discussed: C’est ça, ton fameux plan ? C’est nul ! Is that the plan that you’ve been talking about? It’s rubbish!"},{"ref":"6","text":"In a subordinate clause beginning with a time expression, such as quand, the verb is in the future tense if the verb in the main clause is also in the future. This contrasts with English, which uses the present. Nous serons tous là quand il arrivera, We will all be there when he arrives."},{"ref":"7","text":"Remember that adjectives always agree in gender with the person they refer to, but those ending in -e (like triste, sad) in both masculine and feminine agree only in the plural: des choses tristes, sad things (See lesson 14)."},{"ref":"8","text":"devoir is a verb, must (lesson 17 note 5), but also a masculine noun meaning duty: C’est mon devoir de vous le dire, It’s my duty to tell you. In a school context, it is used in the plural (les devoirs) to mean homework (because the student is obliged to do the work)."},{"ref":"9","text":"l’homme is used in the general sense to mean mankind. For that reason, it is often written with an initial capital: Le Musée de l’Homme à Paris est fermé le mardi, The Museum of Mankind in Paris is closed on Tuesdays. A concerted effort is underway in France to move towards more gender-neutral language, especially in official contexts. So the expression les droits de l’homme (or l’Homme), “the rights of man” is making way for les droits humains, the back translation of human rights. (The Canadian francophone community has long used the term les droits de la personne.)"},{"ref":"","text":"Politics is a perennial topic of conversation in France. The country is organised constitutionally as une république, a republic (the current regime, dating from 1958, is the fifth republic, la cinquième république, since 1792). It is headed by le Président de la République, elected by universal suffrage every five years (the term of office is known as un quinquennat, from the Latin word for five). Les élections législatives (a general election) are also held every five years to elect les députés, representatives who sit in the lower house of parliament, la Chambre des députés (although the president has the right to seek an early dissolution of parliament, le parlement). The members of the upper house, le Sénat, are called les sénateurs and are elected every nine years."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"On a fait de notre mieux, mais le résultat n’est pas fameux.","a":"We did our best but the result isn’t brilliant."},{"q":"Êtes-vous sûrs ? – Sûrs et certains. C’est ça qui nous rend tristes.","a":"Are you sure? – Absolutely sure. That’s what makes us sad."},{"q":"Le candidat n’a rien dit d’original. C’est toujours la même chanson.","a":"The candidate said nothing original. It’s always the same old story."},{"q":"Je regrette mais je ne pourrai pas me libérer demain. Je suis très occupée ces jours-ci.","a":"I’m sorry but I won’t be able to get away tomorrow. I’m very busy these days."},{"q":"Tu as sans doute raison : il ne sait rien parce qu’il a toujours douté de tout !","a":"You’re probably right: he knows nothing because he has always doubted everything!"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You will never have to doubt your decision because you have made the right choice.","t":"Vous {{n’aurez jamais}} à {{douter de}} votre décision car {{vous avez fait le bon choix}}."},{"cue":"The politician will talk for hours in order to say nothing. That’s why I won’t come.","t":"Le {{politicien}} {{parlera}} pendant des heures pour {{ne rien dire}}. C’est pour ça que {{je ne viendrai pas}}."},{"cue":"They will present their programme when they talk on the TV this evening, but without telling the truth.","t":"Ils {{présenteront}} leur programme {{quand ils parleront}} ce soir à la télévision, mais sans {{dire la vérité}}."},{"cue":"In any case, the party will not field a candidate at the general election.","t":"{{En tout cas}}, le parti {{ne présentera pas}} de candidat {{aux élections législatives}}."},{"cue":"I’ll have to leave just after lunch because there will be a lot of people on the roads.","t":"Je {{devrai partir}} juste après le déjeuner car {{il y aura beaucoup de monde}} sur les routes."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"les élections législatives auront lieu","en":"the general election will take place","zh":"议会选举将举行","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"élection ≈ election","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les élections législatives auront lieu dans quelques mois","exEn":"The general election will take place in a few months’ time","src":1},{"fr":"sans doute","en":"probably, no doubt","zh":"很可能，大概","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"doute ≈ doubt","ff":"sans doute = “probably”, not “certainly”","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “son doot” – “son, doot!” – he will probably toot his horn","ex":"et nous aurons sans doute un nouveau gouvernement.","exEn":"and we shall probably have a new government.","src":2},{"fr":"un seul candidat","en":"a single candidate","zh":"唯一的一位候选人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"candidat ≈ candidate","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et présenteront un seul candidat, qui ne sera pas un politique professionnel.","exEn":"and field a single candidate, who will not be a professional politician.","src":4},{"fr":"Est-ce que vous viendrez ?","en":"Will you come?","zh":"你们会来吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils tiendront un grand meeting à Grenoble ce week-end-ci. Est-ce que vous viendrez ?","exEn":"They will hold a big meeting in Grenoble this weekend. Will you be coming?","src":5},{"fr":"il ne pourra pas se libérer","en":"he won’t be able to get away","zh":"他抽不出空来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"libérer ≈ liberate (set oneself free)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, nous ne viendrons pas. Je ne serai pas en France et Romain ne pourra pas se libérer.","exEn":"No, we won’t. I won’t be in France and Romain won’t be able to get away.","src":6},{"fr":"en tout cas","en":"in any case","zh":"无论如何","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En tout cas, le fameux candidat ne dira rien d’original.","exEn":"In any case, the much-vaunted candidate won’t say anything original.","src":7},{"fr":"c’est toujours la même chanson","en":"it’s always the same old story","zh":"总是老一套","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"literally “the same song”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est toujours la même chanson.","exEn":"It’s always the same old story.","src":7},{"fr":"j’en suis sûre et certaine","en":"I’m absolutely sure","zh":"我百分之百确定","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"certain = same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en suis sûre et certaine, et c’est ça qui me rend triste !","exEn":"I’m absolutely sure, and that’s what makes me sad!","src":9},{"fr":"ça me rend triste","en":"it makes me sad","zh":"这让我难过","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’en suis sûre et certaine, et c’est ça qui me rend triste !","exEn":"I’m absolutely sure, and that’s what makes me sad!","src":9},{"fr":"rassurez-vous","en":"rest assured, don’t worry","zh":"请放心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ reassure","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Français, Françaises, mes chers compatriotes, rassurez-vous : je ne serai pas long.","exEn":"“Frenchmen, Frenchwomen, my dear countrymen, rest assured: I won’t be long-winded.","src":11},{"fr":"pour ne rien dire","en":"to say nothing","zh":"什么也不说（说了等于没说）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne parlerai pas pendant des heures pour ne rien dire.","exEn":"I won’t talk for hours to say nothing.","src":12},{"fr":"c’est mon devoir de dire la vérité","en":"it’s my duty to tell the truth","zh":"说实话是我的责任","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"vérité ≈ verity (truth)","ff":"les devoirs = homework","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais c’est mon devoir de dire la vérité : mon adversaire ne sera ni de droite, ni de gauche :","exEn":"But it’s my duty to tell the truth: my opponent won’t be from the right or from the left:","src":14},{"fr":"vous n’aurez jamais à douter de","en":"you will never have to doubt","zh":"您永远不必怀疑","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"douter ≈ doubt","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous n’aurez jamais à douter de mon intégrité, je vous le jure.","exEn":"You will never have to doubt my integrity, I swear to you.","src":16},{"fr":"faire le bon choix","en":"to make the right choice","zh":"做出正确的选择","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, vous savez que vous devrez faire le bon choix, pour vous et votre portefeuille, euh, votre pays.","exEn":"Now, you know you will have to make the right choice, for you and your wallet, er, your country.","src":17},{"fr":"vous ne pourrez plus dire","en":"you will no longer be able to say","zh":"您将不能再说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La situation est urgente et vous ne pourrez plus dire : « Ça ne me concerne pas ».","exEn":"The situation is urgent and you will no longer be able to say: “It doesn’t concern me.”","src":18}],"uncertain":["note 4: the scan says -ci has no plural form but then gives ces jours-ci; wording changed to say it can be used in the plural","politics culture note: the clause about the fifth republic and the year 1792 is garbled in the scan; kept as printed"],"pdfPage":297,"gaps":[[2.02,3.92],[6.1,9.24],[34.96,36.08],[79.86,80.94],[84.14,85.14],[96.62,97.62],[106.92,110.34],[112.86,115.08],[118,121.14],[125.52,128.48],[132.02,135.04],[140.78,143.78],[147.12,150.64]],"dur":150.6},{"n":55,"kind":"lesson","title":"Mais c’est horrible, ton film !","titleEn":"But your movie is awful!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai vu « La Mort atroce » hier. Je te le conseille vraiment.","en":"I saw “Dreadful Death” yesterday. I really recommend it.","lit":"atrocious"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Il a battu les records d’audience pour un film d’épouvante.","en":"It has broken viewing records for a horror movie.","lit":"audience"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Où l’as-tu vu ? En vidéo ?","en":"Where did you see it? On video?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, à côté de chez moi il y a un complexe multisalles qui vient d’ouvrir.","en":"No, next to my place there’s a multiplex that has just opened.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"La programmation est variée, les salles sont spacieuses, et on y vend de très bonnes glaces !","en":"The programming is varied, the theatres are spacious, and they sell very good ice cream!","lit":"rooms"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"De quoi parle le film ?","en":"What is the movie about?","lit":"of what speaks the film"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"L’action se passe à Annecy, où j’ai passé toutes mes vacances quand j’étais petite.","en":"The action takes place in Annecy, where I spent all my holidays when I was little.","lit":"small"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"L’une des vedettes est Jeanne Morteau, un monstre sacré du cinéma.","en":"One of the stars is Jeanne Morteau, a giant of the movies.","lit":"a holy monster"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Elle m’amuse bien avec ses airs de grande dame ! Mais quelle actrice !","en":"She really amuses me with her fine-lady airs! But what an actress!","lit":"her big-lady airs"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est l’histoire d’un vieux couple qui, en apparence, s’entend à merveille.","en":"It’s the story of an old couple who, apparently, get on marvellously.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils habitent dans un hameau qui se trouve tout près d’un vieil immeuble.","en":"They live in a hamlet which is right near an old apartment building.","lit":"finds itself"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils pensent que ce bâtiment est abandonné, mais les apparences peuvent tromper…","en":"They think that this building is abandoned, but appearances can be deceptive…","lit":"deceive"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Un jour, la femme est seule à la maison lorsqu’elle entend des bruits étranges dehors.","en":"One day, the woman is alone in the house when she hears strange noises outside.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Elle sort dans la cour, où elle trouve des dizaines d’oiseaux morts par terre.","en":"She goes out into the courtyard, where she finds dozens of dead birds on the ground.","lit":"tens of"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Elle croit d’abord que c’est son chat qui les a tués.","en":"She believes at first that it’s her cat that has killed them.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais elle se trompe ! Soudain elle voit son mari avec une énorme hache à la main.","en":"But she is mistaken! Suddenly she sees her husband with a huge axe in his hand.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Elle se bat avec lui mais il est beaucoup plus fort et il la bat à coups de bâton.","en":"She fights with him but he is much stronger and he hits her with a stick.","lit":"at blows of stick"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais c’est horrible ! Qu’est-ce qui se passe ensuite ?","en":"But that’s horrible! What happens next?","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne sais pas parce que je me suis cachée sous la table jusqu’à la fin.","en":"I don’t know because I hid under the table until the end.","lit":"I hid myself"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Néanmoins, je me suis amusée comme une gosse et j’ai adoré le film.","en":"Even so, I enjoyed myself like a kid and I loved the movie.","lit":"nevertheless"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Du moins, la première moitié…","en":"At least, the first half…","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The feminine noun épouvante means terror, dread. It is the root of a common adjective, épouvantable, which means dreadful or appalling. Quel temps épouvantable ! What dreadful weather! In everyday language, the term un film d’épouvante is often replaced by the back translation un film d’horreur."},{"ref":"2","text":"La glace means ice, as in l’ère de glace, the ice age. In everyday language, however, une glace means an ice cream. French and English have both experienced a similar elision: the “proper” French term is une crème glacée, equivalent to an iced cream, the correct term for an ice cream."},{"ref":"3","text":"We know (lesson 48 note 10) that passer means to pass and the reflexive form se passer means to happen. To remember the difference, try memorising the sentence: Le film se passe à Paris, où je passe mes vacances, The film takes place in Paris, where I spend my holidays. This lesson features other examples of verbs that have a slightly different meaning in the non-pronominal and pronominal forms."},{"ref":"4","text":"un monstre sacré (“a holy monster”) is an admiring term similar to the English epithet a giant or a legendary figure: Gérard Grandieu est un monstre sacré du cinéma français, Gérard Grandieu is a giant of French cinema. The expression applies to both men and women. However, the noun un monstre is used pejoratively, as in English: C’est un monstre horrible qui fait peur aux enfants, He’s a dreadful monster who scares children."},{"ref":"5","text":"amuser means to amuse (someone): Il m’amuse avec ses histoires drôles, He amuses me with his funny stories. The pronominal form, seen in lesson 47 line 2, generally means to enjoy or to have fun. Il joue mal de la guitare mais il s’amuse, He plays the guitar badly but he enjoys himself."},{"ref":"6","text":"entendre means to hear (and we know the past participle entendu, lesson 38 note 8, meaning I see or I understand). And, as a simple reflexive, s’entendre means to hear oneself. Faites moins de bruit, je ne m’entends pas parler au téléphone, Make less noise, I can’t hear myself speaking on the phone. As an idiomatic pronominal verb, s’entendre means to reach an agreement with or, more commonly, to get on well with someone. Je m’entends très bien avec le frère de Jacques, I get on well with Jacques’ brother."},{"ref":"7","text":"See lesson 51 note 8."},{"ref":"8","text":"se tromper (lesson 50 line 5), to make a mistake, is the pronominal form of tromper, to deceive or mislead: Le vendeur m’a trompé sur le prix des appels téléphoniques, The salesman misled me about the price of phone calls. The transitive verb can also mean to be unfaithful to, cheat on: Si une femme trompe son mari, c’est souvent parce que le mari trompe sa femme : If a wife is unfaithful to her husband, it’s often because the husband is cheating on his wife."},{"ref":"9","text":"We have already seen un coup in several expressions (un coup d’œil, un coup de main, lesson 32, for example). Here we have the original meaning, a blow, a knock, etc.: J’ai pris un coup sur la tête, I banged my head. In these contexts, English is more flexible than French. For instance, donner un coup de poing / de pied (“to give a blow of the fist / foot”) is to punch / kick. The expression à coups de describes the thing delivering the blow: Elle le battait à coups de brosse, She was hitting him with a brush."},{"ref":"10","text":"un(e) gosse, a common informal word for a child, is the equivalent of a kid: Ils vont en vacances sans les gosses ce mois-ci, They’re going on holiday without the kids this month. Like monstre sacré (note 4), the noun is masculine but can apply to both sexes. Two common related idioms are un sale gosse (a brat) and un beau gosse, a good-looker. (Be careful: in Canadian French, gosse is a slang word for a part of the male anatomy!)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Tout le monde dit que la situation économique du pays est épouvantable.","a":"Everyone says that the country’s economic situation is dreadful."},{"q":"Je vous conseille son nouveau film : vous vous amuserez beaucoup.","a":"I recommend his/her new film: you’ll really enjoy yourselves."},{"q":"As-tu entendu la nouvelle ? Jeanne est morte. Son mari l’a tuée.","a":"Have you heard the news? Jeanne is dead. Her husband killed her."},{"q":"Ils passent toute une semaine seuls, sans les gosses, pour la première fois.","a":"They are spending a whole week alone, without the kids, for the first time."},{"q":"Nous avons battu tous les records de froid pour un mois de décembre.","a":"We have broken all the cold-temperature records for a month of December."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"What’s happening for the holidays this summer? – We’re spending them near Annecy.","t":"Qu’est-ce qui {{se passe}} pour les vacances {{cet été}} ? – Nous les {{passons près d}}’Annecy."},{"cue":"We’re fighting to help our team but I think that it will be beaten this time.","t":"{{Nous nous battons}} pour aider {{notre équipe}} mais je {{pense qu’elle sera}} battue cette {{fois-ci}}."},{"cue":"A hundred euros? You (masc.) made a mistake. – No, I believe it’s the salesman who cheated me (masc.) on the sale.","t":"Cent euros ? Tu {{t’es trompé}}. – Non, {{je crois}} que c’est le vendeur {{qui m’a trompé}} sur mon achat."},{"cue":"In principle they get on well together but I hear people say that it’s not true.","t":"En principe ils {{s’entendent bien}} mais {{j’entends des gens}} qui disent que {{ce n’est pas vrai}}."},{"cue":"The knife has fallen. It’s under the table. – I’ve found it.","t":"Le couteau {{est tombé}}. Il {{se trouve sous}} la table. – Je {{l’ai trouvé}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je te le conseille vraiment","en":"I really recommend it to you","zh":"我真心推荐给你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"conseiller ≈ counsel (advise)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai vu « La Mort atroce » hier. Je te le conseille vraiment.","exEn":"I saw “Dreadful Death” yesterday. I really recommend it.","src":1},{"fr":"un film d’épouvante","en":"a horror film","zh":"恐怖片","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"épouvantable ≈ dreadful","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il a battu les records d’audience pour un film d’épouvante.","exEn":"It has broken viewing records for a horror movie.","src":2},{"fr":"à côté de chez moi","en":"next to my place","zh":"我家旁边","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, à côté de chez moi il y a un complexe multisalles qui vient d’ouvrir.","exEn":"No, next to my place there’s a multiplex that has just opened.","src":4},{"fr":"de très bonnes glaces","en":"very good ice creams","zh":"非常好吃的冰淇淋","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"une glace = ice cream (also ice, mirror)","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"La programmation est variée, les salles sont spacieuses, et on y vend de très bonnes glaces !","exEn":"The programming is varied, the theatres are spacious, and they sell very good ice cream!","src":5},{"fr":"De quoi parle le film ?","en":"What is the film about?","zh":"这部电影讲的是什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"De quoi parle le film ?","exEn":"What is the movie about?","src":6},{"fr":"l’action se passe à Annecy","en":"the action takes place in Annecy","zh":"故事发生在安纳西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"action = same as English","ff":"se passer = to happen; passer = to spend (time)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’action se passe à Annecy, où j’ai passé toutes mes vacances quand j’étais petite.","exEn":"The action takes place in Annecy, where I spent all my holidays when I was little.","src":7},{"fr":"un monstre sacré","en":"a giant, a legendary figure","zh":"（某领域的）泰斗，传奇人物","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"monstre ≈ monster","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’une des vedettes est Jeanne Morteau, un monstre sacré du cinéma.","exEn":"One of the stars is Jeanne Morteau, a giant of the movies.","src":8},{"fr":"s’entend à merveille","en":"gets on marvellously","zh":"相处得极好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"merveille ≈ marvel","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est l’histoire d’un vieux couple qui, en apparence, s’entend à merveille.","exEn":"It’s the story of an old couple who, apparently, get on marvellously.","src":10},{"fr":"les apparences peuvent tromper","en":"appearances can be deceptive","zh":"外表可能骗人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"apparence ≈ appearance","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “trom-pay” – a trumpet player tricks you with a fake tune","ex":"Ils pensent que ce bâtiment est abandonné, mais les apparences peuvent tromper…","exEn":"They think that this building is abandoned, but appearances can be deceptive…","src":12},{"fr":"elle entend des bruits étranges","en":"she hears strange noises","zh":"她听到奇怪的声音","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"étrange ≈ strange","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un jour, la femme est seule à la maison lorsqu’elle entend des bruits étranges dehors.","exEn":"One day, the woman is alone in the house when she hears strange noises outside.","src":13},{"fr":"elle se trompe","en":"she is mistaken","zh":"她搞错了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"se tromper = to make a mistake","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais elle se trompe ! Soudain elle voit son mari avec une énorme hache à la main.","exEn":"But she is mistaken! Suddenly she sees her husband with a huge axe in his hand.","src":16},{"fr":"elle se bat avec lui","en":"she fights with him","zh":"她和他打了起来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Elle se bat avec lui mais il est beaucoup plus fort et il la bat à coups de bâton.","exEn":"She fights with him but he is much stronger and he hits her with a stick.","src":17},{"fr":"je me suis cachée sous la table","en":"I hid under the table","zh":"我躲在桌子底下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “cash-ay” – you hid the cash under the table","ex":"Je ne sais pas parce que je me suis cachée sous la table jusqu’à la fin.","exEn":"I don’t know because I hid under the table until the end.","src":19},{"fr":"je me suis amusée comme une gosse","en":"I enjoyed myself like a kid","zh":"我玩得像个小孩一样开心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"gosse = kid (informal)","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “gauss” – a kid called Goss giggling at the film","ex":"Néanmoins, je me suis amusée comme une gosse et j’ai adoré le film.","exEn":"Even so, I enjoyed myself like a kid and I loved the movie.","src":20},{"fr":"du moins","en":"at least","zh":"至少","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Du moins, la première moitié…","exEn":"At least, the first half…","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 7: scan says “See lesson 57 note 8”; changed to lesson 51 note 8 (se trouver)","note 3: scan says “indicative and pronominal forms”; reworded to non-pronominal and pronominal"],"pdfPage":303,"gaps":[[1.96,3.7],[5.22,8.38],[96.56,99.74],[102,103.84],[107.32,110.16],[113.38,116.3],[121.52,124.34],[128.14,130.94],[133.94,137.52]],"dur":137.5},{"n":56,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment va Hélène ? Est-ce qu’elle est toujours en Allemagne ?","en":"How’s Hélène? Is she still in Germany?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, elle est rentrée il y a six semaines et elle travaille maintenant à Grenoble.","en":"No, she came back six weeks ago and she’s now working in Grenoble.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça se passe bien pour elle ? Elle s’amuse, j’espère ?","en":"Are things going well for her? She’s enjoying herself, I hope?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, elle s’ennuie parce qu’il n’y a pas assez à faire au boulot.","en":"No, she’s bored because there isn’t enough to do at work.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Hier, elle s’est connectée à Internet pour faire ses courses en ligne !","en":"Yesterday, she went online to do her shopping!","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas comme moi, alors ! Je n’ai pas le temps de m’ennuyer.","en":"Not like me, then! I don’t have time to get bored.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je me suis levée à cinq heures ce matin pour aller au bureau.","en":"I got up at five o’clock this morning to go to the office.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne me suis même pas douchée parce que je n’avais pas assez de temps.","en":"I didn’t even have a shower because I didn’t have enough time.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai pris la voiture pour arriver plus vite mais, en arrivant,","en":"I took the car to get there sooner but, when I arrived,","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"je me suis arrêtée devant l’immeuble et j’ai vu que la porte était fermée à clé.","en":"I stopped in front of the building and saw the door was locked.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Je n’ai même pas arrêté le moteur.","en":"I didn’t even turn off the engine.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’as-tu fait ?","en":"What did you do?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis repartie très en colère.","en":"I left, very angry.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Pronominal verbs","text":"Pronominal verbs are divided into two classes: reflexive, where the subject and object of an action are the same, and reciprocal, used when two or more subjects do the same thing to each other. Both classes are preceded by se (s’ before an initial vowel or h) in the infinitive and are conjugated in the same way, with a subject pronoun and a reflexive pronoun:\nse laver, to wash\nje me lave – I wash | nous nous lavons – we wash\ntu te laves – you wash | vous vous lavez – you wash\nil/elle se lave – he/she/it washes | ils/elles se lavent – they wash\n(Note that the reflexive pronoun for the third person singular is the same as the infinitive marker (se, s’), but there is little chance of confusing the two.)\n\nIn the perfect tense, pronominal verbs are conjugated with être and the past participle, which generally agrees with the subject.\nje me suis lavé/-ée | nous nous sommes lavés/-ées\ntu t’es lavé/-ée | vous vous êtes lavé / lavés/-ées\nil s’est lavé / elle s’est lavée | ils se sont lavés / elles se sont lavées\n(In the vous form, the past participle depends among other things on whether the subject is singular or plural.)\n\nTo form the negative, place ne before the reflexive pronoun and pas (or jamais, etc.) after the verb: Il ne se lave pas, He doesn’t wash.\n\nThe first interrogative (rising intonation) is formed with the subject pronoun followed by the reflexive pronoun: Tu te laves ?, Are you washing? For the second interrogative, place est-ce que before the subject pronoun: Est-ce que tu te laves ? The third interrogative (inversion, Te laves-tu ?) is slightly more complex – and less common – so we will look at it in greater detail later in the course.\n\nFor the imperative, the position of the pronoun depends on whether the command is affirmative or negative. In both cases, the subject pronoun is dropped. With a negative imperative, beginning with Ne…, the reflexive pronoun comes before the verb, as usual: Ne te lave pas, Don’t wash. In the affirmative, however, the reflexive pronoun is replaced by a stressed pronoun, placed after the verb and joined with a hyphen: Lave-toi !\n\n• Reflexive verbs: These are much more common in French than in English. In particular, those indicating a change of state (se réveiller, to wake up, s’endormir, to go to sleep) or describing an everyday action or movement (se laver, to wash, s’habiller, to get dressed, se promener, to go for a walk) are not reflexive in English. In this case, the reflexive pronoun is not translated (Ils s’habillent, They’re getting dressed).\nSome of these verbs are “exclusively pronominal,” so that without the reflexive pronoun, they have no meaning. Among the most frequently used are s’absenter (to leave, go away from), s’enfuir (to run away, to flee), s’envoler (to fly away), se fier (to rely on), and se méfier (to be wary of). In the past tense, the past participle always agrees in number and gender with the subject: Anne s’est absentée la semaine dernière, Anne went away last week; Ils se sont absentés, etc.\nBy contrast, some verbs are “occasionally pronominal” and can be used transitively or reflexively depending on the direct object. For example: Je lave ma voiture, I’m washing my car; Je me lave le visage, I’m washing my face. When conjugated in the past, the participle agrees in exactly the same way as it does for avoir, i.e. with the direct object if it comes before the verb. For example, elle s’est lavée (elle is both the subject and the object of the verb). We will look at this rule in greater detail in another revision lesson once you are used to using reflexive verbs.\n\n• Reciprocal verbs are used when the subject or subjects are acting on each other. For example: se comprendre: Nous ne nous comprenons pas, We don’t understand each other. Other common reciprocals include se détester (Ils se détestent, They hate each other), se regarder (Nous nous regardons, We are looking at each other) and se sourire (Elles se sourient, They’re smiling at each other). Logically, many verbs can be used reciprocally and are translated by adding each other / one another (French makes no difference between these two English notions).\n\n• Some pronominal verbs have a slightly different meaning when used non-reciprocally, as we saw in lesson 55. These include passer (to pass, to spend time) and se passer (to happen); entendre (to hear) and s’entendre (to get on well with); tromper (to deceive) and se tromper (to make a mistake)."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Future tense","text":"The future tense is used in very much the same way as its English equivalent (but remember that French makes no difference between the simple and the progressive). To form the future of regular verbs, the endings of avoir are added to the stem, which, for the first two verb groups, is usually the infinitive:\najouter, to add\nj’ajouterai – I will add | nous ajouterons – we will add\ntu ajouteras – you will add | vous ajouterez – you will add\nil/elle ajoutera – he/she/it will add | ils/elles ajouteront – they will add\nfinir, to finish\nje finirai – I will finish | nous finirons – we will finish\ntu finiras – you will finish | vous finirez – you will finish\nil/elle finira – he/she/it will finish | ils/elles finiront – they will finish\n\nVerbs ending in -re use the same endings but drop the final -e from the infinitive:\nvendre, to sell\nje vendrai – I will sell | nous vendrons – we will sell\ntu vendras – you will sell | vous vendrez – you will sell\nil/elle vendra – he/she/it will sell | ils/elles vendront – they will sell\n\nThere are a few spelling changes when the future endings are added to certain -er verbs, as we saw in lesson 53 with acheter and essayer. The same changes occur with lever, to lift (the initial e takes a grave accent) and payer, to pay (the y changes to i): j’achèterai, je lèverai; j’essaierai, je paierai.\nAnother change concerns verbs such as jeter and appeler, which double the first letter of the last syllable: je jetterai, j’appellerai. We will see later how these changes affect the pronunciation slightly.\n\nSome important irregular verbs, particularly the two auxiliaries avoir and être, change significantly in the future. Here they are, for reference purposes:\nj’aurai | je serai\ntu auras | tu seras\nil/elle aura | il/elle sera\nnous aurons | nous serons\nvous aurez | vous serez\nils/elles auront | ils/elles seront\nTwo other important irregular verbs are faire and aller (see lesson 53 notes 1 and 9).\n\nA major structural difference between French and English concerns subordinate clauses linked to a future-tense main clause by a time conjunction, particularly quand, when. In French, the verbs in both clauses are in the future: Quand je saurai ce qu’il veut, je te le dirai, When I know what he wants, I’ll tell you. The same rule applies to the future perfect, which we see at the end of the course."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Possessive pronouns","text":"Possessive pronouns – the equivalent of yours, mine, etc. – are formed with a definite article and a pronominal word.\nle mien, la mienne, les miens, les miennes – mine\nle nôtre, la nôtre, les nôtres – ours\nle tien, la tienne, les tiens, les tiennes – yours\nle vôtre, la vôtre, les vôtres – yours\nle sien, la sienne, les siens, les siennes – his / hers / its\nle leur, la leur, les leurs – theirs\n\nLike possessive adjectives, these pronouns agree in number and gender with the noun(s) they replace, not with the possessor. For example:\nC’est mon stylo, It’s my pen ➔ C’est le mien, It’s mine;\nC’est ma voiture, It’s my car ➔ C’est la mienne, It’s mine.\nBecause of the agreement, it is not possible to identify the gender of the possessor in the third person singular (le sien, la sienne). C’est son manteau ➔ C’est le sien can mean his or hers (and, at a stretch, its if the coat in question is worn by a dog or cat!). Note that the circumflex over the o on nôtre and vôtre makes it possible to distinguish between the pronoun and the possessive adjective (votre, notre):\nCeci est votre clé ➔ C’est la vôtre.\nIn terms of pronunciation, the o of the pronoun is the longer of the two ([vohtr / votr, nohtr / notr])."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Prepositions: en, dans and à","text":"While most French prepositions are easy to master, these three are often confused (in addition, en is also an adverbial pronoun, like y). Let’s revise the different prepositional uses we have seen since you started this course.\n\n4.1 Dans and en\n• One basic difference is that en is never followed by a definite or partitive article (except in a number of fixed expressions, such as en l’air, in the air). It is used, among others, to talk about languages (en français, en anglais), modes of transport (en avion, en train, etc.), materials (en papier, en or), periods and dates (en 1789, en avril), and seasons with the exception of spring: (en été, en automne, en hiver but au printemps). En can also be used with places, Il est en prison, and countries that are feminine nouns: Je vis en France (masculine countries take au, as seen earlier in the course). Last, it is used before a present participle: en arrivant, on arriving. (In this case, the English translation does not necessarily require a participle: En arrivant je leur ai dit bonjour, When I arrived I said hello to them.)\n• In contrast to en, dans has to be followed by an article, whether definite (le/la/les), indefinite (un/une) or partitive (du, de la, des). Dans designates the location of something (dans ma poche) and, in this context, is more precise than en. For example, you would say Mes vacances en montagne, My holidays in the mountains but Mes vacances dans les montagnes du Jura, My holidays in the Jura mountains. When followed by an article and a noun, dans means in: Elle est dans son bureau, She is in her office.\nDans is also used for a specific measure of time (dans dix minutes), but remember (lesson 51 note 5) that en can also be used in this context, but with a slight difference in meaning: Je peux faire le travail en vingt minutes, I can do the job in 20 minutes (i.e. it will take me 20 minutes) compared with Je peux faire le travail dans vingt minutes, I can do the job in 20 minutes’ time (i.e. in the future).\n\n4.2 Dans and à\nHere, too, the differences are subtle. The one that seems to most confuse English-speakers is the use of à to translate in when the preposition does not mean inside. A typical usage is à la maison, at home. Où est Rosie ? – Elle est à la maison, Where’s Rosie? – She’s at home. By contrast, J’entends quelqu’un dans la maison, I can hear someone inside the house. Likewise, J’ai vu le film au cinéma, I saw the movie at the cinema, versus J’ai perdu ma montre dans le cinéma, I lost my watch in (i.e. inside) the cinema.\nOne big difference with English, and a source of many mistakes, concerns parts of the body: Il a un couteau à la main, He has a knife in (not inside) his hand, compared with Tiens-le dans ta main, Hold it in your hand (i.e. close your fingers around it).\nÀ is also used to talk about cities or towns: Elle habite à Paris, She lives in Paris. (Remember that à becomes au / aux with le / les: Elle habite au quatrième étage, She lives on the fourth floor.) Last, à is used with certain communications media: à la télévision, à la radio, au téléphone.\nThere are other subtleties, which you will assimilate as you move forward. And if you do mistake en for dans, for example, you will almost certainly be understood – and, with any luck, corrected – by the person you are talking to."},{"ref":"","text":"From now on, we will no longer systematically give the translations of words – notably the main verbs such as être, avoir and faire – in our examples. By now, you should be thoroughly familiar with them."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"elle est rentrée il y a six semaines","en":"she came back six weeks ago","zh":"她六周前回来了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Non, elle est rentrée il y a six semaines et elle travaille maintenant à Grenoble.","exEn":"No, she came back six weeks ago and she’s now working in Grenoble.","src":2},{"fr":"Ça se passe bien pour elle ?","en":"Are things going well for her?","zh":"她那边一切顺利吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça se passe bien pour elle ? Elle s’amuse, j’espère ?","exEn":"Are things going well for her? She’s enjoying herself, I hope?","src":3},{"fr":"elle s’ennuie","en":"she is bored","zh":"她觉得无聊","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"ennui ≈ ennui (boredom)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, elle s’ennuie parce qu’il n’y a pas assez à faire au boulot.","exEn":"No, she’s bored because there isn’t enough to do at work.","src":4},{"fr":"il n’y a pas assez à faire au boulot","en":"there isn’t enough to do at work","zh":"工作里事情不够做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “boo-lo” – the boss goes “boo!” at slackers on the job","ex":"Non, elle s’ennuie parce qu’il n’y a pas assez à faire au boulot.","exEn":"No, she’s bored because there isn’t enough to do at work.","src":4},{"fr":"elle s’est connectée à Internet","en":"she went online","zh":"她上了网","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"connecter ≈ connect","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Hier, elle s’est connectée à Internet pour faire ses courses en ligne !","exEn":"Yesterday, she went online to do her shopping!","src":5},{"fr":"je me suis levée à cinq heures","en":"I got up at five o’clock","zh":"我五点起的床","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me suis levée à cinq heures ce matin pour aller au bureau.","exEn":"I got up at five o’clock this morning to go to the office.","src":7},{"fr":"je ne me suis même pas douchée","en":"I didn’t even have a shower","zh":"我连澡都没洗","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"douche ≈ douche (shower)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne me suis même pas douchée parce que je n’avais pas assez de temps.","exEn":"I didn’t even have a shower because I didn’t have enough time.","src":8},{"fr":"la porte était fermée à clé","en":"the door was locked","zh":"门被锁上了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"je me suis arrêtée devant l’immeuble et j’ai vu que la porte était fermée à clé.","exEn":"I stopped in front of the building and saw the door was locked.","src":10},{"fr":"je suis repartie très en colère","en":"I left again, very angry","zh":"我很生气地又走了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"colère ≈ choler (anger)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis repartie très en colère.","exEn":"I left, very angry.","src":13}],"uncertain":["note 4.1: the scan cites “lesson 57” for masculine countries taking au; replaced with a general reference","note 4.2: scan says “the pronoun does not mean inside”; read as preposition"],"pdfPage":309,"gaps":[[1.8,3.88],[5.06,8.18],[55.28,59.72]],"dur":59.7},{"n":57,"kind":"lesson","title":"Vive les Bretons !","titleEn":"[Long] live the Bretons!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment ça se fait que vous parlez tous les deux le breton ?","en":"How come you both speak Breton?","lit":"how that does itself; all the two"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"L’explication est simple. Nous avons vécu à Vannes pendant dix ans quand nous étions plus jeunes.","en":"The explanation is simple. We lived in Vannes for ten years when we were younger.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"J’enseignais au collège et mon mari travaillait dans une entreprise de construction navale.","en":"I was teaching in middle school and my husband was working in a ship-building company.","lit":"naval construction"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Beaucoup de gens autour de nous parlaient cette langue, ce qui nous a motivés à l’apprendre.","en":"Many people around us spoke the language, which motivated us to learn it.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce que c’était difficile à maîtriser ?","en":"Was it hard to master?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous avions beaucoup de chance parce que nous habitions un quartier de la ville","en":"We were very lucky because we were living in a part of the town","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"où les commerçants avaient presque tous le breton comme première langue,","en":"where almost all the shopkeepers spoke Breton as their first language,","lit":"had"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"donc nous étions obligés de le parler, rien que pour acheter du pain !","en":"so we were obliged to speak it, if only to buy bread!","lit":"nothing that for"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"À propos, saviez-vous que le verbe « baragouiner » était d’origine bretonne ?","en":"By the way, did you know that the verb baragouiner to talk gibberish was of Breton origin?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est vrai ? Je ne le savais pas. Vous m’apprenez quelque chose !","en":"Is that true? I didn’t know. That’s news to me!","lit":"you teach me something"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Autrefois, beaucoup de Bretons montaient à Paris pour trouver du travail.","en":"In the past, many Bretons came up to Paris to find work.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Le voyage était très long et ils arrivaient souvent affamés.","en":"The journey was very long and they were often famished when they arrived.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils entraient dans le premier café qu’ils trouvaient","en":"They went into the first café that they came across","lit":"found"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"et commandaient du pain et du vin. Mais ils ne parlaient que le breton,","en":"and ordered bread and wine. But they only spoke Breton,","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"donc ils demandaient du « bara » et du « guin » – et personne ne les comprenait.","en":"so they asked for “bara” and “guin” – and no one understood them.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"« Que disent ces Bretons ? On n’y comprend rien », disaient les Parisiens.","en":"“What are these Bretons saying? We can’t make head nor tail of it,” the Parisians used to say.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"« Ils baragouinent ! » Et voilà d’où vient ce mot.","en":"“They’re talking gibberish!” And that’s where the word comes from.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Elle est absolument fascinante, ton histoire. Je ne la connaissais pas.","en":"Your story is absolutely fascinating. I didn’t know it.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Pourtant j’allais souvent en Bretagne avec mes parents quand j’étais petit.","en":"And yet I often used to go to Brittany with my parents when I was little.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"J’aimais bien la région, mais il pleuvait tout le temps et on ne sortait presque jamais de la maison.","en":"I really liked the region but it rained all the time and we almost never left the house.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Il pleuvait sans doute, mais au moins en été la pluie bretonne est plus chaude.","en":"It was certainly raining, but at least the Breton rain is warmer in summer.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(Sentence 7) commerçant [komairssœhⁿ]: Note how the cedilla beneath the letter c changes the pronunciation from a hard [k] to a sibilant, or soft, [s]. The ç is used only before the vowels a (ça), o (garçon) and u (reçu) because c is automatically sibilant before e, i and y."},{"ref":"1","text":"Comment ça se fait ? (“How that does itself?”) is an idiomatic way of asking a question, a little like How come? Il n’est pas parti en vacances cette année. – Comment ça se fait ? He didn’t go on holiday this year. – How come? The expression can also be used in a full sentence: Comment ça se fait que le train est en retard ? How come the train is late? In this case, however, it is often necessary to put the main verb into the subjunctive, which we will learn later on."},{"ref":"2","text":"French does not have a word for both, relying on various constructions according to the grammatical function. As an adjective, you can use les / des / aux deux: J’ai mal aux deux genoux, Both my knees hurt. As a pronoun, use tous les deux: Ils sont bretons, tous les deux, They’re both Breton."},{"ref":"3","text":"Here is the imperfect tense, glimpsed in lesson 55 line 7. It is used to describe situations or actions in the past, without specifying when they began or ended. The imperfect form of the irregular verb être is j’étais, tu étais, il/elle/on était, nous étions, vous étiez, ils/elles étaient. The nearest equivalent in English is was / were."},{"ref":"4","text":"The imperfect tense of all verbs other than être (see note 3) is formed by dropping the -ons ending from the nous form of the present and adding the following endings: enseignons: j’enseignais, tu enseignais, il/elle enseignait, nous enseignions, vous enseigniez, ils/elles enseignaient. Depending on the context, the translation uses was teaching or used to teach. In this same sentence, travaillait is the third-person singular imperfect of travailler."},{"ref":"5","text":"un commerçant comes from the noun le commerce, trade. (Here is another example of Latin/Anglo-Saxon variations: trade concerns the exchange of goods and some services, whereas commerce encompasses a broader range of ancillary activities.) Depending on the context, un(e) commerçant(e) can be a trader but also a shopkeeper or merchant."},{"ref":"6","text":"This is the imperfect of savoir: savons ➔ je savais, tu savais, il/elle/on savait, nous savions, vous saviez, ils/elles savaient. (See also note 10.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"baragouiner is a familiar verb meaning to talk gibberish or, less insultingly, to speak a language badly, to mumble a few words: Il baragouinait le russe, He was speaking bad Russian."},{"ref":"8","text":"We know that apprendre means to learn and enseigner to teach. However, when used with a direct object and an indirect object in the same phrase, apprendre means to teach (something to someone). So, J’apprends le français, I’m learning French but Je leur apprends le français, I’m teaching them French. By contrast, enseigner means only to teach: Je leur enseigne le français. The idiomatic expression Vous m’apprenez / Tu m’apprends quelque chose is the equivalent of That’s news to me (or, more simply, I didn’t know that)."},{"ref":"9","text":"We know that adjectives of nationality do not take an initial capital, whereas nouns do (lesson 20 note 3). Logically, the lower-case rule applies to adjectives derived from regions, towns, etc., as well as to languages: C’est un Normand qui parle mal l’anglais, He’s a Norman who speaks bad English. Be careful not to confuse nouns and adjectives of nationality: Ce sont des Bretons but Ils sont bretons. (Alternatively, Ils sont d’origine bretonne, They’re of Breton origin.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"We know that the adverbial pronoun y refers back to a previously mentioned indirect object, usually a thing or place, generally introduced by à (see lesson 35 section 3.1.) Thus, in the idiomatic phrase on n’y comprend rien, y replaces that indirect object le film: À la fin du film, on n’y comprend rien (i.e. au film), By the end of the movie, nobody understands anything. If the indirect object comes after the verb, however, y is not used, and rien returns to its usual place, directly after the verb: On n’a rien compris au film."},{"ref":"11","text":"To remind yourself of how to use connaître (line 18) and savoir (line 10), look back at lesson 35 section 4."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Loïc ? C’est un Breton qui ne parle pas le breton. Il le baragouine un tout petit peu, c’est tout.","a":"Loïc? He’s a Breton who doesn’t speak Breton. He can mumble a few words, that’s all."},{"q":"Leur explication est beaucoup trop compliquée. On n’y comprend rien.","a":"Their explanation is much too complicated. It’s as clear as mud."},{"q":"Comment ça se fait que nous n’avons pas reçu la lettre qu’il a promise ?","a":"How come we haven’t received the letter that he promised?"},{"q":"Ta cousine et son amie étaient invitées à la fête, et elles sont venues toutes les deux.","a":"Your cousin and her friend were invited to the party, and both of them came."},{"q":"Votre histoire est fascinante. Nous ne la connaissions pas.","a":"Your story is fascinating. We didn’t know it."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"They were speaking English and nobody understood them. – That surprises me.","t":"{{Ils parlaient}} en {{anglais}} et {{personne ne les comprenait}} ! – Ça {{m’étonne}}."},{"cue":"Mona was very lucky because she was living in a really nice area.","t":"Mona {{avait beaucoup de chance}} parce qu’{{elle habitait}} dans un quartier très sympathique."},{"cue":"By the way, did you know that the word “to talk gibberish” was of Breton origin? – No, I did not know that.","t":"{{À propos}}, {{savais-tu}} que le mot « {{baragouiner}} » {{était}} d’origine {{bretonne}} ? – Non, {{je ne le savais pas}}."},{"cue":"People around me were speaking Russian all day, which motivated me to learn that language.","t":"Des gens {{autour de moi parlaient}} le russe {{toute la journée}}, {{ce qui m’a motivé}} à {{apprendre}} cette langue."},{"cue":"We both were teaching in Vannes, which we liked a lot even though it rained all the time.","t":"Nous {{enseignions tous les deux}} à Vannes, que nous {{aimions}} beaucoup même s’il {{pleuvait tout le temps}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Comment ça se fait que","en":"How come…?","zh":"怎么会……？为什么……？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment ça se fait que vous parlez tous les deux le breton ?","exEn":"How come you both speak Breton?","src":1},{"fr":"nous avons vécu à Vannes pendant dix ans","en":"we lived in Vannes for ten years","zh":"我们在瓦讷住了十年","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’explication est simple. Nous avons vécu à Vannes pendant dix ans quand nous étions plus jeunes.","exEn":"The explanation is simple. We lived in Vannes for ten years when we were younger.","src":2},{"fr":"quand nous étions plus jeunes","en":"when we were younger","zh":"我们年轻的时候","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’explication est simple. Nous avons vécu à Vannes pendant dix ans quand nous étions plus jeunes.","exEn":"The explanation is simple. We lived in Vannes for ten years when we were younger.","src":2},{"fr":"j’enseignais au collège","en":"I was teaching in a middle school","zh":"我当时在初中教书","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"collège = middle school (ages 11–15), not “college”","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"J’enseignais au collège et mon mari travaillait dans une entreprise de construction navale.","exEn":"I was teaching in middle school and my husband was working in a ship-building company.","src":3},{"fr":"les commerçants","en":"the shopkeepers","zh":"店主，商人","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ commerce","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"où les commerçants avaient presque tous le breton comme première langue,","exEn":"where almost all the shopkeepers spoke Breton as their first language,","src":7},{"fr":"nous étions obligés de le parler","en":"we were obliged to speak it","zh":"我们不得不说它","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"obligé ≈ obliged","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"donc nous étions obligés de le parler, rien que pour acheter du pain !","exEn":"so we were obliged to speak it, if only to buy bread!","src":8},{"fr":"rien que pour acheter du pain","en":"if only to buy bread","zh":"哪怕只是为了买面包","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"donc nous étions obligés de le parler, rien que pour acheter du pain !","exEn":"so we were obliged to speak it, if only to buy bread!","src":8},{"fr":"À propos","en":"By the way","zh":"顺便说一下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ apropos","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À propos, saviez-vous que le verbe « baragouiner » était d’origine bretonne ?","exEn":"By the way, did you know that the verb baragouiner to talk gibberish was of Breton origin?","src":9},{"fr":"Je ne le savais pas.","en":"I didn’t know that.","zh":"我之前不知道。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est vrai ? Je ne le savais pas. Vous m’apprenez quelque chose !","exEn":"Is that true? I didn’t know. That’s news to me!","src":10},{"fr":"Vous m’apprenez quelque chose !","en":"That’s news to me!","zh":"我今天长知识了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est vrai ? Je ne le savais pas. Vous m’apprenez quelque chose !","exEn":"Is that true? I didn’t know. That’s news to me!","src":10},{"fr":"autrefois","en":"in the past, formerly","zh":"从前，过去","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"sounds like “oh-truh-fwah” – “oh, truffles!” – the old days when truffles were cheap","ex":"Autrefois, beaucoup de Bretons montaient à Paris pour trouver du travail.","exEn":"In the past, many Bretons came up to Paris to find work.","src":11},{"fr":"ils arrivaient souvent affamés","en":"they often arrived famished","zh":"他们到达时常常饿坏了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “a-fa-may” – a man so hungry he eats “a farm, hey!”","ex":"Le voyage était très long et ils arrivaient souvent affamés.","exEn":"The journey was very long and they were often famished when they arrived.","src":12},{"fr":"On n’y comprend rien.","en":"We can’t make head nor tail of it.","zh":"完全听不懂。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Que disent ces Bretons ? On n’y comprend rien », disaient les Parisiens.","exEn":"“What are these Bretons saying? We can’t make head nor tail of it,” the Parisians used to say.","src":16},{"fr":"Je ne la connaissais pas.","en":"I didn’t know it/her.","zh":"我之前不认识（不了解）它。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"connaître = to know a person/place; savoir = to know a fact","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Elle est absolument fascinante, ton histoire. Je ne la connaissais pas.","exEn":"Your story is absolutely fascinating. I didn’t know it.","src":18},{"fr":"il pleuvait tout le temps","en":"it rained all the time","zh":"一直在下雨","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"J’aimais bien la région, mais il pleuvait tout le temps et on ne sortait presque jamais de la maison.","exEn":"I really liked the region but it rained all the time and we almost never left the house.","src":20}],"uncertain":["sentence 4: scan reads nous a motivé; written motivés (agreement with the preceding object nous)","note 10: the English “that indirect object le film” kept as in the book; the book’s exact wording is slightly unclear"],"pdfPage":317,"gaps":[[1.8,3.62],[4.56,7.62],[98.86,99.86],[105.22,108.78],[110.72,112.42],[118.14,121.34],[124.48,127.44],[130.6,133.6],[138.5,141.48],[144.74,148.28]],"dur":148.3},{"n":58,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un grincheux","titleEn":"A grumpy person","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Allons à Auxerre ce week-end, puisque mardi est férié.","en":"Let’s go to Auxerre this weekend, since Tuesday is a public holiday.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous pourrons faire le pont du premier novembre et passer quatre jours complets.","en":"We can take the long weekend of 1st November and spend four full days.","lit":"make the bridge"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça sera chouette, non ? Un peu de ciel bleu, ça nous changera les idées.","en":"It will be great, won’t it? A bit of blue sky will give us something different to think about.","lit":"change us the ideas"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu n’as pas l’air enthousiaste. Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ?","en":"You don’t seem very keen. What’s up?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"D’abord, qu’est-ce qu’il y a à voir à Auxerre ? C’est une ville comme une autre.","en":"First, what is there to see in Auxerre? It’s a town like any other.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais il y a plein de choses à visiter : regarde dans le guide de voyage :","en":"But there are loads of things to visit: look in this travel guide:","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"« La vieille ville, la Tour de l’Horloge, l’abbaye, les beaux hôtels particuliers,","en":"“The old town, the Clock Tower, the abbey, handsome private mansions,","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"et des maisons à pans de bois sont parmi les sites incontournables à apprécier ».","en":"and half-timbered houses are among the key things to enjoy.”","lit":"sections of wood; unavoidable"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça vaut la peine, je t’assure. Tu ne le regretteras pas.","en":"It’s worth it, I assure you. You won’t regret it.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"À vrai dire, j’ai la flemme de quitter la maison, surtout pour un long week-end.","en":"To tell the truth, I can’t be bothered to leave the house, especially for a long weekend.","lit":"to true say"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"D’abord, il faudra partir au petit matin car la plupart des Parisiens seront sur la route, et il y aura un monde fou.","en":"First, we’ll have to leave in the early hours because most Parisians will be on the road and there’ll be loads of people.","lit":"little morning; a mad world"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"La dernière fois que je suis allé en Bourgogne, j’ai mis une demi-journée à faire cinquante kilomètres !","en":"The last time that I went to Burgundy, I spent half a day doing fifty kilometres!","lit":"put"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis resté coincé dans les embouteillages pendant quatre heures et demie, et ce n’était pourtant pas un jour férié.","en":"I was stuck in traffic jams for four and a half hours and yet it wasn’t a public holiday.","lit":"stayed"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Pire, il faisait un froid de canard et il tombait des cordes.","en":"Worse, it was freezing and it was pouring with rain.","lit":"a cold of duck; it fell ropes"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Selon la météo, il fera beau, chaud et ensoleillé toute la semaine.","en":"According to the weather forecast, it will be fine, hot and sunny all week.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-être bien, mais les hôtels seront complets ou ruineux,","en":"Maybe, but the hotels will be either full or ruinously expensive,","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"et je n’ai pas les moyens de jeter l’argent par les fenêtres.","en":"and I can’t afford to throw money away.","lit":"by the windows"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"J’abandonne ! Tu es têtu comme une mule.","en":"I give up! You are as stubborn as a mule.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(Sentence 4) enthousiaste: remember that the phoneme th is always pronounced as a t: [œhⁿtooziast].\n(Sentence 5) a à: the two letters are pronounced identically and each must be sounded: don’t try to elide the second one."},{"ref":"1","text":"Although une chouette means an owl, the word is often used as a familiar adjective meaning great, cool, nice, etc. Il est vraiment chouette, ton nouveau sac, Your new handbag is really nice. (And, just like nice, the meaning is vague!) Chouette can also be used as an exclamation, sometimes followed by alors: On ira à Auxerre demain. – Chouette alors ! We’ll go to Auxerre tomorrow. – Great!"},{"ref":"2","text":"The idiom Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ? means What’s wrong? or What’s up? Unlike Qu’est-ce qui ne va pas ? (lesson 24 line 1), where something is visibly wrong, Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ? is often a more general way of making an enquiry: Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ? – Je veux vous parler, What is it? – I want to talk to you."},{"ref":"3","text":"Here are two false friends. The adjective particulier, particular, distinctive, can also mean private when used in the compound noun un hôtel particulier, a private mansion. (In this context, un hôtel takes no guests!) The noun un particulier means a private individual, as opposed to a company. Website contact pages often ask the question: Êtes-vous un particulier ou une entreprise ? Are you an individual or a business?"},{"ref":"4","text":"Here is another use of the third person of valoir (lesson 44 line 18): the impersonal expression Ça vaut… followed by a noun means It’s worth… Ça vaut la peine, It’s worth the trouble (see lesson 46 note 8). Some tourist guides rate important monuments, sites and even restaurants simply as Vaut le voyage, Worth the trip."},{"ref":"5","text":"The feminine noun la flemme is derived from the medical term le flegme, phlegm and conveys the idea of laziness. It is used almost exclusively in the expression avoir la flemme: J’ai la flemme de faire le lit, I can’t be bothered to make the bed."},{"ref":"6","text":"We know that le monde, the world, is a synonym for people (tout le monde, beaucoup de monde). The idiomatic expression Il y a un monde fou (“a mad world”) means It’s really crowded / There are loads of people, etc. Il y a un monde fou dans les magasins à Noël, The shops are really crowded at Christmas."},{"ref":"7","text":"The adverb pourtant, seen in the previous lesson, is a useful way of expressing a contradiction, like yet or however. It can be used directly after a verb: Il reste pourtant beaucoup de choses à faire, But there’s still a lot to do, or at the beginning of a sentence, usually with Et, as a rejoinder. Tu es en retard. – Et pourtant, je me suis levé tôt, You’re late. – And yet I got up early. Note where the adverb is positioned in comparison to English."},{"ref":"8","text":"un canard means a duck: Suzanne vend des poulets et des canards au marché depuis dix ans, Suzanne has been selling chickens and ducks at the market for ten years. Since ducks are apparently easier to hunt in the winter, they have become synonymous with bitter cold: Il fait un froid de canard dehors, It’s freezing outside."},{"ref":"9","text":"une corde means a rope or, on a musical instrument, a string. Since streams of heavy rain can resemble thick ropes, the expression tomber (or pleuvoir) des cordes means to pour with rain. (The English noun a cord is generally translated by un cordon.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"un bulletin météorologique is the official term for a weather forecast. For simplicity, it is usually abbreviated to la météo, another example of the tendency to shorten longer phrases in the everyday language (lesson 17 note 7). As-tu entendu la météo pour aujourd’hui ? Have you heard today’s weather forecast? And la météo can also mean simply the weather. Si la météo est mauvaise, nous ne sortirons pas le bateau, If the weather is bad, we won’t get the boat out."},{"ref":"","text":"Although France is a secular country (un pays laïc), it has a strong Catholic tradition, and many public holidays – in addition to Noël (Christmas) and Pâques (Easter) – are religious in origin. Chief among them are le quinze août or l’Assomption, the Assumption on 15 August, and le premier novembre, or la Toussaint, All Saints’ Day on 1 November. These, and other holidays, are known collectively as les jours fériés (from a Latin word for leisure). Similarly, une fête, generally translated as a party, is also a feast day or saint’s day. The tradition of wishing a person Bonne fête, or “good name-day” on the feast day of the saint after whom they are named is rapidly disappearing as France becomes increasingly multicultural. (For the expression faire le pont, see the Culture Note in lesson 18.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Il pleut des cordes ! Pourtant, la météo était bonne hier.","a":"It’s pouring! And yet the weather was fine yesterday."},{"q":"Il y aura un monde fou sur les routes ce week-end : la plupart des Parisiens sont en vacances.","a":"The roads will be crowded this weekend: most Parisians are on holiday."},{"q":"Avez-vous visité la vieille ville ? Selon moi, ça ne vaut pas la peine.","a":"Have you visited the old town? In my view, it isn’t worth it."},{"q":"J’ai la flemme de sortir : il fait un froid de canard.","a":"I can’t be bothered to go out. It’s freezing."},{"q":"Le voyage est ruineux. Ce n’est pas la peine de jeter l’argent par les fenêtres.","a":"The trip is ruinously expensive. There’s no point throwing money away."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We were stuck in traffic jams for half an hour, until eight thirty.","t":"Nous {{sommes restés coincés}} dans {{les embouteillages}} pendant {{une demi-heure}}, jusqu’à {{huit heures et demie}}."},{"cue":"We’ll have to leave in the early hours because there will be a lot of people on the roads. But it’s worth it.","t":"{{Il faudra}} partir au petit matin car {{il y aura beaucoup de monde}} sur les routes. {{Mais ça vaut la peine}}."},{"cue":"What’s up? – It’s too expensive. I don’t want to throw money away.","t":"{{Qu’est-ce qu’il y a}} ? – C’est trop {{cher}}. Je ne veux pas {{jeter l’argent par les fenêtres}}."},{"cue":"We’re going to Burgundy this weekend. – Great! That will give me something else to think about.","t":"Nous allons {{en Bourgogne}} ce week-end. – {{Chouette}} ! Ça {{me changera les idées}}."},{"cue":"I assure you (formal) that there’s nothing to visit. – You’re as stubborn as a mule.","t":"Je {{vous assure}} qu’{{il n’y a rien à visiter}}. – Vous {{êtes têtu comme une mule}} !"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"mardi est férié","en":"Tuesday is a public holiday","zh":"星期二是公共假日","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “fay-ree” – a fairy has the day off on public holidays","ex":"Allons à Auxerre ce week-end, puisque mardi est férié.","exEn":"Let’s go to Auxerre this weekend, since Tuesday is a public holiday.","src":1},{"fr":"faire le pont","en":"to take a long weekend (bridge day)","zh":"“搭桥”连休（在两个假日之间多请一天假）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous pourrons faire le pont du premier novembre et passer quatre jours complets.","exEn":"We can take the long weekend of 1st November and spend four full days.","src":2},{"fr":"Ça sera chouette","en":"It will be great","zh":"那会很棒","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"une chouette = an owl; chouette = nice (informal)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça sera chouette, non ? Un peu de ciel bleu, ça nous changera les idées.","exEn":"It will be great, won’t it? A bit of blue sky will give us something different to think about.","src":3},{"fr":"ça nous changera les idées","en":"it will take our minds off things","zh":"这会让我们换换心情","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça sera chouette, non ? Un peu de ciel bleu, ça nous changera les idées.","exEn":"It will be great, won’t it? A bit of blue sky will give us something different to think about.","src":3},{"fr":"Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ?","en":"What’s up? What’s wrong?","zh":"怎么了？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu n’as pas l’air enthousiaste. Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ?","exEn":"You don’t seem very keen. What’s up?","src":4},{"fr":"tu n’as pas l’air enthousiaste","en":"you don’t seem enthusiastic","zh":"你看起来不太积极","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"enthousiaste ≈ enthusiastic","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu n’as pas l’air enthousiaste. Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ?","exEn":"You don’t seem very keen. What’s up?","src":4},{"fr":"Ça vaut la peine.","en":"It’s worth it.","zh":"值得。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça vaut la peine, je t’assure. Tu ne le regretteras pas.","exEn":"It’s worth it, I assure you. You won’t regret it.","src":9},{"fr":"À vrai dire","en":"To tell the truth","zh":"说实话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À vrai dire, j’ai la flemme de quitter la maison, surtout pour un long week-end.","exEn":"To tell the truth, I can’t be bothered to leave the house, especially for a long weekend.","src":10},{"fr":"j’ai la flemme de quitter la maison","en":"I can’t be bothered to leave the house","zh":"我懒得出门","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “flem” – phlegm makes you too sluggish to move","ex":"À vrai dire, j’ai la flemme de quitter la maison, surtout pour un long week-end.","exEn":"To tell the truth, I can’t be bothered to leave the house, especially for a long weekend.","src":10},{"fr":"la plupart des Parisiens","en":"most Parisians","zh":"大多数巴黎人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord, il faudra partir au petit matin car la plupart des Parisiens seront sur la route, et il y aura un monde fou.","exEn":"First, we’ll have to leave in the early hours because most Parisians will be on the road and there’ll be loads of people.","src":11},{"fr":"il y aura un monde fou","en":"it will be really crowded","zh":"人会多得要命","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"literally “a mad world”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord, il faudra partir au petit matin car la plupart des Parisiens seront sur la route, et il y aura un monde fou.","exEn":"First, we’ll have to leave in the early hours because most Parisians will be on the road and there’ll be loads of people.","src":11},{"fr":"coincé dans les embouteillages","en":"stuck in traffic jams","zh":"堵在车流里","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"sounds like “bouteille” – a bottle neck jams the road","ex":"Je suis resté coincé dans les embouteillages pendant quatre heures et demie, et ce n’était pourtant pas un jour férié.","exEn":"I was stuck in traffic jams for four and a half hours and yet it wasn’t a public holiday.","src":13},{"fr":"il faisait un froid de canard","en":"it was freezing cold","zh":"冷得要命","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"literally “a duck’s cold”","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Pire, il faisait un froid de canard et il tombait des cordes.","exEn":"Worse, it was freezing and it was pouring with rain.","src":14},{"fr":"il tombait des cordes","en":"it was pouring with rain","zh":"下着倾盆大雨","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"literally “ropes were falling”","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Pire, il faisait un froid de canard et il tombait des cordes.","exEn":"Worse, it was freezing and it was pouring with rain.","src":14},{"fr":"jeter l’argent par les fenêtres","en":"to throw money away","zh":"乱花钱，白白浪费钱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"et je n’ai pas les moyens de jeter l’argent par les fenêtres.","exEn":"and I can’t afford to throw money away.","src":17},{"fr":"têtu comme une mule","en":"as stubborn as a mule","zh":"像骡子一样固执","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’abandonne ! Tu es têtu comme une mule.","exEn":"I give up! You are as stubborn as a mule.","src":18}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":323,"gaps":[[1.94,3.98],[4.9,7.94],[47.04,48.16],[90.82,94.32],[96.68,98.68],[102.08,105],[109.84,113.14],[117.38,120.5],[124.3,127.28],[131.16,135.66]],"dur":135.7},{"n":59,"kind":"lesson","title":"Il ne gagnait (presque) jamais","titleEn":"He (almost) never won","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Quand mon père était jeune, les moyens de communication que nous avons aujourd’hui n’existaient pas.","en":"When my father was young, the means of communication that we have today did not exist.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"S’il voulait contacter un parent ou un ami, il devait écrire une lettre ou, en cas d’urgence, envoyer un télégramme…","en":"If he wanted to contact a relative or a friend, he had to write a letter or, in case of emergency, send a telegram…","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Un quoi ? Je pensais qu’un télégramme était un journal breton !","en":"A what? I thought a telegram was a Breton newspaper!","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, imbécile, les télégrammes permettaient… Laisse tomber, tu n’étais même pas né.","en":"No, idiot, telegrams allowed… Drop it, you weren’t even born.","lit":"leave fall"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Je disais donc que papa ne pouvait jamais s’attendre à une réponse instantanée.","en":"So I was saying that dad could never expect an instant answer.","lit":"wait himself"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Il ne pouvait pas non plus obtenir une information immédiatement.","en":"Neither could he get information immediately.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Comme c’est affreux ! Il n’avait pas le téléphone ? Ça existait alors, non ?","en":"How awful! He didn’t have a phone? It existed back then, didn’t it?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr, mais les appels coûtaient très cher donc on ne téléphonait que rarement.","en":"Of course, but calls were very expensive and people telephoned only rarely.","lit":"cost"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Si j’ai bien compris, les forfaits illimités n’existaient pas encore ?","en":"If I have understood correctly, unlimited phone plans didn’t yet exist?","lit":"well understood"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est ça. Et on utilisait des pigeons voyageurs pour communiquer. Non, je plaisante.","en":"That’s right. And we used homing pigeons to communicate. No, I’m joking.","lit":"travelling pigeons"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"À cette époque, papa jouait au Tiercé toutes les semaines.","en":"At that time, dad used to play the Tiercé every week.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Comme lui et maman vivaient loin du village voisin, il prenait son vélo pour aller au PMU.","en":"As he and mum lived far from the neighbouring village, he would take his bike to go to the betting office.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Il ne gagnait jamais mais ça l’amusait de jouer et il aimait bien se balader à la campagne.","en":"He never won but he enjoyed playing and he liked riding around the countryside.","lit":"it amused him"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis sûr que tes parents espéraient toucher le gros lot.","en":"I’m sure that your parents were hoping to get the jackpot.","lit":"touch the fat lot"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est possible mais j’en doute. Malheureusement, mon père choisissait ses chevaux au hasard.","en":"It’s possible but I doubt it. Unfortunately, dad used to choose horses at random.","lit":"unhappily"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Il lançait des dés ou jouait à pile ou face, c’est pourquoi il perdait toujours.","en":"He would throw dice or flip a coin, which is why he always lost.","lit":"played at tails or heads"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Enfin, presque toujours. Une nuit, pendant qu’il dormait, il a fait un rêve…","en":"Well, almost always. One night, while he was sleeping, he had a dream…","lit":"made a dream"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Le lendemain, il ne se souvenait pas exactement du nom du gagnant mais pensait qu’il s’appelait « Fortune ».","en":"The next day, he didn’t remember exactly the name of the winner but he thought it was called “Fortune.”","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais ce jour-là il pleuvait tellement fort que papa n’a pas pu aller au village.","en":"But that day, it was raining so hard that dad was unable to go to the village.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Et heureusement, car il n’y avait pas de « Fortune ».","en":"And fortunately, because there was no “Fortune.”","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est la semaine suivante que « Bonne Fortune » a remporté la course et papa a touché… une petite fortune !","en":"It’s the following week that “Good Fortune” took the race and dad got… a small fortune!","lit":"touched"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know that les moyens can mean financial resources (lesson 37 note 6) but it is also the word for means, i.e. a method for achieving something. However, while the English word takes an s, even in the singular, un moyen / les moyens depends on the context. J’ai trouvé un moyen de le contacter, I’ve found a means of contacting him. Contrast that with Il y a deux moyens de payer : par téléphone ou par carte, There are two means of payment: by phone or by card."},{"ref":"2","text":"un parent means a parent but also a relative (cousin, niece, etc.). So mes parents et amis can mean my parents OR my relatives and friends. The word is also used in the expression le parent pauvre: Le changement climatique est le parent pauvre des politiques de ce gouvernement, Climate change is the poor relative of this government’s policies. Always check the context!"},{"ref":"3","text":"The feminine noun urgence means urgency, and, in some cases, rush: Il n’y a pas d’urgence, ça peut attendre, There’s no rush; it can wait. With the partitive d’, it means emergency: La mairie a pris des mesures d’urgence, The town hall has taken emergency measures. And the emergency department of a hospital, le service des urgences, is often referred to simply as les urgences."},{"ref":"4","text":"The compound verb laisser tomber (“to allow to fall”) means to drop: Attention, il va laisser tomber le sac par terre ! Watch out, he’s going to drop the bag on the floor! Idiomatically, the expression can mean – as in English – to abandon: Il a laissé tomber ses études de médecine, He dropped his medical studies. (Note the construction; we’ll meet it again shortly.) As an interjection, Laisse(z) tomber ! is equivalent to Forget it!, expressing irritation or boredom: Cet exercice est trop difficile. – Laissez tomber, This exercise is too hard. – Forget it. In this context, there is no need for a direct object."},{"ref":"5","text":"alors, seen several times with the meaning so or then, literally means at the time, back then (from lors, which we will see later): Je l’ai connue à la fac. Elle était alors étudiante, I knew her at uni. Back then, she was a student. A shorter, more elegant way of saying à cette époque (line 11), alors is never used at the beginning of a sentence in this context."},{"ref":"6","text":"un forfait basically means an all-inclusive package or a fixed sum. It is widely used in two contexts: travel (for example, un forfait avion-hôtel, a flight and hotel package) and telephony, where the basic meaning is a phone plan: J’ai dépassé mon forfait, I’ve exceeded my plan limit."},{"ref":"7","text":"papa and the feminine equivalent maman are the equivalents of dad(dy) and mum(my). Papa et maman sont mariés depuis plus de trente ans, Dad and mum have been married for more than 30 years. In everyday usage, une maman is more common than une mère."},{"ref":"8","text":"un voisin means a neighbour (we saw the feminine form in lesson 36) but the word can also be an adjective, meaning neighbouring, nearby, etc. Ils habitent dans la rue voisine, They live in the next street. By extension, le voisinage means the neighbourhood."},{"ref":"9","text":"The little-used English word velocipede, a forerunner of the bicycle, comes from the French un vélocipède, which passed long ago into everyday language as un vélo, a bike. The “technical” term is une bicyclette. Such is the longstanding popularity of cycling in France that the affectionate term for the machine is la petite reine (“little queen”)."},{"ref":"10","text":"un hasard (lesson 22 line 1) is another faux-ami, meaning chance or luck. Je l’ai rencontré par hasard dans la rue hier, I met him by chance in the street yesterday. The expression au hasard means randomly (or haphazardly). Il répondait toujours au hasard à mes questions, He always used to answer my questions at random. (A hazard is translated as un risque or un danger.)"},{"ref":"","text":"France has a state-operated system for horse race betting. It is controlled through an organisation called le Pari mutuel urbain, or PMU, named for a mutual betting system (un pari, a bet) also used in North America (although un PMU can also mean a betting office). Bets were originally placed on a combination of three horses, hence the name Tiercé. Although the system has since been expanded to four (Quarté) or five (Quinté) winners, le Tiercé is widely used as a generic term for horse betting. Most punters would love to win le gros lot (“fat prize”), also known as le jackpot…"},{"ref":"","text":"Although telegrams were phased out in France in 2018, the word is used on the masthead of several popular newspapers in Brittany, notably Le Télégramme de Brest."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Seulement deux restaurants grecs existaient alors dans le voisinage.","a":"At the time there were only two Greek restaurants in the neighbourhood."},{"q":"Si je comprends bien, tu as dépassé ton forfait ?","a":"If I understand rightly, you’ve exceeded your plan limit?"},{"q":"C’est vrai que tes parents gagnaient régulièrement au Tiercé ?","a":"Is it true that your parents/relatives used to win the Tiercé regularly?"},{"q":"Cette décision est trop importante. On ne peut pas lancer des dés ou jouer à pile ou face.","a":"That decision is too important. We can’t throw dice or toss a coin."},{"q":"Est-ce que Léa est toujours à la fac ? – Non, elle a laissé tomber ces études.","a":"Is Léa still at uni? – No, she has dropped her studies."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Mum and dad never used to win the lottery but even so they enjoyed playing it.","t":"{{Maman}} et {{papa}} ne {{gagnaient jamais}} à la loterie mais ça {{les amusait}} quand même d’y {{jouer}}."},{"cue":"The internet did not exist back then, and we didn’t even have a phone.","t":"Internet {{n’existait pas à cette époque}}, et {{nous n’avions même pas}} de téléphone."},{"cue":"I abandoned my phone plan because it was much too expensive.","t":"J’ai {{laissé tomber mon forfait}} parce qu’il {{était}} beaucoup trop cher."},{"cue":"They used to love that old show so much they even remembered the name of the compere.","t":"{{Ils aimaient}} tellement {{cette vieille}} émission qu’{{ils se souvenaient}} même du nom {{de la présentatrice}}."},{"cue":"When we were young we had to go to school until the age of thirteen only. – That’s awful!","t":"Quand {{nous étions jeunes}}, nous {{devions aller}} à l’école {{jusqu’à l’âge}} de treize ans seulement. – C’est {{affreux}} !"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"les moyens de communication","en":"the means of communication","zh":"通讯手段","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"communication = same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quand mon père était jeune, les moyens de communication que nous avons aujourd’hui n’existaient pas.","exEn":"When my father was young, the means of communication that we have today did not exist.","src":1},{"fr":"en cas d’urgence","en":"in case of emergency","zh":"紧急情况下","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"urgence ≈ urgency","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"S’il voulait contacter un parent ou un ami, il devait écrire une lettre ou, en cas d’urgence, envoyer un télégramme…","exEn":"If he wanted to contact a relative or a friend, he had to write a letter or, in case of emergency, send a telegram…","src":2},{"fr":"Laisse tomber.","en":"Drop it. Forget it.","zh":"算了吧。别提了。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “less tom-bay” – “less tomb, bay!” – drop it, don’t dig a grave of an argument","ex":"Non, imbécile, les télégrammes permettaient… Laisse tomber, tu n’étais même pas né.","exEn":"No, idiot, telegrams allowed… Drop it, you weren’t even born.","src":4},{"fr":"il ne pouvait pas non plus","en":"neither could he","zh":"他也不能","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ne pouvait pas non plus obtenir une information immédiatement.","exEn":"Neither could he get information immediately.","src":6},{"fr":"Comme c’est affreux !","en":"How awful!","zh":"多可怕啊！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “a-fruh” – afraid: “afraid of how awful it is”","ex":"Comme c’est affreux ! Il n’avait pas le téléphone ? Ça existait alors, non ?","exEn":"How awful! He didn’t have a phone? It existed back then, didn’t it?","src":7},{"fr":"si j’ai bien compris","en":"if I have understood correctly","zh":"如果我没理解错","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si j’ai bien compris, les forfaits illimités n’existaient pas encore ?","exEn":"If I have understood correctly, unlimited phone plans didn’t yet exist?","src":9},{"fr":"un forfait illimité","en":"an unlimited (phone) plan","zh":"无限量套餐","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"illimité ≈ unlimited","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si j’ai bien compris, les forfaits illimités n’existaient pas encore ?","exEn":"If I have understood correctly, unlimited phone plans didn’t yet exist?","src":9},{"fr":"À cette époque","en":"At that time","zh":"在那个时代","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"époque ≈ epoch","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"À cette époque, papa jouait au Tiercé toutes les semaines.","exEn":"At that time, dad used to play the Tiercé every week.","src":11},{"fr":"le village voisin","en":"the neighbouring village","zh":"邻村","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comme lui et maman vivaient loin du village voisin, il prenait son vélo pour aller au PMU.","exEn":"As he and mum lived far from the neighbouring village, he would take his bike to go to the betting office.","src":12},{"fr":"il ne gagnait jamais","en":"he never won","zh":"他从来没赢过","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ne gagnait jamais mais ça l’amusait de jouer et il aimait bien se balader à la campagne.","exEn":"He never won but he enjoyed playing and he liked riding around the countryside.","src":13},{"fr":"se balader à la campagne","en":"to go for a stroll in the countryside","zh":"在乡间闲逛","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ballad-ay” – strolling through the fields while singing a ballad","ex":"Il ne gagnait jamais mais ça l’amusait de jouer et il aimait bien se balader à la campagne.","exEn":"He never won but he enjoyed playing and he liked riding around the countryside.","src":13},{"fr":"le gros lot","en":"the jackpot","zh":"头奖，大奖","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis sûr que tes parents espéraient toucher le gros lot.","exEn":"I’m sure that your parents were hoping to get the jackpot.","src":14},{"fr":"au hasard","en":"at random","zh":"随机地，随意","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"hasard ≈ hazard","ff":"hasard = chance, luck – NOT “hazard” (danger)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est possible mais j’en doute. Malheureusement, mon père choisissait ses chevaux au hasard.","exEn":"It’s possible but I doubt it. Unfortunately, dad used to choose horses at random.","src":15},{"fr":"jouer à pile ou face","en":"to toss a coin","zh":"抛硬币","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il lançait des dés ou jouait à pile ou face, c’est pourquoi il perdait toujours.","exEn":"He would throw dice or flip a coin, which is why he always lost.","src":16},{"fr":"pendant qu’il dormait","en":"while he was sleeping","zh":"他睡觉的时候","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Enfin, presque toujours. Une nuit, pendant qu’il dormait, il a fait un rêve…","exEn":"Well, almost always. One night, while he was sleeping, he had a dream…","src":17},{"fr":"Et heureusement","en":"And fortunately","zh":"幸好","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et heureusement, car il n’y avait pas de « Fortune ».","exEn":"And fortunately, because there was no “Fortune.”","src":20}],"uncertain":["note 7: the scan’s English “mum(my) and dad(dy)” order inverted to match papa/maman"],"pdfPage":331,"gaps":[[1.82,3.8],[5.68,8.58],[25.94,27],[29.82,30.9],[33.16,34.56],[73.12,74.12],[95.38,96.64],[99.92,101.06],[108,109.1],[125.62,128.96],[131.08,133.2],[136.76,139.68],[142.4,145.56],[147.98,150.84],[156,158.9],[163.26,166.24]],"dur":166.2},{"n":60,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le voisinage est de plus en plus dangereux !","titleEn":"The neighbourhood is increasingly dangerous!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"L’incendie a commencé peu après la fermeture du bar, aux environs de minuit.","en":"The fire began shortly after the bar closed, around midnight.","lit":"the closing of the bar"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Que faisiez-vous à ce moment-là ? Étiez-vous dans les environs ?","en":"What were you doing at that time? Were you in the vicinity?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je n’y étais plus. Je rentrais chez moi à pied et je n’ai rien vu du tout.","en":"No, I was no longer there. I was walking home and I didn’t see anything at all.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai quelque chose à vous dire, monsieur le brigadier…","en":"I have something to tell you, officer…","lit":"mister the brigadier"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Un peu de patience, madame, s’il vous plaît. Et vous monsieur ? Qu’avez-vous vu ?","en":"A little patience, madam, please. And you, sir? What did you see?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je sortais de la bouche du métro lorsque j’ai aperçu un grand nuage de fumée.","en":"I was coming out of the metro entrance when I noticed a big cloud of smoke.","lit":"mouth"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pensais d’abord que c’était du brouillard car le temps était maussade et il bruinait depuis peu.","en":"I thought at first that it was fog because the weather was gloomy and it had been drizzling for a short while.","lit":"since little"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais je me suis rendu compte que l’immeuble à l’angle de l’avenue brûlait.","en":"But I realised that the building on the corner of the avenue was burning.","lit":"I rendered myself account"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai pris mon téléphone pour appeler les secours mais quelqu’un l’avait déjà fait.","en":"I took my phone to call the emergency services but someone had already done it.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Les pompiers ont mis peu de temps à venir – moins de trois minutes, pas plus.","en":"The firefighters came quickly – less than three minutes, no more.","lit":"put little of time to come"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"En tout cas, ils sont arrivés juste à temps car le toit était complètement en flammes.","en":"In any case, they arrived just in time because the roof was completely in flames.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais laissez-moi parler ! Saviez-vous que le voisinage était de plus en plus dangereux ?","en":"Let me speak! Did you know that the neighbourhood was increasingly dangerous?","lit":"from more to more"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Peu importe. Continuez, monsieur. Combien y avait-il de camions, plus ou moins ?","en":"Never mind. Go on, sir. How many trucks were there, more or less?","lit":"little matters"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Au début il y en avait peu – trois ou quatre je crois, pas plus –,","en":"At the beginning, there weren’t many – three or four I believe, no more –,","lit":"there of-them was few"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"mais d’autres sont arrivés peu de temps après. J’en ai vu plus d’une dizaine, environ.","en":"but others arrived shortly afterwards. I saw around ten of them.","lit":"a little time after"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils sont arrivés pendant que l’immeuble continuait à brûler de plus en plus fort.","en":"They arrived while the building continued to burn increasingly fiercely.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Les équipages ont réalisé un travail formidable.","en":"The crews did an amazing job.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"S’il vous plaît, j’attends depuis longtemps. Écoutez-moi et, après, je ne dirai plus rien.","en":"Please, I’ve been waiting for a long time. Listen to me and, afterwards, I won’t say anything else.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Encore vous ! Mais que voulez-vous ?","en":"You again! But what do you want?","lit":"still you"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Je voulais vous prévenir que pendant que vous parliez avec ces gens quelqu’un a volé votre voiture, celle qui était garée là-bas.","en":"I wanted to warn you that while you were talking to these people someone stole your car, the one that was parked over there.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Et quand aviez-vous l’intention de m’en parler ? Plus tard, peut-être ?","en":"And when did you intend to talk to me about it? Later, maybe?","lit":"had you the intention"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"The pronunciation of the adverb plus changes according to its function. Listen carefully to the text and recorded exercise. Detailed rules can be found in lesson 63."},{"ref":"1","text":"The adverb peu is used in numerous contexts. On its own in a time clause, it means shortly or just: Je suis arrivé peu avant la fermeture du magasin, I arrived shortly before the shop closed."},{"ref":"2","text":"We know that environ means about, approximately (lesson 46 line 7). As an adverb, it does not agree. However, it can take an s in the expression dans les environs, meaning in the vicinity: Il habite dans les environs, He lives in the vicinity / neighbourhood. If further information is added, we need the preposition de: Ils habitent dans les environs de la gare, They live somewhere around the station. When the idea of approximation applies to time we use the expression aux environs de: Il est arrivé aux environs de midi, He arrived at around noon…"},{"ref":"3","text":"We saw un peu de, a little, a bit of, in lesson 11. Do not confuse the noun un peu with the adverb peu de, which means little / few (remember that French makes no difference between countable and uncountable nouns in this context). Here’s a simple mnemonic: J’ai peu d’argent mais un peu de temps, I have little money but a bit of time."},{"ref":"4","text":"The conjunction lorsque, when, is a synonym of quand. There is no difference in meaning between the two, but lorsque is more elegant. (The root word is lors, seen in alors, lesson 59 note 5.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"depuis with the present tense, meaning for or since, is generally followed by a specific time period (lesson 41 note 2). But it can also be followed by less precise adverbs such as peu and longtemps. In the first case, Je la connais depuis peu, the English equivalent would use either a negative verb or an affirmative with only: I have not known her for long / I have known her for only a short time. With longtemps, the translation is more straightforward: Je les connais depuis longtemps, I have known them for a long time. Note the grammatical simplicity of the French constructions."},{"ref":"6","text":"The reflexive verb se rendre compte (“to render account to oneself”) means to realise or be aware. When followed by a direct object, it takes de: Est-ce qu’il se rend compte de la situation ? Is he aware of the situation? (Because compte is the direct object of the reflexive verb, rendu does not agree in number or gender: Elle s’est rendu compte…) The verb réaliser (line 17) is a faux-ami, meaning to do or to carry out."},{"ref":"7","text":"When plus is used at the end of a sentence to mean more, the final s is pronounced. The pronunciation rules for plus are quite complex (see lesson 63)."},{"ref":"8","text":"The verb importer means to import (goods, etc.) but also to matter, to be important (as in English: a matter of import). It is often found in the exclamation Peu importe, It doesn’t matter or Never mind. C’est très cher ! – Peu importe. It’s very expensive! – Never mind. In the next set of lessons, we will see some other expressions that use the negative form n’importe."},{"ref":"9","text":"pendant followed by a period of time means during (see table, lesson 49). Followed by the conjunction que it means while. In a past-tense sentence, the verb that follows is generally in the imperfect tense: Le téléphone a sonné pendant qu’ils dormaient, The phone rang while they were sleeping (i.e. the action lasted a certain period of time)."},{"ref":"","text":"Les secours d’urgence or, more commonly, les secours (the origin of the English word succour), is the blanket term for the emergency services. In addition to la police, the police force, which in France is organised on a nationwide basis, whence the official name la Police nationale, the other main service is the fire brigade, les sapeurs-pompiers (although the first part of the title – the root of the English word a sapper – is generally omitted in everyday speech). The origin of the term un pompier is une pompe, a pump."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Nous avons besoin de plus de moyens car nous ne voulons plus travailler vingt-quatre heures sur vingt-quatre.","a":"We need more resources because we no longer want to work 24/7."},{"q":"Comme disait un des candidats à l’époque : « Travailler plus pour gagner plus ».","a":"As one of the candidates used to say at the time: “Work more to earn more.”"},{"q":"Plus tard dans la nuit, les bruits devenaient de plus en plus forts, mais je n’avais plus peur.","a":"Later in the night, the noises got louder and louder, but I was no longer scared."},{"q":"Prenez le métro pour y aller. Tu mettras plus de temps en bus.","a":"Take the metro to get there. You’ll take longer by bus."},{"q":"Tu connais l’expression « Plus ça change, plus c’est la même chose » ?","a":"Do you know the expression “The more things change, the more they stay the same”?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I think he was sleeping because I couldn’t hear any noise in his bedroom.","t":"{{Je pense qu’il dormait}} car {{je n’entendais aucun}} bruit dans sa chambre."},{"cue":"My son and my daughter were coming out of the house when they saw flames that were rising towards the roof.","t":"{{Mon fils et ma fille sortaient de}} la maison quand {{ils ont vu}} des flammes qui {{montaient}} vers le {{toit}}."},{"cue":"The first firefighters took more or less three minutes to reach the spot.","t":"Les premiers {{pompiers ont mis plus ou moins}} trois minutes à {{arriver}}."},{"cue":"I realised that you did an amazing job. I just wanted to say thank you.","t":"{{Je me suis rendu compte}} que {{vous avez réalisé un travail}} formidable. {{Je voulais juste}} vous dire merci."},{"cue":"While we were looking for a car park, someone stole our truck.","t":"{{Pendant que nous cherchions}} un parking, quelqu’un {{a volé notre}} camion."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"peu après la fermeture du bar","en":"shortly after the bar closed","zh":"酒吧关门后不久","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’incendie a commencé peu après la fermeture du bar, aux environs de minuit.","exEn":"The fire began shortly after the bar closed, around midnight.","src":1},{"fr":"aux environs de minuit","en":"at around midnight","zh":"大约午夜时分","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"environ ≈ environs","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"L’incendie a commencé peu après la fermeture du bar, aux environs de minuit.","exEn":"The fire began shortly after the bar closed, around midnight.","src":1},{"fr":"Que faisiez-vous à ce moment-là ?","en":"What were you doing at that moment?","zh":"那时候您在做什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Que faisiez-vous à ce moment-là ? Étiez-vous dans les environs ?","exEn":"What were you doing at that time? Were you in the vicinity?","src":2},{"fr":"je n’ai rien vu du tout","en":"I didn’t see anything at all","zh":"我什么也没看见","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je n’y étais plus. Je rentrais chez moi à pied et je n’ai rien vu du tout.","exEn":"No, I was no longer there. I was walking home and I didn’t see anything at all.","src":3},{"fr":"Un peu de patience","en":"A little patience","zh":"请耐心一点","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"patience = same as English","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un peu de patience, madame, s’il vous plaît. Et vous monsieur ? Qu’avez-vous vu ?","exEn":"A little patience, madam, please. And you, sir? What did you see?","src":5},{"fr":"j’ai aperçu un grand nuage de fumée","en":"I noticed a big cloud of smoke","zh":"我瞥见一大团烟雾","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ah-pair-sue” – a pair of eyes catching sight of a sudden cloud","ex":"Je sortais de la bouche du métro lorsque j’ai aperçu un grand nuage de fumée.","exEn":"I was coming out of the metro entrance when I noticed a big cloud of smoke.","src":6},{"fr":"je me suis rendu compte que","en":"I realised that","zh":"我意识到……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"se rendre compte = to realise (réaliser = to carry out)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais je me suis rendu compte que l’immeuble à l’angle de l’avenue brûlait.","exEn":"But I realised that the building on the corner of the avenue was burning.","src":8},{"fr":"appeler les secours","en":"to call the emergency services","zh":"叫救援（急救）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “suh-koor” – “sue, cure!” – emergency help rushes in to cure you","ex":"J’ai pris mon téléphone pour appeler les secours mais quelqu’un l’avait déjà fait.","exEn":"I took my phone to call the emergency services but someone had already done it.","src":9},{"fr":"juste à temps","en":"just in time","zh":"恰好及时","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En tout cas, ils sont arrivés juste à temps car le toit était complètement en flammes.","exEn":"In any case, they arrived just in time because the roof was completely in flames.","src":11},{"fr":"de plus en plus dangereux","en":"more and more dangerous","zh":"越来越危险","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"dangereux ≈ dangerous","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais laissez-moi parler ! Saviez-vous que le voisinage était de plus en plus dangereux ?","exEn":"Let me speak! Did you know that the neighbourhood was increasingly dangerous?","src":12},{"fr":"Peu importe.","en":"Never mind. It doesn’t matter.","zh":"不要紧。无所谓。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peu importe. Continuez, monsieur. Combien y avait-il de camions, plus ou moins ?","exEn":"Never mind. Go on, sir. How many trucks were there, more or less?","src":13},{"fr":"plus ou moins","en":"more or less","zh":"或多或少，大约","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peu importe. Continuez, monsieur. Combien y avait-il de camions, plus ou moins ?","exEn":"Never mind. Go on, sir. How many trucks were there, more or less?","src":13},{"fr":"j’attends depuis longtemps","en":"I’ve been waiting for a long time","zh":"我已经等了很久了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"S’il vous plaît, j’attends depuis longtemps. Écoutez-moi et, après, je ne dirai plus rien.","exEn":"Please, I’ve been waiting for a long time. Listen to me and, afterwards, I won’t say anything else.","src":18},{"fr":"Je voulais vous prévenir que","en":"I wanted to warn you that","zh":"我想提醒您……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"prévenir = to warn/inform, not “to prevent”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je voulais vous prévenir que pendant que vous parliez avec ces gens quelqu’un a volé votre voiture, celle qui était garée là-bas.","exEn":"I wanted to warn you that while you were talking to these people someone stole your car, the one that was parked over there.","src":20},{"fr":"quelqu’un a volé votre voiture","en":"someone stole your car","zh":"有人偷了您的车","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"voler = to steal (and to fly)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je voulais vous prévenir que pendant que vous parliez avec ces gens quelqu’un a volé votre voiture, celle qui était garée là-bas.","exEn":"I wanted to warn you that while you were talking to these people someone stole your car, the one that was parked over there.","src":20},{"fr":"aviez-vous l’intention de","en":"did you intend to","zh":"您打算……吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"intention ≈ intention","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et quand aviez-vous l’intention de m’en parler ? Plus tard, peut-être ?","exEn":"And when did you intend to talk to me about it? Later, maybe?","src":21}],"uncertain":["exercise 1 item 4: the book mixes vous (Prenez) and tu (Tu mettras); kept as printed","sentence 8 (scan): l’angle du l’avenue corrected to l’angle de l’avenue"],"pdfPage":339,"gaps":[[1.7,3.54],[5.84,8.52],[12.54,13.56],[49.24,50.26],[108.44,112.06],[113.88,116.18],[121.76,124.82],[129.18,132.16],[136.86,139.94],[143.46,146.56],[150,153.08]],"dur":153.1},{"n":61,"kind":"lesson","title":"La célébrité ne me dit pas grand-chose","titleEn":"Fame doesn’t mean much to me","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Mon invitée est la photojournaliste Isabelle Rossi, pour son nouveau livre « L’Histoire en images ».","en":"My guest is the photojournalist Isabelle Rossi, for her new book “History in Pictures”.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Bonjour, Isabelle. Pouvez-vous raconter à nos auditeurs comment vous avez choisi votre carrière ?","en":"Hello, Isabelle. Can you tell our listeners how you chose your career?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Avec plaisir. Je suis née il y a soixante-dix ans à Ajaccio dans une famille plutôt modeste.","en":"With pleasure. I was born seventy years ago in Ajaccio to a fairly modest family.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Je savais très jeune que je voulais devenir peintre et que, pour ça, il fallait quitter la Corse,","en":"I knew when I was very young that I wanted to be a painter and that, for that, it was necessary to leave Corsica,","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"m’installer en Métropole, et fréquenter des gens avec les mêmes intérêts que les miens.","en":"settle on the mainland, and keep company with people with the same interests as mine.","lit":"install myself; frequent"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"C’était dur au début car j’étais plutôt timide et je ne connaissais pas grand monde.","en":"It was tough at the beginning because I was quite shy and I didn’t know a lot of people.","lit":"great people"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais petit à petit je me suis fait des amis et aussi j’ai fait la connaissance d’autres gens comme moi.","en":"But little by little I made friends and also made the acquaintance of other people like me.","lit":"made myself friends"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais vous avez laissé tomber assez vite la peinture. Pourquoi cette décision ?","en":"But you abandoned painting quite quickly. Why did you make that decision?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Je me suis rendu compte que, en définitive, je n’étais pas si douée que ça.","en":"I realised that, in fact, I wasn’t as gifted as that.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"En revanche, j’avais un don pour la photographie – pas des paysages ou la mode –","en":"By contrast, I had a gift for photography – not landscapes or fashion –","lit":"in revenge"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"mais plutôt pour, à la fois, des portraits et des scènes de la vie ordinaire.","en":"but both, at the same time, portraits and scenes from ordinary life.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Cependant il y avait peu de femmes photographes professionnelles à ce moment-là ;","en":"However, there were few professional women photographers at that time,","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"et, pour réussir, j’ai dû surmonter des obstacles et apprendre à ne jamais baisser les bras.","en":"and, to succeed, I had to overcome obstacles and learn to never give up.","lit":"lower the arms"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai enfin trouvé ma voie – et mon bonheur – en devenant photographe de rue.","en":"I finally found my way in life – and my happiness – by becoming a street photographer.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"J’adorais flâner à travers la ville, le matin de bonne heure, quand la journée commençait,","en":"I loved wandering through the city, early in the morning, when the day was beginning,","lit":"of good time"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"et prendre en photo les gens qui s’occupaient de leurs affaires et vivaient leur vie.","en":"and photographing people who were going about their business and living their lives.","lit":"taking in photo"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Il fallait leur demander la permission d’être photographiés mais la plupart d’entre eux disaient oui.","en":"It was necessary to ask their permission to be photographed, but most of them said yes.","lit":"among them"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Certains refusaient, souvent par timidité, et je savais que ça ne valait pas la peine d’insister.","en":"Some would refuse, often out of shyness, and I knew that it wasn’t worth insisting.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais vous avez réussi brillamment et vous êtes devenue très vite célèbre.","en":"But you succeeded brilliantly and you quickly became very famous.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous savez, la célébrité ne me dit pas grand-chose. En fait ça m’énerve,","en":"You know, fame doesn’t mean much to me. In fact, it annoys me","lit":"celebrity doesn’t say to me great thing"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"car une belle photo vaut mieux qu’un long discours ou le succès médiatique.","en":"because a beautiful photo is worth more than a long speech or media success.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We are familiar with falloir, to be necessary / to have to. This impersonal verb is also defective, meaning that it has only one conjugation in each tense: the third person singular. The imperfect form is fallait: Il ne fallait pas inviter ce journaliste, That journalist shouldn’t have been invited."},{"ref":"2","text":"fréquenter is a good example of a French verb with both a Latin-based and an Anglo-Saxon translation, depending on the level of formality. De nombreux oiseaux fréquentent cette région, Numerous birds frequent this region or, less formally, Évite de fréquenter les bars du centre-ville, Avoid going to the bars in the city centre. The verb can also be translated as to keep company with, spend time with, etc. By contrast, the adjective fréquent simply means frequent."},{"ref":"3","text":"We know that the adjective grand, big, can also mean great, eminent, etc. (lesson 16 note 2). It can also mean main, principal. Note the subtle difference in meaning in this sentence: La grande difficulté est de trouver une salle assez grande, The main difficulty is finding a room that is big enough. In short, grand designates anything of size, importance, value or seniority. (See also note 10.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"In the expression faire la connaissance, to make the acquaintance of, connaissance is still abstract. But it can also be a concrete noun: Simon n’est pas un ami, plutôt une connaissance, Simon is an acquaintance rather than a friend. (The noun remains feminine, regardless of the acquaintance’s sex.) Remember that the root verb is connaître."},{"ref":"5","text":"We know laisser tomber means to drop (lesson 59 note 4). In the perfect tense, it is not possible to put two participles together, so, in any two-word compound verb – faire cuire, to cook, for example – the second verb stays in the infinitive: J’ai fait cuire des pommes de terre, I’ve cooked some potatoes, or, as in lesson 59, Il a laissé tomber son sac, He dropped his bag."},{"ref":"6","text":"Faux-ami alert: un(e) photographe, a photographer, une photographie, a photograph (often abbreviated to une photo, a photo), and la photographie (or la photo), photography. The latter is classified as either numérique (digital) or argentique, a neologism from l’argent, silver, usually translated as film or analogue."},{"ref":"7","text":"à la fois means at the same time, at once: N’essaie pas de faire trop de choses à la fois, Don’t try to do too many things at once. The expression can also translate both in the case of two nouns: La solution est à la fois simple et pas chère, The solution is both simple and inexpensive. In a longer construction, however, the expression can simply be dropped, La solution est simple, rapide et pas chère, The solution is simple, quick and inexpensive."},{"ref":"8","text":"dû is the past participle of devoir: Nous avons dû partir car nous étions en retard, We had to leave because we were late. The circumflex is important in order to distinguish the word from the partitive du, just as the grave accent allows us to distinguish between ou (or) and où (where). In neither case does the accent change the pronunciation."},{"ref":"9","text":"valait is the imperfect of valoir (lesson 44 note 10)."},{"ref":"10","text":"The expression pas grand-chose means not much, nothing much. On ne sait pas grand-chose de lui, We don’t know much about him. / Not much is known about him. The expression Ça ne me dit pas grand-chose means It doesn’t mean very much to me or, in a more familiar register, It doesn’t grab me. Note that we use grand even though the noun is feminine. The same form is found in a couple of other nouns, notably une grand-mère, a grandmother (the masculine being un grand-père, grandfather)."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(14) (15) Since an initial h is a half-vowel, it is usually mute when liaised to the final consonant of the previous word. Thus bonne heure is pronounced identically to bonheur [bonuhr]."},{"ref":"","text":"La Corse (Corsica both in English and the Corsican language: le corse) is an island 170 kilometres off the Mediterranean coast of France. Known as l’île de Beauté, the Isle of Beauty, it is famed for dramatic scenery, mountainous terrain, sandy beaches and lush vegetation. Corsica has its own language (le corse – note the masculine gender) and a very strong cultural identity combining influences from all over the Mediterranean and beyond.\nCorsicans – among others – often refer to mainland France as la Métropole (“metropolitan France”), often without a capital m, since the word was once used to differentiate a country from its colonies or possessions."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ils se sont rendu compte de leur erreur, mais il était déjà trop tard.","a":"They realised their mistake but it was already too late."},{"q":"Il ne faut jamais baisser les bras, car on est toujours doué pour quelque chose.","a":"Never give up, because you’re always gifted for something."},{"q":"La plupart d’entre vous ont déjà entendu cette histoire, mais ça vaut la peine de la raconter.","a":"Most of you have already heard this story but it’s worth telling."},{"q":"En cas d’urgence, deux têtes valent mieux qu’une, et même s’il n’y a pas d’urgence.","a":"In case of emergency, two heads are better than one, and even if there isn’t an emergency."},{"q":"Le restaurant est excellent : les plats sont à la fois simples, bons et pas chers.","a":"The restaurant is excellent: the dishes are simple, good and inexpensive."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Where is the cinema? You must have told me but I’ve forgotten.","t":"Où est le cinéma ? Tu {{as dû}} me le dire, mais {{j’ai oublié}}."},{"cue":"Juliette became a grandmother at the age of fifty but we don’t know a lot about her.","t":"Juliette {{est devenue grand-mère}} à l’âge de cinquante ans mais on ne {{sait pas grand-chose d’elle}}."},{"cue":"Raymond is a famous photographer: he started photography when he was very young.","t":"Raymond est {{un photographe célèbre}} : il {{a commencé}} très jeune {{la photographie}}."},{"cue":"I’ve dropped my bag on the floor. – You’re trying to do too many things at the same time.","t":"{{J’ai laissé tomber}} mon sac par terre. – Tu {{essaies}} de faire {{trop de choses à la fois}}."},{"cue":"He had to leave early to avoid the traffic jams. – Getting up early is the secret of happiness.","t":"Il {{a dû partir de bonne heure}} pour éviter les {{embouteillages}}. – Se lever tôt est le {{secret du bonheur}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"laisser tomber (to drop, to give up)","en":"to drop, to give up","zh":"放弃；使掉落","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais vous avez laissé tomber assez vite la peinture.","exEn":"But you abandoned painting quite quickly.","src":8},{"fr":"ça vaut la peine","en":"it’s worth it","zh":"值得","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Certains refusaient, souvent par timidité, et je savais que ça ne valait pas la peine d’insister.","exEn":"Some would refuse, often out of shyness, and I knew that it wasn’t worth insisting.","src":18},{"fr":"en revanche","en":"by contrast, on the other hand","zh":"相反；另一方面","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ revenge (in revenge = in return)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “on re-vonsh” – imagine saying “in revenge, I prefer photography!” and snapping a photo of the rival painter","ex":"En revanche, j’avais un don pour la photographie – pas des paysages ou la mode –","exEn":"By contrast, I had a gift for photography – not landscapes or fashion –","src":10},{"fr":"pas grand-chose","en":"not much, nothing much","zh":"没什么；不多","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous savez, la célébrité ne me dit pas grand-chose. En fait ça m’énerve,","exEn":"You know, fame doesn’t mean much to me. In fact, it annoys me","src":20},{"fr":"il fallait quitter la Corse","en":"it was necessary to leave Corsica","zh":"当时必须离开科西嘉岛","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je savais très jeune que je voulais devenir peintre et que, pour ça, il fallait quitter la Corse,","exEn":"I knew when I was very young that I wanted to be a painter and that, for that, it was necessary to leave Corsica,","src":4},{"fr":"ne jamais baisser les bras","en":"never to give up","zh":"永不放弃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et, pour réussir, j’ai dû surmonter des obstacles et apprendre à ne jamais baisser les bras.","exEn":"and, to succeed, I had to overcome obstacles and learn to never give up.","src":13},{"fr":"j’avais un don pour la photographie","en":"I had a gift for photography","zh":"我在摄影方面有天赋","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En revanche, j’avais un don pour la photographie – pas des paysages ou la mode –","exEn":"By contrast, I had a gift for photography – not landscapes or fashion –","src":10},{"fr":"pas si douée que ça","en":"not as gifted as that","zh":"没有那么有天分","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me suis rendu compte que, en définitive, je n’étais pas si douée que ça.","exEn":"I realised that, in fact, I wasn’t as gifted as that.","src":9},{"fr":"surmonter des obstacles","en":"to overcome obstacles","zh":"克服障碍","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ surmount obstacles","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et, pour réussir, j’ai dû surmonter des obstacles et apprendre à ne jamais baisser les bras.","exEn":"and, to succeed, I had to overcome obstacles and learn to never give up.","src":13},{"fr":"la plupart d’entre eux","en":"most of them","zh":"他们中的大多数","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il fallait leur demander la permission d’être photographiés mais la plupart d’entre eux disaient oui.","exEn":"It was necessary to ask their permission to be photographed, but most of them said yes.","src":17},{"fr":"à la fois","en":"at the same time, both","zh":"同时；既……又……","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le restaurant est excellent : les plats sont à la fois simples, bons et pas chers.","exEn":"The restaurant is excellent: the dishes are simple, good and inexpensive.","src":0},{"fr":"de bonne heure","en":"early","zh":"一大早；很早","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"sounds like “duh bonn-ur” – picture a bonny hour at dawn, the early hour when the bonny sun rises","ex":"J’adorais flâner à travers la ville, le matin de bonne heure, quand la journée commençait,","exEn":"I loved wandering through the city, early in the morning, when the day was beginning,","src":15},{"fr":"petit à petit","en":"little by little","zh":"一点一点地；逐渐","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “puh-tee ah puh-tee” – a petite ant taking tiny steps, step by step","ex":"Mais petit à petit je me suis fait des amis et aussi j’ai fait la connaissance d’autres gens comme moi.","exEn":"But little by little I made friends and also made the acquaintance of other people like me.","src":7},{"fr":"une belle photo vaut mieux qu’un long discours","en":"a beautiful photo is worth more than a long speech","zh":"一张好照片胜过长篇大论","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"car une belle photo vaut mieux qu’un long discours ou le succès médiatique.","exEn":"because a beautiful photo is worth more than a long speech or media success.","src":21},{"fr":"vous êtes devenue très vite célèbre","en":"you quickly became very famous","zh":"您很快就出名了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ celebrity (célèbre = famous)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais vous avez réussi brillamment et vous êtes devenue très vite célèbre.","exEn":"But you succeeded brilliantly and you quickly became very famous.","src":19}],"uncertain":["Intro/title page: the lesson 61 end-of-lesson Corsica culture note spans the last lines of the page preceding lesson 62 and the first lines of the next page; merged into one note","dialogue 11: comma after “à la fois” added to match English punctuation (OCR shows none)","dialogue 1: book title read as « L’Histoire en images » (OCR garbled: “T H istoire”)"],"pdfPage":345,"gaps":[[1.84,3.84],[6.1,9.12],[88.56,89.84],[134.32,137.48],[139.74,141.76],[145.2,148.42],[152.42,155.08],[159.52,162.6],[167.22,170.3],[174.86,178.88]],"dur":178.9},{"n":62,"kind":"lesson","title":"J’ai mis le doigt sur ton problème…","titleEn":"I’ve put my finger on your problem…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"On m’a dit « Apprends à coder et les entreprises t’accueilleront les bras ouverts ! ».","en":"I was told “Learn to code and companies will welcome you with open arms!”","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"« Elles font les yeux doux aux informaticiens qui ont les dents longues. »","en":"“They make eyes at ambitious computer scientists.”","lit":"who have long teeth"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai eu un coup de cœur pour le métier de programmateur – eh oui, ça arrive –","en":"I was taken with the job of programmer – oh yes, it happens –","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’ai fait la sourde oreille à ceux qui me déconseillaient de poursuivre.","en":"and I turned a deaf ear to those who advised me against continuing.","lit":"dis-advised; pursuing"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai commencé une formation mais j’ai vite appris que ce n’était pas si évident que ça.","en":"I started a training session but I quickly learned that it wasn’t as easy as that.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Si certains concepts sont simples, d’autres me passent au-dessus de la tête.","en":"If some concepts are simple, others go over my head.","lit":"pass"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je me creuse la tête pour essayer de comprendre,","en":"I rack my brains to try to understand,","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"mais c’est souvent impossible et je m’arrache les cheveux.","en":"but it’s often impossible and I tear my hair out.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai envie de prendre mes jambes à mon cou et fuir l’école,","en":"I want to take to my heels and flee the school,","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"mais j’ai peur de mettre les pieds dans le plat parce que mon prof est très sympa.","en":"but I’m scared to put my foot in it because my teacher is very nice.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Par contre, les autres étudiants ne lèvent pas le petit doigt pour m’aider.","en":"By contrast, the other students don’t lift a finger to help me.","lit":"by against; little finger"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pourrais serrer les dents et leur demander de l’aide, mais l’idée me fait froid dans le dos.","en":"I could grit my teeth and ask them for help, but the idea sends a shiver down my spine.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Si seulement quelqu’un voulait me donner un coup de main…","en":"If only someone would give me a hand…","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Doucement ! Ne te casse pas la tête.","en":"Take it easy! Don’t worry about it.","lit":"Gently"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Je te conseille de changer ton fusil d’épaule et d’essayer une approche différente.","en":"I advise you to change your stance and to try a different approach.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"D’abord il faut mettre un peu d’huile de coude, travailler davantage,","en":"First you need to use a little elbow grease and to work harder,","lit":"add; oil; more"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"mais en utilisant des nouveaux outils pédagogiques pour surmonter les difficultés.","en":"but using new teaching tools to overcome the difficulties.","lit":"pedagogical"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"On pense souvent qu’ils coûtent les yeux de la tête mais c’est faux !","en":"It’s often thought that they cost a fortune but that’s wrong!","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu peux même en avoir certains à l’œil. Mais il faut les utiliser.","en":"You can even get some of them free. But you have to use them.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Oh, quelle barbe ! J’en ai marre d’étudier continuellement.","en":"Oh, what a pain! I’m fed up with studying continuously.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est décourageant, peut-être, mais fais ce que je te dis maintenant.","en":"It may be disheartening, but do what I tell you now.","lit":"discouraging"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu seras bientôt diplômé et tu pourras dormir sur tes deux oreilles.","en":"You’ll soon have your diploma and you’ll be able to rest easy.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The adjective doux (fem. douce; adverb doucement) means gentle, sweet, mild, soft, etc. (Think dulcet.) Il fait un temps doux et sec, The weather is mild and dry. Ce savon laisse vos mains douces, This soap leaves your hands soft. The idiomatic expression faire les yeux doux (à) is the equivalent of to make eyes at someone. By extension it can mean to woo, to cosy up to, etc. L’équipe fait les yeux doux au jeune joueur marocain, The team is eyeing up the young Moroccan player."},{"ref":"2","text":"avoir les dents longues (“to have long teeth”) means to be ambitious: Il ira loin, Jean, il a les dents longues, Jean will go far. He’s ambitious. Be careful: it is not the translation of to be long in the tooth, translated by ne pas être tout jeune (“not to be very young”)."},{"ref":"3","text":"avoir un coup de cœur (pour quelque chose / quelqu’un) (“have a blow of the heart”) means to fall in love with or have a crush on (something or someone) or, more abstractly, to be taken with. A common usage of the noun un coup de cœur is as a synonym for a favourite. Nos coups de cœur de cette semaine, This week’s favourites."},{"ref":"4","text":"The adjective sourd means deaf. Faire la sourde oreille, to turn a deaf ear to. (French can use adjectives as nouns, which is not always possible in English: un sourd, a deaf person, un jeune, a young person, etc. We will see more examples in another lesson.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"la tête, the head, is used in many French idioms. With the reflexive form of the verb creuser, to dig, se creuser la tête is the equivalent of to rack one’s brains. Je me suis creusé la tête mais je ne m’en souviens pas, I’ve racked my brains but I can’t remember. By contrast, se casser la tête (“to break one’s head”) means to worry or fret. The verb is often used in the expression Ne te casse / vous cassez pas la tête, Don’t worry (about it)."},{"ref":"6","text":"prendre ses jambes à son cou (“take one’s legs to one’s neck”) paints a cartoonish image of a person running away quickly: to take to one’s heels. Note that, unlike many other idioms, this one does not use the reflexive form of the verb."},{"ref":"7","text":"Another idiom that resembles its English equivalent: mettre les pieds dans le plat, to put one’s foot in it. Spot the little differences: a plural noun (pieds) and an extra bit of detail (le plat, the dish)."},{"ref":"8","text":"Despite the final -s, le dos, the back (lesson 48), like le bras (lesson 61), is singular and does not change in the plural. The expression faire froid dans le dos means to send shivers down the spine. It can be used impersonally (Ça fait froid dans le dos) or with an indirect object pronoun: Ce qu’ils ont trouvé leur fait froid dans le dos, What they’ve found sends shivers down their spine."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here are two more French words that have passed into English: une épaule, a shoulder (think of an epaulette, a shoulder-worn decoration) and un fusil, a gun (a fusilier, a type of soldier). The idiom changer son fusil d’épaule (“to change one’s gun to the other shoulder”) means to shift one’s stance, to have a change of heart: Le ministre n’est pas prêt à changer son fusil d’épaule au sujet de la santé, The minister isn’t prepared to shift his stance on the health issue."},{"ref":"10","text":"Many idioms are formed from un œil / les yeux. If something is obtained à l’œil, it is free (tradespeople used to judge a person’s eligibility for free credit by casting an eye over them). By contrast, if something is exorbitantly expensive, il coûte les yeux de la tête, it costs the earth (“eyes of the head”). Interestingly, British English has integrated the American expression to cost an arm and a leg, which French has now transformed into coûter un bras."},{"ref":"11","text":"une barbe, a beard (the origin of barber) is used as an exclamation: La barbe ! / Quelle barbe !, What a drag! Whence the idiomatic adjective barbant, boring."},{"ref":"12","text":"If you sleep soundly, you hear nothing. This is probably the origin of dormir sur ses deux oreilles (“to sleep on both ears”), which also means to rest easy. And while English-speakers sleep like a log, a francophone will dormir comme une souche, literally “to sleep like a tree stump”!"},{"ref":"","text":"We’re now beginning to learn the kinds of idiomatic expressions that are used on a regular basis by native speakers. Some of the ones in this lesson will look familiar; others may surprise you. But once you get the hang of them (vous en prenez l’habitude), you’ll be speaking truly colloquial French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Tu peux dormir sur tes deux oreilles. Demain, il fera doux et sec.","a":"You can rest easy. Tomorrow it will be fine and dry."},{"q":"Je pense que j’ai mis les pieds dans le plat. J’ai dit que Max n’est pas tout jeune.","a":"I think I’ve put my foot in it. I said that Max was long in the tooth."},{"q":"Ne te casse pas la tête, mon frère peut me donner un coup de main.","a":"Don’t worry, my brother can give me a hand."},{"q":"Il faut faire davantage pour aider les sourds à surmonter les obstacles qu’ils rencontrent.","a":"We have to do more to help deaf people overcome the hurdles that they meet."},{"q":"Mon patron n’a pas levé le petit doigt pour m’aider ! C’est très décourageant.","a":"My boss didn’t lift a finger to help me. It’s very disheartening."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We’re tearing our hair out because the mayors are turning a deaf ear to our problems.","t":"Nous {{nous arrachons les cheveux}} car les maires {{font la sourde oreille}} à nos difficultés."},{"cue":"I fell in love with his last book, even if some of the texts are over my head.","t":"J’ai eu {{un coup de cœur}} pour son dernier livre, {{même si}} certains textes me {{passent au-dessus de la tête}}."},{"cue":"Maurice wants to succeed; he’s very ambitious. – Yes, but he’s a bit long in the tooth.","t":"Maurice veut réussir ; il {{a les dents longues}}. – Oui, mais {{il n’est pas tout jeune}}."},{"cue":"I’m racking my brains to find a solution. – It’s easy: use some elbow grease.","t":"Je {{me creuse la tête}} pour trouver une solution. – C’est simple : mettez {{de l’huile de coude}}."},{"cue":"You’ve got to grit your teeth and smile. – The idea sends a shiver down my spine.","t":"Il faut {{serrer les dents}} et sourire. – L’idée {{me fait froid dans le dos}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai eu un coup de cœur pour…","en":"I fell in love with… / I was taken with…","zh":"我对……一见钟情；特别喜欢","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai eu un coup de cœur pour le métier de programmateur – eh oui, ça arrive –","exEn":"I was taken with the job of programmer – oh yes, it happens –","src":3},{"fr":"il a les dents longues","en":"he is ambitious","zh":"他野心勃勃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"not “long in the tooth” (= pas tout jeune)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ira loin, Jean, il a les dents longues.","exEn":"Jean will go far. He’s ambitious.","src":0},{"fr":"j’ai fait la sourde oreille","en":"I turned a deaf ear","zh":"我装作没听见","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et j’ai fait la sourde oreille à ceux qui me déconseillaient de poursuivre.","exEn":"and I turned a deaf ear to those who advised me against continuing.","src":4},{"fr":"prendre ses jambes à son cou","en":"to take to one’s heels","zh":"拔腿就跑","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai envie de prendre mes jambes à mon cou et fuir l’école,","exEn":"I want to take to my heels and flee the school,","src":9},{"fr":"mettre les pieds dans le plat","en":"to put one’s foot in it","zh":"说错话；闯祸","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais j’ai peur de mettre les pieds dans le plat parce que mon prof est très sympa.","exEn":"but I’m scared to put my foot in it because my teacher is very nice.","src":10},{"fr":"ne pas lever le petit doigt","en":"not to lift a finger","zh":"一点忙都不帮","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Par contre, les autres étudiants ne lèvent pas le petit doigt pour m’aider.","exEn":"By contrast, the other students don’t lift a finger to help me.","src":11},{"fr":"serrer les dents","en":"to grit one’s teeth","zh":"咬紧牙关","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je pourrais serrer les dents et leur demander de l’aide, mais l’idée me fait froid dans le dos.","exEn":"I could grit my teeth and ask them for help, but the idea sends a shiver down my spine.","src":12},{"fr":"l’idée me fait froid dans le dos","en":"the idea sends a shiver down my spine","zh":"这个想法让我背脊发凉","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je pourrais serrer les dents et leur demander de l’aide, mais l’idée me fait froid dans le dos.","exEn":"I could grit my teeth and ask them for help, but the idea sends a shiver down my spine.","src":12},{"fr":"me donner un coup de main","en":"to give me a hand","zh":"帮我一把","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si seulement quelqu’un voulait me donner un coup de main…","exEn":"If only someone would give me a hand…","src":13},{"fr":"ne te casse pas la tête","en":"don’t worry about it","zh":"别费心；别操心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Doucement ! Ne te casse pas la tête.","exEn":"Take it easy! Don’t worry about it.","src":14},{"fr":"changer son fusil d’épaule","en":"to change one’s stance","zh":"改变立场；改变策略","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je te conseille de changer ton fusil d’épaule et d’essayer une approche différente.","exEn":"I advise you to change your stance and to try a different approach.","src":15},{"fr":"un peu d’huile de coude","en":"a little elbow grease","zh":"多下点功夫","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord il faut mettre un peu d’huile de coude, travailler davantage,","exEn":"First you need to use a little elbow grease and to work harder,","src":16},{"fr":"ça coûte les yeux de la tête","en":"it costs the earth","zh":"贵得要命","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On pense souvent qu’ils coûtent les yeux de la tête mais c’est faux !","exEn":"It’s often thought that they cost a fortune but that’s wrong!","src":18},{"fr":"quelle barbe !","en":"what a drag!","zh":"真烦人！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oh, quelle barbe ! J’en ai marre d’étudier continuellement.","exEn":"Oh, what a pain! I’m fed up with studying continuously.","src":20},{"fr":"il faut travailler davantage","en":"you have to work more","zh":"必须更加努力","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord il faut mettre un peu d’huile de coude, travailler davantage,","exEn":"First you need to use a little elbow grease and to work harder,","src":16}],"uncertain":["dialogue 3: “programmateur” kept as printed (book text; English says “programmer”)","dialogue 7/8: OCR placed number 8 oddly; split as 7 = “Je me creuse la tête pour essayer de comprendre,” and 8 = “mais c’est souvent impossible…” following the English","card 2 ex is from note 2, src 0"],"pdfPage":353,"gaps":[[1.8,3.94],[5.78,8.62],[12.74,13.78],[16.48,17.54],[24.56,25.62],[29.5,30.5],[67.52,69.14],[77.5,78.68],[86.2,87.2],[94.5,95.6],[99.24,103],[105.2,107.16],[111.22,114.24],[117.96,120.88],[123.76,126.78],[131.68,134.62],[138.26,142.1]],"dur":142.1},{"n":63,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous avons vécu en Bretagne quand nous étions jeunes.","en":"We lived in Brittany when we were young.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Jacques enseignait le français et moi, je travaillais dans le tourisme.","en":"Jacques taught French and I worked in tourism.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous comprenez le breton, alors ?","en":"So you understand Breton?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, je n’y comprends rien. Pourtant, j’ai essayé de l’apprendre quand j’y habitais.","en":"No, I don’t understand a thing. And yet I tried to learn it when I lived there.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’aimais beaucoup cette région, même s’il faisait souvent un froid de canard !","en":"I used to like the region a lot, even though it was often freezing cold!","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Les gens étaient très sympathiques : ils nous ont accueillis les bras ouverts,","en":"The people were very nice: they welcomed us with open arms,","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"même s’ils n’ont pas levé le petit doigt pour nous aider avec leur langue !","en":"even if they didn’t lift a finger to help us with their language!","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai commencé à prendre des leçons, mais j’ai laissé tomber au bout de quelques mois.","en":"I started to take lessons, but I dropped them after a few months.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Il fallait continuer ! Il ne faut jamais baisser les bras.","en":"You should have continued! You must never give up.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, ça ne valait pas la peine parce que je savais que nous n’allions rester que deux ou trois années.","en":"No, it wasn’t worth it because I knew that we were going to stay only two or three years.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"En fait, nous avons dû revenir à Paris au bout d’un an. Ensuite, nous sommes repartis en Corse.","en":"In fact, we had to come back to Paris after a year. Then we went off to Corsica.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai eu tout de suite un coup de cœur pour l’île de Beauté : c’est un des plus beaux endroits du monde.","en":"I fell in love straight away with the Isle of Beauty: it’s one of the most beautiful places in the world.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 L’imparfait: the imperfect tense","text":"The imperfect is used to describe actions performed regularly or for an extended period in the past, with no mention of when they began or ended. (The tense owes its name to the fact such actions are not “perfected,” or completed.) There is no direct equivalent in English. Depending on the context, the imperfect can be translated by the past continuous (I was teaching) or the past habitual (I used to teach, I would teach). In some cases, particularly descriptions, the best equivalent is the past simple.\nThe imperfect for all three verb groups (including irregular verbs) is formed from the first person plural of the present tense, deleting the -ons ending and adding -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, and -aient to the stem. Here are examples from the three groups:\nenseigner, to teach ➔ enseign-\nj’enseignais   nous enseignions\ntu enseignais   vous enseigniez\nil/elle enseignait   ils/elles enseignaient\nfinir, to finish ➔ finiss-\nje finissais   nous finissions\ntu finissais   vous finissiez\nil/elle finissait   ils/elles finissaient\nsavoir, to know ➔ sav-\nje savais   nous savions\ntu savais   vous saviez\nil/elle savait   ils/elles savaient\nThe only exception is être:\nj’étais   nous étions\ntu étais   vous étiez\nil/elle était   ils/elles étaient\nThere are two special cases: the impersonal verb falloir (see section 2) and the defective verb pleuvoir, to rain, are both used in the third person singular only: il fallait; il pleuvait.\nThe negative and interrogative forms of the imperfect are formed in the usual way: tu ne connaissais pas ?; est-ce que tu connaissais ? / connaissais-tu ?, etc.\nIn some cases, the stem changes very slightly. In the case of -er verbs like lancer, to throw (lesson 59), the c takes a cedilla before the letter a: il lançait (if not, the c would be a hard k). Likewise, in verbs such as manger, to eat, and déranger, to disturb, the g becomes ge before a and o: tu mangeais, il dérangeait (otherwise, the g would be a hard g).\nThe imperfect tense is used for continuous or habitual actions in the past, notably those that, in English, are in the past continuous (Elle vivait à Lyon à l’époque, She was living in Lyon at the time). It is used particularly in sentences where a continuing action is interrupted by a single action in the past:\nElle dormait quand son téléphone a sonné.\nShe was sleeping when her phone rang.\nOne very common use is narration:\nQuand nous habitions à Grenoble, j’enseignais le français et mon ami travaillait à Colombe. Nous avions beaucoup de chance car nous habitions à trente kilomètres de la montagne. En été, j’allais faire de la randonnée toutes les semaines.\nIn this case, the imperfect is translated by the past simple tense or with would because the actions are all habitual:\nWhen we lived in Grenoble, I taught French and my friend worked at Colombe. We were very lucky because we lived thirty kilometres away from the mountains. In summer, I would / used to go hiking every week.\nNote the difference between En été, j’allais faire de la randonnée toutes les semaines and this sentence: En été, je suis allé faire de la randonnée quatre fois, In summer, I went hiking four times. In both cases, there is a repetitive action, but the first sentence describes a habitual (and less precise) action."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 valoir and falloir","text":"These two irregular verbs, which are sometimes confused, are used idiomatically. Let’s take a look:\n• valoir\nPresent / Future / Imperfect / Past\nje/j’   vaux   vaudrai   valais   ai valu\ntu   vaux   vaudras   valais   as valu\nil/elle   vaut   vaudra   valait   a valu\nnous   valons   vaudrons   valions   avons valu\nvous   valez   vaudrez   valiez   avez valu\nils/elles   valent   vaudront   valaient   ont valu\nValoir means to be worth:\nCette maison vaut deux millions, This house is worth two million.\nThe derived noun, value, is la valeur:\nJe n’ai aucune idée de la valeur de ce produit.\nI have no idea of the value of this product.\nPecuniary value aside, valoir conveys the notion of merit or quality:\nQue vaut ce nouveau jeu ? How good is this new game?\nCes nouvelles mesures ne valent pas grand-chose.\nThese new measures don’t mean very much.\nIn this context, the impersonal idiom valoir par is used to emphasise the main quality of something, expressed in a noun:\nCet hôtel vaut par son ambiance.\nThe good thing about this hotel is its atmosphere.\nThe impersonal third-person singular, vaut, is coupled with the comparative mieux, better, to talk about something preferable or more desirable:\nIl vaut mieux partir de bonne heure.\nIt’s better to leave early.\nAnother common impersonal use is the expression Ça vaut la peine, It’s worth it, or, more idiomatically, Ça vaut le coup.\nThere are many other idiomatic uses of valoir, some of which we will see in future lessons, but the underlying theme is that of worth or value. Try to learn as many as you can: Ça vaut le coup !\n• falloir (with a double l) is an impersonal verb whose basic meaning is to need or to be necessary. It is conjugated only in the third person singular:\nPresent / Future / Imperfect / Past\nil faut   faudra   fallait   a fallu\nThe verb is impersonal – it can never take a subject – and is generally followed by a noun or an infinitive, just like need. The translation depends, as always, on the context. For example, Il faut beaucoup d’argent pour voyager can be translated formally with one or informally with the impersonal “you”: One needs / You need a lot of money to travel. Another possibility is to use the passive: A lot of money is needed, etc.\nTo personalise falloir, add an indirect object pronoun (me, lui, etc.) before the conjugated form: Il me/lui faut de l’argent (see lessons 44 line 3 and 53 line 5). And just as American English uses need to when giving instructions, so French uses falloir:\nIl vous faut tourner à gauche.\nYou need to take a left.\nIn the interrogative, the inverted question form is very common, particularly in a polite register. When shopping, for instance, you might be asked: Vous faut-il autre chose ? Do you need anything else? rather than Est-ce qu’il vous faut autre chose ?\nNaturally, falloir is used in idiomatic expressions, many of which concern the idea of appropriate action. For example:\nIl a dépensé une fortune ! – Il faut ce qu’il faut.\nHe spent a fortune! – Whatever it takes (“it’s necessary what is necessary”).\nAnother concise expression – so concise that it has been imported into English – is comme il faut, suggesting proper or necessary behaviour:\nLe travail doit être fait comme il faut.\nThe work has to be done properly.\nKeep an eye out for other falloir expressions, and make a note of them."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 When to pronounce the “s” in plus","text":"The pronunciation, or not, of the final s in the adverb and noun plus depends, among other things, on the word’s grammatical function and position in the sentence or expression.\nA basic rule is that s is voiced if plus has a positive meaning and unvoiced if negative.\n• Pronounced\nThe s is pronounced [plewss] when plus means more:\nIl faut plus de moyens… More resources are needed…\nSimilarly, at the end of a sentence:\nCe n’est pas assez ; il faut un peu plus.\nIt’s not enough; you need a little more.\n… and as a noun:\nCe stage est un plus pour ta carrière.\nThat course is a plus for your career.\nMore generally, it is pronounced – always as a [z] – when followed by an adjective starting with a vowel: plus intéressant (liaison: [plewz-…]).\n• Silent\nThe s is silent ([plew]) when plus is used in the negation ne… plus or, at the end of a phrase in non plus:\nNous n’avons plus d’argent, We have no more money.\n– Et nous n’en avons pas, non plus, And we have none either.\nLikewise, it is silent in the comparative form if the adjective it precedes starts with a consonant: Ils sont plus grands, They are bigger. However, in the intensifier de plus en plus, the first s is pronounced (as a z) to make the liaison: [duh plewz-…-plew].\nIn short, it is very important to remember the grammatical function of plus: Est-ce qu’il y a un peu plus ([plewss]) de lait ? – Non, je n’en ai plus ([plew])."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Conjunctive adverbs (pourtant, cependant, etc.)","text":"Conjunctive adverbs can modify or qualify the statements or sentences they precede or follow. Here are some of the ones you have met so far:\n• Pourtant is used to contrast or contradict, in the same way as yet, however, still, or even so.\nJe ne la connais pas bien. Pourtant, je lui fais confiance.\nI don’t know her well, and yet I trust her.\nThe contrast is often highlighted with mais:\nC’est difficile à croire mais pourtant vrai.\nIt’s hard to believe and yet it’s true.\nor, especially, et:\n« Et pourtant elle tourne ! » disait Galilée.\n“And yet it moves (‘turns’),” said Galileo.\nThe adverb is often used before the verb, rather than after it, or at the beginning of the phrase, as in English:\nIl faut pourtant le faire.\nIt has to be done even so / And yet it has to be done.\n• Cependant has almost exactly the same meaning but is more formal than pourtant. Most importantly, it is not preceded by et:\nC’est difficile à croire, cependant c’est vrai.\nIt’s hard to believe, and yet it’s true (see above).\n• Néanmoins is almost a direct translation of nevertheless (néant means nothingness):\nJ’espère néanmoins que vous vous êtes amusés.\nI hope you had fun nevertheless.\n• Alors has several meanings. As an adverb of consequence, it means so or then. In the same way that many English speakers begin a sentence with So…, francophones will say:\nAlors, tu viens ?, So, are you coming?\nThe adverb is frequently used in exclamations, too:\nAlors ça, ça me fait plaisir, Now that makes me happy.\nBut alors also refers back to the past, like at that time, back then:\nJe l’ai connu en 2018. Il était alors mon directeur.\nI knew him in 2018. At that time, he was my director.\nIn every case, the translation will depend on the context, the register and the person speaking. (And remember, there may be no precise English equivalent.) We recommend that you note down any examples of usage that surprise you."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Idioms (1): body parts","text":"Idioms are a vital part of a language, but they can be hard to understand because their overall meaning is different from the literal meaning of the individual words they are composed of. Because of the similarities between French and English, some idioms are common to both languages, as seen in lesson 62 (s’arracher les cheveux); others are almost the same but vary slightly (mettre les pieds dans le plat); and still others are either totally different (avoir les dents longues) or have no direct equivalent (à l’œil).\nOne of the best ways of learning idioms is to group them by category. In the last few lessons, we saw some of those in the “body part” category:\naccueillir (quelqu’un) les bras ouverts – welcome (someone) with open arms\nbaisser les bras – give up (abandon)\ncoûter un bras – cost an arm and a leg\ns’arracher les cheveux – tear one’s hair out\navoir un coup de cœur – fall in love with / be smitten by\nmettre de l’huile de coude – use elbow grease\nserrer les dents – grit one’s teeth\nne pas lever le doigt – not lift a finger\nmettre le doigt sur quelque chose – put one’s finger on something\nfaire froid dans le dos – make one’s blood run cold\nchanger son fusil d’épaule – change one’s stance\nprendre ses jambes à son cou – take to one’s heels\ndonner un coup de main (à quelqu’un) – give (someone) a hand\navoir quelque chose à l’œil – get something for free\ndormir sur ses deux oreilles – sleep soundly\nfaire la sourde oreille – turn a deaf ear\nmettre les pieds dans le plat – put one’s foot in it\nse casser la tête – worry / fret about\nse creuser la tête – rack one’s brains\npasser au-dessus de la tête (de quelqu’un) – go over (someone’s) head\ncoûter les yeux de la tête – cost the earth\nfaire les yeux doux (à) – make eyes (at)\nObviously, the exact translation will depend on the context, but the equivalents above are standard."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"je n’y comprends rien","en":"I don’t understand a thing","zh":"我一点也不懂","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, je n’y comprends rien. Pourtant, j’ai essayé de l’apprendre quand j’y habitais.","exEn":"No, I don’t understand a thing. And yet I tried to learn it when I lived there.","src":4},{"fr":"pourtant","en":"and yet, however","zh":"然而；可是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne la connais pas bien. Pourtant, je lui fais confiance.","exEn":"I don’t know her well, and yet I trust her.","src":0},{"fr":"même s’il faisait souvent un froid de canard","en":"even though it was often freezing cold","zh":"尽管常常冷得要命","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"J’aimais beaucoup cette région, même s’il faisait souvent un froid de canard !","exEn":"I used to like the region a lot, even though it was often freezing cold!","src":5},{"fr":"au bout de quelques mois","en":"after a few months","zh":"几个月之后","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai commencé à prendre des leçons, mais j’ai laissé tomber au bout de quelques mois.","exEn":"I started to take lessons, but I dropped them after a few months.","src":8},{"fr":"il ne faut jamais baisser les bras","en":"you must never give up","zh":"绝不能放弃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il fallait continuer ! Il ne faut jamais baisser les bras.","exEn":"You should have continued! You must never give up.","src":9},{"fr":"ça ne valait pas la peine","en":"it wasn’t worth it","zh":"那不值得","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, ça ne valait pas la peine parce que je savais que nous n’allions rester que deux ou trois années.","exEn":"No, it wasn’t worth it because I knew that we were going to stay only two or three years.","src":10},{"fr":"nous avons dû revenir à Paris","en":"we had to come back to Paris","zh":"我们不得不回到巴黎","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En fait, nous avons dû revenir à Paris au bout d’un an. Ensuite, nous sommes repartis en Corse.","exEn":"In fact, we had to come back to Paris after a year. Then we went off to Corsica.","src":11},{"fr":"il vaut mieux partir de bonne heure","en":"it’s better to leave early","zh":"最好早点出发","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il vaut mieux partir de bonne heure.","exEn":"It’s better to leave early.","src":0}],"uncertain":["notes section 3: the book’s respelling of nasal sounds (e.g. for plus intéressant, de plus en plus) was too garbled in OCR; replaced by “[plewz-…]”","section 1: “travaillait à Colombe” kept as printed (place name)"],"pdfPage":359,"gaps":[[1.88,3.58],[4.84,7.56],[28.82,29.96],[38.48,39.62],[60.84,61.94],[67.48,71.1]],"dur":71.1},{"n":64,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je crains le pire…","titleEn":"I fear the worst…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Entrez, entrez monsieur. Est-ce que je peux vous renseigner ?","en":"Come in, come in sir. Can I give you some information?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Il me faut des vacances parce que je suis épuisé. Qu’est-ce que vous pouvez me proposer ?","en":"I need a holiday because I’m exhausted. What can you offer me?","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout dépend : combien de temps pouvez-vous partir ? À quelles conditions et à quel moment ?","en":"It all depends: how long can you go away for? On what terms and at what period?","lit":"conditions; moment"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Si vous partez avant le mois de juillet, normalement vous serez tranquille,","en":"If you leave before the month of July, normally you should have some peace and quiet,","lit":"will be tranquil"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"mais si vous attendez les grandes vacances, ça sera réellement l’enfer.","en":"but if you wait for the summer holiday, it really will be hellish.","lit":"big holidays"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout est bondé en cette période, les prix grimpent et les températures aussi.","en":"Everything is crowded during that period, prices climb and temperatures too.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Les moments les plus agréables sont soit début-juin soit fin-septembre.","en":"The nicest times are either early June or late September.","lit":"most agreeable moments"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce sont les moments où il y a le moins de monde. Pour moi, ce sont les plus beaux mois.","en":"These are the times when there are fewer people. For me, they are the nicest months.","lit":"moments"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Et quel est le pire mois de l’année, d’après vous : juillet ou août ?","en":"And what’s the worst month of the year, according to you: July or August?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Ils sont aussi mauvais l’un que l’autre, mais je pense qu’août est pire que juillet.","en":"Each is as bad as the other, but I think that August is worse than July.","lit":"they are as bad the one as the other"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"S’il fait beau, les plages sont saturées de touristes, et s’il fait mauvais on ne peut pas se baigner.","en":"If it’s fine, the beaches are inundated with tourists, and if the weather’s bad you can’t go swimming.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour éviter ça, si vous avez envie, vous pourrez essayer quelque chose de plus original.","en":"To avoid that, if you want to, you can try something more original.","lit":"will be able"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Par exemple, vous pourrez faire de la plongée sous-marine dans le Var,","en":"For example, you can go underwater diving in the Var,","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"ou bien de la randonnée à cheval dans le Massif central. C’est merveilleux à cette période de l’année.","en":"or horse trekking in the Massif Central. It’s wonderful at that time of year.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, aucun des deux ne me tente. Je ne sais pas nager,","en":"No, neither of the two tempts me. I can’t swim,","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"je ne sais pas monter à cheval, et je n’ai pas envie de prendre des cours non plus.","en":"I can’t ride, and I don’t want to take lessons, either.","lit":"mount a horse"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai une autre possibilité, qui est très reposante, très confortable mais pas donnée.","en":"I have another possibility, which is very relaxing, very comfortable, but not cheap.","lit":"not given"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Une semaine dans un grand hôtel, Le Domaine des Dieux, sur la Côte d’Azur.","en":"A week in a grand hotel, the Domain of the Gods, on the Riviera.","lit":"azure coast"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un des plus beaux palaces de la région.","en":"It’s one of the nicest luxury hotels in the region.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y a une offre de dernière minute avec une réduction exceptionnelle de deux pour cent.","en":"There is a last-minute offer with an exceptional reduction of two per cent.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Et c’est combien ? Je crains le pire…","en":"And how much is it? I fear the worst…","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"La formule tout compris est à dix mille euros, hors taxes pour quatre jours en demi-pension.","en":"The all-in package is ten thousand euros, before tax, for four days with half-board.","lit":"at; outside tax"},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Mm, je vois. Et des cours de plongée, ça serait combien ?","en":"Mm, I see. And how much would diving lessons cost?","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"renseigner is the verb from which the masculine noun renseignement (lesson 37 line 7) is derived. It means to give information or directions to. Pouvez-vous me renseigner ? is a formal way of asking for information. The question Est-ce que je peux / Puis-je vous renseigner ? is used by service industry staff to greet customers: Can I help you? The information desk in a public building is signposted RENSEIGNEMENTS."},{"ref":"2","text":"Here is the first, or “zero,” conditional which, like English, uses the present + future construction: Si vous partez maintenant, vous arriverez avant midi, If you leave now, you’ll arrive before noon. In some cases, the si clause introduces a simple observation, in which case the present is used in both parts of the sentence, and can be replaced by quand: S’il fait / Quand il fait mauvais, les plages sont vides, If / When the weather is bad, the beaches are empty."},{"ref":"3","text":"normalement, normally (lesson 8 line 4) is also used to express a condition: Il vient tout à l’heure ? – Normalement oui, Is he coming later? – Yes, he should do. In this context, it can be understood as under normal circumstances or if all goes well."},{"ref":"4","text":"The construction of a superlative clause depends on whether the adjective comes after the noun, which is the general rule, or before it (the BAGS rule, lesson 14). In the first case, the article is repeated: les mois les plus agréables; but not in the second: les plus beaux mois."},{"ref":"5","text":"après vous means after you, Je suis arrivé(e) après vous, I arrived after you. It is also a polite way of allowing someone to go ahead of you. But d’après with a noun or pronoun means according to: D’après Janet, il y a beaucoup de monde au mois d’août, According to Janet, August is very crowded."},{"ref":"6","text":"faire is used when talking about sports or physical activity, whereas in English we generally use to go: Je fais du ski deux fois par an, I go skiing twice a year. In other cases, faire du / de la / de l’ + sport can be translated by a single verb in English: faire de la natation, to go swimming OR to swim; faire du cheval, to go horse-riding OR to ride. Quand elle était au lycée, elle faisait de la natation tous les jours, When she was at school, she used to swim every day."},{"ref":"7","text":"Another difference with English is the use of savoir to translate the notion of ability (i.e. to know how to): Je ne sais pas nager et ma femme ne sait pas conduire, I can’t swim and my wife can’t drive."},{"ref":"8","text":"un cours (singular, despite the s) is a synonym of une leçon, a lesson. We can say un cours de français or une leçon de français."},{"ref":"9","text":"The idiom Ce n’est pas donné means It’s not cheap (“not given [away]”). And, as in our example, pas donné can be incorporated into a sentence: Leurs produits ne sont pas donnés mais ils sont de très bonne qualité, Their products aren’t cheap but the quality is very good."},{"ref":"10","text":"The technical term une formule, a formula, is frequently used in a commercial context to mean a package, an offering: Notre formule « tout compris » comprend les vols, les hôtels et les repas, Our all-in deal or package includes flights, hotels and meals. The term is commonly found in restaurants offering une formule déjeuner, the equivalent of the “English” term a prix fixe lunch."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(18) grand hôtel: Since h is a half-vowel, the pronunciation rule explained in lesson 16 applies here, i.e. the terminal d is pronounced [t]: [grahn-t-ohtel]."},{"ref":"","text":"Listen very carefully to the recordings and remember to repeat the dialogue aloud – along with the productive phase dialogue that goes with it."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Après vous, madame. – Non, après vous, monsieur. L’âge avant la beauté.","a":"After you, madam. – No, after you, sir. Age before beauty."},{"q":"Nous avons passé plusieurs jours dans un bel hôtel sur la côte, où j’ai pris des leçons de tennis.","a":"We spent several days in a nice hotel on the coast, where I took tennis lessons."},{"q":"Si tu pars tout de suite en voiture, tu arriveras avant eux. Mais sais-tu conduire ?","a":"If you leave immediately by car, you’ll arrive before they do. But can you drive?"},{"q":"Pouvez-vous me renseigner ? Est-ce que Madame Marceau est là ? – Normalement oui.","a":"Can you give me some information? Is Mrs Marceau here? – Yes, she should be."},{"q":"Si tu vas sur la Côte d’Azur tu pourras faire de la natation ou même de la plongée.","a":"If you go to the Riviera, you’ll be able to go swimming or even diving."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"It’s the least crowded and the most beautiful beach on the coast.","t":"C’est {{la plage la moins bondée}} et {{la plus belle}} de la côte."},{"cue":"If the weather is bad tomorrow, we won’t go to the beach.","t":"{{S’il fait mauvais}} demain, {{nous n’irons pas}} à la plage."},{"cue":"What time of the year should we go? – Don’t wait for the summer holidays: everything is packed.","t":"{{À quel moment}} de l’année {{faut-il partir}} ? – {{N’attendez pas}} les grandes vacances : {{tout est bondé}}."},{"cue":"They are each as bad as the other. – Yes, I fear the worst.","t":"Ils sont {{aussi mauvais l’un que l’autre}}. – Oui, je {{crains le pire}}."},{"cue":"If you want to do something original, you can try that all-in package.","t":"Si vous avez envie de {{faire quelque chose d’original}}, vous {{pouvez}} essayer {{cette formule « tout compris »}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Est-ce que je peux vous renseigner ?","en":"Can I give you some information? / Can I help you?","zh":"我能为您提供什么帮助吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Entrez, entrez monsieur. Est-ce que je peux vous renseigner ?","exEn":"Come in, come in sir. Can I give you some information?","src":1},{"fr":"il me faut des vacances","en":"I need a holiday","zh":"我需要度假","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il me faut des vacances parce que je suis épuisé. Qu’est-ce que vous pouvez me proposer ?","exEn":"I need a holiday because I’m exhausted. What can you offer me?","src":2},{"fr":"je suis épuisé","en":"I’m exhausted","zh":"我精疲力竭","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il me faut des vacances parce que je suis épuisé. Qu’est-ce que vous pouvez me proposer ?","exEn":"I need a holiday because I’m exhausted. What can you offer me?","src":2},{"fr":"tout dépend","en":"it all depends","zh":"这要看情况","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ depends","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout dépend : combien de temps pouvez-vous partir ? À quelles conditions et à quel moment ?","exEn":"It all depends: how long can you go away for? On what terms and at what period?","src":3},{"fr":"normalement vous serez tranquille","en":"normally you should have peace and quiet","zh":"通常您会很清静","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ normally, tranquil","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si vous partez avant le mois de juillet, normalement vous serez tranquille,","exEn":"If you leave before the month of July, normally you should have some peace and quiet,","src":4},{"fr":"les grandes vacances","en":"the summer holidays","zh":"暑假；长假","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"mais si vous attendez les grandes vacances, ça sera réellement l’enfer.","exEn":"but if you wait for the summer holiday, it really will be hellish.","src":5},{"fr":"tout est bondé","en":"everything is crowded","zh":"到处都挤满了人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout est bondé en cette période, les prix grimpent et les températures aussi.","exEn":"Everything is crowded during that period, prices climb and temperatures too.","src":6},{"fr":"les prix grimpent","en":"prices are climbing","zh":"价格攀升","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Tout est bondé en cette période, les prix grimpent et les températures aussi.","exEn":"Everything is crowded during that period, prices climb and temperatures too.","src":6},{"fr":"d’après vous","en":"according to you","zh":"依您看；据您所说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"d’après vous ≠ après vous (= after you)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et quel est le pire mois de l’année, d’après vous : juillet ou août ?","exEn":"And what’s the worst month of the year, according to you: July or August?","src":9},{"fr":"aussi mauvais l’un que l’autre","en":"as bad as each other","zh":"半斤八两；一样糟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont aussi mauvais l’un que l’autre, mais je pense qu’août est pire que juillet.","exEn":"Each is as bad as the other, but I think that August is worse than July.","src":10},{"fr":"je ne sais pas nager","en":"I can’t swim","zh":"我不会游泳","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, aucun des deux ne me tente. Je ne sais pas nager,","exEn":"No, neither of the two tempts me. I can’t swim,","src":15},{"fr":"je n’ai pas envie de prendre des cours","en":"I don’t want to take lessons","zh":"我不想上课","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"je ne sais pas monter à cheval, et je n’ai pas envie de prendre des cours non plus.","exEn":"I can’t ride, and I don’t want to take lessons, either.","src":16},{"fr":"ce n’est pas donné","en":"it’s not cheap","zh":"价格不便宜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai une autre possibilité, qui est très reposante, très confortable mais pas donnée.","exEn":"I have another possibility, which is very relaxing, very comfortable, but not cheap.","src":17},{"fr":"une réduction exceptionnelle","en":"an exceptional reduction","zh":"特别优惠","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ reduction, exceptional","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Il y a une offre de dernière minute avec une réduction exceptionnelle de deux pour cent.","exEn":"There is a last-minute offer with an exceptional reduction of two per cent.","src":20},{"fr":"je crains le pire","en":"I fear the worst","zh":"我担心最坏的情况","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et c’est combien ? Je crains le pire…","exEn":"And how much is it? I fear the worst…","src":21}],"uncertain":["pron note (18): respelling of “grand hôtel” rewritten as [grahn-t-ohtel] (OCR garbled)","note 5: example name “Janet” read from OCR “Jarne!”","note 2 states “first, or zero, conditional” as printed in the book"],"pdfPage":369,"gaps":[[2.08,4.3],[5.86,8.82],[88.76,89.86],[115.04,118.5],[120.72,122.98],[128.06,131],[135.9,138.96],[143.44,146.56],[151.66,154.5],[159.28,163.7]],"dur":163.7},{"n":65,"kind":"lesson","title":"Cent pour cent des gagnants ont tenté leur chance !","titleEn":"100% of winners tried their luck!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Voici une bonne occasion de partir en vacances gratuitement !","en":"Here’s a good opportunity to go on holiday for free!","lit":"freely"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"La bibliothèque municipale organise un jeu-concours avec un tirage au sort des billets gagnants.","en":"The municipal library is organising a competition with a random draw of the winning tickets.","lit":"game-competition; of fate"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Si tu gagnais le premier prix, tu pourrais passer une semaine aux sports d’hiver.","en":"If you won the first prize, you could spend a week at winter sports.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"J’irais tous les ans à la montagne si je pouvais. Pas toi ?","en":"I would go to the mountains every year if I could. Wouldn’t you?","lit":"not you"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’y suis jamais allée, et je dois dire que ça ne m’attire pas vraiment.","en":"I’ve never been, and I must say that it doesn’t really attract me.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"J’aurais besoin d’équipements spéciaux et je suppose qu’ils me coûteraient une fortune.","en":"I would need special equipment and I suppose that it would cost me a fortune.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas nécessairement : on peut soit les acheter, soit les louer, et la location serait beaucoup moins chère.","en":"Not necessarily: you can either buy it or rent it, and rental would be much cheaper.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Si tu voulais, tu pourrais demander à Amir et Yasmine s’ils accepteraient de te prêter le nécessaire.","en":"If you wanted to, you could ask Amir and Yasmine if they would agree to lend you what you need.","lit":"the necessary"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis sûr qu’ils diraient oui. De quoi d’autre aurais-tu besoin ?","en":"I’m sure that they would say yes. What else would you need?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout, absolument tout. Il me faudrait une combinaison, des gants, des chaussures…","en":"Everything, absolutely everything. I would need a ski suit, gloves, boots…","lit":"combination; shoes"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vois ce que tu veux dire ! Te faut-il autre chose ?","en":"I see what you mean! Do you need anything else?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Si j’allais dans une station à la mode, comme celles en Suisse,","en":"If I went to a fashionable resort, like the ones in Switzerland,","lit":"station; those"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"il me faudrait au moins un nouveau tailleur ainsi qu’un ensemble décontracté pour l’après-ski.","en":"I would need at least another suit and a casual outfit for the après-ski.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Je devrais me renseigner pour la tenue vestimentaire, d’ailleurs.","en":"I should enquire about the dress code, actually.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Penses-tu que les stations françaises sont aussi huppées que les stations suisses ?","en":"Do you think that the French resorts are as posh as the Swiss resorts?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Elles sont pareilles, je crois. Elles sont toutes fréquentées par les mêmes millionnaires !","en":"They’re the same, I believe. They are all frequented by the same millionaires!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Et si tu allais ailleurs ? Si tu avais la possibilité, où choisirais-tu d’aller ?","en":"And if you went somewhere else? If you had the possibility, where would you choose to go?","lit":"and"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’en sais rien. Qu’est-ce que tu me conseillerais, toi ?","en":"I’ve no idea. What would you advise me?","lit":"I don’t of-it know nothing"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Je serais prête à aller n’importe où.","en":"I’d be willing to go anywhere.","lit":"ready"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Si j’étais toi, je choisirais une petite station quelque part dans les Pyrénées.","en":"If I were you, I would choose a small resort somewhere in the Pyrenees.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais, avant de faire tous ces projets, tu devrais acheter un billet pour le jeu…","en":"But before making all these plans, you should buy a ticket for the game…","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"tirer means to pull or to draw: Tirez les rideaux et laissez entrer la lumière, Draw the curtains and let the light in. Used with the noun le sort, fate (with definite article changing to the preposition au), it means to draw lots, to make a random draw: Les élèves ont tiré au sort quatre gagnants, The students drew four winners at random."},{"ref":"2","text":"This is the second conditional, expressing the possible result of a hypothetical action. Pouvoir is an irregular verb, the conditional mood being je pourrais, tu pourrais, il/elle pourrait, nous pourrions, vous pourriez, ils/elles pourraient. In an if…then sentence, French uses the imperfect tense (never the conditional) after si, instead of the past simple in English: Il pourrait t’aider si tu voulais, He could help you if you wanted."},{"ref":"3","text":"The present conditional of aller is j’irais, tu irais, il/elle irait, nous irions, vous iriez, ils/elles iraient. There is no need for an auxiliary (e.g. would). The first person singular is pronounced identically to the future form (j’irai), i.e. the final s is silent. The same rule applies to all verbs."},{"ref":"4","text":"The conditional of avoir is j’aurais, tu aurais, il/elle aurait, nous aurions, vous auriez, ils/elles auraient. (Remember j’aurais is a homophone of the future form j’aurai.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"With most ordinary verbs, the conditional is formed by adding the endings of the imperfect tense to the infinitive. Thus the conditional of coûter is je coûterais, tu coûterais, il/elle coûterait, nous coûterions, vous coûteriez, ils/elles coûteraient."},{"ref":"6","text":"The conditional of être is je serais, tu serais, il/elle serait, nous serions, vous seriez, ils/elles seraient."},{"ref":"7","text":"You’ll find the conditional of vouloir in lesson 70. See also lesson 45 note 6."},{"ref":"8","text":"For the conditional of -re verbs, the final e is dropped (like for the future): Thus dire ➔ je dirais, tu dirais, il/elle dirait, nous dirions, vous diriez, ils/elles diraient."},{"ref":"9","text":"The conditional of the defective verb falloir (see lesson 63 note 2) is il faudrait."},{"ref":"10","text":"la mode generally means fashion (clothing, etc., see lessons 33 and 61), so the adjectival phrase à la mode means fashionable, trendy: Les investissements verts sont à la mode cette année, Green investments are fashionable this year."},{"ref":"11","text":"The masculine adjective pareil (fem. pareille) means the same or similar. Les deux mots sont pareils, The two words are the same. The basic difference between pareil and même is that the former is used alone while the latter requires an article and a noun, usually chose. Tu viens jeudi ou vendredi ? – Pour moi, c’est pareil / c’est la même chose. The expression C’est du pareil au même (“It’s from the same to the same”) means It’s the same thing, etc. Lire ou écouter un livre audio, c’est du pareil au même, Reading or listening to an audio book is six of one, half a dozen of the other."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(16) pareille: the feminine and the masculine (pareil) are pronounced identically in both the singular and the plural: [parayy]."},{"ref":"","text":"Les Pyrénées, the Pyrenees, is a range of mountains forming a natural border between France and Spain and spanning several départements, including les Pyrénées-Orientales (“eastern Pyrenees”), les Hautes-Pyrénées (“high Pyrenees”) and les Pyrénées-Atlantiques (bordering the Atlantic Ocean). One section of the range is a beautiful national park, le parc national des Pyrénées."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Si nous avions les investissements nécessaires, nous pourrions commencer le projet demain.","a":"If we had the necessary investments, we could begin the project tomorrow."},{"q":"Droite, gauche, centre, c’est du pareil au même. Les gens en ont marre de la politique.","a":"Right, left, centre, it’s all the same. People are fed up with politics."},{"q":"Je pense que notre méthode pourrait être utilisée avec des étudiants étrangers.","a":"I think that our method could be used with foreign students."},{"q":"Je ne pense pas que tu gagneras un prix mais tu devrais quand même tenter ta chance.","a":"I don’t think that you’ll win a prize, but you should try your luck anyway."},{"q":"Ça serait une bonne occasion de tirer des leçons pour l’avenir, n’est-ce pas ?","a":"It would be a good opportunity to learn lessons for the future, wouldn’t it?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"If I were you, sir, I would ask the opinion of a colleague. It could help you to choose.","t":"{{Si j’étais vous}}, monsieur, je {{demanderais}} l’avis d’un collègue. Cela {{pourrait}} vous aider {{à choisir}}."},{"cue":"You men, you’re all the same. – But women, too, are all the same.","t":"Vous {{les hommes}}, vous êtes {{tous pareils}} ! – Mais {{les femmes}} aussi {{sont toutes pareilles}} !"},{"cue":"They would need a special key but it would cost a fortune.","t":"Ils {{auraient besoin}} d’une clé {{spéciale}} mais ça {{coûterait}} une fortune."},{"cue":"Amir and I would be willing to go anywhere. What would you advise us?","t":"Amir et moi {{serions prêts à}} aller {{n’importe où}}. Qu’est-ce que {{vous nous conseilleriez}} ?"},{"cue":"What would your friends say if they knew that we were not married?","t":"Qu’est-ce qu’{{ils diraient}}, tes amis, s’ils {{savaient}} que nous {{ne sommes pas}} mariés ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"une bonne occasion","en":"a good opportunity","zh":"一个好机会","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ occasion","ff":"occasion = opportunity (also “second-hand deal”), not only “occasion”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici une bonne occasion de partir en vacances gratuitement !","exEn":"Here’s a good opportunity to go on holiday for free!","src":1},{"fr":"gratuitement","en":"for free","zh":"免费地","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ gratuitous / gratis","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Voici une bonne occasion de partir en vacances gratuitement !","exEn":"Here’s a good opportunity to go on holiday for free!","src":1},{"fr":"un tirage au sort","en":"a random draw","zh":"抽签；抽奖","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La bibliothèque municipale organise un jeu-concours avec un tirage au sort des billets gagnants.","exEn":"The municipal library is organising a competition with a random draw of the winning tickets.","src":2},{"fr":"ça ne m’attire pas vraiment","en":"it doesn’t really attract me","zh":"我并不是很感兴趣","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’y suis jamais allée, et je dois dire que ça ne m’attire pas vraiment.","exEn":"I’ve never been, and I must say that it doesn’t really attract me.","src":5},{"fr":"j’aurais besoin de…","en":"I would need…","zh":"我会需要……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’aurais besoin d’équipements spéciaux et je suppose qu’ils me coûteraient une fortune.","exEn":"I would need special equipment and I suppose that it would cost me a fortune.","src":6},{"fr":"ça coûterait une fortune","en":"it would cost a fortune","zh":"那要花一大笔钱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ cost a fortune","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ils auraient besoin d’une clé spéciale mais ça coûterait une fortune.","exEn":"They would need a special key but it would cost a fortune.","src":0},{"fr":"la location","en":"rental, renting","zh":"租赁；租金","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ location (but means rental)","ff":"location = rental, NOT location/place","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas nécessairement : on peut soit les acheter, soit les louer, et la location serait beaucoup moins chère.","exEn":"Not necessarily: you can either buy it or rent it, and rental would be much cheaper.","src":7},{"fr":"te prêter le nécessaire","en":"to lend you what you need","zh":"把你需要的东西借给你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si tu voulais, tu pourrais demander à Amir et Yasmine s’ils accepteraient de te prêter le nécessaire.","exEn":"If you wanted to, you could ask Amir and Yasmine if they would agree to lend you what you need.","src":8},{"fr":"une station à la mode","en":"a fashionable resort","zh":"一个时髦的度假胜地","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"station = (ski) resort here, not only “station”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si j’allais dans une station à la mode, comme celles en Suisse,","exEn":"If I went to a fashionable resort, like the ones in Switzerland,","src":12},{"fr":"d’ailleurs","en":"besides, actually, by the way","zh":"另外；再说","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “dye-yur” – a hair dye in the corner of the room, “by the way”, you notice the colour elsewhere","ex":"Je devrais me renseigner pour la tenue vestimentaire, d’ailleurs.","exEn":"I should enquire about the dress code, actually.","src":14},{"fr":"elles sont pareilles","en":"they are the same","zh":"它们都一样","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Elles sont pareilles, je crois. Elles sont toutes fréquentées par les mêmes millionnaires !","exEn":"They’re the same, I believe. They are all frequented by the same millionaires!","src":16},{"fr":"c’est du pareil au même","en":"it’s all the same","zh":"都一样；半斤八两","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Droite, gauche, centre, c’est du pareil au même. Les gens en ont marre de la politique.","exEn":"Right, left, centre, it’s all the same. People are fed up with politics.","src":0},{"fr":"je n’en sais rien","en":"I have no idea","zh":"我一点也不知道","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’en sais rien. Qu’est-ce que tu me conseillerais, toi ?","exEn":"I’ve no idea. What would you advise me?","src":18},{"fr":"n’importe où","en":"anywhere","zh":"任何地方","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je serais prête à aller n’importe où.","exEn":"I’d be willing to go anywhere.","src":19},{"fr":"tu devrais quand même tenter ta chance","en":"you should try your luck anyway","zh":"你还是应该试试运气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne pense pas que tu gagneras un prix mais tu devrais quand même tenter ta chance.","exEn":"I don’t think that you’ll win a prize, but you should try your luck anyway.","src":0}],"uncertain":["dialogue 2: “jeu-concours” hyphen inferred from the English gloss “(game-)competition”","ex2 item 3: gap “spéciale” inferred; the answer key gives “auraient besoin - spéciale - coûterait”","pron note (16): note header in the book attached to “pareilles”; text reworded"],"pdfPage":375,"gaps":[[1.96,4],[6.68,9.62],[67.92,68.92],[101.34,104.68],[107.38,109.5],[113.9,116.92],[123.22,126.26],[130.1,133.22],[137.4,140.14],[144.06,147.78]],"dur":147.8},{"n":66,"kind":"lesson","title":"Président d’un jour","titleEn":"President for a day","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"Les élèves d’un lycée lyonnais sont invités à participer au débat sur le climat à l’Assemblée nationale,","en":"The students from a high school in Lyon are invited to take part in the debate on the climate in the National Assembly,","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"avec, comme thème, « Que feriez-vous si vous étiez Président de la République ? »","en":"on the theme “What would you do if you were President of the Republic?”","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"« Mesdames et messieurs les députés, j’invite Mademoiselle Noura Bouris à prendre la parole.","en":"Fellow Members of Parliament, I invite Ms Noura Bouris to take the floor.","lit":"ladies and gentlemen the Deputies; word"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Alors, Noura, si vous étiez présidente, que feriez-vous pour combattre le changement climatique ?","en":"Well, Noura, if you were president, what would you do to fight climate change?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Est-ce qu’il serait possible, à votre avis, de prendre rapidement des mesures efficaces ? »","en":"Would it be possible, in your opinion, to quickly take effective measures?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous croyons que oui, et il faudrait en effet agir vite car nous sommes tous en danger.","en":"We believe so, and we should in fact act quickly because we are all in danger.","lit":"that yes"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Que feriez-vous ? Interdiriez-vous à tous les véhicules à essence et diesel de rouler en ville ?","en":"What would you do? Would you prohibit all petrol and diesel vehicles from driving in cities?","lit":"rolling; in town"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’interdirais pas les petits véhicules utilitaires car les artisans ont besoin de se déplacer,","en":"I wouldn’t prohibit small goods vehicles because artisans need to travel,","lit":"utility; move themselves"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"mais je ne permettrais pas aux camions de livraison de circuler pendant la journée.","en":"but I wouldn’t allow delivery trucks to drive during the day.","lit":"circulate"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Et que feriez-vous pour approvisionner les magasins ? Interdiriez-vous n’importe quel camion","en":"And what would you do to supply the shops? Would you prevent any lorry","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"à n’importe quelle heure, n’importe où dans la ville ? Ça ne serait pas très pratique.","en":"at any time, anywhere in town? That wouldn’t be very practical.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-être, mais je n’aimerais pas que mes enfants respirent constamment de l’air pollué.","en":"Perhaps, but I wouldn’t like my children to constantly breathe polluted air.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout le monde est contre les voitures particulières, mais elles sont un mal nécessaire, non ?","en":"Everybody is against passenger cars, but they are a necessary evil, aren’t they?","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous ne sommes pas contre les voitures, monsieur, mais contre le réchauffement planétaire.","en":"We’re not against cars, sir, but against global warming.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Tous les véhicules polluent mais certains sont pires que d’autres, et de loin.","en":"All vehicles pollute but some are worse than others, and by far.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Et, au bout d’un certain temps, les résultats seront catastrophiques.","en":"And, after a certain time, the results will be disastrous.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut à tout prix réduire notre empreinte carbone.","en":"It’s necessary to reduce our carbon footprint at all costs.","lit":"price"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"N’importe quoi ! Si nous savions comment éliminer les gaz à effet de serre, nous l’aurions fait il y a longtemps.","en":"Nonsense! If we knew how to eliminate greenhouse gases, we would have done it a long time ago.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous avons la nette impression que vous ne nous écoutez pas.","en":"We have the distinct impression that you are not listening to us.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais si, mais si, ma petite. Sinon, nous ne vous aurions pas invités aujourd’hui.","en":"Yes, yes, my little one. Otherwise, we wouldn’t have invited you here today.","lit":"but yes, but yes"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, deux autocars attendent dehors pour vous ramener à la maison.","en":"Now, two coaches are waiting outside to take you home.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est bien ce que nous pensions ! Vous ne nous prenez pas au sérieux.","en":"That’s exactly what we thought! You don’t take us seriously.","lit":"well"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know that adjectives do not take an initial capital. This applies not only to nationalities (lesson 65: la Suisse ➔ suisse) but also to adjectives of location, here the city of Lyon ➔ lyonnais. Like all adjectives, agreement is obligatory: les écoles lyonnaises, schools in Lyon. But remember that, with an indefinite article (often preceded by un(e)), such words are nouns, and therefore take a capital: Notre magazine est lu par un Lyonnais sur deux, Our magazine is read by one Lyonnais in two. (See Culture Note.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"The third interrogative (inversion) is commonly used with the conditional form because it is easier on the ear than the est-ce que form (qu’est-ce que vous feriez), which is nevertheless used."},{"ref":"3","text":"une parole, like un mot, means a word, but refers more to the underlying thoughts or meaning than to the words themselves. Combien de mots y a-t-il dans cette phrase ? How many words are there in this sentence? but Je te donne ma parole, I give you my word. Prendre la parole (“to take the word”) is a formal way of saying to speak or to take the floor, usually in a public forum."},{"ref":"4","text":"As in English, croire, to believe, can be a synonym for penser, followed by que if used in a clause: Je pense / crois qu’il était surpris, I think / believe that he was surprised. The relative is also used when either verb is followed by oui or non: Je crois / pense que non, I believe / think not."},{"ref":"5","text":"interdire means to forbid (the origin of the English technical word interdict) and is usually used in the impersonal form with de: Il est interdit de fumer dans la plupart des lieux publics, Smoking is forbidden in most public places. If the verb has a direct object, however, it needs the preposition à: Je vais interdire à mon fils d’utiliser son téléphone le soir, I’m going to forbid my son to use his phone in the evening. The past participle, interdit, is used in warning signs: INTERDIT DE VAPOTER, NO VAPING."},{"ref":"6","text":"circuler literally means to circulate: L’air circule partout dans la chambre, Air circulates throughout the bedroom. But the verb is used more broadly in the sense of to walk or move around, or, for traffic, to drive: N’oubliez pas qu’en Grande-Bretagne, on circule à droite, Don’t forget that in Great Britain, they drive on the right. (Thus la circulation can mean traffic (see lesson 44): La circulation est dense ce soir, Traffic is heavy this evening.) The difference with conduire, seen in the previous lesson, is that circuler is intransitive."},{"ref":"7","text":"In lesson 65 line 19, we saw the indefinite pronominal expression n’importe où, anywhere. N’importe can be followed by other prepositions or pronouns: Choisissez n’importe quelle carte, Choose any card. N’importe qui peut entrer dans mon bureau, Anyone can come into my office. In such cases, the basic meaning is any. N’importe quoi ! is a dismissive exclamation: Nonsense!"},{"ref":"8","text":"We know that un mal can mean a pain, an ache, harm, etc. (lesson 34 note 3). But the root is le mal, evil (think: malevolent, etc.). So un mal nécessaire is a necessary evil. Less diabolically, le mal means wrong: le bien et le mal, right and wrong or even just a difficulty, Nous avons réussi à la joindre, mais non sans mal, We managed to get hold of her, but not without difficulty."},{"ref":"9","text":"certain means not only certain (as in line 16: un certain temps, a certain time) but, more broadly, some. In this case, the adjective must agree with its noun: Certaines personnes, notamment des écologistes, sont végétariennes, Some people, particularly ecologists, are vegetarian. In some cases, either translation is appropriate (dans certains cas, in some / certain cases), but you should be aware of the basic difference."},{"ref":"10","text":"net (feminine nette) means neat or tidy (see nettoyer, to clean, lesson 30 line 9 and lesson 36 note 8). By extension, the adjective can mean clear, sharp or precise. Je n’aime pas les photos numériques car les images sont moins nettes, I don’t like digital photos because the pictures are less sharp."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(19) net is irregular because the final t is pronounced, exactly the same as the feminine nette: [net]."},{"ref":"","text":"Inhabitants of villages, towns and cities throughout France can all be referred to by a gentilic, derived from the place name and usually formed by adding the suffix -ien(ne) or -ais(e): un(e) Parisien(ne), un(e) Lyonnais(e), etc. When mayors make a formal address to their fellow citizens, they will begin with these proper nouns (always putting the feminine first): « Parisiennes, Parisiens, je compte sur vous ». The nearest English equivalent would be “My fellow Parisians, I am counting on you.”\nl’Assemblée nationale (National Assembly) is the lower house of France’s two-chamber parliament. Its lawmakers, les député(e)s, sit in le Palais Bourbon, the official seat, on the left bank of the River Seine. (The upper house is le Sénat, as we saw in lesson 54.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je vois ce qu’il veut dire : ses explications sont claires et nettes.","a":"I see what he means: his explanations are clear and precise."},{"q":"Qu’est-ce que tu ferais si tu étais millionnaire ? – Rien du tout !","a":"What would you do if you were a millionaire? – Nothing at all!"},{"q":"Je pense que les députés ne prennent pas au sérieux la jeune écologiste.","a":"I think that the members of parliament don’t take the young ecologist seriously."},{"q":"J’ai eu du mal à lui faire comprendre qu’il faut agir vite et interdire le tabac au bureau.","a":"I had difficulty getting him to understand that it’s necessary to act quickly and outlaw tobacco in the office."},{"q":"Je ne devrais plus conduire ma voiture. De toute façon, on ne peut plus circuler dans Paris.","a":"I shouldn’t drive my car any more. In any case, you can no longer drive around Paris."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The [female] MP said “If I knew how to reduce our carbon footprint, I would do it immediately.”","t":"Madame {{la Députée}} a dit « {{Si je savais comment réduire}} notre empreinte carbone, {{je le ferais}} immédiatement »."},{"cue":"The internet did not exist back then, and we didn’t even have a phone.","t":"Internet {{n’existait pas à cette époque}} et {{nous n’avions même pas}} de téléphone."},{"cue":"You could do these exercises at any time and on any day if you wanted to.","t":"Tu {{pourrais faire}} ces exercices {{à n’importe quelle heure}} et {{n’importe quel}} jour si tu {{voulais}}."},{"cue":"We wouldn’t like it if our children breathed this polluted air every day.","t":"{{Nous n’aimerions pas}} que nos enfants respirent {{cet air}} pollué {{tous}} les jours."},{"cue":"But I am glad to see you, Jacques. Otherwise, I wouldn’t have invited you.","t":"{{Mais si}}, je suis content de te voir, Jacques. {{Sinon, je ne t’aurais pas invité}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"prendre la parole","en":"to take the floor, to speak","zh":"发言","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mesdames et messieurs les députés, j’invite Mademoiselle Noura Bouris à prendre la parole.","exEn":"Fellow Members of Parliament, I invite Ms Noura Bouris to take the floor.","src":3},{"fr":"à votre avis","en":"in your opinion","zh":"依您看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Est-ce qu’il serait possible, à votre avis, de prendre rapidement des mesures efficaces ?","exEn":"Would it be possible, in your opinion, to quickly take effective measures?","src":5},{"fr":"il faudrait agir vite","en":"we should act quickly","zh":"应该迅速行动","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous croyons que oui, et il faudrait en effet agir vite car nous sommes tous en danger.","exEn":"We believe so, and we should in fact act quickly because we are all in danger.","src":6},{"fr":"les artisans ont besoin de se déplacer","en":"artisans need to travel around","zh":"手艺人需要出行","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’interdirais pas les petits véhicules utilitaires car les artisans ont besoin de se déplacer,","exEn":"I wouldn’t prohibit small goods vehicles because artisans need to travel,","src":8},{"fr":"circuler","en":"to drive, to move around (traffic)","zh":"（车辆）行驶；通行","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ circulate","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"mais je ne permettrais pas aux camions de livraison de circuler pendant la journée.","exEn":"but I wouldn’t allow delivery trucks to drive during the day.","src":9},{"fr":"n’importe quoi !","en":"nonsense!","zh":"胡说八道！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’importe quoi ! Si nous savions comment éliminer les gaz à effet de serre, nous l’aurions fait il y a longtemps.","exEn":"Nonsense! If we knew how to eliminate greenhouse gases, we would have done it a long time ago.","src":18},{"fr":"je n’aimerais pas que mes enfants respirent de l’air pollué","en":"I wouldn’t like my children to breathe polluted air","zh":"我不希望我的孩子呼吸污染的空气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ pollute","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peut-être, mais je n’aimerais pas que mes enfants respirent constamment de l’air pollué.","exEn":"Perhaps, but I wouldn’t like my children to constantly breathe polluted air.","src":12},{"fr":"un mal nécessaire","en":"a necessary evil","zh":"必要之恶","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ necessary evil","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout le monde est contre les voitures particulières, mais elles sont un mal nécessaire, non ?","exEn":"Everybody is against passenger cars, but they are a necessary evil, aren’t they?","src":13},{"fr":"le réchauffement planétaire","en":"global warming","zh":"全球变暖","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous ne sommes pas contre les voitures, monsieur, mais contre le réchauffement planétaire.","exEn":"We’re not against cars, sir, but against global warming.","src":14},{"fr":"au bout d’un certain temps","en":"after a certain time","zh":"过了一段时间之后","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et, au bout d’un certain temps, les résultats seront catastrophiques.","exEn":"And, after a certain time, the results will be disastrous.","src":16},{"fr":"il faut à tout prix réduire notre empreinte carbone","en":"we must reduce our carbon footprint at all costs","zh":"我们必须不惜一切代价减少碳足迹","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ carbon","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il faut à tout prix réduire notre empreinte carbone.","exEn":"It’s necessary to reduce our carbon footprint at all costs.","src":17},{"fr":"nous avons la nette impression que","en":"we have the distinct impression that","zh":"我们清楚地感觉到……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ impression","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons la nette impression que vous ne nous écoutez pas.","exEn":"We have the distinct impression that you are not listening to us.","src":19},{"fr":"mais si","en":"yes, of course (contradicting a negative)","zh":"怎么不是呢（反驳否定）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais si, mais si, ma petite. Sinon, nous ne vous aurions pas invités aujourd’hui.","exEn":"Yes, yes, my little one. Otherwise, we wouldn’t have invited you here today.","src":20},{"fr":"vous ne nous prenez pas au sérieux","en":"you don’t take us seriously","zh":"你们没把我们当回事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ seriously","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est bien ce que nous pensions ! Vous ne nous prenez pas au sérieux.","exEn":"That’s exactly what we thought! You don’t take us seriously.","src":22},{"fr":"j’ai eu du mal à lui faire comprendre","en":"I had difficulty getting him to understand","zh":"我费了好大劲才让他明白","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai eu du mal à lui faire comprendre qu’il faut agir vite et interdire le tabac au bureau.","exEn":"I had difficulty getting him to understand that it’s necessary to act quickly and outlaw tobacco in the office.","src":0}],"uncertain":["dialogue 3: surname “Bouris” (French OCR) vs “Souris” (English OCR); kept “Bouris”","dialogue 3/5: the book’s opening and closing quotation marks around the president’s speech placed on lines 3 and 5 (OCR shows only a stray mark)","ex2 item 5: English gap “Mais si” supplied from answer key"],"pdfPage":383,"gaps":[[1.86,3.9],[5.06,8.04],[43.92,44.92],[115.84,119.38],[121.34,123.16],[127.28,130.34],[134.24,137.44],[140.56,143.6],[148.84,151.82],[156.66,160.38]],"dur":160.4},{"n":67,"kind":"lesson","title":"L’encre la plus pâle…","titleEn":"The palest ink…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"Madame Claudie Marchand est rentrée à la maison lundi dernier après avoir passé un mois à l’hôpital.","en":"Mrs Claudie Marchand came home last Monday after having spent a month in hospital.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Claudie est toujours coincée dans son appartement au douzième étage, la pauvre.","en":"Claudie is still stuck in her flat on the twelfth floor, the poor thing.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment ça ?","en":"How so?","lit":"how that"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"L’ascenseur est tombé en panne il y a trois semaines et il ne marche toujours pas.","en":"The lift broke down three weeks ago and it still isn’t working.","lit":"fell in breakdown"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est pénible – surtout pour elle, qui n’est pas sortie de chez elle depuis trois jours.","en":"It’s annoying – especially for her, who hasn’t left her house for three days.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Quand je suis rentré hier, je suis monté la voir pour savoir si elle avait besoin de quelque chose.","en":"When I got back yesterday, I went up to see if she needed anything.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai rentré ses plantes, qui étaient sur le balcon depuis un mois et demi, et j’ai descendu sa poubelle.","en":"I brought in her plants, which had been on the balcony for a month and a half, and I took down her rubbish bin.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai même sorti son petit chien pour le promener.","en":"I even took out her little dog to walk it.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi, je suis passée la voir ce matin. J’ai monté quelques courses et son courrier.","en":"I went by to see her this morning. I brought up some errands and her mail.","lit":"passed"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Après, j’ai passé l’aspirateur, fait un peu de ménage et rangé le désordre.","en":"After, I passed the vacuum cleaner, did a bit of housework and cleared up the mess.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Magali, sa voisine du dessus, est descendue la voir et elles ont bavardé pendant une bonne heure.","en":"Magali, her upstairs neighbour, came down to see her and they chatted for a good hour.","lit":"of above"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis sûr que, à son âge, Claudie n’a pas d’ordinateur.","en":"I’m sure that, at her age, Claudie doesn’t have a computer.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Sinon, elle aurait pu entrer sa liste de courses dans une appli et demander à être livrée à domicile.","en":"Otherwise, she could have entered her shopping list into an app and asked to be delivered at home.","lit":"domicile"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu serais surpris ! Madame Marchand est très bien équipée pour une sénior.","en":"You’d be surprised! Mrs Marchand is very well equipped for a senior.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"La première fois que je suis allée chez elle, je suis entrée dans une petite pièce à côté de la cuisine","en":"The first time I went to her place, I went into a little room next to the kitchen","lit":"that I went at her"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"et j’ai vu qu’elle avait un nouveau portable, une imprimante, et même une connexion Internet.","en":"and I saw that she had a new laptop, a printer and even an internet connection.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Le problème, c’est qu’après un mois à l’hôpital, Claudie a oublié ses identifiants.","en":"The problem is that after a month in hospital, Claudie has forgotten her login details.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis retournée avant-hier et j’ai regardé partout, sur les étagères,","en":"I went back the day before yesterday and I looked everywhere, on the shelves,","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"dans ses classeurs et ses boîtes de rangement, mais je n’ai rien trouvé.","en":"in her filing cabinets and storage boxes, but I didn’t find anything.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"En dernier ressort, j’ai retourné son tapis de souris.","en":"As a last resort, I turned over her mouse mat.","lit":"in last resort; carpet"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Bingo ! Les codes et les mots de passe étaient collés là-dessous.","en":"Bingo! The codes and passwords were glued underneath.","lit":"there-under"},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Comme on dit : « L’encre la plus pâle vaut mieux que la meilleure mémoire » !","en":"As they say: “The palest ink is worth more than the best memory”!","lit":"better"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"A handful of verbs can be conjugated with both avoir and être, but with a slight change of meaning. In the intransitive form, rentrer means to go / come back: Tu es rentré(e) tard hier soir ! You came back late last night! The transitive form means to bring in: As-tu rentré ta voiture au garage ? Did you bring your car into the garage?"},{"ref":"2","text":"The interrogative Comment ça se fait ? (lesson 57 note 1) is often shortened to Comment ça ? in idiomatic French when asking for an explanation: Il n’est pas dans son bureau. – Comment ça ? He’s not in his office. – How come? Other, similar forms can be used, notably Où ça ? – Regarde ce petit chien ! – Où ça ? Look at that little dog! – Whereabouts?"},{"ref":"3","text":"The adjective pénible, from la peine (lesson 46 note 8), describes something that is difficult, laborious, etc. Le voyage en avion était pénible, The flight was difficult. When used to describe a person, it means tiresome, irritating, etc. Qu’est-ce qu’il est pénible !, He’s so annoying!"},{"ref":"4","text":"Used intransitively for to go out (lesson 35 section 1.3), sortir is conjugated with être (Elle est sortie, She’s gone out). The transitive form – note the direct object (clés) – means to put / take out: Il a sorti ses clés de sa poche, He took his keys from his pocket."},{"ref":"5","text":"The intransitive monter, to go up, has a similar meaning in the transitive form: to bring / take up: J’ai laissé mes lunettes en bas. Peux-tu les monter s’il te plaît ? I’ve left my glasses downstairs. Can you bring them up please? (Remember that monter à cheval means to ride.)"},{"ref":"6","text":"The opposite of monter is descendre, to get off / to go / come down (lesson 16 line 10). It follows the same pattern when used with a direct object: Pouvez-vous m’aider à descendre ma valise ? Can you help me bring down my suitcase?"},{"ref":"7","text":"We know the intransitive use of passer: À quelle heure passe le bus pour le centre-ville ?, What time does the bus for the town centre come by? The transitive form is self-evident: Passe-moi le savon, Hand me the soap. Remember the difference between passer and se passer, to happen (lesson 56 section 1)."},{"ref":"8","text":"The regular verb bavarder means to chat or, depending on the context, to gossip. On a bavardé un bon quart d’heure, We chatted for a good half hour. However, both the adjective (bavard(e)) and the noun (un(e) bavard(e)) are slightly more judgmental: Qu’est-ce qu’il est bavard !, What a chatterbox!"},{"ref":"9","text":"The transitive form of entrer is used, as in English, to mean to introduce or insert something: Entrez votre code PIN et validez, Enter your PIN and press OK."},{"ref":"10","text":"In the intransitive form, retourner (generally with à) means to return, to go back (to): Nous sommes retournés à notre hôtel après la réunion, We went back to our hotel after the meeting. The transitive verb means to turn over, up, etc.: Retournez le poisson au bout de deux minutes et ajoutez les légumes, Turn the fish over after two minutes and add the vegetables."},{"ref":"","text":"You will have noticed that we are gradually whittling down the pronunciation section because, by now, you should be familiar with most of the sounds in French, and, in particular, the various verb tenses. From now on, we will indicate only the new words that we introduce and any unusual or irregular pronunciations. You can gauge yourself just how much progress you have made so far! (If in doubt about how a particular word or group of words is pronounced, listen carefully to the recordings.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Le voisin du dessus n’a pas l’air sympa. – Et pourtant il est très gentil, et très bavard !","a":"The upstairs neighbour doesn’t seem very nice. – And yet he’s very pleasant and very chatty!"},{"q":"Je suis passé chez elle tout à l’heure, mais elle était en train de passer l’aspirateur.","a":"I stopped by her house earlier, but she was using the vacuum cleaner."},{"q":"Vous n’auriez pas dû entrer votre code PIN avant d’avoir validé votre commande.","a":"You shouldn’t have entered your PIN before confirming your order."},{"q":"Peux-tu m’aider à descendre ces six valises ? C’est tellement pénible !","a":"Can you help me take down these six suitcases? It’s so annoying!"},{"q":"J’ai monté le courrier et je l’ai mis quelque part dans leur bureau. – Où ça ?","a":"I took up the mail and I put it somewhere in their office. – Whereabouts?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Have you brought the car into the garage, darling? – Not yet. I only got back two minutes ago.","t":"{{As-tu rentré}} la voiture {{au garage}}, chéri ? – Pas encore. {{Je suis rentré il y a}} deux minutes seulement."},{"cue":"We went up to the fourth floor and I brought down the dustbin that had been there for two days.","t":"{{Nous sommes montés}} au quatrième étage et {{j’ai descendu}} la poubelle qui était là {{depuis deux jours}}."},{"cue":"I haven’t been out this morning because it is too cold. But my wife took the dog out.","t":"{{Je ne suis pas sorti}} ce matin, car il {{fait trop froid}}. Mais {{ma}} femme {{a sorti le chien}}."},{"cue":"What’s happening? – The bus is coming by in a few minutes. Hand me my coat.","t":"Qu’est-ce {{qui se passe}} ? – Le bus {{passe}} dans quelques minutes. {{Passe-moi mon}} manteau."},{"cue":"Remember: “The palest ink is better than the best memory”.","t":"{{Souviens}}-toi : « L’encre {{la plus pâle vaut mieux que la meilleure}} mémoire »."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"l’ascenseur est tombé en panne","en":"the lift broke down","zh":"电梯坏了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"L’ascenseur est tombé en panne il y a trois semaines et il ne marche toujours pas.","exEn":"The lift broke down three weeks ago and it still isn’t working.","src":4},{"fr":"comment ça ?","en":"how so? how come?","zh":"怎么会这样？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment ça ?","exEn":"How so?","src":3},{"fr":"c’est pénible","en":"it’s annoying, it’s a nuisance","zh":"真烦人；真麻烦","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"pénible ≠ painful: it means annoying/difficult","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est pénible – surtout pour elle, qui n’est pas sortie de chez elle depuis trois jours.","exEn":"It’s annoying – especially for her, who hasn’t left her house for three days.","src":5},{"fr":"elle avait besoin de quelque chose","en":"she needed something","zh":"她需要点什么","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quand je suis rentré hier, je suis monté la voir pour savoir si elle avait besoin de quelque chose.","exEn":"When I got back yesterday, I went up to see if she needed anything.","src":6},{"fr":"j’ai descendu sa poubelle","en":"I took down her rubbish bin","zh":"我把她的垃圾桶拿了下楼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"descendre + object takes avoir","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai rentré ses plantes, qui étaient sur le balcon depuis un mois et demi, et j’ai descendu sa poubelle.","exEn":"I brought in her plants, which had been on the balcony for a month and a half, and I took down her rubbish bin.","src":7},{"fr":"j’ai sorti son petit chien","en":"I took out her little dog","zh":"我带她的小狗出去","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"sortir + object takes avoir","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai même sorti son petit chien pour le promener.","exEn":"I even took out her little dog to walk it.","src":8},{"fr":"j’ai passé l’aspirateur","en":"I did the vacuuming","zh":"我吸了尘","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Après, j’ai passé l’aspirateur, fait un peu de ménage et rangé le désordre.","exEn":"After, I passed the vacuum cleaner, did a bit of housework and cleared up the mess.","src":10},{"fr":"faire un peu de ménage","en":"to do a bit of housework","zh":"做点家务","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Après, j’ai passé l’aspirateur, fait un peu de ménage et rangé le désordre.","exEn":"After, I passed the vacuum cleaner, did a bit of housework and cleared up the mess.","src":10},{"fr":"elles ont bavardé","en":"they chatted","zh":"她们聊了天","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Magali, sa voisine du dessus, est descendue la voir et elles ont bavardé pendant une bonne heure.","exEn":"Magali, her upstairs neighbour, came down to see her and they chatted for a good hour.","src":11},{"fr":"livrée à domicile","en":"delivered at home","zh":"送货上门","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ domicile","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Sinon, elle aurait pu entrer sa liste de courses dans une appli et demander à être livrée à domicile.","exEn":"Otherwise, she could have entered her shopping list into an app and asked to be delivered at home.","src":13},{"fr":"ses identifiants","en":"her login details","zh":"她的登录账号信息","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le problème, c’est qu’après un mois à l’hôpital, Claudie a oublié ses identifiants.","exEn":"The problem is that after a month in hospital, Claudie has forgotten her login details.","src":17},{"fr":"un mot de passe","en":"a password","zh":"密码","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bingo ! Les codes et les mots de passe étaient collés là-dessous.","exEn":"Bingo! The codes and passwords were glued underneath.","src":21},{"fr":"en dernier ressort","en":"as a last resort","zh":"作为最后手段","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ last resort","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En dernier ressort, j’ai retourné son tapis de souris.","exEn":"As a last resort, I turned over her mouse mat.","src":20},{"fr":"avant-hier","en":"the day before yesterday","zh":"前天","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Je suis retournée avant-hier et j’ai regardé partout, sur les étagères,","exEn":"I went back the day before yesterday and I looked everywhere, on the shelves,","src":18}],"uncertain":["dialogue 18/20: footnote markers for retourner appear as stray boxes in OCR; text read as “Je suis retournée avant-hier” and “j’ai retourné son tapis de souris”","note 8: French example reads “un bon quart d’heure” while the English in the OCR ends “…half hour”; kept both as printed","dialogue 14: “sénior” kept as printed"],"pdfPage":389,"gaps":[[1.92,4.14],[5.36,8.2],[14.18,15.42],[89.18,90.62],[120.4,123.84],[126.14,128.2],[134.22,136.9],[140.54,143.7],[147.7,150.62],[154.76,157.8],[161.58,165.36]],"dur":165.4},{"n":68,"kind":"lesson","title":"La visite de la petite-fille","titleEn":"The granddaughter’s visit","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Salut Papy, salut Mamie ! Ça fait longtemps que je ne vous ai pas vus !","en":"Hi Grandad, hi Granny! I haven’t seen you for a long time!","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous allez bien tous les deux ? En tout cas, vous avez bonne mine.","en":"Are you both well? In any case, you look well.","lit":"you have good look"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour ma petite ! C’est très gentil de nous rendre visite.","en":"Hello my dear! It’s very kind of you to visit us.","lit":"my little"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Ton grand-père et moi sommes tous les deux contents.","en":"Your grandfather and I are both happy.","lit":"and me"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"On te voit si peu ces jours-ci : tu dois être très occupée.","en":"We see so little of you these days: you must be very busy.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, je fais un tas de choses et je cours à droite et à gauche, comme toujours,","en":"Yes, I’m doing loads of things and I’m running around left, right and centre, as always,","lit":"a pile of things; to right and to left"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"mais dès que j’ai un moment, je viens vous voir, vous le savez bien.","en":"but as soon as I have a moment, I come and see you, as you know very well.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais tu viens toujours seule : tu n’as pas un amoureux ? Un petit-ami ? Ou même une petite-amie ?","en":"But you always come on your own: don’t you have a sweetheart? A boyfriend? Or even a girlfriend?","lit":"lover"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu sais bien que tu peux nous présenter qui que ce soit : nous sommes des vieux jeunes !","en":"You know very well that you can introduce us to anyone: we’re young old people!","lit":"old young"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Mamie, je t’en prie ! Chaque chose en son temps.","en":"Granny, please! Everything in its own time.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai une blague à te raconter : « Un muet dit à un sourd qu’un aveugle les espionne,","en":"I’ve got a joke to tell you: “A dumb person says to a deaf person that a blind person is spying on them,","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"en se cachant derrière les cheveux d’un chauve. » C’est drôle, non ?","en":"hiding behind the hair of a bald person.” That’s funny, isn’t it?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Papy, comment te dire… ? Je ne suis pas morte de rire.","en":"Grandad, how can I put this? I’m not dying of laughter.","lit":"say to-you"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu devrais savoir que notre vocabulaire a changé depuis que toi et Mamie étiez jeunes.","en":"You should know that our vocabulary has changed since you and Granny were young.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Par exemple, on ne dit plus un aveugle mais un « non-voyant » ;","en":"For instance, we no longer say “a blind person” but a “non-sighted person”;","lit":"seeing"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"et « un malentendant » est plus juste que « un sourd », tu comprends ?","en":"and “a hearing impaired person” is more accurate than “a deaf person”, you understand?","lit":"bad-hearer"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon, changeons de sujet : comment vas-tu rentrer à Paris ?","en":"Right, let’s change the subject: how are you going to get back to Paris?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Une amie m’a déposée ici en voiture mais je vais rentrer en train.","en":"A friend dropped me here by car but I’m going to go back by train.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Je crois qu’il y en a un vers dix-huit heures. Avez-vous un horaire ?","en":"I think that there is one around 6 pm. Do you have a timetable?","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui il circule toutes les heures dès treize heures. Je t’amènerai à la gare.","en":"Yes, it runs every hour from 1 pm onwards. I’ll take you to the station.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci, je reviendrai dès que possible, c’est promis. Au fait, peux-tu me prêter vingt euros ?","en":"Thanks, I’ll come back as soon as possible, I promise. By the way, can you lend me twenty euros?","lit":"it’s promised"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis temporairement sans ressources. Autrement dit, fauchée.","en":"I’m temporarily without resources. In other words, broke.","lit":"otherwise said"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"une petite-fille (masc. un petit-fils) means a granddaughter (resp. grandson). The hyphen is essential because both words are compound nouns; without it, une petite fille (un petit fils) would mean a small girl (resp. small son). The plural forms are petites-filles and petits-fils. There is almost no difference in pronunciation between the compound noun and the adjective + noun formation, in both the singular and the plural, so context is all-important."},{"ref":"2","text":"papy (or papi) et mamie are the equivalents of grandad and granny. As in English, the terms are sometimes used pejoratively (old man, old woman) or humorously: le papy boom, the grey boom (retirement of the baby boomers)."},{"ref":"3","text":"Ça fait… can introduce a time clause (lesson 37 line 1). On its own Ça fait longtemps means It’s been a long time. It can also be used in a sentence, usually with the present or the perfect: Ça fait longtemps que tu apprends le français ? Have you been learning French for long? Ça fait longtemps qu’elle a quitté son pays, She left her country a long time ago. Note how the clause comes at the beginning of the French sentence but the end of the English one."},{"ref":"4","text":"We know that there is no word for both (lesson 57 note 2). Where English uses the word as an adverb (both you and I, for example), we translate it as tous les deux / toutes les deux, generally after the verb: Michel et moi aimons tous les deux la campagne française, Both Michel and I love the French countryside. (Note that French uses the stressed pronoun moi, not the subject pronoun je.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"une mine refers to a person’s face or physical aspect: Elle a mauvaise mine. Qu’est-ce qu’elle a ? She doesn’t look well. What’s the matter with her? Ne faites pas cette mine triste, Don’t pull that sad face. (Une mine also means a mine, but the context will clear up any ambiguity.)"},{"ref":"6","text":"Adjectives can easily be used as singular nouns in French. Mon petit (fem. ma petite) is usually used affectionately to address a younger person, a bit like my dear. Other examples in this lesson of adjectives used as nouns include: Il est amoureux de toi, He’s in love with you ➔ C’est un amoureux de la musique, He’s a music-lover. In many cases, English needs to add a noun to convey the same idea: aveugle, blind ➔ un(e) aveugle, a blind man / woman; sourd, deaf ➔ un(e) sourd(e), a deaf man / woman. (This rule generally applies to descriptions of people or other living things.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"un tas means a heap or pile. Il y avait un grand tas de livres dans le coin de la chambre, There was a big pile of books in the corner of the bedroom. In a familiar register, though, un tas de means loads of or heaps of. J’ai un tas de choses à vous raconter, I’ve loads of things to tell you. The plural is des tas: J’ai des tas de choses…"},{"ref":"8","text":"Used in a time clause, the preposition dès – note the grave accent – means (as) from: L’agence ouvrira ses portes dès midi, The agency will open its doors from noon onwards. Dès always marks the start of the action but is not used in a from…to context (L’agence ouvrira ses portes de midi à dix-huit heures). Dès que means as soon as or as early as: Donne-moi des nouvelles dès que possible, Send me news as soon as possible."},{"ref":"9","text":"soit is the third person singular subjunctive of être (the equivalent of est in the present tense). The phrase… que ce soit, preceded by a pronoun like qui, quoi or où, has the indefinite sense of any + pronoun: La règle est la même, où que ce soit en France, The rule is the same, anywhere in France. We’ll learn more about the subjunctive later on."},{"ref":"10","text":"The verb faucher literally means to mow, reap or cut down. However, the adjectival participle fauché(e) is used idiomatically to mean broke or hard up. The link between the literal and metaphorical meanings is evident in the expression être fauché comme les blés, i.e. to be stony-broke."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(6) un tas: as usual, the final s is silent [ta]. Of course, the plural is pronounced identically."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(8) [puhteet-amee / puhteet-amee]. Both the masculine and the feminine forms have the same pronunciation so it is important to listen to the article (un / une)."},{"ref":"","text":"Imported from the US and the UK, terminology designed to avoid offence to particular groups of people has entered French. This mode of expression applies in particular to certain forms of physical and mental disadvantage (for example non-voyant, non-seeing, rather than aveugle, blind). Culturally sensitive language is increasingly common, particularly in the media, and is worth paying close attention to. In Canada, in particular, a major effort has been made to find appropriate terminology in French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Salut, Maxime ! Ça fait longtemps. – Oui, ça fait au moins deux ans.","a":"Hi Maxime! It’s been a long time. – Yes, it’s been at least two years."},{"q":"Nous verrons ça plus tard : chaque chose en son temps.","a":"We’ll see that later on: everything in its own time."},{"q":"Nous aimerions beaucoup t’accompagner à la soirée mais nous sommes fauchés.","a":"We would love to come with you to the party but we’re broke."},{"q":"Pouvez-vous nous amener à la gare ? Il n’y a plus de bus après vingt heures trente.","a":"Can you take us to the station? There are no more buses after 8.30 pm."},{"q":"Chaque fois que j’entends cette blague, je suis mort de rire.","a":"Each time I hear that joke, I die laughing."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Must one say “a blind person” or a “non-sighted person”?","t":"{{Doit-on}} dire « {{un aveugle}} » ou un « {{non-voyant}} » ?"},{"cue":"You’re not looking well. What’s the matter?","t":"Vous {{avez mauvaise mine}}. Qu’est-ce {{qu’il y a}} ?"},{"cue":"We’ll come back to see you as soon as possible. As from next week if all goes well.","t":"{{Nous reviendrons}} vous voir {{dès que}} possible. {{Dès la semaine prochaine}}, si tout va bien."},{"cue":"My wife and I both appreciate what you do.","t":"{{Ma femme et moi}} apprécions {{tous les deux ce que}} vous faites."},{"cue":"You’re running around, as always. Can I do anything to help you?","t":"Vous {{courez à droite et à gauche}}, comme toujours. Est-ce que je peux faire {{quoi que ce soit}} pour vous aider ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"ça fait longtemps que","en":"it’s been a long time since","zh":"好久没……了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Salut Papy, salut Mamie ! Ça fait longtemps que je ne vous ai pas vus !","exEn":"Hi Grandad, hi Granny! I haven’t seen you for a long time!","src":1},{"fr":"vous avez bonne mine","en":"you look well","zh":"您气色很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"mine here = look, appearance (not a coal mine)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous allez bien tous les deux ? En tout cas, vous avez bonne mine.","exEn":"Are you both well? In any case, you look well.","src":2},{"fr":"c’est très gentil de nous rendre visite","en":"it’s very kind of you to visit us","zh":"你来看我们真是太好了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bonjour ma petite ! C’est très gentil de nous rendre visite.","exEn":"Hello my dear! It’s very kind of you to visit us.","src":3},{"fr":"tous les deux","en":"both","zh":"两个都；两人都","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ton grand-père et moi sommes tous les deux contents.","exEn":"Your grandfather and I are both happy.","src":4},{"fr":"un tas de choses","en":"loads of things","zh":"许多事情","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, je fais un tas de choses et je cours à droite et à gauche, comme toujours,","exEn":"Yes, I’m doing loads of things and I’m running around left, right and centre, as always,","src":6},{"fr":"dès que j’ai un moment","en":"as soon as I have a moment","zh":"我一有空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"mais dès que j’ai un moment, je viens vous voir, vous le savez bien.","exEn":"but as soon as I have a moment, I come and see you, as you know very well.","src":7},{"fr":"chaque chose en son temps","en":"everything in its own time","zh":"凡事都有其时","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mamie, je t’en prie ! Chaque chose en son temps.","exEn":"Granny, please! Everything in its own time.","src":10},{"fr":"je t’en prie","en":"please, I beg you / you’re welcome","zh":"拜托；别客气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Mamie, je t’en prie ! Chaque chose en son temps.","exEn":"Granny, please! Everything in its own time.","src":10},{"fr":"comment te dire… ?","en":"how can I put this…?","zh":"我该怎么跟你说呢……？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Papy, comment te dire… ? Je ne suis pas morte de rire.","exEn":"Grandad, how can I put this? I’m not dying of laughter.","src":13},{"fr":"je suis mort de rire","en":"I’m dying of laughter","zh":"我笑死了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Chaque fois que j’entends cette blague, je suis mort de rire.","exEn":"Each time I hear that joke, I die laughing.","src":0},{"fr":"changeons de sujet","en":"let’s change the subject","zh":"我们换个话题吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon, changeons de sujet : comment vas-tu rentrer à Paris ?","exEn":"Right, let’s change the subject: how are you going to get back to Paris?","src":17},{"fr":"je reviendrai dès que possible","en":"I’ll come back as soon as possible","zh":"我会尽快回来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci, je reviendrai dès que possible, c’est promis. Au fait, peux-tu me prêter vingt euros ?","exEn":"Thanks, I’ll come back as soon as possible, I promise. By the way, can you lend me twenty euros?","src":21},{"fr":"peux-tu me prêter vingt euros ?","en":"can you lend me twenty euros?","zh":"你能借我二十欧元吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Merci, je reviendrai dès que possible, c’est promis. Au fait, peux-tu me prêter vingt euros ?","exEn":"Thanks, I’ll come back as soon as possible, I promise. By the way, can you lend me twenty euros?","src":21},{"fr":"autrement dit","en":"in other words","zh":"换句话说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis temporairement sans ressources. Autrement dit, fauchée.","exEn":"I’m temporarily without resources. In other words, broke.","src":22}],"uncertain":["dialogue 8: “petit-ami / petite-amie” hyphens kept as printed","dialogue 16: quotation « un sourd » kept as printed in the book"],"pdfPage":397,"gaps":[[2.06,4.32],[6.08,8.92],[21.34,22.42],[45.24,46.32],[51.1,52.16],[91.34,92.38],[112.68,116.82],[118.58,120.52],[124.98,128.22],[131.24,134.42],[137.92,141.12],[144.96,148.14],[151.22,155.12]],"dur":155.1},{"n":69,"kind":"lesson","title":"« Les animaux ne mentent pas… »","titleEn":"“Animals do not lie…”","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"As-tu le temps de manger un morceau avec moi ce midi ?","en":"Do you have time to get a bite to eat with me at noon?","lit":"eat a bite"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Je devais déjeuner avec mon fils, mais il m’a posé un lapin.","en":"I was supposed to have lunch with my son but he stood me up.","lit":"had to"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pense qu’il a d’autres chats à fouetter que de sortir avec son vieux papa.","en":"I think that he’s got other fish to fry rather than to go out with his old dad.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça serait avec le plus grand plaisir !","en":"With great pleasure!","lit":"it would be"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Il y a quelques jours, j’étais malade comme un chien.","en":"A few days ago, I was as sick as a dog.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"J’avais une fièvre de cheval et plein de courbatures.","en":"I had a raging temperature and I was aching all over.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je commençais à avoir le cafard parce que j’étais clouée au lit depuis une semaine.","en":"I was beginning to feel down in the dumps because I’d been stuck in bed for a week.","lit":"nailed"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Dehors, il faisait toujours gris et moche – un vrai temps de chien.","en":"Outside, it was always grey and dull – really lousy weather.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je m’ennuyais comme un rat mort et j’avais un appétit d’oiseau.","en":"I was bored to death and I was eating like a bird.","lit":"as a dead rat; appetite of bird"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Julien, mon voisin du dessous, venait parfois me rendre visite,","en":"Julien, my downstairs neighbour, sometimes came to see me,","lit":"of the underneath"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"mais il est bavard comme une pie et j’avais juste envie de dormir comme un loir.","en":"but he’s a chatterbox and I just wanted to sleep like a log.","lit":"a dormouse"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais depuis deux jours, je vais mieux","en":"But for the past two days I’ve been getting better","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"et je commence tout doucement à reprendre du poil de la bête.","en":"and I’m beginning quite slowly to pick up again.","lit":"retake the hair of the beast"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Surtout, j’ai une faim de loup !","en":"Above all, I’m famished!","lit":"hunger of wolf"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Je passe du coq à l’âne, mais savais-tu que Patrice a trouvé un nouveau boulot ?","en":"I’m jumping from one thing to another, but did you know that Patrice has found a new job?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est malin comme un singe, celui-là !","en":"He’s as smart as a whip, that one!","lit":"a monkey"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Il a l’air doux comme un agneau, mais il est vachement ambitieux. Qu’est-ce qu’il va faire ?","en":"He looks as gentle as a lamb, but he’s damned ambitious. What’s he going to do?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu ne devineras jamais. Vas-y : essaie !","en":"You’ll never guess. Go on, try!","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Je sais qu’il court toujours plusieurs lièvres à la fois et il peut tout faire.","en":"I know that he’s got several irons in the fire at once and he can do anything.","lit":"runs several hares"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Bijoutier ? Chirurgien ? Éboueur ? Chauffeur de taxi ? Jardinier ?","en":"Jeweller? Surgeon? Refuse collector? Taxi driver? Gardener?","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Non ? Alors je donne ma langue au chat ! Dis-le-moi s’il te plaît.","en":"No? Then I give up! Tell me please.","lit":"I give my tongue to the cat"},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Il va être vétérinaire, et il est heureux comme un poisson dans l’eau.","en":"He’s going to become a vet, and he’s as happy as a lark.","lit":"a fish in water"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"un lapin means a rabbit, and poser, to put or place (lesson 53 line 12). The idiomatic expression poser un lapin (à quelqu’un) means to stand someone up or, simply, not to show up: La chanteuse Nala a posé un lapin à ses fans, The singer Nala didn’t turn up to meet her fans."},{"ref":"2","text":"un fouet means a whip, whence the verb fouetter (note the double t). Avoir d’autres chats à fouetter (“to have other cats to whip”) is one of several idioms using different animals to convey the same idea as an English equivalent: to have other fish to fry."},{"ref":"3","text":"A symbol of strength or size, un cheval, a horse (plur. des chevaux, lesson 22) turns up in many idioms and similes, like une fièvre de cheval, a raging fever, and its antidote un remède de cheval, a drastic remedy or cure."},{"ref":"4","text":"un cafard is the universally unpopular insect a cockroach. And since a roach infestation can cause depression, avoir le cafard means to be feeling low or down in the dumps. Another common expression is avoir un coup de cafard, to have a fit of the blues."},{"ref":"5","text":"The idiomatic adjective moche is useful for describing ugliness (Il est moche comme un pou (“ugly as a louse”), He’s as ugly as sin), or unpleasantness in general: Le match contre Lyon était assez moche, The game against Lyon was pretty awful. The word is often used to describe bad weather."},{"ref":"6","text":"In French as in English, dogs feature in many idioms, generally expressing drudgery, hardship, etc. Une vie de chien, a dog’s life. Likewise, J’ai eu un mal de chien à le convaincre, I had a tough time convincing him."},{"ref":"7","text":"une pie, a magpie, features in the metaphor être bavard comme une pie, which can be translated simply as to be a chatterbox."},{"ref":"8","text":"Some French idioms are confusing because they resemble English expressions but mean something different. Here, reprendre du poil de la bête shares the same origin as to take a hair of the dog but has nothing to do with hangovers. It means to regain strength, to pick up, etc. Le marché a repris du poil de la bête depuis le début du mois, The market has rallied since the start of the month. Note that un poil / des poils refers to body and animal hair, whereas un cheveu / des cheveux is used for human head hair."},{"ref":"9","text":"The link (or lack of one) between the two animals in this expression – un coq, a cock (rooster), and un âne, a donkey – is lost in the mists of time, but the expression passer (or sauter) du coq à l’âne means to go or jump from one subject to another, unrelated one."},{"ref":"10","text":"The adjective malin (fem. maligne) comes from le mal, evil, seen in lesson 66 note 8 (whence the English cognate malignant). In everyday French, though, it means clever, smart: Ce n’est pas très malin, ce que tu viens de faire, What you’ve just done isn’t very clever. Because of the word’s “devilish” origins, the emphasis is on shrewdness rather than just intelligence. Un singe, a monkey."},{"ref":"11","text":"vachement, from une vache, a cow, is an idiomatic intensifier commonly used both with adjectives, Elle est vachement intelligente, She’s really smart, and with verbs, Il pleut vachement, It’s raining really hard. See lesson 70. The word should be used with caution because it is quite familiar."},{"ref":"12","text":"un lièvre means a hare. The verb courir, to run or to race, has entered English as to course, i.e. to hunt, as in hare coursing. “Hunting several hares at a time” – courir plusieurs lièvres à la fois – means to concentrate on more than one thing, or to have several irons in the fire. (One can also have trop de lièvres, too many irons.)"},{"ref":"13","text":"donner sa langue au chat (“to give one’s tongue to the cat”) means to give up or stop guessing: Si tu donnes ta langue au chat, je te donnerai la réponse, If you give up, I’ll tell you the answer. The same idea of being unable to speak is found in the English idiom Has the cat got your tongue? but the scope is broader than in French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je la trouve moche comme un pou mais elle est maligne comme un singe !","a":"I find her as ugly as sin but she’s as smart as a whip!"},{"q":"Stéphane s’ennuie comme un rat mort dans son boulot.","a":"Stéphane is getting bored to death in his job."},{"q":"Elle a dormi comme un loir cette nuit et maintenant elle a une faim de loup.","a":"She slept like a log last night and now she’s famished."},{"q":"J’ai du mal à vous suivre car vous sautez du coq à l’âne tout le temps.","a":"I find it hard to follow you because you’re jumping from one thing to another."},{"q":"J’ai eu un mal de chien à trouver un taxi ce matin.","a":"I had a heck of a time finding a taxi this morning."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The markets have picked up since last week.","t":"Les marchés {{ont repris du poil de la bête depuis}} la semaine dernière."},{"cue":"You’ll never guess what I’ve learned. – No, I give up.","t":"Vous {{ne devinerez jamais}} ce que j’ai appris ! – Non, {{je donne ma langue au chat}}."},{"cue":"Your proposal doesn’t interest them. They’ve got other fish to fry.","t":"Votre proposition {{ne les intéresse pas}}. Ils {{ont d’autres chats à fouetter}}."},{"cue":"Lisa got a fit of the blues when she learned the news but now she’s as happy as a lark.","t":"Lisa a eu {{un coup de cafard}} en apprenant la nouvelle mais maintenant elle est {{heureuse comme un poisson dans l’eau}}."},{"cue":"But you eat like a bird! – No, I was as sick as a dog yesterday.","t":"Mais tu {{as un appétit d’oiseau}} ! – Non, j’étais {{malade comme un chien}} hier."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"manger un morceau","en":"to get a bite to eat","zh":"吃点东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"As-tu le temps de manger un morceau avec moi ce midi ?","exEn":"Do you have time to get a bite to eat with me at noon?","src":1},{"fr":"il m’a posé un lapin","en":"he stood me up","zh":"他放了我鸽子","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “la-pan” – a rabbit (lapin) that never turns up to meet you, leaving you standing there with a frying pan","ex":"Je devais déjeuner avec mon fils, mais il m’a posé un lapin.","exEn":"I was supposed to have lunch with my son but he stood me up.","src":2},{"fr":"avoir d’autres chats à fouetter","en":"to have other fish to fry","zh":"有更重要的事要做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je pense qu’il a d’autres chats à fouetter que de sortir avec son vieux papa.","exEn":"I think that he’s got other fish to fry rather than to go out with his old dad.","src":3},{"fr":"j’étais malade comme un chien","en":"I was as sick as a dog","zh":"我病得很厉害","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il y a quelques jours, j’étais malade comme un chien.","exEn":"A few days ago, I was as sick as a dog.","src":5},{"fr":"avoir le cafard","en":"to feel down in the dumps","zh":"情绪低落","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “kah-far” – a cockroach crawling on your pillow makes you feel low and gloomy","ex":"Je commençais à avoir le cafard parce que j’étais clouée au lit depuis une semaine.","exEn":"I was beginning to feel down in the dumps because I’d been stuck in bed for a week.","src":7},{"fr":"un temps de chien","en":"lousy weather","zh":"糟糕的天气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Dehors, il faisait toujours gris et moche – un vrai temps de chien.","exEn":"Outside, it was always grey and dull – really lousy weather.","src":8},{"fr":"je m’ennuyais comme un rat mort","en":"I was bored to death","zh":"我无聊得要命","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’ennuyais comme un rat mort et j’avais un appétit d’oiseau.","exEn":"I was bored to death and I was eating like a bird.","src":9},{"fr":"reprendre du poil de la bête","en":"to pick up again, to regain strength","zh":"恢复元气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"not “a hair of the dog” (hangover cure)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et je commence tout doucement à reprendre du poil de la bête.","exEn":"and I’m beginning quite slowly to pick up again.","src":13},{"fr":"j’ai une faim de loup","en":"I’m famished","zh":"我饿极了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Surtout, j’ai une faim de loup !","exEn":"Above all, I’m famished!","src":14},{"fr":"passer du coq à l’âne","en":"to jump from one subject to another","zh":"东拉西扯；话题乱跳","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je passe du coq à l’âne, mais savais-tu que Patrice a trouvé un nouveau boulot ?","exEn":"I’m jumping from one thing to another, but did you know that Patrice has found a new job?","src":15},{"fr":"un boulot","en":"a job (informal)","zh":"工作（口语）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Je passe du coq à l’âne, mais savais-tu que Patrice a trouvé un nouveau boulot ?","exEn":"I’m jumping from one thing to another, but did you know that Patrice has found a new job?","src":15},{"fr":"il est malin comme un singe","en":"he’s as smart as a whip","zh":"他精明得很","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"malin ≠ malignant: it means clever","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est malin comme un singe, celui-là !","exEn":"He’s as smart as a whip, that one!","src":16},{"fr":"tu ne devineras jamais","en":"you’ll never guess","zh":"你绝对猜不到","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu ne devineras jamais. Vas-y : essaie !","exEn":"You’ll never guess. Go on, try!","src":18},{"fr":"je donne ma langue au chat","en":"I give up (guessing)","zh":"我猜不出来，认输","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non ? Alors je donne ma langue au chat ! Dis-le-moi s’il te plaît.","exEn":"No? Then I give up! Tell me please.","src":21},{"fr":"heureux comme un poisson dans l’eau","en":"as happy as a lark","zh":"如鱼得水；非常快乐","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il va être vétérinaire, et il est heureux comme un poisson dans l’eau.","exEn":"He’s going to become a vet, and he’s as happy as a lark.","src":22}],"uncertain":["note 12: OCR “une lièvre” corrected to “un lièvre” (masculine)","dialogue 4: English sentence 4 rendered “With great pleasure!” from “(It would be) With great pleasure!”"],"pdfPage":403,"gaps":[[1.82,3.66],[5.18,8.06],[14.48,15.56],[36.66,37.8],[42.12,43.26],[47.04,48.2],[57.08,58.4],[103.42,106.66],[108.74,110.58],[114.96,117.92],[120.8,123.76],[127.42,130.76],[134.14,137.28],[140.38,144.1]],"dur":144.1},{"n":70,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Salut Gérard, ça fait longtemps ! Comment vas-tu ?","en":"Hi Gérard, it’s been a long time! How are you?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça va mieux, merci. J’étais malade comme un chien toute la semaine dernière,","en":"I’m doing better, thanks. I was as sick as a dog all last week,","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"mais je reprends du poil de la bête depuis quelques jours.","en":"but I’ve been picking up in the past few days.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, ma femme et moi aimerions beaucoup aller quelque part en vacances.","en":"Now my wife and I would very much like to go somewhere on holiday.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"D’après mon fils, nous devrions tous aller dans le sud, quelque part où il ferait meilleur.","en":"According to my son, we should all go south, somewhere the weather would be better.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est vrai que si vous alliez dans le Midi, vous trouveriez peut-être du soleil.","en":"It’s true that if you went to the Midi, you’d perhaps find some sun.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que tu me conseillerais ?","en":"What would you advise me?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Quels sont les endroits les plus agréables pour passer une semaine ?","en":"What are the nicest places to spend a week?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Si tu vas dans le Vaucluse, vers Avignon, tu pourrais avoir du beau temps.","en":"If you go to the Vaucluse, towards Avignon, you might get good weather.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais dès que tu descendras vers la côte, tu trouveras certainement ce que tu cherches.","en":"But as soon as you go down towards the coast, you’ll certainly find what you are looking for.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"La température de la mer est agréable, donc vous pourriez vous baigner n’importe quand.","en":"The sea is a nice temperature, so you could go swimming any time.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce n’est pas la peine : je ne sais pas nager. Et ma femme n’aimerait pas la plage en hiver.","en":"Don’t bother: I can’t swim. And my wife wouldn’t like the beach in winter.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Dans ce cas, pourquoi ne pas aller à la montagne ?","en":"In that case, why not go to the mountains?","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais qu’est-ce que je ferais s’il n’y avait pas de neige, ou s’il faisait un temps de chien ?","en":"But what would I do if there were no snow, or if the weather were dreadful?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Je m’ennuierais comme un rat mort ! Finalement, on ferait mieux de rester chez nous.","en":"I’d be bored to tears! In the end, we’d do better to stay at home.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Le conditionnel: the conditional","text":"So far, we have seen three types of conditional sentence formed with a si (if) clause and a main clause:\n• The “zero conditional” simply expresses a general truth, using the same verb tenses as in English:\nS’il fait froid, les plages sont vides, If it is cold, the beaches are empty.\nIn most cases the first part of the proposition can be introduced by quand (when) rather than si (if).\n• The first conditional describes a cause-effect proposition (i.e. if the condition is fulfilled, then the result is certain). In this case, the verb in the si clause is in the present tense and the second verb is in the future, as in English:\nSi vous achetez ce sac en ligne, vous le payerez plus cher.\nIf you buy that bag online, you will pay more for it.\n• The second conditional is a hypothetical, describing what would happen if a condition were satisfied. In an “if ➔ then” construction, the verb governed by si is in the imperfect tense (see lesson 63) and the verb in the main clause is in the conditional.\nTo form the conditional tense, the imperfect endings (-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient) are added to the infinitive of the verb. For Group 3 verbs ending in -re, the final letter is omitted before adding the endings:\n   gagner   finir   boire\nje   gagnerais   finirais   boirais\ntu   gagnerais   finirais   boirais\nil/elle   gagnerait   finirait   boirait\nnous   gagnerions   finirions   boirions\nvous   gagneriez   finiriez   boiriez\nils/elles   gagneraient   finiraient   boiraient\nThis rule applies to all verbs. However, four of the most important irregular verbs change their stems:\n   avoir   être   aller   vouloir\nj’/je   aurais   serais   irais   voudrais\ntu   aurais   serais   irais   voudrais\nil/elle   aurait   serait   irait   voudrait\nnous   aurions   serions   irions   voudrions\nvous   auriez   seriez   iriez   voudriez\nils/elles   auraient   seraient   iraient   voudraient\nThese are not the only exceptional forms – major verbs such as devoir, pouvoir, venir and voir are all irregular – so please check the appendix.\nAs regards spelling, a handful of Group 1 verbs change very slightly. This applies to verbs ending in -eler and -eter, which double the final consonant: j’appellerais, vous appelleriez; je jetterais, vous jetteriez, etc. As we saw with the future (lesson 56 section 2), verbs such as acheter and lever take a grave accent (j’achèterais, tu lèverais). Finally, the conditional of -ayer verbs, like essayer, can be spelled in two ways: j’essayerais or j’essaierais. Both forms are correct and are pronounced identically.\nThe most important thing to remember with a second-conditional construction is that the verb following si is in the imperfect, not the conditional. This is easy to see in a sentence with être:\nSi j’étais vous, je serais content de partir.\nIf I were you I would be happy to leave.\nAs in English, the conditional is used in many expressions of politeness (Je voudrais un café s’il vous plaît). However, for the “tag” response to an invitation (Would you like to come? – Yes, I’d like to.), we do not use the conditional in French because it would suggest a reserve: (I would like to but I can’t). The usual answer to such an invitation, Voudriez-vous venir ?, is usually Avec plaisir. Similarly, where English uses would to express a habitual action in the past (When we were young we would go on holiday together), would go is not a real conditional; in French we use the imperfect for both actions: Quand nous étions jeunes, nous allions en vacances ensemble.\nThere is a fourth construction, the past conditional, which we will not see in this course."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Verbs that can be conjugated with être and avoir","text":"As we saw in lesson 67, the passé composé of some common verbs can be formed with être or avoir, depending on the meaning. The basic difference is between transitive and non-transitive usage. To remember the difference, meet a colleague of Dr Vandertramp’s: DR PRE MS: Descendre, Rentrer, Passer, Retourner, Entrer, Monter, Sortir.\nIl est descendu du bus. / Il a descendu l’escalier.\nHe got off the bus. / He went down the stairs.\nNous sommes rentrés tard. / Nous avons rentré la poubelle.\nWe came back late. / We’ve brought in the bin.\nElle est passée tout à l’heure. / Elle m’a passé le sel.\nShe came by earlier. / She passed me the salt.\nJe suis retourné en Bretagne. / J’ai retourné la crêpe.\nI went back to Brittany. / I turned over the pancake.\nIls sont entrés sans payer. / Ils ont entré toutes les données.\nThey went in without paying. / They entered all the data.\nElles sont montées au premier étage. / Elles ont monté les courses.\nThey went up to the first floor. / They brought up the shopping.\nJe suis sorti trop tard. / J’ai sorti la voiture du parking.\nI went out too late. / I brought the car out of the carpark.\nIn each pair, the two meanings are related but different. In terms of construction, the intransitive form is followed immediately by a preposition or an adverb while the transitive form takes a direct object.\nLike entrer and rentrer, some of these dual-auxiliary verbs can take a prefix: descendre / redescendre (to come down again), monter / remonter (to go back up), and passer / repasser (to come by again – although repasser can also mean to iron!). All these prefixed verbs follow the same rules."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 All about tout","text":"Tout, meaning all or every (and in some cases very), can be confusing because it has several functions and can be used as an adjective, adverb, pronoun or noun.\n• As an adjective, tout always precedes and agrees in gender and number with the nouns it qualifies. The four forms are tout (masculine singular), toute (feminine singular), tous (masculine plural) and toutes (feminine plural).\nHere’s a simple sentence to help you remember:\nTout le temps et toute la journée, tous mes frères et toutes mes sœurs sont à la maison.\n(Where tout refers to two nouns of different genders (here, frères, sœurs), it must be repeated in the appropriate form.)\nIn addition to nouns, tout can qualify adjectives, both possessive:\nToute ma famille vit à Marseille, All my family lives in Marseille;\nand demonstrative:\nTous ces gens sont ici pour nous aider, All these people are here to help us.\nRegarding pronunciation, the adjectives tout and tous are identical ([too]) as are toute and toutes ([toot]).\n• As an adverb, tout is usually invariable:\nAllez tout droit et tournez au feu rouge.\nGo straight on and turn at the traffic light.\nIf used with another adverb, tout means very or quite:\nJe commence tout doucement à m’ennuyer.\nI’m very slowly getting bored.\nThree other common expressions in which tout is an adverb are tout à fait, absolutely (lesson 23), tout droit, straight on (lesson 25), and tout à l’heure, later (lesson 30).\n• As a pronoun, there are only three forms: tout, tous and toutes.\nThe singular pronoun means everything, anything, all, etc.\nTout est possible ! Everything / Anything is possible!\nLes enfants sont tous dans la chambre, The children are all in the bedroom.\nLes commandes sont toutes arrivées en retard.\nAll the orders arrived late.\nIn terms of pronunciation, tout and toutes are pronounced in the same way as the adjectival forms but the final s of tous is voiced: [tooss]:\nTous [too] les hommes sont pareils. / Les hommes sont tous [tooss] pareils.\nAll men are the same. / Men are all the same.\nWhen tout is followed by a relative clause starting with que, we have to add ce, whereas English usually omits the relative that / which:\nTout ce que vous voulez, Everything you want.\n• Other expressions seen so far include: tout le monde, everybody (lesson 20), tout de suite, right away (26), tout le temps, all the time (34), tout à l’heure, earlier / later (30), en tout cas, in any case (54), also written en tous cas, and rien du tout, nothing at all (66).\nC’est tout pour le moment, That’s all for now!"},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 N’importe","text":"We know (lesson 60 note 8) that importer means to be important / of import / to matter. In the affirmative, the verb is quite formal and often used with the subjunctive. But in the negative, it is included in many common indefinite expressions. The simplest are N’importe and Peu importe. Generally used in response to a question, they mean No matter, I don’t mind, etc.\nQue voulez-vous boire ? – N’importe.\nWhat do you want to drink? – Anything. (i.e. “It matters not.”)\nA number of indefinites are formed simply by adding a pronoun or adverb after n’importe – the equivalent of anyone, anywhere, etc.\nJ’irais n’importe où avec lui, I’d go anywhere with him.\nN’importe qui peut y aller, Anyone can go there.\nTu peux venir à n’importe quelle heure ou n’importe quel jour.\nYou can come at any time or on any day.\nEn fait, tu peux venir n’importe quand, In fact, you can come anytime.\nIl faut cliquer sur une page, n’importe laquelle.\nYou have to click on a page, any one.\nWith quoi, there are several possibilities. The literal meaning is anything at all:\nIl ferait n’importe quoi pour attirer mon attention.\nHe would do anything to attract my attention.\nEn ligne, on trouve tout et n’importe quoi.\nYou can find anything and everything online.\nBut n’importe quoi can also refer to something that is absurd or nonsensical.\nTu dis n’importe quoi, You’re talking rubbish.\nIn this sense, it is often used as an exclamation to criticise or dismiss a previous statement:\nLes Français sont les meilleurs ! – N’importe quoi !\nThe French are the best! – Rubbish.\nA longer form, but with the identical meaning, is C’est du n’importe quoi."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Idioms (2): animals","text":"As we saw in the last revision lesson, it is a good idea to learn idiomatic expressions in thematic groups. This second instalment looks at animal-based idioms, which feature in most languages.\nêtre doux comme un agneau – be as gentle as a lamb\nreprendre du poil de la bête – pick up, to recover\navoir le cafard – be down in the dumps\ndonner sa langue au chat – give up\navoir d’autres chats à fouetter – have other fish to fry\navoir une fièvre de cheval – have a raging fever\nêtre malade comme un chien – be as sick as a dog\nfaire un temps de chien – be / have lousy weather\npasser / sauter du coq à l’âne – jump from one thing to another\nposer un lapin – stand someone up\ncourir plusieurs lièvres à la fois – have several irons in the fire\ndormir comme un loir – sleep like a log\navoir une faim de loup – be famished\navoir un appétit d’oiseau – eat like a bird\nêtre bavard comme une pie – be a chatterbox\nêtre heureux comme un poisson dans l’eau – be as happy as a lark / clam\ns’ennuyer comme un rat mort – be bored to tears\nêtre malin comme un singe – be as smart as a whip\nAs mentioned before, the exact translation will depend on the context, but the equivalents above are the ones generally used in English.\nOne word seen in lesson 69 is the adjective vachement, which apparently comes from une vache, a cow. The word was originally an adverb meaning nastily or unpleasantly (the adjective vache still has that meaning: une remarque vache, a nasty comment) but is now used as an intensifier. Although very common, vachement is nevertheless familiar and, depending on the register of language, can be the equivalent of bloody or damned. Thus, as with slang and idioms in general, it should not be used by anyone who does not already have a good grasp of standard French."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"ça va mieux","en":"I’m doing better","zh":"好些了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça va mieux, merci. J’étais malade comme un chien toute la semaine dernière,","exEn":"I’m doing better, thanks. I was as sick as a dog all last week,","src":2},{"fr":"aller quelque part en vacances","en":"to go somewhere on holiday","zh":"去某个地方度假","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, ma femme et moi aimerions beaucoup aller quelque part en vacances.","exEn":"Now my wife and I would very much like to go somewhere on holiday.","src":4},{"fr":"d’après mon fils","en":"according to my son","zh":"据我儿子说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’après mon fils, nous devrions tous aller dans le sud, quelque part où il ferait meilleur.","exEn":"According to my son, we should all go south, somewhere the weather would be better.","src":5},{"fr":"si vous alliez dans le Midi, vous trouveriez du soleil","en":"if you went to the south of France, you’d find sun","zh":"如果你们去法国南部，会找到阳光","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est vrai que si vous alliez dans le Midi, vous trouveriez peut-être du soleil.","exEn":"It’s true that if you went to the Midi, you’d perhaps find some sun.","src":6},{"fr":"vous pourriez vous baigner n’importe quand","en":"you could go swimming any time","zh":"你们随时都可以游泳","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La température de la mer est agréable, donc vous pourriez vous baigner n’importe quand.","exEn":"The sea is a nice temperature, so you could go swimming any time.","src":11},{"fr":"ce n’est pas la peine","en":"don’t bother, it’s not worth it","zh":"不必了；没有必要","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce n’est pas la peine : je ne sais pas nager. Et ma femme n’aimerait pas la plage en hiver.","exEn":"Don’t bother: I can’t swim. And my wife wouldn’t like the beach in winter.","src":12},{"fr":"pourquoi ne pas aller à la montagne ?","en":"why not go to the mountains?","zh":"为什么不去山里呢？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Dans ce cas, pourquoi ne pas aller à la montagne ?","exEn":"In that case, why not go to the mountains?","src":13},{"fr":"on ferait mieux de rester chez nous","en":"we’d do better to stay at home","zh":"我们最好还是待在家里","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’ennuierais comme un rat mort ! Finalement, on ferait mieux de rester chez nous.","exEn":"I’d be bored to tears! In the end, we’d do better to stay at home.","src":15}],"uncertain":["notes section 1: the book’s tag-response paragraph (Would you like to come? – Yes, I’d like to) is slightly garbled in OCR; reconstructed from context","notes section 3: pronunciation brackets [too]/[toot]/[tooss] reconstructed from OCR “[tao]/[foot]/[tooss]”","notes section 3: “Allez tout droit et tournez au feu rouge” (OCR “tourner” corrected to the imperative)"],"pdfPage":411,"gaps":[[1.8,3.7],[5.2,8.2],[71.86,75.48]],"dur":75.5},{"n":71,"kind":"lesson","title":"La Dame de fer","titleEn":"The Iron Lady","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Écoutons le commentaire sur ce nouvel audioguide touristique qu’on m’a recommandé.","en":"Let’s listen to the commentary on this new tourist audio guide that was recommended to me.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"“La Tour Eiffel, le monument emblématique de Paris, est construite à la fin du dix-neuvième siècle.","en":"“The Eiffel Tower, the emblematic monument of Paris, was built at the end of the nineteenth century.","lit":"is built"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Aujourd’hui, elle est visitée par à peu près sept millions de visiteurs, bon an, mal an.","en":"Today, it is visited by nearly seven million visitors, year in, year out.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais peu connaissent la vraie histoire de la Dame de Fer, comme on l’appelle affectueusement.","en":"But few know the true story of the Iron Lady, as it is affectionately known.","lit":"one her-calls"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"D’abord, elle n’est pas conçue par Gustave Eiffel lui-même mais par deux de ses collègues.","en":"First of all, it was not designed by Gustave Eiffel himself but by two of his colleagues.","lit":"is not designed"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Inspirée par deux ingénieurs américains, elle est d’abord baptisée “La tour de trois cents mètres”","en":"Inspired by two American engineers, it was first named “The Three Hundred Meter Tower”","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"et construite en deux ans pour l’Exposition universelle de mil huit cent quatre-vingt-neuf.","en":"and built in two years for the Universal Exhibition of 1889.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"La tour n’est pas destinée à durer plus de vingt ans, mais l’Histoire en décide autrement.","en":"The tower was not intended to last more than twenty years, but history decided otherwise.","lit":"is not intended ... history decides otherwise"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Comme elle est utilisée non seulement pour la météorologie mais aussi pour la téléphonie,","en":"As it was used not only for meteorology but also for telephony,","lit":"is used"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"la décision est prise de ne pas la démolir.","en":"the decision was taken not to demolish it.","lit":"is taken"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"La Tour Eiffel est restée le monument le plus élevé du monde pendant quarante ans,","en":"The Eiffel Tower remained the highest monument in the world for forty years,","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"mais, à l’époque, elle n’est pas appréciée par tout le monde.","en":"but at the time it was not well-liked by everyone.","lit":"is not appreciated"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Beaucoup de gens la trouvent laide, et elle est vivement critiquée par des écrivains et d’autres artistes.","en":"A lot of people found it ugly, and it was sharply criticised by writers and other artists.","lit":"find ... is sharply criticised"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Rira bien qui rira le dernier, car elle est déjà plus que centenaire !","en":"He who laughs last laughs longest, because it is already more than a hundred years old!","lit":"will-laugh well who will-laugh the last ... centenarian"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Depuis qu’elle est ouverte, la Tour a reçu plus de deux cent cinquante millions de visiteurs !","en":"Since it opened, the Tower has welcomed more than two hundred and fifty million visitors!","lit":"since that-it is open ... received"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Elle est même “vendue” dans les années mil neuf cent vingt par un escroc à un entrepreneur naïf,","en":"It was even “sold” in the 1920s by a crook to a naive entrepreneur,","lit":"is sold"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"qui pense faire une bonne affaire en l’achetant pour récupérer la ferraille !","en":"who thought he was getting a good deal by buying it for scrap metal!","lit":"thinks ... to recover the scrap metal"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Plus récemment, elle est convoitée par une jeune femme, qui l’épouse et adopte son nom !","en":"More recently, it was lusted after by a young woman, who married it and adopted its name!","lit":"is coveted ... marries"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Même si des copies ont été construites par d’autres villes dans le monde, il n’y a qu’une seule Tour Eiffel.","en":"Even if copies have been built by other cities in the world, there is only one Eiffel Tower.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Alors “gigantesque cheminée d’usine” ou magnifique symbole dont la France est fière ?","en":"So “gigantic factory chimney” or magnificent symbol of which France is proud?","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"La réponse est donnée d’avance par des millions de touristes qui viennent chaque année rendre hommage à la belle Dame de fer.”","en":"The answer is a foregone conclusion for millions of tourists who come every year to pay tribute to the beautiful Iron Lady.”","lit":"given in advance by ... render homage"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"French often uses the present tense to describe past actions (le présent historique, lesson 42). Here, être is the auxiliary of the passive voice, along with the past participle, as in English: Eiffel construit son monument en deux ans ➔ Le monument est construit en deux ans par Eiffel. (Eiffel built the monument in two years ➔ The monument was built in two years by Eiffel.) Since the past participle behaves as an adjective, it has to agree with the subject: Eiffel construit sa tour… ➔ Sa tour est construite…"},{"ref":"2","text":"à peu près (“at little near”) is a useful adverbial phrase meaning approximately. Il est à peu près huit heures / vingt heures, It’s around 8 o’clock. Although a synonym of environ (lesson 60 note 2), it can also be used to qualify adverbs, replacing presque: Cet acteur est connu à peu près partout, That actor is known almost everywhere."},{"ref":"3","text":"The idiom bon an, mal an (“good year, bad year”) means year in, year out, in other words, the average of good and bad years taken together. Bon an, mal an, la tour attire des millions de touristes, Year in, year out, the tower attracts millions of tourists. Always use an, never année, in this expression."},{"ref":"4","text":"The impersonal pronoun on often does the same job as the passive voice. Thus Il est appelé l’Homme de fer and On l’appelle l’Homme de fer both mean He’s called the Iron Man. Look again at the first sentence: Le guide qu’on m’a recommandé, The guide that was recommended to me. In neither case would on be the equivalent of the impersonal one in this type of passive-equivalent construction."},{"ref":"5","text":"The adjective vif (fem. vive) means lively or keen (it is also the origin of vivacious and vivid). The adverb vivement means keenly, vividly, sharply, etc. En ville, il est vivement recommandé d’utiliser les transports en commun, In town, it is strongly/highly recommended to use public transport."},{"ref":"6","text":"Proverbs in French often use old or literary constructions. Rira bien qui rira le dernier is the equivalent of He who laughs last laughs longest. Note how the two clauses are inverted in the French proverb (and the antecedent qui is omitted from the first clause)."},{"ref":"7","text":"As you already know, les affaires can mean business in the sense of an economic activity (lesson 47) or personal affairs (lesson 61). In the singular, une affaire generally means a matter, a case or an affair – for example l’affaire Dreyfus, the Dreyfus affair. (However, the French word never refers to a romantic attachment.) The expression une bonne affaire means a good deal, a bargain. It is often used with faire: Nous avons fait une bonne affaire en vendant notre maison, We got a good deal when we sold our house."},{"ref":"8","text":"The adjective fier (fem. fière) means proud (see Pronunciation notes). Elle est fière de ses origines basques, She’s proud of her Basque heritage. The word can also be used with the subjunctive mood, as we will see later on. One very common, invariable expression, similar to its English counterpart, is Il n’y a pas de quoi être fier, That’s nothing to be proud of. The noun is la fierté, pride. (Do not confuse fier with the verb se fier, pronounced [feeyay], to trust, lesson 50 note 3.)"},{"ref":"pron","text":"(6) The p in baptisé is not pronounced in any of the verb’s different forms: here, [bateezay]."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(20) The masculine adjective fier and the feminine fière are both pronounced [fyair]."},{"ref":"","text":"One of France’s – and the world’s – biggest tourist attractions, the Eiffel Tower was inspired by the work of two American engineers who wanted to design and build a 300-metre-tall tower. Completed to coincide with the hundredth anniversary of the 1789 French Revolution, the monument was to have been dismantled after a decade but Gustave Eiffel made every effort to ensure that his work would endure, despite opposition from writers, architects and intellectuals, who likened it to a factory chimney. The anecdotes about the tower being sold off for scrap metal by a confidence trickster and being wedded to an eccentric American are both true. And although a certain British politician once tried to make the Iron Lady sobriquet her own, there is really only one Dame de fer!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"On trouve de bons ordinateurs pas chers à peu près partout maintenant.","a":"You can find good cheap laptops almost everywhere now."},{"q":"On l’appelle tous Willi : il est très fier de ses origines alsaciennes.","a":"We all call him Willi: he’s very proud of his Alsatian origins."},{"q":"Tu n’as payé que cinq euros pour cet audioguide ? Tu as vraiment fait une bonne affaire.","a":"You paid only five euros for that audio guide? You really got a bargain."},{"q":"Le résultat n’est pas donné d’avance. Tout peut arriver dans ce sport.","a":"The result can’t be taken for granted. Anything can happen in this sport."},{"q":"Il est vivement recommandé de réserver dans cet hôtel car, bon an, mal an, il est souvent plein.","a":"You are strongly recommended to book in this hotel because it is often full, year in year out."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The monument has welcomed more than two million visitors since it opened.","t":"Le monument {{a reçu}} plus de deux millions de visiteurs {{depuis qu’il est ouvert}}."},{"cue":"French is used not only in France but also in several other countries in the world.","t":"Le {{français est utilisé non seulement}} en France {{mais aussi}} dans {{plusieurs autres}} pays {{dans le monde}}."},{"cue":"Gérard is not well-liked by his colleagues. He’s sharply criticised.","t":"Gérard {{n’est pas apprécié par}} ses collègues. Il {{est vivement critiqué}}."},{"cue":"My decision is taken. He who laughs last laughs longest.","t":"{{Ma}} décision {{est prise}}. {{Rira bien qui rira}} le {{dernier}}."},{"cue":"This building has posed problems since it was opened. (Use the présent historique.)","t":"Ce bâtiment {{pose}} des problèmes {{depuis qu’il est ouvert}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"à peu près","en":"approximately, around","zh":"大约，差不多","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aujourd’hui, elle est visitée par à peu près sept millions de visiteurs, bon an, mal an.","exEn":"Today, it is visited by nearly seven million visitors, year in, year out.","src":3},{"fr":"bon an, mal an","en":"year in, year out","zh":"年复一年，平均而言","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aujourd’hui, elle est visitée par à peu près sept millions de visiteurs, bon an, mal an.","exEn":"Today, it is visited by nearly seven million visitors, year in, year out.","src":3},{"fr":"une bonne affaire","en":"a good deal, a bargain","zh":"一笔划算的买卖","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"qui pense faire une bonne affaire en l’achetant pour récupérer la ferraille !","exEn":"who thought he was getting a good deal by buying it for scrap metal!","src":17},{"fr":"il est très fier de ses origines","en":"he is very proud of his origins","zh":"他对自己的出身非常自豪","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On l’appelle tous Willi : il est très fier de ses origines alsaciennes.","exEn":"We all call him Willi: he’s very proud of his Alsatian origins.","src":0},{"fr":"il est vivement recommandé de réserver","en":"it is strongly recommended to book","zh":"强烈建议预订","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est vivement recommandé de réserver dans cet hôtel car, bon an, mal an, il est souvent plein.","exEn":"You are strongly recommended to book in this hotel because it is often full, year in year out.","src":0},{"fr":"Rira bien qui rira le dernier","en":"He who laughs last laughs longest","zh":"谁笑到最后谁笑得最好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Rira bien qui rira le dernier, car elle est déjà plus que centenaire !","exEn":"He who laughs last laughs longest, because it is already more than a hundred years old!","src":14},{"fr":"depuis qu’elle est ouverte","en":"since it opened","zh":"自从它开放以来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Depuis qu’elle est ouverte, la Tour a reçu plus de deux cent cinquante millions de visiteurs !","exEn":"Since it opened, the Tower has welcomed more than two hundred and fifty million visitors!","src":15},{"fr":"un siècle","en":"a century","zh":"一个世纪","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ century (-cle ≈ -ury)","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"La Tour Eiffel, le monument emblématique de Paris, est construite à la fin du dix-neuvième siècle.","exEn":"The Eiffel Tower, the emblematic monument of Paris, was built at the end of the nineteenth century.","src":2},{"fr":"tout le monde","en":"everybody, everyone","zh":"每个人，大家","pos":"pron","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais, à l’époque, elle n’est pas appréciée par tout le monde.","exEn":"but at the time it was not well-liked by everyone.","src":12},{"fr":"d’abord","en":"first (of all)","zh":"首先，起初","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’abord, elle n’est pas conçue par Gustave Eiffel lui-même mais par deux de ses collègues.","exEn":"First of all, it was not designed by Gustave Eiffel himself but by two of his colleagues.","src":5},{"fr":"même si","en":"even if","zh":"即使","pos":"conj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Même si des copies ont été construites par d’autres villes dans le monde, il n’y a qu’une seule Tour Eiffel.","exEn":"Even if copies have been built by other cities in the world, there is only one Eiffel Tower.","src":19},{"fr":"il n’y a qu’une seule Tour Eiffel","en":"there is only one Eiffel Tower","zh":"只有一座埃菲尔铁塔","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Même si des copies ont été construites par d’autres villes dans le monde, il n’y a qu’une seule Tour Eiffel.","exEn":"Even if copies have been built by other cities in the world, there is only one Eiffel Tower.","src":19},{"fr":"un escroc","en":"a crook, a swindler","zh":"骗子","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “es-crow”: picture a crow in a suit selling you a tower","ex":"Elle est même “vendue” dans les années mil neuf cent vingt par un escroc à un entrepreneur naïf,","exEn":"It was even “sold” in the 1920s by a crook to a naive entrepreneur,","src":16},{"fr":"la ferraille","en":"scrap metal","zh":"废铁","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “fer-eye”: iron (fer) that makes your eye hurt, a pile of rusty scrap","ex":"qui pense faire une bonne affaire en l’achetant pour récupérer la ferraille !","exEn":"who thought he was getting a good deal by buying it for scrap metal!","src":17},{"fr":"chaque année","en":"every year","zh":"每年","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"La réponse est donnée d’avance par des millions de touristes qui viennent chaque année rendre hommage à la belle Dame de fer.","exEn":"The answer is a foregone conclusion for millions of tourists who come every year to pay tribute to the beautiful Iron Lady.","src":21}],"uncertain":[],"pdfPage":419,"gaps":[[1.88,3.9],[4.84,7.66],[86.48,87.54],[133.04,136.42],[138.3,140.46],[143.9,146.88],[150.86,153.9],[157.6,160.8],[164.22,167.2],[171.84,175.6]],"dur":175.6},{"n":72,"kind":"lesson","title":"Ça ne se fait pas !","titleEn":"That’s not the done thing!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Éric Camus nous quitte à la fin du mois et nous allons organiser un pot de départ avec tous ses amis","en":"Éric Camus is leaving us at the end of the month and we’re going to organise a farewell drinks party with all his friends","lit":"a pot of departure"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"pour lui souhaiter bonne chance dans les années à venir.","en":"to wish him good luck in the years to come.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Depuis combien de temps est-ce qu’il gère le service ? On me dit qu’il est là depuis des lustres.","en":"How long has he managed the department for? They say he’s been here for ages.","lit":"one to-me says"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Il ne faut pas exagérer. Éric nous a rejoints il y a une dizaine d’années.","en":"Let’s not exaggerate. Éric joined us about ten years ago.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Auparavant, il était salarié dans une compagnie d’assurances en province,","en":"Beforehand, he was an employee in an insurance company in the provinces","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"mais on lui a fait comprendre qu’il n’aurait pas d’avancement parce qu’il était trop jeune.","en":"but he was made to understand that he wouldn’t get a promotion because he was too young.","lit":"one to-him has made understand"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un de ses amis qui a mentionné notre entreprise : Éric a postulé et on l’a embauché aussitôt.","en":"One of his friends mentioned our company: Éric applied and was hired right away.","lit":"it’s one of his friends who"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"On dit qu’il est très apprécié de ses collègues. Est-ce vrai ?","en":"They say that his colleagues appreciate him very much. Is that true?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, Éric est aimé par tous ceux qui ont travaillé avec lui, surtout les chefs d’équipe.","en":"Yes, Éric is liked by everyone who has worked with him, especially the team leaders.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est toujours de bonne humeur et ne se dispute jamais avec personne ;","en":"He’s always in a good mood and never argues with anyone;","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"il sait écouter et prendre des décisions. Il a fait de grandes choses pour cette société.","en":"he knows how to listen and take decisions. He has done great things for this company.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Pour le pot, à quel moment allez-vous le faire ? En fin de journée ?","en":"For the reception, what time are you going to do it? At the end of the day?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est préférable de le faire à midi : comme ça il y aura plus de monde.","en":"It’s preferable to do it at noon: that way there will be more people.","lit":"like that"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’allez-vous faire : l’organiser vous-mêmes ou faire appel à quelqu’un ?","en":"What are you going to do: organise it yourselves or get someone in?","lit":"make call to someone"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est nous qui allons tout préparer, à part le buffet, qui a été commandé chez un traiteur.","en":"We’re going to prepare everything, apart from the buffet, which has been ordered from a caterer.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Des petits fours salés et sucrés seront livrés vers onze heures, onze heures trente au plus tard.","en":"Savoury and sweet petits-fours will be delivered around 11 am, 11.30 at the latest.","lit":"salted and sugared; little ovens"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout se mange froid, donc il n’y a pas besoin de cuisine.","en":"Everything is eaten cold so there’s no need for a kitchen.","lit":"everything itself eats cold"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Et c’est vous qui achetez le cadeau de départ ?","en":"And you’re going to buy the farewell gift?","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, c’est un autre collègue qui s’en charge, mais il sera choisi par un comité de quatre personnes,","en":"No, another colleague is taking care of it, but it will be chosen by a four-person committee,","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"et un discours sera prononcé lors du buffet.","en":"and a speech will be made during the buffet.","lit":"will be pronounced"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Et il se fera en anglais. Après tout, nous sommes une entreprise moderne et dynamique.","en":"And it will be done in English. After all, we’re a modern and dynamic company.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah non, ça ne se fait pas ! Ici, on parle français, monsieur.","en":"Ah no, that’s not the done thing! We speak French here, sir.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"un pot means a pot – un pot de fleurs, a pot of flowers – or a jar, un pot de confiture, a jar of jam. (Remember the important difference between à and de in this case, lesson 48 note 9.) In a familiar register, un pot means a drink (usually alcoholic): Tu viens boire un pot avec nous ? Coming for a drink with us? In a work context, it means a drinks party, or, specifically, un pot de départ or d’adieu, an office farewell/leaving party."},{"ref":"2","text":"un lustre means shine (or lustre). However, the familiar expression depuis des lustres (from the Latin word for a five-year period, and always plural) simply means for a long time / for ages. Il est ici depuis des lustres, He’s been here for ages."},{"ref":"3","text":"One way of avoiding the passive voice in a sentence is to put the emphasis on the subject (the person performing the action). For this, we use c’est (sing.) or ce sont (plur.) at the beginning of the clause followed by qui/que. So instead of Votre entreprise a été mentionnée par un ami we say C’est un ami qui a mentionné votre entreprise. In English, c’est / ce sont … qui does not need translating: A friend mentioned your firm."},{"ref":"4","text":"The adverb aussitôt, formed from aussi, as, and tôt, early, is an elegant way of saying straight away, immediately. La neige est tombée cette nuit mais a disparu aussitôt, The snow fell last night but disappeared immediately. It can often be replaced by immédiatement. One common and easy-to-remember expression is Aussitôt dit, aussitôt fait, No sooner said than done."},{"ref":"5","text":"The passive agent is generally introduced with par: La décision a été prise par le directeur, The decision was taken by the manager (see lesson 71 note 1). But if the verb expresses a feeling or emotion, par may be replaced by de: Il est aimé par or de ses collègues, He is liked by his colleagues. There is no difference in meaning."},{"ref":"6","text":"Be careful not to confuse the masculine noun l’humour, humour (see lesson 52 for pronunciation) with the feminine l’humeur, mood. Elle est de bonne humeur ce matin, She’s in a good mood this morning. The word can also refer to a person’s temperament: Il est d’humeur changeante depuis qu’il a arrêté de fumer, He’s been moody since he stopped smoking. Another “animal” expression (lesson 69) is être d’une humeur de chien: Le chef est d’une humeur de chien depuis deux jours, The boss has been in a foul mood for two days."},{"ref":"7","text":"We know that grand means not only big but also great, eminent (lesson 16 note 2). Thus faire / accomplir de grandes choses means to do / accomplish great things. Don’t confuse this adjective-plus-noun construction with the idiom ne pas faire grand-chose (always singular), seen in lesson 61."},{"ref":"8","text":"un appel, from the verb appeler, to call, means a call (phone, appeal, etc., lesson 38, 16). Thus to make a (phone) call is passer un appel (téléphonique). The expression faire appel à thus means to call upon or appeal to. The direct object of the verb can be a person – Nous avons fait appel à un expert pour nous aider, We called on an expert for help – but also an abstract noun: Je fais appel à vos connaissances en informatique, I’m calling on your computer skills."},{"ref":"9","text":"Another way to avoid the passive is with the pronominal verb form in the third person singular: Tout se fait en français dans notre entreprise, Everything is done in French in our company. This workaround is generally used in situations where the agent is unknown or unimportant: Le billet s’achète en ligne, The ticket is bought online. It can also be used if the agent is “understood,” notably when addressing them directly, in which case the previous sentence means You buy the ticket online. The expression Ça ne se fait pas, meaning That’s not the done thing, can be used in a variety of contexts (it’s a no-no, You can’t do that, etc.)."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(5) province: Note the open vowel [ah] (nasal) in the second syllable: [provah’ss]. Don’t confuse this word with Provence, the beautiful region of southeastern France, pronounced [provœh’ss]."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Attendons un peu pour aller boire un pot. Comme ça il y aura plus de monde.","a":"Let’s wait a bit to go for a drink. That way, there’ll be more people."},{"q":"Je n’ai pas vu Benoît depuis des lustres. Toujours de mauvaise humeur ?","a":"I haven’t seen Benoît for donkey’s years. Still in a bad mood?"},{"q":"On fait appel à elle parce que c’est une experte dans ce domaine.","a":"We call on her because she’s an expert in this field."},{"q":"Lors de la réunion, je leur ai demandé une copie du discours. Aussitôt dit, aussitôt fait !","a":"During the meeting, I asked them for a copy of the speech. No sooner said than done."},{"q":"Qui achètera le cadeau de départ ? – Ce sont eux qui vont tout faire, je pense.","a":"Who will buy the farewell gift? – They will do everything, I think."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Who said: “I need a jam pot to make a pot of jam”? – No one!","t":"Qui a dit : “J’ai besoin {{d’un pot à confiture}} pour en faire {{un pot de confiture}}” ? – {{Personne}} !"},{"cue":"Train tickets are bought online. Everything has been done on the internet or by smartphone for years.","t":"Les billets de train {{s’achètent en ligne}}. {{Tout se fait}} sur Internet ou smartphone {{depuis des années}}."},{"cue":"Do we have to tip the caterer? – No, that’s not the done thing.","t":"Est-ce qu’on {{doit}} donner {{un pourboire au traiteur}} ? – Non, {{ça ne se fait pas}}."},{"cue":"Three colleagues will take charge of organising the drinks party for Éric, whom everybody greatly appreciates.","t":"{{Ce sont}} trois collègues qui {{se chargeront d’organiser le pot}} pour Éric, qui {{est très apprécié de tout le monde}}."},{"cue":"We’ve been made to understand that the moment is badly chosen and that it’s preferable to wait a few months.","t":"{{On nous a fait comprendre}} que le moment est mal choisi et qu’{{il est préférable d’attendre}} quelques mois."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un pot de départ","en":"a farewell drinks party","zh":"欢送酒会","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Éric Camus nous quitte à la fin du mois et nous allons organiser un pot de départ avec tous ses amis","exEn":"Éric Camus is leaving us at the end of the month and we’re going to organise a farewell drinks party with all his friends","src":1},{"fr":"il est là depuis des lustres","en":"he’s been here for ages","zh":"他在这里已经很久了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Depuis combien de temps est-ce qu’il gère le service ? On me dit qu’il est là depuis des lustres.","exEn":"How long has he managed the department for? They say he’s been here for ages.","src":3},{"fr":"il y a une dizaine d’années","en":"about ten years ago","zh":"大约十年前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"numbers","kw":"","ex":"Il ne faut pas exagérer. Éric nous a rejoints il y a une dizaine d’années.","exEn":"Let’s not exaggerate. Éric joined us about ten years ago.","src":4},{"fr":"auparavant","en":"beforehand, previously","zh":"以前，先前","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Auparavant, il était salarié dans une compagnie d’assurances en province,","exEn":"Beforehand, he was an employee in an insurance company in the provinces","src":5},{"fr":"on l’a embauché aussitôt","en":"he was hired right away","zh":"他立刻就被录用了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est un de ses amis qui a mentionné notre entreprise : Éric a postulé et on l’a embauché aussitôt.","exEn":"One of his friends mentioned our company: Éric applied and was hired right away.","src":7},{"fr":"il est toujours de bonne humeur","en":"he is always in a good mood","zh":"他总是心情很好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"humeur (f) = mood, NOT humour (l’humour, m)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est toujours de bonne humeur et ne se dispute jamais avec personne ;","exEn":"He’s always in a good mood and never argues with anyone;","src":10},{"fr":"faire appel à quelqu’un","en":"to call on someone, to get someone in","zh":"请某人帮忙，求助于某人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’allez-vous faire : l’organiser vous-mêmes ou faire appel à quelqu’un ?","exEn":"What are you going to do: organise it yourselves or get someone in?","src":14},{"fr":"à part le buffet","en":"apart from the buffet","zh":"除了自助餐之外","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est nous qui allons tout préparer, à part le buffet, qui a été commandé chez un traiteur.","exEn":"We’re going to prepare everything, apart from the buffet, which has been ordered from a caterer.","src":15},{"fr":"un traiteur","en":"a caterer","zh":"餐饮承办商","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"sounds like “tray-tur”: a man carrying a tray to your party","ex":"C’est nous qui allons tout préparer, à part le buffet, qui a été commandé chez un traiteur.","exEn":"We’re going to prepare everything, apart from the buffet, which has been ordered from a caterer.","src":15},{"fr":"un cadeau de départ","en":"a farewell gift","zh":"送别礼物","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et c’est vous qui achetez le cadeau de départ ?","exEn":"And you’re going to buy the farewell gift?","src":18},{"fr":"c’est un autre collègue qui s’en charge","en":"another colleague is taking care of it","zh":"另一位同事负责这件事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, c’est un autre collègue qui s’en charge, mais il sera choisi par un comité de quatre personnes,","exEn":"No, another colleague is taking care of it, but it will be chosen by a four-person committee,","src":19},{"fr":"un discours sera prononcé lors du buffet","en":"a speech will be made during the buffet","zh":"自助餐期间将发表讲话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ discourse (un discours = a speech)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et un discours sera prononcé lors du buffet.","exEn":"and a speech will be made during the buffet.","src":20},{"fr":"ça ne se fait pas","en":"that’s not the done thing","zh":"这样做不合适，这是不礼貌的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah non, ça ne se fait pas ! Ici, on parle français, monsieur.","exEn":"Ah no, that’s not the done thing! We speak French here, sir.","src":22},{"fr":"il est préférable de le faire à midi","en":"it’s preferable to do it at noon","zh":"最好在中午做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ preferable","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est préférable de le faire à midi : comme ça il y aura plus de monde.","exEn":"It’s preferable to do it at noon: that way there will be more people.","src":13},{"fr":"comme ça il y aura plus de monde","en":"that way there will be more people","zh":"这样人会更多","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est préférable de le faire à midi : comme ça il y aura plus de monde.","exEn":"It’s preferable to do it at noon: that way there will be more people.","src":13}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 2: printed text may read ‘sur Internet ou (par) smartphone’; answers list only ‘s’achètent en ligne. Tout se fait’ and ‘depuis des années’, so text kept as printed"],"pdfPage":427,"gaps":[[1.8,3.78],[4.84,7.7],[105.42,108.62],[110.92,113.06],[116.88,120.04],[123.32,126.32],[129.42,132.4],[138.44,141.54],[145.8,149.1]],"dur":149.1},{"n":73,"kind":"lesson","title":"Je n’en peux plus !","titleEn":"I can’t take it any more!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais qu’est-ce que tu as ? On dirait que ça ne va pas fort.","en":"What’s wrong with you? It looks like things aren’t going well.","lit":"one would say that"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Effectivement. J’en peux plus. J’en ai plein le dos.","en":"It does, indeed. I can’t take it anymore. I’m sick and tired.","lit":"I of-it have full the back"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Avant-hier, je me réveille en pleine nuit en mauvais état avec une rage de dents","en":"The day before yesterday, I woke up in the middle of the night in a bad state with a toothache","lit":"rage of teeth"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"et je n’ai rien dans l’armoire à pharmacie pour soulager la douleur.","en":"and I had nothing in the medicine cabinet to relieve the pain.","lit":"have"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai dû attendre le matin pour appeler mon dentiste, mais j’avais oublié que son cabinet est fermé le week-end.","en":"I had to wait until the morning to call my dentist, but I had forgotten that his office closes at weekends.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout comme la pharmacie du coin. Ce sont tous des fainéants !","en":"Just like the local pharmacy. They’re all lazy!","lit":"all like"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai voulu prendre le métro pour aller aux urgences, mais il était en grève.","en":"I wanted to take the metro to go to the emergency room, but it was on strike.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai réussi à trouver un taxi mais on a été pris dans les bouchons pendant une heure.","en":"I managed to find a taxi but we were stuck in traffic for an hour.","lit":"taken in the corks"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"À l’hôpital, les urgences étaient débordées et ils m’ont fait patienter pendant trois heures.","en":"At the hospital, the emergency room was overwhelmed and they made me wait for three hours.","lit":"overflowed"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Trois heures ? C’est pas possible ! Quelle galère !","en":"Three hours? That’s not possible! What a drag!","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Après, ils m’ont cassé les pieds parce que je n’avais pas ma carte d’assurance maladie.","en":"Afterwards, they pestered me because I didn’t have my health insurance card.","lit":"broke my feet; insurance illness"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour couronner le tout, j’ai été soigné par un stagiaire, pas un vrai dentiste, et j’ai toujours mal.","en":"To top it all off, I was treated by a trainee, not a real dentist, and I’m still in pain.","lit":"to crown the all"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est dingue ! Quelle histoire de fou.","en":"That’s mad! What a crazy story.","lit":"story of mad"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Tout est comme ça maintenant, et il y en a marre.","en":"Everything is like that nowadays, and I’m fed up with it.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"N’importe qui fait n’importe quoi, n’importe comment – c’est toujours pareil.","en":"Anyone can do anything, anyhow – it’s always the same.","lit":"no-matter who does no-matter what"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Rien ne marche comme il faut : tout est sens dessus dessous, et ça m’énerve.","en":"Nothing works as it should: everything’s upside down, and that annoys me.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Et quand tu le fais remarquer aux gens, ils te répondent “Ah bon ? Nous, on ne peut pas se plaindre.”","en":"And when you point this out to people, they say, “Really? We mustn’t grumble.”","lit":"make notice"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Qu’est-ce qu’ils sont fatigants ! Même toi, tu commences à me fatiguer !","en":"They’re so tiring! Even you are starting to tire me!","lit":"how they are tiring"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce que je pourrais faire pour te remonter le moral ? T’inviter à dîner ?","en":"What could I do to cheer you up? Invite you to dinner?","lit":"to raise the morale to you"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Ne t’en fais pas. C’est vrai que j’en ai ras le bol,","en":"Don’t worry. It’s true that I’m fed up,","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"mais tu connais les Français : nous ne sommes heureux que lorsque nous nous plaignons.","en":"but you know the French: we’re only happy when we’re complaining.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We saw le dos, the back, in lesson 62 note 8. The idiomatic expression en avoir plein le dos basically means to be fed up, to be sick and tired. It can be used as an exclamation, as here, or with a direct object, in which case it takes de: J’en ai plein le dos de leurs excuses, I’m sick and tired of their excuses. Likewise, Je n’en peux plus expresses inability to deal with unpleasantness, using the same image as English expressions like I can’t take any more, I’ve had it up to here."},{"ref":"2","text":"une grève means a strike, a work stoppage. It is generally used either with faire (sometimes without an article) or être en (never with the article): Les ouvriers font [la] grève / L’usine est en grève, The workers are striking / The plant is on strike."},{"ref":"3","text":"un bouchon means a cork. While English uses a bottleneck to describe traffic congestion, French prefers the image of a bottle stopper. Un bouchon is more or less synonymous with un embouteillage (lesson 58), which literally means bottling and is generally used to describe urban congestion. However, only bouchon can be used in a verb: Ça commence à bouchonner sur la route des vacances, Traffic jams are starting to form on holiday roads."},{"ref":"4","text":"une galère is literally a galley (an oar-propelled vessel). The sheer effort and exertion made by the ship’s oarsmen provides the context for a word that describes anything from hardship and difficulty to a hellish journey. For now, simply learn the exclamation Quelle galère !, What a pain / mess / nightmare!"},{"ref":"5","text":"The idiom casser les pieds (“break the feet”) is an idiomatic way of saying that someone is importuning you, i.e. to bore, annoy, irritate, pester, etc. Il me casse les pieds avec toutes ces questions ! He’s pestering me with all those questions! There is also a derived adjective – C’est casse-pieds de changer son mot de passe chaque mois, It’s a drag to change your password every month – and noun: Mon nouveau voisin est un vrai casse-pieds, My new neighbour is a real pain."},{"ref":"6","text":"The familiar adjective dingue, derived from an old slang word for malaria, basically means crazy, barmy, incredible, etc. Ce truc me rend dingue ! This thing is driving me nuts! In this sense, it is a synonym for fou (lesson 58). Derived meanings include unbelievable, extraordinary, etc. Les prix dans ce restaurant sont dingues ! That restaurant charges unbelievable prices! The nominal form is un(e) dingue: C’est un dingue de moto, He’s a motorcycle nut."},{"ref":"7","text":"en avoir marre, as we saw in lesson 44 note 7, means to be fed up with. Il y en a marre ! is an impersonal exclamation that translates roughly as That’s enough!"},{"ref":"8","text":"The adverbs dessus, on top of, and dessous, underneath, are combined in the expression sens dessus dessous, upside down (or, in some cases, topsy-turvy), the word order being the same as in English: Tu as mis ma vie sens dessus dessous, You have turned my life upside down. Note the order of the two adverbs: the one with the extra letter (o) comes second."},{"ref":"9","text":"plaindre means to pity, feel sorry for: Si vous n’avez pas encore la réponse, je vous plains, If you still don’t have the answer I feel sorry for you. The pronominal form se plaindre, with its past participle plaint, is the origin of to complain and a complaint (une plainte). Il se plaint de maux de tête, He’s complaining of headaches. It can also mean to grumble, moan, etc. Arrête de te plaindre tout le temps, Stop moaning all the time. The expression On ne peut pas se (or Je ne peux pas me) plaindre is the equivalent of the stoic response Mustn’t grumble."},{"ref":"10","text":"le moral means morale, i.e. enthusiasm, confidence: Qu’est-ce qu’il y a ? Tu n’as pas le moral ? What’s the matter? Are you feeling down? Be careful not to confuse it with la morale, morals, moral standards."},{"ref":"11","text":"s’en faire, to worry / to be concerned about, is a synonym of s’inquiéter (lesson 51 line 9). It is mainly used in the expression Ne vous en faites pas (fam. Ne t’en fais pas): Don’t worry. A direct object, introduced by pour, can be added: Ne t’en fais pas pour lui, il va bien, Don’t worry about him, he’s fine."},{"ref":"pron","text":"In informal French, the adverb ne is often omitted from a negative construction when speaking quickly. Thus j’en peux plus (line 2), pronounced [zhœh’ puh plew], is the informal way of saying je n’en peux plus [zhuh nœh’ puh plew]. Similarly, c’est pas possible (line 10) replaces ce n’est pas possible. You will find these grammatically incorrect forms written out in song lyrics, blog entries, social media postings and the like. Needless to say, do not imitate them, but do be prepared for a missing ne."},{"ref":"","text":"We often stress the importance of etymology and association in building up a substantial vocabulary. Take une grève, a strike. Until the 18th century, one of the main public gathering places in Paris was a patch of sandy ground on the right shore, or strand (grève), of the River Seine, where jobless labourers would gather in search of a day’s work unloading barges at the nearby docks. The place became known as Place de Grève (just as London’s Thames riverbank was named The Strand), and the name eventually became synonymous with idleness and, ironically, with deliberate work stoppages. More ominously, the square was also the venue for public executions. (It was renamed Place de l’Hôtel de Ville, i.e. City Hall Square, at the beginning of the 19th century.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Ils font grève juste avant Noël. J’en ai plein le dos !","a":"They’re striking just before Christmas. I’m sick and tired."},{"q":"Ces jeunes nous cassent les pieds avec ces histoires de réchauffement climatique.","a":"These youngsters really annoy me with these climate change stories."},{"q":"Quelle galère ! Nous étions coincés dans les bouchons sur l’autoroute pendant trois heures.","a":"What a pain! We were stuck in traffic jams on the motorway for three hours."},{"q":"Je n’en peux plus de ce bruit, qui me réveille à trois heures toutes les nuits.","a":"I can’t stand any more of this noise, which wakes me up at three o’clock every night."},{"q":"Le service est débordé et, pour couronner le tout, ils ne répondent plus au téléphone.","a":"The department is overwhelmed and, to top it all off, they’re no longer answering the phone."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"They made me wait for 20 minutes but I mustn’t grumble.","t":"On {{m’a fait patienter pendant}} vingt minutes, mais {{je ne peux pas me plaindre}}."},{"cue":"They complain all the time and it’s driving me mad because the problem isn’t as serious as that.","t":"Ils {{se plaignent}} tout le temps et {{ça me rend dingue}}, car le problème n’est pas {{si grave}} que ça."},{"cue":"It was pointed out to me that everything is topsy turvy, and it’s true. What a crazy story!","t":"On {{m’a fait remarquer}} que tout est {{sens dessus dessous}}, et {{c’est vrai}}. {{Quelle}} histoire {{de fou}} !"},{"cue":"You’re beginning to tire me with your questions. You’re a real pain in the neck.","t":"Vous {{commencez à me fatiguer}} avec vos questions. {{Vous êtes}} vraiment {{casse-pieds}}."},{"cue":"I’m down in the dumps: anybody can do anything, any old how.","t":"Je {{n’ai pas le moral}} : {{n’importe qui}} fait {{n’importe quoi}}, {{n’importe}} comment."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je n’en peux plus","en":"I can’t take it any more","zh":"我受不了了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Effectivement. J’en peux plus. J’en ai plein le dos.","exEn":"It does, indeed. I can’t take it anymore. I’m sick and tired.","src":2},{"fr":"une rage de dents","en":"a toothache","zh":"牙疼","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"rage de dents = toothache, not anger","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"Avant-hier, je me réveille en pleine nuit en mauvais état avec une rage de dents","exEn":"The day before yesterday, I woke up in the middle of the night in a bad state with a toothache","src":3},{"fr":"il était en grève","en":"it was on strike","zh":"它在罢工","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai voulu prendre le métro pour aller aux urgences, mais il était en grève.","exEn":"I wanted to take the metro to go to the emergency room, but it was on strike.","src":7},{"fr":"pris dans les bouchons","en":"stuck in traffic","zh":"堵在车流中","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"bouchon = cork; also a traffic jam","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"J’ai réussi à trouver un taxi mais on a été pris dans les bouchons pendant une heure.","exEn":"I managed to find a taxi but we were stuck in traffic for an hour.","src":8},{"fr":"ils m’ont fait patienter pendant trois heures","en":"they made me wait for three hours","zh":"他们让我等了三个小时","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ patient","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À l’hôpital, les urgences étaient débordées et ils m’ont fait patienter pendant trois heures.","exEn":"At the hospital, the emergency room was overwhelmed and they made me wait for three hours.","src":9},{"fr":"Quelle galère !","en":"What a drag! What a nightmare!","zh":"真是糟糕透顶！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “gal-air”: a girl rowing a heavy galley ship in the air, exhausted","ex":"Trois heures ? C’est pas possible ! Quelle galère !","exEn":"Three hours? That’s not possible! What a drag!","src":10},{"fr":"ils m’ont cassé les pieds","en":"they pestered me, they annoyed me","zh":"他们烦死我了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Après, ils m’ont cassé les pieds parce que je n’avais pas ma carte d’assurance maladie.","exEn":"Afterwards, they pestered me because I didn’t have my health insurance card.","src":11},{"fr":"pour couronner le tout","en":"to top it all off","zh":"更糟的是，雪上加霜","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ to crown (couronner)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour couronner le tout, j’ai été soigné par un stagiaire, pas un vrai dentiste, et j’ai toujours mal.","exEn":"To top it all off, I was treated by a trainee, not a real dentist, and I’m still in pain.","src":12},{"fr":"un stagiaire","en":"a trainee, an intern","zh":"实习生","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Pour couronner le tout, j’ai été soigné par un stagiaire, pas un vrai dentiste, et j’ai toujours mal.","exEn":"To top it all off, I was treated by a trainee, not a real dentist, and I’m still in pain.","src":12},{"fr":"C’est dingue !","en":"That’s crazy!","zh":"太疯狂了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est dingue ! Quelle histoire de fou.","exEn":"That’s mad! What a crazy story.","src":13},{"fr":"n’importe qui fait n’importe quoi","en":"anyone does anything","zh":"谁都在乱来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’importe qui fait n’importe quoi, n’importe comment – c’est toujours pareil.","exEn":"Anyone can do anything, anyhow – it’s always the same.","src":15},{"fr":"tout est sens dessus dessous","en":"everything is upside down","zh":"一切都乱成一团","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Rien ne marche comme il faut : tout est sens dessus dessous, et ça m’énerve.","exEn":"Nothing works as it should: everything’s upside down, and that annoys me.","src":16},{"fr":"on ne peut pas se plaindre","en":"we mustn’t grumble","zh":"没什么好抱怨的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ complain (se plaindre)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et quand tu le fais remarquer aux gens, ils te répondent “Ah bon ? Nous, on ne peut pas se plaindre.”","exEn":"And when you point this out to people, they say, “Really? We mustn’t grumble.”","src":17},{"fr":"pour te remonter le moral","en":"to cheer you up","zh":"为了让你振作起来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ morale","ff":"le moral (morale) ≠ la morale (morals)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’est-ce que je pourrais faire pour te remonter le moral ? T’inviter à dîner ?","exEn":"What could I do to cheer you up? Invite you to dinner?","src":19},{"fr":"Ne t’en fais pas.","en":"Don’t worry.","zh":"别担心。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ne t’en fais pas. C’est vrai que j’en ai ras le bol,","exEn":"Don’t worry. It’s true that I’m fed up,","src":20}],"uncertain":["note 3: line reference for un embouteillage in lesson 58 was garbled in the OCR, so only the lesson number is given"],"pdfPage":433,"gaps":[[2.08,4.28],[5.22,8.12],[15.24,16.38],[101.44,104.58],[106.56,108.8],[111.8,114.92],[118.46,121.56],[127.18,130.34],[133.7,137.12],[141.58,145.84]],"dur":145.8},{"n":74,"kind":"lesson","title":"Les soldes","titleEn":"The sales","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce sont les soldes en janvier, et ça tombe bien parce que j’ai grand besoin d’un nouveau chapeau.","en":"It’s the sales in January, and it’s good timing because I really need a new hat.","lit":"it falls well"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah bon ? Tu es vraiment courageuse d’aller à la chasse aux bonnes affaires.","en":"Really? You’re really brave to go bargain-hunting.","lit":"to the hunt for good deals"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce n’est pas une question de courage. Il y a des bons plans à saisir partout.","en":"It’s not a question of courage. There are good deals to be had everywhere.","lit":"to seize"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Si tu voulais, tu pourrais m’accompagner. Ça me ferait plaisir.","en":"If you wanted to, you could come with me. I’d like that.","lit":"it would make me pleasure"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Qui sait ? Peut-être que tu trouveras ces caleçons que tu cherchais.","en":"Who knows? Perhaps you’ll find those boxer shorts that you need.","lit":"that you were looking for"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je hais les centres commerciaux, tu le sais bien. C’est la pagaille à tous les étages !","en":"I hate shopping centres, you know that. It’s chaos on every floor!","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Et les grands magasins sont pires car il y a toujours la foule.","en":"And the department stores are worse because there’s always a crowd.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Quant aux boutiques de mode, n’en parlons pas. On peut à peine respirer, tellement il y a de monde.","en":"As for the fashion stores, don’t mention them. You can barely breathe, there are so many people.","lit":"let’s not talk of them"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Arrête de râler tout le temps et viens avec moi. […]","en":"Stop grumbling all the time and come with me. …","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour madame, bonjour monsieur. Vous cherchez quelque chose ? Puis-je vous aider ?","en":"Hello ma’am, hello sir. Are you looking for something? Can I help you?","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci, mais pas pour l’instant. Nous ne faisons que regarder.","en":"No thank you, not at the moment. We’re just looking.","lit":"thank you"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Toi, peut-être. Mais moi, je suis à la recherche de mon chapeau et, éventuellement, de bottes.","en":"You, perhaps. But I’m looking for my hat and possibly some boots.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Et pourquoi pas un nouveau manteau pendant que tu y es ?","en":"And why not a new coat while you’re at it?","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai justement ce qu’il vous faut, madame. Par ici, s’il vous plaît.","en":"I’ve got just the thing for you, ma’am. This way, please.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"J’hésite entre cette parka et cet imperméable. Lequel des deux me va le mieux ?","en":"I’m hesitating between this parka and this raincoat. Which of the two suits me best?","lit":"which one"},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Prenez l’imperméable. Il est bien à votre taille, il tombe bien, et il vous va à merveille.","en":"Take the raincoat. It’s your size, it hangs well and it suits you perfectly.","lit":"it goes to you to marvel"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce qu’il me va vraiment ? Je ne suis pas folle de la couleur. L’avez-vous en gris clair ?","en":"Does it really suit me? I’m not crazy about the colour. Do you have it in light grey?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, mais il se trouve que ce modèle est soldé à moins soixante pour cent aujourd’hui.","en":"No, but it just so happens that this model is on sale at a sixty per cent discount today.","lit":"it finds itself"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Je le prends ! Et toi, Paul : ne veux-tu pas cette belle casquette, décorée de plumes…","en":"I’ll take it! And you, Paul: don’t you want this nice cap, decorated with feathers…","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Il n’en est pas question ! Où se trouve le rayon bricolage ?","en":"No way! Where’s the DIY department?","lit":"of-it there is no question"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The masculine noun les soldes, usually plural in this context, means sales (reduced-price goods). The singular, un solde, means a balance, i.e. a remaining amount. Thus le solde des marchandises means remaining goods. The verb solder means to wind up or pay off a balance, while the participle adjective soldé generally means reduced in price: Cette veste est soldée à moins quinze pour cent, This jacket is reduced by 15% in the sales."},{"ref":"2","text":"The expression tomber bien (“to fall well”) is used in two ways. Applied to clothing (coat, skirt, etc.), it means to hang well (line 16). By extension, the idiomatic meaning is to come at the right moment. Ce chèque tombe bien : j’ai besoin d’argent, This cheque has arrived at just the right moment. I need cash. A useful exclamation is Ça tombe bien ! meaning That’s convenient! or Great timing! The opposite, of course, is Ça tombe mal."},{"ref":"3","text":"un plan means a map (lesson 5 line 3) or a plan (lesson 54 note 5). But the idiomatic expression un bon plan has a similar meaning to une bonne affaire (lesson 71 note 7). Depending on the context, it can mean a good deal or bargain or, in tourism, a top tip or recommendation, etc. On est déjà vendredi ! Voici nos bons plans pour le week-end, It’s already Friday. Here are our top tips for the weekend."},{"ref":"4","text":"la / une pagaille means mess, shambles, chaos. À cause de la grève, c’est la pagaille dans les transports en commun, Public transport is a mess because of the strike. Preceded by the preposition en, the meaning is huge amounts or loads of: Elle a des idées en pagaille, She has loads of ideas. (An alternative spelling is une pagaïe, but this should be avoided because it is too similar to une pagaie [pagay], a paddle or short oar.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"quant à means with regard to, as for: Quant à lui, il n’est même pas venu, As for him, he never came. This adverbial phrase is often used to stress a contrast, in which case it does not need to be translated: J’aime les sports mais mon mari, quant à lui, les hait, I love sports but my husband hates them. Do not confuse quant with quand, when (tip: quant is always followed by à)."},{"ref":"6","text":"Merci, Thank you, is also a polite way to express refusal. Vous voulez un thé ? – Merci. Do you want some tea? – No thanks. This can be confusing for English-speakers, who often use merci thinking that it is a positive response (yes thank you). If in doubt, you can always insist: Non merci."},{"ref":"7","text":"Here is a common example of how the masculine gender dominates the feminine. If two nouns with different genders are used together, any subsequent pronoun – especially lequel / laquelle, etc. – is in the masculine form: Je peux vous montrer un passeport ou une carte d’identité. Lequel voulez-vous ? I can show you a passport or an identity card. Which [one] do you want?"},{"ref":"8","text":"French has no direct equivalent of to suit. Convenir can be used to express the notion of being acceptable or convenient (lesson 26 note 2). But, if the meaning is to enhance or look good on, we use a paraphrase such as aller bien. Cet imperméable bleu vous va très bien, That blue raincoat really suits you. The usual preposition is à, so the pronoun is indirect: La parka lui va bien, The parka suits him/her."},{"ref":"9","text":"se trouver, to be located (lesson 51 note 8), can also be used in the third person to express a coincidence or occurrence, like to happen or turn out (that). Il se trouve que j’ai le même prénom que leur mère, It turns out that I have the same first name as their mother. As a rule, if the usual translation of a word doesn’t help you to understand the sentence (like tomber bien in note 2), always look at the broader context. And, of course, check in a good dictionary!"},{"ref":"10","text":"le bricolage, from the root verb bricoler, is an institution in France. It is the nearest equivalent to the English concept of home repairs and do-it-yourself (DIY). Le dimanche, j’adore bricoler / faire du bricolage, I love doing stuff around the house on Sundays. Used disparagingly, the meaning is to do something hastily, mess about with: Le travail est mal fait : c’est du bricolage, The work is badly done: it’s a rush job."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(6) The verb haïr, to hate, is awkward. The diaeresis, showing that both a and i must be pronounced, is present in the infinitive [eye-eer] and the three present-plural forms (haïssons, [eye-eessoh’], haïssez [eye-eessay], haïssent [eye-eess]) but not the singular: hais and hait, both of which are pronounced [ay]."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Jacques, tu tombes bien. Tu peux m’aider à faire un peu de bricolage ?","a":"Great timing, Jacques. Can you help me with a bit of DIY?"},{"q":"Voulez-vous manger quelque chose ? Moi, j’ai mangé tout à l’heure et je n’ai plus faim.","a":"Do you want something to eat? Me, I ate earlier and I’m not hungry any more."},{"q":"Vous ne pourriez pas sortir, même si vous le vouliez. Il fait un temps de chien.","a":"You couldn’t go out, even if you wanted to. The weather is dreadful."},{"q":"Le ministre a dit : “Nous haïssons cette taxe et nous allons la faire disparaître”.","a":"The minister said: “We hate this tax and we’re going to make it go away.”"},{"q":"Mon mari râle toujours quand je l’amène dans le rayon lingerie du magasin.","a":"My husband always grumbles when I take him to the shop’s underwear department."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You’re going to the shopping centre? Get some coffee and milk while you’re at it.","t":"Vous {{allez au centre commercial}} ? Prenez du café et du lait {{pendant que vous y êtes}}."},{"cue":"My wife likes the grey coat, over there. Myself, I prefer this one. It suits me.","t":"Ma femme aime {{le manteau gris, là-bas}}. {{Quant à moi}}, je préfère {{celui-ci}}. Il {{me va bien}}."},{"cue":"We have what you need, sir. Look, these parkas are reduced by 30 per cent in the sales.","t":"Nous avons {{ce qu’il vous faut}}, monsieur. Regardez, {{ces}} parkas {{sont soldées à moins}} trente {{pour cent}}."},{"cue":"Have you got any good tips for the weekend? – No, I’m staying home. Public transport is a mess.","t":"{{Avez-vous}} des {{bons plans}} pour le week-end ? – Non, je reste {{chez moi}}. C’est {{la pagaille}} dans les transports."},{"cue":"I’m just looking. But, who knows? Perhaps I’ll find something that I like.","t":"{{Je ne fais que regarder}} mais, {{qui sait ? Je trouverai}} peut-être quelque chose qui {{me plaît}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"les soldes","en":"the sales","zh":"打折季，减价","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce sont les soldes en janvier, et ça tombe bien parce que j’ai grand besoin d’un nouveau chapeau.","exEn":"It’s the sales in January, and it’s good timing because I really need a new hat.","src":1},{"fr":"ça tombe bien","en":"that’s good timing, how convenient","zh":"来得正好，真巧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce sont les soldes en janvier, et ça tombe bien parce que j’ai grand besoin d’un nouveau chapeau.","exEn":"It’s the sales in January, and it’s good timing because I really need a new hat.","src":1},{"fr":"un nouveau chapeau","en":"a new hat","zh":"一顶新帽子","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"Ce sont les soldes en janvier, et ça tombe bien parce que j’ai grand besoin d’un nouveau chapeau.","exEn":"It’s the sales in January, and it’s good timing because I really need a new hat.","src":1},{"fr":"un bon plan","en":"a good deal, a top tip","zh":"好主意，划算的门路","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce n’est pas une question de courage. Il y a des bons plans à saisir partout.","exEn":"It’s not a question of courage. There are good deals to be had everywhere.","src":3},{"fr":"ça me ferait plaisir","en":"I’d like that, it would make me happy","zh":"我会很高兴的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si tu voulais, tu pourrais m’accompagner. Ça me ferait plaisir.","exEn":"If you wanted to, you could come with me. I’d like that.","src":4},{"fr":"la pagaille","en":"chaos, a mess","zh":"混乱","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “pag-eye”: a pie in your eye makes a mess and chaos","ex":"Je hais les centres commerciaux, tu le sais bien. C’est la pagaille à tous les étages !","exEn":"I hate shopping centres, you know that. It’s chaos on every floor!","src":6},{"fr":"quant aux boutiques de mode","en":"as for the fashion stores","zh":"至于时装店","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"quant à ≠ quand (when)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quant aux boutiques de mode, n’en parlons pas. On peut à peine respirer, tellement il y a de monde.","exEn":"As for the fashion stores, don’t mention them. You can barely breathe, there are so many people.","src":8},{"fr":"on peut à peine respirer","en":"you can barely breathe","zh":"几乎无法呼吸","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quant aux boutiques de mode, n’en parlons pas. On peut à peine respirer, tellement il y a de monde.","exEn":"As for the fashion stores, don’t mention them. You can barely breathe, there are so many people.","src":8},{"fr":"nous ne faisons que regarder","en":"we’re just looking","zh":"我们只是看看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Merci, mais pas pour l’instant. Nous ne faisons que regarder.","exEn":"No thank you, not at the moment. We’re just looking.","src":11},{"fr":"je suis à la recherche de mon chapeau","en":"I’m looking for my hat","zh":"我在找我的帽子","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Toi, peut-être. Mais moi, je suis à la recherche de mon chapeau et, éventuellement, de bottes.","exEn":"You, perhaps. But I’m looking for my hat and possibly some boots.","src":12},{"fr":"pendant que tu y es","en":"while you’re at it","zh":"顺便，既然你在那儿","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et pourquoi pas un nouveau manteau pendant que tu y es ?","exEn":"And why not a new coat while you’re at it?","src":13},{"fr":"j’ai justement ce qu’il vous faut","en":"I’ve got just the thing for you","zh":"我正好有您需要的东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai justement ce qu’il vous faut, madame. Par ici, s’il vous plaît.","exEn":"I’ve got just the thing for you, ma’am. This way, please.","src":14},{"fr":"Lequel des deux me va le mieux ?","en":"Which of the two suits me best?","zh":"两件里哪一件更适合我？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’hésite entre cette parka et cet imperméable. Lequel des deux me va le mieux ?","exEn":"I’m hesitating between this parka and this raincoat. Which of the two suits me best?","src":15},{"fr":"il vous va à merveille","en":"it suits you perfectly","zh":"它非常适合您","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Prenez l’imperméable. Il est bien à votre taille, il tombe bien, et il vous va à merveille.","exEn":"Take the raincoat. It’s your size, it hangs well and it suits you perfectly.","src":16},{"fr":"il se trouve que","en":"it so happens that","zh":"碰巧，恰好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, mais il se trouve que ce modèle est soldé à moins soixante pour cent aujourd’hui.","exEn":"No, but it just so happens that this model is on sale at a sixty per cent discount today.","src":18},{"fr":"Il n’en est pas question !","en":"No way! Out of the question!","zh":"绝对不行！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il n’en est pas question ! Où se trouve le rayon bricolage ?","exEn":"No way! Where’s the DIY department?","src":20}],"uncertain":["ex2 item 5: gap division after ‘regarder’ assumed from the answer list (‘Je ne fais que regarder - qui sait ? Je trouverai - me plaît’)"],"pdfPage":441,"gaps":[[2.14,4.12],[5.04,7.84],[45.24,46.32],[98.6,101.94],[104.26,106.62],[109.82,113.14],[117.72,120.72],[125.36,128.46],[133.3,136.26],[139.84,143.84]],"dur":143.9},{"n":75,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le Prix “Accord majeur”","titleEn":"The “Major Chord” Prize","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bienvenue à l’émission qui récompense tous les talents de toutes les musiques.","en":"Welcome to the show that rewards all talents in all types of music.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons l’immense plaisir d’accueillir le célèbre chanteur Alain Poupon","en":"We have the great pleasure of welcoming the famous singer Alain Poupon","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"qui entame sa toute dernière tournée après une carrière longue de quatre décennies.","en":"who is beginning his final tour after a four-decade career.","lit":"his all last tour; career long of four decades"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Il recevra le prix “Accord majeur”, décerné à un musicien ou une musicienne d’exception pour l’ensemble de sa carrière.","en":"He will receive the “Major Chord” prize, awarded to an outstanding musician for his or her entire career.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Bonsoir Alain, et merci d’être venu. Vous avez eu une vie fabuleuse, n’est-ce pas ?","en":"Good evening Alain, and thanks for coming. You’ve had a fabulous life, haven’t you?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous avez même dit dans une interview que personne n’avait accompli autant de choses que vous.","en":"You even said in an interview that no one had accomplished as much as you have.","lit":"as much of things"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Je doute d’avoir dit exactement ça, mais je n’ai jamais douté de mon talent.","en":"I doubt that I said exactly that, but I never doubted my talent.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Je m’en doutais ! Vous avez eu une carrière passionnante, et très mouvementée.","en":"I thought so! You’ve had an exciting and very eventful career.","lit":"I of-it doubted"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça vous pouvez le dire ! J’ai fait un tas de choses que je n’aurais jamais imaginées","en":"You can say that again! I’ve done a lot of things that I would never have imagined","lit":"that you can say it; a heap of things"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"avant de devenir chanteur, mais j’ai toujours été très apprécié.","en":"before I became a singer, but I’ve always been very much appreciated.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"À l’école, j’avais beaucoup de succès et plein de potes.","en":"At school, I was very popular and I had loads of mates.","lit":"much of success; full of mates"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut dire que j’étais très fort dans toutes les matières, pas seulement la musique.","en":"It has to be said that I was very good at all subjects, not only music.","lit":"strong"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai quitté le lycée à seize ans parce qu’on ne pouvait plus rien m’apprendre.","en":"I left high school at sixteen because no one could teach me anything else.","lit":"one could not more nothing me teach"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Ensuite, j’ai touché à tout. J’ai bossé dans une librairie, puis comme maçon, et électricien.","en":"After that, I did a bit of everything. I worked in a bookstore, then as a builder and electrician.","lit":"I touched to everything; mason"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Enfin j’ai rencontré le guitariste Charlot, avec qui j’ai entamé ma brillante carrière.","en":"Finally I met the guitarist Charlot, with whom I started my brilliant career.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai d’abord été pianiste et trompettiste avant de me mettre à chanter.","en":"I started out as a pianist and trumpet player before starting to sing.","lit":"I first was"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous saviez faire tout ça ? Mais vous étiez surdoué !","en":"You knew how to do all that? But you were exceptionally gifted!","lit":"over-gifted"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"J’étais bien obligé ! Vous savez, il faut avoir confiance en soi et en son talent","en":"I had to be! You know, you have to have confidence in yourself and in your talent","lit":"one must have confidence in oneself and in one’s talent"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"et je n’en ai jamais douté. Je dois avouer que je suis confiant de nature.","en":"and I never doubted it. I must admit that I am confident by nature.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Qui suis-je pour vous contredire ? Et maintenant, notre Questionnaire de Proust :","en":"Who am I to contradict you? And now, our Proust Questionnaire:","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"“Quelle qualité appréciez-vous le plus chez les gens ?”","en":"“What quality do you appreciate most in people?”","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"La modestie. Il faut aimer les autres comme soi-même.","en":"Modesty. You have to love other people as you do yourself.","lit":"as oneself"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The regular verb entamer literally means to cut or slice into (la tranche d’entame is the first slice of a loaf or ham). It is often used as a synonym for commencer, i.e. to begin an action lasting a certain length of time (Nous avons commencé / entamé notre voyage). But while commencer can be both transitive and intransitive (Le voyage a commencé), entamer is transitive only."},{"ref":"2","text":"Time periods are not used as preceding adjectives in French. For example, a three-year programme is un programme de trois ans. But they can be used with the partitive de to add extra information: longue de trois ans. (See also lesson 77.)"},{"ref":"3","text":"The regular verb décerner (the root of discern) means to award: Le Prix Goncourt a été décerné à Proust en 1919, The Goncourt Prize was awarded to Proust in 1919. Moreover, un prix, a prize (lesson 39 line 12), can also be translated as an award. In certain contexts, especially music and business prizes, French has unfortunately adopted les Awards. To be avoided at all costs!"},{"ref":"4","text":"douter (the root of doubt) can be used transitively and pronominally, with a slight difference in meaning. The transitive form (lesson 54 line 16) means to doubt and, with a direct object, is followed by de: Malgré trois excellents albums, elle a toujours douté de ses qualités de chanteuse, Despite three excellent albums, she has always doubted her abilities as a singer. By contrast, se douter de means to suspect or guess something: Je ne savais pas mais je m’en suis douté, I didn’t know but I suspected it. A useful expression, used as a rejoinder, is Je m’en doute ! I can imagine, I’m sure you do/are!, etc."},{"ref":"5","text":"Remember that the past participle behaves as an adjective and agrees with the direct object (here, the feminine plural choses) if it comes before the verb. Compare that with Il a utilisé des mots (m.) que je n’aurais jamais imaginés, He used words that I never would have imagined. Of course, the two forms of the participle are pronounced identically."},{"ref":"6","text":"Another commonly used idiomatic noun is un pote, a mate, a buddy. Unlike un copain (lesson 34 note 6) it does not imply a romantic interest. Formerly used only in closed circles, the word came back to prominence in the late 20th century with the anti-racist catchphrase Touche pas à mon pote, Don’t touch my buddy, now the official slogan of an NGO collective."},{"ref":"7","text":"toucher means to touch: Cette armoire touche le plafond, This cupboard is touching the ceiling. As a transitive indirect verb (which acts on or for its object), it requires the preposition à: Il n’a pas touché à sa nourriture, He hasn’t touched his food. The idiom toucher à tout means to try one’s hand at everything."},{"ref":"8","text":"Yet another common slang word, bosser means to work and is used instead of travailler. Depending on the context, it can also mean to slog away, to swot, etc. Tu viens ? – Non, je bosse mes cours, Are you coming? – No, I’m swotting up on my lessons."},{"ref":"9","text":"The pronominal form of mettre, like most common verbs, has several meanings. When followed by à (thus au/aux) and either an object or an infinitive, it can mean to start or get down to something: Je vais me mettre au travail dès demain, I’m going to get down to work tomorrow. (See lesson 77 for more about the difference between base and pronominal verbs.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"The impersonal third-person emphatic pronoun soi (referenced in lesson 49 section 2) means one or oneself but is much less formal in French than in English. It is often used with an indefinite pronoun: Chacun pour soi, Every person for themselves, or in a sentence with no antecedent: Il faut tout faire soi-même, You have to do everything yourself (very formally, One has to do everything oneself). This impersonal context is underlined by the use of the indefinite pronoun on: On est toujours mieux chez soi, There’s no place like home."},{"ref":"","text":"Marcel Proust (1871–1922) is one of the greatest French novelists and prose stylists. His masterwork, À la recherche du temps perdu (In Search of Lost Time, aka Remembrance of Things Past), was published between 1913 and 1927. Proust coined the term la mémoire involontaire, involuntary memory, to describe the recollections triggered by cues encountered by chance, specifically the memories of childhood triggered when the novel’s protagonist dips a cake, une madeleine, into his tea. Today, the expression la madeleine de Proust refers to a tiny event that stirs up strong emotions. Proust was a fan of an English game called Confessions, where seemingly trivial questions (Quel est votre animal préféré ? What’s your favourite animal?) are framed to discover a person’s inner nature. The game subsequently became known as le questionnaire de Proust and is often used today by chat show hosts to find out more about their guests."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Damien est un vrai touche-à-tout : il était maçon avant de se mettre à la musique.","a":"Damien is a real Jack-of-all-trades: he was a builder before turning his hand to music."},{"q":"Saviez-vous que ce médicament peut être très dangereux ? – Je m’en doutais.","a":"Did you know that this drug can be very dangerous? – I suspected as much."},{"q":"Nous allons nous mettre au travail demain, c’est promis.","a":"We’ll get down to work tomorrow, I promise."},{"q":"Marion a beaucoup de succès, mais je n’ai toujours pas confiance en elle.","a":"Marion is really popular but I still don’t trust her."},{"q":"On lui a décerné deux fois le même prix. Quelle histoire de fou !","a":"He was twice awarded the same prize. What a crazy story!"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"I have to admit that they’re really good. There’s nothing more we can teach them.","t":"{{Je dois avouer}} qu’ils sont très {{forts}}. On {{ne peut plus rien leur}} apprendre."},{"cue":"Even if you don’t want to, you have to do everything yourself and have self-confidence.","t":"{{Même si}} on ne veut pas, il faut {{tout faire soi-même}} et {{avoir confiance en soi}} !"},{"cue":"He doubts that he’s always made the right choices, but he’s never doubted his talents.","t":"{{Il doute d’avoir fait}} toujours les bons choix mais {{il n’a jamais douté de}} ses talents."},{"cue":"Jérémie hasn’t touched his meal. He’s working hard on his French lessons since this morning.","t":"Jérémie {{n’a pas touché à son repas}}. {{Il bosse ses cours}} de français {{depuis ce}} matin."},{"cue":"This week, I learned something that I would never have imagined.","t":"Cette semaine, j’ai appris {{une chose que je n’aurais jamais imaginée}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"l’immense plaisir d’accueillir","en":"the great pleasure of welcoming","zh":"非常荣幸地迎接","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ immense pleasure","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons l’immense plaisir d’accueillir le célèbre chanteur Alain Poupon","exEn":"We have the great pleasure of welcoming the famous singer Alain Poupon","src":2},{"fr":"une carrière","en":"a career","zh":"职业生涯","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ career (same word, French spelling)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il recevra le prix “Accord majeur”, décerné à un musicien ou une musicienne d’exception pour l’ensemble de sa carrière.","exEn":"He will receive the “Major Chord” prize, awarded to an outstanding musician for his or her entire career.","src":4},{"fr":"Je m’en doutais !","en":"I thought so! I suspected as much!","zh":"我就猜到了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je m’en doutais ! Vous avez eu une carrière passionnante, et très mouvementée.","exEn":"I thought so! You’ve had an exciting and very eventful career.","src":8},{"fr":"je n’ai jamais douté de mon talent","en":"I never doubted my talent","zh":"我从未怀疑过自己的才华","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ doubt (douter)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je doute d’avoir dit exactement ça, mais je n’ai jamais douté de mon talent.","exEn":"I doubt that I said exactly that, but I never doubted my talent.","src":7},{"fr":"Ça vous pouvez le dire !","en":"You can say that again!","zh":"你说得太对了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça vous pouvez le dire ! J’ai fait un tas de choses que je n’aurais jamais imaginées","exEn":"You can say that again! I’ve done a lot of things that I would never have imagined","src":9},{"fr":"un pote","en":"a mate, a buddy","zh":"哥们，朋友（口语）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “pot”: your best mate is the one you share a pot of tea with","ex":"À l’école, j’avais beaucoup de succès et plein de potes.","exEn":"At school, I was very popular and I had loads of mates.","src":11},{"fr":"j’avais beaucoup de succès","en":"I was very popular, I was very successful","zh":"我很受欢迎，很成功","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ success (succès)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À l’école, j’avais beaucoup de succès et plein de potes.","exEn":"At school, I was very popular and I had loads of mates.","src":11},{"fr":"j’étais très fort dans toutes les matières","en":"I was very good at all subjects","zh":"我所有科目都很强","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il faut dire que j’étais très fort dans toutes les matières, pas seulement la musique.","exEn":"It has to be said that I was very good at all subjects, not only music.","src":12},{"fr":"j’ai touché à tout","en":"I tried my hand at everything","zh":"我什么都尝试过","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ensuite, j’ai touché à tout. J’ai bossé dans une librairie, puis comme maçon, et électricien.","exEn":"After that, I did a bit of everything. I worked in a bookstore, then as a builder and electrician.","src":14},{"fr":"j’ai bossé dans une librairie","en":"I worked in a bookstore","zh":"我在书店干过活","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"librairie = bookshop, NOT library (bibliothèque)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ensuite, j’ai touché à tout. J’ai bossé dans une librairie, puis comme maçon, et électricien.","exEn":"After that, I did a bit of everything. I worked in a bookstore, then as a builder and electrician.","src":14},{"fr":"avant de me mettre à chanter","en":"before starting to sing","zh":"在开始唱歌之前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai d’abord été pianiste et trompettiste avant de me mettre à chanter.","exEn":"I started out as a pianist and trumpet player before starting to sing.","src":16},{"fr":"il faut avoir confiance en soi","en":"you have to have confidence in yourself","zh":"要有自信","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ confidence (confiance)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’étais bien obligé ! Vous savez, il faut avoir confiance en soi et en son talent","exEn":"I had to be! You know, you have to have confidence in yourself and in your talent","src":18},{"fr":"je dois avouer que","en":"I must admit that","zh":"我必须承认","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et je n’en ai jamais douté. Je dois avouer que je suis confiant de nature.","exEn":"and I never doubted it. I must admit that I am confident by nature.","src":19},{"fr":"Qui suis-je pour vous contredire ?","en":"Who am I to contradict you?","zh":"我怎么敢反驳您呢？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ contradict (contredire)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qui suis-je pour vous contredire ? Et maintenant, notre Questionnaire de Proust :","exEn":"Who am I to contradict you? And now, our Proust Questionnaire:","src":20}],"uncertain":["note 5: second example (words ‘que je n’aurais jamais imaginés’) reconstructed from partly garbled OCR","note 10: ‘referenced in lesson 49 section 2’ reconstructed from garbled OCR"],"pdfPage":447,"gaps":[[1.88,3.84],[5.84,8.76],[111.46,114.96],[117.16,119.08],[123.98,126.66],[130.74,133.68],[135.9,138.68],[142.04,145.1],[149.1,152.86]],"dur":152.9},{"n":76,"kind":"lesson","title":"“Culture générale” : le jeu radiophonique","titleEn":"“General Knowledge”: the radio quiz show","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Cette semaine nous sommes dans le beau village de Montluc, avec deux candidats : Marthe et Lucien.","en":"This week we’re in the beautiful village of Montluc, with two contestants: Marthe and Lucien.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais d’abord, une petite précision car une erreur s’est glissée dans notre dernière émission.","en":"But first, a little clarification because an error crept into our last show.","lit":"slid itself into"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Des dizaines de mails nous ont été envoyés pour préciser que Napoléon est né en Corse.","en":"Dozens of emails have been sent to us to specify that Napoleon was born in Corsica.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"On a donc répondu brillamment à notre question. C’est nous qui ne sommes pas brillants !","en":"So our question was answered brilliantly. We’re the ones who aren’t brilliant!","lit":"one has answered; it is us who"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, place au jeu ! Marthe, on commence avec vous. Allez, c’est parti !","en":"Now, on with the game! Marthe, we’ll start with you. And we’re off!","lit":"place to-the game"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Quel tableau, qui mesure soixante mètres de large et dix mètres de haut, est la plus grande fresque du monde ?","en":"Which painting, which is 60 metres wide and 10 metres high, is the world’s largest fresco?","lit":"measures ... of wide and of high"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Que c’est difficile ! Je ne m’y connais pas en art mais je vais tenter le coup : est-ce la Joconde ?","en":"That’s so difficult! I don’t know anything about art, but I’ll give it a try: is it the Mona Lisa?","lit":"try the blow"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonne tentative mais, non. Celle-ci ne mesure que quatre-vingts centimètres de hauteur et un peu plus de cinquante de longueur.","en":"Nice try, but no. That one is only 80 centimetres high and a little over 50 in length.","lit":"measures only ... of height"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"La bonne réponse sera donnée à la fin de l’émission. Lucien, c’est à votre tour.","en":"The correct answer will be given at the end of the show. Lucien, it’s your turn.","lit":"it is at your turn"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Quel est le fleuve le plus profond de France, qui prend sa source en Suisse ?","en":"What is the deepest river in France, which rises in Switzerland?","lit":"takes its source"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Euh… je l’ai sur le bout de la langue…","en":"Er… it’s on the tip of my tongue…","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"On se dépêche s’il vous plaît !","en":"Please hurry up!","lit":"one hurries itself"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous n’êtes pas très patient !","en":"You are not very patient!","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Au contraire, j’attends patiemment depuis deux minutes mais votre temps est écoulé.","en":"On the contrary, I have been waiting patiently for two minutes but your time is up.","lit":"your time is elapsed"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est une question fréquente dans ce type de jeux : on la pose fréquemment.","en":"This is a frequent question in this type of game: it is asked frequently.","lit":"one it asks"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Et quand on y réfléchit, la réponse est évidente : c’est le Rhône, évidemment !","en":"And when you think about it, the answer is obvious: it’s the Rhône, obviously!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Qui a écrit “Les Misérables” et a créé quelques-uns des personnages les plus mémorables de notre littérature ?","en":"Who wrote “Les Misérables” and created some of the most memorable characters in our literature?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Mm… Ça me dit quelque chose. Est-ce un auteur récent ?","en":"Mm… Sounds familiar. Is it a recent author?","lit":"that to-me says something"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, il n’a rien écrit récemment car il est mort depuis presque un siècle et demi.","en":"No, he hasn’t written anything recently because he’s been dead for almost a century and a half.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour moi, la réponse est évidente mais pas pour vous évidemment : c’était Victor Hugo.","en":"For me, the answer is obvious, but obviously not for you: it was Victor Hugo.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"On va recommencer l’émission la semaine prochaine avec deux candidats un peu plus cultivés.","en":"We’re going to start the show again next week with two slightly more cultured candidates.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Au revoir et à bientôt !","en":"Goodbye and see you soon!","lit":"to soon"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"We know the adjective précis (lesson 29 line 8) and the adverb précisément (lesson 17 line 9). But the regular verb préciser has no direct equivalent. It means to specify, clarify, etc. Nous voudrions préciser quelques détails, We would like to clarify a few details. Thus, while the general noun la précision means precision, accuracy, the countable form means a clarification, detail, etc.: J’ai besoin d’une précision, I need a clarification."},{"ref":"2","text":"Unlike précis / précisément, some adverb formations are slightly irregular. Adjectives ending in -ant form their adverb by replacing the last two letters with m, then adding the usual -ment suffix: brillant ➔ brillamment (lesson 61 line 19). See also note 8 and the pronunciation notes."},{"ref":"3","text":"Unlike in English, the French passive voice can’t be used with an indirect object. So a sentence like My question has been answered by the audience is not directly translatable. The options are to use the impersonal pronoun: On a répondu à ma question, in which case the indirect object public is dropped, or the demonstrative c’est… + qui: C’est le public qui a répondu à ma question."},{"ref":"4","text":"faire place à means to make way for or give way to: La croissance économique rapide a fait place à une expansion plus modérée, Rapid economic growth has made way for a more moderate expansion. The expression Place à (Place aux jeunes, Make way for the young) is often used as an introduction. Et maintenant, place à la fête, Now it’s party time."},{"ref":"5","text":"Dimensions, such as height (la hauteur), length (la longueur) and width (la largeur), are among the kinds of information introduced by de (lesson 75 note 2). For example: L’horloge de la gare mesure neuf mètres de hauteur, The station clock is 9 metres high. But we can also use the adjectives haut, large and long as (masculine) nouns: …neuf mètres de haut. There is no difference in meaning between the two forms."},{"ref":"6","text":"que can be used, like quel, to introduce an exclamatory expression, often as the equivalent of how: Que c’est beau ! How beautiful! When followed by a personal pronoun, however, que is not necessarily translated: Que je suis fatigué ! I’m really tired!"},{"ref":"7","text":"Literally, se connaître means to know oneself: Je me connais : si je ne le fais pas maintenant, je ne le ferai pas du tout, I know myself: if I don’t do it now, I won’t do it at all. But with the pronoun y, the meaning is to know about or be skilled in something. Elle s’y connaît en littérature française, She knows a lot about French literature. Note the use of en to introduce the complement."},{"ref":"8","text":"Adjectives ending in -ent form their adverb by replacing the last two letters with m, then adding the usual -ment suffix: patient ➔ patiemment. See also note 2 and the pronunciation notes."},{"ref":"pron","text":"Two common adverbial suffixes are -emment, formed from adjectives ending in -ent (fréquent ➔ fréquemment), and -amment, from adjectives ending in -ant (brillant ➔ brillamment). In both cases, the initial vowel of the suffix (e, a) is pronounced as an open [a]: [breeyahmœh’] / [fraykahmœh’]. Listen carefully to sentences 4 and 15."},{"ref":"","text":"Victor Hugo (1802–1885), a novelist, poet, dramatist, politician and social historian, is one of France’s greatest literary figures. Outside his home country, he is best known for his novels Notre-Dame de Paris (The Hunchback of Notre Dame), published in 1831, and Les Misérables (literally: “the wretched poor,” “the dispossessed,” 1862). The latter book is so sweeping in its scale and scope (one chapter even recounts the Battle of Waterloo) that it is justifiably known as une épopée du peuple, a people’s epic.\n(To answer the first quiz question, the world’s largest painting is La Fée Électricité, The Electricity Fairy, by Raoul Dufy, which hangs in le Musée d’Art Moderne in Paris.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"On se dépêche, s’il te plaît ! Allez, c’est parti. À bientôt, j’espère.","a":"Let’s hurry up please. Right, we’re off. See you soon, I hope."},{"q":"Son nom nous dit quelque chose mais nous ne pensons pas l’avoir rencontré.","a":"His name sounds familiar but we don’t think we’ve met him."},{"q":"Il va te demander des conseils car il ne s’y connaît pas en investissement.","a":"He’s going to ask you for some advice because he doesn’t know anything about investment."},{"q":"Ça sera difficile mais tentons le coup quand même.","a":"It will be difficult but let’s give it a try anyway."},{"q":"L’auteur de “Notre-Dame de Paris” ? Attends, je l’ai sur le bout de la langue…","a":"The author of “The Hunchback of Notre Dame”? Wait, it’s on the tip of my tongue…"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Jean-Philippe is brilliant. He brilliantly answered all the questions that he was asked.","t":"Jean-Philippe est {{brillant}}. Il {{a répondu brillamment à}} toutes les questions {{qu’on lui a posées}}."},{"cue":"Nathalie knows herself very well. She knows that if she doesn’t leave tomorrow, she will never leave.","t":"Nathalie {{se connaît très bien}}. Elle sait que {{si elle ne part pas}} demain {{elle ne partira jamais}}."},{"cue":"It’s so beautiful! Who wrote this poem? – The answer is obvious.","t":"{{Que c’est}} beau ! {{Qui a écrit}} ce poème ? – {{La réponse}} est {{évidente}}."},{"cue":"My girlfriend isn’t very patient. – On the contrary, she’s been waiting patiently for you for two hours.","t":"Ma copine {{n’est pas très patiente}}. – {{Au contraire}}, {{elle t’attend patiemment depuis}} deux heures."},{"cue":"Annie wrote the letter, but a few errors crept into it. We need clarifications.","t":"Annie a écrit la lettre mais {{quelques erreurs}} s’y {{sont glissées}}. Nous avons {{besoin de précisions}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"une petite précision","en":"a little clarification","zh":"一点说明，补充","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ precision (but countable: a clarification)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais d’abord, une petite précision car une erreur s’est glissée dans notre dernière émission.","exEn":"But first, a little clarification because an error crept into our last show.","src":2},{"fr":"une erreur s’est glissée","en":"an error crept in","zh":"出现了一个错误","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais d’abord, une petite précision car une erreur s’est glissée dans notre dernière émission.","exEn":"But first, a little clarification because an error crept into our last show.","src":2},{"fr":"on a répondu brillamment à notre question","en":"our question was answered brilliantly","zh":"我们的问题被出色地回答了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ brilliant → brillamment (-ant → -amment)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On a donc répondu brillamment à notre question. C’est nous qui ne sommes pas brillants !","exEn":"So our question was answered brilliantly. We’re the ones who aren’t brilliant!","src":4},{"fr":"Allez, c’est parti !","en":"Right, we’re off!","zh":"好，开始吧！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, place au jeu ! Marthe, on commence avec vous. Allez, c’est parti !","exEn":"Now, on with the game! Marthe, we’ll start with you. And we’re off!","src":5},{"fr":"je ne m’y connais pas en art","en":"I don’t know anything about art","zh":"我对艺术一窍不通","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Que c’est difficile ! Je ne m’y connais pas en art mais je vais tenter le coup : est-ce la Joconde ?","exEn":"That’s so difficult! I don’t know anything about art, but I’ll give it a try: is it the Mona Lisa?","src":7},{"fr":"je vais tenter le coup","en":"I’ll give it a try","zh":"我来试试看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Que c’est difficile ! Je ne m’y connais pas en art mais je vais tenter le coup : est-ce la Joconde ?","exEn":"That’s so difficult! I don’t know anything about art, but I’ll give it a try: is it the Mona Lisa?","src":7},{"fr":"c’est à votre tour","en":"it’s your turn","zh":"轮到您了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La bonne réponse sera donnée à la fin de l’émission. Lucien, c’est à votre tour.","exEn":"The correct answer will be given at the end of the show. Lucien, it’s your turn.","src":9},{"fr":"un fleuve","en":"a river (flowing to the sea)","zh":"大河（入海的河）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"fleuve = river that flows into the sea; rivière = tributary","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel est le fleuve le plus profond de France, qui prend sa source en Suisse ?","exEn":"What is the deepest river in France, which rises in Switzerland?","src":10},{"fr":"je l’ai sur le bout de la langue","en":"it’s on the tip of my tongue","zh":"就在嘴边，想不起来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Euh… je l’ai sur le bout de la langue…","exEn":"Er… it’s on the tip of my tongue…","src":11},{"fr":"On se dépêche s’il vous plaît !","en":"Please hurry up!","zh":"请快一点！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On se dépêche s’il vous plaît !","exEn":"Please hurry up!","src":12},{"fr":"Vous n’êtes pas très patient !","en":"You are not very patient!","zh":"您不太有耐心啊！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ patient","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous n’êtes pas très patient !","exEn":"You are not very patient!","src":13},{"fr":"votre temps est écoulé","en":"your time is up","zh":"您的时间到了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Au contraire, j’attends patiemment depuis deux minutes mais votre temps est écoulé.","exEn":"On the contrary, I have been waiting patiently for two minutes but your time is up.","src":14},{"fr":"la réponse est évidente","en":"the answer is obvious","zh":"答案显而易见","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ evident","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et quand on y réfléchit, la réponse est évidente : c’est le Rhône, évidemment !","exEn":"And when you think about it, the answer is obvious: it’s the Rhône, obviously!","src":16},{"fr":"Ça me dit quelque chose.","en":"That rings a bell. It sounds familiar.","zh":"这个听起来有点耳熟。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mm… Ça me dit quelque chose. Est-ce un auteur récent ?","exEn":"Mm… Sounds familiar. Is it a recent author?","src":18}],"uncertain":["note 5: example sentence reconstructed (‘L’horloge de la gare mesure neuf mètres de hauteur’) from garbled OCR","note 1: lesson cross-references (29 line 8, 17 line 9) as read from OCR"],"pdfPage":455,"gaps":[[1.8,3.9],[7.02,9.7],[15.62,16.9],[37.46,38.74],[47.12,48.14],[74.82,75.86],[92.88,93.88],[121.36,124.96],[127,129.02],[133.16,136.02],[139.32,141.98],[145.22,147.84],[150.12,153.06],[159.16,163]],"dur":163},{"n":77,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi es-tu de mauvaise humeur ? On dirait que ça ne va pas fort.","en":"Why are you in a bad mood? It looks like things aren’t going well.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’en peux plus. Avant-hier, j’ai bossé toute la journée jusqu’à vingt-deux heures,","en":"I can’t take it anymore. The day before yesterday I worked all day until 10 pm","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"mais je n’ai pas pu rentrer chez moi en voiture parce que l’autoroute a été fermée.","en":"but I couldn’t drive home because the motorway was closed.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Finalement, j’ai dû rentrer à la maison à pied. Quelle galère !","en":"Finally I had to walk home. What a pain!","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"On a fermé l’autoroute ? Mais pourquoi ?","en":"The motorway was closed? But why?","lit":"one has closed"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’en sais rien. Tu me casses les pieds avec tes questions !","en":"I don’t know. You’re pestering me with your questions!","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"La décision a été prise de la fermer pendant plusieurs jours. C’est tout.","en":"The decision was made to close it for several days. That’s it.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"On nous annonce qu’il est “vivement recommandé de prendre les transports en commun”.","en":"We are told that it is “strongly recommended to take public transport”.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Au fait, la route est en mauvais état et il faut la réparer.","en":"By the way, the road is in bad condition and needs to be repaired.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Il paraît qu’ils ont fait appel à une entreprise étrangère pour faire les travaux.","en":"Apparently they have called in a foreign company to do the work.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"En tout cas, rien ne marche comme il faut et il est impossible de circuler normalement.","en":"In any case, nothing is working as it should and it’s impossible to drive normally.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça tombe mal car je suis débordée cette semaine.","en":"It’s bad timing because I’m snowed under this week.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est la pagaille dans les transports en commun, qui sont en grève.","en":"It’s a mess on public transport, which is on strike.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais être obligée de me mettre au vélo.","en":"I’m going to have to get on my bike.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Je m’en doutais ! C’est pour ça que tu es d’une humeur de chien.","en":"I thought so! That’s why you are in a foul mood.","lit":"of a dog’s mood"}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Le passif : the passive voice","text":"The passive is used if the object – a person or thing – of an active statement is more important than the subject. The French passive voice is almost identical to its English counterpart in terms of construction. But it is used less frequently, for various reasons.\n1.1 Formation\nThe passive voice of a verb is formed with être and the past participle.\nLes gens l’aiment, People love him ➔ Il est aimé, He’s loved.\nIn the past tense, être becomes été and the two participles follow each other:\nIls ont fermé le pont hier, They closed the bridge yesterday ➔ Le pont a été fermé hier, The bridge was closed yesterday.\nIn these two examples, the passive is used because the fact of being loved or closed is more important than whoever is doing the loving or closing.\nOne slight difference with the English construction is that the past participle, being an adjective, has to agree with the subject of the passive verb: Ils ont fermé la fenêtre ➔ La fenêtre a été fermée. (Now’s the time to revise your past participles!)\nIf the active subject is incorporated as an agent into the passive sentence, it is preceded by par, by:\nLe maire a fermé le pont, The mayor closed the bridge ➔ Le pont a été fermé par le maire, The bridge was closed by the mayor.\nOf course, the participle agreement rule does not change (L’école a été fermée par le maire). Where the agent is mentioned, par is used for almost all verbs. However, those expressing a feeling or an emotion (aimer, respecter, admirer) or a state (entourer, couvrir, décorer, etc.) tend to use de rather than par:\nLe metteur en scène est respecté de tout le monde. The director is respected by everybody.\nThere are only a handful of such verbs, so they are easy to learn.\nThe negative and first and second interrogatives follow the usual rules:\nLe pont n’a pas été fermé ; Est-ce que le pont a été fermé ?\nThe third interrogative is slightly more complicated – Le pont a-t-il été fermé ? – but is used much less than the other two forms.\n1.2 Use\nThe passive is used less in French than in English, firstly because active forms are preferable when describing actions, and secondly because French has several ways of sidestepping passive constructions. There are three main workarounds: with on, with the pronominal form, and with c’est … qui.\n• with on:\nThe third-person impersonal pronoun on is used when the subject is implied or unimportant. It replaces nous, ils/elles, quelqu’un, tout le monde, etc. So a sentence like Ici, on parle anglais can be used by a shop to inform potential customers that all its staff speak English (We speak English here), or by residents of an area to insist that everyone is anglophone (Everyone here speaks English). As you know, on is also commonly used instead of nous, simply because the first-person plural verb form often sounds too formal. In very formal situations, on can be translated by its English cognate, one. For example, On dit que… can be the equivalent of One says that… but it would generally be rendered by the passive It is said that… In a more colloquial setting, the usual equivalent is They say that…\n• with the pronominal verbs, formed with se:\nThese are more common in French than in English precisely because they are used instead of the passive voice. In this case, there is no notion of myself, itself, etc. For example: Ce plat se mange chaud ou froid means This dish is eaten hot or cold. This form is possible only with the third person singular or plural: Les voitures neuves se vendent bien cette année, New cars are selling well this year. One very common set of short passive phrases is formed with ça (or, more formally, cela) followed by a pronominal verb: Il est français. – Ça s’entend, He’s French. – You can hear it. Elle a très mal. – Cela se voit, She’s in great pain. – That’s obvious, etc.\n• with c’est (plural: ce sont) … qui:\nThis workaround avoids the passive by emphasising the subject. So a sentence like The report was written by my German colleague can be translated literally by Le rapport a été écrit par mon collègue allemand, which is grammatically correct, or, more colloquially, by C’est mon collègue allemand qui a écrit le rapport. A word-for-word translation (“It’s my German colleague who…,” etc.) does not have the same resonance, precisely because French prefers the active to the passive.\nThe main difference between the two languages is that the passive cannot be used with an indirect object in French, unlike English. For example, the English sentence Someone has sent me an email can, if necessary, be turned into a passive by converting the indirect object to a subject: I have been sent an email by someone. This is not possible in French. In cases like this, on can be used as a workaround: On m’a envoyé un mail, I have been sent an email.\nLikewise, if an English passive sentence does not contain a direct object (for example, The email has been answered), it cannot be passive in French: On a répondu au mail."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Adverbs of time","text":"Let’s review this important category of adverbs, most of which you have already learned and assimilated. Remember that many adverbs are formed from the feminine form of adjectives (dernier ➔ dernière ➔ dernièrement). They are placed after a verb consisting of a single word and between the component parts of two-word verbs or a conjugated verb and its auxiliary.\nNow that you have been using most of these words for several months, the etymological explanations below will make them clearer and help you remember them.\n- actuellement, now, at present, at the moment, currently: from the adjective actuel, current. Elle travaille actuellement à Brest, She’s currently working in Brest. Do NOT confuse actuellement and actually (en réalité / en fait).\n- alors: from the adverb lors (see below), alors means at the / that time: Il était alors directeur de l’usine, He was the factory manager at the time. It also means so, then: Alors, tu viens ? So, are you coming?\n- après, next or after(wards): Je l’ai revue longtemps après, I saw her a long time afterwards.\n- auparavant, before(hand), previously, earlier: Elle avait annoncé son départ une semaine auparavant. She had announced her departure a week earlier. A synonym of avant (as an adverb, not a preposition), auparavant is more common in writing than in speech.\n- aussitôt: a synonym of immédiatement, meaning straight away: J’ai appelé un taxi et il est arrivé aussitôt. I called a taxi and it came immediately. Formed from aussi and tôt but always written as a single word.\n- autrefois, formerly, in the past: Autrefois, on vivait différemment, In the past, people lived differently. (from autre, other, and fois, time). Synonyms include dans le passé, in the past and avant (see below).\n- avant: as an adverb, avant is a less formal synonym of autrefois and auparavant (with which it shares an etymology). See dorénavant.\n- bientôt, soon, shortly: On arrive bientôt, We’ll be arriving shortly. Used commonly in the expression à bientôt, See you soon.\n- d’abord, first (of all): Nous allons d’abord t’acheter une casquette. First we’re going to buy you a cap. When citing the main reason for something, d’abord means in the first place: D’abord tu es trop jeune, In the first place, you’re too young.\n- déjà, already: Il est déjà dix-sept heures ! It’s 5 pm already.\n- depuis, for / since, depending on the context: depuis Noël, since Christmas; depuis dix ans, for ten years.\n- dernièrement, lately, recently: Je ne l’ai pas vue dernièrement, I haven’t seen her lately.\n- dorénavant, from now on: Dorénavant, c’est moi qui prendrai les décisions, From now on, I’ll take the decisions. (Note the root word, avant.)\n- enfin, at last, finally: Ils sont enfin arrivés, They’ve arrived at last. (en + fin, in the end). Also used in declarative or explicative contexts: C’est mon mari, enfin, mon copain, He’s my husband, well, my boyfriend.\n- ensuite, next, afterwards: Je téléphonerai d’abord et j’écrirai ensuite. I’ll phone first, then afterwards I’ll write. (suite, from suivre, means continuation, follow-up).\n- jamais, never: Elle n’a jamais lu “Notre-Dame de Paris”. She has never read “The Hunchback of Notre Dame”. In some cases, jamais can be used without ne: Jamais plus ! Never again.\n- longtemps, for a long time, (for) long: Je n’ai pas attendu longtemps, I didn’t wait long. The root adjective (long) is masculine; it cannot be used as an adverb, unlike in English.\n- lors de, during, at the time of: L’ascenseur a été installé lors de la construction de la tour. The lift was installed during the construction of the tower. In modern French, lors is not used alone.\nAs you can see, many of these adverbs are easy to understand intuitively thanks to the knowledge you have already acquired. One sure way to expand your vocabulary is through word association."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Base and pronominal verbs with different meanings","text":"We know that French uses pronominal (i.e. reflexive or reciprocal) verbs extensively in order to avoid the passive voice. In some cases, there are slight idiomatic differences in meaning between the base verb and its pronominal form. As always, the most important factor is context.\n- Faire: The base verb means to do or make, so the literal meaning of the reflexive form is to make oneself: Je me fais un sandwich au jambon, I’m making myself a ham sandwich. Idiomatically, se faire un film or un restaurant means to go out to a movie or for a meal. Another very common idiom is s’en faire, to worry, be concerned about. It is often used in the negative: Ne t’en fais pas, je t’aiderai, Don’t worry, I’ll help you. And the expression Ça se fait / Ça ne se fait pas states what is or is not a proper thing to do: Chez nous, ça ne se fait pas, We don’t do that sort of thing at our house. (See also lesson 57 note 1.)\n- The reflexive form of mettre, to put, is used in relation to clothing: Je n’ai rien à me mettre, I’ve got nothing to wear. It can also indicate movement towards something: Mettez-vous au chaud, Get into the warm. Followed by à, se mettre means to take up (an activity): Il s’est mis au tennis, He’s started playing tennis (see lesson 75 note 9). And where the object is understood, it is replaced by y. Je vais m’y mettre, I’ll get started on it.\n- Other common verb pairs with slight differences include attendre, to wait, and s’attendre, to expect (lesson 59); entendre, to hear, and s’entendre, to get along with (lesson 55); plaindre, to pity, and se plaindre, to complain; douter (de), to doubt, and se douter (de), to guess or suspect (both in lesson 73).\nA simple rule of thumb is that if you can’t extrapolate the meaning of a pronominal verb from its base form, then look in a good bilingual dictionary."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Adjectives of dimension","text":"Dimensions can be expressed in several ways. The adjectives long(ue), haut(e), large and profond(e) are placed before the noun, along with the partitive de: Le bâtiment est haut de trente étages, The building is 30 storeys high. Of course, they have to agree: La Tour Eiffel est haute de trois cent vingt mètres. The Eiffel Tower is 320 metres high.\nAnother possibility is to put the nouns la longueur (length), la hauteur (height), la largeur (width) and la profondeur (depth), preceded by de, after the noun: Le salon fait trois mètres de largeur or Une tour de cent mètres de hauteur. In this case, however, we still use an adjective when translating into English: The living room is three metres wide, A 100-metre high tower.\nLastly, and slightly confusingly, long, haut and large are also masculine nouns (le haut du visage, the upper part of the face, for example) and can be used in the same predicative position as the equivalent adjectives. Thus twenty kilometres long is either vingt kilomètres de long or de longueur; one metre high is un mètre de haut or de hauteur; and two centimetres wide is deux centimètres de large or de largeur. The exception to this duality is profondeur, depth: trois mètres de profondeur (*de profond is incorrect). The only, slight difference is register: the substantive is considered slightly more elegant than the adjective. In practical terms, though, the two are equivalent."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Proverbs and sayings","text":"Maxims are an integral part of any language and are used regularly, either directly or obliquely (“You know what they say about rolling stones…”). In any case, they are an important part of popular culture. Because French and English are closely related, many proverbs are identical or very similar in both languages. We’ve learned Rira bien qui rira le dernier, He who laughs last laughs longest. Here are five more:\nPierre qui roule n’amasse pas mousse, A rolling stone gathers no moss;\nÀ mauvais ouvrier point de bons outils, A bad workman always blames his tools;\nTout ce qui brille n’est pas or, All that glitters is not gold;\nMieux vaut tard que jamais, Better late than never;\nIl ne faut pas mettre tous ses œufs dans le même panier, Don’t put all your eggs in one (or the same) basket.\nWe have also learned an axiom which, though not identical, does have a direct equivalent: C’est l’hôpital qui se moque de la charité (“It’s the hospital that is mocking the charity”), The pot calling the kettle black. In upcoming lessons, we’ll learn more about sayings with the same meaning but slightly different wording."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"Pourquoi es-tu de mauvaise humeur ?","en":"Why are you in a bad mood?","zh":"你为什么心情不好？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pourquoi es-tu de mauvaise humeur ? On dirait que ça ne va pas fort.","exEn":"Why are you in a bad mood? It looks like things aren’t going well.","src":1},{"fr":"l’autoroute a été fermée","en":"the motorway was closed","zh":"高速公路被关闭了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"mais je n’ai pas pu rentrer chez moi en voiture parce que l’autoroute a été fermée.","exEn":"but I couldn’t drive home because the motorway was closed.","src":3},{"fr":"j’ai dû rentrer à la maison à pied","en":"I had to walk home","zh":"我只好步行回家","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Finalement, j’ai dû rentrer à la maison à pied. Quelle galère !","exEn":"Finally I had to walk home. What a pain!","src":4},{"fr":"Je n’en sais rien.","en":"I don’t know (anything about it).","zh":"我一点也不知道。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’en sais rien. Tu me casses les pieds avec tes questions !","exEn":"I don’t know. You’re pestering me with your questions!","src":6},{"fr":"Au fait, la route est en mauvais état","en":"By the way, the road is in bad condition","zh":"顺便说一下，路况很差","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au fait, la route est en mauvais état et il faut la réparer.","exEn":"By the way, the road is in bad condition and needs to be repaired.","src":9},{"fr":"En tout cas, rien ne marche comme il faut","en":"In any case, nothing works as it should","zh":"无论如何，什么都不正常","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En tout cas, rien ne marche comme il faut et il est impossible de circuler normalement.","exEn":"In any case, nothing is working as it should and it’s impossible to drive normally.","src":11},{"fr":"je suis débordée cette semaine","en":"I’m snowed under this week","zh":"这周我忙得不可开交","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça tombe mal car je suis débordée cette semaine.","exEn":"It’s bad timing because I’m snowed under this week.","src":12}],"uncertain":["rev note 4: OCR says the adjectives ‘are placed before the noun, along with the partitive de’; kept as printed although the examples suggest the adjective precedes the measurement","rev note 2: ‘dans le passé’ (OCR ‘dans la passé’) corrected"],"pdfPage":461,"gaps":[[1.88,3.92],[5.08,7.86],[22.54,23.58],[58.62,59.62],[72.86,77.04]],"dur":77},{"n":78,"kind":"lesson","title":"Les voyages forment la jeunesse","titleEn":"Travel broadens the mind","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous avez des projets pour le pont de l’Ascension ? Je suis à la recherche d’inspiration.","en":"Do you have plans for the long weekend of the Ascension? I’m looking for inspiration.","lit":"bridge of the Ascent"},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous allons rendre visite à mes neveux et ma nièce à Nîmes, enfin pas à Nîmes même, mais juste à côté.","en":"We’re going to visit my nephews and niece in Nîmes, well not in Nîmes itself but just next door.","lit":"render visit"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous y allez avec les enfants, j’imagine. Quelle chance ils ont !","en":"You’re going with the children, I imagine. How lucky they are!","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, pas cette fois-ci.","en":"No, not this time.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous le leur avons proposé mais ils n’étaient pas très chauds.","en":"We suggested it to them, but they didn’t seem too keen.","lit":"were not very warm"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Ils sont difficiles, vos gamins. Moi, je sauterais sur l’occasion de visiter la Provence.","en":"Your kids are difficult. Me, I’d jump at the opportunity to visit Provence.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne leur en veux pas. On les y a souvent emmenés quand ils étaient jeunes.","en":"I don’t hold it against them. We often took them there when they were young.","lit":"I of-them of-it want not"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils s’y sont beaucoup amusés.","en":"They had a lot of fun there.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais maintenant ils préfèrent s’amuser avec les copains plutôt que de rester avec leurs parents !","en":"But now they prefer to have fun with their friends rather than stay with their parents!","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Et pourtant, les voyages forment la jeunesse, dit-on.","en":"And yet, they say that travel broadens the mind.","lit":"travels shape the youth"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Je le leur ai dit mais ils n’en ont rien à faire et je n’arrive pas à les convaincre.","en":"I told them that, but they don’t care and I can’t manage to convince them.","lit":"they of-it have nothing to do; I do not arrive to"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils me disent qu’ils ont peur de s’embêter avec les vieux, qu’ils trouvent trop ennuyeux.","en":"They tell me they are afraid of getting bored with old people, whom they find too boring.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Je leur réponds que c’est dans les vieux pots qu’on fait la meilleure soupe.","en":"I tell them that the best soup is made in old pots.","lit":"it’s in the old pots that one makes the best soup"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Qu’importe. Ce sont eux les perdants dans l’histoire.","en":"It doesn’t matter. They’re the losers in the story.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Revenons à notre sujet. Vu que vous serez dans la région nîmoise,","en":"Let’s get back to the subject. Since you’ll be in the Nîmes region,","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"est-ce que vous pouvez m’acheter du miel de lavande et des herbes de Provence ?","en":"can you buy me some lavender honey and Provence herbs?","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr, on en rapporte aussi pour Alex et Théo. Nous leur en avons promis.","en":"Of course, we’re also bringing some back for Alex and Théo. We promised them some.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Et si vous voyez un beau vase en céramique avec un motif de cigales dessus","en":"And if you see a beautiful ceramic vase with a cicada design on it","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"– le souvenir touristique par excellence –, vous pouvez me le prendre ?","en":"– the ultimate tourist souvenir – can you get it for me?","lit":"by excellence"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, je connais une boutique qui en vend : nous y en avons déjà acheté pour d’autres amis.","en":"Yes, I know a shop that sells them: we’ve already bought some there for other friends.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais vu les prix, ça risque de te revenir cher, tu sais.","en":"But given the prices, it could cost you a lot, you know.","lit":"it risks to come back to you expensive"},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors je vais te faire un chèque tout de suite.","en":"Then I’ll write you a cheque right away.","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Ne me le donne pas maintenant ; donne-le-moi à la fin du mois.","en":"Don’t give it to me now; give it to me at the end of the month.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"la recherche, research (see lesson 37 note 3), is used in the expression à la recherche de, to be in search of or, simply, to be looking for. Nous sommes à la recherche de nouveaux locaux, We’re looking for new premises."},{"ref":"2","text":"It is important not to confuse rendre visite, to visit or to go and see a person (see lessons 68 line 3 and 69 line 10), and visiter, to visit a place (lesson 37 line 8). One way of avoiding rendre visite is simply to say aller voir: Nous sommes allés voir Théo hier, We went to see Théo yesterday."},{"ref":"3","text":"When a sentence contains both a direct object pronoun and an indirect object pronoun, they have to follow a strict order. In an affirmative sentence, the direct object le has to precede the indirect leur. (In the singular, and with a feminine direct object, the sentence would read Nous la lui avons proposée.) In a negative sentence (line 23), ne precedes the first pronoun and pas follows the verb: Ne me le donne pas. Look at the examples in this lesson (noted 3), then check lesson 84 for a fuller explanation."},{"ref":"4","text":"être chaud pour has nothing to do with temperature; it means to be enthusiastic about or keen to do something. Fabien est chaud pour venir avec nous à Nîmes, Fabien is enthusiastic about coming with us to Nîmes. Arguably, the expression is used more often in the negative. Tu viens ? – Je ne suis pas très chaud. Are you coming? – I’m not too keen. (Of course, to be hot is avoir chaud.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"un gamin is a synonym of un(e) gosse (lesson 55 note 10), both of which mean a kid. (The feminine form, une gamine, is the origin of our word gamine, meaning an attractively boyish girl or woman.) The two words are familiar, not slang."},{"ref":"6","text":"Here’s yet another idiomatic construction with faire: n’en avoir rien à faire means not to care or be bothered. Ils sont trop occupés ou, peut-être, ils n’en ont rien à faire, They’re too busy or perhaps they don’t care. The clue to the idiomatic nature of the expression is en, because Ils n’ont rien à faire simply means They have nothing to do."},{"ref":"7","text":"Another meaning of arriver, to arrive, is to manage or succeed. In this case, it is followed by à, and any subsequent verb is in the infinitive: Je n’arrive pas à la joindre par téléphone, I can’t get through to her on the phone."},{"ref":"8","text":"Here is another verb that changes meaning slightly in the pronominal form (see lesson 77): embêter means to pester, annoy, etc.: N’embêtez pas votre frère, il travaille, Don’t pester your brother, he’s working. But s’embêter, a synonym of s’ennuyer, means to be or get bored: Elle s’embête en cours d’anglais, She gets bored in English lessons. In the sense of to bother, the pronominal and base forms can have the same meaning: Ne vous embêtez pas avec ça, Don’t bother with that."},{"ref":"9","text":"Qu’importe is a more elegant form of Peu importe. Both mean It doesn’t matter (lesson 60 note 8)."},{"ref":"10","text":"vu, the past participle of voir, to see, is a useful way of saying in view of (or, more simply, since). It can be followed by a noun: Vu le temps, on restera à la maison, In view of the weather, we’ll stay at home, or by que and a verb clause: Vu qu’il fait beau, on ira à la plage, Since the weather’s fine, we’ll go to the beach. In the que construction, vu que can be used like puisque (lesson 58 line 1)."},{"ref":"11","text":"revenir, to come back, is used idiomatically to talk about cost: Avec l’essence, le voyage reviendra à cent euros, With petrol, the journey will work out at 100 euros. A common expression is Ça revient cher. That costs a lot."},{"ref":"","text":"L’Ascension (“the Ascension”) is a Christian holiday celebrated forty days after Easter. Since it always falls on a Thursday, many people take advantage of a long weekend. (See Culture Note, lesson 18.)\nYour knowledge of French is becoming not only broader, with more grammatical content, but also deeper and more colloquial. In this lesson, you learned two more axioms: Les voyages forment la jeunesse and C’est dans les vieux pots qu’on fait la bonne/meilleure soupe, that are used regularly by native speakers. In other words, you are learning “real” French!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"La dernière fois que je l’ai vue, elle n’était qu’une gamine. Aujourd’hui elle étudie à la fac de droit.","a":"The last time that I saw her, she was just a kid. Now she’s studying law at university."},{"q":"Je peux donner un coup de main, vu que j’ai terminé mon travail et n’ai rien à faire avant ce soir.","a":"I can give a hand, since I’ve finished my work and have nothing to do until tonight."},{"q":"Si votre enfant est malade, ne vous embêtez pas à lui prendre la température. Laissez-le dormir.","a":"If your child is sick, don’t bother taking his temperature. Let him sleep."},{"q":"Il n’arrivera pas à convaincre les gens de voter pour lui, mais qu’importe !","a":"He won’t succeed in convincing people to vote for him, but it doesn’t matter."},{"q":"Moi, je préfère écrire à mes copains plutôt que de leur téléphoner. Ça revient moins cher.","a":"I prefer to write to my friends rather than to phone them. It works out less expensive."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The money? I’ll go and get it for you right now. – Don’t give it to me now; give it to me tomorrow.","t":"L’argent ? Je {{vais vous le chercher}} tout de suite. – {{Ne me le donnez pas}} maintenant ; {{donnez-le-moi demain}}."},{"cue":"She prefers to have fun with her girlfriends. It’s her choice and we don’t hold it against her.","t":"Elle préfère {{s’amuser avec ses copines}}. C’est son choix et {{nous ne lui en voulons pas}}."},{"cue":"Will your children go with you? – I suggested it to them but they didn’t jump at the opportunity.","t":"Est-ce que vos enfants {{vous accompagneront}} ? – Je {{le leur ai proposé}} mais ils {{n’ont pas sauté sur l’occasion}}."},{"cue":"We’re going to visit my niece in Nîmes, then we’ll visit the city. – How lucky you are!","t":"Nous allons {{rendre visite à ma nièce}} à Nîmes, puis nous {{visiterons la ville}}. – {{Quelle chance vous avez}} !"},{"cue":"I’m going to bring back some honey for my best friend: I promised her some. – Can you get some for me too, please?","t":"Je vais {{rapporter du miel}} pour ma {{meilleure amie}} : je {{lui en ai promis}}. – Peux-tu {{m’en prendre}} aussi s’il te plaît ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je suis à la recherche d’inspiration","en":"I’m looking for inspiration","zh":"我在寻找灵感","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ research, search","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous avez des projets pour le pont de l’Ascension ? Je suis à la recherche d’inspiration.","exEn":"Do you have plans for the long weekend of the Ascension? I’m looking for inspiration.","src":1},{"fr":"rendre visite à mes neveux","en":"to visit my nephews (people)","zh":"去看望我的侄子们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"rendre visite à = visit a person; visiter = visit a place","cluster":"family","kw":"","ex":"Nous allons rendre visite à mes neveux et ma nièce à Nîmes, enfin pas à Nîmes même, mais juste à côté.","exEn":"We’re going to visit my nephews and niece in Nîmes, well not in Nîmes itself but just next door.","src":2},{"fr":"ils n’étaient pas très chauds","en":"they weren’t very keen","zh":"他们不太热衷","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"être chaud pour = to be keen on (not hot)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous le leur avons proposé mais ils n’étaient pas très chauds.","exEn":"We suggested it to them, but they didn’t seem too keen.","src":5},{"fr":"je sauterais sur l’occasion","en":"I’d jump at the opportunity","zh":"我会抓住这个机会","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont difficiles, vos gamins. Moi, je sauterais sur l’occasion de visiter la Provence.","exEn":"Your kids are difficult. Me, I’d jump at the opportunity to visit Provence.","src":6},{"fr":"un gamin","en":"a kid","zh":"小孩（口语）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils sont difficiles, vos gamins. Moi, je sauterais sur l’occasion de visiter la Provence.","exEn":"Your kids are difficult. Me, I’d jump at the opportunity to visit Provence.","src":6},{"fr":"je ne leur en veux pas","en":"I don’t hold it against them","zh":"我不怪他们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne leur en veux pas. On les y a souvent emmenés quand ils étaient jeunes.","exEn":"I don’t hold it against them. We often took them there when they were young.","src":7},{"fr":"ils s’y sont beaucoup amusés","en":"they had a lot of fun there","zh":"他们在那里玩得很开心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils s’y sont beaucoup amusés.","exEn":"They had a lot of fun there.","src":8},{"fr":"ils n’en ont rien à faire","en":"they don’t care","zh":"他们根本不在乎","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je le leur ai dit mais ils n’en ont rien à faire et je n’arrive pas à les convaincre.","exEn":"I told them that, but they don’t care and I can’t manage to convince them.","src":11},{"fr":"je n’arrive pas à les convaincre","en":"I can’t manage to convince them","zh":"我没能说服他们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ convince (convaincre)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je le leur ai dit mais ils n’en ont rien à faire et je n’arrive pas à les convaincre.","exEn":"I told them that, but they don’t care and I can’t manage to convince them.","src":11},{"fr":"ils ont peur de s’embêter","en":"they’re afraid of getting bored","zh":"他们怕会觉得无聊","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ils me disent qu’ils ont peur de s’embêter avec les vieux, qu’ils trouvent trop ennuyeux.","exEn":"They tell me they are afraid of getting bored with old people, whom they find too boring.","src":12},{"fr":"Qu’importe.","en":"It doesn’t matter.","zh":"没关系。无所谓。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’importe. Ce sont eux les perdants dans l’histoire.","exEn":"It doesn’t matter. They’re the losers in the story.","src":14},{"fr":"vu que vous serez dans la région","en":"since you’ll be in the region","zh":"既然你们会在那个地区","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Revenons à notre sujet. Vu que vous serez dans la région nîmoise,","exEn":"Let’s get back to the subject. Since you’ll be in the Nîmes region,","src":15},{"fr":"du miel de lavande","en":"lavender honey","zh":"薰衣草蜂蜜","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"sounds like “me-yell”: you yell “me!” when you want the honey","ex":"est-ce que vous pouvez m’acheter du miel de lavande et des herbes de Provence ?","exEn":"can you buy me some lavender honey and Provence herbs?","src":16},{"fr":"nous leur en avons promis","en":"we promised them some","zh":"我们答应给他们带一些","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr, on en rapporte aussi pour Alex et Théo. Nous leur en avons promis.","exEn":"Of course, we’re also bringing some back for Alex and Théo. We promised them some.","src":17},{"fr":"ça risque de te revenir cher","en":"it could cost you a lot","zh":"这可能会让你花不少钱","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Mais vu les prix, ça risque de te revenir cher, tu sais.","exEn":"But given the prices, it could cost you a lot, you know.","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 3: the negative example sentence is garbled in the OCR; I used the lesson’s own line 23 (Ne me le donne pas)","note 2: line references (68 line 3, 69 line 10) as read from OCR","sentence 19: opens with a parenthetical dash, not a speaker change (dash=false)"],"pdfPage":471,"gaps":[[2.12,4.38],[6.58,9.62],[107.5,110.76],[112.56,114.44],[119.16,122.38],[127.16,130.36],[135.08,138.28],[141.94,145.06],[150.12,154.08]],"dur":154.1},{"n":79,"kind":"lesson","title":"Il faut que je trouve une bonne excuse…","titleEn":"I have to find a good excuse…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Allô, c’est toi, Thomas ? Je suis content que tu sois là.","en":"Hello, is that you, Thomas? I’m glad that you’re there.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut que je te parle, et il faut que tu sois franc avec moi. Je ne te dérange pas ?","en":"I have to talk to you, and you have to be frank with me. I’m not disturbing you?","lit":"it is necessary that I speak to you"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, non. Je suis heureux que tu m’appelles, Martin. C’est à quel sujet ?","en":"No, no. I’m happy that you’re calling, Martin. What’s this about?","lit":"it is to what subject"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est à propos d’Alexis, l’associé de mon père.","en":"It’s about Alexis, my father’s business partner.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Il veut que je l’aide à peindre son salon et sa salle à manger.","en":"He wants me to help him paint his living room and dining room.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Et alors ? Qu’est-ce qui t’en empêche ? Tu es bien peintre professionnel, non ?","en":"So what? What’s stopping you? You’re a professional painter, aren’t you?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui, c’est vrai, mais tu connais Alexis : il est aussi exigeant que maniaque.","en":"Yes, that’s true, but you know Alexis: he’s as demanding as he is finicky.","lit":"maniacal"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Par exemple, il aimerait que je sois là à l’aube et que je passe toute la journée avec lui.","en":"For example, he’d like me to be there at dawn and spend the whole day with him.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut que j’apporte le matériel – pinceaux, rouleaux, couteaux et tout et tout –","en":"I have to bring the equipment – brushes, rollers, scrapers and all that –","lit":"knives; everything and everything"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"mais aussi que j’amène mon employé pour faire les plafonds et les endroits les plus délicats.","en":"but also that I bring my employee to do the ceilings and the most awkward places.","lit":"delicate"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Et, quand on aura terminé, il faudra sans doute que j’emporte les déchets à la déchetterie !","en":"And, when we’ve finished, I’ll probably have to take the rubbish to the waste collection centre!","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"En résumé, je ne sais pas quoi lui dire. Je ne veux pas qu’il soit fâché contre moi.","en":"In short, I don’t know what to say to him. I don’t want him to be angry with me.","lit":"in summary"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu permets que je sois tout à fait honnête et que je te donne un bon conseil ?","en":"Do you mind if I’m completely honest and give you some good advice?","lit":"you allow that"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Il suffit de le regarder droit dans les yeux et lui dire, gentiment mais fermement :","en":"All you have to do is look him straight in the eye and say to him, gently but firmly:","lit":"it suffices"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"“Je préfère que vous trouviez quelqu’un d’autre, Alexis.”","en":"“I’d rather you found someone else, Alexis.”","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"À défaut, il vaudrait mieux que tu ne sois pas disponible ce jour-là,","en":"Failing that, it would be better if you weren’t available that day,","lit":"at fault"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"que toi et ton apprenti, vous soyez sur un autre chantier","en":"that you and your apprentice were on another job site,","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"ou, au pire, que vous soyez malades tous les deux. Rien de trop grave, bien sûr,","en":"or, at worst, that both of you were sick. Nothing too serious, of course,","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"mais des épidémies de rougeole ou de scarlatine sont bien utiles dans des cas pareils !","en":"but epidemics of measles or scarlet fever are very useful in such cases!","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"N’empêche, j’ai peur qu’il soit vexé quand même.","en":"Even so, I’m afraid he’ll be offended anyway.","lit":"vexed"},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Il ne t’en voudra pas. Il sera triste que tu ne puisses pas l’aider, c’est tout.","en":"He won’t hold it against you. He’ll be sad that you can’t help him, that’s all.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Et comment le sais-tu ?","en":"And how do you know that?","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Parce qu’il m’a posé exactement la même question hier et je lui ai donné exactement la même excuse.","en":"Because he asked me exactly the same question yesterday and I gave him exactly the same excuse.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"This lesson deals with the subjunctive mood, used to express feelings or doubts about a situation or action. It is used with certain verbs in two-part sentences having different subjects. The verbs in our examples (être content, falloir, être heureux, être triste, avoir peur, etc.) are followed by que, which links (or “sub-joins”) them to the second, subjunctive verb in the sentence: J’ai peur qu’il soit fâché, I’m afraid he may be angry."},{"ref":"2","text":"The full, irregular conjugation of être in the subjunctive is (que) je sois, tu sois, il/elle soit, nous soyons, vous soyez, ils/elles soient."},{"ref":"3","text":"See lesson 61 note 1. Since falloir is impersonal, the invariable pronoun il is equivalent to it. In principle, the English subjunctive can be used here to translate sentences like Il faut que nous soyons à l’heure (It is necessary that we be on time). In practice, though, alternative constructions are used (We have to / must / need to be on time)."},{"ref":"4","text":"The subjunctive form of regular -er verbs is the same as the present tense, except for the first and second person plural, which add an i: (que) j’appelle, tu appelles, il/elle appelle, nous appelions, vous appeliez, ils/elles appellent. Remember: since the second verb is subjoined to an initial verb, it is preceded by que: Il faut que nous appelions notre bureau, We have to call our office."},{"ref":"5","text":"empêcher means to prevent or stop: On m’a empêché de sortir, I was prevented from leaving. The associated noun, un empêchement (the root of impeachment), means a difficulty, an obstacle, a hitch, often unexpected: Elle voulait y aller mais elle a eu un empêchement, She wanted to go but something came up. The invariable expression n’empêche, which is informal, is the equivalent of even so, all the same: N’empêche, ils auraient pu téléphoner, All the same, they could have called. Later we’ll see how to use the expression in a less informal context."},{"ref":"6","text":"See lesson 45 note 9. Aussi… que can also be used to compare two adjectives: Elle est aussi intelligente que sympathique, She’s as intelligent as she is nice. In contrast to English, there is no need to repeat the verb. (While the adjective maniaque means fussy, finicky, the noun un maniaque means a maniac or a fanatic.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"The verbs apporter (lesson 26), amener (68) and emmener (78), basically meaning to bring or to take, are often confused. The basic rule is that apporter / emporter means to take something to the place where you are going, amener means the same thing but applies to people or animals that are taken, and emmener means to take someone or living beings away from the place of origin: J’apporte mes outils, j’amène mon assistant et j’emporte les déchets, I bring my tools and my assistant and I take away the rubbish. More details in a later lesson."},{"ref":"8","text":"Here, Thomas switches from tu, used when chatting with his friend, to vous because he is talking about two people: Martin and his apprentice."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here’s another use of a common verb: en vouloir à means to hold something against (see lesson 78 line 7) or to be mad at someone. Martin m’en veut parce que j’ai oublié son anniversaire, Martin is mad at me because I forgot his birthday. A useful expression, used apologetically, is Vous ne m’en voulez pas ? You won’t resent / hold it against me?"},{"ref":"10","text":"The subjunctive of the irregular verb pouvoir is (que) je puisse, tu puisses, il/elle puisse, nous puissions, vous puissiez, ils/elles puissent."},{"ref":"","text":"You’re certainly aware that we have been scaling down the figurative pronunciation section beneath the main dialogue. By now you should be familiar with French sounds and be able to extrapolate from, say, an infinitive like aimer to its past participle aimé or the imperfect aimait. We will continue to provide guidance for new or unusual sounds, but the aim now is to remove the crutch on which you’ve been relying for the past 79 lessons. What’s more, the grammar is becoming more complex. But because you’ve already assimilated many of the constructions before they are explained in the revision lessons, you will find it easier to understand and acquire the underlying mechanisms.\nBonne continuation ! (Onward and upward!)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Nous avons une question à vous poser. – À quel sujet ?","a":"We have a question for you. – What about?"},{"q":"Je ne sais pas quoi leur dire. J’ai peur qu’elles soient vexées si je refuse de les aider.","a":"I don’t know what to tell them. I’m afraid that they will be offended if I refuse to help them."},{"q":"Je sais que c’est un empêchement de dernière minute mais, n’empêche, tu aurais pu me prévenir.","a":"I know it’s a last-minute hitch but, even so, you could have warned me."},{"q":"Il faut qu’on se parle. C’est à propos d’Alexis : il arrive à l’aube demain, et rien n’est encore prêt.","a":"We need to talk. It’s about Alexis: he’s arriving at dawn tomorrow, and nothing’s ready yet."},{"q":"Je suis triste que ta femme soit occupée ce soir car je voulais que nous dînions ensemble, tous les quatre.","a":"I am sad that your wife is busy tonight because I wanted the four of us to have dinner together."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Alex and his son are as intelligent as they are funny. We must invite them one of these days.","t":"Axel et son fils sont {{aussi intelligents que drôles}}. Il {{faut que nous les invitions}} un de ces jours."},{"cue":"He’s bringing his friends, who are going to bring some cakes for the party. Afterwards, I’ll take them to the station.","t":"Il {{amène}} ses amis, qui vont {{apporter}} des {{gâteaux}} pour la fête. Après, je {{les emmène}} à la gare."},{"cue":"My wife is happy that you can spend the day here. Girlfriends are important in cases like this.","t":"Ma femme est {{heureuse que vous puissiez passer}} la journée ici. Les amies sont {{importantes dans des cas pareils}}."},{"cue":"Do you mind if we’re honest and give you some advice?","t":"Vous {{permettez}} que {{nous soyons honnêtes}} et {{que nous vous donnions}} un conseil ?"},{"cue":"It would be better if you and your colleague were the only ones who knew that password.","t":"{{Il vaudrait mieux que}} vous et votre collègue {{soyez les seuls à connaître}} ce mot de passe."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Je suis content que tu sois là.","en":"I’m glad that you’re there.","zh":"你在那儿我很高兴。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Allô, c’est toi, Thomas ? Je suis content que tu sois là.","exEn":"Hello, is that you, Thomas? I’m glad that you’re there.","src":1},{"fr":"Il faut que je te parle.","en":"I have to talk to you.","zh":"我得跟你谈谈。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il faut que je te parle, et il faut que tu sois franc avec moi. Je ne te dérange pas ?","exEn":"I have to talk to you, and you have to be frank with me. I’m not disturbing you?","src":2},{"fr":"C’est à quel sujet ?","en":"What is it about?","zh":"是关于什么的？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, non. Je suis heureux que tu m’appelles, Martin. C’est à quel sujet ?","exEn":"No, no. I’m happy that you’re calling, Martin. What’s this about?","src":3},{"fr":"c’est à propos d’Alexis","en":"it’s about Alexis","zh":"是关于亚历克西斯的事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ apropos","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est à propos d’Alexis, l’associé de mon père.","exEn":"It’s about Alexis, my father’s business partner.","src":4},{"fr":"Qu’est-ce qui t’en empêche ?","en":"What’s stopping you?","zh":"有什么阻止你吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ impeach (empêcher)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et alors ? Qu’est-ce qui t’en empêche ? Tu es bien peintre professionnel, non ?","exEn":"So what? What’s stopping you? You’re a professional painter, aren’t you?","src":6},{"fr":"il est aussi exigeant que maniaque","en":"he’s as demanding as he is finicky","zh":"他既苛刻又吹毛求疵","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oui, c’est vrai, mais tu connais Alexis : il est aussi exigeant que maniaque.","exEn":"Yes, that’s true, but you know Alexis: he’s as demanding as he is finicky.","src":7},{"fr":"à l’aube","en":"at dawn","zh":"在黎明时","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"sounds like “low-b”: at dawn the sun is low, a low bell rings","ex":"Par exemple, il aimerait que je sois là à l’aube et que je passe toute la journée avec lui.","exEn":"For example, he’d like me to be there at dawn and spend the whole day with him.","src":8},{"fr":"le matériel","en":"equipment, materials","zh":"设备，材料","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ material","ff":"le matériel = equipment, tools (not just ‘material’)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il faut que j’apporte le matériel – pinceaux, rouleaux, couteaux et tout et tout –","exEn":"I have to bring the equipment – brushes, rollers, scrapers and all that –","src":9},{"fr":"il faudra sans doute que j’emporte les déchets","en":"I’ll probably have to take away the rubbish","zh":"我大概得把垃圾带走","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"sans doute = probably (not ‘without doubt’)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et, quand on aura terminé, il faudra sans doute que j’emporte les déchets à la déchetterie !","exEn":"And, when we’ve finished, I’ll probably have to take the rubbish to the waste collection centre!","src":11},{"fr":"en résumé","en":"in short, to sum up","zh":"总之，概括地说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ in summary","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En résumé, je ne sais pas quoi lui dire. Je ne veux pas qu’il soit fâché contre moi.","exEn":"In short, I don’t know what to say to him. I don’t want him to be angry with me.","src":12},{"fr":"je ne veux pas qu’il soit fâché contre moi","en":"I don’t want him to be angry with me","zh":"我不想让他生我的气","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En résumé, je ne sais pas quoi lui dire. Je ne veux pas qu’il soit fâché contre moi.","exEn":"In short, I don’t know what to say to him. I don’t want him to be angry with me.","src":12},{"fr":"il suffit de le regarder droit dans les yeux","en":"all you have to do is look him straight in the eye","zh":"你只需直视他的眼睛","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"body","kw":"","ex":"Il suffit de le regarder droit dans les yeux et lui dire, gentiment mais fermement :","exEn":"All you have to do is look him straight in the eye and say to him, gently but firmly:","src":14},{"fr":"il vaudrait mieux que tu ne sois pas disponible","en":"it would be better if you weren’t available","zh":"你最好不要有空","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À défaut, il vaudrait mieux que tu ne sois pas disponible ce jour-là,","exEn":"Failing that, it would be better if you weren’t available that day,","src":16},{"fr":"N’empêche","en":"even so, all the same","zh":"尽管如此，还是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’empêche, j’ai peur qu’il soit vexé quand même.","exEn":"Even so, I’m afraid he’ll be offended anyway.","src":20},{"fr":"Il ne t’en voudra pas.","en":"He won’t hold it against you.","zh":"他不会怪你的。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ne t’en voudra pas. Il sera triste que tu ne puisses pas l’aider, c’est tout.","exEn":"He won’t hold it against you. He’ll be sad that you can’t help him, that’s all.","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 7: lesson cross-references (26, 68, 78) as read from OCR"],"pdfPage":479,"gaps":[[1.92,3.7],[5.82,8.54],[65.8,66.88],[72.04,73.1],[109.38,112.84],[115,117.06],[120.08,123.1],[127.1,130.24],[135.96,138.86],[144.44,147.48],[152.88,156.9]],"dur":156.9},{"n":80,"kind":"lesson","title":"“Notre action ne dépend que de vous”","titleEn":"“Our action depends only on you”","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Les syndicalistes du SSP, le Syndicat des services publics, ont déposé un préavis de grève pour la fin janvier.","en":"The members of the SSP, the Public Services Union, have given advance notice of a strike for the end of January.","lit":"dropped off a notice"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils réclament des augmentations de salaire, mais leurs exigences vont au-delà de la seule question salariale","en":"They are demanding wage increases, but their demands go beyond the wage issue alone","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"et des négociations sur les conditions d’accès aux congés maladie.","en":"and negotiations on the conditions of access to sick leave.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Le SSP exige que ces réclamations soient entendues par le gouvernement et le patronat","en":"The SSP demands that these claims should be heard by the government and employers","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"dans le cadre de la réduction du temps de travail.","en":"as part of the reduction in working time.","lit":"in the frame of"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour l’instant, la grogne est limitée à une trentaine de grandes entreprises","en":"For the time being, the discontent is limited to some thirty large companies","lit":"the grumbling"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"mais elle pourrait devenir un mouvement massif touchant plusieurs secteurs,","en":"but it could become a massive movement affecting several sectors,","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"y compris la sidérurgie et l’agroalimentaire.","en":"including the steel industry and food processing.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Ce matin, un des leaders syndicaux a prononcé un discours devant ses militants.","en":"This morning, one of the union leaders gave a speech to his activists.","lit":"pronounced a speech"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Camarades, notre action ne dépend que de vous !","en":"Comrades, our action depends only on you!","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut que nous élargissions et durcissions le mouvement pour qu’il ne faiblisse pas dans les semaines à venir.","en":"We must broaden and toughen the movement so that it does not weaken in the weeks to come.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"En matière de congés maladie, il est flagrant que les employeurs nous imposent deux poids, deux mesures.","en":"As regards sick leave, it is flagrant that employers are imposing double standards on us.","lit":"in matter of; two weights, two measures"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Si un ouvrier oublie de faire quelque chose, il est paresseux ;","en":"If a worker forgets to do something, he’s lazy;","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"mais si son patron l’oublie, c’est simplement parce qu’il est trop occupé.","en":"but if his boss forgets, it’s simply because he’s too busy.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est dommage que vous n’ayez pas la reconnaissance que vous méritez.","en":"It’s a pity that you don’t get the recognition you deserve.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Supposez que vous ayez la grippe et que vous choisissiez de ne pas aller au boulot","en":"Suppose you have the flu and you choose not to go to work","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"pour ne pas incommoder vos camarades. Qu’est-ce qui va se passer ?","en":"so as not to inconvenience your comrades. What’s going to happen?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Le patron ne va pas attendre que vous finissiez votre traitement et que vous soyez guéri ;","en":"The boss isn’t going to wait for you to finish your treatment and get better;","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"il va exiger que vous reveniez travailler dans les plus brefs délais ! Quelle honte !","en":"he will demand that you come back to work as soon as possible! It’s disgraceful!","lit":"in the shortest delays; what a shame"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Car si c’est lui qui est malade, ses collègues vont insister pour qu’il obéisse au médecin du travail,","en":"Because if it’s he who is sick, his colleagues will insist that he obey the occupational physician,","lit":"doctor of work"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"et qu’il y réfléchisse à deux fois avant de revenir au bureau.","en":"and think twice before coming back to the office.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Cela démontre bien que tous les hommes sont égaux,","en":"This goes to show that all men are equal,","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"mais que certains sont plus égaux que d’autres !","en":"but that some are more equal than others!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The regular verb réclamer is a faux ami, meaning to demand or call for: Les syndicats réclament des résultats, The unions are demanding results. In a legal context, it can mean to lodge a complaint, hence the noun une réclamation. If a consumer wishes to complain about a company’s products or services, they can contact le service / le bureau des réclamations."},{"ref":"2","text":"un congé, often translated simply as a holiday (lesson 45 line 12), also covers the notion of leave, i.e. absence from work, and is used in compound nouns such as les congés payés, paid leave, un congé de maladie, sick leave, or un congé de maternité, maternity leave. (In common usage, de is often omitted: un congé maladie, etc.)"},{"ref":"3","text":"This lesson builds on the constructions explained in lesson 79 note 1. Verbs such as exiger que, to demand that, être dommage que, to be a shame that and attendre que, to expect that all imply an element of uncertainty or contingency about the action described in the second, subordinate-clause verb, which is therefore in the subjunctive. (Note that insister requires pour before que: J’insiste pour que tu choisisses entre nous deux, I insist that you choose between the two of us.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"The masculine noun l’acier means steel: Cette porte est en acier, This door is made of steel. The noun la sidérurgie covers both steel and iron (le fer, see lesson 71) and thus refers to the metalworking or the iron and steel industry."},{"ref":"5","text":"Although French has perfectly good native nouns like un dirigeant or un responsable, meaning a leader or an official, it often uses un leader in a political or managerial context (along with the horrible le leadership)."},{"ref":"6","text":"The negative form ne… que means only: Elle ne m’appelle qu’une fois par semaine, She calls me only once a week. The construction can be used instead of seulement (Elle m’appelle seulement une fois par semaine). Que can also be used with another two-part negative construction, such as ne… plus, with the same meaning: Il ne reste plus que trois candidats, There are only three candidates left."},{"ref":"7","text":"The present subjunctive endings of regular -ir verbs are slightly more complex than for -er verbs. Thus élargir, to broaden (from large, broad, wide): (que) j’élargisse, tu élargisses, il/elle élargisse, nous élargissions, vous élargissiez, ils/elles élargissent. Remember that the subjunctive form is usually preceded by que."},{"ref":"8","text":"le poids, the weight, takes an -s in the singular as well as the plural: Quel poids faites-vous ? How much do you weigh? Une mesure means a measurement as well as a measure (lesson 66 line 5). The expression deux poids, deux mesures means a double standard: Le gouvernement applique deux poids, deux mesures sur cette question, The government is applying double standards to this issue."},{"ref":"9","text":"The present subjunctive of avoir is (que) j’aie, tu aies, il/elle ait, nous ayons, vous ayez, ils/elles aient."},{"ref":"10","text":"la honte means shame, disgrace. Leurs actions sont une source de honte pour nous tous, Their actions are a source of shame for all of us. However, it does not translate the notion of shame or pity contained in English expressions like What a pity, which is Quel dommage (lesson 48 line 19). C’est une honte ! It’s a disgrace!"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Où est Martine ? Il est dommage qu’elle ne soit pas avec nous.","a":"Where’s Martine? It’s a shame that she’s not with us."},{"q":"J’insiste pour que vous y réfléchissiez à deux fois avant de poursuivre le projet.","a":"I insist that you think twice before going ahead with the project."},{"q":"Je pense qu’on devrait attendre que tu finisses la fac avant de prendre une décision sur ton avenir.","a":"I think we should wait until you finish uni before we make a decision about your future."},{"q":"Nous réclamons des résultats et nous n’allons pas attendre que vous ayez fini vos petites discussions.","a":"We’re calling for results, and we won’t wait until you’ve finished your little discussions."},{"q":"Les dirigeants n’entendent pas la grogne des syndicalistes, qui veulent élargir et durcir leur grève.","a":"The leaders aren’t hearing the discontent of the trade unionists, who want to widen their strike and make it tougher."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"They insist that we choose the most expensive equipment, but that doesn’t depend only on me.","t":"Ils {{insistent pour que nous choisissions}} le matériel {{le plus cher}}, mais ça {{ne dépend pas que de moi}}."},{"cue":"We demand that the employees have pay rises and that their demand be heard.","t":"Nous {{exigeons que}} les employés {{aient des augmentations de salaire}} et {{que}} leur {{réclamation soit entendue}}."},{"cue":"It’s a shame that he didn’t take part in the strike! – No, it’s a disgrace.","t":"C’est {{dommage qu’il n’ait pas}} participé à la grève. – Non, {{c’est une honte}} !"},{"cue":"Let’s suppose that we need a doctor and that he’s on holiday. What’s going to happen?","t":"{{Supposons que}} nous {{ayons besoin}} du médecin et qu’{{il soit en congé}}. Qu’est-ce {{qu’il va se passer}} ?"},{"cue":"We mustn’t have a double standard; not all cases are equal.","t":"Il ne faut pas {{que nous ayons deux poids, deux mesures}} ; tous les cas {{ne sont pas égaux}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un préavis de grève","en":"advance notice of a strike","zh":"罢工预告通知","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les syndicalistes du SSP, le Syndicat des services publics, ont déposé un préavis de grève pour la fin janvier.","exEn":"The members of the SSP, the Public Services Union, have given advance notice of a strike for the end of January.","src":1},{"fr":"ils réclament des augmentations de salaire","en":"they are demanding wage increases","zh":"他们要求加薪","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"réclamer = to demand (not ‘reclaim’)","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Ils réclament des augmentations de salaire, mais leurs exigences vont au-delà de la seule question salariale","exEn":"They are demanding wage increases, but their demands go beyond the wage issue alone","src":2},{"fr":"un congé maladie","en":"sick leave","zh":"病假","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et des négociations sur les conditions d’accès aux congés maladie.","exEn":"and negotiations on the conditions of access to sick leave.","src":3},{"fr":"le SSP exige que ces réclamations soient entendues","en":"the SSP demands that these claims be heard","zh":"工会要求这些诉求被听取","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le SSP exige que ces réclamations soient entendues par le gouvernement et le patronat","exEn":"The SSP demands that these claims should be heard by the government and employers","src":4},{"fr":"la grogne","en":"discontent, grumbling","zh":"不满情绪","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “grown”: a grown man growling about his pay","ex":"Pour l’instant, la grogne est limitée à une trentaine de grandes entreprises","exEn":"For the time being, the discontent is limited to some thirty large companies","src":6},{"fr":"notre action ne dépend que de vous","en":"our action depends only on you","zh":"我们的行动只取决于你们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ depends","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Camarades, notre action ne dépend que de vous !","exEn":"Comrades, our action depends only on you!","src":10},{"fr":"deux poids, deux mesures","en":"double standards","zh":"双重标准","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En matière de congés maladie, il est flagrant que les employeurs nous imposent deux poids, deux mesures.","exEn":"As regards sick leave, it is flagrant that employers are imposing double standards on us.","src":12},{"fr":"un ouvrier","en":"a (manual) worker","zh":"工人","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Si un ouvrier oublie de faire quelque chose, il est paresseux ;","exEn":"If a worker forgets to do something, he’s lazy;","src":13},{"fr":"Il est dommage que vous n’ayez pas la reconnaissance que vous méritez.","en":"It’s a pity you don’t get the recognition you deserve.","zh":"很遗憾你们没有得到应得的认可。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est dommage que vous n’ayez pas la reconnaissance que vous méritez.","exEn":"It’s a pity that you don’t get the recognition you deserve.","src":15},{"fr":"la grippe","en":"the flu","zh":"流感","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Supposez que vous ayez la grippe et que vous choisissiez de ne pas aller au boulot","exEn":"Suppose you have the flu and you choose not to go to work","src":16},{"fr":"dans les plus brefs délais","en":"as soon as possible","zh":"尽快","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il va exiger que vous reveniez travailler dans les plus brefs délais ! Quelle honte !","exEn":"he will demand that you come back to work as soon as possible! It’s disgraceful!","src":19},{"fr":"Quelle honte !","en":"How disgraceful! What a shame!","zh":"真丢脸！真是可耻！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"la honte = disgrace; ‘what a pity’ = quel dommage","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il va exiger que vous reveniez travailler dans les plus brefs délais ! Quelle honte !","exEn":"he will demand that you come back to work as soon as possible! It’s disgraceful!","src":19},{"fr":"ses collègues vont insister pour qu’il obéisse","en":"his colleagues will insist that he obey","zh":"他的同事会坚持要求他听从","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ insist, obey","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Car si c’est lui qui est malade, ses collègues vont insister pour qu’il obéisse au médecin du travail,","exEn":"Because if it’s he who is sick, his colleagues will insist that he obey the occupational physician,","src":20},{"fr":"il y réfléchisse à deux fois","en":"he thinks twice about it","zh":"他要三思","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et qu’il y réfléchisse à deux fois avant de revenir au bureau.","exEn":"and think twice before coming back to the office.","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 6: OCR example ‘ne … pas’ garbled; I used ‘ne… plus’ following the example ‘Il ne reste plus que trois candidats’","note 4: ‘lesson 71’ cross-reference for le fer as read from OCR","intro: dialogue is narration plus a speech, dash only on lines 1 and 10 as in the book"],"pdfPage":485,"gaps":[[1.5,3.48],[5.16,8.1],[53.44,54.56],[64.56,65.66],[79.52,80.66],[102.16,103.18],[118.76,122.54],[124.36,126.46],[129.48,132.48],[137.42,140.48],[145.48,148.52],[152.84,156.26],[161.16,165.12]],"dur":165.1},{"n":81,"kind":"lesson","title":"La réaction des patrons","titleEn":"The bosses’ reaction","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Le Groupement des Employeurs et de l’Emploi s’est réuni hier à Lille pour discuter des demandes des grévistes.","en":"The Groupement des Employeurs et de l’Emploi (the employers’ and employment association) met yesterday in Lille to discuss the demands of the strikers.","lit":"grouping of employers and employment"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Voici un extrait du compte-rendu de la réunion qui s’est déroulée pendant plus de quatre heures :","en":"Here is an extract from the minutes of the meeting, which went on for more than four hours:","lit":"account rendered"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien que nous fassions tous les efforts nécessaires, mes amis, nous sommes dans un dialogue de sourds.","en":"Although we are making every effort, my friends, we are in a dialogue of the deaf.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous nous sommes fait attaquer par les médias qui nous désignent comme les méchants.","en":"We have been attacked by the media, who are pointing at us as the bad guys.","lit":"designating"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est ridicule, bien entendu. Il n’empêche que nous devons être très attentifs.","en":"That’s ridiculous, of course. Nevertheless, we must be very careful.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Leur déléguée du personnel, Inès Laurent, est très adroite et, de plus, c’est une négociatrice expérimentée.","en":"Their employee representative, Inès Laurent, is very skilful and, what’s more, she is an experienced negotiator.","lit":"staff delegate"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Il n’est pas certain qu’elle attende la fin des pourparlers avant de déclencher un grand mouvement social.","en":"It’s not certain that she will wait until the end of the talks before triggering major industrial action.","lit":"a social movement"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Quoi qu’il en soit, ses idées gagnent du terrain et on doit être sur le qui-vive.","en":"In any case, her ideas are gaining ground and we need to be on the alert.","lit":"who-lives"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis d’accord. Il faut qu’on fasse très attention car tout peut arriver.","en":"I agree. We have to be very careful because anything can happen.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Il est peu probable que Madame Laurent comprenne notre stratégie ou qu’elle connaisse notre vraie position.","en":"It is unlikely that Mrs Laurent understands our strategy or that she knows our true position.","lit":"little likely"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"En revanche, il est tout à fait probable qu’elle suspendra les négociations si elle n’obtient pas ce qu’elle veut.","en":"On the other hand, it is quite likely that she will suspend the negotiations if she does not get what she wants.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"De toutes les façons, les délégués du personnel en général nous en veulent à tous.","en":"In any case, the staff representatives in general are angry with all of us.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce qui est sûr c’est que nous ne pouvons pas nous tourner les pouces en attendant que le problème se règle tout seul.","en":"What’s certain is that we can’t twiddle our thumbs and wait for the problem to resolve itself.","lit":"turn the thumbs ... regulate itself"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Au contraire, il faut patienter jusqu’à ce que Laurent soumette un document avec ses propositions.","en":"On the contrary, we have to wait until Laurent submits a document with her proposals.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Si elle ne le fait pas dans un délai de quarante-huit heures, nous aurons le droit de rompre les négociations sans préavis.","en":"If she doesn’t do so within 48 hours, we’ll have the right to break off the talks without notice.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Disons d’ici à vendredi ? Pourvu que nous soyons tous du même avis,","en":"Let’s say by Friday? Provided that we are all of the same opinion,","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"je préparerai un communiqué de presse, comme prévu et on le publiera plus tard.","en":"I’ll prepare a press release, as planned, and we will publish it later.","lit":"foreseen"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Avant que tu finisses, Yann, j’ai une anecdote qui illustre le genre d’individus à qui nous avons affaire.","en":"Before you finish, Yann, I have an anecdote that illustrates the kind of individual we are dealing with.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Un jeune homme, fraîchement sorti d’une école de commerce, me sollicite pour un poste de commercial et me demande le salaire.","en":"A young man, fresh out of business school, applies to me for a sales position and asks me about the salary.","lit":"freshly ... trade"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Je lui propose le salaire minimum pour commencer,","en":"I offered him the minimum wage to begin with,","lit":"offer"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"en lui disant qu’il serait augmenté de manière conséquente au bout de sa période d’essai de six mois.","en":"telling him that it would be increased significantly at the end of his six-month trial period.","lit":"of significant manner"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous savez ce qu’il m’a répondu ?","en":"You know what he answered me?","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"« D’accord, dans ce cas je reviendrai dans vingt-quatre semaines. »","en":"“All right, then I’ll come back in twenty-four weeks.”","lit":""},{"i":24,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est inadmissible, sans doute, mais c’est logique.","en":"It is inadmissible, perhaps, but it is logical.","lit":"(it is)"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Some conjunctions introducing a condition or conjecture (although, etc.) are followed by a verb in the subjunctive. For example, bien que, usually placed at the beginning of a sentence or clause, means although, etc. Bien que nous soyons souvent absents, nous pensons à toi, Even though we are often away, we think of you. Note once again how the subjunctive is introduced by que. Be careful, though, to distinguish between the conjunction bien que and the adverb bien introducing a dependent clause starting with que (lesson 80 line 22)."},{"ref":"2","text":"The present subjunctive of the irregular verb faire is (que) je fasse, tu fasses, il/elle fasse, nous fassions, vous fassiez, ils/elles fassent."},{"ref":"3","text":"The adjective méchant literally means nasty, unkind, etc. Il faut que tu apprennes à ne pas être méchant avec les autres, You have to learn not to be nasty to other people. We will see other uses later on. The personal noun un(e) méchant(e) means a nasty or unpleasant person and, by extension, “a bad guy” in a novel or movie. Dans les tout premiers films, le méchant portait toujours un chapeau noir, In the very early movies, the baddy always used to wear a black hat."},{"ref":"4","text":"Il n’empêche que has the same meaning as n’empêche, seen in lesson 79 note 5, but is a little more formal (be that as it may). Any verb following the expression is in the indicative, not the subjunctive: Je sais que vous ne les aimez pas. Il n’empêche qu’ils ont raison, I know that you do not like them. Be that as it may, they are right."},{"ref":"5","text":"When used in the negative, impersonal verbs such as être certain / être sûr indicate doubt and therefore require the subjunctive: Il n’est pas sûr qu’elle soit d’accord avec nous, It’s not sure that she’ll agree with us. In the indicative, however, the doubt disappears, as does the subjunctive. In such cases, the verb is often in the future tense: Il est certain qu’elle sera d’accord avec nous, She’s bound to agree with us. The same applies, of course, to probability: see lines 10 (subjunctive) and 11 (indicative)."},{"ref":"6","text":"This is the subjunctive of Group 3 verbs (in -re): (que) j’attende, tu attendes, il/elle attende, nous attendions, vous attendiez, ils/elles attendent. Be careful not to confuse the subjunctive with the imperfect in the first and second persons plural, which are identical."},{"ref":"7","text":"See lesson 68 and lesson 9. This expression, formed with the relative pronoun and the subjunctive, means whatever, no matter what. Quoi que nous fassions, nous aurons toujours tort, Whatever we do we will always be in the wrong."},{"ref":"8","text":"A few time conjunctions also take the subjunctive. The most common are jusqu’à ce que, until, and avant que (note the tell-tale que in both cases): Avant que tu partes, j’ai quelque chose à te dire, Before you go, I’ve got something to say to you. Tu peux le garder jusqu’à ce que je revienne ? Can you keep it until I come back?"},{"ref":"9","text":"un délai is a faux-ami (a delay, un retard). It basically means a time period or limit: Il faut le faire dans un délai de dix jours, It has to be done within a ten-day period. The actual translation depends on the context - for example Quel est le délai pour ce travail ? What’s the deadline for this job? - but the underlying notion is the passage of time. (In some expressions, délai can be plural, see lesson 80 line 19.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"pourvu que, provided that, as long as, is an invariable conjunction that automatically introduces a condition and therefore requires the subjunctive: Ma fille peut faire ce qu’elle veut de sa vie, pourvu qu’elle soit heureuse, My daughter can do what she wants with her life, as long as she is happy. (Remember that to provide is fournir, see lesson 37 line 5.)"},{"ref":"","text":"Even a casual reading of French news media will reveal an entire vocabulary of words and terms relating to employment (l’emploi), joblessness (le chômage) and labour relations (les relations de travail, literally “work relations”). The adjective social refers to interactions between employers and employees, referred to collectively as les partenaires sociaux (“social partners”). Key players include management (la direction) and staff representatives (le/la délégué(e) du personnel), who seek to maintain labour relations (le dialogue social) and avert any industrial action (un mouvement social)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Pourvu que vous ayez tous les documents nécessaires, tout ira bien.","a":"As long as you have all the necessary paperwork, everything will be fine."},{"q":"Je comprends votre décision de partir mais je vous en veux à tous les deux.","a":"I understand your decision to leave but I hold it against both of you."},{"q":"Il faut lui répondre dans un délai de deux semaines.","a":"You have to answer him within two weeks."},{"q":"Ce n’est pas sûr qu’il attende le mois prochain.","a":"It’s not sure that he’ll wait until next month."},{"q":"Quoi que tu fasses, ton patron ne sera jamais d’accord avec toi : tu auras tort !","a":"Whatever you do, your boss will never agree with you: you’ll be wrong!"},{"q":"Il est peu probable qu’elle connaisse tous les détails des négociations.","a":"She’s unlikely to know all the details of the negotiations."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Even though you’re right, it’s not certain that the employee representative [masculine] is on your side.","t":"{{Bien que vous ayez}} raison, il n’est pas certain que {{le délégué du personnel soit}} de votre côté."},{"cue":"Roger left without his hat and his gloves when he left. Can you keep them until he gets back?","t":"Roger {{est parti}} sans {{son chapeau}} et {{ses gants}}. Pouvez-vous les garder {{jusqu’à ce qu’il revienne}} ?"},{"cue":"It’s not possible to break off the dialogue before we complete the wage negotiations.","t":"Il n’est pas possible {{de rompre le dialogue avant qu’on finisse}} les négociations {{salariales}}."},{"cue":"You’re very skilful, Michel. All the same, you don’t have the experience to submit this project.","t":"Vous êtes très {{adroit}}, Michel. {{Il n’empêche que vous n’avez pas}} l’expérience pour {{soumettre}} ce projet."},{"cue":"If we have to wait some more, we will wait. – What precisely were you expecting?","t":"S’il {{faut que nous attendions}} encore, nous {{attendrons}}. – Qu’{{attendiez}}-vous précisément ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"bien que nous fassions tous les efforts nécessaires","en":"although we are making every effort","zh":"尽管我们尽了一切必要的努力","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien que nous fassions tous les efforts nécessaires, mes amis, nous sommes dans un dialogue de sourds.","exEn":"Although we are making every effort, my friends, we are in a dialogue of the deaf.","src":3},{"fr":"un dialogue de sourds","en":"a dialogue of the deaf (neither side listens)","zh":"聋子对话（各说各的）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien que nous fassions tous les efforts nécessaires, mes amis, nous sommes dans un dialogue de sourds.","exEn":"Although we are making every effort, my friends, we are in a dialogue of the deaf.","src":3},{"fr":"Il n’empêche que nous devons être très attentifs.","en":"Be that as it may, we must be very careful.","zh":"尽管如此，我们还是必须非常小心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est ridicule, bien entendu. Il n’empêche que nous devons être très attentifs.","exEn":"That’s ridiculous, of course. Nevertheless, we must be very careful.","src":5},{"fr":"de plus, c’est une négociatrice expérimentée","en":"what’s more, she is an experienced negotiator","zh":"而且，她是一位经验丰富的谈判者","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ experienced (expérimenté)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Leur déléguée du personnel, Inès Laurent, est très adroite et, de plus, c’est une négociatrice expérimentée.","exEn":"Their employee representative, Inès Laurent, is very skilful and, what’s more, she is an experienced negotiator.","src":6},{"fr":"Quoi qu’il en soit, ses idées gagnent du terrain.","en":"In any case, her ideas are gaining ground.","zh":"无论如何，她的想法正在赢得支持","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quoi qu’il en soit, ses idées gagnent du terrain et on doit être sur le qui-vive.","exEn":"In any case, her ideas are gaining ground and we need to be on the alert.","src":8},{"fr":"on doit être sur le qui-vive","en":"we must be on the alert","zh":"我们必须保持警惕","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “key-veev”: a guard shouting “Who lives?” (qui vive) at night, so you stay on the alert","ex":"Quoi qu’il en soit, ses idées gagnent du terrain et on doit être sur le qui-vive.","exEn":"In any case, her ideas are gaining ground and we need to be on the alert.","src":8},{"fr":"Il est peu probable que Madame Laurent comprenne","en":"It is unlikely that Mrs Laurent understands","zh":"劳朗夫人不太可能理解","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ probable","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est peu probable que Madame Laurent comprenne notre stratégie ou qu’elle connaisse notre vraie position.","exEn":"It is unlikely that Mrs Laurent understands our strategy or that she knows our true position.","src":10},{"fr":"nous en veulent à tous","en":"they are angry with all of us","zh":"他们对我们所有人都怀恨在心","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"De toutes les façons, les délégués du personnel en général nous en veulent à tous.","exEn":"In any case, the staff representatives in general are angry with all of us.","src":12},{"fr":"nous tourner les pouces","en":"to twiddle our thumbs","zh":"无所事事，干等着","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ce qui est sûr c’est que nous ne pouvons pas nous tourner les pouces en attendant que le problème se règle tout seul.","exEn":"What’s certain is that we can’t twiddle our thumbs and wait for the problem to resolve itself.","src":13},{"fr":"dans un délai de quarante-huit heures","en":"within 48 hours","zh":"在四十八小时之内","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un délai = a time limit / deadline, NOT only “a delay” (= un retard)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si elle ne le fait pas dans un délai de quarante-huit heures, nous aurons le droit de rompre les négociations sans préavis.","exEn":"If she doesn’t do so within 48 hours, we’ll have the right to break off the talks without notice.","src":15},{"fr":"sans préavis","en":"without notice","zh":"不事先通知","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “pray-a-vee”: you pray, ‘a V!’ — and you leave with no warning","ex":"Si elle ne le fait pas dans un délai de quarante-huit heures, nous aurons le droit de rompre les négociations sans préavis.","exEn":"If she doesn’t do so within 48 hours, we’ll have the right to break off the talks without notice.","src":15},{"fr":"Pourvu que nous soyons tous du même avis","en":"Provided that we are all of the same opinion","zh":"只要我们大家意见一致","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Disons d’ici à vendredi ? Pourvu que nous soyons tous du même avis,","exEn":"Let’s say by Friday? Provided that we are all of the same opinion,","src":16},{"fr":"un communiqué de presse","en":"a press release","zh":"新闻稿","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ communiqué","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"je préparerai un communiqué de presse, comme prévu et on le publiera plus tard.","exEn":"I’ll prepare a press release, as planned, and we will publish it later.","src":17},{"fr":"le genre d’individus à qui nous avons affaire","en":"the kind of people we are dealing with","zh":"我们要对付的这类人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Avant que tu finisses, Yann, j’ai une anecdote qui illustre le genre d’individus à qui nous avons affaire.","exEn":"Before you finish, Yann, I have an anecdote that illustrates the kind of individual we are dealing with.","src":18},{"fr":"une période d’essai de six mois","en":"a six-month trial period","zh":"六个月的试用期","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"en lui disant qu’il serait augmenté de manière conséquente au bout de sa période d’essai de six mois.","exEn":"telling him that it would be increased significantly at the end of his six-month trial period.","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 7: cross-reference printed garbled in the scan (‘See lesson 68 … lesson 9’)","note 4 tout premiers: scan read ‘tous premiers’, corrected to ‘tout premiers’"],"pdfPage":493,"gaps":[[1.94,3.96],[5.74,8.74],[40.9,41.92],[46.24,47.26],[82.94,84.08],[99.34,100.36],[129.9,133.52],[135.84,137.54],[141.28,144.3],[148.34,151.2],[155.8,158.82],[163.22,166.4],[170.08,173.62]],"dur":173.6},{"n":82,"kind":"lesson","title":"Qu’ils sont idiots !","titleEn":"How idiotic they are!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"Qu’on le veuille ou non, les sondages font partie intégrante de la période pré-électorale.","en":"Whether we like it or not, opinion polls are an integral part of the pre-election period.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour certains, s’entretenir dans la rue avec un inconnu ne pose aucun problème ;","en":"For some people, talking on the street with a stranger is not a problem;","lit":"unknown ... poses no"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"alors que pour d’autres, ça n’a aucun intérêt – et ils le font savoir.","en":"while for others, it is of no interest – and they let it be known.","lit":"make"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonjour. Accepteriez-vous de répondre à un court questionnaire sur nos institutions politiques ?","en":"Good morning. Would you agree to answer a short questionnaire about our political institutions?","lit":"accept"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’ai ni le temps ni l’envie de discuter avec quelqu’un que je ne connais pas sur un trottoir encombré.","en":"I have neither the time nor the inclination to chat with someone I don’t know on a crowded pavement.","lit":"envy"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Il ne s’agit pas de parler de tout et de rien mais simplement de répondre à quelques petites questions.","en":"It’s not a question of talking about any old thing but simply of answering a few short questions.","lit":"everything and nothing"},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Puisque vous insistez. Mais je n’ai que quelques instants à vous accorder, alors faites vite.","en":"Since you insist. But I only have a few minutes to give you, so hurry up.","lit":"moments ... do quickly"},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne dépasserai même pas cinq minutes, je vous le jure. Commençons avec le premier ministre :","en":"I won’t even take more than five minutes, I swear. Let’s start with the prime minister:","lit":"exceed"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"ferait-il un meilleur ou un moins bon président que le locataire actuel de l’Élysée ?","en":"Would he make a better or worse president than the current one?","lit":"tenant of the Élysée Palace"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"A-t-il plus ou moins de qualités ? Qu’en pensez-vous ?","en":"Does he have more or fewer qualities? What do you think?","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’ai pas d’opinion tranchée là-dessus. En vérité, je n’ai aucune opinion, tout court.","en":"I don’t have a definite opinion on this. In truth, I have no opinion at all.","lit":"sliced ... all short"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Le mandat présidentiel, dure-t-il trop longtemps, est-il trop court, ou n’y a-t-il rien à changer ?","en":"Is the presidential term too long, is it too short, or is there nothing to be changed?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça m’est indifférent. Ça durera le temps que ça durera, ni plus, ni moins.","en":"I don’t care. It will last as long as it lasts, no more, no less.","lit":"this to-me is indifferent"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est noté. Venons-en maintenant aux questions relatives à la majorité et l’opposition, si vous permettez.","en":"Noted. Let us now get to the questions relating to the majority and the opposition, if I may.","lit":"(it’s) noted ... come"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Si vous y tenez.","en":"If you insist.","lit":"hold it"},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Le nouveau président de l’Assemblée, est-il efficace en ce qui concerne la direction des débats ?","en":"Is the new President of the Assembly effective in managing debates?","lit":"in what concerns the"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Arrivera-t-il à imposer son autorité et à se faire respecter par les députés ?","en":"Will he manage to impose his authority and earn the respect of the deputies?","lit":"arrive at"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Connaissez-vous les députés, au moins ?","en":"Do you at least know the deputies?","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Je n’en connais aucun. Ils sont sans aucun intérêt. Personne ne me passionne.","en":"I don’t know any of them. They’re of no interest. I’m not keen on any of them.","lit":"no one impassions me"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Le sondeur s’en va, découragé. Mais, en partant, il entend la voix de l’homme qui marmonne :","en":"The pollster goes away discouraged. But as he’s leaving, he hears the voice of the man mumbling:","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’ils sont idiots, ces sondeurs ! Ils ne posent aucune question intéressante.","en":"These pollsters are so idiotic! They don’t ask any interesting questions.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(1) The vowel middle sound in veuille / veuillez is pronounced the same as the ligature in œil (see lesson 32). (19) In conversational French, the ne is usually elided (see the note in lesson 73). Thus you’ll probably hear some people say j’en connais aucun."},{"ref":"1","text":"que can be used at the beginning of an adverbial clause to introduce an alternative, like whether … or. In this case, the following verb is in the subjunctive: Que vous l’aimiez ou non, il tient à vous, Whether you like him or not, he’s fond of you. The negative non can be replaced by pas (Que tu l’aimes ou pas …) with no change in meaning."},{"ref":"2","text":"The subjunctive of the irregular verb vouloir is (que) je veuille, tu veuilles, il/elle veuille, nous voulions, vous vouliez, ils/elles veuillent. As with Group 3 verbs, be careful not to confuse the subjunctive with the imperfect in the first and second persons plural (lesson 81 note 6)."},{"ref":"3","text":"The plural indefinite pronoun certains can be used for a group of people (lesson 61 line 18), like some or some people. Certains sont d’accord, d’autres pas. Some (people) agree, others don’t."},{"ref":"4","text":"The impersonal verb s’agir de, usually found in the third person singular il s’agit de, can be hard to pin down. The nearest equivalent is: it has to do with or it’s a question of. Je dois vous parler : il s’agit de votre salaire, I have to talk to you. It has to do with your salary. We’ll return to this later on."},{"ref":"5","text":"Remember that the negative form ne … que means only: Le gouvernement n’a que deux choix : accepter ou démissionner, The government has only two choices: accept or resign. In this context, ne … que can be replaced by seulement, with no change in meaning: Le gouvernement a seulement deux choix. We’ll see other uses of ne … que shortly."},{"ref":"6","text":"The questions asked by the pollster are formal, which is why the type 3 interrogative is used. To avoid the juxtaposition of two initial vowels (a il), the letter t is placed between hyphens: a-t-il ? This rule is not confined to avoir or to the present tense. See line 12, dure-t-il, and line 17, arrivera-t-il."},{"ref":"7","text":"Là-dessus, on there, can be used literally to describe a physical position or idiomatically to replace sur cela/ça: Tu peux compter sur ça ➔ Tu peux compter là-dessus, You can count on that. By extension, it can also mean thereupon, with that: Charles a marmonné quelque chose et, là-dessus, il est parti en courant, Charles muttered something and, with that, he ran away."},{"ref":"8","text":"The adverbial phrase tout court (“all short”) is useful for indicating brevity or sufficiency: Ne m’appelez pas Madame la Présidente mais Madame, tout court, Don’t call me Madam President, just Madam. The translation changes depending on the context: L’opposition veut le pouvoir, tout court, The opposition wants power, pure and simple. In short, keep the translation short!"},{"ref":"9","text":"The meaning of tenir, to hold, shifts slightly when followed by à: to value, to be attached to or fond of. Ils tiennent à leur indépendance, They value their independence. The direct object can be replaced by y: C’est mon mari qui m’a donné cette écharpe et j’y tiens, It’s my husband who gave me this scarf and I’m very fond of it. The expression si tu y tiens / si vous y tenez means If you insist (i.e. “if you are attached to” or “value that thing”), which can also be expressed by si tu insistes / si vous insistez."},{"ref":"10","text":"Just as qu’est-ce que is dropped in a type-3 interrogative, so it can be omitted from an exclamation: Qu’est-ce qu’il est pénible ! (lesson 67 note 3) ➔ Qu’il est pénible ! If a subject noun is added, it comes after the exclamation: Qu’ils sont sympathiques, ces gens ! How nice those people are!"},{"ref":"","text":"French-speakers often refer to a place or an institution by using a word closely related to it (the technical term for this is metonymy). Le Palais de l’Élysée (the Élysée Palace), usually shortened to l’Élysée, is the official residence of the French president, located at the bottom of les Champs-Élysées (Culture Note, lesson 15). Thus l’Élysée is a metonymy for the presidency, while le locataire de l’Élysée (literally “the renter” or tenant) is the president himself or (one day) herself."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Que vous le vouliez ou non, vous n’aurez pas d’autre choix que d’accepter leur proposition.","a":"Whether you like it or not, you’ll have no choice but to accept their proposal."},{"q":"Tu veux regarder « Certains l’aiment chaud » avec moi ce soir ?","a":"Do you want to watch “Some Like It Hot” with me this evening?"},{"q":"Il a joué plusieurs chansons, toutes très belles, mais je n’en connaissais aucune.","a":"He played several songs, all very beautiful, but I didn’t know any of them."},{"q":"Marine n’arrivera pas à imposer son autorité, il n’y a pas de doute là-dessus.","a":"Marine won’t manage to impose her authority, there’s no doubt about that."},{"q":"Est-ce qu’il a vraiment autant de qualités que ça ? Qu’est-ce que vous en pensez ?","a":"Does he really have as many qualities as that? What do you think?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You’ve only got two possibilities: leave or stay. – It’s all the same to me.","t":"Vous {{n’avez que}} deux possibilités : partir ou rester. – {{Ça m’est indifférent}}."},{"cue":"This debate is so boring! – We agree on that!","t":"{{Qu’il est ennuyeux}}, ce débat ! – {{Là-dessus}}, on est d’accord."},{"cue":"We’ve neither the time nor the inclination to talk about everything and nothing. – And you’re making it very clear!","t":"Nous {{n’avons ni}} le temps {{ni l’envie}} de {{parler de tout et de rien}}. – Et {{vous le faites savoir}} !"},{"cue":"Isn’t there anything to change? – No, because it’s not a question of understanding but of believing.","t":"{{N’y a-t-il rien}} à changer ? – Non, car {{il ne s’agit pas de}} comprendre {{mais de croire}}."},{"cue":"Do you know these men? – No, I don’t know any of them. I assure you, they’re strangers to me.","t":"{{Connaissez-vous}} ces hommes ? – Non, {{je n’en connais aucun}}. Je vous assure, ce sont des {{inconnus}} pour moi."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Que vous le vouliez ou non","en":"Whether you like it or not","zh":"不管你愿不愿意","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’on le veuille ou non, les sondages font partie intégrante de la période pré-électorale.","exEn":"Whether we like it or not, opinion polls are an integral part of the pre-election period.","src":1},{"fr":"les sondages font partie intégrante de la période pré-électorale","en":"polls are an integral part of the pre-election period","zh":"民调是选前时期不可分割的一部分","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ integral part","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’on le veuille ou non, les sondages font partie intégrante de la période pré-électorale.","exEn":"Whether we like it or not, opinion polls are an integral part of the pre-election period.","src":1},{"fr":"ça n’a aucun intérêt","en":"it is of no interest","zh":"这毫无意思/毫无价值","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ interest","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"alors que pour d’autres, ça n’a aucun intérêt – et ils le font savoir.","exEn":"while for others, it is of no interest – and they let it be known.","src":3},{"fr":"un trottoir","en":"a pavement / sidewalk","zh":"人行道","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"sounds like “trot-twar”: a horse trotting along the pavement, trot-trot","ex":"Je n’ai ni le temps ni l’envie de discuter avec quelqu’un que je ne connais pas sur un trottoir encombré.","exEn":"I have neither the time nor the inclination to chat with someone I don’t know on a crowded pavement.","src":5},{"fr":"Je n’ai ni le temps ni l’envie","en":"I have neither the time nor the inclination","zh":"我既没有时间也没有兴致","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai ni le temps ni l’envie de discuter avec quelqu’un que je ne connais pas sur un trottoir encombré.","exEn":"I have neither the time nor the inclination to chat with someone I don’t know on a crowded pavement.","src":5},{"fr":"Il ne s’agit pas de parler de tout et de rien","en":"It’s not a question of talking about any old thing","zh":"不是要谈天说地、漫无边际","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il ne s’agit pas de parler de tout et de rien mais simplement de répondre à quelques petites questions.","exEn":"It’s not a question of talking about any old thing but simply of answering a few short questions.","src":6},{"fr":"je n’ai que quelques instants à vous accorder","en":"I only have a few moments to give you","zh":"我只能给您几分钟时间","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Puisque vous insistez. Mais je n’ai que quelques instants à vous accorder, alors faites vite.","exEn":"Since you insist. But I only have a few minutes to give you, so hurry up.","src":7},{"fr":"faites vite","en":"hurry up","zh":"请快点","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Puisque vous insistez. Mais je n’ai que quelques instants à vous accorder, alors faites vite.","exEn":"Since you insist. But I only have a few minutes to give you, so hurry up.","src":7},{"fr":"je vous le jure","en":"I swear (to you)","zh":"我向您发誓","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne dépasserai même pas cinq minutes, je vous le jure. Commençons avec le premier ministre :","exEn":"I won’t even take more than five minutes, I swear. Let’s start with the prime minister:","src":8},{"fr":"Je n’ai pas d’opinion tranchée là-dessus.","en":"I don’t have a definite opinion on this.","zh":"对此我没有明确的看法","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ opinion","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai pas d’opinion tranchée là-dessus. En vérité, je n’ai aucune opinion, tout court.","exEn":"I don’t have a definite opinion on this. In truth, I have no opinion at all.","src":11},{"fr":"je n’ai aucune opinion, tout court","en":"I have no opinion at all, full stop","zh":"我根本没有意见，仅此而已","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai pas d’opinion tranchée là-dessus. En vérité, je n’ai aucune opinion, tout court.","exEn":"I don’t have a definite opinion on this. In truth, I have no opinion at all.","src":11},{"fr":"Ça m’est indifférent.","en":"I don’t care. / It’s all the same to me.","zh":"我无所谓","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ indifferent","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça m’est indifférent. Ça durera le temps que ça durera, ni plus, ni moins.","exEn":"I don’t care. It will last as long as it lasts, no more, no less.","src":13},{"fr":"Si vous y tenez.","en":"If you insist.","zh":"如果您坚持的话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si vous y tenez.","exEn":"If you insist.","src":15},{"fr":"Je n’en connais aucun.","en":"I don’t know any of them.","zh":"我一个都不认识","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je n’en connais aucun. Ils sont sans aucun intérêt. Personne ne me passionne.","exEn":"I don’t know any of them. They’re of no interest. I’m not keen on any of them.","src":19},{"fr":"Qu’ils sont idiots, ces sondeurs !","en":"How idiotic these pollsters are!","zh":"这些民调员真蠢！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ idiot","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’ils sont idiots, ces sondeurs ! Ils ne posent aucune question intéressante.","exEn":"These pollsters are so idiotic! They don’t ask any interesting questions.","src":21}],"uncertain":["note 2 reference ‘lesson 81 note 6’ taken from the printed cross-reference; the exact digits in the scan were slightly garbled","culture note: ‘Champs-Élysées’ printed ‘Champs-Élysée’ in the scan, corrected"],"pdfPage":501,"gaps":[[1.68,3.54],[4.74,7.78],[24.86,26.32],[113.38,117.16],[118.98,121.08],[125.24,128.16],[131.54,134.36],[138.58,141.52],[145.32,148.38],[152.72,156.6]],"dur":156.6},{"n":83,"kind":"lesson","title":"Du beurre dans les épinards…","titleEn":"A little bit extra…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon réveil à toutes et à tous. Merci de votre fidélité.","en":"Good morning to all of you. Thanks for being regular listeners.","lit":"good wakeup ... your fidelity"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est un plaisir de vous retrouver sur nos antennes, chères auditrices et chers auditeurs.","en":"It’s a pleasure to find you once again on our airwaves, dear listeners.","lit":"antennas"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Comme tous les jours, nous sommes en direct dès le petit matin, avec ma coéquipière Mireille.","en":"As every day, we are broadcasting live from the early morning onwards, with my partner Mireille.","lit":"teammate"},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Guy vous attend au standard pour notre jeu : « Complétez l’expression ».","en":"Guy is waiting for you at the switchboard for our game: “Complete the expression.”","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Il faut que vous sachiez que, personne n’ayant gagné cette semaine, la cagnotte s’élève dorénavant à mille euros.","en":"You should know that, since no one has won this week, the prize pool is now a thousand euros.","lit":"rises"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"De quoi mettre du beurre dans les épinards, quoi ! Eh oui, le sujet de la semaine, c’est la nourriture !","en":"Enough to earn a little extra, in other words! Yes, this week’s topic is food!","lit":"of what put some butter in the spinach ... what"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Donc vous avez du pain sur la planche ce matin. J’espère que vous avez la pêche.","en":"So you’ve got your work cut out for you this morning. I hope you’re on good form.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Bon, je m’arrête là. Je ne vais pas en faire tout un fromage.","en":"Right, I’ll stop here. I’m not going to make a big deal of it.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, mais ça suffit Fabrice. Arrête de faire l’andouille.","en":"For goodness’ sake! That’s enough, Fabrice. Stop acting the fool.","lit":"no but"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu vois, c’est contagieux ! Bon, puisque tu insistes, je vais être sérieux.","en":"You see, it’s contagious! Well, since you insist, I’ll be serious.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Notre premier candidat ce matin s’appelle Olivier, qui habite à Montpellier.","en":"Our first candidate this morning is Olivier, who lives in Montpellier.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Salut, Olivier. Quel aliment trouve-t-on dans une expression qui veut dire « partager équitablement quelque chose » ?","en":"Hi, Olivier. What kind of food is there in an expression that means “to share something equitably”?","lit":"does one find"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Par exemple : « Pendant longtemps je ne pouvais pas me décider puis, tout à coup, j’ai… »","en":"For example: “For a long time I couldn’t make up my mind and then, suddenly, I …”","lit":"decide myself"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Celle-là, je la connais. C’est une poire. « J’ai coupé la poire en deux ». C’est-à-dire, je l’ai partagée.","en":"I know this one. It’s a pear. “I cut the pear in half” I compromised. That is to say, I shared it.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Bravo ! Mais vous n’êtes pas sorti de l’auberge, si je puis dire, car les questions deviennent de plus en plus dures.","en":"Bravo! But you’re not out of the woods, if I may say so, because the questions get harder and harder.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Par exemple : on dit de quelqu’un qui s’évanouit qu’il ou elle tombe dans un fruit ou un légume. Lequel ?","en":"For example: it’s said of someone who faints that he or she falls into a fruit or vegetable. Which one?","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Ah, ça m’est complètement sorti de la tête. Je vais donner une réponse au hasard : les choux ?","en":"Ah, it’s completely slipped my mind. I’ll give a random answer: cabbages?","lit":"gone-out of the head"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais non ! Si vous êtes dans les choux, vous êtes dans une mauvaise situation.","en":"No! If you’re “in the cabbages,” you’re in a bad situation.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Quelqu’un qui perd connaissance tombe dans les… ? Allez, c’est une spécialité de la Normandie. Toujours pas ?","en":"Someone who passes out falls into the … ? Come on, it’s a specialty of Normandy. Still nothing?","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Les pommes ! On dit qu’il tombe dans les pommes. Il faut que vous alliez plus souvent en vacances dans le nord.","en":"Apples! They say he “falls into the apples” passes out. You need to go north on holiday more often.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Allez, un petit effort mon cher Olivier. Sinon, vous n’allez pas gagner un radis… Désolé.","en":"Come on, make an effort, my dear Olivier. Otherwise you won’t earn a bean … I’m sorry.","lit":"radish"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous reviendrons après une courte page de publicité. À tout de suite.","en":"We’ll be back after a short commercial. See you soon.","lit":"page of"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The subjunctive of the irregular verb savoir is (que) je sache, tu saches, il/elle sache, nous sachions, vous sachiez, ils/elles sachent."},{"ref":"2","text":"The masculine plural les épinards means spinach (the singular is rarely used). Butter adds a little something to spinach, making it more palatable. So the idiom mettre du beurre dans les épinards means to top up one’s income, to earn a little extra: Souvent les étudiants cherchent des petits travaux afin de mettre du beurre dans les épinards, Students often look for odd jobs in order to earn a little extra."},{"ref":"3","text":"une planche means a plank or board, as in une planche à découper, a chopping (“cutting up”) board. The expression avoir du pain sur la planche (“have bread on the board”) resembles to have one’s work cut out, to have a lot on one’s plate: J’ai du pain sur la planche si je veux finir avant midi, I’ve got my work cut out if I want to finish by noon."},{"ref":"4","text":"une pêche, a peach (lesson 53 line 3), apparently gives plenty of energy because avoir la pêche means to be on top form or, to use a food idiom, to be full of beans."},{"ref":"5","text":"The idiom en faire tout un fromage (“make a whole cheese of it”) means to make a big fuss or song and dance. Elle en a fait tout un fromage parce que j’avais dix minutes de retard, She made a song and dance because I was ten minutes late."},{"ref":"6","text":"une andouille is a cold sausage made of pork. The idiom faire l’andouille has nothing to do with butchery, however, because it means to act the fool. Used as an interjection, Andouille ! means Nitwit!"},{"ref":"7","text":"une poire, a pear, is used in the idiom couper la poire en deux (“to cut the pear in half”), meaning to compromise or meet someone halfway. Coupons la poire en deux : tu prends les enfants cette semaine et moi je les prendrai le week-end, Let’s meet halfway: you take the children this week and I’ll take them for the weekend."},{"ref":"8","text":"une auberge, an inn, is often found on restaurant signs, and also in the name of the youth hostel movement (les auberges de jeunesse). The expression ne pas être sorti de l’auberge (always the negative form) means not to be out of the woods."},{"ref":"9","text":"tomber dans les pommes (“fall into the apples”) means to pass out, to faint: Hier, elle était tellement fatiguée qu’elle est tombée dans les pommes, She was so tired yesterday that she passed out. (The expression probably derives from se pâmer, to swoon.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"The subjunctive of the irregular verb aller is (que) j’aille, tu ailles, il/elle aille, nous allions, vous alliez, ils/elles aillent."},{"ref":"","text":"One problem arising from the shortage of French epicene nouns - those having only one form for the male and the female - is the need to use both forms, notably in public address. In these contexts, particularly speeches and media commentary, a noun’s feminine and masculine forms (in that order) are used jointly to ensure gender inclusivity (see lesson 66 line 3). TV presenters, for example, often start their shows with the greeting Chères téléspectatrices, chers téléspectateurs (whereas the English equivalent would be Dear viewers), and radio presenters say Chères auditrices, chers auditeurs (Dear listeners). Likewise, a magazine director might start an editorial with Chères lectrices, chers lecteurs (Dear readers), while a public official in, say, Lyon, might start a blog post with Chères Lyonnaises, chers Lyonnais. Cumbersome as they might seem, these dual forms are very important, for political reasons."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je connaissais la réponse à cette question mais elle m’est sortie de la tête. – Ah bon ?","a":"I knew the answer to that question, but it slipped my mind. – Really?"},{"q":"Ça fait trois ans que tu joues au loto et tu n’as toujours pas gagné un radis.","a":"You’ve been playing the lottery for three years and you still haven’t won a bean."},{"q":"Le candidat est dans les choux, avec seulement un pour cent des intentions de vote.","a":"The candidate’s in a bad way, with only one percent of voting intentions."},{"q":"J’ai vu qu’il n’avait pas la pêche. Il avait l’air très fatigué, puis, tout d’un coup, il s’est évanoui.","a":"I saw that he wasn’t on form. He looked very tired and then, all of a sudden, he fainted."},{"q":"Il faut que j’aille à Montpellier tôt demain matin mais je reviendrai le soir.","a":"I have to go to Montpellier early tomorrow morning but I’ll come back in the evening."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Where can one find the list of competition results? Have I won? I have to know.","t":"{{Où trouve-t-on}} la liste des {{résultats du concours}} ? {{Ai-je}} gagné ? Il faut {{que je sache}} !"},{"cue":"They won the first game, for sure, but there’s no need to make a big deal of it.","t":"{{Ils ont gagné}} le premier jeu, mais ce n’est pas la peine {{d’en faire tout un fromage}}."},{"cue":"We’ve decided to meet halfway and to share the two-thousand-euro prize pool between the three of us.","t":"Nous avons {{décidé}} de {{couper la poire en deux}} et de {{partager la cagnotte}} de {{deux mille}} euros {{entre}} nous trois."},{"cue":"He earns only the minimum wage, so he’s looking to top up his income.","t":"Il {{ne gagne que}} le salaire minimum, donc il cherche à {{mettre du beurre dans les épinards}}."},{"cue":"Don’t be a nitwit. I’ve got a lot on my plate and I have to finish by midnight.","t":"{{Ne fais pas}} l’andouille ! J’ai {{du pain sur la planche}} et je {{dois terminer}} avant minuit."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Merci de votre fidélité.","en":"Thank you for your loyalty.","zh":"感谢您的忠实收听/支持","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ fidelity","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon réveil à toutes et à tous. Merci de votre fidélité.","exEn":"Good morning to all of you. Thanks for being regular listeners.","src":1},{"fr":"nous sommes en direct","en":"we are live","zh":"我们正在直播","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ direct","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comme tous les jours, nous sommes en direct dès le petit matin, avec ma coéquipière Mireille.","exEn":"As every day, we are broadcasting live from the early morning onwards, with my partner Mireille.","src":3},{"fr":"la cagnotte","en":"the prize pool / jackpot","zh":"奖池；累积奖金","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"sounds like “can-yot”: a tin can on a yacht stuffed with the jackpot coins","ex":"Il faut que vous sachiez que, personne n’ayant gagné cette semaine, la cagnotte s’élève dorénavant à mille euros.","exEn":"You should know that, since no one has won this week, the prize pool is now a thousand euros.","src":5},{"fr":"Il faut que vous sachiez que…","en":"You should know that…","zh":"您应该知道……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il faut que vous sachiez que, personne n’ayant gagné cette semaine, la cagnotte s’élève dorénavant à mille euros.","exEn":"You should know that, since no one has won this week, the prize pool is now a thousand euros.","src":5},{"fr":"mettre du beurre dans les épinards","en":"to earn a little extra","zh":"贴补一点收入","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"De quoi mettre du beurre dans les épinards, quoi ! Eh oui, le sujet de la semaine, c’est la nourriture !","exEn":"Enough to earn a little extra, in other words! Yes, this week’s topic is food!","src":6},{"fr":"avoir du pain sur la planche","en":"to have one’s work cut out","zh":"有一大堆活要干","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Donc vous avez du pain sur la planche ce matin. J’espère que vous avez la pêche.","exEn":"So you’ve got your work cut out for you this morning. I hope you’re on good form.","src":7},{"fr":"avoir la pêche","en":"to be on top form","zh":"精力充沛，状态极佳","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"la pêche = peach, but also “energy” in this idiom","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Donc vous avez du pain sur la planche ce matin. J’espère que vous avez la pêche.","exEn":"So you’ve got your work cut out for you this morning. I hope you’re on good form.","src":7},{"fr":"en faire tout un fromage","en":"to make a big fuss","zh":"小题大做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon, je m’arrête là. Je ne vais pas en faire tout un fromage.","exEn":"Right, I’ll stop here. I’m not going to make a big deal of it.","src":8},{"fr":"couper la poire en deux","en":"to meet halfway, to compromise","zh":"各让一步，折中","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Celle-là, je la connais. C’est une poire. « J’ai coupé la poire en deux ». C’est-à-dire, je l’ai partagée.","exEn":"I know this one. It’s a pear. “I cut the pear in half” I compromised. That is to say, I shared it.","src":14},{"fr":"partager équitablement quelque chose","en":"to share something fairly","zh":"公平地分享某物","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ equitably","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Salut, Olivier. Quel aliment trouve-t-on dans une expression qui veut dire « partager équitablement quelque chose » ?","exEn":"Hi, Olivier. What kind of food is there in an expression that means “to share something equitably”?","src":12},{"fr":"Ça m’est complètement sorti de la tête.","en":"It’s completely slipped my mind.","zh":"我完全忘了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah, ça m’est complètement sorti de la tête. Je vais donner une réponse au hasard : les choux ?","exEn":"Ah, it’s completely slipped my mind. I’ll give a random answer: cabbages?","src":17},{"fr":"une réponse au hasard","en":"a random answer","zh":"随便猜的答案","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ah, ça m’est complètement sorti de la tête. Je vais donner une réponse au hasard : les choux ?","exEn":"Ah, it’s completely slipped my mind. I’ll give a random answer: cabbages?","src":17},{"fr":"quelqu’un qui s’évanouit","en":"someone who faints","zh":"晕倒的人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “say-va-nwee”: you ‘say va!’ then everything goes ‘noir’ and you faint","ex":"Par exemple : on dit de quelqu’un qui s’évanouit qu’il ou elle tombe dans un fruit ou un légume. Lequel ?","exEn":"For example: it’s said of someone who faints that he or she falls into a fruit or vegetable. Which one?","src":16},{"fr":"tomber dans les pommes","en":"to faint, to pass out","zh":"晕倒","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les pommes ! On dit qu’il tombe dans les pommes. Il faut que vous alliez plus souvent en vacances dans le nord.","exEn":"Apples! They say he “falls into the apples” passes out. You need to go north on holiday more often.","src":20},{"fr":"une courte page de publicité","en":"a short commercial break","zh":"一小段广告","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ publicity","ff":"publicité = advertising, not “publicity” in general","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous reviendrons après une courte page de publicité. À tout de suite.","exEn":"We’ll be back after a short commercial. See you soon.","src":22}],"uncertain":["ex1 item 1: scan garbled the start of the sentence; reconstructed from the answer","ex2 item 3: ‘deux mille’ and ‘entre’ taken from the answer line"],"pdfPage":507,"gaps":[[1.92,4.02],[5.56,8.54],[25.92,26.94],[128.38,132.4],[134.34,136.54],[140.86,143.74],[147.4,150.56],[155.26,158.12],[164.08,167.32],[171.32,175.46]],"dur":175.5},{"n":84,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vous appelle à propos des travaux de peinture que vous nous avez commandés.","en":"I’m calling you about the painting work you ordered from us.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je crains une mauvaise nouvelle… Dites-la-moi.","en":"I fear bad news… Tell me.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Personne ne vous a prévenue ? Je pensais que mon collègue avait appelé.","en":"Didn’t anyone warn you? I thought my colleague had called.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, personne ne m’a appelée. De quoi s’agit-il ?","en":"No, nobody called me. What’s this about?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Bon, il n’est pas certain que nous puissions venir la semaine prochaine.","en":"Well, it’s not certain that we can come next week.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis dans les choux car j’ai deux ouvriers qui sont malades et personne pour les remplacer.","en":"I’m in a bit of a bind because I have two workers who are ill and no one to replace them.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je veux que vous sachiez que je ferai tout pour venir, comme prévu, mais il faut que vous soyez prête à reporter le rendez-vous.","en":"I want you to know that I’ll do everything I can to come, as planned, but I need you to be prepared to postpone the appointment.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"Eh bien, vu que je n’ai pas le choix. Que me proposez-vous comme nouvelle date ?","en":"Well, since I have no choice. What new date are you offering me?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis désolé de ne pas pouvoir être plus précis, mais ça ne dépend pas que de moi.","en":"I’m sorry I can’t be more precise, but it’s not up to me alone.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vais faire appel à un collègue, mais il est sur un autre chantier en ce moment.","en":"I’m going to call in a colleague, but he’s on another site at the moment.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Quand il aura terminé, peut-il se libérer rapidement ?","en":"When he’s finished, can he be free quickly?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne sais pas quoi vous dire. Il n’est pas certain que son chantier soit terminé d’ici à la fin de la semaine.","en":"I don’t know what to tell you. He’s not sure that his job will be finished by the end of the week.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Je sens que nous ne sommes pas sortis de l’auberge !","en":"I feel that we’re not out of the woods!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Le subjonctif: the subjunctive","text":"The subjunctive mood describes a hypothetical situation. It is generally used in a subordinate clause to talk about actions or events that are desirable, imagined or possible, and that generally depend on an initial verb in the indicative.\nThe subjunctive exists in English but, being almost identical to the indicative, is rarely identified as such (except in phrases such as I wish I were on holiday). In French, however, it is both common and obligatory. This is especially the case in subordinate clauses where the subject is different from the one in the main clause and where the action being described is in some way conditional, theoretical or subjective. In this case, the subordinate verb is preceded by que.\nFor example, Je veux que tu sois heureuse, I want you to be happy (i.e. the person I’m talking to seems miserable - my subjective opinion - and I wish they weren’t).\nThere are four subjunctive tenses (present, past, imperfect and pluperfect) but in modern, conversational French, we generally use the present form (and occasionally the past).\n\n1.1 Formation\nThe present subjunctive is formed by taking the third person plural of the present tense, dropping the final -ent and adding the endings shown below.\n\naimer | finir | attendre\nj’aime | je finisse | j’attende\ntu aimes | tu finisses | tu attendes\nil/elle aime | il/elle finisse | il/elle attende\nnous aimions | nous finissions | nous attendions\nvous aimiez | vous finissiez | vous attendiez\nils/elles aiment | ils/elles finissent | ils/elles attendent\n\nNote that the first and second persons plural of the subjunctive are identical to the equivalent imperfect forms, but the structure of the sentence (and, often, the presence of que) should make it easy to distinguish between the two.\nThe negative follows the usual pattern (vous n’aimiez pas, etc.), while the interrogative is almost never used.\nThese rules apply to all regular and most irregular verbs. Nevertheless there are some irregular forms, especially with major verbs, which have to be memorised:\n\nêtre | avoir\nje sois | j’aie\ntu sois | tu aies\nil/elle soit | il/elle ait\nnous soyons | nous ayons\nvous soyez | vous ayez\nils/elles soient | ils/elles aient\n\nfaire | pouvoir | vouloir\nje fasse | je puisse | je veuille\ntu fasses | tu puisses | tu veuilles\nil/elle fasse | il/elle puisse | il/elle veuille\nnous fassions | nous puissions | nous voulions\nvous fassiez | vous puissiez | vous vouliez\nils/elles fassent | ils/elles puissent | ils/elles veuillent\n\naller\nj’aille | nous allions\ntu ailles | vous alliez\nil/elle aille | ils/elles aillent\n\nThere are other, stem-changing verbs, which we will see in the coming lessons.\n\n1.2 Use\nOne of the most common uses of the subjunctive is when the subject of a sentence expresses a personal feeling, such as regret, hope, preference or expectation, about an action performed - or not - by another person. In this case the first verb is in the indicative, followed by que, and the second is in the subjunctive. Here are some examples:\nJe suis content / heureux / ravi que vous veniez ce soir. I’m pleased / happy / delighted that you’re coming this evening.\nElles veulent que vous téléphoniez tout à l’heure. They want you to phone later on.\nIl espère que son film puisse être distribué en France. He is hoping that his movie can be distributed in France.\nNous préférons que vous leur demandiez. We prefer you to ask them.\nSimilar verbs include aimer que (to wish that), attendre que, s’attendre à ce que (expect), demander que (ask that), douter que (doubt that), être désolé que (be sorry that), être triste que (be sad that), être dommage que (be a shame that), exiger que (demand that) and insister pour que (insist that). Go back to lesson 80 and review these verbs in context, noting how the subjunctive in the subordinate clause is “sub-joined” to the indicative verb in the main clause.\n- The subjunctive is also obligatory with impersonal verbs, especially falloir: to be necessary that, etc.\nIl faut que tu finisses rapidement. You have to finish quickly.\nOther impersonal forms, such as être certain / être sûr que, logically take the indicative in the affirmative:\nIl est certain que nous pouvons les aider. We can certainly help them.\nIn the negative, however, the outcome is in doubt, so the subjunctive is used:\nIl n’est pas certain que nous puissions les aider. It’s not certain that we can help them.\n- The subjunctive is used after subordinating conjunctions, such as bien que, quoi que and pourvu que, that introduce a hypothesis or condition. These, too, are followed by the subjunctive.\nBien que nous soyons amis, je ne peux pas vous aider. Even though we’re friends, I can’t help you.\nQuoi que tu fasses, rien ne changera. Whatever you do, nothing will change.\nPourvu qu’il vienne ! As long as he comes!\nWe’ll see other examples in the coming lessons.\n- Similarly, the subjunctive is necessary if we use an imperative to posit a condition:\nSupposons que quelqu’un vienne te dire : « Vous avez gagné au loto ». Que ferais-tu ? Let’s assume that someone comes along and says: “You’ve won the lotto.” What would you do?\n- The subjunctive is also obligatory with certain time conjunctions:\nFaites vite, avant qu’elle parte en vacances. Do it quickly, before she leaves on holiday.\nJe reste ici jusqu’à ce que tu me dises la vérité. I’m staying here until you tell me the truth.\nIn the next six lessons, we will look at other types of construction in which the subjunctive is obligatory, as well as cases where it may or may not be used, depending on the shade of meaning."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Pronoun order","text":"When several object pronouns are used before a verb, they have to follow a strict order. You are already familiar with the basic patterns, seen in the course of previous lessons. One way to remember the proper sequence is to visualise a soccer team playing in a 5-3-2 formation, with a goalkeeper (y) and the ball (en).\n\nColumn 1: me, te, se, nous, vous\nColumn 2: le, la, les\nColumn 3: lui, leur\nColumn 4: y\nColumn 5: en\n\nThe sequence is actually easier than it might seem. In most cases only two object pronouns are ever used together (the maximum is three), with the most usual combination being Column 1 and Column 2. The pronouns in the first and third columns never go together. Similarly, pronouns in the same column cannot be placed together. For example, Ils se présentent à nous, They introduce themselves to us (never *Ils nous se présentent).\nLook at these examples, all of which start with a subject pronoun, which is closest to the verb.\nJe te donnerai l’adresse de la boutique. ➔ Je te la donnerai. I’ll give you the address of the shop. ➔ I will give it to you.\nElle veut donner le vase à Alex. ➔ Elle veut le lui donner. She wants to give the vase to Alex. ➔ She wants to give it to him.\nNous avons proposé le voyage à nos enfants. ➔ Nous le leur avons proposé. We offered the trip to our children. ➔ We offered it to them.\nVous me parlez souvent de vos amis. ➔ Vous m’en parlez souvent. You often tell me about your friends. ➔ You often tell me about them.\nNous emmenons nos enfants à Nîmes demain. ➔ Nous les y emmenons demain. We’re taking our children to Nîmes tomorrow. ➔ We’re taking them there tomorrow.\nIn the negative, the pronouns come immediately after the first negative particle, ne, while pas comes after the verb, as usual: Je ne te le donnerai pas, Il ne la lui donnera pas, etc.\nWith an imperative, the word order is slightly different. First, we know that me and te become moi and toi (lesson 33 note 3). Second, the second-column pronouns precede those in the first column:\nPassez-moi la tablette. ➔ Passez-la-moi.\nDonnez-lui le miel. ➔ Donnez-le-lui."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 Negative constructions","text":"The simple way to negate a verb is with ne … pas (Il comprend ➔ Il ne comprend pas). But there are other constructions in which pas is replaced by an adverb. Let’s review them:\n- ne … jamais, never\nIls ne veulent jamais venir en vacances avec nous. They never want to come on holiday with us.\nFor emphasis, the adverb sometimes comes first: Jamais ils ne veulent venir en vacances… Never do they want to come on holiday…\n- ne … plus, no longer, no more\nOn ne peut plus continuer comme ça. We can’t go on like this any longer.\nRemember that a definite or indefinite article coming after plus becomes de: Il y a une grève / Il y a des grèves ➔ Il n’y a plus de grève/grèves.\n- ne … que, only\nJe n’ai que deux minutes à vous accorder. I can only give you two minutes.\nIn some cases, ne … que can be replaced by seulement: J’ai seulement deux minutes à vous accorder, but this is considered less elegant.\n- ne … aucun(e), no, none\nNous n’avons aucune nouvelle de lui. We have no news from him.\nAucun(e) can be used alone in response to a question:\nAvez-vous des nouvelles de lui ? - Aucune. Have you had any news from him? - None at all.\n- ne … rien, nothing\nJe n’ai rien à vous dire. I’ve got nothing to say to you.\nWhen negating a compound verb, such as avoir besoin, rien comes after the partitive article: Je n’ai besoin de rien. However, where rien is used as the direct object of a sentence, it is not part of the negative construction: Elle ne mange jamais rien, She never eats anything.\n- ne … personne, no one / anyone, nobody / anybody\nIl n’aime personne. He doesn’t like anyone.\nIf personne is the subject of the sentence, then the verb needs only the particle ne (remember the rule against double negatives): Personne ne l’aime. No one likes him/her.\n- ne … ni … ni, neither … nor\nJe n’ai ni le temps ni l’envie. I have neither the time nor the inclination.\nAs with ne … personne, ni … ni … can negate the subjects of the sentence. In this case, unlike English, the verb is generally in the plural:\nNi la pluie ni la neige ne me gênent. Neither the rain nor the snow bothers me.\nIn some cases, though, the singular can also be used, especially if each of the subjects is considered individually (Ni Rachida ni Anne n’est la mère de cet enfant). But the rules for this are quite complex, so, if in doubt, use the plural.\nIn some cases, two negative adverbs can be used together - for example, Je ne l’ai plus jamais revu, I never saw him again - but the meaning will always be clear.\nFinally, in spoken French, the ne part of a negative construction is often “swallowed,” leaving only the adverb (“Il vient jamais”, for example). Of course, this is incorrect and should not be imitated, but you will certainly hear it."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 The euphonic t in the interrogative","text":"Whereas est-ce que is the most common interrogative construction in everyday French, especially for oral expression (Est-ce qu’il vit à Paris ?), the inverted interrogative is more frequent in written and formal contexts. In terms of pronunciation, this poses no problem if the verb form ends in a consonant (vit-il…), which can be liaised with the initial vowel of the pronoun (vit-il is pronounced like “veet-eel”); but if the verb ends in a vowel (Il parle le français), the liaison is impossible and an inverted form would be hard (and ugly) to pronounce. So, to avoid this, a euphonic t, which does not change the meaning, is inserted between the verb and the pronoun and joined to them by hyphens: Parle-t-il le français ? (In the third person plural, however, this device is unnecessary: Parlent-ils ?) Lastly, if the third person singular ends in d (il prend, for example), a simple inversion is sufficient (prend-il); however, that d is pronounced as a euphonic t."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 More idioms: food","text":"Since France has an enviable gastronomic reputation, it is hardly surprising that many of the idioms in its language are concerned with food and eating. Here is a brief recap of what you have learned:\navoir du pain sur la planche - have one’s work cut out\navoir la pêche / la frite - be full of beans / on top form\ncouper la poire en deux - meet halfway, compromise\nêtre dans les choux - be in a tough spot, in trouble\nfaire l’andouille - act the fool\nen faire tout un fromage - make a song and dance\nmettre du beurre dans les épinards - earn a little extra\nne pas être sorti de l’auberge - not to be out of the woods\nne pas gagner un radis - not to earn a bean\ntomber dans les pommes - pass out\nAll these expressions are used in everyday communications. Two other common expressions relating to food are un navet (literally a turnip but, idiomatically, applied to a film, a flop, a turkey, etc.): Son dernier film est un vrai navet, His/Her latest movie is rubbish; and avoir un cœur d’artichaut (literally “to have an artichoke’s heart”), to be flighty, to fall in love easily. In the latter expression, the person so described (Il a un cœur d’artichaut, celui-là) has as many paramours as an artichoke heart has leaves.\nAnd don’t forget the two proverbs seen in lesson 78:\nLes voyages forment la jeunesse, Travel broadens the mind,\nand\nC’est dans les vieux pots qu’on fait la bonne/meilleure soupe. The best wine comes in old bottles."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"Je vous appelle à propos des travaux de peinture","en":"I’m calling you about the painting work","zh":"我打电话是关于油漆工程的事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je vous appelle à propos des travaux de peinture que vous nous avez commandés.","exEn":"I’m calling you about the painting work you ordered from us.","src":1},{"fr":"Dites-la-moi.","en":"Tell it to me.","zh":"请告诉我吧","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je crains une mauvaise nouvelle… Dites-la-moi.","exEn":"I fear bad news… Tell me.","src":2},{"fr":"Personne ne m’a appelée.","en":"Nobody called me.","zh":"没有人给我打过电话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, personne ne m’a appelée. De quoi s’agit-il ?","exEn":"No, nobody called me. What’s this about?","src":4},{"fr":"De quoi s’agit-il ?","en":"What is it about?","zh":"是关于什么的？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, personne ne m’a appelée. De quoi s’agit-il ?","exEn":"No, nobody called me. What’s this about?","src":4},{"fr":"il n’est pas certain que nous puissions venir","en":"it’s not certain that we can come","zh":"我们不一定能来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon, il n’est pas certain que nous puissions venir la semaine prochaine.","exEn":"Well, it’s not certain that we can come next week.","src":5},{"fr":"Je suis dans les choux.","en":"I’m in a bind / in trouble.","zh":"我陷入困境了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis dans les choux car j’ai deux ouvriers qui sont malades et personne pour les remplacer.","exEn":"I’m in a bit of a bind because I have two workers who are ill and no one to replace them.","src":6},{"fr":"reporter le rendez-vous","en":"to postpone the appointment","zh":"推迟约会/预约","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"reporter = to postpone, not “to report”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je veux que vous sachiez que je ferai tout pour venir, comme prévu, mais il faut que vous soyez prête à reporter le rendez-vous.","exEn":"I want you to know that I’ll do everything I can to come, as planned, but I need you to be prepared to postpone the appointment.","src":7},{"fr":"vu que je n’ai pas le choix","en":"since I have no choice","zh":"既然我别无选择","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Eh bien, vu que je n’ai pas le choix. Que me proposez-vous comme nouvelle date ?","exEn":"Well, since I have no choice. What new date are you offering me?","src":8},{"fr":"ça ne dépend pas que de moi","en":"it doesn’t depend on me alone","zh":"这不是只由我决定的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis désolé de ne pas pouvoir être plus précis, mais ça ne dépend pas que de moi.","exEn":"I’m sorry I can’t be more precise, but it’s not up to me alone.","src":9},{"fr":"un chantier","en":"a building site / work site","zh":"工地；施工现场","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"sounds like “shahn-tyay”: a shanty-yard full of builders hammering","ex":"Je vais faire appel à un collègue, mais il est sur un autre chantier en ce moment.","exEn":"I’m going to call in a colleague, but he’s on another site at the moment.","src":10}],"uncertain":["notes 2: English gloss of ‘Nous les y emmenons demain’ was garbled in the scan; rewritten as ‘We’re taking them there tomorrow’","notes 4: pronunciation respelling of prend-il illegible, omitted","notes 5: proverb printed as ‘la bonne/meilleure soupe’ in the scan, kept","no exercises in this lesson"],"pdfPage":515,"gaps":[[1.82,4.16],[5.64,8.72],[44.26,45.26],[65.12,68.98]],"dur":69},{"n":85,"kind":"lesson","title":"Comment ça, je massacre le français ?","titleEn":"What do you mean, I’m slaughtering French?","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu sors par ce temps, Michel ? Tu ne vois pas qu’il fait un froid glacial ?","en":"You’re going out in this weather, Michel? Can’t you see it’s freezing cold?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Et ils annoncent à nouveau de gros orages pour tout à l’heure.","en":"And they’re again forecasting big storms for later on.","lit":"announcing to new"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Il est où ton pardessus ? Et, de toute façon, où tu vas à cette heure-ci ?","en":"Where’s your overcoat? And, anyway, where are you going at this time of day?","lit":"hour"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Pourquoi tu veux savoir ? Ce ne sont pas tes oignons, mon frère.","en":"Why do you want to know? It’s none of your business, brother.","lit":"(my) brother"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est quoi, ton problème ? Pourquoi tu es si grognon ce matin ?","en":"What’s your problem? Why are you so grumpy this morning?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vais à ma banque, si tu veux savoir. Ça te va comme explication ?","en":"I’m going to my bank, if you must know. How’s that for an explanation?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"On m’a appelé et on m’a demandé de passer à l’agence au plus vite.","en":"I got a call and was asked to come by the branch as soon as possible.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Il paraît que mon compte est à découvert. Je suis tombé des nues quand je l’ai appris.","en":"It appears that my account is overdrawn. I was stunned when I found out.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Ils t’ont appelé quand ? C’est quand le rendez-vous ?","en":"When did they call you? When’s the appointment?","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu pars à quelle heure ? Ton conseiller de clientèle sera là-bas, au rendez-vous ?","en":"What time are you leaving? Will your customer adviser be there at the meeting?","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu poses toutes ces questions pourquoi ? Tu mets ton nez partout.","en":"Why are you asking all these questions? You stick your nose into everything.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu ne sais pas qu’il ne faut pas être curieux et se mêler des affaires des autres ?","en":"Don’t you know not to be curious and to meddle in other people’s business?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Bon, assez discuté. Quelle heure il est ? Il faut que je file.","en":"Right, enough talk. What time is it? I’ve got to dash.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Arrêtez, vous deux, pour l’amour du ciel ! Où avez-vous appris le français ?","en":"Stop it, you two, for heaven’s sake! Where did you learn French?","lit":"love of the sky"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Je vous écoute depuis cinq minutes, et vous massacrez la langue de Molière !","en":"I’ve been listening to you for five minutes, and you’re slaughtering the language of Molière!","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"On doit dire « Où allez-vous ? » ou « Quand est ton rendez-vous ? »","en":"You have to say: “Where are you going?” or “When is your appointment?”","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"De même, on ne dit pas « Il est arrivé quand ? » mais « Quand est-il arrivé ? ».","en":"Likewise, you don’t say “He arrived when?” but “When did he arrive?”.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Et « À quelle heure pars-tu ? » est dix fois plus élégant que « Tu pars à quelle heure ? »","en":"And “What time are you leaving?” is ten times more elegant than “What time you off?”","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Pour en revenir à toi, Michel. Pourquoi tu vas… euh… vas-tu à la banque ?","en":"To get back to you, Michel. Why’re … erm, why are you going to the bank?","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est-à-dire que j’ai emprunté ta carte de crédit et je me suis acheté quelques petits trucs…","en":"That is to say that I borrowed your credit card and bought myself a few things …","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu as dépensé combien, espèce de petit voyou ? Pardon : combien as-tu dépensé, mon frère adoré ?","en":"How much did you spend, you little brat? Excuse me: how much did you spend, my beloved brother?","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Occupe-toi de tes affaires. Je veux dire : la curiosité est un vilain défaut, cher ami.","en":"Mind your own business. I mean: curiosity killed the cat, dear friend.","lit":"is an ugly flaw"},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Vilain défaut ? Et comment !","en":"Ugly flaw? You said it!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"comment, how, is used as an interjection to express surprise, annoyance or disbelief (lesson 40 line 15), just like What…? Comment, tu as perdu mes clés de voiture ? What, you’ve lost my car keys? It can be used like Comment ça (lesson 67 note 2): Comment ça, je parle trop fort ? What do you mean, I’m talking too loudly? Comment can be used as a positive intensifier, Et comment ! , in response to a comment or question, like I should say so, You bet, etc. Donc tu as aimé le jeu ? - Et comment ! So you enjoyed the game? - Didn’t I just! (Don’t confuse the intensifier and the interrogative: see the exercises below.)"},{"ref":"2","text":"un pardessus is an overcoat. The word comes from the hyphenated preposition par-dessus (“by above”), meaning over: Mets un pull par-dessus ta chemise, il fait un froid glacial, Put a sweater over your shirt, it’s freezing cold."},{"ref":"3","text":"In a familiar register, where the subject of an interrogative sentence is a personal pronoun (je, tu, etc.), the question can be formed simply by starting with an interrogative word and avoiding the inversion or est-ce que: Où vous allez ? Where are you going? instead of Où allez-vous ? / Où est-ce que vous allez ? Though not recommended, particularly when writing, these idiomatic interrogatives are very common."},{"ref":"4","text":"un oignon (sometimes spelled ognon), an onion, is used in several idiomatic expressions where the English equivalent is business. The most common are Ce ne sont pas (or simply Ce n’est pas) mes oignons, It’s none of my business, and Occupe-toi de tes oignons, Mind your own business."},{"ref":"5","text":"Another idiomatic way of forming a question is to precede the interrogative word (qui, comment, etc.) with C’est… : C’est qui, ton meilleur ami ? Who’s your best friend? Comment tu es venu ce matin ? How did you get here this morning? These idiomatic forms are often used in advertisements, song lyrics, etc. to reflect everyday speech."},{"ref":"6","text":"découvrir (from couvrir, to cover, see lesson 41 note 5) means both to discover and to uncover. The past participle, découvert, is used as an adjective: une piscine découverte, an open-air swimming pool. But the noun un découvert means an overdraft (i.e. outgoings not covered by incoming amounts). The expression être à découvert means to be overdrawn, to have an overdraft."},{"ref":"7","text":"The adjective nu means naked, bare (and, of course, nude), etc. Il faisait tellement chaud que nous étions torse nu, It was so hot that we were shirtless (“bare torso”). However, in the expression tomber des nues, nues is the plural of an old word for a cloud (un nuage in modern French). The meaning of the idiom is to be flabbergasted, stunned: Je suis tombé des nues quand j’ai appris la nouvelle, I was flabbergasted when I got the news."},{"ref":"8","text":"filer literally means to spin (cotton, etc.), hence un fil, a thread, a wire (remember passer un coup de fil, lesson 41 note 4). Used idiomatically, the verb means to leave, run off, etc. A very common expression is Il faut que je file, I have to dash."},{"ref":"9","text":"In technical French, une espèce is a species. But in ordinary conversation, it means a sort / kind of. La galette est une espèce de crêpe au blé noir, The “galette” is a sort of buckwheat pancake. The word is also used in a familiar register, without the indefinite article and followed by an interjection (just like you little … in English). Espèce d’idiot ! You little idiot! (Un voyou means a lout or ruffian, and, when applied to a child, a rascal.)"},{"ref":"","text":"Molière, the stage name of Jean-Baptiste Poquelin, is one of France’s greatest playwrights, who lived from around 1622 to 1673. In works such as Tartuffe (The Imposter), Le Misanthrope (The Misanthropist) and L’Avare (The Miser), he skewered pretension, power and hypocrisy with biting satire and brilliant wit. His influence on the language is such that French is often referred to figuratively as la langue de Molière (for comparison, English is la langue de Shakespeare).\nMuch of the language you will be learning in these last sixteen lessons is idiomatic and colloquial. By now, you should be able not only to detect these registers but also to recognise more or less instinctively when and where they are used. Nevertheless, you can count on us to continue informing and advising you. Amusez-vous ! (Enjoy!)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Gérard est tombé des nues quand il a découvert que son compte en banque était à découvert.","a":"Gérard was stunned when he discovered that his account was overdrawn."},{"q":"Où est-ce que vous avez appris le français ? Et comment ?","a":"Where did you learn French? And how?"},{"q":"Je sais que je ne dois pas me mêler des affaires des autres, mais je suis quand même curieux.","a":"I know that I’m not supposed to meddle in other people’s business, but I’m still curious."},{"q":"Pourquoi êtes-vous si grognon aujourd’hui ? – Ce ne sont pas vos oignons.","a":"Why are you so grumpy today? – It’s none of your business."},{"q":"Assez discuté, il faut qu’on file. – Quand est-il, votre rendez-vous ?","a":"Enough talk, we need to go. – When is your appointment?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Why didn’t they write to you? – Why are you asking me all these questions?","t":"Pourquoi ne {{vous ont-ils pas écrit}} ? – Pourquoi {{me posez-vous toutes ces}} questions ?"},{"cue":"He has been listening to us, without saying anything, for half an hour. – Curiosity killed the cat.","t":"Il {{nous écoute}} sans {{rien dire depuis une demi-heure}}. – La {{curiosité}} est un {{vilain défaut}}."},{"cue":"What do you mean, you borrowed 1,000 euros from your best friend? – Mind your own business.","t":"{{Comment ça}}, tu {{as emprunté}} mille euros {{à ton meilleur ami}} ? – {{Occupe-toi}} de {{tes oignons}}."},{"cue":"How did you two get here yesterday? And at what time did you leave?","t":"Comment {{êtes-vous venus}} hier, vous deux ? Et {{à quelle heure êtes-vous}} partis ?"},{"cue":"Does that satisfy you as an answer or do you need more explanations?","t":"Est-ce que {{ça vous va comme réponse}} ou {{vous faut-il}} plus {{d’explications}} ?"}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Il fait un froid glacial.","en":"It’s freezing cold.","zh":"天气冷得刺骨","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ glacial","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Tu sors par ce temps, Michel ? Tu ne vois pas qu’il fait un froid glacial ?","exEn":"You’re going out in this weather, Michel? Can’t you see it’s freezing cold?","src":1},{"fr":"de toute façon","en":"anyway, in any case","zh":"无论如何","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il est où ton pardessus ? Et, de toute façon, où tu vas à cette heure-ci ?","exEn":"Where’s your overcoat? And, anyway, where are you going at this time of day?","src":3},{"fr":"Ce ne sont pas tes oignons.","en":"It’s none of your business.","zh":"这不关你的事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pourquoi tu veux savoir ? Ce ne sont pas tes oignons, mon frère.","exEn":"Why do you want to know? It’s none of your business, brother.","src":4},{"fr":"Pourquoi tu es si grognon ce matin ?","en":"Why are you so grumpy this morning?","zh":"你今天早上为什么这么暴躁？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “gron-yon”: a grumpy pig grunting “gronnn-yon!”","ex":"C’est quoi, ton problème ? Pourquoi tu es si grognon ce matin ?","exEn":"What’s your problem? Why are you so grumpy this morning?","src":5},{"fr":"passer à l’agence au plus vite","en":"to come by the branch as soon as possible","zh":"尽快到分行去一趟","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On m’a appelé et on m’a demandé de passer à l’agence au plus vite.","exEn":"I got a call and was asked to come by the branch as soon as possible.","src":7},{"fr":"mon compte est à découvert","en":"my account is overdrawn","zh":"我的账户透支了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un découvert = an overdraft, not “a discovery”","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Il paraît que mon compte est à découvert. Je suis tombé des nues quand je l’ai appris.","exEn":"It appears that my account is overdrawn. I was stunned when I found out.","src":8},{"fr":"Je suis tombé des nues.","en":"I was flabbergasted.","zh":"我惊呆了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il paraît que mon compte est à découvert. Je suis tombé des nues quand je l’ai appris.","exEn":"It appears that my account is overdrawn. I was stunned when I found out.","src":8},{"fr":"Tu mets ton nez partout.","en":"You stick your nose into everything.","zh":"你什么事都要插一脚","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu poses toutes ces questions pourquoi ? Tu mets ton nez partout.","exEn":"Why are you asking all these questions? You stick your nose into everything.","src":11},{"fr":"se mêler des affaires des autres","en":"to meddle in other people’s business","zh":"管别人的闲事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu ne sais pas qu’il ne faut pas être curieux et se mêler des affaires des autres ?","exEn":"Don’t you know not to be curious and to meddle in other people’s business?","src":12},{"fr":"Il faut que je file.","en":"I’ve got to dash.","zh":"我得赶紧走了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bon, assez discuté. Quelle heure il est ? Il faut que je file.","exEn":"Right, enough talk. What time is it? I’ve got to dash.","src":13},{"fr":"pour l’amour du ciel","en":"for heaven’s sake","zh":"看在上天的份上","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Arrêtez, vous deux, pour l’amour du ciel ! Où avez-vous appris le français ?","exEn":"Stop it, you two, for heaven’s sake! Where did you learn French?","src":14},{"fr":"j’ai emprunté ta carte de crédit","en":"I borrowed your credit card","zh":"我借了你的信用卡","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ credit card","ff":"emprunter = to borrow (prêter = to lend)","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"C’est-à-dire que j’ai emprunté ta carte de crédit et je me suis acheté quelques petits trucs…","exEn":"That is to say that I borrowed your credit card and bought myself a few things …","src":20},{"fr":"espèce de petit voyou","en":"you little brat / rascal","zh":"你这个小捣蛋鬼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “vwah-yoo”: a rascal shouting “Voilà, you!” and running off","ex":"Tu as dépensé combien, espèce de petit voyou ? Pardon : combien as-tu dépensé, mon frère adoré ?","exEn":"How much did you spend, you little brat? Excuse me: how much did you spend, my beloved brother?","src":21},{"fr":"La curiosité est un vilain défaut.","en":"Curiosity is an ugly flaw (= curiosity killed the cat).","zh":"好奇心是个坏毛病","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ curiosity (-té = -ty)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Occupe-toi de tes affaires. Je veux dire : la curiosité est un vilain défaut, cher ami.","exEn":"Mind your own business. I mean: curiosity killed the cat, dear friend.","src":22},{"fr":"Et comment !","en":"You bet! / I should say so!","zh":"当然！那还用说！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vilain défaut ? Et comment !","exEn":"Ugly flaw? You said it!","src":23},{"fr":"Comment ça, je parle trop fort ?","en":"What do you mean, I’m talking too loudly?","zh":"什么意思？我说话太大声了吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment ça, je parle trop fort ?","exEn":"What do you mean, I’m talking too loudly?","src":0}],"uncertain":["dialogue 7/8: no leading dash in the scan, treated as same speaker","dialogue 16: ‘Quand est ton rendez-vous ?’ kept as printed","ex1 item 5: ‘Quand est-il, votre rendez-vous ?’ printed as ‘Quand est-il votre rendez-vous ?’, comma added","ex2 item 1 and 2: answer lines were split/mixed by the OCR; order reconstructed from the sentences"],"pdfPage":525,"gaps":[[1.78,3.68],[5.62,8.46],[12.08,13.1],[15.44,16.58],[20.52,21.56],[44.12,45.22],[48.98,50.06],[53.1,54.14],[56.98,57.98],[61.04,62.2],[113.68,116.94],[118.96,121.04],[125.44,128.38],[131.38,134.34],[138.18,141.34],[145.62,148.84],[152.8,157.26]],"dur":157.3},{"n":86,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un entretien avec un chasseur de têtes","titleEn":"An interview with a head-hunter","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous sommes à la recherche d’un responsable du commerce international","en":"We are looking for an international trade manager","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"qui s’occupera plus particulièrement des pays voisins de la France.","en":"who will take care in particular of France’s neighbouring countries.","lit":"occupy himself"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"La Suisse est un des marchés que nous essayons de pénétrer depuis très longtemps.","en":"Switzerland is one of the markets that we have been trying to penetrate for a very long time.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"En fait, c’est le marché le plus difficile que nous ayons connu jusqu’à présent.","en":"In fact, it’s the most difficult market we have experienced so far.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"La personne à qui nous confierons le poste sera quelqu’un d’exceptionnel –","en":"The person we will entrust the job to will be someone exceptional –","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"une femme ou un homme dont les compétences techniques sont reconnues et dont nous pouvons être sûrs.","en":"a woman or a man whose technical skills are acknowledged and of whom we can be sure.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous n’avons pas encore finalisé le profil de ce poste ni le périmètre dont le nouveau directeur sera responsable","en":"We haven’t yet finalised the profile of this position nor the scope for which the new director will be responsible","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"car cela dépendra en partie de la personne que nous trouverons ou qui nous sera présentée par votre cabinet.","en":"because it will depend in part on who we find or who is introduced to us by your firm.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Il se peut que ce soit quelqu’un en interne, qui travaille dans le groupe, mais ce n’est pas la piste que nous privilégions pour l’instant.","en":"It may be someone in-house, working in the group, but that is not the avenue that we favour at the moment.","lit":"in internal"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"En tout état de cause, il ou elle aura une excellente expression écrite et orale,","en":"In any case, he or she will have excellent written and oral expression,","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"une parfaite connaissance des marchés français, suisse et belge et de leurs différentes langues,","en":"a perfect knowledge of the French, Swiss and Belgian markets and their different languages,","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"ainsi que de la rigueur, une vision stratégique, et, surtout, l’esprit cartésien.","en":"as well as discipline, a strategic vision, and, above all, a rational mindset.","lit":"rigour"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est pour cette raison que nous avons besoin de vos services, dont on nous dit le plus grand bien.","en":"That’s why we need your services, which are highly spoken of.","lit":"it is for this reason ... of which one tells us the greatest good"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Le recrutement en ligne a ses limites, dont vous êtes certainement au courant.","en":"Online recruitment has its limits, of which you are certainly aware.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Je vous remercie de votre confiance, dont nous nous montrerons dignes, je vous le promets.","en":"I thank you for your confidence, which we will prove worthy of, I promise you.","lit":"of which we will show"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous saurons dénicher la personne dont vous avez besoin, celui ou celle qui correspondra au mieux à vos exigences.","en":"We’ll track down the person you need, the one who best suits your requirements.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons plusieurs possibilités, dont un Belge. Mais je pense connaître LE candidat qu’il vous faut.","en":"We have several possibilities, including a Belgian. But I think I know THE candidate you need.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Il s’agit d’une femme ayant une grande expérience à l’export et dont les qualités sont reconnues par tous ses collègues.","en":"This is a woman with huge experience in export and whose qualities are recognised by all her colleagues.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Je la connais personnellement. Je peux vous affirmer qu’elle pourra fidéliser les clients,","en":"I know her personally. I can tell you that she will be able to build customer loyalty,","lit":"make faithful"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"accroître les ventes et développer votre groupe, dont l’activité est en pleine croissance.","en":"increase sales and develop your group, whose business is growing strongly.","lit":"activity ... in full growth"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Parmi ses nombreux atouts, elle est trilingue et, surtout, elle est suisse.","en":"Among her many assets, she is trilingual and, above all, Swiss.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Il me la faut ! Qui est-ce, cette perle rare, et quand pouvez-vous me la présenter ?","en":"I need her! Who is this rare gem, and when can you introduce her to me?","lit":"pearl"},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Rien de plus facile : elle est assise en face de vous en ce moment !","en":"Nothing could be easier: she’s sitting in front of you right now!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Remember that, in a relative clause, qui replaces the subject and que the object (lesson 49 section 3). Both can refer to either people or things. And whereas the relative can often be omitted in English (one of the markets we have been trying to penetrate), this is not the case in French (un des marchés que nous essayons de pénétrer). Qui can also replace the indirect object after a preposition: la personne à qui nous confierons le poste, the person to whom we will give the job (or, in more colloquial English, the person we will give the job to)."},{"ref":"2","text":"The relative pronoun dont, seen in lesson 71 line 20, basically means of whom, whose or of which, in reference to the object of a relative clause: Je suis fier de cette jeune équipe ➔ L’équipe dont je suis fier est jeune. The team of which I am proud (or, in informal English, the team I am proud of) is young. Dont can also be used, as in line 17, to translate including (or of which): Deux chambres d’hôtel, dont une avec une salle de bain, Two hotel rooms, one of which with a bathroom."},{"ref":"3","text":"The idiomatic construction il se peut que (from pouvoir) indicates a possibility, like it may be that, etc. And because of the conditionality, the accompanying verb is in the subjunctive: Il se peut que je sois en retard car j’ai une réunion à midi, I may be late because I have a noon meeting."},{"ref":"4","text":"une piste means a track or trail. Among the many other meanings are an airport runway (piste d’aéroport), a dance floor (piste de danse) or, for skiers, a run, slope (or … piste!). But the noun is often used figuratively, as in Il faut trouver une solution rapidement, et nous avons plusieurs pistes à explorer, We need to find a solution quickly and there are several avenues to explore. In short, une piste is something that leads somewhere!"},{"ref":"5","text":"The regular verb privilégier (the root of privilege) is a faux-ami, meaning to favour: Notre société privilégie une approche éthique, Our company favours an ethical approach. However, the past participle retains the idea of privileged or special: La France a une relation privilégiée avec plusieurs pays du sud, France has a special relationship with several southern countries. As always, the context will dictate the appropriate translation."},{"ref":"6","text":"une cause, a cause, a reason, features in a number of expressions, notably à cause de, because of (lesson 18 line 6) and en tout état de cause (“in any state of cause”), in any case, anyway: En tout état de cause, vous devez prendre vos vacances avant la fin du mois de décembre, In any case, you must take your holidays before the end of December. The expression is synonymous with quoi qu’il en soit (lesson 81 note 7), and both are quite formal."},{"ref":"7","text":"In many ways, French is more grammatically precise than English. Here is a good example: les marchés suisse et belge refers to the Swiss market and the Belgian market, each considered individually, whereas les marchés suisses et belges implies several markets in both countries. In both cases, the English translation is the Swiss and Belgian markets. In spoken French, there is no difference in pronunciation between the singular and plural forms, but the difference is obvious in the written language."},{"ref":"8","text":"When penser means to think about, to consider, it can be followed directly by an infinitive: Je pense acheter une voiture neuve, I’m thinking about buying a new car. Of course, if we repeat the pronoun, the verb that follows penser has to be conjugated: Je pense que je vais acheter une voiture neuve, but there is no difference in meaning between the two constructions. Another common verb that can be followed by the infinitive is croire, to believe: Je crois savoir que vous êtes avocat, I understand that you are a lawyer."},{"ref":"9","text":"Here’s another example of a verb that is formed from an adjective and can be tricky to translate: fidèle means faithful, so fidéliser (“make faithful”) means something along the lines of to develop / build loyalty: Nous devons fidéliser notre clientèle et analyser ses besoins, We need to develop our customers’ loyalty and analyse their needs. As is often the case with this type of verb, there is no word-for-word translation. You need to identify the root word in order to find the right shade of meaning."},{"ref":"10","text":"This is the personalised form of falloir (lesson 63 section 2) with the indirect object pronoun (me) and the direct object pronoun replacing the noun (here, la femme, line 18). So, for example, if the direct object is masculine plural (les atouts) and the indirect object pronoun is nous, the sentence is Il nous les faut, We must have them. When faced with a sentence containing multiple pronouns, always try to break it down into its component parts."},{"ref":"","text":"René Descartes was a 17th century mathematician and philosopher who argued that knowledge can be achieved only through rational reasoning. His most famous formulation, Je pense, donc je suis (I think, therefore I am), is part of French culture, both highbrow and popular. Descartes’ influence and his reputation for rational reasoning is such that the nation prides itself on being cartésien (Cartesian), that is to say dispassionate and logical."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Nous n’avons pas encore finalisé les activités dont sera responsable le nouveau directeur.","a":"We haven’t yet finalized the activities the new manager will be responsible for."},{"q":"Les pays voisins de la France sont la Belgique, la Suisse, l’Allemagne, l’Italie et l’Espagne.","a":"France’s neighbouring countries are Belgium, Switzerland, Germany, Italy and Spain."},{"q":"J’essaierai de me montrer digne de ta confiance. En tout état de cause, je ferai de mon mieux.","a":"I’ll try to be worthy of your trust. In any case, I will do my best."},{"q":"Cette perle rare dont tu me parles depuis une heure est en face de toi.","a":"This absolute gem that you’ve been telling me about for an hour is right in front of you."},{"q":"Leur directrice commerciale est la femme la plus intelligente que j’aie connue depuis très longtemps.","a":"Their sales manager is the smartest woman I’ve known in a very long time."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"We think we know the person you need for the job. He’s a Swiss guy.","t":"Nous {{pensons connaître}} la personne {{dont vous avez besoin}} pour ce poste. {{Il s’agit}} d’un {{Suisse}}."},{"cue":"The police officer told me that several leads were possible but that he favoured the simplest solution.","t":"Le policier m’a dit que {{plusieurs pistes étaient possibles}} mais qu’il {{privilégiait}} la solution {{la plus simple}}."},{"cue":"We are demanding free access to all the Belgian market. By contrast, the Swiss gas market has reopened.","t":"Nous {{exigeons}} un accès libre au {{marché belge}}. En revanche, {{le marché suisse}} du gaz {{est rouvert}}."},{"cue":"We understand that the document you are talking about has not yet been published? – Unfortunately, not.","t":"Nous {{croyons savoir que}} le document {{dont vous parlez}} n’a pas encore été publié. – {{Malheureusement}}, non."},{"cue":"Jean Garnier will be able to build loyalty with all of our customers, whose needs are very varied.","t":"Jean Garnier {{pourra fidéliser}} tous nos clients, {{dont les besoins}} sont très variés."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Nous sommes à la recherche d’un responsable","en":"We are looking for a manager","zh":"我们正在寻找一位负责人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous sommes à la recherche d’un responsable du commerce international","exEn":"We are looking for an international trade manager","src":1},{"fr":"En fait, c’est le marché le plus difficile","en":"In fact, it’s the most difficult market","zh":"事实上，这是最难的市场","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"en fait = in fact / actually","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En fait, c’est le marché le plus difficile que nous ayons connu jusqu’à présent.","exEn":"In fact, it’s the most difficult market we have experienced so far.","src":4},{"fr":"jusqu’à présent","en":"so far, until now","zh":"到目前为止","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En fait, c’est le marché le plus difficile que nous ayons connu jusqu’à présent.","exEn":"In fact, it’s the most difficult market we have experienced so far.","src":4},{"fr":"Il se peut que ce soit quelqu’un en interne.","en":"It may be someone in-house.","zh":"也许是公司内部的人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il se peut que ce soit quelqu’un en interne, qui travaille dans le groupe, mais ce n’est pas la piste que nous privilégions pour l’instant.","exEn":"It may be someone in-house, working in the group, but that is not the avenue that we favour at the moment.","src":9},{"fr":"en tout état de cause","en":"in any case","zh":"无论如何（较正式）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"En tout état de cause, il ou elle aura une excellente expression écrite et orale,","exEn":"In any case, he or she will have excellent written and oral expression,","src":10},{"fr":"nous avons besoin de vos services","en":"we need your services","zh":"我们需要您的服务","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est pour cette raison que nous avons besoin de vos services, dont on nous dit le plus grand bien.","exEn":"That’s why we need your services, which are highly spoken of.","src":13},{"fr":"vous êtes certainement au courant","en":"you are certainly aware","zh":"您当然知道（了解情况）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le recrutement en ligne a ses limites, dont vous êtes certainement au courant.","exEn":"Online recruitment has its limits, of which you are certainly aware.","src":14},{"fr":"Je vous remercie de votre confiance.","en":"Thank you for your trust.","zh":"感谢您的信任","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Je vous remercie de votre confiance, dont nous nous montrerons dignes, je vous le promets.","exEn":"I thank you for your confidence, which we will prove worthy of, I promise you.","src":15},{"fr":"Nous saurons dénicher la personne dont vous avez besoin.","en":"We’ll track down the person you need.","zh":"我们会找到您需要的人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “day-nee-shay”: you find the person hiding in a niche (‘day niche’)","ex":"Nous saurons dénicher la personne dont vous avez besoin, celui ou celle qui correspondra au mieux à vos exigences.","exEn":"We’ll track down the person you need, the one who best suits your requirements.","src":16},{"fr":"elle pourra fidéliser les clients","en":"she will be able to build customer loyalty","zh":"她能够留住客户、建立客户忠诚度","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ fidelity","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je la connais personnellement. Je peux vous affirmer qu’elle pourra fidéliser les clients,","exEn":"I know her personally. I can tell you that she will be able to build customer loyalty,","src":19},{"fr":"accroître les ventes","en":"to increase sales","zh":"增加销量","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “ah-krwatr”: a croissant (‘croît’) grows bigger, so sales grow","ex":"accroître les ventes et développer votre groupe, dont l’activité est en pleine croissance.","exEn":"increase sales and develop your group, whose business is growing strongly.","src":20},{"fr":"une perle rare","en":"a rare gem (an ideal person)","zh":"难得的人才","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il me la faut ! Qui est-ce, cette perle rare, et quand pouvez-vous me la présenter ?","exEn":"I need her! Who is this rare gem, and when can you introduce her to me?","src":22},{"fr":"Il me la faut !","en":"I need her! / I must have her!","zh":"我必须得到她！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il me la faut ! Qui est-ce, cette perle rare, et quand pouvez-vous me la présenter ?","exEn":"I need her! Who is this rare gem, and when can you introduce her to me?","src":22},{"fr":"Rien de plus facile","en":"Nothing could be easier","zh":"再容易不过了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Rien de plus facile : elle est assise en face de vous en ce moment !","exEn":"Nothing could be easier: she’s sitting in front of you right now!","src":23},{"fr":"elle est assise en face de vous","en":"she is sitting in front of you","zh":"她就坐在您对面","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Rien de plus facile : elle est assise en face de vous en ce moment !","exEn":"Nothing could be easier: she’s sitting in front of you right now!","src":23}],"uncertain":["note 7: scan printed ‘German’ in two places where the sense (Swiss and Belgian) requires ‘Belgian’; corrected","ex2 item 3: ‘est rouvert’ from the answer list; English cue says ‘has been open’, rendered ‘has reopened’","ex2 item 5: surname printed ‘Çarnier’ in the scan, read as ‘Garnier’"],"pdfPage":531,"gaps":[[1.7,3.62],[6.22,9.26],[45.32,46.4],[52.36,53.4],[78.22,79.26],[90.18,91.2],[129.5,133.08],[135.02,136.96],[142.14,145.24],[151.7,154.74],[160.98,164.06],[167.54,170.48],[175.22,179.12]],"dur":179.1},{"n":87,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le commissaire Périer mène l’enquête","titleEn":"Superintendent Périer leads the investigation","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Mes chers collègues, j’ai la nette impression que la guerre froide vient de se réchauffer.","en":"Dear colleagues, I have the distinct impression that the cold war has just warmed up.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Un agent secret travaillant pour les services de sécurité de nos ennemis","en":"A secret agent working for the security services of our enemies","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"a été trouvé sans vie sur les marches du Trocadéro.","en":"was found lifeless on the steps of the Trocadéro.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu veux dire qu’on a descendu un de leurs espions ? C’est super !","en":"You mean we have taken out one of their spies? That’s great!","lit":"descended"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Pas si vite. L’histoire est plus compliquée qu’il ne paraît.","en":"Not so fast. The story’s more complicated than it appears.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"L’homme qu’on a retrouvé mort était un agent double, un des nôtres,","en":"The man we found dead was a double agent, one of ours,","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"qui avait obtenu des renseignements à propos d’une arme secrète redoutable,","en":"who had obtained information about a formidable secret weapon,","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"capable de contaminer les réserves d’eau potable de nos plus grandes agglomérations.","en":"capable of contaminating the drinking water supplies of our biggest urban areas.","lit":"agglomerations"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Antoine était sur le point de nous transmettre ces informations vitales quand…","en":"Antoine was about to pass us this vital information when …","lit":"transmit"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est honteux ! Voilà quelqu’un qui a risqué sa peau en travaillant dans l’ombre, et nous étions incapables de le protéger.","en":"It’s shameful! Here’s someone who risked his life working in the shadows, and we were unable to protect him.","lit":"skin"},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Taisez-vous, Lucas ! Vous ne connaissez pas le fin mot de l’histoire. Antoine a été trahi par quelqu’un de chez nous.","en":"Shut up, Lucas! You don’t know the real story. Antoine was betrayed by one of our people.","lit":"fine word of the story ... ours"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Qui oserait faire une chose pareille ?","en":"Who would dare to do such a thing?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est justement ce qu’on va essayer de savoir. Vous pouvez compter sur moi.","en":"That’s exactly what we’re going to try to find out. You can count on me.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Je ne lâcherai pas l’enquête tant qu’on n’aura pas mis la main sur le tueur et le commanditaire du meurtre.","en":"I won’t let go of the investigation until we’ve got our hands on the killer and the person who ordered the murder.","lit":"put the hand"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Est-ce qu’on sait au moins comment est mort notre collègue ? Abattu ? Poignardé ?","en":"Do we at least know how our colleague died? Shot? Stabbed?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Selon toute vraisemblance, il a été empoisonné.","en":"In all likelihood, he was poisoned.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Sur le lieu du crime, il flottait comme une drôle d’odeur – on aurait dit des amandes amères.","en":"At the crime scene, there was a strange smell – it was like bitter almonds.","lit":"place ... floated ... one would have said"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout laisse à penser qu’il a bu quelque chose, probablement une tisane, avec son assassin, inconscient du danger qui le menaçait.","en":"Everything suggests that he drank something, probably a herbal tea, with his murderer, unaware of the danger that was threatening him.","lit":"leaves to think"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Il a disparu de la circulation, jusqu’à ce qu’on trouve le cadavre.","en":"He disappeared from circulation until we found the corpse.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Avant de me lancer à la recherche du meurtrier, vous devez savoir que vous êtes tous des suspects.","en":"Before I start looking for the murderer, you should know that you are all suspects.","lit":"throw myself at the search of"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Je veux que vous sachiez que je n’épargnerai personne dans mon enquête et que le coupable n’ira pas en prison.","en":"I want you to know that I will spare no one in my investigation and that the culprit won’t go to jail.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment ? Vous allez le laisser filer ?","en":"What? Are you going to let him get away?","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Surtout pas ! Mais les accidents malheureux sont si vite arrivés, vous savez…","en":"Certainly not! But unfortunate accidents happen so quickly, you know …","lit":"unhappy"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(18) (20) Beware of words that are spelled the same in both languages. Although you may know the rules, it’s so easy to confuse the English and French pronunciations. Thus assassin and suspect are not pronounced like their English twins: the final -in of assassin is a nasal sound, and the final -t of suspect is silent."},{"ref":"1","text":"Remember that descendre is one of those verbs that can be used transitively and intransitively (lesson 70 section 2). Among its transitive meanings, based on the notion of to lower, is to shoot down or, in slang, to bump off."},{"ref":"2","text":"Like its English cognate, super can be used as an (invariable) adjective - Elle est super, la nouvelle pharmacienne, The new pharmacist is great - and an intensifier, before an adjective: Elle est super sympa, She’s super nice. The word can also be used as an exclamation: Super ! Excellent! Great, etc. Don’t confuse it with another invariable adjective, superbe, superb, glorious, etc. Quelle journée superbe ! What a glorious day!"},{"ref":"3","text":"With some verbs, ne is used as an expletive, a word that fills out a sentence without adding to the meaning. This usage is often found in subjunctive constructions: Le mauvais temps a empêché qu’ils ne partent, The bad weather prevented them from leaving. More details in an upcoming lesson."},{"ref":"4","text":"This is the pluperfect tense, formed with the imperfect of avoir (or être, depending on the verb) and the past participle. It is used in more or less the same way as its English counterpart: Elle était déjà partie quand je suis arrivé, She had already left when I arrived."},{"ref":"5","text":"The irregular verb se taire means to fall silent or keep quiet: Quand je demande qui va payer, tout le monde se tait, When I ask who is going to pay, everyone keeps quiet. The imperative Taisez-vous (familiar: Tais-toi) means Be quiet or Shut up, depending on the context. The past participle (following the same pattern as plaire, plu, to please / pleased) is tu: Tout à coup, il s’est tu, Suddenly, he fell silent."},{"ref":"6","text":"Don’t confuse the feminine noun la fin, the end, with the adjective fin(e), meaning thin: Ajoutez une fine couche de chocolat, Add a thin layer of chocolate. By extension, it can also mean fine, delicate, high class, etc. Notre magasin propose une gamme complète de vins fins, Our shop offers a full range of fine wines. Fin can also be used as an intensifier, meaning last and final: Ils habitent au fin fond de la campagne, They live deep in the countryside. Whence the meaning of the expression le fin mot de l’histoire, “the last word of the story,” i.e. the real truth, the bottom line."},{"ref":"7","text":"The adverb tant que means as long as: Tant que vous buvez avec modération, vous n’avez pas de soucis, As long as you drink in moderation, you have nothing to worry about. In some contexts, tant que can also mean until: Vous ne pouvez pas accéder à votre compte tant que vous n’avez pas payé, You can’t access your account until you have paid. In this case, it has the same meaning as jusqu’à ce que: … jusqu’à ce que Laurent soumette un document … (see lesson 81 line 14)."},{"ref":"8","text":"Formed from vrai, true, and sembler, to seem, la vraisemblance means likelihood. It is generally used in the expression selon toute vraisemblance, in all likelihood / probability. The adjective vraisemblable therefore means likely: Il est très vraisemblable qu’elle perdra son travail, It’s very likely that she will lose her job."},{"ref":"9","text":"We know that drôle means funny (lesson 41 line 15). And, as in English, the underlying meaning can be strange, peculiar: C’est un drôle de type, ton ami, Your friend is a strange guy."},{"ref":"10","text":"One way to avoid the subjunctive is to change the structure of the sentence. For example, Avant qu’il soit un espion, notre agent était comptable, Before he was a spy, our agent was an accountant, can be recast as Avant de devenir espion, etc. We’ll see more ways of getting around the subjunctive later on."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Il y a une nette différence aujourd’hui entre le nord, pluvieux, et le sud, ensoleillé.","a":"There’s a sharp difference today between the rainy north and the sunny south."},{"q":"Nous étions sur le point de sortir quand, tout à coup, j’ai aperçu un homme dans l’ombre.","a":"We were about to go out when suddenly I saw a man in the shadows."},{"q":"C’est un drôle de type, Arnaud. Il habite au fin fond de la campagne, sans voiture ni téléphone.","a":"He’s a funny guy, Arnaud. He lives deep in the countryside, without a car or a telephone."},{"q":"C’est honteux : elle était si faible qu’on aurait dit un cadavre.","a":"It’s shameful: she was so weak that she seemed like a corpse."},{"q":"Est-ce que tu pourrais me parler de ton expérience avec les élections, avant que je me lance, moi aussi ?","a":"Could you tell me about your experience with elections, before I start out, too?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"The superintendent wants us to know that he will spare no one and that he’ll find the culprits.","t":"Le commissaire {{veut que nous sachions}} qu’il {{n’épargnera personne}} et qu’il trouvera {{les coupables}}."},{"cue":"Shut up! You won’t be able to do anything until we get our hands on that information.","t":"{{Tais-toi}} ! Tu ne pourras rien faire {{tant que nous n’aurons pas mis la main}} sur ces {{informations}}."},{"cue":"Nolan never got to the bottom of the story because he died in an unfortunate accident.","t":"Nolan {{n’avait}} jamais {{connu le fin mot de}} l’histoire car {{il est mort dans un accident malheureux}}."},{"cue":"Try reading this book. You’ll see, it’s much more interesting than it appears.","t":"{{Essaie}} de lire ce livre. Tu verras, c’est {{beaucoup plus intéressant qu’il ne paraît}}."},{"cue":"Will you dare ask the question before it’s too late? – Everything suggests that he won’t answer.","t":"{{Oserez-vous}} lui poser la question, {{avant qu’il ne soit trop tard}} ? – {{Tout laisse à penser}} qu’il ne répondra pas."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"j’ai la nette impression que","en":"I have the distinct impression that","zh":"我有明显的感觉……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ impression","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mes chers collègues, j’ai la nette impression que la guerre froide vient de se réchauffer.","exEn":"Dear colleagues, I have the distinct impression that the cold war has just warmed up.","src":1},{"fr":"trouvé sans vie","en":"found lifeless","zh":"被发现已经死亡","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"a été trouvé sans vie sur les marches du Trocadéro.","exEn":"was found lifeless on the steps of the Trocadéro.","src":3},{"fr":"C’est super !","en":"That’s great!","zh":"太棒了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ super","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu veux dire qu’on a descendu un de leurs espions ? C’est super !","exEn":"You mean we have taken out one of their spies? That’s great!","src":4},{"fr":"Pas si vite.","en":"Not so fast.","zh":"别急，没那么快","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pas si vite. L’histoire est plus compliquée qu’il ne paraît.","exEn":"Not so fast. The story’s more complicated than it appears.","src":5},{"fr":"Antoine était sur le point de nous transmettre ces informations","en":"Antoine was about to pass us this information","zh":"安托万正要把这些信息传给我们","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Antoine était sur le point de nous transmettre ces informations vitales quand…","exEn":"Antoine was about to pass us this vital information when …","src":9},{"fr":"C’est honteux !","en":"It’s shameful!","zh":"真丢人！太可耻了！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “on-tuh”: ‘on you!’ everyone points at you in shame","ex":"C’est honteux ! Voilà quelqu’un qui a risqué sa peau en travaillant dans l’ombre, et nous étions incapables de le protéger.","exEn":"It’s shameful! Here’s someone who risked his life working in the shadows, and we were unable to protect him.","src":10},{"fr":"quelqu’un qui a risqué sa peau","en":"someone who risked his life","zh":"冒着生命危险的人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est honteux ! Voilà quelqu’un qui a risqué sa peau en travaillant dans l’ombre, et nous étions incapables de le protéger.","exEn":"It’s shameful! Here’s someone who risked his life working in the shadows, and we were unable to protect him.","src":10},{"fr":"Taisez-vous !","en":"Shut up! / Be quiet!","zh":"闭嘴！安静！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “tay-zay-voo”: “Tay! Say-voo!” — a teacher shouting for silence","ex":"Taisez-vous, Lucas ! Vous ne connaissez pas le fin mot de l’histoire. Antoine a été trahi par quelqu’un de chez nous.","exEn":"Shut up, Lucas! You don’t know the real story. Antoine was betrayed by one of our people.","src":11},{"fr":"le fin mot de l’histoire","en":"the real story / the bottom line","zh":"事情的真相","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Taisez-vous, Lucas ! Vous ne connaissez pas le fin mot de l’histoire. Antoine a été trahi par quelqu’un de chez nous.","exEn":"Shut up, Lucas! You don’t know the real story. Antoine was betrayed by one of our people.","src":11},{"fr":"Qui oserait faire une chose pareille ?","en":"Who would dare to do such a thing?","zh":"谁敢做这样的事？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qui oserait faire une chose pareille ?","exEn":"Who would dare to do such a thing?","src":12},{"fr":"Vous pouvez compter sur moi.","en":"You can count on me.","zh":"您可以信赖我","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est justement ce qu’on va essayer de savoir. Vous pouvez compter sur moi.","exEn":"That’s exactly what we’re going to try to find out. You can count on me.","src":13},{"fr":"tant qu’on n’aura pas mis la main sur le tueur","en":"until we have got our hands on the killer","zh":"在我们抓到凶手之前","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne lâcherai pas l’enquête tant qu’on n’aura pas mis la main sur le tueur et le commanditaire du meurtre.","exEn":"I won’t let go of the investigation until we’ve got our hands on the killer and the person who ordered the murder.","src":14},{"fr":"selon toute vraisemblance","en":"in all likelihood","zh":"十有八九；很可能","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ verisimilitude / likelihood","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Selon toute vraisemblance, il a été empoisonné.","exEn":"In all likelihood, he was poisoned.","src":16},{"fr":"il flottait comme une drôle d’odeur","en":"there was a strange smell in the air","zh":"空气中飘着一股奇怪的气味","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"drôle = funny, but also “strange” here","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Sur le lieu du crime, il flottait comme une drôle d’odeur – on aurait dit des amandes amères.","exEn":"At the crime scene, there was a strange smell – it was like bitter almonds.","src":17},{"fr":"Tout laisse à penser qu’il a bu quelque chose","en":"Everything suggests that he drank something","zh":"一切迹象表明他喝了什么东西","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tout laisse à penser qu’il a bu quelque chose, probablement une tisane, avec son assassin, inconscient du danger qui le menaçait.","exEn":"Everything suggests that he drank something, probably a herbal tea, with his murderer, unaware of the danger that was threatening him.","src":18},{"fr":"Surtout pas !","en":"Certainly not! / Definitely not!","zh":"绝对不行！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Surtout pas ! Mais les accidents malheureux sont si vite arrivés, vous savez…","exEn":"Certainly not! But unfortunate accidents happen so quickly, you know …","src":23}],"uncertain":["note pron: the printed respellings of assassin and suspect were illegible in the scan; replaced by a short description of the sounds","note 7: cross-reference garbled in the scan (‘lesson 87 line 74’); read as lesson 81 line 14, where jusqu’à ce que Laurent soumette appears","note 2: ‘Elle est super sympa’ reconstructed from ‘super sym pa’","culture note: none printed for this lesson (the Descartes box belongs to lesson 86)"],"pdfPage":539,"gaps":[[2.22,3.94],[6.08,8.88],[13.5,14.56],[44.04,45.16],[106.74,107.8],[120.42,123.7],[126.06,127.94],[133.34,136.26],[140.64,143.5],[150.4,153.36],[156.86,159.72],[164.68,168.26]],"dur":168.3},{"n":88,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le commissaire Périer mène l’enquête (suite)","titleEn":"Superintendent Périer leads the investigation (continued)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Qui qu’il soit, où qu’il se cache, et quoi qu’il fasse pour s’échapper,","en":"Whoever he is, wherever he’s hiding, and whatever he does to escape,","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"on le retrouvera, ce flingueur, quelle que soit sa vraie identité.","en":"we will find him, this gunman, whatever his true identity.","lit":"(is)"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Il fera sans doute une gaffe. Et, là, je lui mettrai personnellement trois balles dans le corps.","en":"He’s bound to make a mistake. And then I will personally put three bullets in his body.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous êtes avec moi, les gars ? Je ne veux pas faire le sale boulot tout seul.","en":"Are you with me, guys? I don’t want to do the dirty work alone.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Carrément ! Mais Gaëtan a quelque chose à nous dire, il me semble.","en":"Too right! But it seems to me that Gaëtan has something to tell us.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est exact. Un mec vient me voir hier et me dit qu’il a un tuyau sur l’assassinat d’Antoine.","en":"That’s right. A guy came to see me yesterday and told me he had a tip-off about Antoine’s murder.","lit":"comes ... tells ... pipe"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Je crois qu’il bosse pour une boîte plus ou moins réglo qui revend des bagnoles d’occasion.","en":"I think he works for a more or less straight-up company that sells used cars.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je lui demande combien il veut et il me répond qu’il va me faire une fleur,","en":"I asked him how much he wanted and he told me he’s going to do me a favour,","lit":"ask ... wants ... answers"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"qu’il ne veut pas de fric parce qu’Antoine était un de ses potes.","en":"that he didn’t want any dosh because Antoine was one of his buddies.","lit":"doesn’t want"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"D’après lui, les services secrets ont démasqué notre homme et découvert son secret.","en":"According to him, the secret service unmasked our man and discovered his secret.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Il était fichu, le pauvre Antoine. Il ne portait même pas de flingue pour se défendre.","en":"He was a goner, poor Antoine. He didn’t even carry a gun to defend himself.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Ce type avec qui tu as causé, il était comment ?","en":"The guy you chatted to, what was he like?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Grand, costaud et mal fringué, pas très propre, avec une vraie gueule de bandit.","en":"Tall, strong and badly dressed, not very clean, with a real gangster’s face.","lit":"bandit"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça se voyait qu’il n’était pas un enfant de chœur, si tu vois ce que je veux dire.","en":"You could tell he was no altar boy, if you see what I mean.","lit":"that saw itself ... child of choir"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est bizarre, tu ne trouves pas ? Pourquoi il n’est pas allé voir les flics directement ?","en":"It’s weird, don’t you think? Why didn’t he go straight to the cops?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis d’accord que c’est une histoire dingue, et ce type est carrément louche,","en":"I agree that it’s a crazy story, and this guy’s totally shady,","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"mais ça ne veut pas dire pour autant qu’il raconte des salades.","en":"yet that doesn’t mean he’s spinning a yarn.","lit":"for as much ... telling salads"},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est clair, et c’est le seul renseignement plus ou moins fiable que nous ayons reçu jusqu’à présent,","en":"That’s clear, and it’s the only more or less reliable information that we’ve received so far,","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"la meilleure piste que nous puissions suivre. Mais aussi, peut-être, le plus gros mensonge qui soit.","en":"the best lead we can follow. But also, perhaps, the biggest lie there is.","lit":"(that) there be"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"N’empêche, il faut y aller carrément, coûte que coûte. La tâche est difficile, mais je m’en fiche.","en":"Still, we’ve got to go all the way, no matter what. The task is difficult, but I don’t care.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"J’en ferai mon affaire, patron. Je suis l’unique personne qui connaisse notre indic.","en":"I’ll make it my business, boss. I’m the only person who knows our informer.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Si tu insistes, mais sois prudent. Il ne faut pas que tu boives une infusion avec lui !","en":"If you insist, but be careful. You mustn’t drink herbal tea with him!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"Certain adverbs are always followed by the subjunctive. We have already seen quoi que (lesson 81 note 7); others include qui que, whoever, où que, wherever, and quel que, whatever (the latter agreeing in number and gender with the following noun)."},{"ref":"2","text":"This lesson is based around a number of common slang terms, which are explained in detail in lesson 91. For now, just read the translation of the dialogue, bearing in mind that many colloquial terms in French have no direct equivalents in English, and vice versa."},{"ref":"3","text":"un gars is a familiar word, derived from un garçon, and is sometimes used like a lad. But the most common usage is as a synonym of un type, a guy (lesson 87 note 9), especially in the plural: Venez voir, les gars, Hey guys, come and look. A synonym is un mec: Ce mec n’a jamais lu un bouquin de sa vie, That guy’s never read a book in his life. (Note how the use of the familiar un bouquin, seen in lesson 47 note 10, reinforces the familiar register of the sentence.) Un mec can also be used in the sense of boyfriend: Elle a quitté son mec, She’s left her man."},{"ref":"4","text":"Derived from un carré, a square, the adverb carrément means candidly, bluntly, etc.: Je lui ai dit carrément ce que je pense de lui, I told him straight out what I thought of him. The word is widely used as an intensifier: Ce gars est carrément dingue, That guy’s totally nuts. (The “standard” word would be complètement, completely.) Lastly, carrément can be used as an assertion expressing agreement or support: Tu aimes ce chanteur ? - Carrément ! Do you like that singer? - Totally!"},{"ref":"5","text":"une fleur, a flower (seen in lesson 41 line 1), is used in several familiar expressions where, as in English, it embodies something prime or excellent. Les deux comédiens sont dans la fleur de l’âge, The two actors are in the prime of life. Thus faire une fleur (à quelqu’un) means to do someone a good turn or favour. L’hôtel nous a fait une fleur en nous donnant une suite au lieu d’une chambre double, The hotel did us a favour by giving us a suite instead of a double room."},{"ref":"6","text":"In a familiar register, ficher (which, in standard French, means to put on file) replaces standard verbs, particularly faire, meaning to do, to get up to, etc. Qu’est-ce qu’il fiche, Georges ? Il est en retard, What’s Georges up to? He’s late. The pronominal form, s’en fiche(r) (line 20), is usually found in expressions such as Je m’en fiche, I don’t care. But the past participle, fichu, used with être, means to be finished, done for, etc. Si les joueurs entrent sur le terrain avec la peur au ventre, ils sont fichus, If the players are afraid when they take to the field, they’re done for."},{"ref":"7","text":"The familiar adjective dingue (see lesson 73 note 6) means crazy, and, as in English, can be both meliorative - On a passé une soirée dingue chez eux, We had a crazy evening at their house - and pejorative: Cette assurance nous coûte un fric dingue, That insurance is costing us a stupid amount of money. The noun un dingue means a nutcase."},{"ref":"8","text":"The subjunctive is used in relative clauses with superlatives (le plus gros, le meilleur) and other expressions of uniqueness (le seul, l’unique). For example, C’est l’hiver le plus froid qu’on ait connu depuis longtemps, It’s the coldest winter we have known in a while; or Tu es la seule personne qui puisse m’aider, You’re the only person who can help me."},{"ref":"9","text":"The imperative form of être is identical to the subjunctive. Il faut que vous soyez / tu sois là à midi, You must be there at noon; Soyez / Sois là à midi, Be there at noon."},{"ref":"","text":"French uses the present tense for narrative and descriptive purposes when talking about past events, as Gaëtan’s story shows. Known as le présent historique, or historical present, this usage is not confined to spoken or colloquial contexts, as is often the case in English (though both languages use the tense in news headlines). One of the reasons for the historical present - apart from bringing past events vividly to life - is to avoid le passé simple (also known as le prétérit), which is used almost exclusively in written prose, particularly literature. We will look briefly at the passé simple in the last set of lessons."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Êtes-vous avec moi, mes amis ? Je ne peux pas faire ce travail difficile tout seul.","a":"Are you with me, my friends? I can’t do this tough job on my own."},{"q":"Il me semble que ce monsieur travaille dans une entreprise qui fait le commerce de voitures d’occasion.","a":"It seems to me that this gentleman works in a company dealing in used cars."},{"q":"À quoi ressemblait cet homme avec qui vous avez eu une conversation ?","a":"What did the man you had a conversation with look like?"},{"q":"On lui a dit carrément de quitter son petit-ami parce qu’il était méchant avec elle, mais elle s’en fiche.","a":"We told her straight out to leave her boyfriend because he was being mean to her, but she didn’t care."},{"q":"Il faut que vous soyez prêt à terminer la tâche, coûte que coûte, car il reste peu de temps.","a":"You have to be ready to finish the task at all costs, because there isn’t much time left."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You mustn’t drink any alcohol. If not, you’ll have a big problem with the cops.","t":"Il {{ne faut pas que vous buviez}} d’alcool. Sinon, {{vous aurez un gros problème}} avec {{les flics}}."},{"cue":"Whoever they are and wherever they live, I don’t care. They’re spinning a yarn, I’m sure of it.","t":"{{Qui qu’ils soient}}, et {{où qu’ils habitent, je m’en fiche}}. Ils {{racontent des salades}}, j’en suis sûr."},{"cue":"That’s right. I asked him where they lived, but he didn’t answer. – That’s crazy!","t":"{{C’est exact}}. Je {{lui ai demandé où}} ils {{habitaient}}, mais il {{ne m’a pas répondu}}. – C’est {{dingue}} !"},{"cue":"Whatever you may think, I’m the only person who knows the whole truth and who knows their true identity.","t":"{{Quoi que}} vous {{pensiez}}, je suis {{la seule personne qui sache}} toute la {{vérité}} et {{qui connaisse}} leur vraie identité."},{"cue":"It’s obvious that they’re no angels, if you get our meaning.","t":"{{Ça se voit}} que ce {{ne sont pas des enfants de chœur}}, si {{vous voyez ce que}} nous {{voulons dire}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Qui qu’il soit, où qu’il se cache","en":"Whoever he is, wherever he hides","zh":"不管他是谁，不管他躲在哪里","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qui qu’il soit, où qu’il se cache, et quoi qu’il fasse pour s’échapper,","exEn":"Whoever he is, wherever he’s hiding, and whatever he does to escape,","src":1},{"fr":"Vous êtes avec moi, les gars ?","en":"Are you with me, guys?","zh":"伙计们，你们跟我一起吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous êtes avec moi, les gars ? Je ne veux pas faire le sale boulot tout seul.","exEn":"Are you with me, guys? I don’t want to do the dirty work alone.","src":4},{"fr":"faire le sale boulot","en":"to do the dirty work","zh":"干脏活累活","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous êtes avec moi, les gars ? Je ne veux pas faire le sale boulot tout seul.","exEn":"Are you with me, guys? I don’t want to do the dirty work alone.","src":4},{"fr":"Carrément !","en":"Totally! / Too right!","zh":"当然！完全同意！","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"carrément is also “bluntly, straight out”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Carrément ! Mais Gaëtan a quelque chose à nous dire, il me semble.","exEn":"Too right! But it seems to me that Gaëtan has something to tell us.","src":5},{"fr":"un mec","en":"a guy (familiar)","zh":"家伙，男人（口语）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est exact. Un mec vient me voir hier et me dit qu’il a un tuyau sur l’assassinat d’Antoine.","exEn":"That’s right. A guy came to see me yesterday and told me he had a tip-off about Antoine’s murder.","src":6},{"fr":"avoir un tuyau sur l’assassinat","en":"to have a tip-off about the murder","zh":"得到有关谋杀案的内幕消息","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un tuyau = a pipe, and (slang) a tip","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “twee-yo”: you hear a tweet through a pipe — a secret tip","ex":"C’est exact. Un mec vient me voir hier et me dit qu’il a un tuyau sur l’assassinat d’Antoine.","exEn":"That’s right. A guy came to see me yesterday and told me he had a tip-off about Antoine’s murder.","src":6},{"fr":"il va me faire une fleur","en":"he’s going to do me a favour","zh":"他要帮我一个忙","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je lui demande combien il veut et il me répond qu’il va me faire une fleur,","exEn":"I asked him how much he wanted and he told me he’s going to do me a favour,","src":8},{"fr":"Il ne veut pas de fric.","en":"He doesn’t want any dosh.","zh":"他不要钱（口语）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"sounds like “freak”: a freak waving a fat wad of cash","ex":"qu’il ne veut pas de fric parce qu’Antoine était un de ses potes.","exEn":"that he didn’t want any dosh because Antoine was one of his buddies.","src":9},{"fr":"mal fringué","en":"badly dressed","zh":"穿得很糟","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"Grand, costaud et mal fringué, pas très propre, avec une vraie gueule de bandit.","exEn":"Tall, strong and badly dressed, not very clean, with a real gangster’s face.","src":13},{"fr":"Pourquoi il n’est pas allé voir les flics ?","en":"Why didn’t he go to the cops?","zh":"他为什么不去找警察？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est bizarre, tu ne trouves pas ? Pourquoi il n’est pas allé voir les flics directement ?","exEn":"It’s weird, don’t you think? Why didn’t he go straight to the cops?","src":15},{"fr":"une histoire dingue","en":"a crazy story","zh":"一个疯狂的故事","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis d’accord que c’est une histoire dingue, et ce type est carrément louche,","exEn":"I agree that it’s a crazy story, and this guy’s totally shady,","src":16},{"fr":"raconter des salades","en":"to spin a yarn, to tell tall tales","zh":"胡扯，编瞎话","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais ça ne veut pas dire pour autant qu’il raconte des salades.","exEn":"yet that doesn’t mean he’s spinning a yarn.","src":17},{"fr":"coûte que coûte","en":"at all costs, no matter what","zh":"不惜一切代价","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’empêche, il faut y aller carrément, coûte que coûte. La tâche est difficile, mais je m’en fiche.","exEn":"Still, we’ve got to go all the way, no matter what. The task is difficult, but I don’t care.","src":20},{"fr":"Je m’en fiche.","en":"I don’t care.","zh":"我不在乎","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’empêche, il faut y aller carrément, coûte que coûte. La tâche est difficile, mais je m’en fiche.","exEn":"Still, we’ve got to go all the way, no matter what. The task is difficult, but I don’t care.","src":20},{"fr":"Il ne faut pas que tu boives une infusion avec lui !","en":"You mustn’t drink herbal tea with him!","zh":"你不可以跟他一起喝花草茶！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"drink","kw":"","ex":"Si tu insistes, mais sois prudent. Il ne faut pas que tu boives une infusion avec lui !","exEn":"If you insist, but be careful. You mustn’t drink herbal tea with him!","src":22}],"uncertain":["dialogue 14, 17 literal glosses reconstructed from garbled italics","dialogue 2 lit ‘(is)’ is a guess at the italic gloss"],"pdfPage":547,"gaps":[[1.74,3.42],[6.86,9.78],[17.46,18.46],[22.86,23.92],[98.2,99.42],[114.56,118.08],[120,123],[126.76,129.96],[134.58,137.6],[141.46,144.44],[149.3,152.44],[156.94,160.88]],"dur":160.9},{"n":89,"kind":"lesson","title":"Il faut le faire !","titleEn":"That takes some doing!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Oh là là, pourquoi est-ce que tu fais cette tête ? Qu’est-ce qu’il t’arrive, ma pauvre amie ?","en":"Oh dear, why are you pulling that long face? What’s happened to you, my poor friend?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je devais partir hier aux sports d’hiver avec une bande de copains.","en":"I was supposed to leave for winter sports yesterday with a bunch of friends.","lit":"band"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"On allait faire la fête – et peut-être un peu de sport – dans un super chalet au pied des pistes.","en":"We were going to party – and maybe do a bit of sport – in a great chalet at the foot of the slopes.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"J’avais fait les valises, préparé mes affaires de ski et commandé un taxi pour cinq heures –","en":"I had packed my bags, prepared my ski gear and ordered a taxi for 5 a.m. –","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"il n’est pas question de faire la grasse matinée quand on est en vacances à la neige !","en":"it’s out of the question to lie in when you’re on a winter holiday!","lit":"snow"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je quittais la maison quand j’ai reçu un message du bureau disant que ma présence était demandée.","en":"I was leaving the house when I received a message from the office saying that my presence was requested.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"J’avais fait le point avec mes collègues la veille, mais ils m’ont quand même fait venir aux aurores.","en":"I had updated my colleagues the day before, but they still made me come in at the crack of dawn.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Du coup, j’ai dû faire une croix sur les vacances. Voilà pourquoi je fais la tête.","en":"As a result, I had to forgo the holidays. That’s why I’m pulling a long face.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais à vrai dire, ça me soulage un petit peu aussi car je devais partir avec Gaspard et Noé.","en":"But to tell the truth, I’m a little bit relieved, too, because I was supposed to leave with Gaspard and Noé.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Partir avec ces deux-là, ensemble ? Il faut le faire !","en":"Leaving with those two, together? That’s quite something!","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Gaspard s’est fait connaître pour son esprit de compétition bien développé.","en":"Gaspard made himself known for his well-developed competitive spirit.","lit":"of competition"},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Pour ne pas dire esprit agressif ! Quand il est en compétition, celui-là, il ne fait pas de cadeau !","en":"Not to say aggressive spirit! When he’s in competition, he gives people a tough time!","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"D’ailleurs, il s’est fait un certain nombre d’ennemis à cause de ça.","en":"Moreover, he’s made a number of enemies because of that.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais il sait aussi se faire respecter. Gaspard est ami avec toutes les stars du sport, à qui il fait la bise.","en":"But he also knows how to make people respect him. Gaspard is friends with all the sports stars, with whom he’s on kissing terms.","lit":"he makes the kiss"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment se fait-il qu’il soit devenu un si bon skieur ? Il a dû se faire aider par quelqu’un, non ?","en":"How is it that he’s become such a good skier? He must have had help from someone, mustn’t he?","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, il s’est fait tout seul, sans entraîneur, sans équipe et sans sponsor.","en":"No, he’s self-made, without a coach, without a team and without a sponsor.","lit":"made himself"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Et Noé ?","en":"What about Noé?","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Il me fait de la peine, celui-là : c’est un désastre ambulant.","en":"I feel sorry for him: he’s a walking disaster.","lit":"he to-me makes some sadness"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"On lui a souvent dit de faire gaffe mais il n’en fait qu’à sa tête.","en":"We’ve often told him to be careful but he does just as he pleases.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"L’année dernière, il n’a fait que quelques mètres avant de tomber et se fracturer les deux bras.","en":"Last year, he only went a few metres before he fell and broke both his arms.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Il a dû se faire secourir par hélicoptère.","en":"He had to be rescued by helicopter.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Cela dit, il a visiblement fait des progrès car l’année d’avant, il s’était cassé la cheville en descendant du train à la station de ski.","en":"Having said that, he has obviously made progress because the year before, he had broken his ankle getting off the train at the ski resort.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The idiomatic expression faire la tête means to sulk, pull a long face, etc. Pourquoi est-ce qu’il me fait la tête ? Je ne lui ai rien fait ! Why’s he sulking? I haven’t done anything to him! You can also use an indirect object: Pourquoi est-ce que tu me fais la tête ? Why are you pulling that face at me? See also note 10."},{"ref":"2","text":"Derived from le gras, fat, blubber, etc. (and the origin of grease), the adjective gras(se), greasy, means fatty (and, by extension, fatty foods): Évitez de manger gras avant une activité physique, Avoid eating fatty foods before physical activity. The expression faire la grasse matinée means to lie in, to sleep late: J’aime faire la grasse matinée le dimanche, I love lying in on Sundays."},{"ref":"3","text":"faire un/le point is a navigational term meaning to take a precise bearing. That notion of precision is echoed in the expression’s colloquial usage, where the basic meaning is to review or sum up a situation, to bring someone up to date, etc. Nous allons faire un point sur les orages dans le Midi, We’re going to update you on the storms in the south of France. Logically, the idiom is often used in connection with news coverage."},{"ref":"4","text":"une croix, a cross, is found in the idiomatic expression faire une croix sur, meaning to forgo, write off, say goodbye to, etc. (like a valedictory sign of the cross): On devra faire une croix sur les vacances à la montagne cette année, We’ll have to forgo our mountain holiday this year. If the object noun has already been mentioned, it is replaced by dessus, on it, to avoid repetition: Si tu lui prêtes ta moto, tu peux faire une croix dessus, If you lend him your motorbike, you can kiss it goodbye."},{"ref":"5","text":"Il faut le faire literally means It has to be done: Si vous voulez le faire, il faut le faire maintenant, If you want to do it, you’ve got to do it now. Used as an exclamation, however, the meaning is something like That takes some doing / beating! It can be admiring or ironic, depending on the context: Il a perdu non seulement ses clés mais aussi son portefeuille : il faut le faire !, He’s lost not only his keys but also his wallet: that’s quite an achievement. (In spoken French, the third-person pronoun is often dropped: Faut le faire !)"},{"ref":"6","text":"This “causative” form of the reflexive, se faire followed by an infinitive (lesson 82 line 17), tells us that the subject of the sentence is acted upon by something or someone. As often with the reflexive, the equivalent structure in English uses got: Il s’est fait aider par un journaliste pour écrire l’article, He got help from a journalist to write the article."},{"ref":"7","text":"faire cadeau de means to give something away, often as a present: Mon copain m’a fait cadeau de cette ceinture, My boyfriend gave me this belt. In the negative, the expression is used figuratively with the meaning not to spare someone or to let off lightly. L’équipe n’a pas fait de cadeau à ses adversaires, The team gave their opponents a hard time."},{"ref":"8","text":"This is the inverted - hence, more elegant - form of comment ça se fait … ? (lesson 57 note 1). Here, however, the following verb is in the subjunctive because the speaker is expressing doubt or uncertainty."},{"ref":"9","text":"The fishery term une gaffe, a gaff or hook, is used in the expression faire une gaffe, to make a blunder (lesson 88 line 3). However, faire gaffe (without a definite article) means to pay attention to or be wary of: Il faut faire gaffe parce que l’étagère est très lourde, We have to be careful because the shelf is very heavy. The verb is more commonly used as an imperative: Faites / Fais gaffe : c’est vachement lourd ! Be careful, it’s damn heavy. Whatever the context, the expression is familiar, replacing the more formal faire attention (lesson 44 line 8)."},{"ref":"10","text":"The idiomatic expression n’en faire qu’à sa tête has nothing to do with sulking (note 1): it means to do exactly as one pleases / wants (listening only to what’s in one’s head, presumably): Quoi qu’on lui dise, elle n’en fera qu’à sa tête, Whatever we say to her, she’ll do exactly as she wants."},{"ref":"","text":"Among the cultural specificities of the French lifestyle, la bise holds a special place. The term faire la bise literally means to give (“make”) a kiss (and, by extension, to be on familiar terms with someone), and refers to the habit of greeting friends with a peck on both cheeks. Just how many pecks? The number varies between two (possibly the most common) and four, but it all depends on the relationship with the person, their age, and even the region of France."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"La vie ne leur a pas fait de cadeau, mais ils ont quand même réussi brillamment.","a":"Life hasn’t been kind to them, but they have still succeeded brilliantly."},{"q":"Noé n’a pas gagné le concours cette fois-ci, mais il n’a certainement pas fait une croix dessus.","a":"Noé didn’t win the contest this time, but he certainly hasn’t given up on it."},{"q":"Gaspard et Sylvie me font de la peine parce qu’ils se disputent constamment.","a":"I feel sorry for Gaspard and Sylvie because they’re constantly arguing."},{"q":"Rémi aurait pu se faire aider par la personne de son choix, mais il n’en a fait qu’à sa tête.","a":"Rémi could have had the help of the person of his choosing, but he did as he pleased."},{"q":"Fais gaffe où tu mets tes pieds : tout est sale et gras.","a":"Watch where you put your feet: everything is dirty and greasy."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"How come he has managed to get himself liked? He’s made too many enemies during his career.","t":"Comment {{se fait-il}} qu’il {{ait pu se faire aimer}} ? Il {{s’est fait trop d’ennemis}} dans sa carrière."},{"cue":"We’re going to get an update on the situation to give us an idea of food requirements.","t":"Nous {{allons faire le point sur}} la situation pour {{nous faire une idée}} des besoins en nourriture."},{"cue":"Why am I sulking? Because we were supposed to go skiing with our friends, but they have just cancelled.","t":"Pourquoi est-ce {{que je fais cette tête}} ? Parce {{que nous devions partir}} au ski avec nos amis, et ils {{viennent d’annuler}}."},{"cue":"Maurice must have had help to succeed like that. – No, you’re making a mistake: he’s self-made.","t":"Maurice {{a dû avoir}} de l’aide pour {{réussir comme ça}}. – Tu {{te trompes}} : il {{s’est fait tout seul}}."},{"cue":"I’ll allow you to have a lie-in on Saturday morning but we’ll have to get up at the crack of dawn on Sunday.","t":"Je te permettrai {{de faire la grasse matinée}} samedi mais il faudra {{se lever aux aurores}} dimanche."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Pourquoi est-ce que tu fais cette tête ?","en":"Why are you pulling that long face?","zh":"你为什么拉着个脸？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Oh là là, pourquoi est-ce que tu fais cette tête ? Qu’est-ce qu’il t’arrive, ma pauvre amie ?","exEn":"Oh dear, why are you pulling that long face? What’s happened to you, my poor friend?","src":1},{"fr":"il n’est pas question de faire la grasse matinée","en":"there’s no question of lying in","zh":"绝对不能睡懒觉","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"il n’est pas question de faire la grasse matinée quand on est en vacances à la neige !","exEn":"it’s out of the question to lie in when you’re on a winter holiday!","src":5},{"fr":"J’avais fait le point avec mes collègues.","en":"I had updated my colleagues.","zh":"我已经和同事们碰过头、通报过情况了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’avais fait le point avec mes collègues la veille, mais ils m’ont quand même fait venir aux aurores.","exEn":"I had updated my colleagues the day before, but they still made me come in at the crack of dawn.","src":7},{"fr":"venir aux aurores","en":"to come in at the crack of dawn","zh":"天一亮就得赶来","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “oh-zo-ror”: “oh, so early!” — an aurora is the dawn light","ex":"J’avais fait le point avec mes collègues la veille, mais ils m’ont quand même fait venir aux aurores.","exEn":"I had updated my colleagues the day before, but they still made me come in at the crack of dawn.","src":7},{"fr":"j’ai dû faire une croix sur les vacances","en":"I had to forgo the holidays","zh":"我不得不放弃度假","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Du coup, j’ai dû faire une croix sur les vacances. Voilà pourquoi je fais la tête.","exEn":"As a result, I had to forgo the holidays. That’s why I’m pulling a long face.","src":8},{"fr":"Il faut le faire !","en":"That takes some doing! / That’s quite something!","zh":"这可不容易啊！（真有你的！）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Partir avec ces deux-là, ensemble ? Il faut le faire !","exEn":"Leaving with those two, together? That’s quite something!","src":10},{"fr":"il ne fait pas de cadeau","en":"he gives no quarter, he doesn’t go easy","zh":"他绝不手下留情","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour ne pas dire esprit agressif ! Quand il est en compétition, celui-là, il ne fait pas de cadeau !","exEn":"Not to say aggressive spirit! When he’s in competition, he gives people a tough time!","src":12},{"fr":"faire la bise","en":"to kiss on the cheeks (in greeting)","zh":"贴面吻（打招呼）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais il sait aussi se faire respecter. Gaspard est ami avec toutes les stars du sport, à qui il fait la bise.","exEn":"But he also knows how to make people respect him. Gaspard is friends with all the sports stars, with whom he’s on kissing terms.","src":14},{"fr":"Comment se fait-il qu’il soit devenu un si bon skieur ?","en":"How is it that he’s become such a good skier?","zh":"他怎么会变成这么棒的滑雪者？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment se fait-il qu’il soit devenu un si bon skieur ? Il a dû se faire aider par quelqu’un, non ?","exEn":"How is it that he’s become such a good skier? He must have had help from someone, mustn’t he?","src":15},{"fr":"il s’est fait tout seul","en":"he’s self-made","zh":"他是白手起家、自己闯出来的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, il s’est fait tout seul, sans entraîneur, sans équipe et sans sponsor.","exEn":"No, he’s self-made, without a coach, without a team and without a sponsor.","src":16},{"fr":"Il me fait de la peine.","en":"I feel sorry for him.","zh":"我为他难过/他让我心疼","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Il me fait de la peine, celui-là : c’est un désastre ambulant.","exEn":"I feel sorry for him: he’s a walking disaster.","src":18},{"fr":"faire gaffe","en":"to be careful (familiar)","zh":"小心（口语）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On lui a souvent dit de faire gaffe mais il n’en fait qu’à sa tête.","exEn":"We’ve often told him to be careful but he does just as he pleases.","src":19},{"fr":"n’en faire qu’à sa tête","en":"to do just as one pleases","zh":"一意孤行，我行我素","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On lui a souvent dit de faire gaffe mais il n’en fait qu’à sa tête.","exEn":"We’ve often told him to be careful but he does just as he pleases.","src":19},{"fr":"se faire secourir par hélicoptère","en":"to be rescued by helicopter","zh":"被直升机救援","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ helicopter","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"Il a dû se faire secourir par hélicoptère.","exEn":"He had to be rescued by helicopter.","src":21},{"fr":"Cela dit, il a fait des progrès.","en":"Having said that, he has made progress.","zh":"话虽如此，他确实有进步","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Cela dit, il a visiblement fait des progrès car l’année d’avant, il s’était cassé la cheville en descendant du train à la station de ski.","exEn":"Having said that, he has obviously made progress because the year before, he had broken his ankle getting off the train at the ski resort.","src":22}],"uncertain":["dialogue 5: English reconstructed from a partly garbled line","culture box (la bise): placed here because it follows the lesson 89 answers","historical-present box printed after lesson 88 answers; included in lesson 88"],"pdfPage":555,"gaps":[[2,4.02],[4.98,7.8],[27.02,28.02],[31.16,32.28],[42.7,43.72],[48.34,49.42],[114.16,117.62],[119.6,121.66],[126.28,129.1],[133.4,136.54],[140.16,143.22],[147.72,150.56],[153.32,157.18]],"dur":157.2},{"n":90,"kind":"lesson","title":"« Le » ou « la » ? – Les deux !","titleEn":"“Le” or “la”? – Both!","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous n’ignorez certainement pas qu’il peut être compliqué d’apprendre le genre des noms,","en":"You’re certainly aware that it can be complicated to learn the genders of nouns,","lit":"you don’t ignore"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"surtout ceux qui peuvent être et masculin et féminin.","en":"especially those that can be both male and female.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Du coup, j’ai préparé un petit jeu pour vous aider, si ça vous convient.","en":"So I’ve prepared a little game to help you, if you like.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça me va. Mais cela ne veut pas dire pour autant que je retiendrai tout !","en":"That’s fine by me. But that doesn’t mean that I’ll remember everything!","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Allons-y. La critique du film faite par le critique du Monde n’était pas tendre. Elle était assez méchante !","en":"Let’s go. The film review by the critic of Le Monde was not very nice. It was quite mean!","lit":"tender"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Quand Bruno a appris que le tableau était un faux, il était tellement furieux qu’il l’a déchiqueté avec une faux.","en":"When Bruno learned that the painting was a fake, he was so furious that he shredded it with a scythe.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Tout le monde a entendu parler du Tour de France et de la Tour Eiffel, même ceux qui ne sont jamais venus dans l’Hexagone.","en":"Everyone has heard about the Tour de France and the Eiffel Tower, even those who have never been to mainland France.","lit":"the hexagon"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Les experts ont annoncé une vague de chaleur cet été, sans préciser quand. Comme d’habitude, ils sont restés dans le vague.","en":"The experts have announced a heat wave this summer, without specifying when. As usual, they remained vague.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Mélangez un peu de cette vase avec de l’eau du ruisseau et mettez-la dans un vase avec vos plantes.","en":"Mix a little of this mud with some stream water and put it in a vase with your plants.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Ayant volé une somme importante d’argent, le voleur a fait un petit somme dans sa voiture. C’est comme ça qu’il a été arrêté !","en":"Having stolen a large sum of money, the thief took a nap in his car. That’s how he was arrested!","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai appris ce matin que son frère a un nouveau poste. Il travaille maintenant à la poste du village.","en":"I learned this morning that his brother has a new job. He now works at the village post office.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai abîmé ma nouvelle poêle à frire en la posant sur un poêle trop chaud.","en":"I damaged my new frying pan when I put it on a stove that was too hot.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Aussitôt son mémoire terminé, l’étudiante a tout oublié. Sa mémoire lui a fait défaut.","en":"As soon as she finished her dissertation, the student forgot everything. Her memory failed her.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"D’après ce livre de cuisine, il faut une livre de haricots verts et une livre et demie de petits pois.","en":"According to this cookbook, you need one pound of green beans and one and a half pounds of peas.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Tu t’y prends comme un manche ! Tu as enfilé le pied dans la manche de ta chemise.","en":"You’re making a mess of things! You’ve put your foot in the sleeve of your shirt.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Suivre la mode peut s’avérer très fatigant. Ce n’est pas un mode de vie pour les vieux !","en":"Following fashion can prove to be very tiring. It’s not a way of life for old people!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Le voile de la mariée était tellement volumineux qu’on aurait dit une voile de bateau.","en":"The bride’s veil was so bulky that it looked like a boat sail.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour fabriquer ce petit bronze, le sculpteur a utilisé la coquille d’une moule comme un moule.","en":"To make this small bronze, the sculptor used the shell of a mussel as a mould.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Je voudrais un crème et un chocolat chaud, avec de la crème chantilly servie à part.","en":"I would like a white coffee and a hot chocolate, with whipped cream served on the side.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Le prof a un physique de jeune premier mais il est connu pour ses recherches portant sur la physique théorique.","en":"The professor has movie-star looks but he is known for his research on theoretical physics.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Merci, tout est très clair !","en":"Thank you, everything is very clear!","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai un petit quelque chose pour vous remercier, mais j’ai une chose à faire avant de vous le donner :","en":"I have a little something to thank you, but I have one thing to do before I give it to you:","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"vous embrasser et vous donner plein de bises.","en":"hug you and give you loads of kisses.","lit":""},{"i":24,"dash":true,"fr":"Quel délice… je veux dire : quelles délices !","en":"What a delight … I mean, what delights!","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"There is no difference in pronunciation between the masculine and feminine forms of the nouns reviewed in this lesson. Context aside, the only way to distinguish between them is through the preceding article. (14) The “h” in haricot is aspirated (in contrast to heure, for example), which is why the vowel in the definite and partitive articles is not elided: le / de / des haricots. Remember that, despite the name, an aspirated “h” is not pronounced."},{"ref":"1","text":"The regular verb ignorer is a faux-ami, meaning not to know or to be unaware of: J’ignore tout de sa vie, I know nothing about his/her life. Thus, in the negative, it means not to be unaware of: L’ex-président n’ignore pas comment il a perdu les élections, The former president is not unaware of how he lost the elections. However, the feminine noun l’ignorance means ignorance."},{"ref":"2","text":"The coordinating conjunction et … et is one of the constructions used to translate both … and (lesson 57 note 2): J’aime et les petits pois et les haricots, I like both peas and beans. In spoken French it is not as common as à la fois (lesson 61 note 7), probably because the open [ay] sound is harder to hear."},{"ref":"3","text":"Yet another use of un coup (lesson 62 note 3). The set phrase du coup, generally found at the beginning of a spoken sentence or clause, means as a result: Le match est annulé ; du coup, ils ne viennent pas samedi. The match has been cancelled. As a result, they’re not coming on Saturday."},{"ref":"4","text":"First seen in lesson 88 line 17, pour autant is the equivalent of the phrase for all that: Il a pris sa retraite, mais sa vie ne s’est pas arrêtée pour autant, He has retired but his life hasn’t stopped for all that. If the sentence contains a contrastive conjunction, such as mais, but, there is often no need to translate pour autant directly (He may have retired but his life hasn’t stopped)."},{"ref":"5","text":"See lesson 72 note 4. The past participle can be used without the auxiliary in elliptical clauses introduced by adverbs such as aussitôt: Aussitôt le dernier invité parti, nous avons éteint toutes les lumières, As soon as the last guest had left, we turned off all the lights. This type of construction is generally used in written rather than spoken French."},{"ref":"6","text":"We know that un défaut is a flaw (hence a defect, lesson 85 line 22) and is also used in the expression à défaut, failing that (lesson 79 line 16). The same notion of lack or defect crops up in the expression faire défaut, to be lacking, to be short of or failed by, etc. Le temps me fait défaut, I don’t have enough time. By extension, another meaning is to be let down by: C’est un bon joueur mais son manque de vitesse lui a fait défaut, He’s a good player but he was let down by his lack of speed."},{"ref":"7","text":"un manche means a handle or shaft (un manche à balai, a broomstick). The word can be used idiomatically to indicate clumsiness or inability: Tu es rentré dans un arbre ! Tu conduis comme un manche, You’ve hit a tree! You’re a useless driver. Thus s’y prendre comme un manche means to go about something badly, to make a mess of things."},{"ref":"8","text":"We know the verb marier (one “r”) and the past participle/adjective marié(e). The derived noun un(e) marié(e) means a groom / a bride. J’ai pris plein de photos de la mariée et du marié, I took loads of photos of the bride and groom. Don’t confuse these words with un mari (a husband) and une femme (a wife). (We have also learned the formal term un époux / une épouse, lesson 46 note 7.)"},{"ref":"9","text":"In the masculine un physique refers to physical appearance (or physique!), particularly in phrases like Elle a un physique agréable, She’s nice-looking. Un jeune premier is a romantic male lead in a movie, hence the expression avoir un physique de jeune premier, to have movie-star good looks."},{"ref":"10","text":"The indefinite pronoun quelque chose is occasionally used as a masculine noun in the expression un petit quelque chose, a little something (even if the thing in question is feminine): Cette comédienne a un petit quelque chose en plus, That actress has a little something extra."},{"ref":"11","text":"Every French schoolchild learns the three nouns that are masculine in the singular and feminine in the plural: un amour, a love; un délice, a delight; un orgue, an organ (instrument). The reasons are obscure and, in our case, any mistakes would be easily excused."},{"ref":"","text":"La France comprises not only the country on the mainland (Culture Note, lesson 61) but also a number of overseas territories and départements, known collectively as la France d’outre-mer. Because of its shape, the continental European part is often referred to, especially in the media, as l’Hexagone."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"L’accueil dans le restaurant était très froid. Du coup, on a décidé d’aller ailleurs.","a":"The welcome in the restaurant was very chilly. As a result, we decided to go elsewhere."},{"q":"Décidément, vous vous y prenez comme un manche ! Laissez-moi vous aider.","a":"You really are making a mess of things! Let me help you."},{"q":"Beaucoup de choses leur font défaut pour terminer le projet à temps mais ils n’abandonneront pas pour autant.","a":"There’s a lot they need to finish the project on time, but they won’t give up."},{"q":"Il me dit que la machine est équipée de plusieurs outils qui pourront s’avérer très utiles.","a":"He tells me that the machine is equipped with several tools that could prove very useful."},{"q":"J’ignore ce qui s’est passé, mais tous les matchs sont annulés.","a":"I don’t know what happened, but all the games have been cancelled."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"My memory failed me and I totally forgot her birthday. – You and I both know that it’s not serious.","t":"{{Ma mémoire m’a fait défaut}} et j’ai totalement {{oublié}} son {{anniversaire}}. – {{Et toi et moi savons}} que ce n’est pas grave."},{"cue":"The film critic wrote his review before the film came out. So he had to throw it in the wastepaper bin.","t":"{{Le critique}} de cinéma a {{écrit sa critique}} avant la sortie du film. {{Du coup}}, il a dû {{la jeter à la poubelle}}."},{"cue":"This director is known for his documentaries, notably those on the Tour de France and the Eiffel Tower.","t":"Ce {{metteur en scène est connu}} pour ses documentaires, {{notamment ceux}} sur {{le Tour}} de France et {{la Tour}} Eiffel."},{"cue":"You’re a dreadful driver. Take care not to damage the engine. Let’s go gently.","t":"Tu {{conduis comme un manche}}. Fais attention à {{ne pas abîmer}} le moteur ! {{Allons-y}} doucement."},{"cue":"As soon as the last guests had left, he turned off the light and had a nap.","t":"{{Aussitôt les derniers}} invités {{partis}}, il {{a éteint}} la lumière et fait {{un petit somme}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Vous n’ignorez certainement pas que…","en":"You are certainly aware that…","zh":"您当然知道……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"ignorer = to be unaware of, NOT “to ignore” (= ne pas tenir compte de)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous n’ignorez certainement pas qu’il peut être compliqué d’apprendre le genre des noms,","exEn":"You’re certainly aware that it can be complicated to learn the genders of nouns,","src":1},{"fr":"Du coup, j’ai préparé un petit jeu","en":"So / As a result, I’ve prepared a little game","zh":"所以，我准备了一个小游戏","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Du coup, j’ai préparé un petit jeu pour vous aider, si ça vous convient.","exEn":"So I’ve prepared a little game to help you, if you like.","src":3},{"fr":"Ça me va.","en":"That’s fine by me.","zh":"我没意见/这样挺好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça me va. Mais cela ne veut pas dire pour autant que je retiendrai tout !","exEn":"That’s fine by me. But that doesn’t mean that I’ll remember everything!","src":4},{"fr":"cela ne veut pas dire pour autant que…","en":"that doesn’t mean that… (for all that)","zh":"这并不意味着……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça me va. Mais cela ne veut pas dire pour autant que je retiendrai tout !","exEn":"That’s fine by me. But that doesn’t mean that I’ll remember everything!","src":4},{"fr":"une vague de chaleur","en":"a heat wave","zh":"热浪","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"weather","kw":"","ex":"Les experts ont annoncé une vague de chaleur cet été, sans préciser quand. Comme d’habitude, ils sont restés dans le vague.","exEn":"The experts have announced a heat wave this summer, without specifying when. As usual, they remained vague.","src":8},{"fr":"faire un petit somme","en":"to take a little nap","zh":"小睡一会儿","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un somme (m) = a nap; une somme (f) = a sum","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “sum”: you count sums until you drift off for a nap","ex":"Ayant volé une somme importante d’argent, le voleur a fait un petit somme dans sa voiture. C’est comme ça qu’il a été arrêté !","exEn":"Having stolen a large sum of money, the thief took a nap in his car. That’s how he was arrested!","src":10},{"fr":"un nouveau poste","en":"a new job / position","zh":"一个新职位","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"un poste = a job; la poste = the post office","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"J’ai appris ce matin que son frère a un nouveau poste. Il travaille maintenant à la poste du village.","exEn":"I learned this morning that his brother has a new job. He now works at the village post office.","src":11},{"fr":"une poêle à frire","en":"a frying pan","zh":"煎锅","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"une poêle = a pan; un poêle = a stove","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai abîmé ma nouvelle poêle à frire en la posant sur un poêle trop chaud.","exEn":"I damaged my new frying pan when I put it on a stove that was too hot.","src":12},{"fr":"Aussitôt son mémoire terminé","en":"As soon as her dissertation was finished","zh":"她的论文一完成","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un mémoire = a dissertation; la mémoire = memory","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aussitôt son mémoire terminé, l’étudiante a tout oublié. Sa mémoire lui a fait défaut.","exEn":"As soon as she finished her dissertation, the student forgot everything. Her memory failed her.","src":13},{"fr":"Sa mémoire lui a fait défaut.","en":"Her memory failed her.","zh":"她的记忆力出了问题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aussitôt son mémoire terminé, l’étudiante a tout oublié. Sa mémoire lui a fait défaut.","exEn":"As soon as she finished her dissertation, the student forgot everything. Her memory failed her.","src":13},{"fr":"une livre de haricots verts","en":"a pound of green beans","zh":"一磅四季豆","pos":"phrase","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"une livre (f) = a pound; un livre (m) = a book","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"D’après ce livre de cuisine, il faut une livre de haricots verts et une livre et demie de petits pois.","exEn":"According to this cookbook, you need one pound of green beans and one and a half pounds of peas.","src":14},{"fr":"Tu t’y prends comme un manche !","en":"You’re making a mess of it!","zh":"你做得一团糟！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “mahnsh”: a clumsy man swinging a broom handle (un manche) and hitting everything","ex":"Tu t’y prends comme un manche ! Tu as enfilé le pied dans la manche de ta chemise.","exEn":"You’re making a mess of things! You’ve put your foot in the sleeve of your shirt.","src":15},{"fr":"ce n’est pas un mode de vie","en":"it’s not a way of life","zh":"这不是一种生活方式","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"un mode = a way / manner; la mode = fashion","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Suivre la mode peut s’avérer très fatigant. Ce n’est pas un mode de vie pour les vieux !","exEn":"Following fashion can prove to be very tiring. It’s not a way of life for old people!","src":16},{"fr":"avec de la crème chantilly servie à part","en":"with whipped cream served on the side","zh":"另附鲜奶油","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"food","kw":"","ex":"Je voudrais un crème et un chocolat chaud, avec de la crème chantilly servie à part.","exEn":"I would like a white coffee and a hot chocolate, with whipped cream served on the side.","src":19},{"fr":"un petit quelque chose","en":"a little something","zh":"一点小心意","pos":"phrase","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai un petit quelque chose pour vous remercier, mais j’ai une chose à faire avant de vous le donner :","exEn":"I have a little something to thank you, but I have one thing to do before I give it to you:","src":22},{"fr":"Quel délice !","en":"What a delight!","zh":"真是美妙！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ delight","ff":"un délice is masculine in the singular but feminine in the plural (de délicieuses… quelles délices)","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quel délice… je veux dire : quelles délices !","exEn":"What a delight … I mean, what delights!","src":24}],"uncertain":["note 10: scan says ‘indefinite adjective’; ‘pronoun’ is the correct term and was used","ex2 item 5 prompt: derived from the answer list ‘a éteint, un petit somme’","ex1 item 3 English: ‘to finish’ cue taken from the answer"],"pdfPage":561,"gaps":[[1.94,3.7],[6.24,9.12],[16.9,18.24],[40.22,41.48],[56.42,57.58],[63.52,64.9],[72.12,73.52],[79.12,80.12],[83.64,84.72],[90.14,91.38],[96.86,98.22],[102.48,103.82],[108.12,109.44],[113.7,114.98],[119.66,121.04],[125.6,127.02],[132.34,133.48],[135.6,136.6],[148.1,152.08],[153.92,155.8],[161.42,164.22],[167.86,171.08],[175.86,178.96],[183.98,186.94],[189.9,194.14]],"dur":194.2},{"n":91,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est quoi votre problème ? Pourquoi vous faites cette tête ?","en":"What’s your problem? Why are you pulling that face?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Je suis de mauvaise humeur parce que mon assistant a démissionné il y a un mois.","en":"I’m in a bad mood because my assistant resigned a month ago.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Mes collègues et moi cherchons quelqu’un dont les compétences soient reconnues.","en":"My colleagues and I are looking for someone whose skills are recognised.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais ça s’avère bien plus compliqué qu’il n’y paraît. J’avais trois candidats, dont un seul était intéressant.","en":"But it’s proving much more complicated than it sounds. I had three applicants, only one of whom was interesting.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"J’ai voulu faire venir le type pour un entretien mais il avait déjà été embauché.","en":"I wanted to bring the guy in for an interview, but he had already been hired.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"J’avais une autre piste, un jeune dont m’a parlé un copain.","en":"I had another lead, a young guy that a friend told me about.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"C’était un drôle de type, assez sympa, mais j’avais l’impression qu’il n’en ferait qu’à sa tête si je le prenais.","en":"He was a strange guy, quite nice, but I had the impression that he would do whatever he wanted if I took him on.","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"De plus, l’expérience lui faisait défaut. Pouvez-vous m’aider ?","en":"Plus, he lacked experience. Can you help me?","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Je peux faire venir quelqu’un que je connais bien, le type le plus intelligent que je connaisse, qui saura analyser vos besoins.","en":"I can bring in someone I know well, the most intelligent guy I know, who will be able to analyse your needs.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça ne veut pas dire pour autant qu’on trouvera tout de suite la perle rare mais au moins c’est un bon début.","en":"That doesn’t mean we’ll find the one-in-a-million person right away but at least it’s a good start.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous en ferons notre affaire et nous ne lâcherons pas tant qu’on n’aura pas trouvé la bonne personne.","en":"We’ll make it our business and we won’t let go until we’ve found the right person.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Super ! Du coup, quand vous aurez trouvé quelqu’un, mes collègues feront la fête.","en":"Great! So, when you’ve found someone, my colleagues will celebrate.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 Formal and informal interrogatives","text":"We know that the first, raised-intonation interrogative (1-1) is customary in everyday and colloquial speech but is not usually written (except, for example, in a dialogue for a book or film script), while the second form (1-2, est-ce que) can be written – for example, in an email or text message – and spoken. The third, inverted interrogative, 1-3, is more formal than the other two constructions (lesson 21 et al.). Let’s revise the various forms and see in greater detail how they behave in different contexts.\nAs we can see in lesson 85, a common feature of 1-1 questions is to place the interrogative word at the end of the question, instead of at the beginning in 1-2 and 1-3:\n1-1 | 1-2 | 1-3 | English\nTu vas où ? | Où est-ce que tu vas ? | Où vas-tu ? | Where are you going?\nIl est parti quand ? | Quand est-ce qu’il est parti ? | Quand est-il parti ? | When did he leave?\nVous voulez combien ? | Combien est-ce que vous voulez ? | Combien voulez-vous ? | How much / How many do you want?\nIl a crié pourquoi ? | Pourquoi est-ce qu’il a crié ? | Pourquoi a-t-il crié ? | Why did he shout?\nElle a su comment ? | Comment est-ce qu’elle a su ? | Comment a-t-elle su ? | How did she know?\nIls veulent voir qui ? | Qui est-ce qu’ils veulent voir ? | Qui veulent-ils voir ? | Who(m) do they want to see?\nQue and quoi, what, behave slightly differently.\nTu penses quoi ? | Qu’est-ce que tu penses ? | Que penses-tu ? | What do you think / are you thinking?\nElles sont devenues quoi ? | Qu’est-ce qu’elles sont devenues ? | Que sont-elles devenues ? | What became of them?\nIf the pronoun is an indirect object, à qui, for example, the preposition comes first:\nTu parles à qui ? | À qui est-ce que tu parles ? | À qui parles-tu ? | Who are you speaking to?\nIl arrive d’où ? | D’où est-ce qu’il arrive ? | D’où arrive-t-il ? | Where’s he arriving from?\nLastly, if the subject of the sentence is not an unstressed pronoun (je, il/elle, etc.), it comes at the beginning of the question in 1-3.\nElle est là, Michelle ? | Est-ce que Michelle est là ? | Michelle est-elle là ? | Is Michelle there?\nChoosing which form to use depends, obviously, on the context and the person speaking or writing, as lesson 85 shows. It should also be remembered that not all native French speakers stick strictly to all the rules (for example, Quel âge elle a ? is often preferred across the board to the more formal Quel âge a-t-elle ?) but, fortunately or unfortunately, non-native speakers are often granted less latitude and must therefore abide by all the rules. It’s a case of Fais ce que je dis, pas ce que je fais, Do as I say, not as I do!"},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 The causative","text":"This construction is used where the subject causes the action of the verb to happen. Often rendered in English with get and a past participle, the causative is formed with faire, conjugated according to the subject, and an infinitive. A direct object can be added if necessary.\nIf, for example, a tourist knows a city, they will say Je connais cette ville, but if they then get other people to discover it, they might boast Je fais connaître cette ville, I’m getting this city known, i.e. I’m telling people all about it. Or if a culinary speciality, such as herbs, comes from a particular region, we say: Les herbes viennent de Provence, but if we order those herbs online and have them sent, we would say J’ai fait venir les herbes de Provence, I had the herbs sent (lit. come) from Provence.\nThe reflexive form of the causative describes an action performed on the subject. The formation rules are the same, except that faire is in the reflexive. The action in question can be either deliberate – Il s’est fait aider par un ami, He got help (lit. “made himself helped by”) from a friend – or not: Elle s’est fait mal, She hurt herself. (Here, fait does not agree with elle, as would usually be the case with a reflexive, because it is not an auxiliary but part of the causative faire mal, to hurt.)\nA good way to remember the difference between the non-deliberate and deliberate forms is Je me suis réveillé(e) à l’aube, I woke up at dawn, and Je me suis fait réveiller de bonne heure, I was woken up at dawn.\nIf a direct object is used, it comes after both parts of the verb:\nIl s’est fait couper les cheveux, He got a haircut.\n(Remember, the second verb is in the infinitive.) In some cases, the other verb may be faire, in which case it follows exactly the same rule:\nElle se fait faire une robe pour le mariage. She’s having a dress made for the wedding.\nWith both the non-reflexive and the reflexive causative form, the direct object may be replaced by a pronoun, which is placed before faire:\nJe fais venir les herbes ➔ Je les fais venir ;\nElle se fait faire une robe ➔ Elle se la fait faire.\nThis may all seem a little complicated at first, but the causative is actually more regular than the English equivalent, with its choice of have/get, etc. – or even a completely different verb:\nIl fait manger de la soupe à sa fille malade, He’s feeding/giving soup to his sick daughter (which sounds more natural than the literal “He’s having his sick daughter eat soup”)."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 The subjunctive: a few last details","text":"This last look at the subjunctive shows how it is used in specific instances. These include clauses containing expressions or constructions relating to superlative adjectives or descriptions of uniqueness, as well as indefinite expressions.\n3.1 With superlative adjectives\nThe subjunctive is used after a superlative asserting the unrivalled status (biggest, best, etc.) of the thing being referred to:\nL’hôtel possède la plus grande piscine que je connaisse. The hotel has the biggest swimming pool that I know.\nC’est le pays le plus chaud que nous ayons jamais visité. It’s the hottest country we have ever visited.\nEst-il vraiment le meilleur président qui ait jamais dirigé le pays ? Is he truly the best president who has ever run the country?\nIn these contexts, there can only be one superlative thing or person (pool, country, president). But, for example, we would say C’est le meilleur film que j’ai vu ce mois-ci, It’s the best film I’ve seen this month, i.e. I have been to the movies several times this month. At this level of language skill, using an indicative instead of a subjunctive is not a major mistake because, in many cases, perceptions of what is unrivalled are personal (particularly when it comes to the prowess of artists, politicians and the like).\n3.2 Descriptions of uniqueness\nIl est le seul qui puisse me comprendre. He’s the only one who can understand me.\nL’unique conseil que j’aie à vous donner est de ne pas démissionner. The only piece of advice I have to give is not to resign.\nIl n’y a personne qui en soit au courant. Nobody is aware of it.\n3.3 In indefinite expressions\nIn addition to being used after time conjunctions (lesson 84), the subjunctive must be used in indefinite expressions equivalent to the -ever adverbs in English:\nOù que tu ailles, je te suivrai. Wherever you go, I’ll follow you.\nQui qu’ils soient, les assassins seront retrouvés. Whoever they are, the murderers will be found.\nQuoi que vous pensiez de moi, je suis la personne de la situation. Whatever you think of me, I’m the person for the situation.\nQuel que soit leur avis, je n’en tiendrai pas compte. Whatever their opinion, I will disregard it.\nQuel que is the only one of these adverbs that agrees in number and gender with the noun it refers to (Quels que soient leurs conseils…). Be careful not to confuse it with quelque, some, a few, etc. Note that in literary French quelque can be used, followed by an adjective, in the sense of whatever:\nQuelque heureux qu’il puisse être, l’homme veut toujours plus. However happy he might be, man always wants more.\nHowever, this construction is rarely found in the spoken language.\nRemember that, in many cases, the subjunctive is not obligatory: the choice of tense depends on several other factors and the speaker’s perceptions. You now have the basic information you need to identify the subjunctive mood when you come across it and to understand why it is used.\nQue la force soit avec vous, May the force be with you."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 The do’s and don’ts of dont","text":"The relative pronoun dont, which you are familiar with by now, merits a final overview. It translates of which, of who(m) in relative clauses. Note how dont relates to the object, whether animate or inanimate, replacing the partitive de in the first sentence when the structure changes: Je suis fier de ce prix ➔ Le prix dont je suis fier. Je vous ai parlé de ce médecin ➔ Voici le médecin dont je vous ai parlé.\nIn English, the equivalent structure will depend on the degree of formality: The prize of which I’m proud or that/which I am proud of or, simply … I’m proud of. There are no such complications with dont, which, unlike the third example here, can never be omitted.\nDont also translates whose and, again, can refer to an animate or inanimate object:\nC’est la femme dont le fils vient d’être tué dans un accident de voiture. She’s the woman whose son was killed in a car accident.\nSami est le rappeur dont le dernier album s’est vendu par millions. Sami is the rapper whose last album sold millions.\nNote that dont always precedes the noun in the clause, unlike of which/of whom in formal English:\nSon album, dont les ventes ont plus que doublé, est produit par son frère. His album, sales of which have more than doubled, is produced by his brother.\n(Here too, modern English has a choice, even if purists disagree: His album, whose sales have more than …). Indeed, of which / of whom structures are often so formal that English finds other ways to deal with dont:\nLe train dont le pare-brise a été abîmé par l’orage est parti en retard de Lyon. The train with the storm-damaged windshield left Lyon late.\nIn this type of construction, usage is more important than rules.\nLastly, dont is used when singling out elements in a group:\nIls ont cinq gosses, dont deux filles. They have five kids, including two daughters.\nHere, too, the English construction can omit of whom/of which:\nTrois cafés, dont un avec lait, s’il vous plaît. Three coffees, one with milk please."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Le plus-que-parfait: The pluperfect tense","text":"The pluperfect is formed with the imperfect of avoir or être as an auxiliary (lesson 43 note 3) and the past participle of the verb:\nJ’avais envoyé la lettre, I had sent the letter.\nIl avait fini son repas, He had finished his meal.\nNous l’avions attendu, We had waited for him.\nVous étiez venu m’aider, You had come to help me.\nThere are no particular problems of usage because the plus-que-parfait is very similar to its English equivalent. However, while the past simple tense is occasionally used in English in compound sentences, the plus-que-parfait is obligatory:\nIl nous disait qu’il avait rencontré maman en Italie. He used to tell us that he met mum in Italy.\nThese clear and regular rules arguably make French a more “logical” language than English (inasmuch as any language is logical). As the saying goes:\nCe qui n’est pas clair n’est pas français, What is not clear is not French."},{"ref":"rev","title":"6 Slang","text":"For a non-native speaker, using slang can be tricky because it implies an intimate knowledge of the culture, mores and habits of a language community. While we recommend avoiding colloquialisms when interacting with French speakers, learners will inevitably encounter l’argot (slang) in the media, movies, music and, in all probability, in conversation with those same native speakers. Here is a selection of terms that might be found in books by writers like Céline, Alphonse Boudard or, more recently, Thierry Jonquet and Azouz Begag. The translations in the right-hand column are approximate equivalents.\nbagnole, caisse (f.) – car\nbalaise/balèze, costaud (adj.) – brawny, beefy\nbaraque (f.) – house\nbécane (f.) – scooter, motorbike\ncauser (vb) – talk\ndingue (m. and adj.) – crazy\nfichu (adj.) – done for\nflingue, flingueur (m.) – gun, gunman\nfric (m.), pognon (m.), blé (m.), thune (f.) – money\nfringues (f.), fringuer (vb) – clothes, to dress\ngars, mec, type (m.) – guy\nindic’ (m.) – informer\npinard (m.) – wine\npote (m.) – mate\nréglo (adj.) – above-board\ntifs (m. pl.) – hair\ntoubib (m.) – doctor\nTop ! / Nickel ! – Great!\nNo attempt has been made to translate these terms, firstly because there is not necessarily a direct equivalent in English. For example, une bagnole is a very common slang word for a car, which in some circumstances could possibly be translated as a ride, wheels, etc.; but in many cases, the word simply denotes that the speaker is using a familiar register. It is more important to recognise these words as slang and to know their meanings than to seek a perfect equivalent.\nIn recent decades, l’argot (sometimes called la langue verte, because it supposedly originated at green-covered gaming tables) has been enriched with words borrowed from other language communities – notably Arabic speakers – in francophone countries. Just as un toubib entered French many years ago, via the Arabic word for a healer, as a result of colonial expansion, so today we find kiffer, to like, love, “dig,” from kif, an Arabic word for hashish; or avoir le seum, to be angry, disgusted (seum meaning venom), and many, many others – some of which will prove ephemeral.\nThis evolution, evident to anyone who follows French and the culture of France, is part of the reason why the term “a living language” is a very accurate description."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"pourquoi vous faites cette tête ?","en":"why are you pulling that face?","zh":"你为什么摆着一张臭脸？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est quoi votre problème ? Pourquoi vous faites cette tête ?","exEn":"What’s your problem? Why are you pulling that face?","src":1},{"fr":"je suis de mauvaise humeur","en":"I’m in a bad mood","zh":"我心情不好","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis de mauvaise humeur parce que mon assistant a démissionné il y a un mois.","exEn":"I’m in a bad mood because my assistant resigned a month ago.","src":2},{"fr":"il a démissionné","en":"he resigned","zh":"他辞职了","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Je suis de mauvaise humeur parce que mon assistant a démissionné il y a un mois.","exEn":"I’m in a bad mood because my assistant resigned a month ago.","src":2},{"fr":"un entretien","en":"an interview","zh":"面试；会谈","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"J’ai voulu faire venir le type pour un entretien mais il avait déjà été embauché.","exEn":"I wanted to bring the guy in for an interview, but he had already been hired.","src":5},{"fr":"il avait déjà été embauché","en":"he had already been hired","zh":"他已经被录用了","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"J’ai voulu faire venir le type pour un entretien mais il avait déjà été embauché.","exEn":"I wanted to bring the guy in for an interview, but he had already been hired.","src":5},{"fr":"j’avais l’impression que","en":"I had the impression that","zh":"我有一种印象，觉得……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ impression","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’était un drôle de type, assez sympa, mais j’avais l’impression qu’il n’en ferait qu’à sa tête si je le prenais.","exEn":"He was a strange guy, quite nice, but I had the impression that he would do whatever he wanted if I took him on.","src":7},{"fr":"la perle rare","en":"the one-in-a-million person (the rare gem)","zh":"难得的人才；稀有的珍宝","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ pearl, rare","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça ne veut pas dire pour autant qu’on trouvera tout de suite la perle rare mais au moins c’est un bon début.","exEn":"That doesn’t mean we’ll find the one-in-a-million person right away but at least it’s a good start.","src":10},{"fr":"du coup","en":"so, as a result","zh":"所以；结果是","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Super ! Du coup, quand vous aurez trouvé quelqu’un, mes collègues feront la fête.","exEn":"Great! So, when you’ve found someone, my colleagues will celebrate.","src":12},{"fr":"mes collègues feront la fête","en":"my colleagues will celebrate","zh":"我的同事们会庆祝","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Super ! Du coup, quand vous aurez trouvé quelqu’un, mes collègues feront la fête.","exEn":"Great! So, when you’ve found someone, my colleagues will celebrate.","src":12}],"uncertain":["Culture note about La France / l’Hexagone at the top of page 570 belongs to lesson 90 and was left out."],"pdfPage":569,"gaps":[[1.68,3.78],[5.2,8.04],[47.9,49],[49.98,51.28],[75.32,78.82]],"dur":78.8},{"n":92,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un magazine littéraire","titleEn":"A literary magazine programme","intro":"This final full week of lessons focuses on the vocabulary, expressions, and language registers used in the media – here, a literary talk show.","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Un grand merci d’être venue sur le plateau de « Virgules » pour parler de votre dernier livre :","en":"Thank you very much for coming on the set of “Virgules” (Commas) to talk about your latest book:","lit":"the set of"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"« Une réputation injuste, une vie écourtée », dans lequel vous superposez deux Marie-Antoinette : une princesse inconstante et dépensière","en":"“An Unfair Reputation, A Life Cut Short,” in which you superimpose two Marie Antoinettes: a fickle and spendthrift princess","lit":"unjust; shortened"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"et une femme courageuse et sous-estimée. Je suis tenté de vous demander :","en":"and a brave and underestimated woman. I’m tempted to ask you:","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"pourquoi un livre de plus – qui n’est pas un roman – sur un personnage qui a déjà fait couler énormément d’encre ?","en":"why another book – which isn’t a novel – on a character about whom so much has already been written?","lit":"made ink flow enormously"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"N’est-ce pas un peu irréaliste d’essayer de rétablir la réputation d’un personnage si controversé ?","en":"Isn’t it a bit unrealistic to try to restore the reputation of such a controversial character?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien au contraire. On ne cesse de revisiter l’histoire de notre pays, nous, les historiens,","en":"Quite the contrary. We historians are constantly revisiting the history of our country,","lit":"one doesn’t cease from"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"et de réexaminer la vie de celles et ceux qui en étaient les acteurs principaux.","en":"and re-examining the lives of those who were the protagonists.","lit":"main actors"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Dans le cas de Marie-Antoinette, on a souvent essayé de défaire et de refaire la réputation de celle que l’on traitait de « Madame Déficit ».","en":"In Marie-Antoinette’s case, attempts have often been made to undo and rebuild the reputation of the woman who was referred to as “Mrs Deficit.”","lit":"one treated"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je crois sincèrement qu’elle a été maltraitée par ces historiens, dont la plupart sont des hommes.","en":"I sincerely believe that she has been mistreated by those historians, most of whom are men.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pensais qu’il fallait la réévaluer d’un point de vue féministe, non pas comme une femme irresponsable et écervelée","en":"I thought she should be re-assessed from a feminist point of view, not as an irresponsible and scatterbrained woman","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"mais comme un personnage fort, en avance sur son temps – et injustement méprisée.","en":"but as a strong character, ahead of her time – and unjustly despised.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Sa réputation est entachée depuis des siècles par un certain nombre de légendes","en":"Her reputation has been stained for centuries by a number of legends","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"dont la fameuse « Qu’ils mangent de la brioche », une phrase qu’elle n’a jamais prononcée.","en":"including the famous “Let them eat cake,” a phrase she never uttered.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais, en lisant votre ouvrage, on a l’impression d’avoir affaire à une sainte. Je cite :","en":"But, reading your book, one gets the impression of dealing with a saint. I quote:","lit":"work"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"« Elle passa son enfance en Autriche, une fille heureuse qui fit le bonheur de sa famille. »","en":"“She spent her childhood in Austria, a happy girl who delighted her family.”","lit":"made the happiness of"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"« Elle épousa le Dauphin, devenant une épouse irréprochable et une excellente mère. » On dirait un ange !","en":"“She married the Dauphin, becoming an irreproachable wife and an excellent mother.” She sounds like an angel!","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais dans l’avant-propos, j’écris : « Elle fut au cœur de nombreux scandales, et elle dépensa des sommes faramineuses en robes et bijoux.","en":"But in the foreword, I write, “She was at the centre of many scandals, and spent enormous sums of money on dresses and jewellery.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Ainsi, elle fut tour à tour adorée et haïe, mais elle était tenue éloignée du monde des hommes. »","en":"Thus she was both loved and hated, but she was kept away from the world of men.”","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Savez-vous quels ont été ses derniers mots ?","en":"Do you know what her last words were?","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Euh, « Vive le Roi ! » ? « Que Dieu bénisse la France ! » ? Quelque chose de ce genre ?","en":"Uh, “Long live the King!”? “God bless France!”? Something like that?","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous n’y êtes pas du tout. Écoutez bien : « Ayant marché par mégarde sur le pied de son bourreau,","en":"You’re wide of the mark. Listen carefully: “Having inadvertently stepped on her executioner’s foot,","lit":"not there at all"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"elle le regarda doucement et lui dit : “Excusez-moi, Monsieur, je ne l’ai pas fait exprès.” »","en":"she looked at him gently and said, ‘Excuse me, sir, I didn’t mean to do it.’”","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"Peut-on dire vraiment que celle qui a écrit dans son testament « Que mon fils ne cherche jamais à venger notre mort »,","en":"Can it really be said that she who wrote in her will, “May my son never seek to avenge our death,”","lit":""},{"i":24,"dash":false,"fr":"peut-on vraiment dire que cette femme n’était qu’une tête de linotte ?","en":"can we really say that this woman was just a scatterbrain?","lit":"linnet’s head"},{"i":25,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous avez sans doute raison, mais connaissant le sort réservé à Marie-Antoinette, à votre place, j’éviterais le mot « tête »…","en":"You are undoubtedly right, but knowing the fate that befell Marie-Antoinette, if I were you, I would avoid the word “head”…","lit":"reserved for"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"un plateau (plural plateaux) has several meanings stemming from the root adjective plat, flat, including a tray and a cheese board, un plateau de fromages. In the entertainment industry, un plateau is a cinema or TV set and, by extension, the line-up of guests or entertainers: Trois invités formeront notre plateau ce soir, We will have three guests with us here this evening."},{"ref":"2","text":"This lesson shows how prefixes are used to alter the meaning of verbs, nouns and adjectives. For example, é- can indicate distance (loin, far ➔ éloigné(e), remote, distanced) or a change of state (court, short ➔ écourté(e)). The noun le cerveau, the brain, forms the basis of écervelé, scatterbrained (line 10). Be careful, though, because é- is not always a standalone prefix, for example: épouse, wife, spouse (line 16)."},{"ref":"3","text":"We know that surnames do not take a plural s (lesson 29 note 5); neither do first names. Nous avons deux Robert dans le studio ce soir, We have two Roberts in the studio with us this evening."},{"ref":"4","text":"Prefixes are used, among other things, to form negative adjectives (constant ➔ inconstant, réaliste ➔ irréaliste, traiter ➔ maltraiter), to express a degree of intensity (estimer ➔ sous-estimer, line 3), and to denote repetition (examiner ➔ réexaminer – see lesson 70 section 2)."},{"ref":"5","text":"If the impersonal pronoun on follows a word ending in a vowel, especially qui, que and ou/où, we sometimes add l’ to facilitate pronunciation (… où l’on habite rather than … où on habite, for example). This is generally used only in formal written language or by careful speakers."},{"ref":"6","text":"Here are some more examples of the imperative use of the subjunctive: Vive les Bretons (lesson 57), Long live the Bretons. Qu’il parte immédiatement, Let him (or He should) leave immediately."},{"ref":"7","text":"This is the past simple tense, or passé simple. Equivalent in meaning to the passé composé, it is used almost exclusively in written French. The conjugation for regular -er verbs, such as passer, to pass (or spend time) is: je passai, tu passas, il/elle passa, nous passâmes, vous passâtes, ils/elles passèrent. Thus Elle passa sa jeunesse en Autriche is identical in meaning to Elle a passé sa jeunesse en Autriche ➔ She spent her youth in Austria."},{"ref":"8","text":"The passé simple of faire is je fis, tu fis, il/elle fit, nous fîmes, vous fîtes, ils/elles firent."},{"ref":"9","text":"The passé simple of être is very irregular: je fus, tu fus, il/elle fut, nous fûmes, vous fûtes, ils/elles furent."},{"ref":"10","text":"une linotte is a linnet, and the expression avoir/être une tête de linotte means to be a bird brain. A less pejorative equivalent is avoir la tête en l’air / être tête en l’air (“to have / to be (one’s) head in the air”). Il oublie toujours tout, Carl. Il a vraiment la tête en l’air, Carl always forgets everything. He’s a real scatterbrain."},{"ref":"","text":"Born in Austria in 1755, Marie-Antoinette de Habsbourg-Lorraine married the heir apparent (le Dauphin – though the word also means dolphin) to the French throne, Louis-Auguste, later Louis XVI. Loved and hated in equal measure, she was a controversial figure who, despite her lavish expenditures – which earned her the nickname Madame Déficit – was possibly more sinned against than sinning. It is a popular myth that, when informed that the people were starving for want of bread, she answered Alors qu’ils mangent de la brioche (usually translated as Let them eat cake, although la brioche is a kind of sweet pastry). Cited by Rousseau, the phrase was attributed to “une grande princesse” and may actually have been the author’s own invention. Marie-Antoinette met her end on the guillotine in 1793."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Notre invité sur le plateau ce soir est l’écrivain Gilles Boursier, qui est venu nous parler de son dernier roman.","a":"Our guest in the studio this evening is the writer Gilles Boursier, who has come to talk to us about his latest novel."},{"q":"C’est une décision qui a déjà fait couler beaucoup d’encre et que le maire tente d’éviter coûte que coûte.","a":"It’s a decision that has been written about extensively and that the mayor is trying to avoid at all costs."},{"q":"N’est-ce pas injuste d’entacher la réputation de quelqu’un que vous ne connaissez pas ?","a":"Isn’t it unfair to stain the reputation of someone you don’t know?"},{"q":"J’ai lu et relu la lettre de Robert, j’ai réexaminé ses explications, mais je n’y comprends toujours rien.","a":"I have read and re-read Robert’s letter, re-examined his explanations, but I still don’t understand."},{"q":"Mais que veulent-ils réellement ? Qu’ils me le disent clairement et je verrai si c’est possible.","a":"But what do they really want? Let them tell me clearly and I will see if it is possible."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"Louis was kept at a distance from his children, to whom he wrote every month. He was both adored and hated.","t":"Louis {{était tenu éloigné}} de ses enfants, à qui {{il écrivit tous les mois}}. Il {{fut}} à la fois {{adoré}} et {{haï}}."},{"cue":"If you think that he is unfair, irresponsible and despised, then you’re wide of the mark. History has mistreated him.","t":"Si tu crois qu’il est {{injuste}}, {{irresponsable}} et {{méprisé}}, alors {{tu n’y es pas du tout}}. L’histoire {{l’a maltraité}}."},{"cue":"These two literary magazines, ahead of their times, delighted readers at the beginning of the century.","t":"Ces deux magazines littéraires, {{en avance sur leur temps}}, {{firent le bonheur}} des lecteurs {{au début du siècle}}."},{"cue":"History has to be re-assessed constantly by historians, just as it’s necessary to revisit classic works of literature.","t":"L’histoire {{doit être réévaluée constamment}} par les historiens, tout comme {{il faut revisiter}} les {{ouvrages littéraires}} classiques."},{"cue":"He spent vast amounts on his re-election, but the result was a disaster.","t":"Il {{dépensa}} des montants {{faramineux}} pour sa {{réélection}}, mais le résultat {{fut un désastre}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"une réputation injuste","en":"an unfair reputation","zh":"不公正的名声","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ reputation, unjust (-tion = -tion)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Une réputation injuste, une vie écourtée », dans lequel vous superposez deux Marie-Antoinette : une princesse inconstante et dépensière","exEn":"“An Unfair Reputation, A Life Cut Short,” in which you superimpose two Marie Antoinettes: a fickle and spendthrift princess","src":2},{"fr":"un livre de plus","en":"one more book, another book","zh":"又一本书","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"pourquoi un livre de plus – qui n’est pas un roman – sur un personnage qui a déjà fait couler énormément d’encre ?","exEn":"why another book – which isn’t a novel – on a character about whom so much has already been written?","src":4},{"fr":"un personnage","en":"a character","zh":"人物；角色","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ personage","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"pourquoi un livre de plus – qui n’est pas un roman – sur un personnage qui a déjà fait couler énormément d’encre ?","exEn":"why another book – which isn’t a novel – on a character about whom so much has already been written?","src":4},{"fr":"faire couler de l’encre","en":"to cause a lot to be written","zh":"引发大量笔墨（被大量报道）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"pourquoi un livre de plus – qui n’est pas un roman – sur un personnage qui a déjà fait couler énormément d’encre ?","exEn":"why another book – which isn’t a novel – on a character about whom so much has already been written?","src":4},{"fr":"Bien au contraire.","en":"Quite the contrary.","zh":"恰恰相反。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien au contraire. On ne cesse de revisiter l’histoire de notre pays, nous, les historiens,","exEn":"Quite the contrary. We historians are constantly revisiting the history of our country,","src":6},{"fr":"essayer de rétablir la réputation","en":"to try to restore the reputation","zh":"试图恢复名誉","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’est-ce pas un peu irréaliste d’essayer de rétablir la réputation d’un personnage si controversé ?","exEn":"Isn’t it a bit unrealistic to try to restore the reputation of such a controversial character?","src":5},{"fr":"je crois sincèrement que","en":"I sincerely believe that","zh":"我真心认为……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ sincerely","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je crois sincèrement qu’elle a été maltraitée par ces historiens, dont la plupart sont des hommes.","exEn":"I sincerely believe that she has been mistreated by those historians, most of whom are men.","src":9},{"fr":"en avance sur son temps","en":"ahead of his/her time","zh":"超前于时代","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais comme un personnage fort, en avance sur son temps – et injustement méprisée.","exEn":"but as a strong character, ahead of her time – and unjustly despised.","src":11},{"fr":"avoir affaire à quelqu’un","en":"to be dealing with someone","zh":"与某人打交道","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais, en lisant votre ouvrage, on a l’impression d’avoir affaire à une sainte. Je cite :","exEn":"But, reading your book, one gets the impression of dealing with a saint. I quote:","src":14},{"fr":"elle fit le bonheur de sa famille","en":"she made her family happy","zh":"她给家人带来幸福","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Elle passa son enfance en Autriche, une fille heureuse qui fit le bonheur de sa famille. »","exEn":"“She spent her childhood in Austria, a happy girl who delighted her family.”","src":15},{"fr":"Vous n’y êtes pas du tout.","en":"You’re wide of the mark.","zh":"您完全没猜对。","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous n’y êtes pas du tout. Écoutez bien : « Ayant marché par mégarde sur le pied de son bourreau,","exEn":"You’re wide of the mark. Listen carefully: “Having inadvertently stepped on her executioner’s foot,","src":21},{"fr":"je ne l’ai pas fait exprès","en":"I didn’t do it on purpose","zh":"我不是故意的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “eggs-pray”: you say a prayer over the broken eggs you smashed – “I didn’t do it on purpose!”","ex":"elle le regarda doucement et lui dit : “Excusez-moi, Monsieur, je ne l’ai pas fait exprès.” »","exEn":"she looked at him gently and said, ‘Excuse me, sir, I didn’t mean to do it.’”","src":22},{"fr":"une tête de linotte","en":"a scatterbrain, a bird brain","zh":"冒失鬼；糊涂虫","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “lino-tt”: a little bird hopping around on your lino floor with its head in the clouds","ex":"peut-on vraiment dire que cette femme n’était qu’une tête de linotte ?","exEn":"can we really say that this woman was just a scatterbrain?","src":24},{"fr":"vous avez sans doute raison","en":"you are undoubtedly right","zh":"您无疑是对的","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"sans doute = probably/undoubtedly, in everyday use more often “probably”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous avez sans doute raison, mais connaissant le sort réservé à Marie-Antoinette, à votre place, j’éviterais le mot « tête »…","exEn":"You are undoubtedly right, but knowing the fate that befell Marie-Antoinette, if I were you, I would avoid the word “head”…","src":25}],"uncertain":["dialogue 15: leading dash taken from the OCR (the English side has none)","ex2 item 2 gap 'tu n’y es pas du tout' follows the printed answer key"],"pdfPage":579,"gaps":[[1.84,3.84],[5.46,8.52],[98.2,99.24],[133,134.12],[143.44,144.48],[150.68,154.26],[156.26,158.18],[165.1,168],[173.38,176.36],[180.26,183.34],[190.46,193.56],[198.22,202.34]],"dur":202.4},{"n":93,"kind":"lesson","title":"Un cours d’histoire : « Le jour de gloire est arrivé ! »","titleEn":"A history lesson: “The day of glory is here”","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"« Le quatorze juillet mil sept cent quatre-vingt-neuf marqua un tournant majeur dans l’histoire de France.","en":"“14 July 1789 marked a major turning point in the history of France.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"En effet, la prise de la Bastille annonça la fin de la monarchie et les débuts de la République.","en":"The storming of the Bastille heralded the end of the monarchy and the beginnings of the Republic.","lit":"taking"},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais qu’en fut-il vraiment ? Que se passa-t-il exactement en ce “jour de gloire” ?","en":"But what was it really like? What exactly happened on that “day of glory”?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Combien de misérables prisonniers furent libérés de ce lieu morne par ces révolutionnaires de fortune ?","en":"How many miserable prisoners were freed from that gloomy place by those makeshift revolutionaries?","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Le récit est palpitant, même si le bilan de la libération n’est pas à la hauteur du mythe.","en":"The story is thrilling, even if the result of the liberation does not live up to the myth.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"En juillet de cette année fatidique, Louis Seize subissait les pressions de la toute nouvelle Assemblée nationale.","en":"In July of that fateful year, Louis XVI was under pressure from the brand-new National Assembly.","lit":"suffered the"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Le roi fit semblant d’accepter leurs exigences, mais en réalité, il fit venir les troupes à Paris","en":"The king pretended to accept their demands, but in reality he brought troops up to Paris","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"et les concentra tout autour de la capitale.","en":"and concentrated them all around the capital.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Les Parisiens s’inquiétèrent de la situation, et l’agitation grandit de minute en minute, d’heure en heure.","en":"The Parisians were worried about the situation, and the agitation grew from minute to minute, from hour to hour.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Le douze juillet une émeute éclata dans la ville et de violents combats firent rage.","en":"On 12 July a riot broke out in the city and violent fighting raged.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Peu à peu, les citoyens descendirent dans la rue, assoiffés de liberté et de justice.","en":"Gradually the citizens took to the streets, thirsty for freedom and justice.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"La foule grossit, les voix s’élevèrent de plus en plus fort, les citoyens prirent les armes.","en":"The crowd grew larger and larger, the voices grew louder and louder, and the citizens took up arms.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Les armes, soit, mais il manquait des munitions ! Où en trouver ? Dans une forteresse, forcément !","en":"Arms, all very well, but there was no ammunition! Where could it be found? In a fortress, of course!","lit":"missing"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Alors s’éleva le cri “À la Bastille, citoyens ! La liberté ou la mort !”","en":"Then the cry went up, “To the Bastille, citizens! Freedom or death!”","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"et, comme un seul homme, la foule se dirigea vers l’effroyable prison, déterminée à en faire tomber ses murs sinistres.","en":"and, as one man, the crowd went to the dreadful prison, determined to bring down its grim walls.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"En débouchant de la rue Saint-Antoine, les révoltés marquèrent un temps d’arrêt, tant l’édifice était menaçant,","en":"When the rebels emerged from Rue Saint-Antoine, they paused for a moment, for the building was so threatening,","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"mais la colère et la passion l’emportèrent et toute la fureur du peuple se concentra sur ce symbole de leur oppression.","en":"but anger and passion took over and all the wrath of the people focused on this symbol of their oppression.","lit":"concentrated"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"Cependant, la Bastille ne fut pas prise, elle se rendit ;","en":"However, the Bastille was not taken, it surrendered;","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"et les révolutionnaires – car il s’agit bien d’une révolution, pas d’une révolte – ne tuèrent qu’un seul homme.","en":"and the revolutionaries – for it was a revolution, not a revolt – killed only one man.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Ainsi, notre Fête nationale ne commémore ni la violence ni la haine,","en":"Thus, our National Day commemorates neither violence nor hatred","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"mais la France libérée des traîtres et des menteurs de l’Ancien Régime.","en":"but France liberated from the traitors and liars of the Ancien Régime.","lit":"Old Regime"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Au fait, au moment de la prise, le donjon funeste ne contenait qu’une petite poignée de prisonniers ! »","en":"By the way, at the time of the capture, the fateful dungeon contained only a handful of prisoners!”","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"This lesson contains a number of words, such as munitions, combats and révolution, that are identical or remarkably similar to the English words derived from them. Before using these loan words in spoken French, always check the pronunciation because the differences between the two languages can be considerable (for example, the double s in passion is pronounced as a sibilant in French rather than [sh] as in English)."},{"ref":"1","text":"une fortune, a fortune (lesson 59 line 27), is the root of the adjective phrase de fortune, meaning makeshift or improvised: J’ai un bureau de fortune dans mon salon, I have a makeshift office in my living room.\nBy extension the phrase means chance (i.e. unplanned): Gilles n’est pas un véritable ami, c’est un compagnon de fortune, Gilles isn’t really a friend, he’s a chance companion."},{"ref":"2","text":"un bilan is a financial term meaning a balance sheet (aka a statement of financial position). The underlying notion of assessment is found in many contexts, such as un bilan de santé, a medical checkup (“health statement”). The word is also used in news reports about injuries or fatalities: Le bilan de l’accident est de trois morts et deux blessés, The accident resulted in three dead and two injured. A much broader meaning, as in line 5, is an outcome, a result."},{"ref":"3","text":"la hauteur, height (lesson 76 line 8), is another example of a noun used in a figurative expression: être à la hauteur, to be up to or equal to (a situation, etc.): Nos athlètes paralympiques se sont montrés à la hauteur du défi, Our Paralympic athletes proved equal to the challenge."},{"ref":"4","text":"The past simple of grandir and other regular -ir verbs is: je grandis, tu grandis, il/elle grandit, nous grandîmes, vous grandîtes, ils/elles grandirent. We saw the passé simple of regular -er verbs in the previous lesson."},{"ref":"5","text":"The past simple of descendre and other regular -re verbs is identical to that of regular -ir verbs: je descendis, tu descendis, il/elle descendit, nous descendîmes, vous descendîtes, ils/elles descendirent."},{"ref":"6","text":"We know that soit is the third person singular subjunctive of être (see lesson 68 note 9 and lesson 79 note 2 for the full conjugation). But in line 13, it is an invariable adverb, and the final t is pronounced. In a formal register, soit is used like the English phrase Be that as it may or, less formally, Very well. Cette brioche est chère, soit, mais elle est délicieuse ! This brioche may be expensive, but it’s so good! (Be careful not to confuse this adverbial usage with the conjunction soit… soit, lesson 64 line 7.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"déboucher is another example of a prefixed verb: boucher means to block (up): L’évier est bouché ; il faut le déboucher, The sink is blocked; it needs unblocking. But it also has an intransitive form. With de, it means to emerge or come out from, whereas with sur, the meaning is to lead, to open out into or run into: La rue débouche sur une grande vallée, The road runs into a big valley."},{"ref":"8","text":"Yet another use of the versatile word tant (first seen in lesson 87 note 7 in the phrase tant que), this time in a more literary context. In a formal register, tant is used at the beginning of a descriptive clause to explain what precedes it. In many cases, it can be translated simply by because: Il était impossible de courir, tant il y avait de monde dans la rue, It was impossible to run because there were so many people in the street."},{"ref":"9","text":"The regular verb emporter, formed from em- (“away,” as in emmener, lesson 79 note 7) and porter, to carry, means to take away, carry off, etc., Une grande pizza s’il vous plaît – À manger sur place ou à emporter ? A large pizza please – To eat here (“on the spot”) or to take away? By extension, emporter can mean to get the upper hand, to prevail: C’est le système le plus simple qui a fini par l’emporter, It was the simplest system that prevailed."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"J’ai acheté le tout dernier modèle, malgré son prix élevé, mais il n’est pas à la hauteur de mes attentes.","a":"I bought the very latest model, despite the high price, but it doesn’t live up to my expectations."},{"q":"Le bilan de cette terrible maladie est très lourd, avec plus de six mille morts déjà.","a":"This terrible disease is taking a heavy toll, with more than six thousand deaths already."},{"q":"Les derniers chiffres font penser que l’entreprise est en bonne santé, mais qu’en est-il vraiment ?","a":"The latest figures suggest that the company is in good health, but what is really going on?"},{"q":"Les voix s’élèvent de plus en plus pour exiger un changement de régime.","a":"There are growing calls for regime change."},{"q":"Vous n’avez qu’à suivre cette rue toute droite, qui débouche sur les quais de la Seine.","a":"Just go straight ahead down this street, which leads to the banks of the Seine."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"They came down onto the streets as one man, and a cry arose: “The day of glory has arrived!”","t":"Ils {{sont descendus}} dans la rue {{comme un seul homme}}, et {{un cri s’est élevé}} : « Le jour de gloire {{est arrivé}} ! »"},{"cue":"A riot broke out and the fighting raged. The deputies grew worried about the situation.","t":"Une {{émeute a éclaté}} et les {{combats ont fait rage}}. Les députés {{se sont inquiétés de}} la situation."},{"cue":"Everyone headed for the prison but, arriving on the spot, they paused before attacking.","t":"Tout le monde {{s’est dirigé vers}} la prison mais, {{en arrivant sur place}}, ils {{ont marqué un temps d’arrêt}} avant d’attaquer."},{"cue":"The sinks were blocked and had to be unblocked very quickly.","t":"Les éviers {{étaient bouchés}} et il {{fallait}} les {{déboucher}} très vite."},{"cue":"I didn’t want to watch the movie because the reviews were so bad, but even so it won two prizes.","t":"Je {{ne voulais pas}} regarder le film, {{tant les critiques}} étaient {{mauvaises}}, mais il {{a}} néanmoins {{remporté}} deux Césars."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un tournant majeur","en":"a major turning point","zh":"重大转折点","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ major","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Le quatorze juillet mil sept cent quatre-vingt-neuf marqua un tournant majeur dans l’histoire de France.","exEn":"“14 July 1789 marked a major turning point in the history of France.","src":1},{"fr":"les Parisiens s’inquiétèrent de la situation","en":"the Parisians were worried about the situation","zh":"巴黎人对局势感到担忧","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ situation (same as English)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les Parisiens s’inquiétèrent de la situation, et l’agitation grandit de minute en minute, d’heure en heure.","exEn":"The Parisians were worried about the situation, and the agitation grew from minute to minute, from hour to hour.","src":9},{"fr":"une émeute","en":"a riot","zh":"暴乱","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “a mutt”: a pack of angry mutts rioting in the street","ex":"Le douze juillet une émeute éclata dans la ville et de violents combats firent rage.","exEn":"On 12 July a riot broke out in the city and violent fighting raged.","src":10},{"fr":"peu à peu","en":"little by little, gradually","zh":"渐渐地","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Peu à peu, les citoyens descendirent dans la rue, assoiffés de liberté et de justice.","exEn":"Gradually the citizens took to the streets, thirsty for freedom and justice.","src":11},{"fr":"la foule grossit","en":"the crowd grows larger","zh":"人群越聚越多","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La foule grossit, les voix s’élevèrent de plus en plus fort, les citoyens prirent les armes.","exEn":"The crowd grew larger and larger, the voices grew louder and louder, and the citizens took up arms.","src":12},{"fr":"de plus en plus fort","en":"louder and louder","zh":"越来越响","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La foule grossit, les voix s’élevèrent de plus en plus fort, les citoyens prirent les armes.","exEn":"The crowd grew larger and larger, the voices grew louder and louder, and the citizens took up arms.","src":12},{"fr":"il manquait des munitions","en":"there was no ammunition (ammunition was lacking)","zh":"缺少弹药","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ munitions","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Les armes, soit, mais il manquait des munitions ! Où en trouver ? Dans une forteresse, forcément !","exEn":"Arms, all very well, but there was no ammunition! Where could it be found? In a fortress, of course!","src":13},{"fr":"comme un seul homme","en":"as one man","zh":"齐心协力；一致地","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et, comme un seul homme, la foule se dirigea vers l’effroyable prison, déterminée à en faire tomber ses murs sinistres.","exEn":"and, as one man, the crowd went to the dreadful prison, determined to bring down its grim walls.","src":15},{"fr":"la colère","en":"anger","zh":"愤怒","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “collar”: someone grabs your collar and the anger boils over","ex":"mais la colère et la passion l’emportèrent et toute la fureur du peuple se concentra sur ce symbole de leur oppression.","exEn":"but anger and passion took over and all the wrath of the people focused on this symbol of their oppression.","src":17},{"fr":"pas à la hauteur","en":"not up to it, not equal to (it)","zh":"达不到要求；配不上","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le récit est palpitant, même si le bilan de la libération n’est pas à la hauteur du mythe.","exEn":"The story is thrilling, even if the result of the liberation does not live up to the myth.","src":5},{"fr":"le bilan","en":"the outcome, the toll, the result","zh":"结果；总结；伤亡情况","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le récit est palpitant, même si le bilan de la libération n’est pas à la hauteur du mythe.","exEn":"The story is thrilling, even if the result of the liberation does not live up to the myth.","src":5},{"fr":"faire semblant d’accepter","en":"to pretend to accept","zh":"假装接受","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Le roi fit semblant d’accepter leurs exigences, mais en réalité, il fit venir les troupes à Paris","exEn":"The king pretended to accept their demands, but in reality he brought troops up to Paris","src":7},{"fr":"une petite poignée de prisonniers","en":"a small handful of prisoners","zh":"一小撮囚犯","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au fait, au moment de la prise, le donjon funeste ne contenait qu’une petite poignée de prisonniers ! »","exEn":"By the way, at the time of the capture, the fateful dungeon contained only a handful of prisoners!”","src":22},{"fr":"vous n’avez qu’à suivre cette rue","en":"just follow this street","zh":"您只要沿着这条街走就行","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous n’avez qu’à suivre cette rue toute droite, qui débouche sur les quais de la Seine.","exEn":"Just go straight ahead down this street, which leads to the banks of the Seine.","src":0}],"uncertain":["note 6: the printed cross-reference 'line 16' was corrected to line 13 (soit is in sentence 13)","note 2: the printed 'line 2' was corrected to line 5 (bilan is in sentence 5)"],"pdfPage":587,"gaps":[[1.74,3.82],[7.24,10.14],[15.52,16.64],[27.98,29.1],[35.02,36.16],[40.72,41.86],[47.94,48.94],[57.2,58.5],[64.62,65.94],[71.04,72.28],[84.54,85.56],[92.16,93.24],[98.02,99.08],[106.16,107.3],[120.98,122.48],[132.56,133.78],[142.62,143.72],[149.96,153.16],[155.06,157.3],[163.98,167.12],[171.64,174.8],[179.16,182.22],[186.42,189.56],[193.12,197.1]],"dur":197.1},{"n":94,"kind":"lesson","title":"Les inconvénients du travail à domicile","titleEn":"The drawbacks of working from (at) home","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Au secours ! Qu’est-ce que j’ai fichu ?","en":"Help! What the heck have I done?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Je faisais des tableaux tranquillement avec mon tableur quand je me suis arrêté pour chercher à manger à la cuisine.","en":"I was quietly doing tables in my spreadsheet when I stopped to get something to eat in the kitchen.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Au retour, je me suis remis devant l’écran, et patatras ! Panne totale.","en":"On my return, I got back in front of the screen, and boom! Total breakdown.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, rien ne marche : ni l’ordinateur, ni le wifi, ni le clavier, ni la prise, rien de rien.","en":"Now nothing works: not the computer, nor the wifi, nor the keyboard, nor the plug, nothing at all.","lit":"nothing of nothing"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Aïe ! C’est embêtant. Mais tu aurais dû faire plus attention –","en":"Ouch! That’s annoying. But you should have paid more attention –","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"ou plus de ménage : t’as vu le fouillis dans ton bureau ?","en":"or done more cleaning: have you seen the mess in your office?","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais par où commencer le rangement quand tout est sens dessus dessous ?","en":"But where do you start the tidying when everything is topsy-turvy?","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est pas vrai ! Il y a juste un petit peu de désordre, c’est tout, deux ou trois choses de travers.","en":"It isn’t true! There’s just a little bit of clutter, that’s all, a couple of things out of place.","lit":"disorder; of through"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je l’aurais rangé hier si j’avais eu le temps mais je travaillais contre la montre.","en":"I would have tidied it yesterday if I’d had the time, but I was working against the clock.","lit":"watch"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"À ta place, j’aurais fait le ménage tous les jours. Comme ça tu n’aurais pas eu ce problème.","en":"If I were you, I would have cleaned it every day. That way you wouldn’t be having this problem.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Comment tu vas faire pour t’en sortir ? Tu n’es ni électricien, ni réparateur informatique.","en":"What are you going to do to sort things out? You’re not an electrician or a computer repairman.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai tenté de contacter le service d’assistance en ligne mais l’attente était interminable.","en":"I tried to contact the online helpdesk but the wait was endless.","lit":"assistance department"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu aurais pu demander à Driss, c’est un as de l’ordinateur. Il aurait remis tout en ordre en moins de deux.","en":"You could have asked Driss, he’s a computer whizzkid. He would have sorted everything out in no time.","lit":"put back everything into order; in less than two"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais il est parti ce matin à Toulouse pour assister à un atelier.","en":"But he left this morning for Toulouse to attend a workshop.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Zut ! Si j’avais su, je l’aurais appelé sur le champ mais je pensais qu’il était toujours dans le coin.","en":"Damn! If I’d known, I would have called him on the spot but I thought he was still in the neighbourhood.","lit":"corner"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Je suis certain qu’il serait venu tout de suite si je lui avais demandé. Il est très serviable.","en":"I’m sure he would have come right away if I had asked him. He’s very helpful.","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Quand même, il aurait pu me dire qu’il partait et qu’il n’était pas disponible !","en":"Even so, he could have told me that he was leaving and that he wasn’t available!","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Là, tu te livres à ton passe-temps favori : te plaindre après coup. Sniff !","en":"Now you’re indulging in your favourite pastime: complaining after the fact. Boo-hoo!","lit":"delivering yourself"},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Et toi, tu me critiques toujours : « T’aurais dû faire ci, tu n’aurais pas dû faire ça », et patati et patata.","en":"And you’re always criticising me: “You should have done this, you shouldn’t have done that,” and so on and so forth.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-être bien, mais j’aurais été contente si tu m’avais appelée. J’aurais pu t’aider.","en":"Maybe so, but I would have been happy if you had called me. I could have helped you.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"Comment cela ? Toi non plus, tu n’es pas un crack de l’informatique, que je sache.","en":"What do you mean? You’re not a computer expert either, as far as I know.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-être pas, et je n’aurais pas su réparer l’équipement. Mais j’ai de bons yeux et je sais regarder.","en":"Maybe not, and I wouldn’t have been able to fix the hardware. But I have good eyesight and I know how to look.","lit":"eyes"},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"Ta machine est débranchée : tu as dû te prendre les pieds bêtement dans le cordon d’alimentation.","en":"Your machine is unplugged: you must have stupidly got your feet caught in the power cord.","lit":"feeding"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(13) as is one of the few nouns whose final -s is always pronounced, in both the singular and the plural: un as / des as [an-ass / dayzass]."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(18) Although Sniff is a loan word, the i is pronounced French-style as [ee]: [sneef]."},{"ref":"1","text":"We know that les secours means the emergency services (Culture Note, lesson 60). The exclamation Au secours ! is the equivalent of Help! (The imperative of aider, to help, can be added: Au secours ! Aidez-moi !) The corresponding verb is secourir, to help, assist or, in some contexts, to rescue (lesson 89 line 21). S’il n’avait pas été secouru par les pompiers, il serait mort, If he hadn’t been rescued by the fire brigade, he would have died."},{"ref":"2","text":"See lesson 88 note 6."},{"ref":"3","text":"French has an arsenal of onomatopoeia and exclamations. Patatras ! is the equivalent of Boom! (also used in French, though spelled Boum !) Et patati et patata (line 19) means and so on and so forth, while the loan word Sniff ! (line 18), imitating a sniffing sound, is the equivalent of boo-hoo. Most of these words (or sounds) are self-explanatory, and the differences with English are often amusing (Vlan ! Wham!)"},{"ref":"4","text":"aurais dû: here is the past conditional, first seen in lesson 67 line 13. It is formed with the present conditional of avoir or être and the past participle, which, as usual, agrees in number and gender."},{"ref":"5","text":"The regular verb fouiller, to search, rummage for, etc. can be both transitive – La police ne nous a pas fouillés à l’entrée du bâtiment, The police didn’t search us at the building entrance – and intransitive, J’ai fouillé dans mes poches, I rummaged in my pockets. The derived noun is une fouille, a search, but the related (singular) noun un fouillis means a jumble, a mess, etc."},{"ref":"6","text":"See lesson 73 note 8."},{"ref":"7","text":"See lesson 20 note 6. Of course, the grammatically correct form is ce n’est pas vrai, but, in this and other exclamations, the ne is often dropped in colloquial spoken French. Since the text of this lesson is a transcription of a dialogue, we have imitated the natural speech pattern to reproduce what you would hear. For example, in line 6, we see t’as vu le fouillis instead of tu as vu, and in line 19, t’aurais instead of tu aurais."},{"ref":"8","text":"The pronominal verb se sortir means to extricate oneself, to get out of: Le pays s’est sorti difficilement de la crise économique, The country emerged with difficulty from the economic crisis. The direct object is replaced by en if it is clear or understood, particularly where the meaning is to manage, cope, get by: Comment t’en sors-tu sans moi, chérie ? – Je m’en sors très bien, merci ! How are you managing without me, darling? – I’m getting by very nicely, thank you!"},{"ref":"9","text":"Two words in this lesson will be familiar to English speakers and have more or less the same meaning. Un as, an ace, can be used literally (l’as de pique – note the singular – the ace of spades) or figuratively (C’est un as du bricolage, He’s an ace DIY-er). Un crack, imported from English, means the same thing: C’est un crack de l’ordinateur, He’s a computer whizzkid. (Another borrowing is le crack, crack cocaine.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"sache, the first and third person singular subjunctive of savoir (see lesson 83 note 1), is found in the expression … que je sache (an ellipsis of pour autant que je sache, insofar as I know), placed at the end of a negative phrase, just like … as far as I know, … that I’m aware of, etc. The negative is pas que je sache : Est-ce qu’il est arrivé ? – Pas que je sache, Has he arrived? – Not that I’m aware of."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Je traversais la pièce sans regarder par terre. Et vlan ! Je me suis pris les deux pieds dans le tapis.","a":"I walked across the room without looking at the floor. And wham! I got both feet caught in the carpet."},{"q":"Ma machine à laver était en panne. Heureusement, Driss a réussi à la réparer en moins de deux.","a":"My washing machine was broken. Fortunately, Driss managed to fix it in no time."},{"q":"On s’en sort difficilement avec trois jeunes enfants à la maison : leur chambre est toujours un fouillis !","a":"It’s hard to manage with three children at home: their room is always a mess."},{"q":"Le policier nous a demandé de partir sur le champ, en disant que le bâtiment était dangereux, et patati et patata.","a":"The policeman asked us to leave right away, saying that the building was dangerous, and so on and so forth."},{"q":"L’ordi ne marche plus ! Qu’est-ce que vous avez fichu ? – Rien, que je sache.","a":"The computer doesn’t work anymore! What the heck did you do? – Nothing, as far as I know."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"If we had known the situation was so urgent, we would have come sooner.","t":"Si {{nous avions su}} que la situation {{était}} si urgente, {{nous serions venus}} plus tôt."},{"cue":"You shouldn’t have sent the e-mail. We could have sorted everything out ourselves.","t":"Vous {{n’auriez pas dû}} envoyer le mail. Nous {{aurions pu}} tout {{remettre en ordre}} nous-mêmes."},{"cue":"You wouldn’t have believed it if it hadn’t been published in every newspaper. – Perhaps not.","t":"Vous {{ne l’auriez pas cru}} si ça {{n’avait pas été publié}} dans tous les journaux. – {{Peut-être pas}}."},{"cue":"She indulged in one of her favourite pastimes: complaining about the bad weather in Brittany.","t":"{{Elle s’est livrée à}} un de {{ses passe-temps favoris}} : {{se plaindre du}} mauvais {{temps}} en Bretagne."},{"cue":"If we were you, we would have done it every week. That way, you would have had less work today.","t":"À votre place, {{nous l’aurions fait}} toutes les semaines. Comme ça, {{vous auriez eu moins de travail}} aujourd’hui."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"Au secours !","en":"Help!","zh":"救命！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au secours ! Qu’est-ce que j’ai fichu ?","exEn":"Help! What the heck have I done?","src":1},{"fr":"panne totale","en":"total breakdown","zh":"彻底瘫痪；完全故障","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “pan”: your frying pan breaks down on the stove – a total “panne”","ex":"Au retour, je me suis remis devant l’écran, et patatras ! Panne totale.","exEn":"On my return, I got back in front of the screen, and boom! Total breakdown.","src":3},{"fr":"rien ne marche","en":"nothing works","zh":"什么都不行了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, rien ne marche : ni l’ordinateur, ni le wifi, ni le clavier, ni la prise, rien de rien.","exEn":"Now nothing works: not the computer, nor the wifi, nor the keyboard, nor the plug, nothing at all.","src":4},{"fr":"tu aurais dû faire plus attention","en":"you should have paid more attention","zh":"你本该更小心些","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aïe ! C’est embêtant. Mais tu aurais dû faire plus attention –","exEn":"Ouch! That’s annoying. But you should have paid more attention –","src":5},{"fr":"un fouillis","en":"a mess, a jumble","zh":"乱七八糟；杂乱","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"ou plus de ménage : t’as vu le fouillis dans ton bureau ?","exEn":"or done more cleaning: have you seen the mess in your office?","src":6},{"fr":"faire le ménage","en":"to do the cleaning","zh":"做家务；打扫卫生","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"ménage = housework/household, NOT “marriage”","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À ta place, j’aurais fait le ménage tous les jours. Comme ça tu n’aurais pas eu ce problème.","exEn":"If I were you, I would have cleaned it every day. That way you wouldn’t be having this problem.","src":10},{"fr":"travailler contre la montre","en":"to work against the clock","zh":"与时间赛跑地工作","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je l’aurais rangé hier si j’avais eu le temps mais je travaillais contre la montre.","exEn":"I would have tidied it yesterday if I’d had the time, but I was working against the clock.","src":9},{"fr":"comment tu vas faire pour t’en sortir ?","en":"how are you going to get out of this / manage?","zh":"你打算怎么摆脱困境？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment tu vas faire pour t’en sortir ? Tu n’es ni électricien, ni réparateur informatique.","exEn":"What are you going to do to sort things out? You’re not an electrician or a computer repairman.","src":11},{"fr":"en moins de deux","en":"in no time, in a flash","zh":"转眼间；很快","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu aurais pu demander à Driss, c’est un as de l’ordinateur. Il aurait remis tout en ordre en moins de deux.","exEn":"You could have asked Driss, he’s a computer whizzkid. He would have sorted everything out in no time.","src":13},{"fr":"si j’avais su, je l’aurais appelé","en":"if I had known, I would have called him","zh":"要是我早知道，我就给他打电话了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Zut ! Si j’avais su, je l’aurais appelé sur le champ mais je pensais qu’il était toujours dans le coin.","exEn":"Damn! If I’d known, I would have called him on the spot but I thought he was still in the neighbourhood.","src":15},{"fr":"il est très serviable","en":"he is very helpful","zh":"他很乐于助人","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je suis certain qu’il serait venu tout de suite si je lui avais demandé. Il est très serviable.","exEn":"I’m sure he would have come right away if I had asked him. He’s very helpful.","src":16},{"fr":"quand même","en":"even so, all the same","zh":"尽管如此；还是","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quand même, il aurait pu me dire qu’il partait et qu’il n’était pas disponible !","exEn":"Even so, he could have told me that he was leaving and that he wasn’t available!","src":17},{"fr":"il n’était pas disponible","en":"he wasn’t available","zh":"他没空；他联系不上","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ disponible = available","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Quand même, il aurait pu me dire qu’il partait et qu’il n’était pas disponible !","exEn":"Even so, he could have told me that he was leaving and that he wasn’t available!","src":17},{"fr":"se plaindre après coup","en":"to complain after the fact","zh":"事后抱怨","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Là, tu te livres à ton passe-temps favori : te plaindre après coup. Sniff !","exEn":"Now you’re indulging in your favourite pastime: complaining after the fact. Boo-hoo!","src":18},{"fr":"que je sache","en":"as far as I know","zh":"据我所知","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Comment cela ? Toi non plus, tu n’es pas un crack de l’informatique, que je sache.","exEn":"What do you mean? You’re not a computer expert either, as far as I know.","src":21}],"uncertain":["dialogue 14/15: dash flags follow the OCR (14 no dash, 15 dash)","ex1 item 3 'On s’en sort difficilement' answer printed as 'It’s hard to manage'"],"pdfPage":595,"gaps":[[1.88,3.88],[6.26,9.26],[36.7,37.96],[62.34,63.38],[127.36,130.96],[133.16,135.08],[140.72,143.72],[149.18,152.2],[158.44,161.26],[168.12,171.04],[175.34,179.1]],"dur":179.1},{"n":95,"kind":"lesson","title":"Le JT du soir","titleEn":"The evening news (televised newspaper)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Bonsoir à tous et bienvenue. Voici les principaux titres de l’actualité de ce soir.","en":"Good evening everyone and welcome. Here are the main headlines of tonight’s news.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Un cambriolage a eu lieu hier aux alentours de minuit dans un palace de la Côte d’Azur :","en":"A burglary took place yesterday around midnight in a luxury hotel on the Riviera:","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"un collier de perles rares a été dérobé dans le coffre-fort d’une suite de cet hôtel luxueux,","en":"a necklace of rare pearls was stolen from the safe of a suite in this luxurious hotel,","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"qui accueille le gratin international pendant le festival du cinéma.","en":"which hosts international A-listers during the film festival.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Une série de vols de bijoux avait déjà défrayé la chronique l’été dernier,","en":"A series of jewellery thefts had already made headlines last summer,","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"et ce même hôtel avait été le théâtre d’un casse record très médiatisé.","en":"and this same hotel had been the scene of a highly publicised, record-breaking theft.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous vous en souvenez peut-être : plusieurs centaines de milliers d’euros en diamants ont été volés,","en":"As you may recall, several hundred thousand euros’ worth of diamonds were stolen","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"sous les yeux sidérés du personnel et des clients, par des hommes armés, qui ont disparu dans la nature.","en":"under the stunned gaze of the staff and clients by armed men, who vanished into thin air.","lit":"dumbfounded eyes; disappeared in the nature"},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Concernant l’incident d’hier, un porte-parole précise que la police ne dispose d’aucun indice à ce stade,","en":"Regarding yesterday’s incident, a spokesperson said that the police have no evidence at this stage","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"mais que plusieurs pistes sont envisagées. Une enquête a été ouverte par le parquet de Nice.","en":"but that several avenues are being considered. An investigation has been opened by the public prosecutor’s office in Nice.","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Les activités vont reprendre à l’usine Dol après une semaine à l’arrêt à cause de dysfonctionnements techniques.","en":"Business will resume at the Dol factory after a week’s shutdown due to technical malfunctions.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"D’après un employé : « Nous répétons depuis des mois qu’il y a un problème au niveau du refroidissement,","en":"According to one employee: “We have been saying for months that there is a problem with the cooling system,","lit":"repeating; at the level of"},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"mais on n’a rien voulu entendre. J’aimerais connaître le fond de leur pensée !","en":"but they didn’t want to hear about it. I would like to know what they were thinking!","lit":"the bottom of their thought"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant on admet du bout des lèvres qu’il y a effectivement “un souci” – mais c’est un peu tard. »","en":"Now they reluctantly admit that there is indeed a ‘concern’ – but it’s a bit late.”","lit":"from the tip of the lips"},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"Le Salon de l’Agriculture – « la plus grande ferme de France » – ouvre ses portes au public à partir de lundi.","en":"The Salon de l’Agriculture – “France’s biggest farm” – opens its doors to the public on Monday.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Veaux, vaches, moutons, cochons et taureaux seront au rendez-vous, pour la plus grande joie des visiteurs, grands et petits.","en":"Calves, cows, sheep, pigs and bulls will be there, to the delight of visitors big and small.","lit":"greatest joy"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Alors que le nombre d’agriculteurs ne cesse de diminuer et que les prévisions sont pessimistes,","en":"While the number of farmers continues to decrease and the forecasts are pessimistic,","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"le secteur du bio montre de nombreux signes de dynamisme, d’après les organisateurs.","en":"the organic sector is showing many signs of dynamism, according to the organisers.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":true,"fr":"Place à la culture. Koffi Wemba – retenez son nom – est la nouvelle vedette de la scène musicale francophone.","en":"Time for culture. Koffi Wemba – remember that name – is the new star of the French-speaking music scene.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Son dernier clip, Je me débrouille, dévoilé hier, a déjà été visionné près de deux millions de fois.","en":"His latest clip, Je me débrouille (I Get By), released yesterday, has already been viewed nearly two million times.","lit":"unveiled"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"On perçoit un sourire malicieux dans le regard du musicien lorsqu’il confie : « Je ne suis pas encore une star, mais j’ai de l’espoir. »","en":"You can make out a mischievous smile in the musician’s eyes when he confides: “I’m not a star yet, but I’m hopeful.”","lit":"perceive; have hope"},{"i":22,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous aimerions tous pouvoir nous débrouiller comme lui !","en":"We all wish we could get by as well as he does!","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Flash de dernière minute : un carambolage vient de se produire sur l’autoroute en direction de Nice.","en":"Here’s a last-minute newsflash: a pile-up has just occurred on the motorway to Nice.","lit":"in direction of"},{"i":24,"dash":false,"fr":"D’après la gendarmerie, il y aurait plusieurs victimes, dont deux seraient dans un état grave.","en":"According to the gendarmerie, there are reportedly several victims, two of whom are in a serious state.","lit":"would be"},{"i":25,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous vous tiendrons informés. Restez à l’antenne pour notre émission-débat : Aux quatre coins de l’Hexagone.","en":"We’ll keep you informed. Stay on the air for our talk show All Over France (Four Corners of the Hexagon).","lit":"programme-debate"}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"l’actualité, current affairs, also means news, while les actualités is a news broadcast/bulletin. On TV and online, the latter is called le journal télévisé, or “televised newspaper,” often abbreviated simply to le JT ([zhee-tay]). Each item in the bulletin is called un titre, a title or, here, a headline."},{"ref":"2","text":"The regular verb dérober has more or less the same meaning as voler, to steal (lesson 27 note 1) but includes the notion of stealth, like to purloin. It always refers to the theft of objects (i.e. not personal robbery). The adjective dérobé also means concealed or hidden: un escalier dérobé, a concealed staircase, often found in châteaux."},{"ref":"3","text":"In journalism, une chronique is a regular column or section in a news medium (un chroniqueur, a columnist). News-writers use an array of set phrases, including défrayer (literally to pay or settle) la chronique, the equivalent of to make headlines. (Chronique is also an adjective, meaning chronic, une maladie chronique, a chronic illness.)"},{"ref":"4","text":"Derived from the verb casser, to break, la casse means breakage, damage: Il y a toujours un peu de casse pendant un déménagement, There’s always a bit of breakage during a removal. By extension, la casse means damage in the broad sense: S’il n’y a pas d’aide gouvernementale, il y aura de la casse dans ce secteur, Without government assistance, the industry will be hard-hit. In familiar or journalistic French, however, the noun is masculine (un casse) and means a robbery, a heist, etc."},{"ref":"5","text":"un parquet is a wooden – or parquet – floor. In mediaeval times, it was the courtroom floor where magistrates would stand during a trial. Today le parquet is the rough equivalent of the public prosecutor’s department, organised regionally around major cities, e.g. le parquet de Lyon. (Unlike magistrates, the justices in mediaeval courts were seated, assis, whence la Cour d’Assises and the English word assizes.)"},{"ref":"6","text":"une lèvre, a lip, is used in physical descriptions: J’ai les lèvres gercées, My lips are cracked, but also in expressions, notably du bout des lèvres (“from the tip of the lips”), reluctantly, without conviction. Look a little further, though, and we recognise a similar expression to pay lip service to: Il n’a donné son accord que du bout des lèvres, He only paid lip service to an agreement."},{"ref":"7","text":"un agriculteur, a farmer, is now the accepted term for this profession, rather than un fermier. However, the latter is widely used as an adjective: les produits fermiers, fresh farm produce. (In the age of industrial agriculture, the noun un paysan, from le pays, the country, literally a peasant, and the identical adjective have returned to the language to signify small-scale, sustainable farming, l’agriculture paysanne.)"},{"ref":"8","text":"The “correct” French word for a star (movies, sports, etc.) is une vedette (lesson 55 line 8), from the Latin verb for to see. However, many French speakers use the loan word une star (always feminine, even if the celebrity is masculine). The word can also be used as an adjective: Jean Gabin et Michèle Morgan formaient le couple star du cinéma français dans les années trente : Jean Gabin and Michèle Morgan were French cinema’s star couple in the 1930s. (The word for a star as a celestial body, a restaurant accolade and other contexts is une étoile, see lesson 27 line 5.)"},{"ref":"9","text":"brouiller means to mix, muddle, etc. Je déteste les œufs brouillés, I hate scrambled eggs. So, logically, débrouiller means to untangle. But the pronominal verb se débrouiller means to manage, get by, etc. (i.e. “unscramble” a difficult situation): Il faudra que tu te débrouilles tout seul, You’ll have to get by on your own. Yet again, context is all-important because the possible translations are very numerous. (The adjective débrouillard means resourceful, smart, street-wise, etc.)"},{"ref":"10","text":"The conditional mood (lesson 70 note 1) can be used if the information conveyed by the verb is unconfirmed or conjectural. This is common in media reports about events yet to be verified. To translate, we use the indicative along with an adverb of supposition, like reportedly or apparently: Selon un porte-parole, l’accident serait dû à une erreur humaine, According to a spokesperson, the accident is apparently due to human error."},{"ref":"","text":"Like their English and American counterparts, many French-language journalists rely on tried-and-tested turns of phrase when reporting the news. In addition to défrayer la chronique and disparaître dans la nature, these stock expressions include être à la Une (to be front-page news), tirer la sonnette d’alarme (to ring alarm bells), caracoler en tête des sondages (to ride high in the polls), revoir sa copie (to go back to the drawing board), renverser la vapeur (“to reverse the steam,” i.e. change direction) and, most puzzlingly, aux quatre coins de l’Hexagone (“the four corners of the hexagon,” i.e. all over France, see lesson 90)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Selon le parquet, mon client aurait volé une voiture et dérobé du matériel d’une très grande valeur.","a":"According to the public prosecutor’s office, my client stole a car and pilfered very valuable material."},{"q":"La jeune star Milo se débrouille plutôt bien dans le film, même si on le sent moins à l’aise dans certaines scènes.","a":"The young star Milo manages quite well in the movie, even if we feel he’s less at ease in some scenes."},{"q":"Le Salon du Livre ouvre à partir de la semaine prochaine, pour la plus grande joie des lectrices et des lecteurs.","a":"The Book Fair opens next week, much to the delight of readers."},{"q":"J’ai été sidéré par leur réaction : ils ont accepté de nous aider, mais du bout des lèvres.","a":"I was amazed by their answer: they agreed to help us, but only reluctantly."},{"q":"Pour dire le fond de ma pensée, la question est loin d’être aussi simple.","a":"To say what I really think, the issue is not nearly that simple."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"What scares us when we travel is having our backpacks stolen.","t":"Ce qui {{nous fait peur quand}} on voyage, c’est de {{se faire voler}} nos {{sacs à dos}}."},{"cue":"According to the news, the accidents are apparently due to human error, but the director reportedly hid the truth.","t":"{{Selon le JT}}, les accidents {{seraient dus à}} des erreurs {{humaines}} mais le directeur {{aurait caché la vérité}}."},{"cue":"During the robbery, there was some damage – not too much – but the theft nevertheless hit the headlines.","t":"{{Pendant le casse}}, il y a eu {{de la casse}} – pas trop – mais {{le vol}} a néanmoins {{défrayé la chronique}}."},{"cue":"The new stars of hip-hop have released their new album, Concealed Doors.","t":"{{Les nouvelles stars}} du hip-hop {{ont dévoilé}} leur {{nouvel album}} : Des {{portes dérobées}}."},{"cue":"We kept telling them that there were signs of hope, but they wouldn’t hear of it.","t":"Nous {{n’avons cessé de leur dire}} qu’il y avait des signes {{d’espoir}}, mais {{ils n’ont rien voulu entendre}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"un cambriolage","en":"a burglary","zh":"入室盗窃","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “cam-brio-large”: a huge burglar in a convertible Cambrio carrying a large sack","ex":"Un cambriolage a eu lieu hier aux alentours de minuit dans un palace de la Côte d’Azur :","exEn":"A burglary took place yesterday around midnight in a luxury hotel on the Riviera:","src":2},{"fr":"aux alentours de minuit","en":"around midnight","zh":"午夜前后","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Un cambriolage a eu lieu hier aux alentours de minuit dans un palace de la Côte d’Azur :","exEn":"A burglary took place yesterday around midnight in a luxury hotel on the Riviera:","src":2},{"fr":"un collier de perles","en":"a pearl necklace","zh":"珍珠项链","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ pearl","ff":"","cluster":"clothes","kw":"","ex":"un collier de perles rares a été dérobé dans le coffre-fort d’une suite de cet hôtel luxueux,","exEn":"a necklace of rare pearls was stolen from the safe of a suite in this luxurious hotel,","src":3},{"fr":"un porte-parole","en":"a spokesperson","zh":"发言人","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Concernant l’incident d’hier, un porte-parole précise que la police ne dispose d’aucun indice à ce stade,","exEn":"Regarding yesterday’s incident, a spokesperson said that the police have no evidence at this stage","src":9},{"fr":"une piste","en":"a lead, an avenue (of inquiry)","zh":"线索；调查方向","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais que plusieurs pistes sont envisagées. Une enquête a été ouverte par le parquet de Nice.","exEn":"but that several avenues are being considered. An investigation has been opened by the public prosecutor’s office in Nice.","src":10},{"fr":"une enquête a été ouverte","en":"an investigation has been opened","zh":"已展开调查","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"mais que plusieurs pistes sont envisagées. Une enquête a été ouverte par le parquet de Nice.","exEn":"but that several avenues are being considered. An investigation has been opened by the public prosecutor’s office in Nice.","src":10},{"fr":"D’après un employé","en":"according to an employee","zh":"据一名员工说","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"D’après un employé : « Nous répétons depuis des mois qu’il y a un problème au niveau du refroidissement,","exEn":"According to one employee: “We have been saying for months that there is a problem with the cooling system,","src":12},{"fr":"du bout des lèvres","en":"reluctantly, half-heartedly","zh":"勉强地；言不由衷地","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant on admet du bout des lèvres qu’il y a effectivement “un souci” – mais c’est un peu tard. »","exEn":"Now they reluctantly admit that there is indeed a ‘concern’ – but it’s a bit late.”","src":14},{"fr":"un souci","en":"a worry, a concern, a problem","zh":"烦恼；问题","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant on admet du bout des lèvres qu’il y a effectivement “un souci” – mais c’est un peu tard. »","exEn":"Now they reluctantly admit that there is indeed a ‘concern’ – but it’s a bit late.”","src":14},{"fr":"pour la plus grande joie des visiteurs","en":"to the great delight of the visitors","zh":"令游客们格外高兴","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Veaux, vaches, moutons, cochons et taureaux seront au rendez-vous, pour la plus grande joie des visiteurs, grands et petits.","exEn":"Calves, cows, sheep, pigs and bulls will be there, to the delight of visitors big and small.","src":16},{"fr":"le nombre d’agriculteurs ne cesse de diminuer","en":"the number of farmers keeps falling","zh":"农民人数不断减少","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Alors que le nombre d’agriculteurs ne cesse de diminuer et que les prévisions sont pessimistes,","exEn":"While the number of farmers continues to decrease and the forecasts are pessimistic,","src":17},{"fr":"le secteur du bio","en":"the organic sector","zh":"有机产业","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ bio = organic","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"le secteur du bio montre de nombreux signes de dynamisme, d’après les organisateurs.","exEn":"the organic sector is showing many signs of dynamism, according to the organisers.","src":18},{"fr":"une vedette","en":"a star (film, music, sport…)","zh":"明星；名人","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Place à la culture. Koffi Wemba – retenez son nom – est la nouvelle vedette de la scène musicale francophone.","exEn":"Time for culture. Koffi Wemba – remember that name – is the new star of the French-speaking music scene.","src":19},{"fr":"je me débrouille","en":"I get by, I manage","zh":"我自己想办法应付","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Son dernier clip, Je me débrouille, dévoilé hier, a déjà été visionné près de deux millions de fois.","exEn":"His latest clip, Je me débrouille (I Get By), released yesterday, has already been viewed nearly two million times.","src":20},{"fr":"nous vous tiendrons informés","en":"we will keep you informed","zh":"我们会随时向您通报","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous vous tiendrons informés. Restez à l’antenne pour notre émission-débat : Aux quatre coins de l’Hexagone.","exEn":"We’ll keep you informed. Stay on the air for our talk show All Over France (Four Corners of the Hexagon).","src":25}],"uncertain":["dialogue 25: 'Aux quatre coins de l’Hexagone' kept without quotation marks as in the OCR","note 4: '(un casse)' added by me for clarity of gender; the book only says the noun is masculine"],"pdfPage":601,"gaps":[[2.04,3.94],[5.04,7.92],[46.62,47.76],[58.16,59.2],[144.3,147.76],[149.9,151.96],[158.18,161.4],[168.14,171.16],[176.74,179.76],[184.56,187.7],[191.6,195.64]],"dur":195.6},{"n":96,"kind":"lesson","title":"Une émission-débat : « Réinventons le rire »","titleEn":"A talk show: “Let’s re-invent laughter”","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Peut-on rire de tout ? C’est le sujet de notre débat ce soir, avec deux invités : un psychiatre et un jeune humoriste.","en":"Can we laugh at everything? That’s the subject of tonight’s debate, with two guests: a psychiatrist and a young comedian.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Mm… je sens qu’on va bien rigoler. Mon petit doigt me dit que ce type est aussi drôle qu’une feuille d’impôts !","en":"Mm… I feel we’re in for a good laugh. A little birdie tells me this guy’s as funny as a tax form!","lit":"my little finger"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Voyez-vous, mon jeune ami, c’est toute la différence entre le monde du spectacle et le monde des arts.","en":"You see, my young friend, that’s the difference between the entertainment world and the arts world.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Un film, on peut le voir et puis le revoir à l’infini, sans qu’il perde sa fraîcheur.","en":"You can see a film and then watch it over and over again, without it losing its freshness.","lit":"endlessly"},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais une histoire drôle, une plaisanterie, une boutade – appelez-la comme vous voudrez –","en":"But a funny story, a joke, a witticism – call it what you will –","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"est périmée dès qu’on l’entend et ne supporte pas la répétition. Il faut renouveler constamment.","en":"is out of date as soon as you hear it and doesn’t bear repetition. You have to constantly renew.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":true,"fr":"Désolé de te contredire, mais tu t’es enfermé dans ton petit monde, qui, lui, est fermé à double tour.","en":"Sorry to contradict you, but you’ve shut yourself up in your little world, which is double-locked.","lit":"closed at two turns"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Une bonne blague doit faire rire et réfléchir en même temps, tu vois.","en":"A good joke should make people laugh and think at the same time, you see.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Je ne vous ai pas autorisé à me tutoyer, jeune homme. Un peu de politesse, je vous prie.","en":"I didn’t authorise you to use the familiar form of address, young man. A little politeness, please.","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"La jeunesse est une belle chose ; quel crime de la laisser gâcher par les jeunes !","en":"Youth is a beautiful thing; what a crime to let it be spoiled by the young!","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu empoches une prime à chaque fois que tu recycles les bons vieux gags ?","en":"Do you pocket a bonus every time you recycle the good old gags?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Ne perdons pas de vue notre thème. Peut-on – doit-on – trouver de l’humour dans tout ?","en":"Let’s not lose sight of our theme. Can we – should we – find humour in everything?","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"La grossesse, la vieillesse, la richesse : ne faudrait-il pas plutôt de la gentillesse et de la tendresse ?","en":"Pregnancy, old age, wealth: shouldn’t we have kindness and affection instead?","lit":"tenderness"},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Faut-il s’affoler si quelqu’un plaisante sur des étrangers, par exemple ?","en":"Should we panic if someone jokes about foreigners, for example?","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":true,"fr":"On se permet de taquiner les Lillois, les Alsaciens, les Toulousains, ou les Marseillais.","en":"We allow ourselves to tease the people of Lille, Alsace, Toulouse, or Marseille.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"Alors pourquoi pas des gens plus lointains ? Ce n’est pas de la méchanceté, c’est de la rigolade !","en":"So why not people further away? It’s not meanness, it’s fun!","lit":"more distant"},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Non : vous rhabillez de vieux préjugés avec des mots nouveaux, monsieur.","en":"No: you are dressing up old prejudices with new words, sir.","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":true,"fr":"Et je te dis qu’il faut se fier à ses instincts et se méfier comme de la peste des idées reçues.","en":"And I’m telling you that you must trust your instincts and be wary of preconceived ideas like the plague.","lit":"as of the plague"},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"L’humour adoucit les mœurs et allège notre quotidien grisâtre, je te – je vous – le garantis.","en":"Humour softens our manners and lightens our greyish daily lives – I guarantee you.","lit":"softens"},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Notre débat touche à sa fin, messieurs, et nous ne disposons pas de suffisamment d’éléments","en":"Our debate is coming to an end, gentlemen, and we don’t have enough information","lit":"elements"},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"pour arriver à une conclusion ferme et définitive, mais…","en":"to come to a firm and final conclusion, but…","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Si si. Tu sais ce qu’on dit : on peut rire de tout – mais pas avec tout le monde.","en":"Oh yes we do. You know what they say: you can laugh at everything – but not with everyone.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(1) In contrast to English, the initial p in Greek-origin French words such as psychiatre is pronounced, hence [pseekee-ahtr]."},{"ref":"pron","text":"(19) In the plural noun les mœurs, the final -s can be voiced ([muhrss]) or silent ([muhr])."},{"ref":"1","text":"The regular verb rigoler is a synonym of rire, to laugh. Though colloquial, it is widely used in many situations: Il n’y a pas de quoi rigoler. C’est triste ! There’s nothing to laugh about. It’s sad! The derivative adjective is rigolo(te) – Ton article était très rigolo, Your article was very funny – which can also be used as a noun: Malgré son air timide, c’est un vrai rigolo, Although he seems shy, he’s a really funny guy. The noun la rigolade means a bit of fun / a good laugh (see line 16)."},{"ref":"2","text":"These three nouns are synonyms for ways of provoking laughter: une histoire drôle, a funny story, une plaisanterie, a joke (from plaisant, pleasing), and une boutade, a witticism. In a more familiar register, the standard noun for a joke is une blague (see lesson 68 line 11)."},{"ref":"3","text":"This lesson gives more examples of how verbs are modified by prefixes. Thus fermer, to close, becomes enfermer, to shut in, lock in: Ma fille s’est enfermée dans sa chambre, My daughter has locked herself in her bedroom. Another, similar prefix is em-, often used to transform nouns into verbs: une poche ➔ empocher, a pocket, to put in one’s pocket. Il a empoché une commission, He pocketed a commission. And mé- has the same function as mal, indicating negativity, badness, etc., like mis- in English: se fier ➔ se méfier, to trust, to mistrust / be wary of (line 18)."},{"ref":"4","text":"This word is a faux ami. Une prime means a bonus or subsidy (it’s also the origin of a premium). The translation is context-dependent: une prime de fin d’année is a year-end bonus, whereas une prime de mobilité is a mobility allowance. But whatever the context, the underlying meaning is the same. However, the verb primer means to award a prize to, Le film a été primé à Cannes, The movie was awarded a prize at the Cannes film festival."},{"ref":"5","text":"As you should know by now, the suffix -esse is added to feminine adjectives in order to form nouns. For example: riche ➔ la richesse, wealth; tendre ➔ la tendresse, tenderness; vieille ➔ la vieillesse, old age. However, la grossesse means pregnancy. (Added to a masculine noun, of course, -esse creates the feminine: maître ➔ maîtresse.)"},{"ref":"6","text":"The prefix a- is added to nouns, adjectives and verbs to convey movement towards a particular state or being. Thus, folle (from fou, mad) gives us s’affoler (to lose one’s head, panic), douce (from doux, gentle) ➔ adoucir, to soften (un adoucissant, a softener). Likewise, légère (from léger) ➔ alléger, to lighten. (In some cases, the initial letter of the adjective is doubled, see lesson 98.)"},{"ref":"7","text":"The rules governing adjectives and nouns denoting a person’s country of origin or their residence are quite complex. The usual endings are -ain(e): (américain-e), -ais(e): (français-e), -ien(ne): (parisien-ne), -ois(e): (strasbourgeois-e) and -on(ne): (breton-ne)."},{"ref":"8","text":"We know that re- is the “repeat” prefix. But since the initial h in habiller is a half-vowel, the e is omitted: Rhabillez-vous, Put your clothes back on, Get dressed again."},{"ref":"9","text":"la peste, the plague, is used in idiomatic expressions with two verbs: éviter quelqu’un comme la peste, to avoid someone like the plague, and se méfier de quelqu’un comme de la peste, to be wary of / to mistrust someone. Less dramatically, la peste can also be applied to a person who is a nuisance or … a pest: Arrête de la taquiner, tu es une vraie petite peste, Stop teasing her, you really are a little pest."},{"ref":"10","text":"The feminine noun les mœurs, always plural, means morals. Cette action est contraire aux bonnes mœurs, That act is contrary to good morals. (La police des mœurs is roughly the equivalent of the vice squad.) More broadly, the noun means customs, manners or mores: Le gouvernement est attentif aux cultures et aux mœurs des nouveaux citoyens, The government is attentive to the cultures and mores of new citizens. (See Pronunciation Notes.)"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"J’ai entendu une histoire très rigolote l’autre jour, mais je l’ai déjà oubliée. Je ne m’en souviens jamais.","a":"I heard a very funny story the other day, but I’ve already forgotten it. I never remember them."},{"q":"Mon psychiatre m’a demandé de le tutoyer, mais il vaut mieux que je continue à le vouvoyer.","a":"My psychiatrist asked me to address him with the familiar tu, but it’s better I keep on using the formal vous."},{"q":"Zut, mon passeport est périmé. J’ai oublié de le renouveler. Ça va gâcher nos vacances !","a":"Damn, my passport has expired. I forgot to renew it. That’s going to ruin our holiday!"},{"q":"Ma copine est enceinte de six mois et elle ne supporte plus de rester debout dans les transports en commun.","a":"My girlfriend is six months’ pregnant and she can’t bear standing up on public transport anymore."},{"q":"Mon petit doigt me dit que quelqu’un va empocher une grosse prime à la fin du mois.","a":"A little birdie tells me that someone is going to pocket a big bonus at the end of the month."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"He’s too old and even too nice. – I would remind you that neither old age nor even kindness is a crime.","t":"Il est trop {{vieux}} et même trop {{gentil}}. – Je te {{rappelle}} que ni {{la vieillesse}}, ni {{la gentillesse}} ne {{sont}} des {{crimes}}."},{"cue":"I’m going to shut Marc in his bedroom, double-lock the door, and pocket the key.","t":"Je vais {{enfermer}} Marc dans sa chambre, {{fermer}} la porte {{à double tour}}, et {{empocher la clé}}."},{"cue":"You know that a joke is funny if you laugh and think at the same time when you hear it.","t":"Vous savez qu’{{une plaisanterie}} est {{drôle}} si vous {{riez}} et {{réfléchissez}} en même temps quand vous {{l’entendez}}."},{"cue":"We’re nearing the end of the show. We have only a few minutes to reach a final conclusion.","t":"Cette émission {{touche à sa fin}}. Nous {{ne disposons que de}} quelques minutes pour {{arriver à une conclusion définitive}}."},{"cue":"You can laugh at anything, but not with everyone, so it’s necessary to rely on one’s instincts.","t":"Vous {{pouvez rire de tout}}, mais {{pas avec tout le monde}}, donc il faut {{se fier à ses instincts}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"je sens qu’on va bien rigoler","en":"I feel we’re in for a good laugh","zh":"我感觉我们要笑个够了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mm… je sens qu’on va bien rigoler. Mon petit doigt me dit que ce type est aussi drôle qu’une feuille d’impôts !","exEn":"Mm… I feel we’re in for a good laugh. A little birdie tells me this guy’s as funny as a tax form!","src":2},{"fr":"mon petit doigt me dit que","en":"a little bird tells me that","zh":"我隐约感觉到……（直译：我的小指告诉我）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mm… je sens qu’on va bien rigoler. Mon petit doigt me dit que ce type est aussi drôle qu’une feuille d’impôts !","exEn":"Mm… I feel we’re in for a good laugh. A little birdie tells me this guy’s as funny as a tax form!","src":2},{"fr":"à l’infini","en":"endlessly, over and over","zh":"无穷无尽地；一遍又一遍","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"≈ infinity","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Un film, on peut le voir et puis le revoir à l’infini, sans qu’il perde sa fraîcheur.","exEn":"You can see a film and then watch it over and over again, without it losing its freshness.","src":4},{"fr":"Désolé de te contredire","en":"Sorry to contradict you","zh":"抱歉反驳你","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ contradict","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Désolé de te contredire, mais tu t’es enfermé dans ton petit monde, qui, lui, est fermé à double tour.","exEn":"Sorry to contradict you, but you’ve shut yourself up in your little world, which is double-locked.","src":7},{"fr":"fermé à double tour","en":"double-locked","zh":"上了双重锁的；锁得严严实实","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Désolé de te contredire, mais tu t’es enfermé dans ton petit monde, qui, lui, est fermé à double tour.","exEn":"Sorry to contradict you, but you’ve shut yourself up in your little world, which is double-locked.","src":7},{"fr":"une bonne blague","en":"a good joke","zh":"一个好笑话","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Une bonne blague doit faire rire et réfléchir en même temps, tu vois.","exEn":"A good joke should make people laugh and think at the same time, you see.","src":8},{"fr":"un peu de politesse, je vous prie","en":"a little politeness, please","zh":"请您礼貌一点","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ politeness","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je ne vous ai pas autorisé à me tutoyer, jeune homme. Un peu de politesse, je vous prie.","exEn":"I didn’t authorise you to use the familiar form of address, young man. A little politeness, please.","src":9},{"fr":"la jeunesse","en":"youth","zh":"青春；青年时代","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ youth (jeune = young)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La jeunesse est une belle chose ; quel crime de la laisser gâcher par les jeunes !","exEn":"Youth is a beautiful thing; what a crime to let it be spoiled by the young!","src":10},{"fr":"une prime","en":"a bonus","zh":"奖金；补贴","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"une prime = a bonus/subsidy, NOT “a prime”","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Tu empoches une prime à chaque fois que tu recycles les bons vieux gags ?","exEn":"Do you pocket a bonus every time you recycle the good old gags?","src":11},{"fr":"ne perdons pas de vue notre thème","en":"let’s not lose sight of our theme","zh":"我们别忘了我们的主题","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ theme","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ne perdons pas de vue notre thème. Peut-on – doit-on – trouver de l’humour dans tout ?","exEn":"Let’s not lose sight of our theme. Can we – should we – find humour in everything?","src":12},{"fr":"la gentillesse","en":"kindness","zh":"善良；亲切","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"La grossesse, la vieillesse, la richesse : ne faudrait-il pas plutôt de la gentillesse et de la tendresse ?","exEn":"Pregnancy, old age, wealth: shouldn’t we have kindness and affection instead?","src":13},{"fr":"taquiner","en":"to tease","zh":"逗弄；开玩笑","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “tack-in-ay”: you tease a friend by putting a tack on their chair","ex":"On se permet de taquiner les Lillois, les Alsaciens, les Toulousains, ou les Marseillais.","exEn":"We allow ourselves to tease the people of Lille, Alsace, Toulouse, or Marseille.","src":15},{"fr":"se fier à ses instincts","en":"to trust one’s instincts","zh":"相信自己的直觉","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ instinct","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et je te dis qu’il faut se fier à ses instincts et se méfier comme de la peste des idées reçues.","exEn":"And I’m telling you that you must trust your instincts and be wary of preconceived ideas like the plague.","src":18},{"fr":"notre débat touche à sa fin","en":"our debate is coming to an end","zh":"我们的辩论接近尾声","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Notre débat touche à sa fin, messieurs, et nous ne disposons pas de suffisamment d’éléments","exEn":"Our debate is coming to an end, gentlemen, and we don’t have enough information","src":20},{"fr":"on peut rire de tout","en":"you can laugh at everything","zh":"什么事都可以拿来笑","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Si si. Tu sais ce qu’on dit : on peut rire de tout – mais pas avec tout le monde.","exEn":"Oh yes we do. You know what they say: you can laugh at everything – but not with everyone.","src":22}],"uncertain":["pronunciation note labelled (10) in the OCR was assigned to line 19 (mœurs)","dialogue 2 vs 3 speaker assignment follows the OCR dash marks"],"pdfPage":609,"gaps":[[1.98,3.96],[7.2,10.08],[23.02,24.04],[124.52,128.92],[131.02,133.1],[139.9,142.9],[147.84,150.78],[156.8,159.84],[164.36,167.38],[172.12,175.94]],"dur":175.9},{"n":97,"kind":"lesson","title":"En plein dans le mille !","titleEn":"Bull’s-eye! (in full in the thousand)","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Aurélien Fauré dirige une jeune pousse normande spécialisée dans l’équipement automobile,","en":"Aurélien Fauré manages a Normandy-based start-up specialising in automotive equipment,","lit":"young shoot"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"qui a mis au point un dispositif pour remplacer les pneus classiques par un coussin d’air. Reportage.","en":"which has developed a system to replace conventional tyres by an air cushion. Here’s the story.","lit":"report"},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Au début, nous avons travaillé sans filet car le projet était innovant et nous n’avions qu’une vision partielle du produit.","en":"In the beginning, we worked without a (safety) net because the project was innovative and we only had a partial vision of the product.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Jusqu’en juin dernier, ayant fait un vrai parcours sans faute, nous avions le vent en poupe.","en":"Until last June, after a really perfect performance, we had the wind in our sails.","lit":"having made; without fault; course"},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"N’avez-vous pas placé la barre trop haut, en cherchant à remplacer quelque chose d’aussi rudimentaire qu’un pneumatique ?","en":"Didn’t you set the bar too high by seeking to replace something as rudimentary as a tyre?","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Au départ, nous avons voulu brûler les étapes pour coiffer nos concurrents au poteau.","en":"At the outset we wanted to cut corners in order to pip our competitors at the post.","lit":"departure; burn the steps; do the hair of"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avions le nez dans le guidon, si bien que nous avons failli déclarer forfait au bout de six mois,","en":"We were totally immersed in what we were doing, so much so that we almost gave up after six months,","lit":"we had the nose in the handlebars; declare forfeit"},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"car les choses se sont compliquées. Je pensais alors que nous avions fait un faux départ.","en":"because things got complicated. I thought then that we had made a false start.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"J’étais même prêt à jeter l’éponge car le projet devenait de plus en plus coûteux.","en":"I was even ready to throw in the towel because the project was getting more and more expensive.","lit":"sponge"},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais mes équipes ont refusé de changer de cap :","en":"But my teams refused to change course:","lit":"cape"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"chaque fois que je leur disais qu’il fallait tout arrêter, ils m’ont renvoyé dans les cordes.","en":"every time I told them they had to stop everything, they sent me packing.","lit":"(re)sent me in the ropes"},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"Je me rends compte maintenant qu’ils ont reculé pour mieux sauter.","en":"I realise now that they were taking a step back in order to better move forward.","lit":"jump"},{"i":13,"dash":true,"fr":"Vous avez soumis un prototype au Fonds régional de capital-investissement, n’est-ce pas ?","en":"You submitted a prototype to the Regional Private Equity Fund, didn’t you?","lit":"investment capital"},{"i":14,"dash":true,"fr":"Oui. Mais il a botté en touche, sous prétexte que les fonds nécessaires n’étaient plus disponibles","en":"Yes. But they sidestepped the issue, on the pretext that the necessary funds were no longer available","lit":"under pretext"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"car un autre projet similaire était plus avancé que le nôtre.","en":"because another similar project was more advanced than ours.","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":true,"fr":"Ça alors ! Qu’avez-vous fait ?","en":"Gosh! What did you do?","lit":""},{"i":17,"dash":true,"fr":"Rebelote. Nous avons modifié légèrement le dossier et l’avons présenté à nouveau,","en":"The same as before. We slightly modified the file and resubmitted it,","lit":"present once-again"},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"mais cette fois-ci au Ministère de l’innovation.","en":"but this time to the Ministry of Innovation.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Là, nous avons visé en plein dans le mille ! Le ministre ne pouvait pas rester sur le banc sans rien faire.","en":"There we hit the bull’s-eye! The minister couldn’t sit on his hands and do nothing.","lit":"aimed; the bench"},{"i":20,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant que nos résultats initiaux sont convaincants, la balle est dans leur camp.","en":"Now that our initial results are convincing, the ball is in their court.","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":false,"fr":"Et je suis sûr que nous allons tirer notre épingle du jeu. C’est la dernière ligne droite !","en":"And I’m sure we’re going to hold our own. We’re entering the home stretch!","lit":"pull our pin from the game; the last straight line"},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Alors ça, c’est formidable !","en":"That’s really great!","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":false,"fr":"On a failli vous mettre KO, mais vous avez encaissé les coups. Chapeau !","en":"They almost knocked you out, but you rolled with the punches. Hats off to you!","lit":"encashed the blows"}],"notes":[{"ref":"pron","text":"(2) (5) Like psychiatre and its derivatives (lesson 96), the initial p in words beginning with pn- is voiced. For example, to pronounce pneu, start by saying [puh-nuh], then gradually shorten the first vowel sound and run it into the n: [p-nuh]. The same applies, of course, for pneumatique."},{"ref":"1","text":"une pousse is a shoot or sprout (des pousses de bambou, bean sprouts). But the term une jeune pousse was coined to describe a newly formed company, in order to avoid the loan word une start-up."},{"ref":"2","text":"mettre au point has the same notion of precision as in faire le point (lesson 89 note 3). In photography, it means to focus. But in everyday French, the meaning is much broader: to develop, finalise, perfect, etc. and the translation changes with the context: Nous allons mettre au point de nouvelles formes d’énergie respectueuses de l’environnement, We are going to develop new, environment-friendly forms of energy. Don’t confuse the past tense (mis au point) with the feminine noun la mise au point, development, finalisation, etc."},{"ref":"3","text":"un dispositif (from disposer, to arrange) literally means an arrangement or layout. By construction, it refers to a mechanism or device (un dispositif de pointage, a pointing device, “mouse”). But the word is widely used in the media and advertising as a blanket term for all kinds of plans, systems, arrangements, etc. Un dispositif d’assurance can be an insurance system, scheme, measure (or set of measures), etc."},{"ref":"4","text":"The adjective pneumatique has the same meaning as its English cognate, but also means inflatable (un matelas pneumatique, an air bed). As a noun, it means a tyre and is almost always contracted to un pneu : Ma voiture n’est pas équipée de pneus neige, My car isn’t fitted with snow tyres. (See Pronunciation Notes.)"},{"ref":"5","text":"The intensifier si bien que indicates an outcome (so that, with the result that) described in a subordinate clause: Elle est arrivée en retard, si bien qu’elle a manqué le début du film, She arrived late and, as a result, missed the beginning of the movie. (In an elevated register, tant et si bien que is used, with no change in meaning.)"},{"ref":"6","text":"failli, the past participle of faillir (the origin of to fail), is used with an infinitive to express the idea of very nearly: Nous avons failli partir avant la fin, We very nearly left before the end. If you hear failli, always listen out for the following verb to find out what was almost done!"},{"ref":"7","text":"Ça alors ! (“that then”) is an exclamation of surprise or disbelief, like Goodness! or How about that! Used disapprovingly, it means Well, really! Finalement, il n’est pas venu à la fête, malgré ses promesses. – Ça, alors ! – In the end, he never came to the party, despite his promises. – Well really! Here, it’s the tone rather than the context that determines the meaning. Don’t confuse this exclamation with Alors ça… generally followed by c’est and a qualifier, which means simply Now that… Alors ça, c’est formidable ! Now that’s really good."},{"ref":"8","text":"La belote is a very popular French card game. Belote and Rebelote are the announcements made by a player taking two consecutive tricks. In colloquial speech, rebelote means something like here we go again, the same thing, etc. Deux heures pour aller au boulot le matin, et rebelote le soir pour rentrer, Two hours to get to work in the morning and the same in the evening to get home. (In “standard” French… et la même chose le soir, etc.)"},{"ref":"9","text":"KO or K-O, pronounced [ka-oh], is a direct, untranslated import from the world of boxing. Le poids lourd tunisien a gagné par KO, The Tunisian heavyweight won by a knockout. Outside the ring, être KO means to be worn out, shattered: Après une journée entière avec les gamins, je suis KO, After a whole day with the kids, I’m exhausted. The pronunciation is exactly the same as that of the noun le chaos, chaos. Be careful!"},{"ref":"10","text":"The expression tirer un coup de chapeau à means to take one’s hat off to, i.e. congratulate, acknowledge, etc. The expression is usually shortened simply to Chapeau ! or, in some cases, Chapeau bas ! (as if bowing deeply with hat in hand). While sometimes translatable by hats-off or hat-tip to, the actual choice of word or term will be context-driven (Well done!, Nice one!, etc.)."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Les étudiants ont mis au point un dispositif simple pour photographier des petits objets sans mise au point.","a":"The students developed a simple device for photographing small objects without focusing."},{"q":"J’ai le nez dans le guidon depuis des mois avec ce prototype car je ne veux pas qu’on me coiffe au poteau.","a":"I’ve had my nose to the grindstone for months with this prototype because I don’t want to be pipped at the post."},{"q":"Nous ne déclarons pas forfait. Nous reculons pour mieux sauter car nous ne voulons pas brûler les étapes.","a":"We are not giving up. We’re taking a step back because we don’t want to cut corners."},{"q":"Ils ont placé la barre très haut mais je pense qu’ils vont réussir.","a":"They’ve set the bar very high but I think they’re going to make it."},{"q":"Tu pensais que le candidat allait tirer son épingle du jeu, n’est-ce pas ?","a":"You thought the candidate was going to hold his own, didn’t you?"}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"You know that I nearly got funding from the ministry, but the minister preferred to sidestep the issue.","t":"Tu sais, j’ai {{failli obtenir des fonds}} du {{ministère}}, mais {{le ministre}} a préféré {{botter en touche}}."},{"cue":"Reza has lost his voice, so he has had to withdraw from this evening’s concert. – What a pity!","t":"Reza {{a perdu sa}} voix, {{si bien qu’il}} a dû {{déclarer forfait}} pour le concert de ce soir. – {{Quel dommage}} !"},{"cue":"Because we refused to change course, the project manager sent us packing.","t":"Puisque nous {{avons refusé de changer de cap}}, le responsable du projet {{nous a renvoyé dans les cordes}}."},{"cue":"Well done, you were bang on target. Now the ball is in their court.","t":"Bravo, vous {{avez visé dans le mille}}. Maintenant, {{la balle est dans leur camp}}."},{"cue":"I’ll be worn out if we continue working like this, without a safety net.","t":"{{Je vais être KO}} si {{nous continuons à travailler}} comme ça, {{sans filet}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"une jeune pousse","en":"a start-up (lit. a young shoot)","zh":"初创公司（字面：嫩芽）","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Aurélien Fauré dirige une jeune pousse normande spécialisée dans l’équipement automobile,","exEn":"Aurélien Fauré manages a Normandy-based start-up specialising in automotive equipment,","src":1},{"fr":"les pneus classiques","en":"conventional tyres","zh":"传统轮胎","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"qui a mis au point un dispositif pour remplacer les pneus classiques par un coussin d’air. Reportage.","exEn":"which has developed a system to replace conventional tyres by an air cushion. Here’s the story.","src":2},{"fr":"travailler sans filet","en":"to work without a safety net","zh":"毫无保障地工作","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au début, nous avons travaillé sans filet car le projet était innovant et nous n’avions qu’une vision partielle du produit.","exEn":"In the beginning, we worked without a (safety) net because the project was innovative and we only had a partial vision of the product.","src":3},{"fr":"avoir le vent en poupe","en":"to be on a roll (to have the wind in one’s sails)","zh":"顺风顺水；势头正劲","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Jusqu’en juin dernier, ayant fait un vrai parcours sans faute, nous avions le vent en poupe.","exEn":"Until last June, after a really perfect performance, we had the wind in our sails.","src":4},{"fr":"placer la barre trop haut","en":"to set the bar too high","zh":"把标准定得太高","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"N’avez-vous pas placé la barre trop haut, en cherchant à remplacer quelque chose d’aussi rudimentaire qu’un pneumatique ?","exEn":"Didn’t you set the bar too high by seeking to replace something as rudimentary as a tyre?","src":5},{"fr":"brûler les étapes","en":"to cut corners, to rush ahead","zh":"操之过急；跳过步骤","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Au départ, nous avons voulu brûler les étapes pour coiffer nos concurrents au poteau.","exEn":"At the outset we wanted to cut corners in order to pip our competitors at the post.","src":6},{"fr":"avoir le nez dans le guidon","en":"to be totally immersed in what one is doing","zh":"埋头苦干，顾不上其他","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avions le nez dans le guidon, si bien que nous avons failli déclarer forfait au bout de six mois,","exEn":"We were totally immersed in what we were doing, so much so that we almost gave up after six months,","src":7},{"fr":"nous avons failli déclarer forfait","en":"we almost gave up","zh":"我们差点就退出了","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avions le nez dans le guidon, si bien que nous avons failli déclarer forfait au bout de six mois,","exEn":"We were totally immersed in what we were doing, so much so that we almost gave up after six months,","src":7},{"fr":"jeter l’éponge","en":"to throw in the towel","zh":"认输；放弃","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’étais même prêt à jeter l’éponge car le projet devenait de plus en plus coûteux.","exEn":"I was even ready to throw in the towel because the project was getting more and more expensive.","src":9},{"fr":"changer de cap","en":"to change course","zh":"改变方向","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais mes équipes ont refusé de changer de cap :","exEn":"But my teams refused to change course:","src":10},{"fr":"reculer pour mieux sauter","en":"to take a step back in order to move forward better","zh":"以退为进","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je me rends compte maintenant qu’ils ont reculé pour mieux sauter.","exEn":"I realise now that they were taking a step back in order to better move forward.","src":12},{"fr":"Ça alors !","en":"Gosh! Well, really!","zh":"哎呀！真没想到！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Ça alors ! Qu’avez-vous fait ?","exEn":"Gosh! What did you do?","src":16},{"fr":"la balle est dans leur camp","en":"the ball is in their court","zh":"球在他们那边了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant que nos résultats initiaux sont convaincants, la balle est dans leur camp.","exEn":"Now that our initial results are convincing, the ball is in their court.","src":20},{"fr":"tirer son épingle du jeu","en":"to hold one’s own, to come out unscathed","zh":"全身而退；成功脱身","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et je suis sûr que nous allons tirer notre épingle du jeu. C’est la dernière ligne droite !","exEn":"And I’m sure we’re going to hold our own. We’re entering the home stretch!","src":21},{"fr":"Chapeau !","en":"Hats off! Well done!","zh":"佩服！干得漂亮！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"On a failli vous mettre KO, mais vous avez encaissé les coups. Chapeau !","exEn":"They almost knocked you out, but you rolled with the punches. Hats off to you!","src":23}],"uncertain":["note 9: the printed text of the end of this note is partly split by the page break; the sentence about the pronunciation of le chaos is placed at the end of note 9","ex1 item 2: answer printed as 'my nose to the grindstone'","ex2 item 1: gap 'failli obtenir des fonds' follows the answer key grouping"],"pdfPage":617,"gaps":[[1.82,3.88],[4.8,7.78],[26.6,27.8],[45.34,46.46],[53.2,54.24],[60.84,61.86],[73.5,74.7],[79.36,80.36],[124.46,128.34],[130.28,132.2],[137.62,140.8],[145.14,148.36],[154.36,157.5],[160.34,163.38],[166.62,170.82]],"dur":170.8},{"n":98,"kind":"revision","title":"Révision","titleEn":"Revision","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Qu’est-ce qu’il y a, Driss ? Tu as l’air complètement KO. Ça ne va pas ?","en":"What’s up, Driss? You look completely exhausted. Aren’t things going well?","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":true,"fr":"Si si, mais tout est sens dessus dessous en ce moment. J’ai le nez dans le guidon depuis cinq jours parce que j’écris un article sur les Dauphins.","en":"Yes they are, but everything is topsy-turvy at the moment. I’ve been totally immersed in things for five days because I’m writing an article about the Dolphins.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":true,"fr":"Cette équipe de foot dont le capitaine, Dulong, a disparu dans la nature en 1980 ?","en":"That football team whose captain, Dulong, disappeared into the wild in 1980?","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Je m’en souviens bien. C’est une histoire qui a défrayé la chronique à l’époque.","en":"I remember it well. It was a story that made the headlines at the time.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"C’est vrai, elle a fait couler beaucoup d’encre mais, d’après moi, on n’a jamais su toute la vérité.","en":"It’s true, a lot has been written about it, but in my opinion, we never knew the whole truth.","lit":""},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Je n’ai pas réussi à parler avec Dulong, ou, plutôt, il a exigé une somme faramineuse pour une interview.","en":"I didn’t manage to talk to Dulong, or rather, he demanded a huge sum of money for an interview.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Mais son ancien agent, Jacques Cartier, lui, a donné son accord tout de suite. Bingo !","en":"But his former agent, Jacques Cartier, agreed immediately. Bingo!","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"Je pensais qu’il allait accepter du bout des lèvres, mais pas du tout ! Il en avait très envie. J’aurais dû m’en méfier, tant il était enthousiaste.","en":"I thought he would do it reluctantly, but not at all! He really wanted to. I should have been suspicious, he was so enthusiastic.","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Je lui posais des questions vachement précises mais, à chaque fois, il bottait en touche. Il me racontait la vérité, soit. Mais sa vérité à lui !","en":"I asked him really precise questions, but each time he evaded the issue. He told me the truth, sure. But his truth!","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu aurais dû me demander de t’accompagner. « Au secours ! J’ai besoin d’aide ! »","en":"You should have asked me to come with you. “Help! I need assistance!”","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"Je serais venue tout de suite. Avec des mecs comme ça, c’est toujours le plus fort qui l’emporte.","en":"I would have come straight away. With guys like that, it’s always the strongest who wins.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"rev","title":"1 The passé simple","text":"Le passé simple, or passé historique, is almost never used in everyday conversation or communication but is worth learning in order to be able to read novels, non-fiction and, in some cases, the media. Known in English as the past historic or the preterite, the tense has exactly the same function as le passé composé.\n1.1 Formation\nTo form the past historic, remove the infinitive markers from the verb (-er, -ir, -re) and add the following endings:\n | fermer | finir | descendre\nje | fermai | finis | descendis\ntu | fermas | finis | descendis\nil/elle | ferma | finit | descendit\nnous | fermâmes | finîmes | descendîmes\nvous | fermâtes | finîtes | descendîtes\nils/elles | fermèrent | finirent | descendirent\nNote that -ir and -re verbs share the same endings.\nOf course, there are several irregular forms. Here are the main verbs:\n | être | avoir | faire\nje / j’ | fus | eus | fis\ntu / t’ | fus | eus | fis\nil/elle | fut | eut | fit\nnous | fûmes | eûmes | fîmes\nvous | fûtes | eûtes | fîtes\nils/elles | furent | eurent | firent\nIn all three cases, the initial vowel of the first and second persons plural takes a circumflex. However, don’t confuse fut with fût, the (increasingly rare) imperfect subjunctive, or with the noun un fût, meaning, among other things, a barrel: Vin élevé en fût de chêne, Wine aged in oak barrels, used much more frequently.\n1.2 Use\nThe passé simple is used to describe a completed, discrete action in the past that has no link to or is unimportant in the present:\nElle fut choisie par le roi, qui l’épousa. She was chosen by the king, who married her.\nToute la Cour fit un grand banquet. The entire court held a huge banquet.\nLe roi et sa reine eurent quatre enfants ensemble. The king and his queen had four children together.\nBut despite the “historic” tag, the tense is not confined to history books. Here’s a passage from a much-loved novel, Le Petit Prince, “The Little Prince,” by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry:\nIl n’y eut rien qu’un éclair jaune près de sa cheville. Il demeura un instant immobile. Il ne cria pas. Il tomba doucement comme tombe un arbre. Ça ne fit même pas de bruit, à cause du sable.\nThere was nothing but a yellow flash near his ankle. He remained motionless for a moment. He did not scream. He fell softly, as a tree falls. It did not even make any noise, because of the sand.\nNowadays, most young French speakers learn only the third person singular and plural; and most contemporary authors narrate using le passé composé and le présent historique (lesson 42 note 2), prompting some grammarians to predict the imminent demise of le passé simple. Even so, a basic knowledge of how to conjugate the main verbs in this case is important.\n1.3 Differences with English\nAll the actions described with le passé simple are completed, whereas repeated actions in the past require the imperfect. In English, though, we can use the past simple in both cases. Compare:\nIl m’envoyait des lettres tous les jours pendant son voyage. He sent me letters every day while he was travelling.\nWith:\nIl m’envoya une lettre le premier jour de son voyage, et une autre à son retour. He sent me one letter on the first day of his trip and another upon his return.\n(In the first example in English, we can use would – he would send me, etc. – but never in French, because the action is habitual, not conditional.)\nAlthough the actions described with le passé simple are completed, they are not necessarily short. For example, a typical commemorative plaque on a building might read:\nThierry Blanquet, écrivain, vécut ici de 1960 à 1980. The writer Thierry Blanquet lived here from 1960 to 1980."},{"ref":"rev","title":"2 Use of affixes","text":"Throughout this course, we have seen how prefixes and suffixes modify the meanings of many verbs. Some of these are obvious because they are also used in English (for example, visiter ➔ revisiter, to visit ➔ to revisit). But English also uses phrasal verbs to the same effect. So chausser, déchausser is translated as to put on / take off shoes. This type of construction is not possible in French, which has no equivalent of these verb-preposition/adverb constructions.\nIn addition, whereas English has a diglossic, or dual-context, vocabulary, consisting of formal Graeco-Latin and informal Anglo-Saxon words, such as regal and kingly (see lesson 7 section 4), French does not. So, for example, améliorer can be translated as to improve, to make better or to ameliorate. This is why it is so important to identify the correct register in French: knowing the meaning of a word is not necessarily the same as knowing how and where to use it.\nHere is an overview of some of the main prefixes and suffixes that we have seen so far. We have omitted some of the more obvious Graeco-Latin ones, such as anti-, hyper- and télé- in order to save space.\n2.1 Prefixes\nPrefix | Meaning | Example\na- / an- | negation | moral ➔ amoral (moral, immoral)\na- | movement or direction | doux ➔ adoucir (soft, to soften)\ndé- / dés- | undo, separate | boucher ➔ déboucher (to block, to unblock)\né- | movement toward/away | loin ➔ éloigner (far, to distance)\nem- | movement into | ménage ➔ emménager (household, to move into a house)\nen- | change / movement to | dette ➔ s’endetter (debt, to get into debt)\ncom- / con- | together | pâtir ➔ compassion (to suffer, compassion, i.e. suffer with)\nim- / in- | negation | mobile ➔ immobile (mobile, motionless)\nmal- | bad | heureux ➔ malheureux (happy, unhappy)\nmé- | negative | se fier ➔ se méfier (to trust, to be wary of)\npro- | movement toward | jeter ➔ projeter (to throw, to plan, i.e. throw forward)\nsous- | under | payer ➔ sous-payer (to pay, to underpay)\nsuper- | above | poser ➔ superposer (to put, to put on top of, superimpose)\n2.2 Suffixes\nSuffix | Meaning | Example\n-aille | result of an action | se battre ➔ bataille (to fight, battle)\n-ateur / -atrice | person performing an action | présenter ➔ présentateur / présentatrice (to present, presenter (m/f))\n-âtre | approximation, -ish | bleu ➔ bleuâtre (blue, blueish)\n-er / -ère | profession (see -ier) | boulange ➔ boulanger / boulangère (bakery industry, baker (m/f))\n-erie | shop | boucher ➔ boucherie (butcher, butchery / butcher’s shop)\n-ée | result of an action | rentrer ➔ la rentrée (to go back, start of new school year)\n-esse | noun derived from adjective | gentil ➔ gentillesse (kind, kindness)\n-ier / -ière | profession (see -er) | police ➔ policier (police, police officer (m))\n-ment | result of an action | abonner ➔ abonnement (to subscribe, subscription)\n-ure | result of an action | brûler ➔ brûlure (to burn, a burn)\nSome of these suffixes do several jobs (-esse can designate a feminine proper noun: un prince, une princesse), but the context will almost always clarify the function if not provide the precise meaning.\nSpelling\nIn many cases, when a prefix is added, the initial consonant of the root word has to be doubled: a + léger ➔ alléger. Likewise, the initial c of most words is doubled: a + calme ➔ accalmie (a lull, respite). Six consonants are never doubled (h, j, q, v, w, x), while five others double only rarely (b, d, g, k, z). The rules are quite complex, with plenty of exceptions, so it is always best to check in a good dictionary.\nHopefully, the examples in the two tables above are immediately obvious, particularly since some of them are similar or identical to English. Sometimes, however, the meaning of a prefixed word demands a little guesswork (se débrouiller, lesson 93 note 7, grisâtre, lesson 96). Nonetheless, these basic indicators should put you on the right track and help you expand your vocabulary."},{"ref":"rev","title":"3 The past conditional – Conditionnel passé","text":"The past conditional (I would have bought/come) is relatively straightforward. It comprises the conditional of the verb’s usual past auxiliary (avoir, être), and its past participle:\n | acheter | venir\nje / j’ | aurais acheté | serais venu/e\ntu | aurais acheté | serais venu/e\nil/elle | aurait acheté | serait venu/e\nnous | aurions acheté | serions venus/-ues\nvous | auriez acheté | seriez venus/-ues\nils/elles | auraient acheté | seraient venus/-ues\nIn a full conditional sentence (I would have… if you had…, etc.), the second verb is in the pluperfect (lesson 87 note 4), as in English:\nJe serais venu si tu m’avais invité. I (m.) would have come if you had invited me.\nThe negative and interrogative forms follow the usual rules:\nSi j’avais su, je ne serais pas venu. I (m.) would never have come if I had known.\nEst-ce que tu serais / Serais-tu venu si tu avais su ? Would you (m.) have come if you had known?\nThe past conditional, like the present, can be used to talk about a possible action:\nAurais-tu oublié quelque chose ? Might you have forgotten something?\nMore idiomatically, it is frequently used in the media when reporting on events that have not yet been confirmed. The English equivalent in this case is generally the present perfect tense with a conjectural adverb:\nL’accident aurait fait deux morts, The accident has apparently/reportedly caused two deaths.\nor, more idiomatically, Two people have apparently/reportedly died in the accident."},{"ref":"rev","title":"4 Gentilics","text":"Lastly, as mentioned in lesson 96 note 7, the rules on the formation of gentilics are quite complex, especially those governing towns and cities. Country adjectives are more straightforward. Rather than a long explanation, here is a list of some of the most common masculine and feminine forms, organised by their endings. Remember that, unlike English, gentilic adjectives do not take an initial capital letter but agree in number and gender with their noun.\nSuffix | Country | Gentilic\n-ain / -aine | l’Amérique (f.) | Américain(e) – American*\n-ain / -aine | la Jamaïque | Jamaïcain(e) – Jamaican\n-ain / -aine | le Mexique | Mexicain(e) – Mexican\n-ais / -aise | l’Angleterre (f.) | Anglais(e) – English*\n-ais / -aise | l’Écosse (f.) | Écossais(e) – Scottish\n-ais / -aise | la France | Français(e) – French\n-ais / -aise | le Portugal | Portugais(e) – Portuguese\n-ois / -oise | la Chine | Chinois(e) – Chinese\n-ois / -oise | le Danemark | Danois(e) – Danish\n-ois / -oise | la Suède | Suédois(e) – Swedish\n-ien / -ienne | l’Australie (f.) | Australien(ne) – Australian\n-ien / -ienne | le Canada | Canadien(ne) – Canadian\n-ien / -ienne | l’Inde (f.) | Indien(ne) – Indian\n-ien / -ienne | l’Italie | Italien(ne) – Italian\n* The “official” gentilic for Les États-Unis is Étatsunien but this is rarely used. The most common gentilic for le Royaume-Uni, United Kingdom, is Britannique (British), although many French speakers persist in using Anglais for all four UK countries. Please avoid!\nThere are numerous exceptions to these rules, and several outliers such as Monaco ➔ Monégasque and Chypre ➔ Chypriote (Cyprus), so it’s easier to learn the most common gentilics and take note of new ones as you encounter them."},{"ref":"rev","title":"5 Idioms (3): Sports","text":"All the idioms in lesson 97 are related to sports. Some have direct (or almost direct) English equivalents, while others are not immediately obvious. But that, after all, is the definition of an idiomatic expression, whose overall meaning is different from its component words.\navoir le nez dans le guidon\nCycling. “to have one’s nose in the handlebars”: to be totally immersed in what one is doing. Note that le guidon is singular.\navoir le vent en poupe\nSailing. “to have the wind on the stern”: The technical expression means to run before the wind, but the idiom resembles to have the wind in one’s sails, i.e. to be on a roll. (La poupe is the origin of the poop (deck).)\nbotter en touche\nRugby. “to boot in touch”: The equivalent idiom is to kick into touch, though other meanings include to sidestep an issue.\nbrûler les étapes\nCycling. “to burn steps/stages”: Depending on the context, the idiom can mean to race ahead or to cut corners. (The Tour de France cycle race is divided into étapes (f.), stages.)\nchanger de cap\nSailing. “to change cape”: The idiomatic meaning is to change course or direction. Note that Le Cap is Cape Horn.\ncoiffer au poteau\nHorse racing. “to do hair at the post”: A similar English idiom is to win by a hair, but the most common equivalent is to pip at the post.\nencaisser les coups\nBoxing. “to encash blows”: The idea behind encaisser is to take in or absorb quite easily. In a broad context, the meaning is to roll with the punches.\nfaire un parcours sans faute\nHorse racing. “to do a course without fault,” i.e. to make a clear round or, idiomatically, to do/get everything right. (Un parcours du combattant, an obstacle course, is the origin of the English word parkour.)\njeter l’éponge\nBoxing. “to throw the sponge”: The idiom is almost identical: to throw in the towel.\nplacer la barre haut\nAthletics. “to place the bar high,” i.e. to set a high standard. Here, haut is an adverb, not an adjective, and therefore does not agree with la barre.\nreculer pour mieux sauter\nAthletics. “to move back in order to jump better”: This idiom has two slightly contrasting meanings, one positive: to make a strategic withdrawal (i.e. in order to come back stronger), the other negative: to put off the inevitable.\nrenvoyer dans les cordes\nBoxing. “to send back in the ropes”: Literally, to be thrown on the ropes but figuratively, to put someone (back) in their place.\ntirer son épingle du jeu\nGame. “to pull one’s pin from the game”: Taken from a Mikado-like game, the expression basically means to emerge unscathed or to get out of a tight spot. It has become a favourite cliché with journalists, meaning anything from to do OK to ride the storm.\nviser plein dans le mille\nArchery. “to aim fully in the thousand”: Here, le mille, thousand, is the highest score obtainable in an old ball-and-bucket game. The nearest equivalent is to score a bull’s-eye. The expression can be used as an exclamation: En plein dans le mille ! Bull’s-eye! / Bang on target!\nNow look back at lesson 97 to review these expressions in context."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[]},"exercise2":{"items":[]},"cards":[{"fr":"tu as l’air complètement KO","en":"you look completely exhausted","zh":"你看起来累坏了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Qu’est-ce qu’il y a, Driss ? Tu as l’air complètement KO. Ça ne va pas ?","exEn":"What’s up, Driss? You look completely exhausted. Aren’t things going well?","src":1},{"fr":"à l’époque","en":"at the time, back then","zh":"当时","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ epoch","ff":"","cluster":"time","kw":"","ex":"Je m’en souviens bien. C’est une histoire qui a défrayé la chronique à l’époque.","exEn":"I remember it well. It was a story that made the headlines at the time.","src":4},{"fr":"d’après moi","en":"in my opinion","zh":"依我看","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"C’est vrai, elle a fait couler beaucoup d’encre mais, d’après moi, on n’a jamais su toute la vérité.","exEn":"It’s true, a lot has been written about it, but in my opinion, we never knew the whole truth.","src":5},{"fr":"une somme faramineuse","en":"a huge sum of money","zh":"一笔天文数字的钱","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"money","kw":"","ex":"Je n’ai pas réussi à parler avec Dulong, ou, plutôt, il a exigé une somme faramineuse pour une interview.","exEn":"I didn’t manage to talk to Dulong, or rather, he demanded a huge sum of money for an interview.","src":6},{"fr":"il a donné son accord tout de suite","en":"he agreed immediately","zh":"他立刻就同意了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Mais son ancien agent, Jacques Cartier, lui, a donné son accord tout de suite. Bingo !","exEn":"But his former agent, Jacques Cartier, agreed immediately. Bingo!","src":7},{"fr":"il en avait très envie","en":"he really wanted to","zh":"他非常想这么做","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je pensais qu’il allait accepter du bout des lèvres, mais pas du tout ! Il en avait très envie. J’aurais dû m’en méfier, tant il était enthousiaste.","exEn":"I thought he would do it reluctantly, but not at all! He really wanted to. I should have been suspicious, he was so enthusiastic.","src":8},{"fr":"à chaque fois","en":"each time, every time","zh":"每一次","pos":"adv","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Je lui posais des questions vachement précises mais, à chaque fois, il bottait en touche. Il me racontait la vérité, soit. Mais sa vérité à lui !","exEn":"I asked him really precise questions, but each time he evaded the issue. He told me the truth, sure. But his truth!","src":9},{"fr":"tu aurais dû me demander de t’accompagner","en":"you should have asked me to go with you","zh":"你本该叫我陪你去","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu aurais dû me demander de t’accompagner. « Au secours ! J’ai besoin d’aide ! »","exEn":"You should have asked me to come with you. “Help! I need assistance!”","src":10}],"uncertain":["notes 2.1: the second 'a-' prefix row (movement or direction) is printed 'a- / an' in the OCR; given as 'a-' (examples adoucir, alléger, accalmie)","dialogue 6: 'J’ai n’a pas réussi' in the OCR corrected to 'Je n’ai pas réussi'","notes: prefix/suffix table layout rebuilt from a scrambled OCR"],"pdfPage":625,"gaps":[[1.98,4.12],[5.46,8.42],[75.02,79.02]],"dur":79},{"n":99,"kind":"lesson","title":"Incroyable mais vrai…","titleEn":"Incredible but true…","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":true,"fr":"Tu aurais pu me faire signe ou me texter : c’est la moindre des choses, entre amis.","en":"You could have got in touch with me or texted me: that’s the least you could do, between friends.","lit":"make sign; the least of things"},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Ça fait des semaines que tu devais m’appeler mais tu n’as pas fait le moindre effort.","en":"It’s been weeks since you were supposed to call me but you haven’t made the slightest effort.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"Bon, passons. Quoi de neuf ? Qu’est-ce que tu deviens ?","en":"But let’s move on. What’s new? What have you been up to?","lit":"what are you becoming"},{"i":4,"dash":true,"fr":"Moi et mes associés travaillons depuis des mois sur quelque chose d’hypersensible.","en":"My partners and I have been working for months on something hyper-sensitive.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":true,"fr":"Quel genre de chose ? Pas le projet dont tu m’as parlé dernièrement ?","en":"What kind of thing? Not the project you told me about recently?","lit":"lastly"},{"i":6,"dash":true,"fr":"Non, pas celui-là. Un autre, qui n’a rien à voir avec ça.","en":"No, not that one. Another one, which has nothing to do with it.","lit":"nothing to see"},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"Dès que nous aurons finalisé les derniers détails, et quand j’aurai complété le dossier,","en":"As soon as we have finalised the last details, and when I have completed the file,","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"je pourrai t’en parler plus longuement. Pour l’instant, je ne peux te donner que les grandes lignes.","en":"I will be able to tell you about it at greater length. For now, I can only give you the broad outlines.","lit":"big lines"},{"i":9,"dash":true,"fr":"Vas-y ! Je suis tout ouïe.","en":"Go ahead! I’m all ears.","lit":"hearing"},{"i":10,"dash":true,"fr":"Te souviens-tu du concours « Concevons la ville de demain » dont tout le monde parle depuis un an ?","en":"Do you remember the “Designing Tomorrow’s City” competition that everyone has been talking about for a year?","lit":""},{"i":11,"dash":true,"fr":"Bien sûr que je m’en souviens ! Et alors ?","en":"Of course I remember! And so?","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":true,"fr":"Disons que nous avons un certain avantage par rapport à nos concurrents.","en":"Let’s say we have a certain advantage over our competitors.","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous avons tout pensé dans les moindres détails, contrairement à eux.","en":"We’ve thought of everything, down to the last detail, unlike them.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Certains ont misé sur l’informatique – les logiciels et les technologies numériques – ainsi que les solutions haut débit.","en":"Some have bet on IT – software and digital technologies – as well as broadband solutions.","lit":"high flow"},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils envisagent l’avenir sans billets ni pièces de monnaie : que de l’argent virtuel.","en":"They envisage a future without notes and coins: just virtual money.","lit":"consider"},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"D’autres portent leur attention sur une question et une seule : la circulation automobile.","en":"Others are focusing their attention on one issue and one issue only: car traffic.","lit":"carrying"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"Ils pensent que la solution miracle serait d’interdire tous les véhicules, qu’ils soient particuliers ou professionnels,","en":"They believe that the miracle solution would be to ban all vehicles, whether private or professional,","lit":""},{"i":18,"dash":false,"fr":"et d’obliger tout le monde de se déplacer autrement, ou à pied, ou par les transports collectifs.","en":"and to oblige everyone to travel by other means, either on foot or by public transport.","lit":""},{"i":19,"dash":false,"fr":"Un des grands avantages, disent-ils, est qu’il n’y aura plus d’accidents de la route.","en":"One of the great advantages, they say, is that there will be no more road accidents.","lit":""},{"i":20,"dash":true,"fr":"Et aussi, il n’y aura plus de PV !","en":"And also, there’ll be no more parking tickets!","lit":""},{"i":21,"dash":true,"fr":"J’ai le sentiment que tu ne prends pas la chose au sérieux.","en":"I get the feeling that you are not taking things seriously.","lit":""},{"i":22,"dash":true,"fr":"Mais si, je te taquinais. Que propose ton équipe ?","en":"Yes I am, I was teasing you. What is your team proposing?","lit":""},{"i":23,"dash":true,"fr":"Nous avons une solution radicale pour s’attaquer aussi bien à la circulation qu’à la pollution :","en":"We have a radical solution to tackle both traffic and pollution:","lit":""},{"i":24,"dash":false,"fr":"nous allons construire les villes à la campagne, où l’air est plus pur.","en":"we’re going to build cities in the countryside, where the air is purer.","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The adjective moindre, meaning less, lesser, least, is used as a comparative or a superlative and also in set expressions: Merci pour votre aide ! – C’est la moindre des choses, Thanks for your help! – That’s the least I could do. Being an adjective, it agrees with the noun: les moindres détails (line 13)."},{"ref":"2","text":"The first person plural of passer (lesson 48 note 10) is used as a standalone imperative expression meaning Let’s move on to something else, Let’s say no more. Le travail n’est pas très bien fait mais, bon, passons (“let’s pass”): The job hasn’t been done well, but let’s say no more about it."},{"ref":"3","text":"Here is another idiomatic expression, with the verb devenir, to become. Qu’est-ce que tu deviens / vous devenez ? (“what are you becoming”) How are you doing? / What have you been up to? If a noun is used instead of a pronoun, it comes directly after the verb: Que devient Richard ? What’s Richard up to? In the past tense, however, the interrogative Qu’est-ce qu’il est devenu ? has the same meaning as its English equivalent: What became of him? As always, the context (and tense) are vital clues."},{"ref":"4","text":"French uses voir (instead of faire) in the expression n’avoir rien à voir, to have nothing to do with. Mon mari n’a rien à voir avec cette affaire, My husband has nothing to do with this business. The expression Ça n’a rien à voir, often shortened to Rien à voir, is a rejoinder, meaning That’s beside the point / That’s irrelevant."},{"ref":"5","text":"Here is the futur antérieur, or future perfect, composed of the future auxiliary and the past participle. Similar in construction to its English equivalent, this tense describes a future event in terms of being completed. J’aurai pris ma décision avant ce soir, I will have taken my decision before this evening. Like the simple future, it is used after time conjunctions (quand, lorsque, dès que, etc.), whereas English uses the present perfect: Je viendrai te voir dès que j’aurai terminé cet exercice, I will come to see you as soon as I have finished this exercise. See lesson 56 section 2."},{"ref":"6","text":"The feminine noun l’ouïe means hearing (it is related to l’oreille, ear, lesson 24 line 3). The word is often used in the expression être tout ouïe, to be all ears. The other four senses are la vue (sight), l’odorat (smell, masc.), le toucher (touch) and le goût (taste)."},{"ref":"7","text":"Like many French abstract nouns, l’informatique (fem.) covers a broad spectrum of meanings, in this case relating to information technology. We have seen it translated as computer (lessons 72 and 94), but it also refers to IT, computer science, data processing, etc. For example, France’s data protection statute is called la loi Informatique et Libertés. (The word is also the origin of the technical English term informatics.)"},{"ref":"8","text":"Another way of saying either… or is ou… ou : Ou tu m’aimes ou tu ne m’aimes pas, Either you love me or you don’t. While in English either can come before or after the noun / pronoun, in French it must come first. An alternative construction with exactly the same meaning is soit… soit (lesson 64 line 7)."},{"ref":"9","text":"un PV is short for un procès-verbal. In a motoring context, the initialism means a parking ticket (PV de stationnement) or speeding ticket (PV pour vitesse excessive). The full term has a broader meaning, usually a statement or a report, and, in a business context, the minutes of a meeting (un procès-verbal de réunion). Despite the literal translation (“verbal trial”), the offender goes to court only if they don’t pay l’amende (fem.), the fine."},{"ref":"10","text":"We know aussi… que as a comparative of equality or inequality: Il joue aussi bien/mal que moi, He plays as well/badly as me. However, the phrase aussi bien… que… is yet another way of saying both (lesson 57 note 2). À cause de la panne, nous avons tout perdu, aussi bien les données que les logiciels, Because of the breakdown, we lost everything, both the data and the software. Emphatic pronouns can be used instead of nouns: aussi bien lui que moi, both he and I."},{"ref":"","text":"Much of the vocabulary connected with advanced technology is imported directly from English. In information technology, however, French has succeeded in coining native words, such as un ordinateur and haut débit (“high flow,” broadband), that have taken root. But some of these terms do not always have a one-on-one equivalent. For example, un logiciel is a software program or package, whereas the uncountable English noun “software” would usually be rendered by a plural. She designs software, Elle conçoit des logiciels.\nAnother example is l’informatique (see note 7). As always, it is the context that will dictate not just the term but its grammatical form."}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Que devient Martin ? – Il est revenu en France il y a deux mois. Il aurait dû te faire signe.","a":"What is Martin up to? – He came back to France two months ago. He should have got in touch with you."},{"q":"Est-ce le livre dont vous m’avez parlé ? – Non, ça n’a rien à voir.","a":"Is this the book you told me about? – No, it has nothing to do with it."},{"q":"Elle ne s’est pas excusée mais, bon, passons. Nous avons d’autres chats à fouetter.","a":"She didn’t apologise but, well, never mind. We’ve got bigger fish to fry."},{"q":"Raconte-moi ton histoire. Je suis tout ouïe. – Je t’en parlerai plus longuement un autre jour.","a":"Tell me your story. I’m all ears. – I’ll tell you more about it another day."},{"q":"Quoi de neuf ? – Mes amis et moi sommes en train de finaliser les derniers détails de notre projet.","a":"What’s new? – My friends and I are putting the final touches to our project."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"When we have taken our decision, we will keep you posted.","t":"Quand nous {{aurons pris}} notre décision, {{nous vous tiendrons au courant}}."},{"cue":"We know each of our products down to the last detail, which gives us an advantage over our competitors.","t":"Nous connaissons {{chacun}} de nos produits dans {{les moindres détails}}, ce qui nous donne un avantage {{par rapport à}} nos concurrents."},{"cue":"We design software and digital solutions, both my husband and I.","t":"Nous {{concevons des logiciels}} et des solutions {{numériques}}, {{aussi bien}} mon mari {{que moi}}."},{"cue":"Either you love IT or you hate it: I have been telling you that for years!","t":"{{Ou}} vous aimez {{l’informatique ou}} vous la détestez : {{ça fait}} des années {{que je vous le dis}} !"},{"cue":"Will you have finished the paintwork before this evening? I hope there will be no more delays.","t":"Est-ce que {{vous aurez terminé}} les travaux de peinture avant ce soir ? J’espère {{qu’il n’y aura plus de retard}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"faire signe","en":"to get in touch (to give a sign)","zh":"联系；打个招呼","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu aurais pu me faire signe ou me texter : c’est la moindre des choses, entre amis.","exEn":"You could have got in touch with me or texted me: that’s the least you could do, between friends.","src":1},{"fr":"c’est la moindre des choses","en":"it’s the least one can do","zh":"这是最起码的（应该的）","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Tu aurais pu me faire signe ou me texter : c’est la moindre des choses, entre amis.","exEn":"You could have got in touch with me or texted me: that’s the least you could do, between friends.","src":1},{"fr":"Quoi de neuf ?","en":"What’s new?","zh":"有什么新鲜事吗？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Bon, passons. Quoi de neuf ? Qu’est-ce que tu deviens ?","exEn":"But let’s move on. What’s new? What have you been up to?","src":3},{"fr":"Qu’est-ce que tu deviens ?","en":"What have you been up to?","zh":"你最近怎么样？在忙什么？","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"greetings","kw":"","ex":"Bon, passons. Quoi de neuf ? Qu’est-ce que tu deviens ?","exEn":"But let’s move on. What’s new? What have you been up to?","src":3},{"fr":"un associé","en":"a partner (business)","zh":"合伙人","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"≈ associate","ff":"","cluster":"jobs","kw":"","ex":"Moi et mes associés travaillons depuis des mois sur quelque chose d’hypersensible.","exEn":"My partners and I have been working for months on something hyper-sensitive.","src":4},{"fr":"ça n’a rien à voir avec ça","en":"it has nothing to do with that","zh":"这跟那个毫无关系","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Non, pas celui-là. Un autre, qui n’a rien à voir avec ça.","exEn":"No, not that one. Another one, which has nothing to do with it.","src":6},{"fr":"je suis tout ouïe","en":"I’m all ears","zh":"我洗耳恭听","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “we”: you say “wee!” with your ear pressed to the door – all ears!","ex":"Vas-y ! Je suis tout ouïe.","exEn":"Go ahead! I’m all ears.","src":9},{"fr":"par rapport à nos concurrents","en":"compared to our competitors","zh":"与我们的竞争对手相比","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Disons que nous avons un certain avantage par rapport à nos concurrents.","exEn":"Let’s say we have a certain advantage over our competitors.","src":12},{"fr":"dans les moindres détails","en":"down to the last detail","zh":"每一个细节","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ detail","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Nous avons tout pensé dans les moindres détails, contrairement à eux.","exEn":"We’ve thought of everything, down to the last detail, unlike them.","src":13},{"fr":"les transports collectifs","en":"public transport","zh":"公共交通","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"et d’obliger tout le monde de se déplacer autrement, ou à pied, ou par les transports collectifs.","exEn":"and to oblige everyone to travel by other means, either on foot or by public transport.","src":18},{"fr":"un PV","en":"a (parking or speeding) ticket","zh":"罚单（停车或超速）","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et aussi, il n’y aura plus de PV !","exEn":"And also, there’ll be no more parking tickets!","src":20},{"fr":"j’ai le sentiment que","en":"I get the feeling that","zh":"我感觉……","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ sentiment","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai le sentiment que tu ne prends pas la chose au sérieux.","exEn":"I get the feeling that you are not taking things seriously.","src":21},{"fr":"prendre la chose au sérieux","en":"to take things seriously","zh":"认真对待此事","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ serious","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"J’ai le sentiment que tu ne prends pas la chose au sérieux.","exEn":"I get the feeling that you are not taking things seriously.","src":21},{"fr":"construire les villes à la campagne","en":"to build cities in the countryside","zh":"在乡下建城市","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"places","kw":"","ex":"nous allons construire les villes à la campagne, où l’air est plus pur.","exEn":"we’re going to build cities in the countryside, where the air is purer.","src":24}],"uncertain":["dialogue 4: 'hypersensible' (OCR 'hyper-sensible') normalised without hyphen","dialogue 18: 'obliger tout le monde de se déplacer' kept as printed (the usual construction is obliger à)"],"pdfPage":637,"gaps":[[2.14,4.06],[5.1,8.16],[116.76,120.52],[122.72,124.7],[129.24,132.42],[136.86,139.86],[143.88,146.78],[152.72,155.86],[161.06,165.24]],"dur":165.3},{"n":100,"kind":"lesson","title":"La langue française","titleEn":"The French language","intro":"","dialogue":[{"i":1,"dash":false,"fr":"Nous voici à la fin du livre, mais pas à la fin du voyage.","en":"Here we are at the end of the book, but not at the end of the journey.","lit":""},{"i":2,"dash":false,"fr":"Pour assurer votre réussite, vous devez continuer à lire, parler, écouter et écrire le plus possible.","en":"To ensure your success, you must continue to read, speak, listen and write as much as possible.","lit":""},{"i":3,"dash":false,"fr":"À propos de voyages, vous pouvez désormais profiter de vos connaissances en faisant un tour du monde francophone.","en":"Speaking of travel, you can now take advantage of what you’ve learned by going on a trip around the French-speaking world.","lit":""},{"i":4,"dash":false,"fr":"Vous serez le bienvenu non seulement en Suisse, en Belgique et au Luxembourg mais partout où l’on parle le français.","en":"You will be welcome not only in Switzerland, Belgium and Luxembourg but everywhere where French is spoken.","lit":""},{"i":5,"dash":false,"fr":"Bien sûr, vous trouverez des différences d’accent et de vocabulaire relatif à des spécificités culturelles.","en":"Of course, you will find differences in accent and vocabulary relating to specific cultural features.","lit":"specificities"},{"i":6,"dash":false,"fr":"Au Canada, par exemple, vous pourrez faire vos emplettes chez un dépanneur, manger un chien-chaud ou encore conduire votre char.","en":"In Canada, for example, you will be able to do your shopping at a convenience store, eat a hot dog or drive your car.","lit":""},{"i":7,"dash":false,"fr":"En Afrique francophone, « un alphabète » est une personne qui sait lire et écrire","en":"In French-speaking Africa, a “literate person” is one who can read and write","lit":""},{"i":8,"dash":false,"fr":"et « un taxieur » vous conduira à votre destination – mais pas avant d’avoir fait le plein à une « essencerie ».","en":"and a “taxi driver” will drive you to your destination – but not before filling up at a “gas station.”","lit":""},{"i":9,"dash":false,"fr":"Des mots de diverses origines ont toujours enrichi notre vocabulaire : d’« arobase » à « vasistas »","en":"Words from various origins have always enriched our vocabulary: from the “At” symbol to “fanlight”","lit":""},{"i":10,"dash":false,"fr":"en passant par « le caoutchouc », « le goudron » ou encore « le paquebot »","en":"along with “rubber,” “tar” or “ocean liner”","lit":"passing by"},{"i":11,"dash":false,"fr":"le français est riche, varié et très accueillant.","en":"French is rich, varied and very welcoming.","lit":""},{"i":12,"dash":false,"fr":"C’est aussi une langue précise et nuancée, forte de son héritage de langue diplomatique :","en":"It is also a precise and nuanced language, with a strong diplomatic heritage:","lit":""},{"i":13,"dash":false,"fr":"« Ce qui n’est pas clair n’est pas français », disait un grand auteur.","en":"“What is not clear cannot be French,” said a great author.","lit":""},{"i":14,"dash":false,"fr":"Et puis il y a la Francophonie, un « pays sans frontières » où des centaines de millions de gens partagent leur passion pour cette belle langue.","en":"And then there is La Francophonie, a “country without borders” where hundreds of millions of people share their passion for this beautiful language.","lit":""},{"i":15,"dash":false,"fr":"Maintenant, vous aussi, vous faites partie de cette communauté francophone,","en":"Now, you too are part of this French-speaking community,","lit":""},{"i":16,"dash":false,"fr":"composée de femmes et d’hommes qui peuvent se permettre de déclarer :","en":"made up of women and men who can afford to declare:","lit":"composed; allow themselves"},{"i":17,"dash":false,"fr":"« Ma patrie, c’est la langue française ! »","en":"“My homeland is the French language!”","lit":""}],"notes":[{"ref":"1","text":"The suffix -phone can be used to form nouns and adjectives relating to speakers of particular languages. Thus the noun un francophone is a French-speaker, and the adjective means French-speaking: un pays francophone, a country where French is spoken. La Francophonie is the usual title of an organisation promoting the French language internationally. For more information, visit www.francophonie.org."},{"ref":"2","text":"bienvenu (“well come”) is an adjective: un accord bienvenu, a welcome agreement. As such, when used in greetings, it agrees in number and gender: Vous êtes le bienvenu and Soyez la bienvenue. But in the expression souhaiter la bienvenue, it is a noun and always takes a final “e”: Je vais leur souhaiter la bienvenue, I’m going to welcome them. At the beginning of a sentence, spoken as a greeting, we always use bienvenue alone: Bienvenue dans notre nouvel appartement, Welcome to our new flat (see lesson 6). This may all seem a little complicated, but the word – however spelled – is instantly understandable."},{"ref":"3","text":"Canadian French has a distinctive accent and vocabulary. For example, un dépanneur, meaning a repairman or mechanic in France, is a convenience store. Some words come from old French (un char, a car, derives from the word for a cart), while others are imported from America and then – importantly – translated: un chien-chaud, a hot dog!"},{"ref":"4","text":"French is an official language in nearly 30 countries on the African continent. Many words and expressions from these regions find their way into “official” French via mainstream dictionaries. Thus un alphabète, a literate person, is a logical back-formation from analphabète, illiterate; un taxieur is a local equivalent to un chauffeur de taxi while une essencerie (from l’essence, lesson 66) is a petrol station (rather than une station-service). Though not used in a formal register, these idioms nevertheless show that French is a living language."},{"ref":"5","text":"Derived from an Arabic word, une arobase is the official term for the At symbol, used in email addresses. When spelling out an address, however, some French speakers say at rather than arobase. We insist on the latter (listen to the first sentence of the first exercise)."},{"ref":"6","text":"un vasistas is a fanlight or small window. The word comes from the German interrogative Was ist das?, What is it?, the gruff greeting supposedly shouted at thirsty French visitors knocking at the spy window of local taverns. Another “gallicised” loan word, this time from English, is un paquebot, an ocean liner, derived from the old term a packet boat. These, and dozens of other borrowings, show how the French vocabulary has been enriched through interactions with other languages and cultures over the centuries."},{"ref":"7","text":"The adjectival phrase fort(e) de is widely used – usually at the beginning of a sentence – in descriptive writing to emphasise a strength, quality or advantage. Fort de son expérience, il a tout organisé, Drawing on a wealth of experience, he organised everything. The actual translation will depend as always on the context because, as with many idiomatic constructions, there is no single translation."},{"ref":"","text":"We hope you have enjoyed the course – but it isn’t over yet! You still have to complete the Productive Phase, from lesson 52 through to lesson 100. And, of course, you should take every opportunity to read, listen to and practise French. As we said before, Bonne continuation !"}],"exercise1":{"dir":"fr-en","items":[{"q":"Envoyez-moi le document tout à l’heure par mail à l’adresse suivante : jean@assimil.fr.","a":"Send me the document later by email at the following address: jean@assimil.fr."},{"q":"Forte de sa réussite en France, la société vise maintenant des marchés dans les pays francophones.","a":"Buoyed by its success in France, the company is now targeting markets in French-speaking countries."},{"q":"Tu peux désormais passer tes appels et partager tes fichiers sans utiliser un logiciel spécifique.","a":"From now on you can make calls and share files without using a specific software program."},{"q":"Voici une étude qui présente la spécificité de notre langue et notre culture.","a":"Here is a study that presents the specific features of our language and our culture."},{"q":"Toi aussi, tu devrais profiter des vacances pour faire un tour au Canada.","a":"You too, you should take advantage of the holidays to do a tour of Canada."}]},"exercise2":{"items":[{"cue":"This product is ideal for getting rid of tar, rubber and paint from furniture and clothes.","t":"{{Ce produit}} est idéal pour éliminer {{le goudron}}, {{le caoutchouc}}, et la peinture des {{meubles}} et des {{vêtements}}."},{"cue":"An illiterate is a person who does not know how to read or to write.","t":"Un {{analphabète}} est une personne {{qui ne sait ni lire ni écrire}}."},{"cue":"Welcome to my place. It’s nice of you to welcome us in person.","t":"Vous {{êtes le bienvenu}} chez moi ! C’est gentil de nous {{souhaiter la bienvenue}} en personne."},{"cue":"This taxi driver will drive you to your destination in Paris but first he has to fill up at a petrol station.","t":"Ce {{chauffeur de taxi}} te conduira à {{ta destination}} à Paris mais d’abord il doit {{faire le plein}} à {{la station-service}}."},{"cue":"Our homeland is the French language, for anything that is not clear cannot be French.","t":"{{Notre patrie}}, c’est {{la langue française}}, car {{ce qui n’est pas clair n’est pas français}}."}]},"cards":[{"fr":"continuer à lire, parler, écouter et écrire","en":"to continue to read, speak, listen and write","zh":"继续读、说、听、写","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour assurer votre réussite, vous devez continuer à lire, parler, écouter et écrire le plus possible.","exEn":"To ensure your success, you must continue to read, speak, listen and write as much as possible.","src":2},{"fr":"le plus possible","en":"as much as possible","zh":"尽可能多","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Pour assurer votre réussite, vous devez continuer à lire, parler, écouter et écrire le plus possible.","exEn":"To ensure your success, you must continue to read, speak, listen and write as much as possible.","src":2},{"fr":"à propos de voyages","en":"speaking of travel","zh":"说到旅行","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À propos de voyages, vous pouvez désormais profiter de vos connaissances en faisant un tour du monde francophone.","exEn":"Speaking of travel, you can now take advantage of what you’ve learned by going on a trip around the French-speaking world.","src":3},{"fr":"vous pouvez désormais profiter de vos connaissances","en":"you can now take advantage of what you know","zh":"您现在可以运用所学的知识了","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À propos de voyages, vous pouvez désormais profiter de vos connaissances en faisant un tour du monde francophone.","exEn":"Speaking of travel, you can now take advantage of what you’ve learned by going on a trip around the French-speaking world.","src":3},{"fr":"un tour du monde","en":"a trip around the world","zh":"环游世界","pos":"n","g":"m","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"À propos de voyages, vous pouvez désormais profiter de vos connaissances en faisant un tour du monde francophone.","exEn":"Speaking of travel, you can now take advantage of what you’ve learned by going on a trip around the French-speaking world.","src":3},{"fr":"non seulement… mais","en":"not only… but","zh":"不仅……而且","pos":"conj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous serez le bienvenu non seulement en Suisse, en Belgique et au Luxembourg mais partout où l’on parle le français.","exEn":"You will be welcome not only in Switzerland, Belgium and Luxembourg but everywhere where French is spoken.","src":4},{"fr":"partout où l’on parle le français","en":"everywhere French is spoken","zh":"所有说法语的地方","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Vous serez le bienvenu non seulement en Suisse, en Belgique et au Luxembourg mais partout où l’on parle le français.","exEn":"You will be welcome not only in Switzerland, Belgium and Luxembourg but everywhere where French is spoken.","src":4},{"fr":"des différences d’accent et de vocabulaire","en":"differences in accent and vocabulary","zh":"口音和词汇上的差异","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ differences, accent, vocabulary","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Bien sûr, vous trouverez des différences d’accent et de vocabulaire relatif à des spécificités culturelles.","exEn":"Of course, you will find differences in accent and vocabulary relating to specific cultural features.","src":5},{"fr":"faire vos emplettes","en":"to do your shopping","zh":"购物；采购","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"sounds like “on-plet”: imagine you plait (braid) your shopping bags on the way home","ex":"Au Canada, par exemple, vous pourrez faire vos emplettes chez un dépanneur, manger un chien-chaud ou encore conduire votre char.","exEn":"In Canada, for example, you will be able to do your shopping at a convenience store, eat a hot dog or drive your car.","src":6},{"fr":"faire le plein","en":"to fill up (with petrol)","zh":"加满油","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"transport","kw":"","ex":"et « un taxieur » vous conduira à votre destination – mais pas avant d’avoir fait le plein à une « essencerie ».","exEn":"and a “taxi driver” will drive you to your destination – but not before filling up at a “gas station.”","src":8},{"fr":"votre destination","en":"your destination","zh":"您的目的地","pos":"n","g":"f","cog":"≈ destination (same as English)","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"et « un taxieur » vous conduira à votre destination – mais pas avant d’avoir fait le plein à une « essencerie ».","exEn":"and a “taxi driver” will drive you to your destination – but not before filling up at a “gas station.”","src":8},{"fr":"très accueillant","en":"very welcoming","zh":"非常热情好客","pos":"adj","g":"","cog":"","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"le français est riche, varié et très accueillant.","exEn":"French is rich, varied and very welcoming.","src":11},{"fr":"partager leur passion","en":"to share their passion","zh":"分享他们的热情","pos":"v","g":"","cog":"≈ share, passion","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Et puis il y a la Francophonie, un « pays sans frontières » où des centaines de millions de gens partagent leur passion pour cette belle langue.","exEn":"And then there is La Francophonie, a “country without borders” where hundreds of millions of people share their passion for this beautiful language.","src":14},{"fr":"vous faites partie de cette communauté","en":"you are part of this community","zh":"您是这个群体的一员","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ community","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"Maintenant, vous aussi, vous faites partie de cette communauté francophone,","exEn":"Now, you too are part of this French-speaking community,","src":15},{"fr":"Ma patrie, c’est la langue française !","en":"My homeland is the French language!","zh":"我的祖国就是法语！","pos":"phrase","g":"","cog":"≈ patriot","ff":"","cluster":"","kw":"","ex":"« Ma patrie, c’est la langue française ! »","exEn":"“My homeland is the French language!”","src":17}],"uncertain":["dialogue 9: 'de arobase' of the OCR given as d’« arobase »","closing note: the OCR text 'lesson 700' read as 'lesson 100'"],"pdfPage":643,"gaps":[[1.5,3.4],[4.78,7.5],[48.56,49.56],[103.02,106.72],[109,111.28],[116.94,119.94],[125.12,128.22],[134.24,137.24],[141.2,144.32],[148.4,150.18]],"dur":150.2}];
